(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["2-vatican-ii"]=[["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p1","Concilium Oecumenicum Vaticanum II Sollemniter Inchoatur","- Mother Church rejoices that, by the singular gift of Divine Providence, the longed-for day has at last dawned when, under the auspices of the Virgin Mother of God, whose maternal dignity is commemorated today in festal rite, here at the tomb of blessed Peter the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council solemnly begins.","On the 11th day of the month of October, 1962"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p2","Venerable Brothers,","","Gaudet Mater Ecclesia quod, singulari Divinae Providentiae munere, optatissimus iam dies illuxit, quo, auspice Deipara Virgine, cuius materna dignitas hodie festo ritu recolitur, hic ad Beati Petri sepulcrum Concilium Oecumenicum Vaticanum Secundum sollemniter initium capit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p3","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","The most recent and humble successor of the same Prince of the Apostles, who is addressing you, by indicting this most ample assembly, proposed this to himself: that the ecclesiastical Magisterium, never failing and persevering until the end of time, might be affirmed again; which Magisterium, having regard for the errors, necessities, and opportunities of our age, is exhibited in an extraordinary way in the present through this council itself to all men, as many as are in the whole world.","Universa Concilia — sive viginti Oecumenica sive innumera eaque non parvi pendenda Provincialia et Regionalia — quae per succedentia tempora sunt celebrata, manifesto vigorem Ecclesiae Catholicae comprobant et quasi fulgentia lumina in eius annalibus recensentur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p4","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","Therefore, beginning this universal Synod, the Vicar of Christ, who speaks to you, as is clear, looks back upon past times and listens, as it were, to their voice, alert and confirming the spirit: for he gladly remembers the well-deserving Supreme Pontiffs who were in the most ancient and recent age, and who from the assemblies of councils, whether held in the East or in the West from the fourth century to the Middle Ages and recent times, transmitted testimonies of such venerable and weighty voice. These, with unceasing study, extol the triumph of that divine and human Society, namely the Church of Christ, which receives its name, the gifts of grace, and its whole strength from the Divine Redeemer.","Recentiorem humilisque eiusdem Principis Apostolorum Successor, qui vos alloquitur, amplissimum hunc Coetum indicens, id sibi proposuit, ut iterum Magisterium Ecclesiasticum, numquam deficiens et ad finem usque temporum perseverans, affirmaretur; quod quidem Magisterium rationem habens errorum, necessitatum, rerum opportunarum nostrae aetatis, per hoc ipsum Concilium omnibus hominibus, quotquot in orbe terrarum sunt, extraordinario modo, in praesenti exhibetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p5","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","But if these are causes of spiritual joy, we cannot, however, deny that many sorrows and bitternesses through the long series of nineteen centuries have darkened this history. For there existed and exists what once the aged Simeon said prophetically to Mary the Mother of Jesus: \"Behold, this child is set for the fall and for the resurrection of many... and for a sign which shall be contradicted\" (Lk 2:34). And Jesus Himself, more mature in age, clearly showed how men through the ages would conduct themselves against Him, uttering these mysterious words: \"He who hears you, hears me\" (Lk 10:16). Moreover, He spoke these words, as we see written in St. Luke, who also relates the above words: \"He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters\" (Lk 11:23).","Hanc igitur universalem incohans Synodum, Christi Vicarius, qui ad vos verba facit, praeterita tempora, ut liquet, respicit eorumque veluti vocem, alacrem animosque confirmantem, auscultat: libenter enim meminit bene meritorum Summorum Pontificum, qui antiquissima et recentiore aetate fuerunt; et qui e Conciliorum conventibus, sive in Oriente sive in Occidente a saeculo quarto ad Medium usque Aevum et recentia tempora actis, venerandae gravisque huiusmodi vocis testimonia transmiserunt. Haec non intermisso studio triumphum divinae et humanae illius Societatis extollunt, scilicet Ecclesiae Christi, quae a Divino Redemptore nomen, gratiae munera, vimque accipit totam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p6","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","Truly grave matters and questions to be resolved by the human race, after nearly twenty centuries, do not change; for Jesus Christ always holds, as it were, the central place in history and in life: men either adhere to Him and His Church, and thus enjoy the goods of light, sweetness, right order, and peace; or they live without Him or act against Him and deliberately remain outside the Church, whence confusion occurs among them, mutual relations become harsh, and the danger of bloody wars impends.","Quodsi haec spiritualis laetitiae causa sunt, negare tamen non possumus dolores et acerbitates plurimas per longam undeviginti saeculorum seriem hanc historiam obscuravisse. Verum enim exstitit et exstat, quod olim Simeon senex ad Mariam Matrem Iesu ore prophetico dixit: \"Ecce positus est hic in ruinam et in resurrectionem multorum... et in signum cui contradicetur\" (Lc 2,34). Atque Iesus ipse, aetate maturior, perspicue ostendit, quomodo homines per tempora adversus Ipsum se essent gesturi, arcana haec proferens verba: \"Qui vos audit, me audit\" (Lc 10,16). Praeterea haec effatus est, ut apud Sanctum Lucam, qui superiora quoque verba refert, scriptum videmus: \"Qui non est mecum, contra me est, et qui non colligit mecum, dispergit\" (Lc 11,23)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p7","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","This union with Christ and His Church, the Ecumenical Councils, whenever it happens that they are celebrated, proclaim in a solemn manner and send forth the light of truth in every direction, direct the life of individuals, domestic life, and society into straight paths, excite and strengthen spiritual forces, and constantly lift up minds to true and everlasting goods.","Gravissimae sane res et quaestiones ab humano genere dissolvendae, post viginti fere saecula non mutantur; etenim Christus Iesus semper medium quasi locum tenet historiae et vitae: homines aut ipsi ipsiusque Ecclesiae adhaerent, atque adeo lucis, suavitatis, recti ordinis pacisque bonis fruuntur; aut sine ipso vivunt vel contra ipsum agunt et consulto extra Ecclesiam commorantur, quo fit ut confusio inter eos habeatur, mutuae rationes asperae efficiantur, cruentorum bellorum impendeat periculum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p8","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","The testimonies of this extraordinary Magisterium of the Church, namely of universal Synods, are before our eyes, while we look upon the various ages of men through these twenty centuries of the Christian era. These documents are contained in many volumes of great weight and are to be esteemed as a sacred treasure, which is hidden in the archives of the city of Rome and in the most celebrated libraries of the whole world.","Hanc coniunctionem cum Christo eiusque Ecclesia Concilia Oecumenica, quotiescumque ea celebrari contingit, sollemni quodam modo praedicant et veritatis lucem quoquoversus emittunt, vitam singulorum hominum, domestici convictus, societatis in rectas semitas dirigunt, spirituales vires excitant atque stabiliunt, ad vera et sempiterna bona continenter animos erigunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p9","Ecumenical Councils in the Church","","Testimonia extraordinarii huius Magisterii Ecclesiae, scilicet universalium Synodorum, ob oculos Nostros versantur, dum varias hominum aetates per haec viginti saecula christiani aevi intuemur. Quae documenta pluribus magnique ponderis voluminibus continentur ac veluti sacer thesaurus sunt aestimanda, qui in tabulariis Urbis Romae ac totius orbis terrarum celebratissimis bibliothecis est reconditus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p10","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","Immediately the minds of those present were suddenly touched, as if sparkling with a ray of supernal light, and all were gently affected in face and eyes. At the same time, a vehement zeal burned throughout the whole world, and all men began to expect the celebration of the council with eager desire.","Quod autem ad originem et causam attinet maximi huius eventus, propter quem placuit Nobis vos hic congregare, satis est iterum afferre testimonium humile quidem, sed quod Nos ipsi experientia possumus probare: primo enim paene ex inopinato hoc Concilium mente concepimus, ac deinde simplicibus verbis enuntiavimus coram Sacro Purpuratorum Patrum Collegio fausto die illo vicesimo quinto mensis Ianuarii anno millesimo nongentesimo quinquagesimo nono, in festo Conversionis Sancti Pauli Apostoli, in ipsa eius Patriarchali Basilica ad viam Ostiensem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p11","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","In the meantime, three years of laborious work were accomplished for preparing the council, which pertained to investigating more accurately and more fully with what honor the Faith, the practice of religion, the vigor of the Christian and especially the Catholic assembly, should be held in our age.","Statim adstantium animi subito tacti sunt, quasi supernae lucis radio coruscante, et suaviter omnes affecti in vultu oculisque. Simul vero vehemens studium toto terrarum orbe exarsit, cunctique homines Concilii celebrationem studiose exspectare coeperunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p12","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","Indeed, it seems to us not undeservedly that this time spent in preparing the Ecumenical Council has been a first sign and gift of heavenly grace.","Interea tres annos ad apparandum Concilium laboriosum est opus patratum, quod eo pertinebat, ut accuratius et amplius inquireretur, quo honore essent hac nostra aetate Fides, religionis usus, vigor coetus christiani ac praesertim catholici."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p13","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","Therefore, illuminated by the light of this Council, the Church will be enriched with spiritual riches, as we trust, and, drawing from it the strength of new powers, will look forward without fear to future times. For, by opportune emendations introduced and mutual helpful work wisely instituted, the Church will bring it about that men, families, and nations truly turn their minds to those things which are above.","Profecto haud immerito Nobis videtur hoc tempus in apparando Concilio Oecumenico insumptum primum quoddam signum ac donum gratiae caelestis fuisse."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p14","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","Thus, on account of the celebration of this Council, the greatest thanks are to be rendered to the Supreme Giver of all goods from the duty of conscience, and the glory of Christ the Lord is to be proclaimed with exultation, who is the invincible and immortal King of the ages and of the nations.","Huius ergo Concilii lumine illustrata, Ecclesia spiritualibus divitiis, ut confidimus, augebitur atque, novarum virium robur ex illo hauriens, intrepide futura prospiciet tempora. Nam, opportunis inductis emendationibus ac mutua auxiliatrice opera sapienter instituta, Ecclesia efficiet, ut homines, familiae, nationes reapse ad ea, quae supra sunt, mentes convertant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p15","The Origin and Cause of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council","Namely, it is permissible for us, in the inflammation of holy joy which at this solemn hour affects our spirits, to declare openly in this most ample assembly that the beginning of this Ecumenical Synod happens at opportune circumstances of time.","Itaque propter Concilii huius celebrationem Summo Largitori omnium bonorum gratiae maximae ex conscientiae officio sunt reddendae, et gloria Christi Domini cum exsultatione praedicanda, qui est Rex invictus et immortalis saeculorum ac gentium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p16","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","For it often happens, as we have come to know in the daily pursuit of the apostolic ministry, that certain voices are brought to our hearing, not without offense to our ears, of those who, although inflamed with religious zeal, nevertheless do not weigh things with sufficiently equitable estimation and prudent judgment. For these, in the present conditions of human society, can see nothing but ruins and calamities; they continually say that our times, if compared with past centuries, have gone completely for the worse; and they behave as if from history, which is the teacher of life, they have nothing to learn, and as if at the time of previous councils everything proceeded prosperously and rightly with respect to Christian doctrine, morals, and the just liberty of the Church.","Scilicet, in accensionem laetitiae sanctae, quae sollemni hac hora animos afficit nostros, liceat Nobis hoc in amplissimo consessu palam asseverare, initium Oecumenicae huius Synodi opportunis sane temporis adiunctis contingere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p17","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","But it seems to us that we must completely disagree with these prophets of misfortune, who always announce worse things, as if the end of the world were at hand.","Saepe quidem accidit, quemadmodum in cotidiano obeundo apostolico ministerio comperimus, ut non sine aurium Nostrarum offensione quorundam voces ad Nos perferantur, qui, licet religionis studio incensi, non satis tamen aequa aestimatione prudentique iudicio res perpendunt. Hi enim, in praesentibus humanae societatis condicionibus, nonnisi ruinas calamitatesque cernere valent; dictitant nostra tempora, si cum elapsis saeculis comparentur, prorsus in peius abiisse; atque adeo ita se habent, quasi ex historia, quae vitae magistra est, nihil habeant quod discant, ac veluti si, superiorum Conciliorum tempore, quoad christianam doctrinam, quoad mores, quoad iustam Ecclesiae libertatem, omnia prospere ac recte processerint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p18","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","In the present course of human events, in which human society seems to be entering a new order of things, the hidden counsels of Divine Providence must rather be acknowledged, which, through succeeding times, through the work of men, and often beyond their expectation, attain their outcome, and wisely dispose all things, even adverse human events, for the good of the Church.","At Nobis plane dissentiendum esse videtur ab his rerum adversarum vaticinatoribus, qui deteriora semper praenuntiant, quasi rerum exitium instet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p19","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","This can be easily seen if one attentively considers the most grave questions of politics and economics and the crises which exist today. All of which hold men so anxious that they turn their cares and thoughts away from religious affairs which pertain to the sacred Magisterium of the Church. This manner of acting is certainly not without fault, and is justly to be disapproved. Yet no one can deny that these new conditions of things have at least this advantage, that countless impediments are now removed by which the sons of the world used to hinder the free action of the Church. For it is enough to run cursorily through ecclesiastical annals to see immediately clearly that even the Ecumenical Councils themselves—whose vicissitudes are recorded in golden letters in the fasti of the Church—were often celebrated not without great difficulties and causes of sorrow, on account of the undue interposed authority of the civil power. For the Princes of this world sometimes indeed proposed to themselves with sincere heart to undertake the patronage of the Church; but this was done for the most part not without spiritual detriment and peril, since they were often led by political reasons and were too studious of their own advantages.","In praesenti humanorum eventuum cursu, quo hominum societas novum rerum ordinem ingredi videtur, potius arcana Divinae Providentiae consilia agnoscenda sunt, quae per tempora succedentia, hominum opera, ac plerumque praeter eorum exspectationem, suum exitum consequuntur, atque omnia, adversos etiam humanos casus, in Ecclesiae bonum sapienter disponunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p20","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","We confess indeed that today we are moved with vehement sorrow that among you many Pastors of the Church are lacking, most dear to us, who are detained in chains on account of the faith of Christ or hindered by other impediments, whose memory moves us to offer most fervent prayers to God for them; yet we see that it has come about today not without hope and great consolation to us that the Church, at length freed from so many profane impediments of the past age, from this Vatican Temple, as if from another Upper Room of the Apostles, through you can lift up her voice, full of majesty and gravity.","Hoc facile cernere est, si attento animo perpendantur gravissimae de re politica et oeconomica quaestiones atque discrimina, quae hodie sunt. Quae omnia tam sollicitos homines tenent, ut eorum curas cogitationesque a religiosis negotiis avertant, quae ad sacrum pertinent Ecclesiae Magisterium. Huiusmodi agendi ratio certe vitio non caret, ac merito est improbanda. Nemo tamen negare potest, has novas inductas rerum condiciones, id saltem commodi habere, ut e medio innumera illa impedimenta iam auferantur, quibus olim saeculi filii liberam Ecclesiae actionem praepedire consueverant. Etenim satis est cursim ecclesiasticos annales pervolvere, ut statim manifesto pateat, vel ipsa Oecumenica Concilia, quorum vicissitudines aureis litteris Ecclesiae fastis consignatae sunt, saepe non sine gravissimis difficultatibus ac doloris causis, ob indebitam civilis potestatis interpositam auctoritatem, celebrata fuisse. Huius enim mundi Principes interdum sibi quidem proponebant sincero animo Ecclesiae patrocinium suscipere; quod tamen plerumque non sine spirituali detrimento ac periculo fiebat, cum iidem saepius rationibus politicis ducerentur suisque utilitatibus nimium studerent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p21","The Opportunity of Celebrating the Council","This doctrine embraces the whole man, composed of body and soul, and commands us, who are inhabitants of this earth, to press forward as pilgrims to the heavenly homeland.","Fatemur quidem hodie Nos vehementi dolore affici, quod inter vos complures Ecclesiae Pastores desiderantur, Nobis sane carissimi, qui ob Christi Fidem in vinculis detinentur vel aliis impedimentis praepediuntur, et quorum recordatio Nos permovet ut pro ipsis flagrantissimas Deo preces admoveamus; attamen non sine spe ac magno solacio Nostro hodie factum esse cernimus, ut Ecclesia, tot profanis praeteritae aetatis impedimentis tandem expedita, ex hoc Vaticano Templo, veluti altero Apostolorum Cenaculo, per vos vocem suam, maiestatis gravitatisque plenam, attollere possit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p22","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","And it shows how this mortal life is to be ordered, so that, satisfying the duties by which we are bound to the earthly and the heavenly City, we may be able to attain the end established for us by God. Namely, all men absolutely, whether individually or united in society, are without interruption held by the duty to pursue heavenly goods as long as this life lasts, and for attaining this, to use earthly things, yet in such a way that the use of temporal goods does not bring their eternal beatitude into peril.","Haec doctrina integrum complectitur hominem, corpore et animo compositum, eademque iubet nos, qui huius terrae incolae sumus, ad caelestem patriam peregrinos contendere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p23","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","It is truly true that Christ the Lord pronounced this sentence: \"Seek first the kingdom of God and His justice\" (Mt 6:33). This word \"first\" declares to what especially our strength and thoughts ought to be directed; nevertheless, the other words of this Lord's precept which follow are by no means to be neglected: \"and all these things shall be added unto you\" (Mt 6:34).","Id autem ostendit, qua ratione mortalis haec vita componenda sit, ut nos officiis satisfacientes, quibus erga terrestrem et caelestem Civitatem obstringimur, finem nobis a Deo praestitutum assequi valeamus. Scilicet, omnes prorsus homines, sive singuli sive inter se societate coniuncti, sine intermissione officio tenentur caelestia bona consectandi, donec haec vita suppetit, atque ad hoc assequendum, terrenas res adhibendi, ita tamen ut temporalium bonorum usus sempiternam eorum beatitatem in discrimen non adducat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p24","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","And indeed there have always been and are in the Church those who, while they strive with all study to attain evangelical perfection, at the same time contribute their helping work to civil culture, since from the examples of their life and from their salutary undertakings of charity, those things which are more excellent and noble in human society receive no small strength and increase.","Verum profecto est, Christum Dominum hanc pronuntiasse sententiam: \"Quaerite primum regnum Dei et iustitiam eius\" (Mt 6,33). Quae vox \"primum\" declarat, quo potissimum vires et cogitationes nostras dirigi oporteat; nihilominus minime neglegenda sunt cetera huius Dominici praecepti verba, quae subsequuntur: \"et haec omnia adicientur vobis\" (Mt 6,34)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p25","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","So that this doctrine may touch the manifold fields of human activity which pertain to individuals, to domestic life, and to social life, it is first necessary that the Church never turn her eyes away from the sacred patrimony of truth received from the fathers; but at the same time she must also look upon the present times, which have introduced new conditions of things, new forms of living, and have opened new paths for the catholic apostolate.","Ac revera semper fuerunt ac sunt in Ecclesia qui, dum evangelicae perfectioni adipiscendae omni studio vacant, simul ad civilem cultum adiutricem operam conferunt, cum ex eorum vitae exemplis, ex eorumque salutaribus caritatis inceptis, ea, quae in humana societate praestantiora ac nobiliora sunt, haud parum accipiant roboris atque incrementi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p26","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","For this reason the Church has not been inert in the presence of those wonderful discoveries of the human mind and those advances of doctrines which we use today, nor has she proved incapable of duly evaluating them; but, pursuing all these things with most watchful care, she does not cease to admonish men to turn their eyes, above the very appearance of visible things, to God the fountain of all wisdom and beauty, lest they—to whom it was said: \"Subdue the earth and have dominion\"—ever forget that most grave precept: \"Thou shalt adore the Lord thy God and Him only shalt thou serve\" (Mt 4:10; Lk 4:8), nor let the fleeting fascination of things impede progress in the true sense.","Ut autem haec doctrina multiplices attingat humanae navitatis campos, qui ad singulos homines, ad domesticum convictum, ad socialem vitam pertinent, in primis necesse est, ne Ecclesia oculos a sacro veritatis patrimonio a maioribus accepto umquam avertat; at simul necesse habet, ut praesentia quoque aspiciat tempora, quae novas induxerunt rerum condiciones, novasque vivendi formas, atque nova catholico apostolatui patefecerunt itinera."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p27","The Principal Office of the Council: Doctrine to Be Defended and Promoted","Namely, the first and twentieth Ecumenical Council—which uses the effective and greatly estimable help of those who excel in the knowledge of sacred disciplines, in the exercise of the apostolate, and in the right ordering of things—wishes to hand on the catholic doctrine whole, undiminished, not distorted, which, although placed among difficulties and contentions, has become as a common patrimony of men. This is not indeed pleasing to all, nevertheless it is set forth as a most abundant treasure ready for all who are endowed with good will.","Hac de causa Ecclesia mirandis illis humani ingenii inventis doctrinarumque incrementis, quibus hodie utimur, non iners affuit, neque iisdem recte aestimandis impar evasit; sed haec omnia pervigili prosequens cura, homines admonere non desinit, ut super ipsam rerum aspectabilium speciem, oculos ad Deum omnis sapientiae et pulchritudinis fontem convertant, ne iidem, quibus dictum fuit: \"Subicite terram et dominamini\", obliviscantur umquam gravissimi illius praecepti: \"Dominum Deum tuum adorabis et illi soli servies (Mt 4,10; Lc 4,8), neu fluxa rerum fascinatio veri nominis progressionem impediat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p28","How the Doctrine Is to Be Promoted Today","But it is not our part to guard this precious treasure only, as if we were devoted only to antiquity; but with alacrity, without fear, let us now insist upon the work which our age requires, continuing the journey which the Church has made for nearly twenty centuries.","Scilicet Concilium Oecumenicum primum et vicesimum - quod efficaci magnique aestimando auxilio utitur eorum, qui scientia sacrarum disciplinarum, apostolatus exercendi resque recto ordine agendi excellunt - integram, non imminutam, non detortam tradere vult doctrinam catholicam, quae, licet inter difficultates et contentiones, veluti patrimonium commune hominum evasit. Hoc non omnibus quidem gratum est, tamen cunctis, qui bona voluntate sunt praediti, quasi paratus thesaurus uberrimus proponitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p29","How the Doctrine Is to Be Promoted Today","Nor does our work, as if for a primary end, look to this, that there be disputation concerning certain principal chapters of ecclesiastical doctrine, and that those things which the fathers and ancient and recent theologians have handed down—which we rightly think not to be unknown to you but to inhere in your minds—be more fully repeated.","Attamen nostrum non est pretiosum hunc thesaurum solum custodire, quasi uni antiquitati studeamus; sed alacres, sine timore, operi, quod nostra exigit aetas, nunc insistamus, iter pergentes, quod Ecclesia a viginti fere saeculis fecit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p30","How the Doctrine Is to Be Promoted Today","For indeed, for such disputations alone to be held it would not have been necessary to indict an Ecumenical Council. Nevertheless at present it is fitting that the whole Christian doctrine, no part subtracted from it, be received here in our times by all with new study, with serene and peaceful minds, handed down with that accurate manner of conceiving and reducing to form words which is especially evident from the acts of the Council of Trent and the First Vatican Council; it is fitting that, as all sincere supporters of the Christian, catholic, apostolic religion vehemently desire, the same doctrine be known more widely and more deeply and that minds be more fully imbued and formed by it; it is fitting that this doctrine, certain and immutable, to which faithful obedience is to be rendered, be investigated and set forth in that manner which our times demand. For it is one thing that the deposit of Faith itself, namely the truths which are contained in our venerable doctrine, is another thing the manner in which they are enunciated, preserving the same sense and the same meaning. Indeed, great importance must be given to this manner, and, if necessary, one must labor patiently in it; namely, those ways of setting forth matters must be introduced which are more congruent with the magisterium, whose character is especially pastoral.","Neque opus nostrum, quasi ad finem primarium, eo spectat, ut de quibusdam capitibus praecipuis doctrinae ecclesiasticae disceptetur, atque adeo fusius repetantur ea, quae Patres ac theologi veteres et recentiores tradiderunt, et quae a vobis non ignorari sed in mentibus vestris inhaerere merito putamus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p31","How the Doctrine Is to Be Promoted Today","For indeed, while age succeds age, we see uncertain opinions of men succeed one another, and emerging errors often quickly vanish like a mist driven away by the sun.","Etenim ad huiusmodi tantum disputationes habendas non opus erat, ut Concilium Oecumenicum indiceretur. Verumtamen in praesenti oportet ut universa doctrina christiana, nulla parte inde detracta, hic temporibus nostris ab omnibus accipiatur novo studio, mentibus serenis atque pacatis, tradita accurata illa ratione verba concipiendi et in formam redigendi, quae ex actis Concilii Tridentini et Vaticani Primi praesertim elucet; oportet ut, quemadmodum cuncti sinceri rei christianae, catholicae, apostolicae fautores vehementer exoptant, eadem doctrina amplius et altius cognoscatur eaque plenius animi imbuantur atque formentur; oportet ut haec doctrina certa et immutabilis, cui fidele obsequium est praestandum, ea ratione pervestigetur et exponatur, quam tempora postulant nostra. Est enim aliud ipsum depositum Fidei, seu veritates, quae veneranda doctrina nostra continentur, aliud modus, quo eaedem enuntiantur, eodem tamen sensu eademque sententia. Huic quippe modo plurimum tribuendum erit et patienter, si opus fuerit, in eo elaborandum; scilicet eae inducendae erunt rationes res exponendi, quae cum magisterio, cuius indoles praesertim pastoralis est, magis congruant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p32","How Errors Are to Be Repressed","The Church has opposed these errors at all times, and has often even condemned them, and indeed with the firmest severity. As for the present time, it pleases the Spouse of Christ to apply the medicine of mercy rather than to take up the arms of severity; she thinks that present needs are to be consulted more by explaining the force of her doctrine more abundantly than by condemning. Not that fallacious doctrines, opinions, perils to be guarded against and dissipated are lacking; but because all these things so openly fight against the principles of right honesty, and have produced such deadly fruits, that today men themselves seem to be beginning to condemn them on their own, and especially those forms of living which put God and His laws last, excessive confidence placed in the progress of technical art, prosperity based solely on the conveniences of life. They themselves more and more know that the dignity of the human person and its fitting perfection is a matter of great importance and very difficult to accomplish. But what is of greatest importance, they have finally learned by experience that external force imposed on others, the power of arms, political domination are by no means sufficient to resolve successfully the most grave questions which afflict them.","Siquidem, dum aetas aetati succedit, incertas cernimus hominum opinationes alias alias excipere, atque enascentes errores saepe cito velut nebulam sole depulsam evanescere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p33","How Errors Are to Be Repressed","In these circumstances of things, the Catholic Church, while through this Ecumenical Council she lifts up the torch of religious truth, wishes to show herself as the most loving mother of all, benign, patient, and moved with mercy and goodness toward sons separated from herself. To the human race, laboring under so many difficulties, she herself, like Peter once to that poor man who asked alms from him, says: \"Silver and gold I have none; but what I have I give you: in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, arise and walk\" (Acts 3:6). Namely, to the men of our times the Church does not hand over fleeting riches, nor does she promise mere earthly beatitude; but she imparts the goods of heavenly grace, which, as they raise men to the dignity of sons of God, are so strong an aid and help for making their life more humane; she opens the more abundant fountains of her doctrine, by which men, illuminated by the light of Christ, are able to understand deeply what they themselves really are, with what dignity they excel, what end they ought to pursue; finally, through her sons, she everywhere extends the spaces of Christian charity, than which nothing is more apt for pulling up the seeds of dissension, and nothing is more efficacious for promoting concord, just peace, and fraternal unity of all.","Quibus erroribus Ecclesia nullo non tempore obstitit, eos saepe etiam damnavit, et quidem severitate firmissima. Ad praesens tempus quod attinet, Christi Sponsae placet misericordiae medicinam adhibere, potius quam severitatis arma suscipere; magis quam damnando, suae doctrinae vim uberius explicando putat hodiernis necessitatibus esse consulendum. Non quod desint fallaces doctrinae, opinationes, pericula praecavenda atque dissipanda; sed quia haec omnia tam aperte pugnant cum rectis honestatis principiis, ac tam exitiales peperere fructus, ut hodie homines per se ipsi ea damnare incipere videantur, ac nominatim illas vivendi formas, quae Deum eiusque leges posthabeant, nimiam in technicae artis progressibus positam confidentiam, prosperitatem unice vitae commodis innixam. Ipsi magis magisque norunt, humanae personae dignitatem eiusque congruam perfectionem negotium esse magni momenti et ad expediendum perarduum. Quod autem maxime interest, iidem experiendo tandem didicerunt, externam vim aliis impositam, armorum potentiam, politicum dominatum minime satis esse, ad gravissimas, quae eos angunt, quaestiones feliciter dissolvendas."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p34","How Errors Are to Be Repressed","","Hisce in rerum adiunctis, Catholica Ecclesia, dum per Oecumenicum hoc Concilium religiosae veritatis facem attollit, amantissimam omnium matrem se vult praebere, benignam, patientem, atque erga filios a se seiunctos misericordia ac bonitate permotam. Humano generi, tot difficultatibus laboranti, ipsa, ut olim Petrus misero illi qui ab eo stipem rogaverat, dicit: \"Argentum et aurum non est mihi: quod autem habeo hoc tibi do; in nomine Iesu Christi Nazareni surge et ambula\" (Act 3,6). Scilicet nostrorum temporum hominibus Ecclesia non caducas porrigit divitias, neque beatitatem dumtaxat terrenam pollicetur; sed supernae gratiae bona impertit, quae cum homines ad filiorum Dei dignitatem erigant, tam valido praesidio et adiumento sunt ad humaniorem efficiendam eorum vitam; uberioris doctrinae suae fontes aperit, qua homines luce Christi collustrati penitus intelligere valent, quid ipsi revera sint, qua dignitate excellant, quem finem prosequi debeant; per filios denique suos ubique christianae caritatis spatia dilatat, qua ad dissensionum semina evellenda nihil est aptius, et ad provehendam concordiam, iustam pacem fraternamque omnium unitatem nihil est efficacius."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p35","Unity in the Christian and Human Family to Be Fostered","But this visible unity in truth, alas, the whole family of Christians has not yet fully and perfectly attained. Yet the Catholic Church judges it to be her duty to contribute sedulous work to accomplish the great mystery of that unity which Christ Jesus, as His sacrifice impended, asked from the Heavenly Father with most fervent prayers; and she enjoys most sweet peace, knowing herself to be most closely joined with these prayers of Christ; nay, she rejoices with sincere heart when she notices that these prayers are producing salutary and more abundant fruits even among those who are outside her fold. For, if we consider the matter rightly, this unity itself, which Jesus Christ obtained for His Church, seems to shine as if with a threefold ray of supernal and salutary light, to which correspond: the unity of Catholics among themselves, which must be kept most firm and shining as an example; moreover, unity consisting of pious prayers and most fervent vows, by which Christians separated from this Apostolic See seek to be joined with us; finally, unity based on esteem and reverence for the Catholic Church, exhibited by those who profess diverse non-Christian forms of religion.","Huiusmodi Ecclesiae sollicitudo de veritate promovenda atque tuenda, ex eo proficiscitur, quod, secundum Dei consilium, \"qui omnes homines vult salvos fieri, et ad agnitionem veritatis venire\" (1Tim 2,4), homines nonnisi integrae revelatae doctrinae praesidio aucti, ad absolutam firmissimamque animorum unitatem pervenire possunt, quacum veri nominis pax sempiternaque salus coniungitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p36","Unity in the Christian and Human Family to Be Fostered","Concerning which matter, it is greatly to be mourned that a huge part of the human race—although all men who are born are redeemed also by the blood of Christ—does not yet participate in the sources of heavenly grace which are held in the Catholic Church. Hence it comes about that these splendid praises of St. Cyprian aptly apply to the Catholic Church, whose light illuminates all things and whose power of supernatural unity redounds to the progress of the whole human family: \"The Church of the Lord, bathed in light, stretches forth her rays throughout the whole world; yet it is one light which is diffused everywhere, and the unity of the body is not separated. She extends her branches in abundant fertility over the whole earth, more widely spreads her flowing streams; yet there is one head and one source and one mother, abundant in the successes of her fecundity; by her offspring we are born, by her milk we are nourished, by her spirit we are given life.\"","Hanc autem visibilem unitatem in veritate, nondum, pro dolor, universa christianorum familia plene perfecteque assecuta est. At Ecclesia Catholica officii sui esse ducit sedulam conferre operam ad magnum complendum mysterium illius unitatis, quam Christus Iesus, imminente sacrificio suo, a Caelesti Patre flagrantissimis rogavit precibus; atque suavissima fruitur pace, sciens se cum hisce Christi precibus arctissime esse coniunctam; quin etiam sincero animo gaudet, cum animadvertit has precationes etiam inter eos, qui extra eiusdem saepta versantur, salutares atque uberiores usque multiplicare fructus. Etenim, si rem probe consideramus, haec ipsa unitas, quam Iesus Christus pro Ecclesia sua impetravit, quasi triplici supernae salutarisque lucis radio coruscare videtur, cui respondent: unitas catholicorum inter se, quae firmissima atque in exemplum praelucens servetur oportet; unitas praeterea ex piis precibus et flagrantissimis votis constans, qua christiani ab hac Apostolica Sede seiuncti expetunt ut nobiscum coniungantur; unitas denique existimatione atque observantia erga Ecclesiam Catholicam innixa, ab iis exhibita, qui diversas religionis formas adhuc non christianas profitentur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p37","Unity in the Christian and Human Family to Be Fostered","This is what the Second Vatican Ecumenical Synod proposes to itself; which, while it gathers the principal forces of the Church into one, and earnestly strives that the message of salvation may be more willingly received by men, as if paves and fortifies the way for achieving that unity of the human race which is as a necessary foundation, so that the earthly City may be ordered after the likeness of the heavenly City, \"whose King is truth, whose law is love, whose measure is eternity.\"","Quam ad rem, est maxime lugendum, quod ingens adhuc humani generis pars - quamvis omnes homines qui nascuntur ipsi quoque Christi Sanguine sint redempti - nondum participet supernae gratiae fontes, qui in Ecclesia Catholica habentur. Quo fit ut de Ecclesia Catholica, cuius lux omnia illuminat et cuius supernaturalis unitatis vis in universae hominum familiae profectum redundat, apte cadant haec Sancti Cypriani praeclara praeconia: \"Ecclesia Domini luce perfusa per orbem totum radios suos porrigit; unum tamen lumen est quod ubique diffunditur nec unitas corporis separatur. Ramos suos in universam terram copia ubertatis extendit, profluentes largiter rivos latius pandit: unum tamen caput est et origo una et una mater fecunditatis successibus copiosa: illius fetu nascimur, illius lacte nutrimur, spiritu eius animamur\"."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-1/index.html","p38","Venerable Brothers,","Behold, we are now gathered together in this Vatican Basilica, where the hinge of the history of the Church turns, where now Heaven and earth are joined in a most close bond; here at the tomb of St. Peter, at the tombs of so many holy predecessors of ours, whose ashes at this solemn hour seem to exult as if with a mysterious murmuring.","Hoc sibi proponit Oecumenica Synodus Vaticana Secunda; quae, dum praecipuas Ecclesiae vires in unum congregat, atque enixe studet, ut salutis nuntium ab hominibus libentius excipiatur, quasi viam sternit ac munit ad efficiendam illam humani generis unitatem, quae veluti necessarium fundamentum est, ut terrena Civitas in similitudinem componatur Civitatis caelestis, \"cuius rex veritas, cuius lex caritas, cuius modus aeternitas\"."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p39","Conclusion","What is being begun, the Council, rises like a day shining with most splendid light. It is only dawn; and already the first rays of the rising sun how sweetly they affect our spirits! All here breathes holiness, excites joy. For we contemplate the stars with their brightness increasing the majesty of this temple, which, as the Apostle John testifies, are you; and through you, as it were, golden candlesticks shining around the tomb of the Prince of the Apostles, which are the churches entrusted to you. At the same time we see men of most ample dignity, who have come to Rome from the five continents, about to act for their nations, and who are present here with all reverence and most humane expectation.","Ecce nos iam in unum congregatos in hac Vaticana Basilica, ubi Ecclesiae historiae cardo vertitur, ubi nunc Caelum et terra artissimo coniunguntur foedere; hic ad Sancti Petri sepulcrum, ad tot Sanctorum Decessorum Nostrorum tumulos, quorum cineres hac sollemni hora quasi arcano quodam fremitu exsultare videntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p40","Conclusion","Wherefore it must be plainly said that the heavenly citizens and men confer united work for celebrating the Council. The part of the blessed citizens is to protect our labors; that of Christ's faithful, to continue to pour forth fervent prayers to God; that of all of you, to give prompt obedience to the supernal impulses of the Holy Spirit, to give alacritous work so that your labors may excellently respond to the wishes and necessities of various nations. So that these things may happen, serene peace of spirits, fraternal concord, temperateness of undertakings, dignity of disputations, wisdom of all deliberations are required of you.","Quod incohatur Concilium, veluti dies in Ecclesia oritur splendidissima luce refulgens. Tantum aurora est; et iam primi orientis solis radii quam suaviter animos afficiunt nostros! Omnia hic sanctitatem spirant, laetitiam excitant. Contemplamur enim stellas claritate sua maiestatem huius templi adaugere, quae, teste Ioanne Apostolo, vos estis; et per vos quasi aurea lucere candelabra circa Principis Apostolorum sepulcrum, quae sunt Ecclesiae vobis concreditae. Simul cernimus viros dignitate amplissimos, qui e quinque continentibus terris Romam convenerunt, suarum Nationum personam acturi, quique omni cum reverentia atque humanissima cum exspectatione hic adsunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p41","Conclusion","May it be that your studies and works, upon which not only the eyes of peoples but also the hope of the whole world are turned, may abundantly fulfill expectation.","Quare plane dicendum est, Caelites et homines ad celebrandum Concilium consociatam conferre operam. Beatorum Caelitum partes hae sunt, ut labores tueantur nostros; christifidelium, ut flagrantes preces ad Deum fundere pergant; vestrum omnium, ut supernis Spiritus Sancti impulsionibus prompte obsecuti, alacriter detis operam, ut labores vestri variarum gentium optatis ac necessitatibus apprime respondeant. Haec ut contingant, a vobis postulantur serena animorum pax, fraterna concordia, coeptorum temperantia, disceptationum dignitas, deliberationum omnium sapientia."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p42","Conclusion","Omnipotent God, in You, distrusting our own strength, we place all our confidence. Look benignly upon these pastors of Your Church. May the light of Your heavenly grace be with us in taking counsel, be with us in making laws; and hear willingly the prayers which we pour forth to You with one faith, one mouth, one spirit.","Utinam studia atque opera vestra, in quae non tantum populorum oculi sed spes quoque universi orbis sunt conversae, exspectationem cumulate expleant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p43","Conclusion","O Mary, Help of Christians, Help of Bishops, whose love we recently in a particular manner experienced in your Lauretan temple, where it pleased us to venerate the mystery of the Incarnation, dispose all things by your help to a happy, favorable, prosperous outcome; and you, together with your Spouse St. Joseph, with the holy Apostles Peter and Paul, with St. John the Baptist and the Evangelist, intercede for us before God.","Omnipotens Deus, in Te, nostris diffisi viribus, fiduciam totam reponimus. Super hos Ecclesiae tuae Pastores benignus respice. Supernae tuae gratiae lumen Nobis adsit consilium capientibus, adsit leges ferentibus; et quas una Fide, uno ore, uno animo ad Te preces fundimus, libenter exaudi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p44","Conclusion","To Jesus Christ, our most loving Redeemer, immortal King of peoples and of times, be love, power, and glory forever and ever. Amen.","O Maria, Auxilium Christianorum, Auxilium Episcoporum, cuius amorem nuper in Lauretano templo tuo, ubi Incarnationis mysterium venerari placuit, peculiari modo experti sumus, omnia ad laetum, faustum, prosperum exitum tua ope dispone; tuque una cum Sancto Ioseph Sponso tuo, cum Sanctis Petro et Paulo Apostolis, Sanctis Ioanne Baptista et Evangelista, apud Deum intercede pro nobis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p45","Conclusion","Since Christ is the Light of the Nations, this sacred Synod, gathered in the Holy Spirit, greatly desires that, by proclaiming the Gospel to every creature (cf. Mk 16:15), it may illuminate all men with the clarity of Him who shines forth on the face of the Church. And since the Church is in Christ as a sacrament, that is, a sign and instrument of intimate union with God and of the unity of the whole human race, it intends, following upon the subject matter of previous councils, to declare more precisely to its faithful and to the whole world the nature and universal mission of the Church. The conditions of this time add greater urgency to this duty of the Church, namely that all men, now more closely joined together by various social, technical, and cultural bonds, may also attain full unity in Christ.","Iesu Christo, Redemptori nostro amantissimo, Regi immortali populorum et temporum, amor, potestas et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p46","Lumen Gentium","The eternal Father, by the most free and hidden counsel of His wisdom and goodness, created the whole world, decreed to raise men to a participation in divine life, and did not abandon them when they had fallen in Adam, always providing them with aids for salvation in view of Christ the Redeemer, who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature (Col 1:15). All the elect the Father foreknew and predestined from before the ages to become conformed to the image of His Son, that He might be the firstborn among many brothers (Rom 8:29). He decreed to call those who believe in Christ into the holy Church, which, prefigured from the beginning of the world, wonderfully prepared in the history of the people of Israel and in the old covenant, established in the last times, manifested by the outpouring of the Spirit, will be gloriously consummated at the end of the ages. Then, as is read in the holy fathers, all the just from Adam, from righteous Abel to the last elect, will be gathered together in the universal Church before the Father.","LUMEN GENTIUM cum sit Christus, haec Sacrosancta Synodus, in Spiritu Sancto congregata, omnes homines claritate Eius, super faciem Ecclesiae resplendente, illuminare vehementer exoptat, omni creaturae Evangelium annuntiando (cf. Mc 16,15). Cum autem Ecclesia sit in Christo veluti sacramentum seu signum et instrumentum intimae cum Deo unionis totiusque generis humani unitatis, naturam missionemque suam universalem, praecedentium Conciliorum argumento instans, pressius fidelibus suis et mundo universo declarare intendit. Condiciones huius temporis huic Ecclesiae officio urgentiorem vim addunt, ut nempe homines cuncti, variis hodie vinculis socialibus, technicis, culturalibus arctius coniuncti, plenam etiam unitatem in Christo consequantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p47","Lumen Gentium","Therefore the Son came, sent by the Father, who chose us in Him before the foundation of the world and predestined us to the adoption of sons, because in Him it pleased Him to restore all things (cf. Eph 1:4-5, 10). Christ therefore, to fulfill the will of the Father, inaugurated the kingdom of heaven on earth and revealed His mystery to us, and by His obedience He accomplished redemption. The Church, that is, the kingdom of Christ already present in mystery, grows visibly in the world through the power of God. This beginning and growth are signified by the blood and water flowing from the open side of crucified Jesus (cf. Jn 19:34), and are foretold by the Lord's words concerning His death on the cross: \"And I, if I be lifted up from the earth, will draw all things to myself\" (Jn 12:32). As often as the sacrifice of the cross, in which Christ our pasch was sacrificed (1 Cor 5:7), is celebrated on the altar, the work of our redemption is exercised. At the same time, by the sacrament of the eucharistic bread, the unity of the faithful, who constitute one body in Christ (cf. 1 Cor 10:17), is represented and brought about. All men are called to this union with Christ, who is the light of the world, from whom we proceed, through whom we live, toward whom we tend.","Aeternus Pater, liberrimo et arcano sapientiae ac bonitatis suae consilio, mundum universum creavit, homines ad participandam vitam divinam elevare decrevit, eosque lapsos in Adamo non dereliquit, semper eis auxilia ad salutem praebens, intuitu Christi, Redemptoris, qui est imago Dei invisibilis, primogenitus omnis creaturae (Col 1,15). Omnes autem electos Pater ante saecula praescivit et praedestinavit conformes fieri imaginis Filii sui, ut sit Ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus (Rm 8,29). Credentes autem in Christum convocare statuit in sancta Ecclesia, quae iam ab origine mundi praefigurata, in historia populi Israel ac foedere antiquo mirabiliter praeparata, in novissimis temporibus constituta, effuso Spiritu est manifestata, et in fine saeculorum gloriose consummabitur. Tunc autem, sicut apud sanctos Patres legitur, omnes iusti inde ab Adam, ab Abel iusto usque ad ultimum electum in Ecclesia universali apud Patrem congregabuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p48","Lumen Gentium","When the work was accomplished which the Father gave the Son to do on earth (cf. Jn 17:4), the Holy Spirit was sent on the day of Pentecost, that He might continually sanctify the Church, and thus believers, through Christ and in one Spirit, might have access to the Father (cf. Eph 2:18). He is the Spirit of life, that is, the fountain of water springing up unto eternal life (cf. Jn 4:14; 7:38-39), through whom the Father gives life to men, dead through sin, until He raises up their mortal bodies in Christ (cf. Rom 8:10-11). The Spirit dwells in the Church and in the hearts of the faithful as in a temple (cf. 1 Cor 3:16; 6:19), and in them He prays and bears witness to the adoption of sons (cf. Gal 4:6; Rom 8:15-16, 26). He leads the Church into all truth (cf. Jn 16:13), unifies her in communion and ministry, instructs and directs her with diverse hierarchical and charismatic gifts, and adorns her with His fruits (cf. Eph 4:11-12; 1 Cor 12:4; Gal 5:22). By the power of the Gospel He makes the Church young again, perpetually renews her, and leads her to consummated union with her Spouse. For the Spirit and the Spouse say to the Lord Jesus, \"Come!\" (cf. Rev 22:17).","Venit igitur Filius, missus a Patre, qui nos in Eo ante mundi constitutionem elegit ac in adoptionem filiorum praedestinavit, quia in Eo omnia instaurare sibi complacuit (cf. Ep 1,4-5 Ep 1,10). Christus ideo, ut voluntatem Patris impleret, regnum caelorum in terris inauguravit nobisque Eius mysterium revelavit, atque oboedientia sua redemptionem effecit. Ecclesia, seu regnum Christi iam praesens in mysterio, ex virtute Dei in mundo visibiliter crescit. Quod exordium et incrementum significantur sanguine et aqua ex aperto latere Iesu crucifixi exeuntibus (cf. Jn 19,34), ac praenuntiantur verbis Domini de morte sua in cruce: Et Ego, si exaltatus fuero a terra, omnes traham ad Meipsum (Jn 12,32 gr.). Quoties sacrificium crucis, quo Pascha nostrum immolatus est Christus (1Co 5,7), in altari celebratur, opus nostrae redemptionis exercetur. Simul sacramento panis eucharistici repraesentatur et efficitur unitas fidelium, qui unum corpus in Christo constituunt (cf. 1Co 10,17). Omnes homines ad hanc vocantur unionem cum Christo, qui est lux mundi, a quo procedimus, per quem vivimus, ad quem tendimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p49","Lumen Gentium","Thus the whole Church appears as a people united from the unity of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit.","Opere autem consummato, quod Pater Filio commisit in terra faciendum (cf. Jn 17,4), missus est Spiritus Sanctus die Pentecostes, ut Ecclesiam iugiter sanctificaret, atque ita credentes per Christum in uno Spiritu accessum haberent ad Patrem (cf. Ep 2,18). Ipse est Spiritus vitae seu fons aquae salientis in vitam aeternam (cf. Jn 4,14 Jn 7,38-39), per quem Pater homines, peccato mortuos, vivificat, donec eorum mortalia corpora in Christo resuscitet (cf. Rm 8,10-11). Spiritus in Ecclesia et in cordibus fidelium tamquam in templo habitat (cf. 1Co 3,16 1Co 6,19), in eisque orat et testimonium adoptionis filiorum reddit (cf. Ga 4,6 Rm 8,15-16 Rm 8,26). Ecclesiam quam in omnem veritatem inducit (cf. Jn 16,13) et in communione et ministratione unificat, diversis donis hierarchicis et charismaticis instruit ac dirigit, et fructibus suis adornat (cf. Ep 4,11-12 1Co 12,4 Ga 5,22). Virtute Evangelii iuvenescere facit Ecclesiam eamque perpetuo renovat et ad consummatam cum Sponso suo unionem perducit.Nam Spiritus et Sponsa ad Dominum Iesum dicunt Veni! (cf. Ap 22,17)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p50","Lumen Gentium","The mystery of the holy Church is manifested in its foundation. For the Lord Jesus made a beginning of His Church by preaching the good news, namely the advent of the Kingdom of God promised from of old in the Scriptures: \"The time is fulfilled, and the Kingdom of God is at hand\" (Mk 1:15; cf. Mt 4:17). This Kingdom truly shines forth before men in the word, works, and presence of Christ. For the word of the Lord is compared to a seed sown in the field (Mk 4:14): those who hear it with faith and are numbered among the little flock of Christ (Lk 12:32) have received the Kingdom itself; then by its own power the seed germinates and grows until the harvest time (cf. Mk 4:26-29). The miracles of Jesus also prove that the Kingdom has already arrived on earth: \"If I by the finger of God cast out devils, then surely the Kingdom of God has come upon you\" (Lk 11:20; cf. Mt 12:28). Above all, however, the Kingdom is manifested in the very Person of Christ, the Son of God and the Son of Man, who came \"to minister and to give His life as a redemption for many\" (Mk 10:45).","Sic apparet universa Ecclesia sicuti de unitate Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti plebs adunata."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p51","Lumen Gentium","When Jesus, having suffered the death of the cross for men, had risen, He appeared as the Lord and Christ and Priest constituted forever (cf. Acts 2:36; Heb 5:6; 7:17-21), and He poured out upon His disciples the Spirit promised by the Father (cf. Acts 2:33). Hence the Church, instructed by the gifts of her Founder and faithfully keeping His precepts of charity, humility, and self-denial, receives the mission to announce and to establish the Kingdom of Christ and of God among all nations, and she constitutes on earth the seed and beginning of this Kingdom. In the meantime, while she grows gradually, she yearns for the consummated Kingdom, and with all her strength hopes and longs to be united with her King in glory.","Ecclesiae sanctae mysterium in eiusdem fundatione manifestatur. Dominus enim Iesus Ecclesiae suae initium fecit praedicando faustum nuntium, adventum scilicet Regni Dei a saeculis in Scripturis promissi: Quoniam impletum est tempus, et appropinquavit Regnum Dei (Mc 1,15 cf. Mt 4,17). Hoc vero Regnum in verbo, operibus et praesentia Christi hominibus elucescit. Verbum nempe Domini comparatur semini, quod in agro seminatur (Mc 4,14): qui illud cum fide audiunt et Christi pusillo gregi (Lc 12,32) adnumerantur, Regnum ipsum susceperunt; propria dein virtute semen germinat et increscit usque ad tempus messis (cf. Mc 4,26-29). Miracula etiam Iesu Regnum iam in terris pervenisse comprobant: Si in digito Dei eiicio daemonia, profecto pervenit in vos Regnum Dei (Lc 11,20 cf. Mt 12,28). Ante omnia tamen Regnum manifestatur in ipsa Persona Christi, Filii Dei et Filii hominis, qui venit ut ministraret, et daret animam suam redemptionem pro multis (Mc 10,45)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p52","Lumen Gentium","Just as in the Old Testament the revelation of the Kingdom is often proposed under figures, so now also the intimate nature of the Church is made known to us through various images, which, whether drawn from pastoral life or agriculture, from building or even from family and betrothal, are prepared in the books of the Prophets.","Cum autem Iesus, mortem crucis pro hominibus passus, resurrexerit, tamquam Dominus et Christus Sacerdosque in aeternum constitutus apparuit (cf. Ac 2,36 He 5,6 He 7,17-21), atque Spiritum a Patre promissum in discipulos suos effudit (cf. Ac 2,33). Unde Ecclesia, donis sui Fundatoris instructa fideliterque eiusdem praecepta caritatis, humilitatis et abnegationis servans, missionem accipit Regnum Christi et Dei annuntiandi et in omnibus gentibus instaurandi, huiusque Regni in terris germen et initium constituit. Ipsa interea, dum paulatim increscit, ad Regnum consummatum anhelat, ac totis viribus sperat et exoptat cum Rege suo in gloria coniungi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p53","Lumen Gentium","For the Church is a sheepfold, whose one and necessary door is Christ (Jn 10:1-10). It is also a flock, of which God Himself foretold He would be the shepherd (cf. Is 40:11; Ez 34:11 ff.), and whose sheep, although governed by human shepherds, are nevertheless unceasingly led and nourished by Christ Himself, the Good Shepherd and Prince of shepherds (cf. Jn 10:11; 1 Pet 5:4), who gave His life for the sheep (cf. Jn 10:11-15).","Sicut in Vetere Testamento revelatio Regni saepe sub figuris proponitur, ita nunc quoque variis imaginibus intima Ecclesiae natura nobis innotescit, quae sive a vita pastorali vel ab agricultura, sive ab aedificatione aut etiam a familia et sponsalibus desumptae, in libris Prophetarum praeparantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p54","Lumen Gentium","The Church is the farm or field of God (1 Cor 3:9). In that field grows the ancient olive tree, whose holy root were the Patriarchs, and in which the reconciliation of Jews and Gentiles has taken place and will take place (Rom 11:13-26). It was planted by the heavenly Farmer as a chosen vine (Mt 21:33-43 par.; cf. Is 5:1 ff.). Christ is the true vine, giving life and fertility to the branches, that is, to us, who through the Church remain in Him, and without whom we can do nothing (Jn 15:1-5).","Est enim Ecclesia ovile, cuius ostium unicum et necessarium Christus est (Jn 10,1-10). Est etiam grex, cuius ipse Deus pastorem se fore praenuntiavit (cf. Is 40,11 Ez 34,11 ss.), et cuius oves, etsi a pastoribus humanis gubernantur, indesinenter tamen deducuntur et nutriuntur ab ipso Christo. bono Pastore Principeque pastorum (cf. Jn 10,11 1P 5,4), qui vitam suam dedit pro ovibus (cf. Jn 10,11-15)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p55","Lumen Gentium","The Church is also often called the building of God (1 Cor 3:9). The Lord Himself compared Himself to the stone which the builders rejected, but which was made the head of the corner (Mt 21:42 par.; cf. Acts 4:11; 1 Pet 2:7; Ps 117:22). Upon that foundation the Church is built by the Apostles (cf. 1 Cor 3:11), and from it she receives firmness and cohesion. This building is adorned with various names: the house of God (1 Tim 3:15), in which indeed His family dwells, the dwelling of God in the Spirit (Eph 2:19-22), the tabernacle of God with men (Rev 21:3), and especially the holy temple, which, represented in stone sanctuaries, is praised by the holy Fathers, and in the Liturgy is not undeservedly assimilated to the holy City, the new Jerusalem. For in her we are built as living stones here on earth (1 Pet 2:5). John contemplates this holy City, at the renewal of the world, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband (Rev 21:1-2).","Est Ecclesia agricultura seu ager Dei (1Co 3,9). In illo agro crescit antiqua oliva, cuius radix sancta fuerunt Patriarchae, et in qua Iudaeorum et Gentium reconciliatio facta est et fiet (Rm 11,13-26). Ipsa plantata est a caelesti Agricola tamquam vinea electa (Mt 21,33-43 par.; cf. Is 5,1 ss.). Vitis vera Christus est, vitam et fecunditatem tribuens palmitibus, scilicet nobis, qui per Ecclesiam in ipso manemus, et sine quo nihil possumus facere (Jn 15,1-5)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p56","Lumen Gentium","The Church, also, which is the Jerusalem above and is called our mother (Gal 4:26; cf. Rev 12:17), is described as the immaculate spouse of the immaculate Lamb (Rev 19:7; 21:2, 9; 22:17), whom Christ loved and delivered Himself up for her, that He might sanctify her (Eph 5:26), whom He joined to Himself by an indissoluble covenant, and continually nourishes and cherishes (Eph 5:29), and whom, purified, He willed to be joined to Himself and subject to Him in love and fidelity (cf. Eph 5:24), and finally enriched with heavenly goods forever, that we may comprehend the charity of God and of Christ toward us, which surpasses all knowledge (cf. Eph 3:19). While, however, the Church pilgrimages here on earth away from the Lord (cf. 2 Cor 5:6), she lives as an exile, so that she seeks and savors the things that are above, where Christ is sitting at the right hand of God, where the life of the Church is hidden with Christ in God, until she appears with her Spouse in glory (cf. Col 3:1-4).","Saepius quoque Ecclesia dicitur aedificatio Dei (1Co 3,9). Dominus ipse se comparavit lapidi, quem reprobaverunt aedificantes, sed qui factus est in caput anguli (Mt 21,42 par.; cf. Ac 4,11 1P 2,7 Ps 117,22). Super illud fundamentum Ecclesia ab Apostolis exstruitur (cf. 1Co 3,11), ab eoque firmitatem et cohaesionem accipit. Quae constructio variis appellationibus decoratur: domus Dei (1Tm 3,15), in qua nempe habitat eius familia, habitaculum Dei in Spiritu (Ep 2,19-22), tabernaculum Dei cum hominibus (Ap 21,3), et praesertim templum sanctum, quod in lapideis sanctuariis repraesentatum a Sanctis Patribus laudatur, et in Liturgia non immerito assimilatur Civitati sanctae, novae Ierusalem. In ipsa enim tamquam lapides vivi his in terris aedificamur (1P 2,5). Quam sanctam civitatem Ioannes contemplatur, in renovatione mundi descendentem de caelis a Deo, paratam sicut sponsam ornatam viro suo (Ap 21,1-2)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p57","Lumen Gentium","The Son of God, in the human nature united to Himself, by His death and resurrection overcame death, redeemed man, and transformed him into a new creation (cf. Gal 6:15; 2 Cor 5:17). For by communicating His Spirit, He mystically constituted His brothers, called from all nations, as His own body.","Ecclesia etiam, quae sursum est Ierusalem et mater nostra appellatur (Ga 4,26 cf. Ap 12,17), describitur ut sponsa immaculata Agni immaculati (Ap 19,7 Ap 21,2 cf. et9; 22, 17), quam Christus dilexit, et seipsum tradidit pro ea, ut illam sanctificaret (Ep 5,26), quam sibi foedere indissolubili sociavit et indesinenter nutrit et fovet (Ep 5,29), et quam mundatam sibi voluit coniunctam et in dilectione ac fidelitate subditam (cf. Ep 5,24), quam tandem bonis caelestibus in aeternum cumulavit, ut Dei et Christi erga nos caritatem, quae omnem scientiam superat, comprehendamus (cf. Ep 3,19). Dum vero his in terris Ecclesia peregrinatur a Domino (cf. 2Co 5,6), tamquam exsulem se habet, ita ut quae sursum sunt quaerat et sapiat, ubi Christus est in dextera Dei sedens, ubi vita Ecclesiae abscondita est cum Christo in Deo, donec cum Sponso suo appareat in gloria (cf. Col 3,1-4)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p58","Lumen Gentium","In that body the life of Christ is poured out into believers, who through the sacraments are united to Christ, suffering and glorified, in an hidden and real way. For through baptism we are conformed to Christ: \"For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body\" (1 Cor 12:13). By this sacred rite the association with the death and resurrection of Christ is represented and brought about: \"For we were buried with Him by baptism into death; if we have been planted together in the likeness of His death, we shall be also in the likeness of His resurrection\" (Rom 6:4-5). In the breaking of the eucharistic bread, really partaking of the Lord's body, we are raised to communion with Him and among ourselves. \"Because there is one bread, we, many, are one body, all we who partake of one bread\" (1 Cor 10:17). Thus we all become members of that body (cf. 1 Cor 12:27), each members one of another (Rom 12:5).","Dei Filius, in natura humana Sibi unita, morte et resurrectione sua mortem superando, hominem redemit et in novam creaturam transformavit (cf. Ga 6,15 2Co 5,17). Communicando enim Spiritum suum, fratres suos, ex omnibus gentibus convocatos, tamquam corpus suum mystice constituit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p59","Lumen Gentium","Just as all the members of the human body, although many, form one body, so the faithful in Christ (cf. 1 Cor 12:12). Diversity of members and functions also flourishes in the building up of the body of Christ. There is one Spirit, who distributes His varied gifts, according to His riches and the necessities of ministries, for the utility of the Church (cf. 1 Cor 12:1-11). Among these gifts stands out the grace of the Apostles, to whose authority even the charismatic persons are subject by the Spirit Himself (cf. 1 Cor 14). The same Spirit, by Himself and by His power and by the internal connection of the members, unifying the body, produces and urges charity among the faithful. Hence, if one member suffers anything, all the members suffer with it; or if one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it (cf. 1 Cor 12:26).","In corpore illo vita Christi in credentes diffunditur, qui Christo passo atque glorificato, per sacramenta arcano ac reali modo uniuntur. Per baptismum enim Christo conformamur: Etenim in uno Spiritu omnes nos in unum corpus baptizati sumus (l Cor. 12, 13). Quo sacro ritu consociatio cum morte et resurrectione Christi repraesentatur et efficitur: Consepulti enim sumus cum Illo per baptismum in mortem; si autem complantati facti sumus similitudini mortis Eius: simul et resurrectionis erimus (Rm 6,4-5). In fractione panis eucharistici de Corpore Domini realiter participantes, ad communionem cum Eo ac inter nos elevamur. Quoniam unus corpus multi sumus, omnes, qui de uno pane participamus (1Co 10,17). Ita nos omnes membra illius Corporis efficimur (cf. 1Co 12,27), singuli autem alter alterius membra (Rm 12,5)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p60","Lumen Gentium","The Head of this body is Christ. He is the image of the invisible God, and in Him all things were created. He is before all, and all things hold together in Him. He is the head of the body, the Church. He is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead, that He might hold first place in everything (cf. Col 1:15-18). By the greatness of His power He rules over heavenly and earthly things, and by His surpassing perfection and action He fills the whole body with the riches of His glory (cf. Eph 1:18-23).","Sicut vero omnia corporis humani membra, licet multa sint, unum tamen corpus efformant, ita fideles in Christo (cf. 1Co 12,12). Etiam in aedificatione corporis Christi diversitas viget membrorum et officiorum. Unus est Spiritus, qui varia sua dona, secundum divitias suas atque ministeriorum necessitates, ad Ecclesiae utilitatem dispertit (cf. 1Co 12,1-11). Inter quae dona praestat gratia Apostolorum, quorum auctoritati ipse Spiritus etiam charismaticos subdit (cf. 1Co 14). Idem Spiritus per Se suaque virtute atque interna membrorum connexione corpus unificans, caritatem inter fideles producit et urget. Unde, si quid patitur unum membrum, compatiuntur omnia membra; sive si unum membrum honoratur, congaudent omnia membra (cf. 1Co 12,26)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p61","Lumen Gentium","All members must be conformed to Him, until Christ be formed in them (cf. Gal 4:19). Therefore we are assumed into the mysteries of His life, configured with Him, comortified and conresuscitated, until we reign with Him (cf. Phil 3:21; 2 Tim 2:11; Eph 2:6; Col 2:12, etc.). Still pilgrimaging on earth, pressing His footprints in tribulation and persecution, we are consociated with His passions as the body to the Head, suffering with Him, that we may be glorified with Him (cf. Rom 8:17).","Huius corporis Caput est Christus. Ipse est imago Dei invisibilis, in Eoque condita sunt universa. Ipse est ante omnes et omnia in Ipso constant. Ipse est caput corporis quod est Ecclesia. Ipse est principium, primogenitus ex mortuis, ut sit in omnibus primatum tenens (cf. Col 1,15-18). Magnitudine virtutis suae caelestibus et terrestribus dominatur, et supereminenti perfectione et operatione sua totum corpus gloriae suae divitiis replet (cf. Ep 1,18-23)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p62","Lumen Gentium","From Him the whole body, supplied and built up through joints and connections, grows into the increase of God (Col 2:19). He constantly disposes in His body, that is, the Church, the gifts of ministries, by which through His power we render services to one another for salvation, so that doing truth in charity, we may grow into Him in all things, who is our Head (cf. Eph 4:11-16). So that we may be continually renewed in Him (cf. Eph 4:23), He gave us of His Spirit, who, being one and the same in Head and members, so vivifies, unifies, and moves the whole body that His function could be compared by the holy Fathers to the office which the principle of life, that is, the soul, fulfills in the human body.","Omnia membra Ei conformari oportet, donec Christus formetur in eis (cf. Ga 4,19). Quapropter in vitae Eius mysteria adsumimur, cum Eo configurati, commortui et conresuscitati, donec cum Eo conregnemus (cf. Ph 3,21 2Tm 2,11 Ep 2,6 Col 2,12 etc.). In terris adhuc peregrinantes, Eiusque vestigia in tribulatione et persecutione prementes, Eius passionibus tamquam corpus Capiti consociamur, Ei compatientes, ut cum Eo conglorificemur (cf. Rm 8,17)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p63","Lumen Gentium","Christ indeed loves the Church as His spouse, having become the pattern of the man who loves his wife as his own body (cf. Eph 5:25-28); the Church herself is subject to her Head (ibid. 23-24). Because in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily (Col 2:9), He fills the Church, which is His body and fullness, with His divine gifts (cf. Eph 1:22-23), that she may extend and attain to all the fullness of God (cf. Eph 3:19).","Ex Eo totum corpus, per nexus et coniunctiones subministratum et constructum, crescit in augmentum Dei (Col 2,19). Ipse in corpore suo, scilicet Ecclesia, dona ministrationum iugiter disponit, quibus Ipsius virtute nobis invicem ad salutem servitia praestamus, ut veritatem facientes in caritate, crescamus in Illum per omnia, qui est Caput nostrum (cf. Ep 4,11-16 gr.) . Ut autem in Illo incessanter renovemur (cf. Ep 4,23), dedit nobis de Spiritu suo, qui unus et idem in Capite et in membris exsistens, totum corpus ita vivificat, unificat et movet, ut Eius officium a sanctis Patribus comparari potuerit cum munere, quod principium vitae seu anima in corpore humano adimplet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p64","Lumen Gentium","Christ, the one Mediator, constituted and unceasingly sustains His holy Church, the community of faith, hope, and charity, here on earth as a visible structure, through which He diffuses truth and grace to all. But the society furnished with hierarchical organs and the mystical Body of Christ, the visible assembly and the spiritual community, the earthly Church and the Church enriched with heavenly goods, are not to be considered as two things, but form one complex reality which coalesces from the human and the divine element. Therefore it is assimilated, for no small analogy, to the mystery of the incarnate Word. For just as the assumed nature serves the divine Word as a living organ of salvation, indissolubly united to Him, not in a dissimilar manner does the social structure of the Church serve the Spirit of Christ, who vivifies her, for the increase of the body (cf. Eph 4:16).","Christus vero diligit Ecclesiam ut sponsam suam, exemplar factus viri diligentis uxorem suam ut corpus suum (cf. Ep 5,25-28); ipsa vero Ecclesia subiecta est Capiti suo (ibid. Ep 23-24). Quia in Ipso inhabitat omnis plenitudo divinitatis corporaliter (Col 2,9), Ecclesiam, quae corpus et plenitudo Eius est, divinis suis donis replet (cf. Ep 1,22-23), ut ipsa protendat et perveniat ad omnem plenitudinem Dei (cf. Ep 3,19)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p65","Lumen Gentium","This is the one Church of Christ, which we profess in the Creed as one, holy, catholic, and apostolic, which our Savior, after His resurrection, handed over to Peter to be fed (Jn 21:17), and entrusted to him and the other Apostles to be spread and governed (cf. Mt 28:18 ff.), and erected forever as the pillar and bulwark of truth (1 Tim 3:15). This Church, constituted and organized in this world as a society, subsists in the Catholic Church, governed by the successor of Peter and the bishops in communion with him, although many elements of sanctification and truth are found outside her structure, which, as gifts proper to the Church of Christ, impel toward catholic unity.","Unicus Mediator Christus Ecclesiam suam sanctam, fidei, spei et caritatis communitatem his in terris ut compaginem visibilem constituit et indesinenter sustentat, qua veritatem et gratiam ad omnes diffundit. Societas autem organis hierarchicis instructa et mysticum Christi Corpus, coetus adspectabilis et communitas spiritualis, Ecclesia terrestris et Ecclesia caelestibus bonis ditata, non ut duae res considerandae sunt, sed unam realitatem complexam efformant, quae humano e divino coalescit elemento. Ideo ob non mediocrem analogiam incarnati Verbi mysterio assimilatur. Sicut enim natura assumpta Verbo divino ut vivum organum salutis, Ei indissolubiliter unitum, inservit, non dissimili modo socialis compago Ecclesiae Spiritui Christi, eam vivificanti, ad augmentum corporis inservit (cf. Ep 4,16)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p66","Lumen Gentium","Just as Christ accomplished the work of redemption in poverty and persecution, so the Church is called to enter the same path, that she may communicate the fruits of salvation to men. Christ Jesus, \"when He was in the form of God... emptied Himself, taking the form of a servant\" (Phil 2:6-7), and \"for our sakes became poor, whereas He was rich\" (2 Cor 8:9): so the Church, although she needs human resources to carry out her mission, is not raised up to seek earthly glory, but to spread abroad humility and self-denial by her example also. Christ was sent by the Father \"to preach the good news to the poor, to heal the contrite of heart\" (Lk 4:18), \"to seek and to save what was lost\" (Lk 19:10): similarly the Church surrounds with love all afflicted by human infirmity, indeed she recognizes in the poor and the suffering the image of her poor and suffering Founder, strives to relieve their want, and intends to serve Christ in them. While Christ, holy, innocent, undefiled (Heb 7:26), \"knew no sin\" (2 Cor 5:21), but came only to propitiate the offenses of the people (cf. Heb 2:17), the Church, embracing sinners in her bosom, holy and always to be purified, continually pursues penance and renewal.","Haec est unica Christi Ecclesia, quam in Symbolo unam, sanctam, catholicam et apostolicam profitemur, quam Salvator noster, post resurrectionem suam Petro pascendam tradidit (Jn 21,17), eique ac ceteris Apostolis diffundendam et regendam commisit (cf. Mt 28,18 ss.), et in perpetuum ut columnam et firmamentum veritatis erexit (1Tm 3,15). Haec Ecclesia, in hoc mundo ut societas constituta et ordinata, subsistit in Ecclesia catholica, a successore Petri et Episcopis in eius communione gubernata, licet extra eius compaginem elementa plura sanctificationis et veritatis inveniantur, quae ut dona Ecclesiae Christi propria, ad unitatem catholicam impellunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p67","Lumen Gentium","Pilgrimaging amid the persecutions of the world and the consolations of God, the Church runs forward, announcing the cross and death of the Lord until He comes (cf. 1 Cor 11:26). She is strengthened by the power of the risen Lord, that, by patience and charity, she may overcome her afflictions and difficulties, both internal and external, and faithfully reveal His mystery in the world, albeit under shadows, until in the end it will be manifested in full light.","Sicut autem Christus opus redemptionis in paupertate et persecutione perfecit, ita Ecclesia ad eamdem viam ingrediendam vocatur, ut fructus salutis hominibus communicet. Christus Iesus, cum in forma Dei esset, ... semetipsum exinanivit, formam servi accipiens (Ph 2,6) et propter nos egenus factus est, cum esset dives (2Co 8,9): ita Ecclesia, licet ad missionem suam exsequendam humanis opibus indigeat, non ad gloriam terrestrem quaerendam erigitur, sed ad humilitatem et abnegationem etiam exemplo suo divulgandas. Christus a Patre missus est evangelizare pauperibus, ... sanare contritos corde (Lc 4,18), quaerere et salvum facere quod perierat (Lc 19,10): similiter Ecclesia omnes infirmitate humana afflictos amore circumdat, imo in pauperibus et patientibus imaginem Fundatoris sui pauperis et patientis agnoscit, eorum inopiam sublevare satagit, et Christo in eis inservire intendit. Dum vero Christus, sanctus, innocens, impollutus (He 7,26), peccatum non novit (2Co 5,21), sed sola delicta populi repropitiare venit (cf. He 2,17), Ecclesia in proprio sinu peccatores complectens, sancta simul et semper purificanda, poenitentiam et renovationem continuo prosequitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p68","Lumen Gentium","At all times and among every nation, anyone who fears God and works justice is acceptable to Him (cf. Acts 10:35). Yet it pleased God to sanctify and save men not individually, apart from any mutual connection, but to make them into a people who would acknowledge Him in truth and serve Him holy. He therefore chose the Israelite people for Himself as a people, with whom He established a covenant and whom He gradually instructed, manifesting Himself and the purpose of His will in their history and sanctifying it for Himself. Yet all these things happened as a preparation and a figure of that new and perfect covenant to be made in Christ, and of the fuller revelation to be handed down through the incarnate Word of God Himself. \"Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah... I will put my law in their bowels, and in their heart I will write it, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people... For all shall know me, from the least to the greatest, says the Lord\" (Jer 31:31-34). Christ instituted this new covenant, namely the new testament in His blood (cf. 1 Cor 11:25), calling a people from Jews and Gentiles, which would coalesce into unity not according to the flesh but in the Spirit, and would be the new People of God.","Inter persecutiones mundi et consolationes Dei peregrinando procurrit Ecclesia, crucem et mortem Domini annuntians, donec veniat (cf. 1Co 11,26). Virtute autem Domini resuscitati roboratur, ut afflictiones et difficultates suas, internas pariter et extrinsecas, patientia et caritate devincat, et mysterium Eius, licet sub umbris, fideliter tamen in mundo revelet, donec in fine lumine pleno manifestabitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p69","Chapter II. on the People of God","For believers in Christ, reborn not of corruptible seed but of incorruptible through the word of the living God (cf. 1 Pet 1:23), not of flesh but of water and the Holy Spirit (cf. Jn 3:5-6), are finally made \"a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people of acquisition... who once were not a people, but now are the people of God\" (1 Pet 2:9-10).","In omni quidem tempore et in omni gente Deo acceptus est quicumque timet Eum et operatur iustitiam (cf. Ac 10,35). Placuit tamen Deo homines non singulatim, quavis mutua connexione seclusa, sanctificare et salvare, sed eos in populum constituere, qui in veritate Ipsum agnosceret Ipsique sancte serviret. Plebem igitur israeliticam Sibi in populum elegit, quocum foedus instituit et quem gradatim instruxit, Sese atque propositum voluntatis suae in eius historia manifestando eumque Sibi sanctificando. Haec tamen omnia in praeparationem et figuram contigerunt foederis illius novi et perfecti, in Christo feriendi, et plenioris revelationis per Ipsum Dei Verbum carnem factum tradendae. Ecce dies veniunt, dicit Dominus, et feriam domui Israel et domui Iuda foedus novum... Dabo legem meam in visceribus eorum, et in corde eorum scribam eam, et ero eis in Deum, et ipsi erunt Mihi in populum... Omnes enim cognoscent Me, a minimo usque ad maximum, ait Dominus (Ier. 31, 31-34). Quod foedus novum Christus instituit, novum scilicet testamentum in suo sanguine (cf. 1Co 11,25), ex Iudaeis ac gentibus plebem vocans, quae non secundum carnem sed in Spiritu ad unitatem coalesceret, essetque novus Populus Dei."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p70","Chapter II. on the People of God","That messianic people has for its head Christ, who was delivered up for our sins and rose again for our justification (Rom 4:25), and now having obtained the name which is above all names, gloriously reigns in heaven. It has for its condition the dignity and liberty of the sons of God, in whose hearts the Holy Spirit dwells as in a temple. It has for its law the new commandment of loving as Christ loved us (cf. Jn 13:34). It has finally for its end the Kingdom of God, which has been begun on earth by God Himself, to be further enlarged, until it be also consummated by Him at the end of the ages, when Christ shall appear, our life (cf. Col 3:4), and \"creation itself will be delivered from the slavery of corruption into the freedom of the glory of the sons of God\" (Rom 8:21). Thus that messianic people, although it does not actually include all men, and often appears as a little flock, is nevertheless the firmest seed of unity, hope, and salvation for the whole human race. Constituted by Christ into a communion of life, charity, and truth, it is also assumed by Him as the instrument of redemption for all, and is sent forth into the whole world as the light of the world and the salt of the earth (cf. Mt 5:13-16).","Credentes enim in Christum, renati non ex semine corruptibili, sed incorruptibili per verbum Dei vivi (cf. 1P 1,23), non ex carne sed ex aqua et Spiritu Sancto (cf. Jn 3,5-6), constituuntur tandem a genus electum, regale sacerdotium, gens sancta, populus acquisitionis ... qui aliquando non populus, nunc autem populus Dei (1P 2,9-10)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p71","Chapter II. on the People of God","Just as Israel according to the flesh, which pilgrimaged in the desert, was already called the Church of God (2 Esd. 13:1; cf. Num 20:4; Dt 23:1 ff.), so the new Israel, which goes forward in the present age seeking the future and abiding city (cf. Heb 13:14), is also called the Church of Christ (cf. Mt 16:18), for He acquired her by His blood (cf. Acts 20:28), filled her with His Spirit, and provided her with suitable means of visible and social union. God convoked and constituted the congregation of those who look believingly upon Jesus, the author of salvation and the principle of unity and peace, as the Church, that she might be for all and each the visible sacrament of this saving unity. To be extended to all regions, she enters into the history of men, yet at the same time transcends times and the boundaries of peoples. Proceeding through temptations and tribulations, the Church is strengthened by the power of God's grace promised to her by the Lord, that in the weakness of the flesh she may not falter from perfect fidelity, but remain a worthy spouse of her Lord, and under the action of the Holy Spirit may not cease to renew herself, until through the cross she may come to the light which knows no setting.","Populus ille messianicus habet pro capite Christum, qui traditus est propter delicta nostra et resurrexit propter iustificationem nostram (Rm 4,25), et nunc nomen quod est super omne nomen adeptus, gloriose regnat in caelis. Habet pro conditione dignitatem libertatemque filiorum Dei, in quorum cordibus Spiritus Sanctus sicut in templo inhabitat. Habet pro lege mandatum novum diligendi sicut ipse Christus dilexit nos (cf. Jn 13,34). Habet tandem pro fine Regnum Dei, ab ipso Deo in terris inchoatum, ulterius dilatandum, donec in fine saeculorum ab Ipso etiam consummetur, cum Christus apparuerit, vita nostra (cf. Col 3,4), et ipsa creatura liberabitur a servitute corruptionis in libertatem gloriae filiorum Dei (Rm 8,21). Itaque populus ille messianicus, quamvis universos homines actu non comprehendat, et non semel ut pusillus grex appareat, pro toto tamen genere humano firmissimum est germen unitatis, spei et salutis. A Christo in communionem vitae, caritatis et veritatis constitutus, ab Eo etiam ut instrumentum redemptionis omnium adsumitur, et tamquam lux mundi et sal terrae (cf. Mt 5,13-16), ad universum mundum emittitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p72","Chapter II. on the People of God","Christ the Lord, the Pontiff taken from among men (cf. Heb 5:1-5), made the new people \"a kingdom and priests to God and His Father\" (Rev 1:6; cf. 5:9-10). For the baptized, by regeneration and the anointing of the Holy Spirit, are consecrated into a spiritual house and a holy priesthood, that through all the works of Christian men they may offer spiritual sacrifices and proclaim the virtues of Him who called them out of darkness into His admirable light (cf. 1 Pet 2:4-10). Therefore all the disciples of Christ, persevering in prayer and praising God (cf. Acts 2:42-47), should present themselves as a living sacrifice, holy, pleasing to God (cf. Rom 12:1), everywhere on earth bear witness to Christ, and give an answer to those who ask concerning the hope of eternal life that is in them (cf. 1 Pet 3:15).","Sicut vero Israel secundum carnem, qui in deserto peregrinabatur, Dei Ecclesia iam appellatur (2 Esd. 13,1 cf. Nb 20,4 Dt 23,1 ss ), ita novus Israel qui in praesenti saeculo incedens, futuram eamque manentem civitatem inquirit (cf. He 13,14), etiam Ecclesia Christi nuncupatur (cf. Mt 16,18), quippe quam Ipse sanguine suo acquisivit (cf. Ac 20,28) suo Spiritu replevit, aptisque mediis unionis visibilis et socialis instruxit. Deus congregationem eorum qui in Iesum, salutis auctorem et unitatis pacisque principium, credentes aspiciunt, convocavit et constituit Ecclesiam, ut sit universis et singulis sacramentum visibile huius salutiferae unitatis. Ad universas regiones extendenda, in historiam hominum intrat, dum tamen simul tempora et fines populorum transcendit. Per tentationes vero et tribulationes procedens Ecclesia virtute gratiae Dei sibi a Domino promissae confortatur, ut in infirmitate carnis a perfecta fidelitate non deficiat, sed Domini sui digna sponsa remaneat, et sub actione Spiritus Sancti, seipsam renovare non desinat, donec per crucem perveniat ad lucem, quae nescit occasum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p73","Chapter II. on the People of God","The common priesthood of the faithful and the ministerial or hierarchical priesthood, although they differ in essence and not only in degree, are nevertheless ordered to one another; for each in its own peculiar way participates in the one priesthood of Christ. For the ministerial priest, by the sacred power which he enjoys, forms and rules the priestly people, confects the eucharistic sacrifice in the person of Christ and offers it to God in the name of the whole people; the faithful, by virtue of their royal priesthood, concur in the offering of the Eucharist, and exercise it in the reception of the sacraments, in prayer and thanksgiving, in the testimony of a holy life, in self-denial and active charity.","Christus Dominus, Pontifex ex hominibus assumptus (cf. He 5,1-5) novum populum fecit regnum et sacerdotes Deo et Patri suo (Ap 1,6 cf. Ap 5,9-10). Baptizati enim, per regenerationem et Spiritus Sancti unctionem consecrantur in domum spiritualem et sacerdotium sanctum, ut per omnia opera hominis christiani spirituales offerant hostias, et virtutes annuntient Eius qui de tenebris eos vocavit in admirabile lumen suum (cf. 1P 2,4-10). Ideo universi discipuli Christi, in oratione perseverantes et collaudantes Deum (cf. Ac 2,42-47), seipsos hostiam viventem, sanctam, Deo placentem exhibeant (cf. Rm 12,1), ubique terrarum de Christo testimonium perhibeant, atque poscentibus rationem reddant de ea quae in eis est spe vitae aeternae (cf. 1P 3,15)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p74","Chapter II. on the People of God","The sacred and organically constructed nature of the priestly community is brought into act both through the sacraments and through the virtues. By baptism the faithful are incorporated into the Church, are deputed by the character to the cult of the Christian religion, and, regenerated as sons of God, are bound to profess before men the faith which they have received from God through the Church. By the sacrament of confirmation they are more perfectly bound to the Church, are enriched with the special strength of the Holy Spirit, and are thus more strictly obliged to spread and defend the faith as true witnesses of Christ both by word and by deed. Partaking of the eucharistic sacrifice, the source and summit of the whole Christian life, they offer the divine Victim to God and themselves with it; thus both by the oblation and by the sacred communion, not promiscuously but each in his own way, all have their own part in the liturgical action. Moreover, restored by the body of Christ in the sacred synaxis, they concretely exhibit the unity of the People of God, which is fittingly signified and wonderfully brought about by this most august sacrament.","Sacerdotium autem commune fidelium et sacerdotium ministeriale seu hierarchicum, licet essentia et non gradu tantum differant, ad invicem tamen ordinantur; unum enim et alterum suo peculiari modo de uno Christi sacerdotio participant. Sacerdos quidem ministerialis, potestate sacra qua gaudet, populum sacerdotalem efformat ac regit, sacrificium eucharisticum in persona Christi conficit illudque nomine totius populi Deo offert; fideles vero, vi regalis sui sacerdotii, in oblationem Eucharistiae concurrunt, illudque in sacramentis suscipiendis, in oratione et gratiarum actione, testimonio vitae sanctae, abnegatione et actuosa caritate exercent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p75","Chapter II. on the People of God","Those who approach the sacrament of penance obtain pardon from God's mercy for the offense committed against Him, and are at the same time reconciled with the Church, which they wounded by sinning, and which by charity, example, and prayer labors for their conversion. By the sacred anointing of the sick and the prayer of the presbyters, the whole Church commends the sick to the suffering and glorified Lord, that He may relieve and save them (cf. Jas 5:14-16), indeed she exhorts them to associate themselves freely with the passion and death of Christ (cf. Rom 8:17; Col 1:24; 2 Tim 2:11-12; 1 Pet 4:13), and to contribute to the good of the People of God. Again, those among the faithful who are marked with sacred Order are instituted in the name of Christ to feed the Church by the word and grace of God. Finally, Christian spouses, by the virtue of the sacrament of matrimony, by which the mystery of unity and fruitful love between Christ and the Church is signified and participated in (cf. Eph 5:32), help one another in conjugal life and in the reception and education of offspring to attain holiness, and so in their state and order of life they have their own gift in the People of God (cf. 1 Cor 7:7). For from this marriage proceeds the family, in which new citizens of human society are born, who by the grace of the Holy Spirit are made sons of God by baptism to perpetuate the People of God through the course of the ages. In this as it were domestic church, parents should be for their children the first heralds of the faith by word and example, and they must foster the vocation proper to each, especially the sacred vocation.","Indoles sacra et organice exstructa communitatis sacerdotalis et per sacramenta et per virtutes ad actum deducitur. Fideles per baptismum in Ecclesia incorporati, ad cultum religionis christianae charactere deputantur et, in filios Dei regenerati, fidem quam a Deo per Ecclesiam acceperunt coram hominibus profiteri tenentur. Sacramento confirmationis perfectius Ecclesiae vinculantur, speciali Spiritus Sancti robore ditantur, sicque ad fidem tamquam veri testes Christi verbo et opere simul diffundendam et defendendam arctius obligantur. Sacrificium eucharisticum, totius vitae christianae fontem et culmen, participantes, divinam Victimam Deo offerunt atque seipsos cum Ea; ita tum oblatione tum sacra communione, non promiscue sed alii aliter, omnes in liturgica actione partem propriam agunt. Porro corpore Christi in sacra synaxi refecti, unitatem Populi Dei, quae hoc augustissimo sacramento apte significatur et mirabiliter efficitur, modo concreto exhibent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p76","Chapter II. on the People of God","Furnished with so many and so great salutary means, all Christ's faithful, of whatever condition and state, are called by the Lord, each in his own way, to the perfection of holiness by which the Father Himself is perfect.","Qui vero ad sacramentum poenitentiae accedunt, veniam offensionis Deo illatae ab Eius misericordia obtinent et simul reconciliantur cum Ecclesia, quam peccando vulneraverunt, et quae eorum conversioni caritate, exemplo, precibus adlaborat. Sacra infirmorum unctione atque oratione presbyterorum Ecclesia tota aegrotantes Domino patienti et glorificato commendat, ut eos alleviet et salvet (cf. Jc 5,14-16), immo eos hortatur ut sese Christi passioni et morti libere sociantes (cf. Rm 8,17 Col 1,24 2Tm 2,11-12 1P 4,13), ad bonum Populi Dei conferant. Iterum, qui inter fideles sacro Ordine insigniuntur, ad Ecclesiam verbo et gratia Dei pascendam, Christi nomine instituuntur Tandem coniuges christiani, virtute matrimonii sacramenti, quo mysterium unitatis et fecundi amoris inter Christum et Ecclesiam significant atque participant (cf. Ep 5,32), se invicem in vita coniugali necnon prolis susceptione et educatione ad sanctitatem adiuvant, adeoque in suo vitae statu et ordine proprium suum in Populo Dei donum habent (cf. 1Co 7,7). Ex hoc enim connubio procedit familia, in qua nascuntur novi societatis humanae cives, qui per Spiritus Sancti gratiam, ad Populum Dei saeculorum decursu perpetuandum, baptismo in filios Dei constituuntur. In hac velut Ecclesia domestica parentes verbo et exemplo sint pro filiis suis primi fidei praecones, et vocationem unicuique propriam, sacram vero peculiari cura, foveant oportet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p77","Chapter II. on the People of God","The holy People of God also participates in the prophetic office of Christ, spreading abroad a living witness of Him especially through a life of faith and charity, and offering to God a sacrifice of praise, the fruit of lips confessing His name (cf. Heb 13:15). The whole body of the faithful, who have the anointing from the Holy One (cf. 1 Jn 2:20, 27), cannot err in believing, and manifests this peculiar property through the supernatural sense of faith of the whole people, when it exhibits universal consent concerning matters of faith and morals from the bishops down to the last of the lay faithful. For by that sense of faith, which is aroused and sustained by the Spirit of truth, the People of God, under the guidance of the sacred magisterium, which it faithfully obeys, receives not the word of men but truly the word of God (cf. 1 Th 2:13), adheres indefectibly to the faith once delivered to the saints (cf. Jude 3), penetrates it more deeply by right judgment, and more fully applies it in life.","Tot ac tantis salutaribus mediis muniti, christifideles omnes, cuiusvis conditionis ac status, ad perfectionem sanctitatis qua Pater ipse perfectus est, sua quisque via, a Domino vocantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p78","Chapter II. on the People of God","Moreover, the same Holy Spirit not only sanctifies and leads the people of God through the sacraments and ministries and adorns them with virtues, but, dividing His gifts to each as He wills (1 Cor 12:11), He distributes special graces also among the faithful of every order, by which He renders them apt and ready to undertake various works or offices for the renewal and greater building up of the Church, according to that: \"To each is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the common good\" (1 Cor 12:7). These charisms, whether the more remarkable or the simpler and more widely diffused, since they are exceedingly suited and useful to the needs of the Church, are to be received with thanksgiving and consolation. Extraordinary gifts, however, are not rashly to be sought after, nor are the fruits of apostolic works to be presumptuously expected from them; but the judgment concerning their genuineness and orderly exercise pertains to those who are in charge of the Church, to whom it belongs especially not to extinguish the Spirit, but to test all things and hold fast to what is good (cf. 1 Th 5:12, 19-21).","Populus Dei sanctus de munere quoque prophetico Christi participat, vivum Eius testimonium maxime per vitam fidei ac caritatis diffundendo, et Deo hostiam laudis offerendo, fructum labiorum confitentium nomini Eius (cf. He 13,15). Universitas fidelium, qui unctionem habent a Sancto (cf. 1Jn 2,20 1Jn 2,27), in credendo falli nequit, atque hanc suam peculiarem proprietatem mediante supernaturali sensu fidei totius populi manifestat, cum ab Episcopis usque ad extremos laicos fideles universalem suum consensum de rebus fidei et morum exhibet. Illo enim sensu fidei, qui a Spiritu veritatis excitatur et sustentatur, Populus Dei sub ductu sacri magisterii, cui fideliter obsequens, iam non verbum hominum, sed vere accipit verbum Dei (cf. 1Th 2,13), semel traditae sanctis fidei (cf. Jud 1,3), indefectibiliter adhaeret, recto iudicio in eam profundius penetrat eamque in vita plenius applicat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p79","Chapter II. on the People of God","All men are called to the new People of God. Therefore this people, remaining one and unique, is to be spread throughout the whole world and through all ages, that the purpose of God's will may be fulfilled, who created human nature in the beginning one, and decreed to gather together at last His sons who were scattered (cf. Jn 11:52). For to this end God sent His Son, whom He appointed heir of all things (cf. Heb 1:2), that He might be Teacher, King, and Priest of all, the Head of the new and universal people of the sons of God. Finally, to this end God sent the Spirit of His Son, the Lord and Giver of life, who is the principle of gathering and unity for the whole Church and for each and every believer in the doctrine of the Apostles and in communion, in the breaking of bread and in prayers (cf. Acts 2:42).","Idem praeterea Spiritus Sanctus non tantum per sacramenta et ministeria populum Dei sanctificat et ducit eumque virtutibus ornat, sed dona sua dividens singulis prout vult (1Co 12,11), inter omnis ordinis fideles distribuit gratias quoque speciales, quibus illos aptos et promptos reddit ad suscipienda varia opera vel officia, pro renovatione et ampliore aedificatione Ecclesiae proficua, secundum illud: Unicuique datur manifestatio Spiritus ad utilitatem (1Co 12,7). Quae charismata, sive clarissima, sive etiam simpliciora et latius diffusa, cum sint necessitatibus Ecclesiae apprime accommodata et utilia, cum gratiarum actione ac consolatione accipienda sunt. Dona autem extraordinaria non sunt temere expetenda, neque praesumptuose ab eis sperandi sunt fructus operarum apostolicarum; sed iudicium de eorum genuinitate et ordinato exercitio ad eos pertinet, qui in Ecclesia praesunt, et quibus speciatim competit, non Spiritum exstinguere, sed omnia probare et quod bonum est tenere (cf. 1Th 5,12 1Th 5,19-21)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p80","Chapter II. on the People of God","Therefore, among all the nations of the earth, there is one People of God, since it borrows its citizens from all nations, of a kingdom indeed not earthly but heavenly. For all the faithful scattered through the whole world communicate with one another in the Holy Spirit, and thus he who sits at Rome knows the Indian to be his member. Since, moreover, the Kingdom of Christ is not of this world (cf. Jn 18:36), therefore the Church, this People of God, bringing in this Kingdom, takes nothing away from the temporal good of any people, but on the contrary fosters and assumes the faculties, resources, and customs of peoples, insofar as they are good, and, in assuming them, purifies, strengthens, and elevates them. For she is mindful that she must gather with that King to whom the nations are given as an inheritance (cf. Ps 2:8), and into whose city they bring gifts and offerings (cf. Ps 71:10; Is 60:4-7; Rev 21:24). This character of universality which adorns the People of God is a gift of the Lord Himself, by which the Catholic Church effectively and perpetually tends toward recapitulating the whole of humanity with all its goods under Christ the Head, in the unity of His Spirit.","Ad novum Populum Dei cuncti vocantur homines. Quapropter hic populus, unus et unicus manens, ad universum mundum et per omnia saecula est dilatandus, ut propositum adimpleatur voluntatis Dei, qui naturam humanam in initio condidit unam, filiosque suos, qui erant dispersi, in unum tandem congregare statuit (cf. Jn 11,52). Ad hoc enim misit Deus Filium suum, quem constituit heredem universorum (cf. He 1,2), ut sit Magister, Rex et Sacerdos omnium, Caput novi et universalis populi filiorum Dei. Ad hoc tandem misit Deus Spiritum Filii sui, Dominum et Vivificantem, qui pro tota Ecclesia et singulis universisque credentibus principium est congregationis et unitatis in doctrina Apostolorum et communione, fractione panis et orationibus (cf. Ac 2,42, gr.) ."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p81","Chapter II. on the People of God","By virtue of this catholicity, each part brings its own gifts to the other parts and to the whole Church, so that the whole and each part are increased by all who communicate with one another and conspire to fullness in unity. Hence it comes about that the People of God is not only gathered from diverse peoples, but also in itself is compounded of various orders. For there is diversity among its members, whether according to offices, while some exercise the sacred ministry for the good of their brothers, or according to condition and ordering of life, while many in the religious state, tending to holiness by a narrower path, stimulate their brothers by their example. Hence also in ecclesiastical communion there legitimately exist particular churches, enjoying their own traditions, the primacy of the Chair of Peter remaining intact, which presides over the whole assembly of charity, protects legitimate varieties, and at the same time watches lest particularities, far from harming unity, rather serve it. Hence, finally, between the various parts of the Church there are bonds of intimate communion with respect to spiritual riches, apostolic workers, and temporal resources. For the members of the People of God are called to communicate goods, and the words of the Apostle apply also to each of the churches: \"As each has received a gift, ministering it to one another, as good stewards of the manifold grace of God\" (1 Pet 4:10).","Omnibus itaque gentibus terrae inest unus Populus Dei, cum ex omnibus gentibus mutuetur suos cives, Regni quidem indolis non terrestris, sed caelestis. Cuncti enim per orbem sparsi fideles cum ceteris in Spiritu Sancto communicant, et sic qui Romae sedet, Indos scit membrum suum esse. Cum autem Regnum Christi de hoc mundo non sit (cf. Jn 18,36), ideo Ecclesia seu Populus Dei, hoc Regnum inducens, nihil bono temporali cuiusvis populi subtrahit, sed e contra facultates et copias moresque populorum, quantum bona sunt, fovet et assumit, assumendo vero purificat, roborat et elevat. Memor est enim se cum illo Rege colligere debere, Cui gentes in hereditatem datae sunt (cf. Ps 2,8), et in Cuius civitatem dona et munera adducunt (cf. Ps 71, 10; Is. 60, 4-7; Apoc. 21, 24). Hic universalitatis character, qui Populum Dei condecorat, ipsius Domini donum est, quo catholica Ecclesia efficaciter et perpetuo tendit ad recapitulandam totam humanitatem cum omnibus bonis eius, sub Capite Christo, in unitate Spiritus Eius."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p82","Chapter II. on the People of God","To this catholic unity of the People of God, which prefigures and promotes universal peace, all men are called, and to it in various ways pertain or are ordered, whether Catholic faithful, or other believers in Christ, or finally all men universally, called by the grace of God to salvation.","Vi huius catholicitatis, singulae partes propria dona ceteris partibus et toti Ecclesiae afferunt, ita ut totum et singulae partes augeantur ex omnibus invicem communicantibus et ad plenitudinem in unitate conspirantibus. Inde fit ut Populus Dei non tantum ex diversis populis congregetur, sed etiam in seipso ex variis ordinibus confletur. Adest enim inter membra eius diversitas, sive secundum officia, dum aliqui sacro ministerio in bonum fratrum suorum funguntur, sive secundum condicionem et vitae ordinationem, dum plures in statu religioso, arctiore via ad sanctitatem tendentes, fratres exemplo suo stimulant. Inde etiam in ecclesiastica communione legitime adsunt Ecclesiae particulares, propriis traditionibus fruentes, integro manente primatu Petri Cathedrae, quae universo caritatis coetui praesidet, legitimas varietates tuetur et simul invigilat ut particularia, nedum unitati noceant, ei potius inserviant. Inde denique inter diversas Ecclesiae partes vincula intimae communionis quoad divitias spirituales, operarios apostolicos et temporalia subsidia. Ad communicandum enim bona vocantur membra Populi Dei, et de singulis etiam Ecclesiis valent verba Apostoli: Unusquisque, sicut accepit gratiam, in alterutrum illam administrantes, sicut boni dispensatores multiformis gratiae Dei (1P 4,10)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p83","Chapter II. on the People of God","Therefore the holy Synod first turns its mind to the Catholic faithful. It teaches, based on Sacred Scripture and Tradition, that this pilgrimaging Church is necessary for salvation. For Christ alone is the Mediator and the way of salvation, who is made present to us in His body, which is the Church, and He Himself, by inculcating the necessity of faith and baptism in express words (cf. Mk 16:16; Jn 3:5), confirmed at the same time the necessity of the Church, into which men enter through baptism as through a door. Hence those men could not be saved who, not ignorant that the Catholic Church was established as necessary by God through Jesus Christ, nevertheless have been unwilling either to enter it or to persevere in it.","Ad hanc igitur catholicam Populi Dei unitatem, quae pacem universalem praesignat et promovet, omnes vocantur homines, ad eamque variis modis pertinent vel ordinantur sive fideles catholici, sive alii credentes in Christo, sive denique omnes universaliter homines, gratia Dei ad salutem vocati."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p84","Chapter II. on the People of God","Those are fully incorporated into the society of the Church who, having the Spirit of Christ, accept her entire order and all the means of salvation established in her, and are joined in the visible structure of the Church with Christ, who governs her through the Supreme Pontiff and the bishops, namely by the bonds of profession of faith, the sacraments, ecclesiastical governance, and communion. Yet he is not saved who, although incorporated into the Church, does not persevere in charity, remaining in the bosom of the Church indeed in body but not in heart. Let all children of the Church be mindful that their eminent condition is to be ascribed not to their own merits but to the special grace of Christ; to which if they do not correspond in thought, word, and deed, far from being saved, they will be more severely judged.","Ad fideles ergo catholicos imprimis Sancta Synodus animum vertit. Docet autem, Sacra Scriptura et Traditione innixa, Ecclesiam hanc peregrinantem necessariam esse ad salutem. Unus enim Christus est Mediator ac via salutis, qui in Corpore suo, quod est Ecclesia, praesens nobis fit, Ipse autem necessitatem fidei et baptismi expressis verbis inculcando (cf. Mc 16,16 Jn 3,5), necessitatem Ecclesiae, in quam homines per baptismum tamquam per ianuam intrant, simul confirmavit. Quare illi homines salvari non possent, qui Ecclesiam Catholicam a Deo per Iesum Christum ut necessariam esse conditam non ignorantes, tamen vel in eam intrare, vel in eadem perseverare noluerint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p85","Chapter II. on the People of God","Catechumens who, moved by the Holy Spirit, seek with explicit will to be incorporated into the Church, are by that very desire joined to her; Mother Church already embraces them as her own with love and care.","Illi plene Ecclesiae societati incorporantur, qui Spiritum Christi habentes, integram eius ordinationem omniaque media salutis in ea instituta accipiunt, et in eiusdem compage visibili cum Christo, eam per Summum Pontificem atque Episcopos regente, iunguntur, vinculis nempe professionis fidei, sacramentorum et ecclesiastici regiminis ac communionis. Non salvatur tamen, licet Ecclesiae incorporetur, qui in caritate non perseverans, in Ecclesiae sinu corpore quidem, sed non corde remanet. Memores autem sint omnes Ecclesiae filii condicionem suam eximiam non propriis meritis sed peculiari gratiae Christi esse adscribendam; cui si cogitatione, verbo et opere non respondent, nedum salventur, severius iudicabuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p86","Chapter II. on the People of God","With those who, baptized, are adorned with the Christian name, yet do not profess the whole faith or do not preserve the unity of communion under the successor of Peter, the Church knows that she is joined for many reasons. For there are many who hold sacred Scripture in honor as the norm of believing and living, show a sincere religious zeal, lovingly believe in God the Father Almighty and in Christ, the Son of God the Savior, are marked by baptism, by which they are joined to Christ, indeed also recognize and receive other sacraments in their own churches or ecclesiastical communities. Many among them also enjoy the episcopate, celebrate the holy Eucharist, and foster piety toward the Virgin Mother of God. There follows a communion of prayers and other spiritual benefits; indeed, a certain true union in the Holy Spirit, who by His sanctifying power operates with gifts and graces also in them, and has strengthened some of them even to the shedding of blood. Thus the Spirit arouses in all Christ's disciples a desire and an action that all may, in the manner established by Christ, be peacefully united in one flock under one Shepherd. That this may be obtained, Mother Church does not cease to pray, hope, and work, and exhorts her sons to purification and renewal, that the sign of Christ may shine more clearly upon the face of the Church.","Catechumeni qui, Spiritu Sancto movente, explicita voluntate ut Ecclesiae incorporentur expetunt, hoc ipso voto cum ea coniunguntur; quos iam ut suos dilectione curaque complectitur Mater Ecclesia."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p87","Chapter II. on the People of God","Those who have not yet received the Gospel are related to the People of God in various ways. First, that people to whom the covenants and promises were given and from whom Christ was born according to the flesh (cf. Rom 9:4-5), a people most dear by election because of the fathers: for the gifts and calling of God are without repentance (cf. Rom 11:28-29). But the plan of salvation also embraces those who acknowledge the Creator, among whom first are the Muslims, who, professing to hold the faith of Abraham, adore together with us the one God, merciful, who will judge men on the last day. Nor is God Himself far from others who seek the unknown God in shadows and images, since He gives to all life and inspiration and all things (cf. Acts 17:25-28), and the Savior wills that all men be saved (cf. 1 Tim 2:4). For those who without fault are ignorant of the Gospel of Christ and His Church, yet seek God with a sincere heart, and try, under the influx of grace, to fulfill His will known through the dictate of conscience, can attain eternal salvation. Nor does divine Providence deny the aids necessary for salvation to those who, without fault, have not yet arrived at explicit knowledge of God and strive to attain a right life not without divine grace. For whatever good and truth is found among them is esteemed by the Church as a preparation for the Gospel, and as given by Him who illuminates every man, that at last he may have life. But often men, deceived by the Evil One, have become vain in their thoughts, and have exchanged the truth of God for a lie, serving the creature rather than the Creator (cf. Rom 1:21, 25), or living and dying without God in this world are exposed to final desperation. Therefore, to promote the glory of God and the salvation of all these, the Church, mindful of the Lord's command saying, \"Preach the Gospel to every creature\" (Mk 16:16), sedulously fosters missions.","Cum illis qui, baptizati, christiano nomine decorantur, integram autem fidem non profitentur vel unitatem communionis sub Successore Petri non servant, Ecclesia semetipsam novit plures ob rationes coniunctam. Sunt enim multi, qui sacram Scripturam ut normam credendi et vivendi in honore habent sincerumque zelum religiosum ostendunt, amanter credunt in Deum Patrem omnipotentem et in Christum, Filium Dei Salvatorem, baptismo signantur, quo Christo coniunguntur, imo et alia sacramenta in propriis Ecclesiis vel communitatibus ecclesiasticis agnoscunt et recipiunt. Plures inter illos et episcopatu gaudent, Sacram Eucharistiam celebrant necnon pietatem erga Deiparam Virginem fovent. Accedit orationum aliorumque beneficiorum spiritualium communio; imo vera quaedam in Spiritu Sancto coniunctio, quippe qui donis et gratiis etiam in illis sua virtute sanctificante operatur, et quosdam illorum usque ad sanguinis effusionem roboravit. Ita Spiritus in cunctis Christi discipulis desiderium actionemque suscitat, ut omnes, modo a Christo statuto, in uno grege sub uno Pastore pacifice uniantur. Quod ut obtineat, Ecclesia Mater precari, sperare et agere non desinit, filiosque ad purificationem et renovationem exhortatur, ut signum Christi super faciem Ecclesiae clarius effulgeat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p88","Chapter II. on the People of God","For just as the Son was sent by the Father, so He also sent the Apostles (cf. Jn 20:21), saying: \"Go therefore and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you. And behold I am with you all days even to the consummation of the world\" (Mt 28:18-20). This solemn command of Christ to announce the saving truth the Church received from the Apostles to fulfill even to the ends of the earth (cf. Acts 1:8). Hence she makes her own the words of the Apostle: \"Woe to me if I do not preach the Gospel!\" (1 Cor 9:16), and therefore she continues unceasingly in sending heralds, until the new churches are fully constituted and they themselves continue the work of evangelizing. For she is compelled by the Holy Spirit to cooperate, that the purpose of God, who made Christ the principle of salvation for the whole world, may be accomplished by effect. By preaching the Gospel, the Church draws her hearers to faith and confession of faith, disposes them for baptism, snatches them from the slavery of error, and incorporates them into Christ, that through charity they may grow into Him to fullness.","Ii tandem qui Evangelium nondum acceperunt, ad Populum Dei diversis rationibus ordinantur. In primis quidem populus ille cui data fuerunt testamenta et promissa et ex quo Christus ortus est secundum carnem (cf. Rm 9,4-5), populus secundum electionem carissimus propter patres: sine poenitentia enim sunt dona et vocatio Dei (cf. Rm 11,28-29). Sed propositum salutis et eos amplectitur, qui Creatorem agnoscunt, inter quos imprimis Musulmanos, qui fidem Abrahae se tenere profitentes, nobiscum Deum adorant unicum, misericordem, homines die novissimo iudicaturum Neque ab aliis, qui in umbris et imaginibus Deum ignotum quaerunt, ab huiusmodi Deus ipse longe est, cum det omnibus vitam et inspirationem et omnia (cf. Ac 17,25-28), et Salvator velit omnes homines salvos fieri (cf. 1Tm 2,4). Qui enim Evangelium Christi Eiusque Ecclesiam sine culpa ignorantes, Deum tamen sincero corde quaerunt, Eiusque voluntatem per conscientiae dictamen agnitam, operibus adimplere, sub influxu gratiae, conantur, aeternam salutem consequi possunt. Nec divina Providentia auxilia ad salutem necessaria denegat his qui sine culpa ad expressam agnitionem Dei nondum pervenerunt et rectam vitam non sine divina gratia assequi nituntur. Quidquid enim boni et veri apud illos invenitur, ab Ecclesia tamquam praeparatio evangelica aestimatur et ab Illo datum qui illuminat omnem hominem, ut tandem vitam habeat. At saepius homines, a Maligno decepti, evanuerunt in cogitationibus suis, et commutaverunt veritatem Dei in mendacium, servientes creaturae magis quam Creatori (cf. Rm 1,21 Rm 1,25) vel sine Deo viventes ac morientes in hoc mundo, extremae desperationi exponuntur. Quapropter ad gloriam Dei et salutem istorum omnium promovendam, Ecclesia, memor mandati Domini dicentis: Praedicate Evangelium omni creaturae (Mc 16,16), missiones fovere sedulo curat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p89","Chapter II. on the People of God","Moreover, she does her work that whatever good is found sown in the heart and mind of men or in the proper rites and cultures of peoples may not only not perish but be healed, elevated, and consummated for the glory of God, the confusion of the demon, and the beatitude of man. Upon each disciple of Christ the burden of disseminating the faith falls according to his part. But although any believer can baptize, it is the priest's part to perfect the building of the Body by the eucharistic sacrifice, fulfilling the words of God through the prophet: \"From the rising of the sun even to the going down, my name is great among the nations, and in every place a clean oblation is sacrificed and offered to my name\" (Mal 1:11). Thus, at the same time, the Church prays and labors, that the fullness of the whole world may pass into the People of God, the Body of the Lord, and the Temple of the Holy Spirit, and that in Christ, the Head of all, all honor and glory be rendered to the Creator and Father of all.","Sicut enim Filius missus est a Patre, et Ipse Apostolos misit (cf. Jn 20,21), dicens: Euntes ergo docete omnes gentes, baptizantes eos in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti, docentes eos servare omnia quaecumque mandavi vobis. Et ecce Ego vobiscum sum omnibus diebus usque ad consummationem saeculi (Mt 28,18-20). Quod solemne Christi mandatum annuntiandi veritatem salutarem Ecclesia ab Apostolis recepit adimplendum usque ad ultimum terrae (cf. Ac 1,8). Unde sua facit verba Apostoli: Vae... mihi est si non evangelizavero! (2Co 9,16), ideoque in mittendis praeconibus indesinenter pergit, usquedum novellae Ecclesiae plene constituantur atque opus evangelizandi et ipsae continuent. A Spiritu Sancto enim ad cooperandum compellitur, ut propositum Dei, qui Christum principium salutis pro universo mundo constituit, effectu compleatur. Praedicando Evangelium, Ecclesia audientes ad fidem confessionemque fidei allicit, ad baptismum disponit, a servitute erroris eripit, eosque Christo incorporat, ut per caritatem in Illum usque ad plenitudinem crescant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p90","Chapter II. on the People of God","Christ the Lord, to feed the People of God and always increase it, instituted various ministries in His Church, which tend to the good of the whole Body. For the ministers, who are endowed with sacred power, serve their brothers, that all who belong to the People of God, and therefore enjoy true Christian dignity, may, conspiring freely and orderly to the same end, attain salvation.","Opera autem sua efficit ut quidquid boni in corde menteque hominum vel in propriis ritibus et culturis populorum seminatum invenitur, non tantum non pereat, sed sanetur, elevetur et consummetur ad gloriam Dei, confusionem daemonis et beatitudinem hominis. Cuilibet discipulo Christi onus fidei disseminandae pro parte sua incumbit. Sed si quilibet credentes baptizare potest, sacerdotis tamen est aedificationem Corporis sacrificio eucharistico perficere, adimplendo verba Dei per prophetam: Ab ortu solis usque ad occasum magnum est nomen meum in gentibus, et in omni loco sacrificatur et offertur nomini meo oblatio munda (Ml 1,11). Ita autem simul orat et laborat Ecclesia, ut in Populum Dei, Corpus Domini et Templum Spiritus Sancti, totius mundi transeat plenitudo, et in Christo, omnium Capite, reddatur universorum Creatori ac Patri omnis honor et gloria."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p91","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","This sacred Synod, following in the footsteps of the First Vatican Council, teaches and declares with it that Jesus Christ the eternal Shepherd built the holy Church, sending the Apostles as He Himself was sent by the Father (cf. Jn 20:21); He willed that their successors, namely the bishops, should be pastors in His Church until the consummation of the world. So that the episcopate itself might be one and undivided, He placed blessed Peter before the other Apostles, and in Him established the perpetual and visible principle and foundation of unity of faith and communion. This doctrine concerning the institution, perpetuity, force, and nature of the sacred Primacy of the Roman Pontiff and concerning his infallible Magisterium, the sacred Synod again proposes to all the faithful to be firmly believed, and, proceeding in the same undertaking, decides to profess and declare before all the doctrine concerning bishops, the successors of the Apostles, who, together with the successor of Peter, the Vicar of Christ and the visible Head of the whole Church, govern the house of the living God.","Christus Dominus, ad Populum Dei pascendum semperque augendum, in Ecclesia sua varia ministeria instituit, quae ad bonum totius Corporis tendunt. Ministri enim, qui sacra potestate pollent, fratribus suis inserviunt, ut omnes qui de Populo Dei sunt, ideoque vera dignitate christiana gaudent, ad eumdem finem libere et ordinatim conspirantes, ad salutem perveniant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p92","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The Lord Jesus, after praying to the Father, calling to Himself those whom He willed, appointed twelve to be with Him and to send them to preach the Kingdom of God (cf. Mk 3:13-19; Mt 10:1-42); whom He also named apostles (cf. Lk 6:13), He instituted in the manner of a college or stable assembly, over which He placed Peter, chosen from among them (cf. Jn 21:15-17). He sent them first to the children of Israel and then to all nations (cf. Rom 1:16), that, as sharers of His power, they might make all peoples disciples of Him, and sanctify and govern them (cf. Mt 28:16-20; Mk 16:15; Lk 24:45-48; Jn 20:21-23), and thus spread the Church, and, ministering under the guidance of the Lord, feed her all days until the consummation of the world (cf. Mt 28:20). In this mission they were fully confirmed on the day of Pentecost (cf. Acts 2:1-26) according to the Lord's promise: \"You shall receive the power of the Holy Spirit coming upon you, and you shall be witnesses to me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and even to the uttermost part of the earth\" (Acts 1:8). The Apostles, preaching the Gospel everywhere (cf. Mk 16:20), received by the hearers through the working of the Holy Spirit, gather together the universal Church, which the Lord founded on the Apostles and built upon blessed Peter, their prince, with Christ Jesus Himself as the chief cornerstone (cf. Rev 21:14; Mt 16:18; Eph 2:20).","Haec Sacrosancta Synodus, Concilii Vaticani primi vestigia premens, cum eo docet et declarat Iesum Christum Pastorem aeternum sanctam aedificasse Ecclesiam, missis Apostolis sicut Ipse missus erat a Patre (cf. Jn 20,21); quorum successores, videlicet Episcopos, in Ecclesia sua usque ad consummationem saeculi pastores esse voluit. Ut vero Episcopatus ipse unus et indivisus esset, beatum Petrum ceteris Apostolis praeposuit in ipsoque instituit perpetuum ac visibile unitatis fidei et communionis principium et fundamentum. Quam doctrinam de institutione, perpetuitate, vi ac ratione sacri Primatus Romani Pontificis deque eius infallibili Magisterio, Sacra Synodus cunctis fidelibus firmiter credendam rursus proponit, et in eodem incepto pergens, doctrinam de Episcopis, successoribus Apostolorum, qui cum successore Petri, Christi Vicario ac totius Ecclesiae visibili Capite, domum Dei viventis regunt, coram omnibus profiteri et declarare constituit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p93","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","That divine mission, entrusted by Christ to the Apostles, will last to the end of the world (cf. Mt 28:20), since the Gospel, to be handed down by them, is for all time the principle of the whole life of the Church. Therefore the Apostles, in this hierarchically ordered society, took care to appoint successors.","Dominus Iesus, precibus ad Patrem fusis, vocans ad Se quos voluit Ipse, duodecim constituit ut essent cum Illo et ut mitteret eos praedicare Regnum Dei (cf. Mc 3,13-19 Mt 10,1-42); quos Apostolos (cf. Lc 6,13) ad modum collegii seu coetus stabilis instituit, cui ex iisdem electum Petrum praefecit (cf. Jn 21,15-17). Eos ad filios Israel primum et ad omnes gentes misit (cf. Rm 1,16), ut suae participes potestatis, omnes populos discipulos Ipsius facerent, eosque sanctificarent et gubernarent (cf. Mt 28,16-20 Mc 16,15 Lc 24,45-48 Jn 20,21-23), sicque Ecclesiam propagarent, eamque sub ductu Domini ministrando pascerent, omnibus diebus usque ad consummationem saeculi (cf. Mt 28,20). In qua missione die Pentecostes plene confirmati sunt (cf. Ac 2,1-26) secundum promissum Domini: Accipietis virtutem supervenientis Spiritus Sancti in vos, et eritis Mihi testes in Ierusalem, et in omni Iudaea et Samaria, et usque ad ultimum terrae (Ac 1,8). Apostoli autem praedicando ubique Evangelium (cf. Mc 16,20), ab audientibus Spiritu Sancto operante acceptum, Ecclesiam congregant universalem, quam Dominus in Apostolis condidit et supra beatum Petrum, eorum principem, aedificavit, ipso summo angulari lapide Christo Iesu (cf. Ap 21,14 Mt 16,18 Ep 2,20)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p94","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","For they had not only various helpers in the ministry, but also, so that the mission entrusted to them might continue after their death, they handed down to their immediate cooperators, as if by a testament, the duty of perfecting and confirming the work begun by them, commending to them that they should attend to the whole flock, in which the Holy Spirit placed them to feed the Church of God (cf. Acts 20:28). They therefore appointed such men and thereafter gave the order that when they died, other approved men should take up their ministry. Among the various ministries which have been exercised in the Church from the first times, according to tradition, the principal place is held by the office of those who, being constituted in the episcopate, by a succession descending from the beginning, have the transmission of the apostolic seed. Thus, as St. Irenaeus testifies, through those who were constituted bishops by the Apostles and their successors down to us, the apostolic tradition is manifested and preserved throughout the whole world.","Missio illa divina, a Christo Apostolis concredita, ad finem saeculi erit duratura (cf. Mt 28,20), cum Evangelium, ab eis tradendum, sit in omne tempus pro Ecclesia totius vitae principium. Quapropter Apostoli, in hac societate hierarchice ordinata, de instituendis successoribus curam egerunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p95","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Bishops therefore have taken up the ministry of the community, with presbyters and deacons as helpers, presiding in the place of God over the flock, whose shepherds they are, as teachers of doctrine, priests of sacred worship, ministers of governance. Just as the office given singularly by the Lord to Peter, the first of the Apostles, and to be transmitted to his successors, remains, so also the office of the Apostles of feeding the Church, to be continually exercised by the sacred order of bishops, remains. Hence the sacred Synod teaches that bishops, by divine institution, have succeeded to the place of the Apostles, as shepherds of the Church; he who hears them hears Christ, and he who despises them despises Christ and Him who sent Christ (cf. Lk 10:16).","Non solum enim varios adiutores in ministerio habuerunt, sed ut missio ipsis concredita post eorum mortem continuaretur, cooperatoribus suis immediatis, quasi per modum testamenti, demandaverunt munus perficiendi et confirmandi opus ab ipsis inceptum, commendantes illis ut attenderent universo gregi, in quo Spiritus Sanctus eos posuit pascere Ecclesiam Dei (cf. Ac 20,28). Constituerunt itaque huius modi viros ac deinceps ordinationem dederunt, ut cum decessissent, ministerium eorum alii viri probati exciperent. Inter varia illa ministeria quae inde a primis temporibus in Ecclesia exercentur, teste traditione, praecipuum locum tenet munus illorum qui, in episcopatum constituti, per successionem ab initio decurrentem, apostolici seminis traduces habent. Ita, ut testatur S.Irenaeus, per eos qui ab Apostolis instituti sunt Episcopi et successores eorum usque ad nos, traditio apostolica in toto mundo manifestatur et custoditur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p96","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","In the bishops, therefore, to whom the presbyters assist, the Lord Jesus Christ, the Supreme Pontiff, is present in the midst of believers. For sitting at the right hand of God the Father, He is not absent from the congregation of His pontiffs, but above all through their eminent service He preaches the word of God to all nations and continually administers the sacraments of faith to believers, by their fatherly office (cf. 1 Cor 4:15) He incorporates new members into His body by supernal regeneration, and finally by their wisdom and prudence He directs and orders the People of the New Testament in their pilgrimage to eternal beatitude. These pastors, chosen to feed the Lord's flock, are ministers of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God (cf. 1 Cor 4:1), to whom is entrusted the testimony of the Gospel of the grace of God (cf. Rom 15:16; Acts 20:24), and the ministry of the Spirit and of justice in glory (cf. 2 Cor 3:8-9).","Episcopi igitur communitatis ministerium cum adiutoribus presbyteris et diaconis susceperunt, loco Dei praesidentes gregi, cuius sunt pastores, ut doctrinae magistri, sacri cultus sacerdotes, gubernationis ministri. Sicut autem permanet munus a Domino singulariter Petro, primo Apostolorum, concessum et successoribus eius transmittendum, ita permanet munus Apostolorum pascendi Ecclesiam, ab ordine sacrato Episcoporum iugiter exercendum. Proinde docet Sacra Synodus Episcopos ex divina institutione in locum Apostolorum successisse, tamquam Ecclesiae pastores, quos qui audit, Christum audit, qui vero spernit, Christum spernit et Eum qui Christum misit (cf. Lc 10,16)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p97","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","To fulfill so great functions, the Apostles were enriched by Christ with a special outpouring of the Holy Spirit coming upon them (cf. Acts 1:8; 2:4; Jn 20:22-23), and they themselves handed down the spiritual gift to their helpers by the imposition of hands (cf. 1 Tim 4:14; 2 Tim 1:6-7), which has been transmitted down to us in episcopal consecration. The holy Synod teaches that by episcopal consecration the fullness of the sacrament of Order is conferred, which indeed both by the liturgical custom of the Church and by the voice of the holy Fathers is called the supreme priesthood, the summit of the sacred ministry. Moreover, episcopal consecration, together with the office of sanctifying, also confers the offices of teaching and governing, which, however, by their very nature can only be exercised in hierarchical communion with the Head and the members of the College. For from tradition, which is especially declared by the liturgical rites and the usage of the Church both in the East and the West, it is clear that by the imposition of hands and the words of consecration, the grace of the Holy Spirit is so conferred and the sacred character so impressed, that bishops, in an eminent and visible manner, sustain the parts of Christ Himself, Teacher, Shepherd, and Pontiff, and act in His person. It pertains to bishops, through the sacrament of Order, to assume new elect into the episcopal body.","In Episcopis igitur, quibus presbyteri assistunt, adest in medio credentium Dominus Iesus Christus, Pontifex Summus. Sedens enim ad dexteram Dei Patris, non deest a suorum congregatione pontificum, sed imprimis per eorum eximium servitium verbum Dei omnibus gentibus praedicat et credentibus sacramenta fidei continuo administrat, eorum paterno munere (cf. 1Co 4,15) nova membra Corpori suo regeneratione superna incorporat, eorum denique sapientia et prudentia Populum Novi Testamenti in sua ad aeternam beatitudinem peregrinatione dirigit et ordinat. Hi pastores ad pascendum dominicum gregem electi, ministri Christi sunt et dispensatores mysteriorum Dei (cf. 1Co 4,1), quibus concredita est testificatio Evangelii gratiae Dei (cf. Rm 15,16 Ac 20,24), atque ministratio Spiritus et iustitiae in gloria (cf. 2Co 3,8-9)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p98","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Just as, by the Lord's disposition, St. Peter and the other Apostles constitute one apostolic college, in like manner the Roman Pontiff, the successor of Peter, and the bishops, the successors of the Apostles, are joined together among themselves. Already the most ancient discipline, by which bishops constituted throughout the whole world communicated with one another and with the Roman bishop in the bond of unity, charity, and peace, and likewise the councils assembled, by which deeper matters were decided in common, the opinion of many having been weighed by counsel, signify the nature and collegial character of the episcopal order; which the ecumenical councils celebrated in the course of the centuries clearly prove. The same is also indicated by the very practice, introduced from ancient times, of summoning several bishops to take part in raising a newly elected to the ministry of the supreme priesthood. One is constituted a member of the episcopal body by virtue of sacramental consecration and hierarchical communion with the Head and the members of the College.","Ad tanta munera explenda, Apostoli speciali effusione supervenientis Spiritus Sancti a Christo ditati sunt (cf. Ac 1,8 Ac 2,4 Jn 20,22-23), et ipsi adiutoribus suis per impositionem manuum donum spirituale tradiderunt (cf. 1Tm 4,14 2Tm 1,6-7), quod usque ad nos in episcopali consecratione transmissum est. Docet autem Sancta Synodus episcopali consecratione plenitudinem conferri sacramenti Ordinis, quae nimirum et liturgica Ecclesiae consuetudine et voce Sanctorum Patrum summum sacerdotium, sacri ministerii summa nuncupatur. Episcopalis autem consecratio, cum munere sanctificandi, munera quoque confert docendi et regendi, quae tamen natura sua nonnisi in hierarchica communione cum Collegii Capite et membris exerceri possunt. Ex traditione enim, quae praesertim liturgicis ritibus et Ecclesiae tum Orientis tum Occidentis usu declaratur, perspicuum est manuum impositione et verbis consecrationis gratiam Spiritus Sancti ita conferri et sacrum characterem ita imprimi, ut Episcopi, eminenti ac adspectabili modo, ipsius Christi Magistri, Pastoris et Pontificis partes sustineant et in Eius persona agant. Episcoporum est per Sacramentum Ordinis novos electos in corpus episcopale assumere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p99","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","But the college or body of bishops has no authority unless it is understood together with the Roman Pontiff, the successor of Peter, as its head, the power of primacy of the same remaining whole, which is over all, both pastors and faithful. For the Roman Pontiff has in the Church, by virtue of his office, namely as the Vicar of Christ and Pastor of the whole Church, full, supreme, and universal power, which he can always exercise freely. The order of bishops, which succeeds the college of apostles in pastoral teaching and governance, indeed in which the apostolic body continuously perseveres, together with its head the Roman Pontiff, and never without this head, also exists as the subject of supreme and full power over the universal Church, which power, however, can only be exercised with the consent of the Roman Pontiff. The Lord placed Simon alone as the rock and key-bearer of the Church (cf. Mt 16:18-19), and constituted him pastor of His whole flock (cf. Jn 21:15 ff.); but the office of binding and loosing, which was given to Peter (Mt 16:19), is known to have been given also to the college of apostles joined with its head (Mt 18:18; 28:16-20). This college, insofar as it is composed of many, expresses the variety and universality of the People of God, but insofar as it is gathered under one head, expresses the unity of the flock of Christ.","Sicut, statuente Domino, sanctus Petrus et ceteri Apostoli unum Collegium apostolicum constituunt, pari ratione Romanus Pontifex, successor Petri, et Episcopi, successores Apostolorum, inter se coniunguntur. Iam perantiqua disciplina, qua Episcopi in universo orbe constituti ad invicem et cum Romano Episcopo communicabant in vinculo unitatis, caritatis et pacis, itemque concilia coadunata, per quae et altiora quaeque in commune statuerentur, sententia multorum consilio ponderata, ordinis episcopalis indolem et rationem collegialem significant; quam manifeste comprobant Concilia oecumenica decursu saeculorum celebrata. Eandem vero iam innuit ipse usus, antiquitus inductus, plures advocandi Episcopos qui in novo electo ad summi sacerdotii ministerium elevando partem haberent. Membrum Corporis episcopalis aliquis constituitur vi sacramentalis consecrationis et hierarchica communione cum Collegii Capite atque membris."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p100","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","In it, the bishops, faithfully preserving the primacy and leadership of their head, exercise their own power for the good of their faithful, indeed of the whole Church, the Holy Spirit continually strengthening the organic structure and its concord. The supreme power over the universal Church, which this college possesses, is exercised in a solemn manner in an Ecumenical Council. An Ecumenical Council never takes place which is not confirmed as such or at least received by the successor of Peter; and it is the prerogative of the Roman Pontiff to convoke these councils, to preside over them, and to confirm them. The same collegial power can be exercised together with the pope by the bishops living throughout the world, provided that the Head of the College calls them to collegial action, or at least approves or freely accepts the united action of the dispersed bishops, so that it becomes a true collegial act.","Collegium autem seu corpus Episcoporum auctoritatem non habet, nisi simul cum Pontifice Romano, successore Petri, ut capite eius intellegatur, huiusque integre manente potestate Primatus in omnes sive Pastores sive fideles Romanus enim Pontifex habet in Ecclesiam, vi muneris sui, Vicarii scilicet Christi et totius Ecclesiae Pastoris, plenam, supremam et universalem potestatem, quam semper libere exercere valet. Ordo autem Episcoporum, qui collegio Apostolorum in magisterio et regimine pastorali succedit, immo in quo corpus apostolicum continuo perseverat, una cum Capite suo Romano Pontifice, et numquam sine hoc Capite, subiectum quoque supremae ac plenae potestatis in universam Ecclesiam exsistit, quae quidem potestas nonnisi consentiente Romano Pontifice exerceri potest. Dominus unum Simonem ut petram et clavigerum Ecclesiae posuit (cf. Mt 16,18-19), eumque Pastorem totius sui gregis constituit (cf. Jn 21,15 ss.); illud autem ligandi ac solvendi munus, quod Petro datum est (Mt 16,19), collegio quoque Apostolorum, suo Capiti coniuncto, tributum esse constat (Mt 18,18 Mt 28,16-20). Collegium hoc quatenus ex multis compositum, varietatem et universalitatem Populi Dei, quatenus vero sub uno capite collectum, unitatem gregis Christi exprimit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p101","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The collegial union also appears in the mutual relations of individual bishops with particular churches and the universal church. The Roman Pontiff, as the successor of Peter, is the perpetual and visible principle and foundation of the unity both of the bishops and of the multitude of the faithful. Individual bishops, however, are the visible principle and foundation of unity in their own particular churches, formed after the image of the universal church, in which and from which the one and only catholic Church exists. For this reason, each individual bishop represents his own church, but all together with the pope represent the whole Church in the bond of peace, love, and unity.","In ipso, Episcopi, primatum et principatum Capitis sui fideliter servantes, propria potestate in bonum fidelium suorum, immo totius Ecclesiae funguntur, Spiritu Sancto organicam structuram eiusque concordiam continenter roborante Suprema in universam Ecclesiam potestas, qua istud Collegium pollet, sollemni modo in Concilio Oecumenico exercetur Concilium Oecumenicum numquam datur, quod a Successore Petri non sit ut tale confirmatum vel saltem receptum; et Romani Pontificis praerogativa est haec Concilia convocare, iisdem praesidere et eadem confirmare. Eadem potestas collegialis una cum Papa exerceri potest ab Episcopis in orbe terrarum degentibus, dummodo Caput Collegii eos ad actionem collegialem vocet, vel saltem Episcoporum dispersorum unitam actionem approbet vel libere recipiat, ita ut verus actus collegialis efficiatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p102","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Individual bishops, who are placed over particular churches, exercise their pastoral governance over the portion of the People of God entrusted to them, not over other churches nor over the universal church. But as members of the episcopal college and legitimate successors of the apostles, each is bound by the institution and precept of Christ to that solicitude for the universal church which, even if not exercised through an act of jurisdiction, nevertheless greatly contributes to the good of the universal church. For all bishops ought to promote and protect the unity of faith and the discipline common to the whole Church, to instruct the faithful to love the whole mystical body of Christ, especially the poor, the suffering, and those who suffer persecution for justice's sake (cf. Mt 5:10), finally to promote every activity which is common to the whole Church, especially that faith may increase and the light of full truth arise for all men. Moreover, this is holy, that by well governing his own church as a portion of the universal church, he effectively contributes to the good of the whole mystical body, which is also the body of churches.","Collegialis unio etiam in mutuis relationibus singulorum Episcoporum cum particularibus Ecclesiis Ecclesiaque universali apparet. Romanus Pontifex, ut successor Petri, est unitatis, tum Episcoporum tum fidelium multitudinis, perpetuum ac visibile principium et fundamentum. Episcopi autem singuli visibile principium et fundamentum sunt unitatis in suis Ecclesiis particularibus, ad imaginem Ecclesiae universalis formatis, in quibus et ex quibus una et unica Ecclesia catholica exsistit. Qua de causa singuli Episcopi suam Ecclesiam, omnes autem simul cum Papa totam Ecclesiam repraesentant in vinculo pacis, amoris et unitatis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p103","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The care to announce the Gospel everywhere on earth pertains to the body of pastors, to whom Christ gave the mandate in common, imposing a common office, as Pope Celestine already commended to the Fathers of the Council of Ephesus. Hence individual bishops, insofar as the performance of their own office permits, are bound to enter into a fellowship of labors among themselves and with the successor of Peter, to whom the great office of propagating the Christian name has been entrusted in a special manner. Therefore they ought to supply to missions both harvest workers and also spiritual and material aids, both directly by themselves, and by arousing the ardent cooperation of the faithful. Finally, bishops, in the universal fellowship of charity, should gladly offer fraternal assistance to other churches, especially to neighboring and more needy ones, according to the venerable example of antiquity.","Singuli Episcopi, qui particularibus Ecclesiis praeficiuntur, regimen suum pastorale super portionem Populi Dei sibi commissam, non super alias Ecclesias neque super Ecclesiam universalem exercent. Sed qua membra Collegii episcopalis et legitimi Apostolorum successores singuli ea sollicitudine pro universa Ecclesia ex Christi institutione et praecepto tenentur, quae, etiamsi per actum iurisdictionis non exerceatur, summopere tamen confert ad Ecclesiae universalis emolumentum. Debent enim omnes Episcopi promovere et tueri unitatem fidei et disciplinam cunctae Ecclesiae communem, fideles edocere ad amorem totius Corporis mystici Christi, praesertim membrorum pauperum, dolentium et eorum qui persecutionem patiuntur propter iustitiam (cf. Mt 5,10), tandem promovere omnem actuositatem quae toti Ecclesiae communis est, praesertim ut fides incrementum capiat et lux plenae veritatis omnibus hominibus oriatur. Ceterum hoc sanctum est quod, bene regendo propriam Ecclesiam ut portionem Ecclesiae universalis, ipsi efficaciter conferunt ad bonum totius mystici Corporis, quod est etiam corpus Ecclesiarum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p104","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","By divine Providence it has come about that various churches established in various places by the Apostles and their successors have in the course of time coalesced into several groups, organically joined, which, preserving the unity of faith and the unique divine constitution of the universal church, enjoy their own discipline, their own liturgical usage, their own theological and spiritual patrimony. Among these, some, notably the ancient Patriarchal churches, as it were mother churches, gave birth to others as daughters, with which they are connected up to our times by a closer bond of charity in sacramental life and in mutual respect of rights and duties. This variety of local churches conspiring in unity more lucidly demonstrates the catholicity of the undivided Church. In like manner, episcopal conferences today can contribute manifold and fruitful aid, that the collegial effect may be brought to concrete application.","Cura Evangelium ubique terrarum annuntiandi ad corpus Pastorum pertinet, quibus omnibus in commune Christus mandatum dedit imponendo commune officium, ut iam Papa Coelestinus Patribus Ephesini Concilii commendavit. Unde singuli Episcopi, quantum propria eorum perfunctio muneris sinit, in laborum societatem venire tenentur inter se et cum successore Petri, cui grande munus christiani nominis propagandi singulari modo demandatum est. Quare missionibus tum messis operarios, tum etiam auxilia spiritualia et materialia, tam per se directe, quam suscitando fidelium ardentem cooperationem, suppeditare omnibus viribus debent. Episcopi denique, in universali caritatis societate, fraternum adiutorium aliis Ecclesiis, praesertim finitimis et egentioribus, secundum venerandum antiquitatis exemplum, libenter praebeant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p105","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Bishops, as successors of the Apostles, receive from the Lord, to whom all power is given in heaven and on earth, the mission to teach all nations and to preach the Gospel to every creature, that all men, through faith, baptism, and the fulfillment of the commandments, may attain salvation (cf. Mt 28:18; Mk 16:15-16; Acts 26:17 ff.). To fulfill this mission, Christ the Lord promised the Holy Spirit to the Apostles and sent Him from heaven on the day of Pentecost, by whose power they would be witnesses to Him to the ends of the earth, before nations and peoples and kings (cf. Acts 1:8; 2:1 ff.; 9:15). That office, however, which the Lord entrusted to the pastors of His people, is a true service which in sacred literature is significantly called diakonia or ministry (cf. Acts 1:17, 25; 21:19; Rom 11:13; 1 Tim 1:12).","Divina autem Providentia factum est ut variae variis in locis ab Apostolis eorumque successoribus institutae Ecclesiae decursu temporum in plures coaluerint coetus, organice coniunctos, qui, salva fidei unitate et unica divina constitutione universalis Ecclesiae, gaudent propria disciplina, proprio liturgico usu, theologico spiritualique patrimonio. Inter quas aliquae, notatim antiquae Patriarchales Ecclesiae, veluti matrices fidei, alias pepererunt quasi filias, quibuscum arctiore vinculo caritatis in vita sacramentali atque in mutua iurium et officiorum reverentia ad nostra usque tempora connectuntur. Quae Ecclesiarum localium in unum conspirans varietas indivisae Ecclesiae catholicitatem luculentius demonstrat. Simili ratione Coetus Episcopales hodie multiplicem atque fecundam opem conferre possunt, ut collegialis effectus ad concretam applicationem perducatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p106","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The canonical mission of bishops can be made through legitimate customs, not revoked by the supreme and universal power of the Church, or through laws enacted or recognized by the same authority, or directly through the successor of Peter Himself; the latter refusing or denying apostolic communion, bishops cannot be assumed into office.","Episcopi, utpote Apostolorum successores, a Domino, cui omnis potestas in caelo et in terra data est, missionem accipiunt docendi omnes gentes et praedicandi Evangelium omni creaturae, ut homines universi, per fidem, baptismum et adimpletionem mandatorum salutem consequantur (cf. Mt 28,18 Mc 16,15-16 Ac 26,17 s.). Ad hanc missionem implendam, Christus Dominus Spiritum Sanctum promisit Apostolis et die Pentecostes e caelo misit, cuius virtute testes Eidem essent usque ad ultimum terrae, coram gentibus et populis et regibus (cf. Ac 1,8 Ac 2,1 ss.; 9, 15). Munus autem illud, quod Dominus pastoribus populi sui commisit, verum est servitium quod in sacris Litteris diakonia seu ministerium significanter nuncupatur (cf. Ac 1,17 Ac 1,25 Ac 21,19 Rm 11,13 1Tm 1,12)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p107","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Among the principal offices of bishops, the preaching of the Gospel stands out. For bishops are heralds of the faith, who lead new disciples to Christ, and authentic teachers, that is, endowed with the authority of Christ, who preach to the people entrusted to them the faith to be believed and to be applied to morals, and illustrate it under the light of the Holy Spirit, bringing forth from the treasure of Revelation new and old (cf. Mt 13:52), make it fruitful, and vigilantly ward off errors threatening their flock (cf. 2 Tim 4:1-4). Bishops, teaching in communion with the Roman Pontiff, are to be venerated by all as witnesses of divine and catholic truth; the faithful, moreover, ought to concur in the sentence of their bishop concerning faith and morals pronounced in the name of Christ, and adhere to it with a religious homage of spirit. This religious homage of will and intellect is to be rendered in a special way to the authentic magisterium of the Roman Pontiff even when he is not speaking ex cathedra; namely, so that his supreme magisterium be reverently acknowledged, and the sentences pronounced by him be sincerely adhered to, according to his manifest mind and will, which shows itself especially either by the nature of the documents, or by the frequent proposition of the same doctrine, or by the manner of speaking.","Episcoporum autem missio canonica fieri potest per legitimas consuetudines, a suprema et universali potestate Ecclesiae non revocatas, vel per leges ab eadem auctoritate latas aut agnitas, vel directe per ipsum Successorem Petri; quo renuente seu communionem Apostolicam denegante, Episcopi in officium assumi nequeunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p108","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Although individual bishops do not enjoy the prerogative of infallibility, when they, even dispersed throughout the world, but preserving the bond of communion among themselves and with the successor of Peter, authentically teaching matters of faith and morals, agree in one sentence as to be held definitively, they infallibly enunciate the doctrine of Christ. This is still more clearly had when, assembled in an Ecumenical Council, they are teachers and judges of faith and morals for the universal Church, whose definitions in faith are to be adhered to with the homage of faith.","Inter praecipua Episcoporum munera eminet praedicatio Evangelii. Episcopi enim sunt fidei praecones, qui novos discipulos ad Christum adducunt, et doctores authentici seu auctoritate Christi praediti, qui populo sibi commisso fidem credendam et moribus applicandam praedicant, et sub lumine Sancti Spiritus illustrant, ex thesauro Revelationis nova et vetera proferentes (cf. Mt 13,52), eam fructificare faciunt erroresque gregi suo impendentes vigilanter arcent (cf. 2Tm 4,1-4). Episcopi in communione cum Romano Pontifice docentes ab omnibus tamquam divinae et catholicae veritatis testes venerandi sunt; fideles autem in sui Episcopi sententiam de fide et moribus nomine Christi prolatam concurrere, eique religioso animi obsequio adhaerere debent. Hoc vero religiosum voluntatis et intellectus obsequium singulari ratione praestandum est Romani Pontificis authentico magisterio etiam cum non ex cathedra loquitur; ita nempe ut magisterium eius supremum reverenter agnoscatur, et sententiis ab eo prolatis sincere adhaereatur, iuxta mentem et voluntatem manifestatam ipsius, quae se prodit praecipue sive indole documentorum, sive ex frequenti propositione eiusdem doctrinae, sive ex dicendi ratione."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p109","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","This infallibility, however, with which the Divine Redeemer willed His Church to be endowed in defining doctrine concerning faith or morals, extends only as far as the deposit of divine Revelation extends, to be holy guarded and faithfully expounded. With this infallibility, the Roman Pontiff, the Head of the College of Bishops, enjoys by virtue of his office, when, as the supreme pastor and teacher of all Christ's faithful, who confirms his brothers in the faith (cf. Lk 22:32), he proclaims doctrine concerning faith or morals by a definitive act. Therefore his definitions, of themselves, and not from the consent of the Church, are justly said to be irreformable, since they were pronounced with the assistance of the Holy Spirit, promised to him in blessed Peter, and therefore need no approval of others, nor do they admit appeal to any other judgment. For then the Roman Pontiff pronounces sentence not as a private person, but as the supreme teacher of the universal Church, in whom the charism of infallibility of the Church itself resides individually, when he expounds or defends the doctrine of catholic faith. The infallibility promised to the Church also resides in the body of bishops when it exercises the supreme magisterium with the successor of Peter. To these definitions the assent of the Church can never be lacking, on account of the action of the same Holy Spirit, by which the whole flock of Christ is kept and progresses in the unity of faith.","Licet singuli praesules infallibilitatis praerogativa non polleant, quando tamen, etiam per orbem dispersi, sed communionis nexum inter se et cum Successore Petri servantes, authentice res fidei et morum docentes in unam sententiam tamquam definitive tenendam conveniunt, doctrinam Christi infallibiliter enuntiant. Quod adhuc manifestius habetur quando, in Concilio Oecumenico coadunati, pro universa Ecclesia fidei et morum doctores et iudices sunt, quorum definitionibus fidei obsequio est adhaerendum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p110","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","When, however, either the Roman Pontiff or the body of bishops with him define a sentence, they pronounce it according to Revelation itself, to which all are bound to stand and be conformed, and which is integrally transmitted in writing or by tradition through the legitimate succession of bishops and especially through the care of the Roman Pontiff Himself, and under the light of the Spirit of truth is holy kept and faithfully expounded in the Church. To rightly investigate and aptly set forth this Revelation, the Roman Pontiff and bishops, according to their office and the gravity of the matter, sedulously work through suitable means; but they do not accept new public revelation as pertaining to the divine deposit of faith.","Haec autem infallibilitas, qua Divinus Redemptor Ecclesiam suam in definienda doctrina de fide vel moribus instructam esse voluit, tantum patet quantum divinae Revelationis patet depositum, sancte custodiendum et fideliter exponendum. Qua quidem infallibilitate Romanus Pontifex, Collegii Episcoporum Caput, vi muneris sui gaudet, quando, ut supremus omnium christifidelium pastor et doctor, qui fratres suos in fide confirmat (cf. Lc 22,32), doctrinam de fide vel moribus definitivo actu proclamat. Quare definitiones eius ex sese, et non ex consensu Ecclesiae, irreformabiles merito dicuntur, quippe quae sub assistentia Spiritus Sancti, ipsi in beato Petro promissa, prolatae sint, ideoque nulla indigeant aliorum approbatione, nec ullam ad aliud iudicium appellationem patiantur. Tunc enim Romanus Pontifex non ut persona privata sententiam profert, sed ut universalis Ecclesiae magister supremus, in quo charisma infallibilitatis ipsius Ecclesiae singulariter inest, doctrinam fidei catholicae exponit vel tuetur. Infallibilitas Ecclesiae promissa in corpore Episcoporum quoque inest, quando supremum magisterium cum Petri Successore exercet. Istis autem definitionibus assensus Ecclesiae numquam deesse potest propter actionem eiusdem Spiritus Sancti, qua universus Christi grex in unitate fidei servatur et proficit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p111","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The bishop, marked with the fullness of the sacrament of Order, is the steward of the grace of the supreme priesthood, especially in the Eucharist, which he himself offers or causes to be offered, and by which the Church continually lives and grows. This Church of Christ is truly present in all legitimate local congregations of the faithful, which, adhering to their shepherds, are themselves called churches in the New Testament. For these are in their own place the new people called by God, in the Holy Spirit and in much fullness (cf. 1 Th 1:5). In them, by the preaching of the Gospel of Christ, the faithful are gathered together and the mystery of the Lord's Supper is celebrated, that through the food and blood of the Lord the whole brotherhood of the body may be bound together. In any altar community, under the sacred ministry of the bishop, is exhibited the symbol of that charity and unity of the mystical body, without which there can be no salvation. In these communities, although often small and poor, or living in dispersion, Christ is present, by whose power the one, holy, catholic, and apostolic Church is united. For the participation in the body and blood of Christ does nothing other than to transform us into that which we receive.","Cum autem sive Romanus Pontifex sive Corpus Episcoporum cum eo sententiam definiunt, eam proferunt secundum ipsam Revelationem, cui omnes stare et conformari tenentur et quae scripta vel tradita per legitimam Episcoporum successionem et imprimis ipsius Romani Pontificis cura integre transmittitur, atque praelucente Spiritu veritatis in Ecclesia sancte servatur et fideliter exponitur. Ad quam rite indagandam et apte enuntiandam, Romanus Pontifex et Episcopi, pro officio suo et rei gravitate, per media apta, sedulo operam navant; novam vero revelationem publicam tamquam ad divinum fidei depositum pertinentem non accipiunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p112","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Moreover, every legitimate celebration of the Eucharist is directed by the bishop, to whom the office has been committed of deferring the cult of the Christian religion to the Divine Majesty and of administering it according to the precepts of the Lord and the laws of the Church, further determined by his particular judgment for the diocese.","Episcopus, plenitudine sacramenti Ordinis insignitus, est oeconomus gratiae supremi sacerdotii, praesertim in Eucharistia, quam ipse offert vel offerri curat, et qua continuo vivit et crescit Ecclesia. Haec Christi Ecclesia vere adest in omnibus legitimis fidelium congregationibus localibus, quae, pastoribus suis adhaerentes, et ipsae in Novo Testamento ecclesiae vocantur. Hae sunt enim loco suo Populus novus a Deo vocatus, in Spiritu Sancto et in plenitudine multa (cf. 1Th 1,5). In eis praedicatione Evangelii Christi congregantur fideles et celebratur mysterium Coenae Domini, ut per escam et sanguinem Domini corporis fraternitas cuncta copuletur. In quavis altaris communitate, sub Episcopi sacro ministerio, exhibetur symbolum illius caritatis et unitatis Corporis mystici, sine qua non potest esse salus. In his communitatibus, licet saepe exiguis et pauperibus, vel in dispersione degentibus, praesens est Christus, cuius virtute consociatur una, sancta, catholica et apostolica Ecclesia. Etenim non aliud agit participatio corporis et sanguinis Christi, quam ut in id quod sumimus transeamus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p113","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Thus bishops, praying and laboring for the people, from the fullness of the holiness of Christ pour forth multiformly and abundantly. Through the ministry of the word they communicate the power of God to believers for salvation (cf. Rom 1:16), and through the sacraments, the regular and fruitful distribution of which they order by their authority, they sanctify the faithful. They rule the conferral of baptism, by which participation in the royal priesthood of Christ is granted. They are the original ministers of confirmation, dispensers of sacred orders, and moderators of penitential discipline, and they solicitously exhort and instruct their people that they may fulfill their parts in the liturgy and especially in the holy sacrifice of the Mass with faith and reverence. Finally, they ought to profit those whom they preside over by the example of their conversation, tempering their morals from all evil and, as much as they can, the Lord helping, changing them to good, that they may come to eternal life, together with the flock entrusted to them.","Omnis autem legitima Eucharistiae celebratio dirigitur ab Episcopo, cui officium commissum est cultum christianae religionis Divinae Maiestati deferendi atque administrandi secundum praecepta Domi ni et Ecclesiae leges, eius particulari iudicio ulterius pro dioecesi determinatas."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p114","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Bishops govern the particular churches entrusted to them as vicars and legates of Christ, by counsels, persuasions, examples, but also by authority and sacred power, which indeed they use only for building up their flock in truth and holiness, mindful that he who is greater should become as the younger, and he who is the leader as the servant (cf. Lk 22:26-27). This power, which they exercise personally in the name of Christ, is proper, ordinary, and immediate, although the exercise of it is ultimately regulated by the supreme authority of the Church and, for the utility of the Church or of the faithful, can be circumscribed by certain limits. By virtue of this power, bishops have the sacred right and duty before the Lord to make laws for their subjects, to pass judgment, and to moderate all things which pertain to the order of worship and apostolate.","Ita Episcopi, orando pro populo et laborando, de plenitudine sanctitatis Christi multiformiter et abundanter effundunt. Per ministerium verbi virtutem Dei credentibus in salutem communicant (cf. Rm 1,16), et per sacramenta, quorum regularem et fructuosam distributionem auctoritate sua ordinant, fideles sanctificant. Ipsi regunt collationem baptismi, quo regalis sacerdotii Christi participatio conceditur. Ipsi sunt ministri originarii confirmationis, dispensatores sacrorum ordinum et moderatores disciplinae poenitentialis, atque populos suos, ut in liturgia et praesertim in sacro Missae sacrificio partes suas fide et reverentia impleant, sollicite exhortantur et instruunt. Eis denique quibus praesunt exemplo conversationis suae proficere debent, mores suos ab omni malo temperantes et quantum poterint, Domino adiuvante, ad bonum commutando, ut ad vitam, una cum grege sibi credito, perveniant sempiternam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p115","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Their pastoral office, that is, the habitual and daily care of their sheep, is fully committed to them, nor are they to be considered vicars of the Roman Pontiffs, because they exercise a power proper to themselves and are truly called prelates of the people they govern. Their power therefore is not destroyed by the supreme and universal power, but is on the contrary asserted, strengthened, and vindicated, the Holy Spirit indefectibly preserving the form of governance established by Christ the Lord in His Church.","Episcopi Ecclesias particulares sibi commissas ut vicarii et legati Christi regunt, consiliis, suasionibus, exemplis, verum etiam auctoritate et sacra potestate, qua quidem nonnisi ad gregem suum in veritate et sanctitate aedificandum utuntur, memores quod qui maior est fiat sicut minor et qui praecessor est sicut ministrator (cf. Lc 22,26-27). Haec potestas qua nomine Christi personaliter funguntur, est propria, ordinaria et immediata, licet a suprema Ecclesiae auctoritate exercitium eiusdem ultimatim regatur et certis limitibus, intuitu utilitatis Ecclesiae vel fidelium, circumscribi possit. Vi huius potestatis Episcopi sacrum ius et coram Domino officium habent in suos subditos leges ferendi, iudicium faciendi, atque omnia, quae ad cultus apostolatusque ordinem pertinent, moderandi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p116","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The bishop, sent by the Father of the family to govern his family, should keep before his eyes the example of the Good Shepherd, who came not to be ministered unto but to minister (cf. Mt 20:28; Mk 10:45) and to lay down his life for the sheep (cf. Jn 10:11). Taken from among men and surrounded with weakness, he can sympathize with those who are ignorant and err (cf. Heb 5:1-2). Let him not refuse to hear his subjects, whom he fosters as true sons and exhorts to cooperate with him readily. He must have care, about to give an account for their souls to God (cf. Heb 13:17), both for them and also for those who are not yet of the one flock, whom he has commended to him in the Lord, through prayer, preaching, and all works of charity. Since, like Paul the Apostle, he is a debtor to all, let him be ready to preach the Gospel to all (cf. Rom 1:14-15), and to exhort his faithful to apostolic and missionary activity. The faithful, moreover, ought to adhere to their bishop as the Church does to Jesus Christ, and as Jesus Christ does to the Father, that all things may consent through unity and abound to the glory of God (cf. 2 Cor 4:15).","Ipsis munus pastorale seu habitualis et cotidiana cura ovium suarum plene committitur, neque vicarii Romanorum Pontificum putandi sunt, quia potestatem gerunt sibi propriam verissimeque populorum quos regunt, Antistites dicuntur. Eorum itaque potestas a suprema et universali potestate non eliditur, sed e contra asseritur, roboratur et vindicatur, Spiritu Sancto constitutam a Christo Domino in sua Ecclesia regiminis formam indefectibiliter servante."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p117","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Christ, whom the Father sanctified and sent into the world (Jn 10:36), made His apostles, and their successors, namely the bishops, sharers in His consecration and mission, who have legitimately handed down the office of their ministry, in various degrees, to various subjects in the Church. Thus the ecclesiastical ministry, divinely instituted, is exercised in different orders by those who from ancient times are called bishops, presbyters, deacons.","Episcopus, missus a Patrefamilias ad gubernandam familiam suam, ante oculos teneat exemplum Boni Pastoris, qui venit non ministrari sed ministrare (cf. Mt 20,28 Mc 10,45) et animam suam pro ovibus ponere (cf. Jn 10,11). Assumptus ex hominibus et circumdatus infirmitate, condolere potest iis qui ignorant et errant (cf. He 5,1-2). Subditos, quos ut veros filios suos fovet et ad alacriter secum cooperandum exhortatur, audire ne renuat. Pro animabus eorum rationem redditurus Deo (cf. He 13,17) oratione, praedicatione omnibusque operibus caritatis curam habeat tum eorundem, tum etiam illorum qui de uno grege nondum sunt, quos in Domino commendatos sibi habeat. Ipse, cum sicut Paulus Apostolus cunctis debitor sit, promptus sit omnibus evangelizare (cf. Rm 1,14-15), fidelesque suos ad operositatem apostolicam et missionalem exhortari. Fideles autem Episcopo adhaerere debent sicut Ecclesia Iesu Christo, et sicut Iesus Christus Patri, ut omnia per unitatem consentiant, et abundent in gloriam Dei (cf. 2Co 4,15)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p118","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Presbyters, although they do not have the summit of the pontificate and depend on bishops in the exercise of their power, are nevertheless joined with them in priestly honor and, by virtue of the sacrament of Order, after the image of Christ, the supreme and eternal Priest (Heb 5:1-10; 7:24; 9:11-28), are consecrated to preach the Gospel, to feed the faithful, and to celebrate divine worship, as true priests of the New Testament. Participants in the office of the one Mediator Christ (1 Tim 2:5) in their degree of ministry, they announce the divine word to all. They exercise their sacred office especially in the eucharistic cult or synaxis, in which, acting in the person of Christ and proclaiming His mystery, they join the prayers of the faithful to the sacrifice of their Head, and represent and apply in the sacrifice of the Mass, until the coming of the Lord (cf. 1 Cor 11:26), the unique sacrifice of the New Testament, namely Christ offering Himself once as an immaculate victim to the Father (cf. Heb 9:11-28). For penitent or sick faithful they highly exercise the ministry of reconciliation and alleviation, and they bring the needs and prayers of the faithful to God the Father (cf. Heb 5:1-4). Exercising in their part of authority the office of Christ the Shepherd and Head, they gather and lead the family of God as a fraternity animated in one spirit, and through Christ in the Spirit they lead it to God the Father. They adore Him in spirit and truth (cf. Jn 4:24) in the midst of the flock. Finally, they labor in word and doctrine (cf. 1 Tim 5:17), believing what they have read meditating on the law of the Lord, teaching what they have believed, imitating what they have taught.","Christus, quem Pater sanctificavit et misit in mundum (Jn 10,36), consecrationis missionisque suae per Apostolos suos, eorum successores, videlicet Episcopos participes effecit, qui munus ministerii sui, vario gradu, variis subiectis in Ecclesia legitime tradiderunt. Sic ministerium ecclesiasticum divinitus institutum diversis ordinibus exercetur ab illis qui iam ab antiquo Episcopi, Presbyteri, Diaconi vocantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p119","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Presbyters, the provident cooperators of the episcopal order and its help and instrument, called to serve the People of God, constitute one presbytery with their bishop, dedicated to different offices. In each local congregation of the faithful they in a certain way make the bishop present, with whom they are joined with confident and great spirit, and take up his functions and solicitude in part and exercise daily care. Under the authority of the bishop they sanctify and govern the portion of the Lord's flock assigned to them, make the universal church visible in their place, and bring strong help to building up the whole body of Christ (cf. Eph 4:12). Always intent on the good of the sons of God, they should strive to contribute their work to the pastoral work of the whole diocese, indeed of the whole Church. On account of this participation in priesthood and mission, let presbyters truly acknowledge the bishop as father and reverently obey him. The bishop, however, should consider his presbyters as his cooperators, as sons and friends, just as Christ called His disciples no longer servants but friends (cf. Jn 15:15). Therefore all priests, both diocesan and religious, are incorporated into the body of bishops by reason of Order and ministry, and serve the good of the whole Church according to their vocation and grace.","Presbyteri, quamvis pontificatus apicem non habeant et in exercenda sua potestate ab Episcopis pendeant, cum eis tamen sacerdotali honore coniuncti sunt et vi sacramenti Ordinis, ad imaginem Christi, summi atque aeterni Sacerdotis (He 5,1-10 He 7,24 He 9,11-28), ad Evangelium praedicandum fidelesque pascendos et ad divinum cultum celebrandum consecrantur, ut veri sacerdotes Novi Testamenti. Muneris unici Mediatoris Christi (1Tm 2,5) participes in suo gradu ministerii, omnibus verbum divinum annuntiant. Suum vero munus sacrum maxime exercent in eucharistico cultu vel synaxi, qua in persona Christi agentes Eiusque mysterium proclamantes, vota fidelium sacrificio Capitis ipsorum coniungunt, et unicum sacrificium Novi Testamenti, Christi scilicet Sese Patri immaculatam hostiam semel offerentis (cf. He 9,11-28), in sacrificio Missae usque ad adventum Domini (cf. 1Co 11,26) repraesentant et applicant. Pro fidelibus autem poenitentibus vel aegrotantibus ministerio reconciliationis et alleviationis summe funguntur, et necessitates ac preces fidelium ad Deum Patrem afferunt (cf. He 5,1-4). Munus Christi Pastoris et Capitis pro sua parte auctoritatis exercentes, familiam Dei, ut fraternitatem in unum animatam, colligunt et per Christum in Spiritu ad Deum Patrem adducunt. In medio gregis Eum in spiritu et veritate adorant (cf. Jn 4,24). In verbo demum et doctrina laborant (cf. 1Tm 5,17), credentes quod in lege Domini meditantes legerint, docentes quod crediderint, imitantes quod docuerint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p120","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","By virtue of the common sacred ordination and mission, all presbyters are bound together in intimate fraternity, which should spontaneously and willingly manifest itself in mutual help, both spiritual and material, both pastoral and personal, in meetings and in communion of life, labor, and charity. Let them exercise the care of the faithful, whom they have spiritually begotten by baptism and doctrine (cf. 1 Cor 4:15; 1 Pet 1:23), as fathers in Christ. Made a form of the flock from the heart (1 Pet 5:3), let them so preside over and serve their local community that it may be able to be called worthily by that name by which the one and whole People of God is marked, namely the Church of God (cf. 1 Cor 1:2; 2 Cor 1:1). Let them be mindful that by their daily conversation and solicitude they ought to exhibit the face of a truly priestly and pastoral ministry to the faithful and the unfaithful, to catholics and non-catholics, and to bear witness to truth and life to all, and as good shepherds seek also those (cf. Lk 15:4-7) who, although baptized in the catholic Church, have fallen away from the practice of the sacraments, or indeed from the faith.","Presbyteri, ordinis Episcopalis providi cooperatores eiusque adiutorium et organum, ad Populo Dei inserviendum vocati, unum presbyterium cum suo Episcopo constituunt, diversis quidem officiis mancipatum. In singulis localibus fidelium congregationibus Episcopum, quocum fidenti et magno animo consociantur, quodammodo praesentem reddunt eiusque munera et sollicitudinem pro parte suscipiunt et cura cotidiana exercent. Qui sub auctoritate Episcopi portionem gregis dominici sibi addictam sanctificant et regunt, Ecclesiam universalem in suo loco visibilem faciunt et in aedificando toto corpore Christi (cf. Ep 4,12) validam opem afferunt. Ad bonum autem filiorum Dei semper intenti operam suam ad opus pastorale totius dioeceseos, immo totius Ecclesiae conferre studeant. Propter hanc in sacerdotio et missione participationem Presbyteri Episcopum vere ut patrem suum agnoscant eique reverenter oboediant. Episcopus vero Sacerdotes cooperatores suos ut filios et amicos consideret, sicut Christus discipulos suos iam non servos, sed amicos vocat (cf. Jn 15,15). Corpori igitur Episcoporum, ratione Ordinis et ministerii, omnes sacerdotes, tum dioecesani tum religiosi coaptantur et bono totius Ecclesiae pro sua vocatione et gratia inserviunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p121","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Because the human race today is more and more coalescing into civil, economic, and social unity, the more it is necessary that priests, with united care and help under the guidance of the bishops and the Supreme Pontiff, remove every form of dispersion, that the whole human race may be led into the unity of the family of God.","Vi communis sacrae ordinationis et missionis Presbyteri omnes inter se intima fraternitate nectuntur, quae sponte ac libenter sese manifestet in mutuo auxilio, tam spirituali quam materiali, tam pastorali quam personali, in conventibus et communione vitae, laboris et caritatis. Fidelium vero, quos spiritualiter baptismate et doctrina genuerunt (cf. 1Co 4,15 1P 1,23), curam tamquam patres in Christo agant. Forma facti gregis ex animo (l PT 5,3) suae communitati locali ita praesint et inserviant, ut ista digne vocari possit illo nomine, quo unus et totus Populus Dei insignitur, Ecclesiae scilicet Dei (cf. 1Co 1,2 2Co 1,1 cf.. Memores sint se sua cotidiana conversatione et sollicitudine fidelibus et infidelibus, catholicis et non catholicis, faciem ministerii vere sacerdotalis et pastoralis exhibere, omnibusque testimonium veritatis et vitae reddere debere, et ut boni pastores illos quoque quaerere (cf. Lc 15,4-7), qui baptizati quidem in Ecclesia catholica a praxi sacramentorum, vel imo a fide defecerunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p122","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","At the lower grade of the hierarchy stand deacons, upon whom hands are imposed not for the priesthood but for the ministry. For, strengthened by sacramental grace, they serve the People of God in the diaconia of liturgy, word, and charity, in communion with the bishop and his presbytery. It is the deacon's office, as assigned to him by competent authority, to administer baptism solemnly, to reserve and distribute the Eucharist, to assist at and bless marriages in the name of the Church, to bring Viaticum to the dying, to read sacred Scripture to the faithful, to instruct and exhort the people, to preside over the cult and prayer of the faithful, to administer sacramentals, to be in charge of the funeral and burial rite. Dedicated to the offices of charity and administration, let deacons be mindful of the admonition of blessed Polycarp: \"Merciful, diligent, walking according to the truth of the Lord, who was made the servant of all.\"","Quia genus humanum hodie magis magisque in unitatem civilem, oeconomicam et socialem coalescit, eo magis oportet ut sacerdotes, coniuncta cura et ope sub ductu Episcoporum et Summi Pontificis, omnem rationem dispersionis elidant, ut in unitatem familiae Dei totum genus humanum adducatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p123","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","Since, however, these offices, most necessary for the life of the Church, can with difficulty be fulfilled in many regions according to the discipline of the Latin Church currently in force, the Diaconate can in the future be restored as a proper and permanent grade of the hierarchy. It pertains to the competent various territorial episcopal conferences, the Supreme Pontiff approving, to decide whether and where it is opportune to institute such deacons for the care of souls. With the consent of the Roman Pontiff, this diaconate can be conferred on men of more mature age, even those living in marriage, as well as on suitable young men, for whom, however, the law of celibacy must remain firm.","In gradu inferiori hierarchiae sistunt Diaconi, quibus non ad sacerdotium, sed ad ministerium manus imponuntur. Gratia etenim sacramentali roborati, in diaconia liturgiae, verbi et caritatis Populo Dei, in communione cum Episcopo eiusque presbyterio, inserviunt. Diaconi est, prout ei a competenti auctoritate assignatum fuerit, solemniter baptismum administrare, Eucharistiam servare et distribuere, matrimonio Ecclesiae nomine adsistere et benedicere, Viaticum moribundis deferre, fidelibus sacram legere Scripturam, populum instruere et exhortari, fidelium cultui et orationi praesidere, sacramentalia ministrare, ritui funeris ac sepulturae praeesse. Caritatis et administrationis officiis dediti, meminerint Diaconi moniti Beati Polycarpi: Misericordes, seduli, incedentes iuxta veritatem Domini, qui omnium minister factus est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p124","Chapter III. on the Hierarchical Constitution of the Church and in Particular on","The holy Synod, having declared the offices of the Hierarchy, gladly turns its attention to the state of those Christ's faithful who are called laity. But if all things which have been said concerning the People of God are directed equally to laity, religious, and clerics, nevertheless certain things pertain particularly to laity, men and women, by reason of their condition and mission, the foundations of which, on account of the special circumstances of our times, must be more fully considered. For sacred pastors know well how much the laity contribute to the good of the whole Church. For pastors know that they were not instituted by Christ to assume upon themselves alone the whole saving mission of the Church toward the world, but that their excellent office is so to feed the faithful and to recognize their ministries and charisms that all may in their own way cooperate in the common work unanimously. For it is necessary that all, doing truth in charity, grow into Him in all things, who is the head Christ: from whom the whole body, compacted and knit together by every joint of supply, according to the operation in the measure of each member, makes increase of the body to the building up of itself in charity (Eph 4:15-16).","Cum vero haec munera, ad vitam Ecclesiae summopere necessaria, in disciplina Ecclesiae latinae hodie vigenti in pluribus regionibus adimpleri difficulter possint, Diaconatus in futurum tamquam proprius ac permanens gradus hierarchiae restitui poterit. Ad competentes autem varii generis territoriales Episcoporum coetus, approbante ipso Summo Pontifice, spectat decernere, utrum et ubinam pro cura animarum huiusmodi diaconos institui opportunum sit. De consensu Romani Pontificis hic diaconatus viris maturioris aetatis etiam in matrimonio viventibus conferri poterit, necnon iuvenibus idoneis, pro quibus tamen lex coelibatus firma remanere debet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p125","Chapter IV. on the Laity","By the name of laity are here understood all Christ's faithful except members of the sacred order and the religious state sanctioned in the Church, namely Christ's faithful who, as being incorporated into Christ by baptism, constituted into the People of God, and made sharers in their own way in the priestly, prophetic, and regal office of Christ, exercise for their own part the mission of the whole Christian people in the Church and in the world.","Sancta Synodus, muneribus Hierarchiae declaratis, libenter animum advertit statui illorum Christifidelium qui laici nuncupantur. Quodsi omnia quae de Populo Dei dicta sunt, ad laicos, religiosos et clericos aequaliter diriguntur, laicis tamen, viris et mulieribus, ratione condicionis et missionis, quaedam particulariter pertinent, quorum fundamenta ob specialia rerum adiuncta nostri temporis magis expendenda sunt. Pastores enim sacri probe norunt quantum laici ad bonum totius Ecclesiae conferant. Sciunt enim Pastores se a Christo non esse institutos, ut totam missionem salvificam Ecclesiae versus mundum in se solos suscipiant, sed praeclarum munus suum esse ita pascere fideles eorumque ministrationes et charismata ita recognoscere, ut cuncti suo modo ad commune opus unanimiter cooperentur. Oportet enim, ut omnes veritatem facientes in caritate, crescamus in Illo per omnia, qui est caput Christus: ex quo totum corpus compactum et connexum per omnem iuncturam subministrationis, secundum operationem in mensuram uniuscuiusque membri, augmentum corporis facit in aedificationem sui in caritate (Ep 4,15-16)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p126","Chapter IV. on the Laity","A secular character is proper and peculiar to laity. For members of the sacred order, although they can sometimes be engaged in secular matters, even by exercising a secular profession, are by reason of their particular vocation especially and professedly ordained to the sacred ministry, while religious by their state give a splendid and outstanding testimony that the world cannot be transfigured and offered to God without the spirit of the beatitudes. It is the laity's proper vocation to seek the kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and ordering them according to God. They live in the world, that is, in all and each of the worldly duties and works and in the ordinary conditions of family and social life, from which their existence is as it were woven together. There they are called by God that, exercising their proper office, led by the spirit of the Gospel, like leaven they may contribute to the sanctification of the world from within, and thus especially by the testimony of their life, shining with faith, hope, and charity, they may manifest Christ to others. Therefore it specially pertains to them to so illuminate and order all temporal things, with which they are closely joined, that they may always come to be and grow and be to the praise of the Creator and Redeemer.","Nomine laicorum hic intelleguntur omnes christifideles praeter membra ordinis sacri et status religiosi in Ecclesia sanciti, christifideles scilicet qui, utpote baptismate Christo concorporati, in Populum Dei constituti, et de munere Christi sacerdotali, prophetico et regali suo modo participes facti, pro parte sua missionem totius populi christiani in Ecclesia et in mundo exercent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p127","Chapter IV. on the Laity","The holy Church, by divine institution, is ordered and governed with a wonderful variety. For just as in one body we have many members, but all the members have not the same function: so we, many, are one body in Christ, but each members one of another (Rom 12:4-5).","Laicis indoles saecularis propria et peculiaris est. Membra enim ordinis sacri, quamquam aliquando in saecularibus versari possunt, etiam saecularem professionem exercendo, ratione suae particularis vocationis praecipue et ex professo ad sacrum ministerium ordinantur, dum religiosi suo statu praeclarum et eximium testimonium reddunt, mundum transfigurari Deoque offerri non posse sine spiritu beatitudinum. Laicorum est, ex vocatione propria, res temporales gerendo et secundum Deum ordinando, regnum Dei quaerere. In saeculo vivunt, scilicet in omnibus et singulis mundi officiis et operibus et in ordinariis vitae familiaris et socialis condicionibus, quibus eorum exsistentia quasi contexitur. Ubi a Deo vocantur, ut suum proprium munus exercendo, spiritu evangelico ducti, fermenti instar ad mundi sanctificationem velut ab intra conferant, sicque praeprimis testimonio vitae suae, fide, spe et caritate fulgentes, Christum aliis manifestent. Ad illos ergo peculiari modo spectat res temporales omnes, quibus arcte coniunguntur, ita illuminare et ordinare, ut secundum Christum iugiter fiant et crescant et sint in laudem Creatoris et Redemptoris."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p128","Chapter IV. on the Laity","There is therefore one chosen People of God: one Lord, one faith, one baptism (Eph 4:5); common dignity of the members from their regeneration in Christ, common grace of sons, common vocation to perfection, one salvation, one hope, and undivided charity. Therefore there is no inequality in Christ and in the Church, regard being had to race or nation, social condition or sex, for there is neither Jew nor Greek: neither servant nor free: neither male nor female. For you are all one in Christ Jesus (Gal 3:28; cf. Col 3:11).","Ecclesia sancta, ex divina institutione, mira varietate ordinatur et regitur. Sicut enim in uno corpore multa membra habemus, omnia autem membra non eundem actum habent: ita multi unum corpus sumus in Christo singuli autem alter alterius membra (Rm 12,4-5)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p129","Chapter IV. on the Laity","If therefore in the Church not all walk by the same path, yet all are called to holiness and have obtained co-equal faith in the justice of God (cf. 2 Pet 1:1). Although some by the will of Christ are constituted as teachers, stewards of the mysteries, and shepherds for others, yet true equality among all flourishes as to dignity and common action for building up the body of Christ. For the distinction which the Lord placed between sacred ministers and the rest of the People of God carries with it conjunction, since pastors and other faithful are bound together by a common mutual relation; the pastors of the Church, following the example of the Lord, minister to one another and to the other faithful, while these gladly offer their cooperative work to the pastors and teachers. Thus in variety all bear witness to the wonderful unity in the body of Christ: for the very diversity of graces, ministries, and works gathers the sons of God into one, because \"all these things work one and the same Spirit\" (1 Cor 12:11).","Unus est ergo Populus Dei electus: unus Dominus, una fides, unum baptisma (Ep 4,5); communis dignitas membrorum ex eorum in Christo regeneratione, communis filiorum gratia, communis ad perfectionem vocatio, una salus, una spes indivisaque caritas. Nulla igitur in Christo et in Ecclesia inaequalitas, spectata stirpe vel natione, condicione sociali vel sexu, quia non est Iudaeus neque Graecus: non est servus neque liber: non est masculus neque femina. Omnes enim vos \"unus\" estis in Christo Iesu (Ga 3,28 gr.; cf. Col 3,11)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p130","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Therefore, just as the laity, by divine condescension, have Christ as a brother, who, although He is Lord of all, came not to be ministered unto but to minister (cf. Mt 20:28), so also they have as brothers those who, placed in the sacred ministry, by the authority of Christ so feed the family of God by teaching, sanctifying, and governing, that the new commandment of charity be fulfilled by all. Wherefore St. Augustine most beautifully says: \"Where I am terrified by what I am to you, I am consoled by what I am with you. To you I am a bishop, with you I am a Christian. The former is the name of an office, the latter of grace; the former is peril, the latter salvation.\"","Si igitur in Ecclesia non omnes eadem via incedunt, omnes tamen ad sanctitatem vocantur et coaequalem sortiti sunt fidem in iustitia Dei (cf. 2P 1,1). Etsi quidam ex voluntate Christi ut doctores, mysteriorum dispensatores et pastores pro aliis constituuntur, vera tamen inter omnes viget aequalitas quoad dignitatem et actionem cunctis fidelibus communem circa aedificationem Corporis Christi. Distinctio enim quam Dominus posuit inter sacros ministros et reliquum Populum Dei, secumfert coniunctionem, cum Pastores et alii fideles inter se communi necessitudine devinciantur; Ecclesiae Pastores, exemplum Domini secuti, sibi invicem aliisque fidelibus ministrent, hi autem alacriter Pastoribus et doctoribus sociam operam praestent. Sic in varietate omnes testimonium perhibent de mirabili unitate in Corpore Christi: ipsa enim diversitas gratiarum, ministrationum et operationum filios Dei in unum colligit, quia haec omnia operatur unus atque idem Spiritus (1Co 12,11)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p131","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Laity, gathered into the People of God and constituted into one body of Christ under one head, whoever they are, are called, as living members, to contribute all their forces, received from the benefit of the Creator and the grace of the Redeemer, to the increase of the Church and her continual sanctification.","Laici igitur sicut ex divina dignatione fratrem habent Christum, qui cum sit Dominus omnium, venit tamen non ministrari sed ministrare (cf. Mt 20,28), ita etiam fratres habent eos, qui in sacro ministerio positi, auctoritate Christi docendo et sanctificando et regendo familiam Dei ita pascunt, ut mandatum novum caritatis ab omnibus impleatur. Quocirca pulcherrime dicit S. Augustinus: Ubi me terret quod vobis sum, ibi me consolatur quod vobiscum sum. Vobis enim sum episcopus, vobiscum sum christianus. Illud est nomen officii, hoc gratiae; illud periculi est, hoc salutis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p132","Chapter IV. on the Laity","The apostolate of the laity is a participation in the saving mission of the Church itself, to which apostolate all are deputed by the Lord Himself through baptism and confirmation. By the sacraments, especially the holy Eucharist, that charity toward God and man, which is the soul of the whole apostolate, is communicated and nourished. Laity are especially called to make the Church present and active in those places and circumstances where she cannot become the salt of the earth except through them. Thus every layman, by the very gifts given to him, is both a witness and a living instrument of the mission of the Church itself according to the measure of the gift of Christ (Eph 4:7).","Laici in Populo Dei congregati et in uno Corpore Christi sub uno capite constituti, quicumque sunt, vocantur, ut tamquam viva membra ad Ecclesiae incrementum eiusque iugem sanctificationem vires suas omnes, beneficio Creatoris et gratia Redemptoris acceptas, conferant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p133","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Besides this apostolate, which pertains to absolutely all Christ's faithful, laity can also be called in various ways to more immediate cooperation with the apostolate of the Hierarchy, after the manner of those men and women who helped Paul the apostle in the Gospel, laboring much in the Lord (cf. Phil 4:3; Rom 16:3 ff.). Moreover, they enjoy aptitude to be assumed by the Hierarchy into certain ecclesiastical offices, to be exercised for a spiritual purpose.","Apostolatus autem laicorum est participatio ipsius salvificae missionis Ecclesiae, ad quem apostolatum omnes ab ipso Domino per baptismum et confirmationem deputantur. Sacramentis autem, praesertim sacra Eucharistia, communicatur et alitur illa caritas erga Deum et homines, quae anima est totius apostolatus. Laici autem speciatim ad hoc vocantur, ut praesentem et actuosam reddant Ecclesiam in eis locis et rerum adiunctis, ubi ipsa nonnisi per eos sal terrae evadere potest. Sic omnis laicus, ex ipsis donis sibi collatis, testis simul et vivum instrumentum missionis ipsius Ecclesiae exsistit secundum mensuram donationis Christi (Ep 4,7)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p134","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Therefore all laity have the splendid duty of working that the divine plan of salvation may reach all men of all times and everywhere on earth more and more. Hence let the way be open to them everywhere, that according to their strength and the necessities of the times they may vigorously participate in the saving work of the Church.","Praeter hunc apostolatum, qui ad omnes omnino christifideles spectat, laici insuper diversis modis ad cooperationem magis immediatam cum apostolatu Hierarchiae vocari possunt, ad modum illorum virorum ac mulierum, qui Paulum apostolum in Evangelio adiuvabant, multum in Domino laborantes (cf. Ph 4,3 Rm 16,3 ss.). Praeterea aptitudine gaudent, ut ad quaedam munera ecclesiastica, ad finem spiritualem exercenda, ab Hierarchia adsumantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p135","Chapter IV. on the Laity","The supreme and eternal Priest, Christ Jesus, since He wishes to continue His testimony and service also through the laity, vivifies them with His Spirit and unceasingly impels them to every good and perfect work.","Omnibus igitur laicis onus praeclarum incumbit adlaborandi, ut divinum salutis propositum ad universos homines omnium temporum et ubique terrarum magis magisque pertingat. Via proinde eisdem undequaque pateat, ut pro suis viribus temporumque necessitatibus opus salutare Ecclesiae naviter et ipsi participent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p136","Chapter IV. on the Laity","For to those whom He intimately joins to His life and mission, He also gives a share in His own priestly office, for the exercise of a spiritual cult, that God may be glorified and men saved. For this reason laity, as dedicated to Christ and anointed by the Holy Spirit, are wonderfully called and instructed that ever more abundant fruits of the Spirit may be produced in them. For all their works, prayers, and apostolic undertakings, conjugal and family life, daily work, relaxation of mind and body, if they are carried out in the Spirit, indeed even the hardships of life if patiently borne, become spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God through Jesus Christ (cf. 1 Pet 2:5), which are most piously offered to the Father in the celebration of the Eucharist, together with the offering of the Lord's body. Thus laity, as holy adorers everywhere acting, consecrate the world itself to God.","Supremus et aeternus Sacerdos Christus Iesus, cum etiam per laicos suum testimonium suumque servitium continuare velit, eos suo Spiritu vivificat indesinenterque impellit ad omne opus bonum et perfectum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p137","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Christ, the great Prophet, who proclaimed the Kingdom of the Father both by the testimony of life and the power of the word, fulfills His prophetic office until the full manifestation of His glory, not only through the Hierarchy, which teaches in His name and power, but also through laity, whom therefore He both constitutes witnesses and supplies with the sense of faith and the grace of the word (cf. Acts 2:17-18; Rev 19:10), that the power of the Gospel may shine in daily, family, and social life. They show themselves sons of the promise if, strong in faith and hope, they redeem the present moment (cf. Eph 5:16; Col 4:5) and by patience await future glory (cf. Rom 8:25). Let them not hide this hope in the interior of their mind, but express it by continual conversion and struggle against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against the spiritual forces of wickedness (Eph 6:12), even through the structures of secular life.","Illis enim, quos vitae et missioni suae intime coniungit, etiam sui muneris sacerdotalis partem tribuit ad cultum spiritualem exercendum, ut glorificetur Deus et salventur homines. Qua de causa laici, utpote Christo dicati et Spiritu Sancto uncti, mirabiliter vocantur et instruuntur, ut uberiores semper fructus Spiritus in ipsis producantur. Omnia enim eorum opera, preces et incepta apostolica, conversatio coniugalis et familiaris, labor quotidianus, animi corporisque relaxatio, si in Spiritu peragantur, immo molestiae vitae si patienter sustineantur, fiunt spirituales hostiae, acceptabiles Deo per Iesum Christum (cf. 1P 2,5), quae in Eucharistiae celebratione, cum dominici Corporis oblatione, Patri piissime offeruntur. Sic et laici, qua adoratores ubique sancte agentes, ipsum mundum Deo consecrant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p138","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Just as the sacraments of the New Law, by which the life and apostolate of the faithful are nourished, prefigure a new heaven and a new earth (cf. Rev 21:1), so laity become powerful heralds of the faith of things to be hoped for (cf. Heb 11:1), if they unhesitatingly join the profession of faith to the life of faith. This evangelization, namely the message of Christ proclaimed both by testimony of life and by word, acquires a certain specific and peculiar efficacy from the fact that it is accomplished in the common conditions of the world.","Christus, Propheta magnus, qui et testimonio vitae et verbi virtute Regnum proclamavit Patris, usque ad plenam manifestationem gloriae suum munus propheticum adimplet, non solum per Hierarchiam, quae nomine et potestate Eius docet, sed etiam per laicos, quos ideo et testes constituit et sensu fidei et gratia verbi instruit (cf. Ac 2,17-18 Ap 19,10), ut virtus Evangelii in vita quotidiana, familiari et sociali eluceat. Ipsi se praebent ut filios repromissionis, si fortes in fide et spe praesens momentum redimunt (cf. Ep 5,16 Col 4,5) et futuram gloriam per patientiam exspectant (cf. Rm 8,25). Hanc autem spem non in animi interioritate abscondant, sed conversione continua et colluctatione adversus mundi rectores tenebrarum harum, contra spiritualia nequitiae (Ep 6,12) etiam per vitae saecularis structuras exprimant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p139","Chapter IV. on the Laity","In this office, that state of life which is sanctified by a special sacrament, namely marital and family life, appears of great value. There the exercise and excellent school of the apostolate of laity is had, where Christian religion pervades the whole institution of life and transforms it more from day to day. There spouses have their own vocation, to be witnesses to each other and to their children of the faith and love of Christ. The Christian family then loudly proclaims both the present virtues of the Kingdom of God and the hope of blessed life. Thus by example and testimony it convicts the world of sin and illuminates those who seek the truth.","Sicut sacramenta Novae Legis, quibus vita et apostolatus fidelium alitur, caelum novum et terram novam (cf. Ap 21,1) praefigurant, ita laici evadunt validi praecones fidei sperandarum rerum (cf. He 11,1), si cum vita ex fide professionem fidei inhaesitanter coniungunt. Haec evangelizatio, nuntium Christi scilicet et testimonio vitae et verbo prolatum, notam quamdam specificam et peculiarem efficacitatem acquirit ex hoc, quod in communibus condicionibus saeculi completur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p140","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Therefore laity, even when occupied with temporal cares, can and ought to exercise a precious action for evangelizing the world. And if some of them, in the absence of sacred ministers or when the same are impeded in governance under persecution, supply certain sacred offices according to their ability; and if many indeed expend all their strength in apostolic work: nevertheless it is necessary that all cooperate in the spread and increase of the Kingdom of Christ in the world. Therefore laity should diligently apply themselves to a deeper knowledge of revealed truth, and urgently ask of God the gift of wisdom.","Quo in munere magni pretii apparet ille status vitae, qui speciali sacramento sanctificatur, scilicet vita matrimonialis et familiaris. Ibi exercitium et schola praeclara apostolatus laicorum habetur, ubi religio christiana totam vitae institutionem pervadit et in dies magis transformat. Ibi coniuges propriam habent vocationem, ut sibi invicem et filiis sint testes fidei et amoris Christi. Familia christiana tum praesentes virtutes Regni Dei tum spem vitae beatae alta voce proclamat. Ita exemplo et testimonio suo arguit mundum de peccato et eos qui veritatem quaerunt illuminat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p141","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Christ, made obedient unto death and on this account exalted by the Father (cf. Phil 2:8-9), entered into the glory of His kingdom. To Him all things are made subject, until He subjects Himself and all created things to the Father, that God may be all in all (cf. 1 Cor 15:27-28). He communicated this power to His disciples, that they also may be constituted in royal liberty and, by self-denial and a holy life, conquer the kingdom of sin in themselves (cf. Rom 6:12), indeed, that serving Christ also in others, by humility and patience they may lead their brothers to the King, to whom to serve is to reign. For the Lord wishes to extend His kingdom also through lay faithful, a kingdom namely of truth and life, a kingdom of holiness and grace, a kingdom of justice, love, and peace; in which kingdom \"creation itself will be delivered from the slavery of corruption into the freedom of the glory of the sons of God\" (Rom 8:21). Truly a great promise, and a great commandment, is given to disciples: \"For all things are yours, and you are Christ's, and Christ is God's\" (1 Cor 3:23).","Proinde laici. etiam quando curis temporalibus occupantur, pretiosam actionem ad evangelizandum mundum exercere possunt et debent. Quodsi quidam eorum, deficientibus sacris ministris, vel iisdem in regimine persecutionis impeditis, quaedam officia sacra pro facultate supplent; et si plures quidem ex eis totas vires suas in opere apostolico impendunt: universos tamen oportet ad dilatationem et incrementum Regni Christi in mundo cooperari. Quapropter laici sollerter in profundiorem cognitionem veritatis revelatae incumbant, et instanter a Deo sapientiae donum impetrent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p142","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Therefore the faithful ought to recognize the intimate nature, value, and ordering of the whole of creation for the praise of God, and by works even secular to help one another to a holier life, that the world may be imbued with the spirit of Christ and more effectively attain its end in justice, charity, and peace. In this universally fulfilling office laity hold the principal place. Therefore let them, with competence in profane disciplines and with activity, inwardly elevated by the grace of Christ, vigorously contribute that goods created according to the ordering of the Creator and the illumination of His Word be cultivated by human labor, technical art, and civil culture for the utility of absolutely all men, and be more fittingly distributed among them, and in their own way lead to universal progress in human and Christian liberty. Thus Christ through the members of His Church will more and more illuminate the whole human society with His salutary light.","Christus, factus oboediens usque ad mortem et propter hoc a Patre exaltatus (cf. Ph 2,8-9), in gloriam regni sui intravit. Cui omnia subiiciuntur, donec Ipse se cunctaque creata Patri subiiciat, ut sit Deus omnia in omnibus (cf. 1Co 15,27-28). Quam potestatem discipulis communicavit, ut et illi in regali libertate constituantur et sui abnegatione vitaque sancta regnum peccati in seipsis devincant (cf. Rm 6,12), immo ut Christo etiam in aliis servientes, fratres suos ad Regem, cui servire regnare est, humilitate et patientia perducant. Dominus enim regnum suum etiam per laicos fideles dilatare cupit, regnum scilicet veritatis et vitae, regnum sanctitatis et gratiae, regnum iustitiae, amoris et pacis; in quo regno ipsa creatura liberabitur a servitute corruptionis in libertatem; gloriae filiorum Dei (cf. Rm 8,21). Magna sane promissio, magnumque mandatum discipulis datur: Omnia enim vestra sunt, vos autem Christi, Christus autem Dei (1Co 3,23)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p143","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Laity moreover, with their forces also joined, should so heal institutions and conditions of the world, if any incite to sin, that all these may be conformed to the norms of justice and rather favor than hinder the exercise of virtues. Thus acting, they will imbue culture and human works with moral value. Thus at the same time the field of the world is better prepared for the seed of the divine word, and the doors of the Church are more widely opened, through which the herald of peace may enter the world.","Fideles igitur totius creaturae intimam naturam, valorem et ordinationem in laudem Dei agnoscere, et per opera etiam saecularia se invicem ad sanctiorem vitam adiuvare debent, ita ut mundus spiritu Christi imbuatur atque in iustitia, caritate et pace finem suum efficacius attingat. In quo officio universaliter adimplendo laici praecipuum locum obtinent. Sua igitur in profanis disciplinis competentia suaque activitate, gratia Christi intrinsecus elevata, valide conferant operam, ut bona creata secundum Creatoris ordinationem Eiusque Verbi illuminationem humano labore, arte technica, civilique cultura ad utilitatem omnium prorsus hominum excolantur, aptiusque inter illos distribuantur, et suo modo ad universalem progressum in humana et christiana libertate conducant. Ita Christus per Ecclesiae membra totam societatem humanam suo salutari lumine magis magisque illuminabit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p144","Chapter IV. on the Laity","By the very economy of salvation, the faithful should learn sedulously to distinguish between the rights and duties which are incumbent on them insofar as they are gathered into the Church, and those which pertain to them as members of human society. They will strive to harmonize the two harmoniously, mindful that in every temporal matter they must be guided by a Christian conscience, since no human activity, even in temporal matters, can be withdrawn from God's rule. But in our time it is especially necessary that this distinction and harmony should shine as clearly as possible in the manner of acting of the faithful, that the mission of the Church may be able to respond more fully to the particular conditions of the modern world. For just as it must be acknowledged that the earthly city, rightly dedicated to secular cares, is ruled by its own principles, so the unhappy doctrine which strives to build society without any regard for religion, and impugns and destroys the religious liberty of citizens, is justly rejected.","Laici praeterea, collatis quoque viribus, instituta et condiciones mundi, si qua mores ad peccatum incitant, ita sanent, ut haec omnia ad iustitiae normas conformentur et virtutum exercitio potius faveant quam obsint. Ita agendo culturam operaque humana valore morali imbuent. Hoc modo simul ager mundi melius pro semine verbi divini paratur, et Ecclesiae latius patent portae, quibus praeconium pacis in mundum introeat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p145","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Laity, like all Christ's faithful, have the right to receive abundantly from the spiritual goods of the Church, especially the aids of the word of God and the sacraments, from the sacred pastors, and to make known to them their needs and desires with that freedom and confidence which befits the sons of God and brothers in Christ. According to the knowledge, competence, and excellence which they possess, they have the faculty, indeed sometimes the duty, of declaring their opinion concerning those things which pertain to the good of the Church. Let this be done, if the case requires, through the institutions established by the Church for this purpose, and always in truth, fortitude, and prudence, with reverence and charity toward those who, by reason of their sacred office, hold the person of Christ.","Propter ipsam oeconomiam salutis, fideles discant sedulo distinguere inter iura et officia quae eis incumbunt, quatenus Ecclesiae aggregantur, et ea quae eis competunt, ut sunt humanae societatis membra. Utraque inter se harmonice consociare satagent, memores se, in quavis re temporali, christiana conscientia duci debere, cum nulla humana activitas, ne in rebus temporalibus quidem, Dei imperio subtrahi possit. Nostro autem tempore maxime oportet ut distinctio haec simul et harmonia quam clarissime in modo agendi fidelium elucescant, ut missio Ecclesiae particularibus mundi hodierni condicionibus plenius respondere valeat. Sicut enim agnoscendum est terrenam civitatem, saecularibus curis iure addictam propriis regi principiis, ita infausta doctrina, quae societatem, nulla habita religionis ratione, exstruere contendit et libertatem religiosam civium impugnat et eruit, merito reiicitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p146","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Laity, like all Christ's faithful, should promptly embrace with Christian obedience those things which the sacred pastors, as representing Christ, establish as teachers and rulers in the Church, following the example of Christ, who by His obedience unto death opened to all men the blessed way of the freedom of the sons of God. Nor should they omit to commend their rulers to God by their prayers, who keep watch as about to render an account for our souls, that they may do this with joy and not with grief (cf. Heb 13:17).","Laici, sicut omnes christifideles, ius habent ex spiritualibus Ecclesiae bonis, verbi Dei praesertim et sacramentorum adiumenta a sacris Pastoribus abundanter accipiendi, hisque necessitates et optata sua ea libertate et fiducia, quae filios Dei et fratres in Christo decet, patefaciant. Pro scientia, competentia et praestantia quibus pollent, facultatem, immo aliquando et omnium habent suam sententiam de iis quae bonum Ecclesiae respiciunt declarandi. Hoc fiat, si casus ferat, per instituta ad hoc ab Ecclesia stabilita, et semper in veracitate, fortitudine et prudentia, cum reverentia et caritate erga illos, qui ratione sacri sui muneris personam Christi gerunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p147","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Sacred pastors, however, should recognize and promote the dignity and responsibility of laity in the Church; let them freely use their prudent counsel, confidently entrust to them all things for the service of the Church, and leave them freedom and space to act, indeed add spirit to them, that they may also undertake works on their own initiative. With fatherly love let them attentively consider in Christ the undertakings, vows, and desires proposed by laity. Moreover, they will reverently acknowledge the just freedom which belongs to all in the earthly city.","Laici, sicut omnes christifideles, illa quae sacri Pastores, utpote Christum repraesentantes, tamquam magistri et rectores in Ecclesia statuunt, christiana oboedientia prompte amplectantur, Christi exemplum secuti, qui, sua oboedientia usque ad mortem, beatam libertatis filiorum Dei viam omnibus hominibus aperuit. Neque omittant precibus suis Praepositos suos Deo commendare, quippe qui pervigilant quasi rationem pro animabus nostris reddituri, ut cum gaudio hoc faciant et non gementes (cf. He 13,17)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p148","Chapter IV. on the Laity","From this familiar intercourse between laity and pastors many good things for the Church are to be expected: for thus in laity the sense of their own responsibility is strengthened, alacrity is fostered, and the forces of laity are more easily associated with the work of the pastors. These, helped by the experience of laity, can judge more distinctly and more aptly both in spiritual and in temporal matters, so that the whole Church, strengthened by all its members, may more effectively fulfill its mission for the life of the world.","Sacri vero Pastores laicorum dignitatem et responsabilitatem in Ecclesia agnoscant et promoveant; libenter eorum prudenti consilio utantur, cum confidentia eis in servitium Ecclesiae omnia committant et eis agendi libertatem et spatium relinquant, immo animum eis addant, ut etiam sua sponte opera aggrediantur. Paterno cum amore coepta, vota et desideria a laicis proposita attente in Christo considerent. Iustam autem libertatem, quae omnibus in civitate terrestri competit, Pastores observanter agnoscent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p149","Chapter IV. on the Laity","Every layman should be before the world a witness of the resurrection and life of the Lord Jesus, and a sign of the living God. All together and each according to his part should nourish the world with spiritual fruits (cf. Gal 5:22), and diffuse in it the spirit with which are animated those poor, meek, and peaceful, whom the Lord proclaimed blessed in the Gospel (cf. Mt 5:3-9). In a word, what the soul is in the body, let Christians be in the world.","Ex hoc familiari commercio inter Laicos et Pastores permulta bona Ecclesiae exspectanda sunt: ita enim in laicis roboratur propriae responsabilitatis sensus, fovetur alacritas, et facilius laicorum vires Pastorum operi associantur. Hi vero, laicorum experientia adiuti, tam in rebus spiritualibus quam in temporalibus, distinctius et aptius iudicare valent, ita ut tota Ecclesia, ab omnibus membris suis roborata, suam pro mundi vita missionem efficacius compleat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p150","Chapter IV. on the Laity","The Church, whose mystery is set forth by the sacred Synod, is believed to be indefectibly holy. For Christ, the Son of God, who with the Father and the Spirit alone is celebrated as holy, loved the Church as His spouse, delivering Himself up for her, that He might sanctify her (cf. Eph 5:25-26), and joined her to Himself as His body and enriched her with the gift of the Holy Spirit, to the glory of God. Therefore in the Church all, whether they belong to the Hierarchy or are fed by it, are called to holiness, according to that of the Apostle: \"For this is the will of God, your sanctification\" (1 Th 4:3; cf. Eph 1:4). This holiness of the Church is constantly manifested and ought to be manifested in the fruits of grace which the Spirit produces in the faithful; it is multiformly expressed among individuals, who in their own order of life tend to the perfection of charity, building up others; it appears in a proper way in the practice of the counsels, which are commonly called evangelical. The practice of these counsels, undertaken by many Christians, whether privately or in a condition or state sanctioned in the Church, bears and ought to bear a splendid testimony and example of the same holiness in the world.","Unusquisque laicus debet esse coram saeculo testis resurrectionis et vitae Domini Iesu atque signum Dei vivi. Omnes insimul et unusquisque pro sua parte mundum fructibus spiritualibus alere debent (cf. Ga 5,22), in eumque spiritum diffundere, quo animantur illi pauperes, mites et pacifici, quos Dominus in Evangelio beatos proclamavit (cf. Mt 5,3-9). Uno verbo, quod anima est in corpore, hoc sint in mundo christiani."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p151","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","The divine Master and Exemplar of all perfection, the Lord Jesus, preached holiness of life, of which He is both author and consummator, to all and each of His disciples of whatever condition: \"Be therefore perfect, as also your heavenly Father is perfect\" (Mt 5:48). For He sent the Holy Spirit into all, who moves them from within, that they may love God from their whole heart, from their whole soul, from their whole mind, and from their whole strength (cf. Mk 12:30), and that they may love one another as Christ loved them (cf. Jn 13:34; 15:12). The followers of Christ, called by God not according to their works but according to His purpose and grace, and justified in the Lord Jesus, have been truly made sons of God and partakers of the divine nature in the baptism of faith, and therefore are really made holy. Hence they must hold and perfect, God giving, the sanctification they have received. They are admonished by the Apostle to live as becomes saints (Eph 5:3), and to put on as the elect of God, holy and beloved, the bowels of mercy, benignity, humility, modesty, patience (Col 3:12), and to have the fruits of the Spirit unto sanctification (cf. Gal 5:22; Rom 6:22). Since, however, in many things we all offend (cf. Jas 3:2), we constantly need the mercy of God and ought to pray daily: \"Forgive us our debts\" (Mt 6:12).","Ecclesia, cuius mysterium a Sacra Synodo proponitur, indefectibiliter sancta creditur. Christus enim, Dei Filius, qui cum Patre et Spiritu solus Sanctus celebratur, Ecclesiam tamquam sponsam suam dilexit, Seipsum tradens pro ea, ut illam sanctificaret (cf. Ep 5,25-26), eamque Sibi ut corpus suum coniunxit atque Spiritus Sancti dono cumulavit, ad gloriam Dei. Ideo in Ecclesia omnes, sive ad Hierarchiam pertinent sive ab ea pascuntur, ad sanctitatem vocantur, iuxta illud Apostoli: Haec est enim voluntas Dei, sanctificatio vestra (1Th 4,3 cf. Ep 1,4). Haec autem Ecclesiae sanctitas in gratiae fructibus quos Spiritus in fidelibus producit, incessanter manifestatur et manifestari debet; multiformiter exprimitur apud singulos, qui in suo vitae ordine ad perfectionem caritatis, aedificantes alios, tendunt; proprio quodam modo apparet in praxi consiliorum, quae evangelica appellari consueverunt. Quae consiliorum praxis, Spiritu Sancto impellente, a multis christianis assumpta, sive privatim sive in conditione vel statu in Ecclesia sancitis, praeclarum in mundo fert, et ferre oportet, eiusdem sanctitatis testimonium et exemplum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p152","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Hence it is clear to all that all Christ's faithful of whatever state or order are called to the fullness of Christian life and the perfection of charity, by which holiness a more human manner of living is also promoted in earthly society. To attain this perfection, let the faithful use the forces received according to the measure of the gift of Christ, that, following His footprints and made conformed to His image, obeying the will of the Father in all things, they may devote themselves with whole spirit to the glory of God and the service of neighbor. Thus the holiness of the People of God will grow into abundant fruits, as is lucidly shown in the history of the Church through the life of so many saints.","Omnis perfectionis divinus Magister et Exemplar, Dominus Iesus, sanctitatem vitae, cuius Ipse et auctor et consummator exstat, omnibus et singulis discipulis suis cuiuscumque conditionis praedicavit: Estote ergo vos perfecti, sicut et Pater vester coelestis perfectus est (Mt 5,48). In omnes enim Spiritum Sanctum misit, qui eos intus moveat, ut Deum diligant ex toto corde, ex tota anima, ex tota mente et ex tota virtute sua (cf. Mc 12,30), et ut invicem se diligant sicut Christus eos dilexit (cf. Jn 13,34 Jn 15,12). Christi asseclae a Deo non secundum opera sua, sed secundum propositum et gratiam Eius vocati atque in Iesu Domino iustificati, in fidei baptismate vere filii Dei et consortes divinae naturae, ideoque reapse sancti effecti sunt. Eos proinde oportet sanctificationem quam acceperunt, Deo dante, vivendo tenere atque perficere. Ab Apostolo monentur, ut vivant sicut decet sanctos (Ep 5,3), et induant sicut electi Dei, sancti et dilecti, viscera misericordiae, benignitatem, humilitatem, modestiam, patientiam (Col 3,12), fructusque Spiritus habeant in sanctificationem (cf. Ga 5,22 Rm 6,22). Cum vero in multis offendimus omnes (cf. Jc 3,2), misericordiae Dei iugiter egemus atque orare quotidie debemus: Et dimitte nobis debita nostra (Mt 6,12)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p153","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","In various types of life and offices, one holiness is cultivated by all who are moved by the Spirit of God, and, obeying the voice of the Father and adoring God the Father in spirit and truth, follow the poor, humble, and cross-bearing Christ, that they may deserve to be partakers of His glory. But each must walk unhesitatingly according to his own gifts and offices by the way of living faith, which excites hope and works through charity.","Cunctis proinde perspicuum est, omnes christifideles cuiuscumque status vel ordinis ad vitae christianae plenitudinem et caritatis perfectionem vocari, qua sanctitate, in societate quoque terrena, humanior vivendi modus promovetur. Ad quam perfectionem adipiscendam fideles vires secundum mensuram donationis Christi acceptas adhibeant, ut Eius vestigia sequentes Eiusque imagini conformes effecti, voluntatem Patris in omnibus obsequentes, gloriae Dei et servitio proximi toto animo sese devoveant. Ita sanctitas Populi Dei in abundantes fructus excrescet, sicut in Ecclesiae historia per tot Sanctorum vitam luculenter commonstratur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p154","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","The pastors of the flock of Christ, first of all, ought to exercise their ministry holily and alacritously, humbly and strongly, after the image of the supreme and eternal Priest, the Shepherd and Bishop of our souls, which ministry, thus fulfilled, will be for them also a most exalted means of sanctification. Chosen to the fullness of the priesthood, they are endowed with sacramental grace, that by praying, sacrificing, and preaching, through every form of episcopal care and service, they may exercise the perfect office of pastoral charity, not fear to lay down their lives for the sheep, and, made a form of the flock (cf. 1 Pet 5:3), may promote the Church to greater holiness day by day also by their example.","In variis vitae generibus et officiis una sanctitas excolitur ab omnibus, qui a Spiritu Dei aguntur, atque voci Patris oboedientes Deumque Patrem in spiritu et veritate adorantes, Christum pauperem, humilem, et crucem baiulantem sequuntur, ut gloriae Eius mereantur esse consortes. Unusquisque vero secundum propria dona et munera per viam fidei vivae, quae spem excitat et per caritatem operatur, incunctanter incedere debet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p155","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Presbyters, in the likeness of the episcopal order, whose spiritual crown they form, by the grace of their office participating through Christ, the eternal and unique Mediator, grow in the love of God and neighbor by the daily exercise of their office, preserve the bond of priestly communion, abound in all spiritual good, and give a living testimony of God to all, emulating those priests who in the course of the ages, often in humble and hidden service, have left a splendid specimen of holiness. Their praise is in the Church of God. Praying and offering sacrifice for their people and the whole People of God by office, recognizing what they do and imitating what they handle, far from being hindered by apostolic cares, perils, and hardships, they ascend by them to higher holiness, nourishing and fostering their action from the abundance of contemplation, for the delight of the whole Church of God. All presbyters, and especially those who by the special title of their ordination are called diocesan priests, should be mindful how much faithful union and generous cooperation with their bishop contributes to their sanctification.","Gregis Christi Pastores imprimis oportet, ut ad imaginem summi et aeterni Sacerdotis, Pastoris et Episcopi animarum nostrarum, sancte et alacriter, humiliter et fortiter exsequantur ministerium suum, quod ita adimpletum, etiam pro eis erit praecelsum sanctificationis medium. Ad plenitudinem sacerdotii electi, sacramentali gratia donantur, ut orando, sacrificando et praedicando, per omnem formam episcopalis curae et servitii, perfectum pastoralis caritatis munus exerceant, animam suam pro ovibus ponere ne timeant et forma facti gregis (cf. 1P 5,3), Ecclesiam etiam exemplo suo ad maiorem in dies sanctitatem promoveant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p156","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Of the ministry and grace of the supreme Priest, those of the lower order of ministers, especially deacons, are sharers in a particular way, who, serving the mysteries of Christ and the Church, ought to keep themselves pure from every vice, and to please God and provide for all good before men (cf. 1 Tim 3:8-10, 12-13). Clerics, who are called by the Lord and set apart for His part, under the vigilance of pastors prepare themselves for the offices of ministers, are bound to conform their minds and hearts to so excellent an election, assiduous in prayer, fervent in love, thinking on whatsoever things are true, just, and of good fame, doing all things for the glory and honor of God. To these are added those laity chosen by God, who, that they may fully devote themselves to apostolic works, are called by the bishop and labor with much fruit in the Lord's field.","Presbyteri in similitudinem ordinis Episcoporum, quorum spiritualem coronam efformant, de eorum muneris gratia participantes per Christum, aeternum et unicum Mediatorem, quotidiano officii sui exercitio in Dei proximique amore crescant, communionis sacerdotalis vinculum servent, in omni bono spirituali abundent atque vivum Dei testimonium omnibus praestent, aemuli eorum sacerdotum, qui saeculorum decursu, in humili saepe et abscondito servitio praeclarum sanctitatis specimen reliquerunt. Quorum laus est in Ecclesia Dei. Pro plebe sua et toto Populo Dei ex officio precantes et sacrificium offerentes, agnoscendo quod agunt et imitando quod tractant, nedum apostolicis curis, periculis et aerumnis impediantur, per ea potius ad altiorem sanctitatem ascendant, ex abundantia contemplationis actionem suam nutriendo et fovendo, in oblectamentum totius Ecclesiae Dei. Omnes presbyteri et speciatim illi qui peculiari suae ordinationis titulo sacerdotes dioecesani vocantur, meminerint quantum ad suam sanctificationem conferant cum suo Episcopo fidelis coniunctio atque generosa cooperatio."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p157","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Christian spouses and parents ought, following their own way, to support one another in grace with faithful love throughout the whole course of life, and imbue their lovingly accepted offspring from God with Christian doctrines and evangelical virtues. For thus they give to all an example of indefatigable and generous love, build up the brotherhood of charity, and are witnesses and cooperators of the fecundity of Mother Church, in sign and participation of that love with which Christ loved His Spouse and delivered Himself up for her. A similar example is given in another way by widows and the unmarried, who themselves also can contribute not a little to holiness and activity in the Church. Those, who are engaged in often hard labors, ought to perfect themselves by human works, to help their fellow citizens, to promote the whole society and creation to a better state, and also to imitate Christ, whose hands were exercised with work, and who always works with the Father for the salvation of all, in active charity, rejoicing in hope, bearing one another's burdens, and by their daily labor itself to ascend to higher, even apostolic, holiness.","Missionis autem et gratiae supremi Sacerdotis peculiari modo participes sunt inferioris quoque ordinis ministri, imprimis Diaconi, qui mysteriis Christi et Ecclesiae servientes, ab omni vitio puros se custodire atque Deo placere et omne bonum coram hominibus providere debent (cf. 1Tm 3,8-10 1Tm 3,12-13). Clerici, qui a Domino vocati et in partem Eius sepositi, sub vigilantia Pastorum ad officia ministrorum se praeparant, mentes et corda sua tam praeclarae electioni conformare tenentur, in oratione assidui, amore ferventes, quaecumque sunt vera, iusta et bonae famae cogitantes, omnia in gloriam et honorem Dei perficientes. Quibus accedunt illi a Deo electi laici, qui, ut plene se dedant apostolicis operibus, ab Episcopo vocantur et in agro Domini cum multo fructu laborant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p158","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Those who are oppressed by poverty, infirmity, sickness, and various hardships, or who suffer persecution for justice's sake, should know that they are specially united to Christ suffering for the salvation of the world, whom the Lord proclaimed blessed in the Gospel, and whom God, the God of all grace, who called us into His eternal glory in Christ Jesus, after having suffered a little, will perfect, confirm, and establish (cf. 1 Pet 5:10).","Coniuges autem parentesque christiani oportet ut propriam viam sequentes, amore fideli, totius vitae decursu se invicem in gratia sustineant, et prolem amanter a Deo acceptam christianis doctrinis et evangelicis virtutibus imbuant. Ita enim exemplum indefessi et generosi amoris omnibus praebent, fraternitatem caritatis aedificant, et foecunditatis Matris Ecclesiae testes et cooperatores exsistunt, in signum et participationem illius dilectionis, qua Christus Sponsam suam dilexit Seque pro ea tradidit. Simile exemplum alio modo praebetur a viduis et innuptis, qui ad sanctitatem et operositatem in Ecclesia, et ipsi haud parum conferre possunt. Ii vero, qui laboribus saepe duris incumbunt, oportet ut humanis operibus seipsos perficiant, concives adiuvent, totamque societatem et creationem ad meliorem statum promoveant, verum etiam ut Christum, cuius manus fabrilibus se exercuerunt et qui semper cum Patre ad salutem omnium operatur, in actuosa caritate imitentur, spe gaudentes, alter alterius onera portantes, atque ipso suo quotidiano labore ad altiorem ascendant sanctitatem etiam apostolicam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p159","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Therefore all Christ's faithful, in their conditions, offices, or circumstances of life, and through all these, will be more and more sanctified day by day, if they receive all things from the hand of the heavenly Father with faith and cooperate with the divine will, manifesting to all in temporal service the charity with which God loved the world.","Specialiter etiam Christo pro salute mundi patienti se uniri sciant ii, qui paupertate, infirmitate, morbo, variisque aerumnis opprimuntur, vel persecutionem propter iustitiam patiuntur, quos Dominus in Evangelio beatos praedicavit et quos Deus... omnis gratiae, qui vocavit nos in aeternam suam gloriam in Christo Iesu, modicum passos, ipse perficiet, confirmabit solidabitque (1P 5,10)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p160","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","God is charity, and he who abides in charity abides in God, and God in him (1 Jn 4:16). But God has diffused His charity in our hearts through the Holy Spirit who is given to us (cf. Rom 5:5); therefore the first and most necessary gift is charity, by which we love God above all things and our neighbor for His sake. So that charity, as a good seed, may grow and fructify in the soul, every faithful must willingly hear the word of God and, with His grace helping, do His will in work, frequently participate in the sacraments, especially the Eucharist, and sacred actions, and constantly apply himself to prayer, self-denial, active fraternal service, and the exercise of all virtues. For charity, as the bond of perfection and the fullness of the law (cf. Col 3:14; Rom 13:10), guides all means of sanctification, informs them, and leads them to the end. Hence the true disciple of Christ is marked by charity both toward God and toward neighbor.","Omnes igitur christifideles in vitae suae conditionibus, officiis vel circumstantiis, et per illa omnia, in dies magis sanctificabuntur, si cuncta e manu Patris coelestis cum fide suscipiunt et voluntati divinae cooperantur, caritatem qua Deus dilexit mundum in ipso temporali servitio omnibus manifestando."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p161","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","Since Jesus, the Son of God, manifested His charity by laying down His soul for us, no one has greater love than he who lays down his soul for Him and for his brothers (cf. 1 Jn 3:16; Jn 15:13). Therefore, to give this greatest testimony of love before all, especially persecutors, some Christians have been called from the first times and will always be called. Martyrdom, therefore, by which the disciple is assimilated to the Master, who freely accepts death for the salvation of the world, and is conformed to Him in the shedding of blood, is esteemed by the Church as an excellent gift and the supreme proof of charity. Although it is given to few, all however must be prepared to confess Christ before men and to follow Him on the way of the cross in the persecutions which never cease from the Church.","Deus caritas est, et qui manet in caritate, in Deo manet, et Deus in eo (1Jn 4,16). Deus autem caritatem suam in cordibus nostris diffudit per Spiritum Sanctum qui datus est nobis (cf. Rm 5,5); ideoque donum primum et maxime necessarium est caritas, qua Deum super omnia et proximum propter Illum diligimus. Ut vero caritas tamquam bonum semen in anima increscat et fructificet, unusquisque fidelis debet verbum Dei libenter audire Eiusque voluntatem, opitulante Eius gratia, opere complere, sacramentis, praesertim Eucharistiae, et sacris actionibus frequenter participare, seseque orationi, sui ipsius abnegationi, fraterno actuoso servitio et omnium virtutum exercitationi constanter applicare. Caritas enim, ut vinculum perfectionis et plenitudo legis (cf. Col 3,14 Rm 13,10), omnia sanctificationis media regit, informat ad finemque perducit. Unde caritate tum in Deum tum in proximum signatur verus Christi discipulus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p162","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","The holiness of the Church is also specially fostered by the multiple counsels which the Lord proposes to His disciples to be observed in the Gospel. Among these stands out the precious gift of divine grace, which is given to some by the Father (cf. Mt 19:11; 1 Cor 7:7), to devote themselves more easily with undivided heart (cf. 1 Cor 7:32-34) to God alone in virginity or celibacy. This perfect continence for the sake of the kingdom of heaven has always been held in special honor by the Church, as a sign and stimulus of charity, and as a peculiar source of spiritual fecundity in the world.","Cum Iesus, Dei Filius, caritatem suam manifestaverit, animam suam pro nobis ponendo, nemo maiorem habet dilectionem, quam qui animam suam pro Eo et fratribus suis ponit (cf. Io. Jn 3,16 Jn 15,13). Ad hoc ergo maximum amoris testimonium reddendum coram omnibus, praesertim persecutoribus, aliqui christiani iam a primo tempore vocati sunt et semper vocabuntur. Martyrium igitur, quo discipulus Magistro pro mundi salute mortem libere accipienti assimilatur, Eique in effusione sanguinis conformatur, ab Ecclesia eximium donum supremaque probatio caritatis aestimatur. Quod si paucis datur, omnes tamen parati sint oportet, Christum coram hominibus confiteri, Eumque inter persecutiones, quae Ecclesiae numquam desunt, in via crucis subsequi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p163","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","The Church also considers the Apostle's admonition, who, provoking the faithful to charity, exhorts them to feel in themselves that which was also in Christ Jesus, who emptied Himself, taking the form of a servant... becoming obedient unto death (Phil 2:7-8), and became poor for our sakes, whereas He was rich (2 Cor 8:9). Since imitation and testimony of this charity and humility of Christ must always be given by disciples, Mother Church rejoices that many men and women are found in her bosom who follow more closely and show more clearly the self-emptying of the Savior, receiving poverty in the freedom of the sons of God and renouncing their own wills: namely those who subject themselves to man for the sake of God in the matter of perfection beyond the measure of the precept, that they may more fully conform themselves to the obedient Christ.","Sanctitas Ecclesiae item speciali modo fovetur multiplicibus consiliis, quae Dominus in Evangelio discipulis suis observanda proponit. Inter quae eminet pretiosum gratiae divinae donum, quod a Patre quibusdam datur (cf. Mt 19,11 1Co 7,7), ut in virginitate vel coelibatu facilius indiviso corde (cf. 1Co 7,32-34) Deo soli se devoveant. Haec perfecta propter Regnum coelorum continentia semper in honore praecipuo ab Ecclesia habita est, tamquam signum et stimulus caritatis, ac quidam peculiaris fons spiritualis foecunditatis in mundo."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p164","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","All Christ's faithful, therefore, are invited and bound to pursue holiness and the perfection of their own state. Let all therefore attend that they direct their affections rightly, lest by the use of worldly things and attachment to riches they be hindered from pursuing perfect charity against the spirit of evangelical poverty, the Apostle admonishing: \"Those who use this world, as if they did not use it: for the figure of this world passes away\" (cf. 1 Cor 7:31).","Ecclesia etiam Apostoli monitionem recogitat, qui fideles ad caritatem provocans, eos exhortatur, ut hoc in se sentiant quod et in Christo Iesu, qui semetipsum exinanivit formam servi accipiens, ... factus oboediens usque ad mortem (Ph 2,7-8) et propter nos egenus factus est, cum esset dives (2Co 8,9). Huius caritatis et humilitatis Christi imitationem et testimonium cum a discipulis semper praeberi necesse sit, gaudet Mater Ecclesia plures in sinu suo inveniri viros ac mulieres, qui exinanitionem Salvatoris pressius sequuntur et clarius demonstrant, paupertatem in filiorum Dei libertate suscipientes et propriis voluntatibus abrenuntiantes: illi scilicet sese homini propter Deum in re perfectionis ultra mensuram praecepti subiiciunt, ut Christo oboedienti sese plenius conforment."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p165","Chapter V. on the Universal Vocation to Holiness in the Church","The evangelical counsels of chastity dedicated to God, poverty, and obedience, as founded on the words and examples of the Lord and commended by the Apostles and the Fathers and doctors and pastors of the Church, are a divine gift, which the Church has received from her Lord and ever preserves by His grace. The authority of the Church, guided by the Holy Spirit, has taken care to interpret them, to moderate their practice, and also to constitute stable forms of living from them. Hence it has come about that, as if in a tree miraculously and multiply branching in the Lord's field from a divinely given germ, various forms of solitary or common life, and various families have grown, which increase resources both for the progress of their members and for the good of the whole body of Christ. For those families confer on their members aids of stability in a firmer manner of living, of approved doctrine for pursuing perfection, of fraternal communion in the militia of Christ, of liberty strengthened by obedience, that they may be able to fulfill and faithfully keep their religious profession securely, and progressing in the way of charity with rejoicing spirit.","Omnes igitur christifideles ad sanctitatem et proprii status perfectionem prosequendam invitantur et tenentur. Attendant igitur omnes, ut affectus suos recte dirigant, ne usu rerum mundanarum et adhaesione ad divitias contra spiritum paupertatis evangelicae a caritate perfecta prosequenda impediantur, monente Apostolo: Qui utuntur hoc mundo, in eo ne sistant: praeterit enim figura huius mundi (cf. 1Co 7,31 gr.) ."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p166","Chapter VI. on Religious","This state, regard being had to the divine and hierarchical constitution of the Church, is not intermediate between the clerical and lay condition, but from both parts certain Christ's faithful are called by God to enjoy a special gift in the life of the Church and, each in his own way, to profit the same saving mission.","Consilia evangelica castitatis Deo dicatae, paupertatis et oboedientiae, utpote in verbis et exemplis Domini fundata et ab Apostolis et Patribus Ecclesiaeque doctoribus et pastoribus commendata, sunt donum divinum, quod Ecclesia a Domino suo accepit et gratia Eius semper conservat. Ipsa autem auctoritas Ecclesiae, duce Spiritu Sancto, ea interpretari, eorum praxim moderari et etiam stabiles inde vivendi formas constituere curavit. Quo factum est ut, quasi in arbore ex germine divinitus dato mirabiliter et multipliciter in agro Domini ramificata, variae formae vitae solitariae vel communis, variaeque familiae creverint, quae tum ad profectum sodalium, tum ad bonum totius Corporis Christi opes augent. Illae enim familiae sodalibus suis adminicula conferunt stabilitatis in modo vivendi firmioris, doctrinae ad perfectionem prosequendam probatae, communionis in militia Christi fraternae, libertatis per oboedientiam roboratae, ita ut suam religiosam professionem secure implere et fideliter custodire valeant, atque in caritatis via spiritu gaudentes progrediantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p167","Chapter VI. on Religious","Through vows or other sacred bonds, assimilated to vows by their own nature, by which the Christian faithful obliges himself to the three aforesaid evangelical counsels, he is totally handed over to God most beloved, so that he is referred to the service of God and His honor by a new and special title. For by baptism he is dead to sin and consecrated to God; but that he may be able to receive a richer fruit of baptismal grace, by the profession of the evangelical counsels in the Church he intends to be freed from impediments which could draw him back from the fervor of charity and the perfection of divine worship, and is more intimately consecrated to divine service. But his consecration will be the more perfect, by how much through firmer and more stable bonds Christ is more represented joined with the Spouse Church by an indissoluble bond.","Status huiusmodi, ratione habita divinae et hierarchicae Ecclesiae constitutionis, non est intermedius inter clericalem et laicalem conditionem, sed ex utraque parte quidam christifideles a Deo vocantur, ut in vita Ecclesiae peculiari dono fruantur et, suo quisque modo, eiusdem missioni salvificae prosint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p168","Chapter VI. on Religious","Since, however, the evangelical counsels join their followers to the Church and its mystery in a special way through the charity to which they lead, the spiritual life of these should be devoted also to the good of the whole Church. Hence arises the duty, according to their strength and according to the form of their own vocation, of laboring, whether by prayer or by active work, to root and strengthen the kingdom of Christ in souls and to spread it to all regions. Hence also the Church protects and fosters the proper character of the various religious institutes.","Per vota autem alia sacra ligamina, votis propria sua ratione assimiIata, quibus christifidelis ad tria praedicta consilia evangelica se obligat, Deo summe dilecto totaliter mancipatur, ita ut ipse ad Dei servitium Eiusque honorem novo et peculiari titulo referatur. Per baptismum quidem mortuus est peccato, et Deo sacratus; ut autem gratiae baptismalis uberiorem fructum percipere queat, consiliorum evangelicorum professione in Ecclesia liberari intendit ab impedimentis, quae ipsum a caritatis fervore et divini cultus perfectione retrahere possent, et divino obsequio intimius consecratur. Tanto autem perfectior erit consecratio, quo per firmiora et stabiliora vincula magis repraesentatur Christus cum sponsa Ecclesia indissolubili vinculo coniunctus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p169","Chapter VI. on Religious","The profession of the evangelical counsels therefore appears as a sign which can and ought effectively to attract all the members of the Church to the duties of the Christian vocation without shirking. For since the People of God here have no abiding city but seek the one to come, the religious state, which more frees its followers from earthly cares, also more manifests to all believers both heavenly goods already present in this age, and testifies to the new and eternal life acquired by the redemption of Christ, and foretells the future resurrection and the glory of the heavenly kingdom. The same state also more closely imitates and perpetually represents in the Church that form of life which the Son of God accepted, entering the world to do the will of the Father, which He proposed to disciples following Him. Finally, it in a special way lays open the elevation of the kingdom of God above all earthly things and its highest necessities; it also shows to all men the surpassing greatness of the power of Christ the King and the infinite power of the Holy Spirit wonderfully working in the Church.","Cum vero evangelica consilia suos asseclas, per caritatem ad quam ducunt, Ecclesiae eiusque mysterio speciali modo coniungant, spiritualis horum vita bono quoque totius Ecclesiae devoveatur oportet. Inde oritur officium pro viribus et secundum formam propriae vocationis, sive oratione, sive actuosa quoque opera, laborandi ad Regnum Christi in animis radicandum et roborandum, illudque ad omnes plagas dilatandum. Unde et Ecclesia propriam indolem variorum Institutorum religiosorum tuetur et fovet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p170","Chapter VI. on Religious","The state, therefore, which is constituted by the profession of the evangelical counsels, although it does not pertain to the hierarchical structure of the Church, nevertheless pertains unshakenly to its life and holiness.","Evangelicorum proinde consiliorum professio tamquam signum apparet, quod omnia Ecclesiae membra ad officia vocationis christianae impigre adimplenda efficaciter attrahere potest ac debet. Cum enim Populus Dei hic manentem civitatem non habeat, sed futuram inquirat, status religiosus, qui suos asseclas a curis terrenis magis liberat, magis etiam tum bona caelestia iam in hoc saeculo praesentia omnibus credentibus manifestat, tum vitam novam et aeternam redemptione Christi acquisitam testificat, tum resurrectionem futuram et gloriam Regni caelestis praenuntiat. Formam quoque vitae, quam Filius Dei accepit, mundum ingressus ut faceret voluntatem Patris, quamque discipulis Ipsum sequentibus proposuit, idem status pressius imitatur atque in Ecclesia perpetuo repraesentat. Regni Dei denique super omnia terrestria elevationem eiusque summas necessitudines peculiari modo patefacit; supereminentem quoque magnitudinem virtutis Christi regnantis atque infinitam Spiritus Sancti potentiam, in Ecclesia mirabiliter operantem, cunctis hominibus demonstrat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p171","Chapter VI. on Religious","Since the office of the ecclesiastical Hierarchy is to feed the People of God and to lead them to most abundant pastures (cf. Ez 34:14), it pertains to it to wisely moderate by its laws the practice of the evangelical counsels, by which the perfection of charity toward God and neighbor is singularly fostered. It also, docilely following the impulses of the Holy Spirit, receives and authentically approves the rules proposed by illustrious men and women, and further arranged, and also, by its watchful and protecting authority, is present to institutes everywhere erected for the building up of the body of Christ, that they may grow and flourish according to the spirit of the founders.","Status ergo, qui professione consiliorum evangelicorum constituitur, licet ad Ecclesiae structuram hierarchicam non spectet, ad eius tamen vitam et sanctitatem inconcusse pertinet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p172","Chapter VI. on Religious","So that the needs of the whole Lord's flock may be better provided for, any institute of perfection and its individual members can be exempted from the jurisdiction of the ordinaries of the place by the Supreme Pontiff, by reason of his primacy over the universal Church, in view of the common utility, and subjected to him alone. Similarly, they can be left or committed to their own patriarchal authorities. The members themselves, in fulfilling their duty toward the Church from the special form of their life, ought to show reverence and obedience to bishops according to the canonical laws, on account of their pastoral authority in the particular churches and on account of the necessary unity and concord in apostolic work.","Cum ecclesiasticae Hierarchiae munus sit Populum Dei pascere et ad pascua uberrima ducere (cf. Ez 34,14), ad ipsam spectat evangelicorum consiliorum praxim, quibus perfectio caritatis erga Deum et proximum singulariter fovetur, legibus suis sapienter moderari. Ipsa etiam, Spiritus Sancti impulsus dociliter sequens, regulas a praeclaris viris et mulieribus propositas recipit et ulterius ordinatas authentice adprobat, necnon Institutis ad aedificationem Corporis Christi passim erectis, ut secundum spiritus fundatorum crescant atque floreant, auctoritate sua invigilante et protegente adest."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p173","Chapter VI. on Religious","The Church, moreover, not only raises religious profession to the dignity of a canonical state by its sanction, but also exhibits it by its liturgical action as a state consecrated to God. For the Church itself, by the authority committed to it by God, receives the vows of the professing, by its public prayer implores for them help and grace from God, commends them to God and imparts to them a spiritual blessing, associating their offering with the eucharistic sacrifice.","Quo autem melius necessitatibus totius dominici gregis provideatur, quodcumque perfectionis Institutum ac sodales singuli a Summo Pontifice, ratione ipsius in universam Ecclesiam primatus, intuitu utilitatis communis, ab Ordinariorum loci iurisdictione eximi et ei soli subiici possunt. Similiter possunt propriis auctoritatibus patriarchalibus relinqui aut committi. Ipsi sodales, in officio erga Ecclesiam ex peculiari suae vitae forma adimplendo, reverentiam et oboedientiam secundum canonicas leges praestare debent Episcopis, ob eorum in Ecclesiis particularibus auctoritatem pastoralem et ob necessariam in labore apostolico unitatem et concordiam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p174","Chapter VI. on Religious","Religious should carefully attend that through them the Church may truly better show Christ day by day, both to the faithful and to the infidels, whether on the mountain contemplating, or to the crowds announcing the kingdom of God, or healing the sick and wounded and converting sinners to good fruit, or blessing children, and doing good to all, always obedient to the will of the Father who sent Him.","Ecclesia autem professionem religiosam non tantum sua sanctione ad status canonici dignitatem erigit, sed eam ut statum Deo consecratum etiam actione sua liturgica exhibet. Ipsa enim Ecclesia, auctoritate sibi a Deo commissa, profitentium vota suscipit, prece sua publica eis auxilia et gratiam a Deo impetrat, eos Deo commendat eisque spiritualem benedictionem impertitur, oblationem eorum sacrificio eucharistico adsocians."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p175","Chapter VI. on Religious","Let all at last have clearly in view that the profession of the evangelical counsels, although it carries with it a renunciation of goods which are undoubtedly to be much esteemed, nevertheless does not stand against the true progress of the human person, but by its nature greatly profits it. For the counsels, voluntarily taken according to anyone's personal vocation, contribute not a little to the purification of the heart and spiritual liberty, constantly excite the fervor of charity, and especially are able more to conform the Christian man to that kind of virginal and poor life which Christ the Lord chose for Himself, and which His Virgin Mother embraced, as is proved by the example of so many holy founders. Nor let anyone think that religious by their consecration are made alien from men or useless in the earthly city. For although they sometimes do not directly assist their contemporaries, yet in a deeper way they have them present in the bowels of Christ and spiritually cooperate with them, that the building up of the earthly city may always be founded in the Lord and directed to Him, lest perhaps those who build it have labored in vain.","Sollicite attendant religiosi, ut per ipsos Ecclesia revera Christum in dies, sive fidelibus sive infidelibus, melius commonstret, vel in monte contemplantem, vel turbis Regnum Dei annuntiantem, vel aegrotos et saucios sanantem ac peccatores ad bonam frugem convertentem, vel pueris benedicentem, et omnibus benefacientem, semper autem voluntati Patris qui Eum misit oboedientem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p176","Chapter VI. on Religious","Therefore, finally, the sacred Synod confirms and praises men and women, Brothers and Sisters, who in monasteries, or in schools and hospitals, or in missions, by constant and humble fidelity in the aforesaid consecration adorn the Spouse of Christ, and render generous and most diverse services to all men.","Omnes tandem perspectum habeant, consiliorum evangelicorum professionem, quamvis renuntiationem secumferat bonorum quae indubie magni aestimanda veniunt, tamen personae humanae vero profectui non obstare, sed natura sua ei summopere prodesse. Consilia enim, secundum cuiusquam personalem vocationem voluntarie suscepta, ad cordis purificationem et spiritualem libertatem non parum conferunt, fervorem caritatis iugiter excitant et praesertim ad genus vitae virginalis ac pauperis, quod sibi elegit Christus Dominus, quodque Mater Eius Virgo amplexa est, hominem christianum magis conformare valent, ut exemplo tot sanctorum fundatorum comprobatur. Nec quisquam aestimet religiosos consecratione sua aut ab hominibus alienos aut inutiles in civitate terrestri fieri. Nam etsi quandoque coaetaneis suis non directe adsistunt, profundiore tamen modo eos in visceribus Christi praesentes habent atque cum eis spiritualiter cooperantur, ut aedificatio terrenae civitatis semper in Domino fundetur ad Ipsumque dirigatur, ne forte in vanum laboraverint qui aedificant eam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p177","Chapter VI. on Religious","Each one, however, who is called to the profession of the counsels should sedulously take care that in the vocation to which he is called by God, he remain and more excel, to the richer holiness of the Church, to the greater glory of the one and undivided Trinity, which in Christ and through Christ is the source and origin of all holiness.","Idcirco denique Sacra Synodus confirmat et laudat viros ac mulieres, Fratres ac Sorores, qui in monasteriis, vel in scholis et nosocomiis, vel in missionibus, constanti et humili fidelitate in praedicta consecratione Sponsam Christi condecorant, omnibusque hominibus generosa ac diversissima servitia praestant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p178","Chapter VI. on Religious","The Church, to which we are all called in Christ Jesus and in which through the grace of God we acquire holiness, will be consummated only in heavenly glory, when the time of the restoration of all things shall come (Acts 3:21), and with the human race the whole world also, which is intimately joined with man and attains its end through him, will be perfectly restored in Christ (cf. Eph 1:10; Col 1:20; 2 Pet 3:10-13).","Unusquisque autem ad professionem consiliorum vocatus sedulo curet, ut in quam vocationem a Deo vocatus est, in ea permaneat atque magis excellat, ad uberiorem Ecclesiae sanctitatem, ad maiorem gloriam unius et indivisae Trinitatis, quae in Christo et per Christum est omnis sanctitatis fons et origo."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p179","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Christ indeed, lifted up from the earth, has drawn all to Himself (cf. Jn 12:32); rising from the dead (cf. Rom 6:9) He sent His life-giving Spirit into His disciples and through Him constituted His body which is the Church as the universal sacrament of salvation; sitting at the right hand of the Father, He continuously works in the world that He may lead men to the Church and more closely join them to Himself through it, and that, nourishing them with His own body and blood, He may make them partakers of His glorious life. Therefore the restoration, which we await promised, has already begun in Christ, is advanced in the mission of the Holy Spirit, and through Him continues in the Church, in which by faith we are also taught about the sense of our temporal life, while we carry out the work entrusted to us in the world by the Father with the hope of future goods and work out our salvation (cf. Phil 2:12).","Ecclesia, ad quam in Christo Iesu vocamur omnes et in qua per gratiam Dei sanctitatem acquirimus, nonnisi in gloria caelesti consummabitur, quando adveniet tempus restitutionis omnium (Ac 3,21) atque cum genere humano universus quoque mundus, qui intime cum homine coniungitur et per eum ad finem suum accedit, perfecte in Christo instaurabitur (cf. Ep 1,10 Col 1,20 2P 3,10-13)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p180","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Already therefore the ends of the ages have come upon us (cf. 1 Cor 10:11) and the renewal of the world is irrevocably established and is in a certain real way anticipated in this age: for the Church already on earth is marked with true though imperfect holiness. Until, however, there be new heavens and a new earth, in which justice dwells (cf. 2 Pet 3:13), the pilgrim Church, in her sacraments and institutions, which pertain to this age, bears the figure of this world which passes away, and she herself dwells among creatures which groan and are in labor until now and await the revelation of the sons of God (cf. Rom 8:19-22).","Christus quidem exaltatus a terra omnes traxit ad seipsum (cf. Jn 12,32 gr.); resurgens ex mortibus (cf. Rm 6,9) Spiritum suum vivificantem in discipulos immisit et per eum Corpus suum quod est Ecclesia ut universale salutis sacramentum constituit; sedens ad dexteram Patris continuo operatur in mundo ut homines ad Ecclesiam perducat arctiusque per eam sibi coniungat ac proprio Corpore et Sanguine illos nutriendo gloriosae vitae suae faciat esse participes. Restitutio ergo quam promissam exspectamus, iam incepit in Christo, provehitur in missione Spiritus Sancti et per Eum pergit in Ecclesia in qua per fidem de sensu quoque vitae nostrae temporalis edocemur, dum opus a Patre nobis in mundo commissum cum spe futurorum bonorum ad finem perducimus et salutem nostram operamur (cf. Ph 2,12)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p181","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Joined therefore to Christ in the Church and sealed by the Holy Spirit who is the pledge of our inheritance (Eph 1:14), we are truly called and are sons of God (cf. 1 Jn 3:1), but not yet have appeared with Christ in glory (cf. Col 3:4), in which we shall be like to God, because we shall see Him as He is (cf. 1 Jn 3:2). Therefore while we are in the body, we are away from the Lord (2 Cor 5:6), and having the first-fruits of the Spirit, we groan within ourselves (cf. Rom 8:23) and desire to be with Christ (cf. Phil 1:23). The same charity urges us that we may live more for Him who died for us and rose again (cf. 2 Cor 5:15). We strive therefore to please the Lord in all things (cf. 2 Cor 5:9), and put on the armor of God, that we may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil and resist in the evil day (cf. Eph 6:11-13). Since, however, we do not know the day nor the hour, the Lord admonishing, we must constantly be watchful, that, having completed the one course of our earthly life (cf. Heb 9:27), we may deserve to enter with Him into the wedding and to be numbered among the blessed (cf. Mt 25:31-46), nor be commanded as evil and lazy servants (cf. Mt 25:26) to depart into eternal fire (cf. Mt 25:41), into the outer darkness where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth (Mt 22:13; cf. Mt 25:30). For before we reign with glorious Christ, we shall all be manifested before the tribunal of Christ, that each may receive according to what he has done in the body, whether good or evil (2 Cor 5:10), and at the end of the world those who have done good shall come forth to the resurrection of life, and those who have done evil to the resurrection of judgment (Jn 5:29; cf. Mt 25:46). Thinking therefore that the sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared to the future glory which shall be revealed in us (Rom 8:18; cf. 2 Tim 2:11-12), strong in faith we await the blessed hope and the advent of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ (Tit 2:13), who will reform the body of our lowliness configured to the body of His glory (Phil 3:21), and who will come to be glorified in His saints and to be admired in all who have believed (2 Th 1:10).","Iam ergo fines saeculorum ad nos pervenerunt (cf. 1Co 10,11) et renovatio mundi irrevocabiliter est constituta atque in hoc saeculo reali quodam modo anticipatur: etenim Ecclesia iam in terris vera sanctitate licet imperfecta insignitur. Donec tamen fuerint novi caeli et nova terra, in quibus iustitia habitat (cf. 2P 3,13), Ecclesia peregrinans, in suis sacramentis et institutionibus, quae ad hoc aevum pertinent, portat figuram huius saeculi quae praeterit et ipsa inter creaturas degit quae ingemiscunt et parturiunt usque adhuc et exspectant revelationem filiorum Dei (cf. Rm 8,19-22)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p182","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Until, therefore, the Lord comes in His majesty and all the angels with Him (cf. Mt 25:31), and death being destroyed, all things are made subject to Him (cf. 1 Cor 15:26-27), some of His disciples pilgrimage on earth, others, this life having been finished, are purified, others are glorified, contemplating clearly God Himself three and one, as He is; all, however, in different degree and mode, communicate in the same charity of God and neighbor, and sing the same hymn of glory to our God. For all who are of Christ, having His Spirit, coalesce into one Church and cohere together in Him (cf. Eph 4:16). Therefore the union of the wayfarers with the brothers who have fallen asleep in the peace of Christ is by no means interrupted, but is, according to the perennial faith of the Church, strengthened by the communication of spiritual goods. For because the heavenly ones are more intimately united with Christ, they more firmly consolidate the whole Church in holiness, and ennoble the cult which she here on earth offers to God, and contribute multiply to its greater building up (cf. 1 Cor 12:12-27). For received into the heavenly homeland and present to the Lord (cf. 2 Cor 5:8), through Him, with Him, and in Him they do not cease to intercede for us before the Father, exhibiting the merits which they have acquired on earth through the one Mediator of God and men, Christ Jesus (cf. 1 Tim 2:5), serving the Lord in all things and fulfilling those things which are lacking of the passions of Christ in their flesh for His body, which is the Church (cf. Col 1:24). Hence our weakness is greatly helped by their fraternal solicitude.","Coniuncti ergo Christo in Ecclesia et signati Spiritu Sancto qui est pignus hereditatis nostrae (Ep 1,14), vere filii Dei nominamur et sumus (cf. 1Jn 3,1), sed nondum apparuimus cum Christo in gloria (cf. Col 3,4), in qua similes Deo erimus, quoniam videbimus Eum sicuti est (cf. 1Jn 3,2). Itaque dum sumus in corpore, peregrinamur a Domino (2Co 5,6) et primitias Spiritus habentes intra nos gemimus (cf. Rm 8,23) et cupimus esse cum Christo (cf. Ph 1,23). Eadem autem caritate urgemur ut magis vivamus Ei, qui pro nobis mortuus est et resurrexit (cf. 2Co 5,15). Contendimus ergo in omnibus placere Domino (cf. 2Co 5,9) et induimus armaturam Dei, ut possimus stare adversus insidias diaboli et resistere in die malo (cf. Ep 6,11-13). Cum vero nesciamus diem neque horam, monente Domino, constanter vigilemus oportet ut, expleto unico terrestris nostrae vitae cursu (cf. He 9,27), cum Ipso ad nuptias intrare et cum benedictis connumerari mereamur (cf. Mt 25,31-46), neque sicut servi mali et pigri (cf. Mt 25,26) iubeamur discedere in ignem aeternum (cf. Mt 25,41), in tenebras exteriores ubi erit fletus et stridor dentium (Mt 22,13 cf. Mt 25,30). Etenim, antequam cum Christo glorioso regnemus, omnes nos manifestabimur ante tribunal Christi, ut referat unusquisque propria corporis, prout gessit sive bonum sive maIum (2Co 5,10) et in fine mundi procedent qui bona fecerunt in resurrectionem vitae, qui vero mala egerunt, in resurrectionem iudicii (Jn 5,29 cf. Mt 25,46). Existimantes proinde quod non sunt condignae passiones huius temporis ad futuram gloriam quae revelabitur in nobis (Rm 8,18 cf. 2Tm 2,11-12), fortes in fide exspectamus beatam spem et adventum gloriae magni Dei et Salvatoris nostri Iesu Christi (Tt 2,13), qui reformabit corpus humilitatis nostrae configuratum corporis claritatis suae (Ph 3,21) et qui veniet glorificari in sanctis suis, et admirabilis fieri in omnibus qui crediderunt (2Th 1,10)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p183","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Acknowledging this communion of the whole mystical body of Jesus Christ, the pilgrim Church from the first times of the Christian religion has cultivated the memory of the dead with great piety, and because it is a holy and wholesome thought to pray for the dead that they may be loosed from their sins (2 Macc 12:46), she has also offered suffrages for them. Moreover, the Church has always believed that the Apostles and martyrs of Christ, who had given the supreme testimony of faith and charity by the shedding of their blood, are more closely joined to us in Christ, has venerated them with the blessed Virgin Mary and the holy angels with special affection, and has piously implored the help of their intercession. To these were soon added others also who more closely imitated the virginity and poverty of Christ, and finally others whom the excellent practice of Christian virtues and divine charisms commended to the pious devotion and imitation of the faithful.","Donec ergo Dominus venerit in maiestate sua et omnes Angeli cum eo (cf. Mt 25,31) et, destructa morte, Illi subiecta fuerint omnia (cf. 1Co 15,26-27), alii e discipulis Eius in terris peregrinantur, alii hac vita functi purificantur, alii vero glorificantur intuentes clare ipsum Deum trinum et unum, sicuti est; omnes tamen, gradu quidem modoque diverso, in eadem Dei et proximi caritate communicamus et eundem hymnum gloriae Deo nostro canimus. Universi enim qui Christi sunt, Spiritum Eius habentes, in unam Ecclesiam coalescunt et invicem cohaerent in Ipso (cf. Ep 4,16). Viatorum igitur unio cum fratribus qui in pace Christi dormierunt, minime intermittitur, immo secundum perennem Ecclesiae fidem, spiritualium bonorum communicatione roboratur. Ex eo enim quod caelites intimius cum Christo uniuntur, totam Ecclesiam in sanctitatem firmius consolidant, cultum, quem ipsa hic ih terris Deo exhibet, nobilitant ac multipliciter ad ampliorem eius aedificationem contribuunt (cf. 1Co 12,12-27). Nam in patriam recepti et praesentes ad Dominum (cf. 2Co 5,8), per Ipsum, cum Ipso et in Ipso non desinunt apud Patrem pro nobis intercedere, exhibentes merita quae per unum Mediatorem Dei et hominum, Christum Iesum (cf. 1Tm 2,5) in terris sunt adepti, Domino in omnibus servientes et adimplentes ea quae desunt passionum Christi in carne sua pro Corpore Eius quod est Ecclesia (cf. Col 1,24). Eorum proinde fraterna sollicitudine infirmitas nostra plurimum iuvatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p184","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","For while we look upon the life of those who have faithfully followed Christ, we are incited in a new way to seek the future City (cf. Heb 13:14; cf. Heb 11:10), and at the same time we are taught the safest way by which, amid the variations of the world, according to the state and condition proper to each, we may be able to attain to perfect union with Christ, that is holiness. In the life of those who, sharing our humanity, are more perfectly transformed into the image of Christ (cf. 2 Cor 3:18), God vividly manifests His presence and face to men. In them He Himself speaks to us, and gives us a sign of His kingdom, to which we are powerfully attracted, having so great a cloud of witnesses placed around us (cf. Heb 12:1) and such a testimony of the truth of the Gospel.","Hanc communionem totius Iesu Christi Mystici Corporis apprime agnoscens, Ecclesia viatorum inde a primaevis christianae religionis temporibus, defunctorum memoriam magna cum pietate excoluit et, quia sancta et salubris est cogitatio pro defunctis exorare ut a peccatis solvantur (Mach.Mach. 12, 46), etiam suffragia pro illis obtulit. Apostolos autem et martyres Christi, qui sui sanguinis effusione supremum fidei et caritatis testimonium dederant, in Christo arctius nobis coniunctos esse Ecclesia semper credidit, eos simul cum Beata Virgine Maria et sanctis Angelis peculiari affectu venerata est, eorumque intercessionis auxilium pie imploravit. Quibus mox adnumerati sunt alii quoque qui Christi virginitatem et paupertatem pressius erant imitati et tandem ceteri quos praeclarum virtutum christianarum exercitium ac divina charismata piae fidelium devotioni et imitationi commendabant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p185","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Nor, however, do we cultivate the memory of the heavenly ones for the title of example only, but rather still more that the union of the whole Church in the Spirit may be strengthened through the exercise of fraternal charity (cf. Eph 4:1-6). For just as Christian communion among the wayfarers brings us nearer to Christ, so the fellowship with the saints joins us to Christ, from whom as from the Fountain and Head all grace and the life of the People of God itself proceeds. Therefore it is eminently fitting that we love these friends of Jesus Christ and co-heirs, also our brothers and excellent benefactors, give due thanks to God for them, suppliantly invoke them, and flee to their prayers, help, and assistance for obtaining benefits from God through His Son Jesus Christ our Lord, who alone is our Redeemer and Savior. For every genuine testimony of love shown by us to the heavenly ones, by its very nature tends and terminates to Christ, who is the crown of all saints, and through Him to God, who is wonderful in His saints and is magnified in them.","Dum enim illorum conspicimus vitam qui Christum fideliter sunt secuti, nova ratione ad futuram Civitatem inquirendam (cf. He 13,14 cf. He 11,10) incitamur simulque tutissimam edocemur viam qua inter mundanas varietates, secundum statum ac condicionem unicuique propriam, ad perfectam cum Christo unionem seu sanctitatem pervenire poterimus. In vita eorum qui, humanitatis nostrae consortes, ad imaginem tamen Christi perfectius transformantur (cf. 2Co 3,18), Deus praesentiam vultumque suum hominibus vivide manifestat. In eis Ipse nos alloquitur, signumque nobis praebet Regni sui, ad quod tantam habentes impositam nubem testium (cf. He 12,1), talemque contestationem veritatis Evangelii, potenter attrahimur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p186","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","Our union with the heavenly Church is in the noblest way actualized when, especially in the sacred Liturgy, in which the power of the Holy Spirit acts upon us through sacramental signs, we celebrate the praise of the divine majesty with social exultation, and when, all redeemed in the blood of Christ from every tribe and tongue and people and nation (cf. Rev 5:9) and gathered into one Church, we magnify God one and three with one song of praise. Therefore, celebrating the eucharistic sacrifice, we are most especially joined to the cult of the heavenly Church, communicating and venerating the memory first of the glorious ever-Virgin Mary, but also of blessed Joseph and the blessed Apostles and Martyrs and of all the saints.","Nec tamen solius exempli titulo caelitum memoriam colimus, sed magis adhuc ut totius Ecclesiae unio in Spiritu roboretur per fraternae caritatis exercitium (cf. Ep 4,1-6). Nam sicut christiana inter viatores communio propinquius nos ad Christum adducit, ita consortium cum Sanctis nos Christo coniungit, a quo tamquam a Fonte et Capite omnis gratia et ipsius Populi Dei vita promanat. Summopere ergo decet ut hos Iesu Christi amicos et coheredes, fratres quoque nostros et benefactores eximios diligamus, debitas pro ipsis Deo rependamus gratias, suppliciter eos invocemus et ob beneficia impetranda a Deo per Filium eius Iesum Christum, Dominum nostrum, qui solus noster Redemptor et Salvator est, ad eorum orationes, opem auxiliumque confugiamus. Omne enim genuinum amoris testimonium caelitibus a nobis exhibitum, suapte natura tendit ac terrninatur ad Christum qui est corona Sanctorum omnium et per Ipsum ad Deum qui est mirabilis in Sanctis suis et in ipsis magnificatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p187","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","This venerable faith of our fathers concerning the vital fellowship with the brothers who are in heavenly glory or are still purified after death, the sacred Synod receives with great piety, and again proposes the decrees of the sacred councils of Nicaea II, Florence, and Trent. At the same time, however, for its pastoral solicitude, it exhorts all to whom it pertains, that if any abuses, excesses, or defects have crept in here or there, they strive to ward or correct them, and restore all things to the fuller praise of Christ and God. Let them therefore teach the faithful that the authentic cult of the saints does not consist so much in the multiplicity of external acts as rather in the intensity of our active love, by which, for our greater good and that of the Church, we seek the example of the saints in their conversation, and fellowship in communion, and help in intercession. On the other hand, let them instruct the faithful that our conversation with the heavenly ones, provided it be conceived in the fuller light of faith, by no means lessens the cult of latria rendered to God the Father through Christ in the Spirit, but rather enriches it more abundantly.","Nobilissima vero ratione unio nostra cum Ecclesia caelesti actuatur, cum, praesertim in sacra Liturgia, in qua virtus Spiritus Sancti per signa sacramentalia super nos agit, divinae maiestatis laudem socia exsultatione concelebramus, et universi, in sanguine Christi ex omni tribu et lingua et populo et natione redempti (cf. Ap 5,9) atque in unam Ecclesiam congregati, uno cantico laudis Deum unum et trinum magnificamus. Eucharisticum ergo sacrificium celebrantes cultui Ecclesiae caelestis vel maxime iungimur communicantes et memoriam venerantes in primis gloriosae semper Virginis Mariae, sed et beati Ioseph et beatorum Apostolorum et Martyrum et omnium Sanctorum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p188","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","For all who are sons of God and constitute one family in Christ (cf. Heb 3:6), while we communicate with one another in mutual charity and in the one praise of the most holy Trinity, we correspond to the intimate vocation of the Church and participate by foretaste in the liturgy of consummated glory. For when Christ shall appear and the glorious resurrection of the dead shall be, the glory of God will illuminate the heavenly City and the Lamb will be its lamp (cf. Rev 21:24). Then the whole Church of the saints in the highest beatitude of charity will adore God and the Lamb who was slain (Rev 5:12), proclaiming with one voice: \"To Him who sits upon the throne, and to the Lamb, benediction and honor and glory and power forever and ever\" (Rev 5:13-14).","Quam venerabilem maiorum nostrorum fidem circa vitale consortium cum fratribus qui in gloria caelesti sunt vel adhuc post mortem purificantur, magna cum pietate haec Sacrosancta Synodus recipit et decreta Sacrorum Conciliorum Nicaeni II, Florentini et Tridentini rursus proponit. Simul autem pro pastorali sua sollicitudine omnes ad quos spectat hortatur, ut si qui abusus, excessus vel defectus hic illicve irrepserint, eos arcere aut corrigere satagant ac omnia ad pleniorem Christi et Dei laudem instaurent. Doceant ergo fideles authenticum Sanctorum cultum non tam in actuum exteriorum multiplicitate quam potius in intensitate amoris nostri actuosi consistere, quo, ad maius nostrum et Ecclesiae bonum, Sanctorum quaerimus et conversatione exemplum et communione consortium, et intercessione subsidium. Ex altera vero parte instruant fideles nostram cum caelitibus conversationem, dummodo haec in pleniore fidei luce concipiatur, nequaquam extenuare latreuticum cultum, Deo Patri per Christum in Spiritu tributum, sed illum e contra impensius ditare."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p189","Chapter VII. on the Eschatological Character of the Pilgrim Church and Its Union","The most benign and wise God, wishing to accomplish the redemption of the world, when the fullness of time came, sent His Son, made of a woman... that we might receive the adoption of sons (Gal 4:4-5). \"Who for us men and for our salvation came down from heaven, and was incarnate by the Holy Spirit of the Virgin Mary.\" This divine mystery of salvation is revealed to us and continued in the Church, which the Lord constituted as His body, and in which the faithful adhering to Christ the Head and communicating with all His saints, ought also to venerate the memory first of the glorious ever-Virgin Mary, the Mother of God and of our Lord Jesus Christ.","Nam omnes qui filii Dei sumus et unam familiam in Christo constituimus (cf. He 3,6), dum in mutua caritate et una sanctissimae Trinitatis laude invicem communicamus, intimae Ecclesiae vocationi correspondemus et consummatae gloriae liturgiam praegustando participamus. Quando enim Christus apparebit et gloriosa mortuorum resurrectio erit, claritas Dei illuminabit caelestem Civitatem et eius lucerna erit Agnus (cf. Ap 21,24). Tunc tota Ecclesia sanctorum in summa caritatis beatitudine adorabit Deum et Agnum qui occisus est (Ap 5,12), una voce proclamans: Sedenti in throno, et Agno: benedictio, et honor, et gloria, et potestas in saecula saeculorum (Ap 5,13-14)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p190","Chapter VIII. on the Blessed Virgin Mary, the Mother of God, in the Mystery of C","For the Virgin Mary, who, the angel announcing, received the word of God in heart and body and brought forth life to the world, is acknowledged and honored as true Mother of God and of the Redeemer. Redeemed in a more sublime way on account of the merits of her Son, and united to Him by a close and indissoluble bond, she is enriched with this highest office and dignity, that she is the Mother of the Son of God, and therefore the beloved daughter of the Father and also the sanctuary of the Holy Spirit, by which gift of excellent grace she far excels all other creatures, heavenly and earthly. At the same time, however, she is found joined with all men to be saved in the stock of Adam, indeed plainly the mother of the members of Christ, because she cooperated by charity that the faithful might be born in the Church, who are members of that Head. Wherefore she is also hailed as the supereminent and altogether singular member of the Church and as its type and most excellent exemplar in faith and charity, and the Catholic Church, taught by the Holy Spirit, follows her as a most beloved mother with filial affection of piety.","Benignissimus et sapientissimus Deus, mundi redemptionem complere volens, ubi venit plenitudo temporis, misit Filium suum, factum ex muliere, ... ut adoptionem filiorum reciperemus (Ga 4,4-5). Qui propter nos homines et propter nostram salutem descendit de caelis, et incarnatus est de Spiritu Sancto ex Maria Virgine. Quod salutis divinum mysterium nobis revelatur et continuatur in Ecclesia, quam Dominus ut corpus suum constituit, et in qua fideles Christo Capiti adhaerentes atque cum omnibus sanctis Eius communicantes, memoriam etiam venerentur oportet in primis gloriosae semper Virginis Mariae, Genetricis Dei et Domini nostri Iesu Christi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p191","Chapter VIII. on the Blessed Virgin Mary, the Mother of God, in the Mystery of C","Therefore the sacred Synod, setting forth the doctrine concerning the Church, in which the Divine Redeemer works salvation, intends sedulously to illustrate both the office of the Blessed Virgin in the mystery of the Incarnate Word and the Mystical Body, and the duties of redeemed men toward the Mother of God, mother of Christ and mother of men, especially the faithful, not having in mind, however, to set forth a complete doctrine concerning Mary, nor to decide questions not yet fully brought to light by the labor of theologians. Therefore, those opinions which are freely proposed in Catholic schools concerning Her, who holds the place after Christ highest and next to us, are preserved in their own right.","Virgo enim Maria, quae Angelo nuntiante Verbum Dei corde et corpore suscepit et Vitam mundo protulit, ut vera Mater Dei ac Redemptoris agnoscitur et honoratur. Intuitu meritorum Filii sui sublimiore modo redempta Eique arcto et indissolubili vinculo unita, hoc summo munere ac dignitate ditatur ut sit Genetrix Dei Filii, ideoque praedilecta filia Patris necnon sacrarium Spiritus Sancti, quo eximiae gratiae dono omnibus aliis creaturis, caelestibus et terrestribus, longe antecellit. Simul autem cum omnibus hominibus salvandis in stirpe Adam invenitur coniuncta, immo plane mater membrorum (Christi), ... quia cooperata est caritate ut fideles in Ecclesia nascerentur, quae illius Capitis membra sunt. Quapropter etiam ut supereminens prorsusque singulare membrum Ecclesiae necnon eius in fide et caritate typus et exemplar spectatissimum salutatur eamque Catholica Ecclesia, a Spiritu Sancto edocta, filialis pietatis affectu tamquam matrem amantissimam prosequitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-2/index.html","p192","Chapter VIII. on the Blessed Virgin Mary, the Mother of God, in the Mystery of C","The sacred Scriptures of the Old and New Testament and the venerable Tradition show the office of the Mother of the Savior in the economy of salvation in a more and more clear light and set it forth as to be seen. The books of the Old Testament describe the history of salvation, by which the coming of Christ into the world is gradually prepared. These early documents, as they are read in the Church and understood under the light of further and fuller revelation, step by step bring more clearly into light the figure of the woman, the Mother of the Redeemer. Under this light, she is already prophetically adumbrated in the promise given to our first parents, fallen into sin, concerning victory over the serpent (cf. Gen 3:15). Similarly, she is the Virgin who shall conceive and bear a Son, whose name shall be called Emmanuel (cf. Is 7:14; cf. Mic 5:2-3; Mt 1:22-23). She excels among the humble and poor of the Lord, who hope for and receive salvation from Him with confidence. With her at length, the exalted Daughter of Zion, after the long expectation of the promise, the times are fulfilled and the new Economy is established, when the Son of God assumed human nature from her, that by the mysteries of His flesh He might free man from sin.","Ideo Sacrosancta Synodus, doctrinam de Ecclesia, in qua divinus Redemptor salutem operatur, exponens, illustrare sedulo intendit tum munus Beatae Virginis in mysterio Incarnati Verbi et Corporis Mystici, tum hominum redemptorum officia erga Deiparam, matrem Christi et matrem hominum, maxime fidelium, quin tamen in animo habeat completam de Maria proponere doctrinam, neque quaestiones labore theologorum nondum ad plenam lucem perductas dirimere. Servantur itaque in suo iure sententiae quae in scholis catholicis libere proponuntur de Illa, quae in Sancta Ecclesia locum occupat post Christum altissimum nobisque maxime propinquum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p193","II. on the Office of the Blessed Virgin in the Economy of Salvation","The Father of mercies willed that the acceptance of the pre-destined mother should precede the incarnation, so that, just as a woman contributed to death, so also a woman should contribute to life. Which is most eminently true of the Mother of Jesus, who poured out upon the world that very Life, renewing all things, and was endowed by God with gifts worthy of so great an office. Hence it is no wonder that among the holy Fathers the custom grew up whereby they called the Mother of God all holy and immune from every stain of sin, as if formed by the Holy Spirit and molded into a new creature. Enriched with the splendors of altogether singular holiness from the first instant of her conception, the Virgin of Nazareth is greeted by the angel announcing, by God's command, as \"full of grace\" (cf. Lk 1:28), and she herself responds to the heavenly message: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it done to me according to thy word\" (Lk 1:38). Thus Mary, daughter of Adam, consenting to the divine word, became the Mother of Jesus, and embracing the saving will of God with full heart, no sin impeding, totally devoted herself as a handmaid of the Lord to the person and work of her Son, under Him and with Him, by the grace of omnipotent God, serving the mystery of redemption. Therefore the holy Fathers rightly think that Mary was not merely passively used by God, but cooperated in human salvation by free faith and obedience. For she, as St. Irenaeus says, \"being obedient, became the cause of salvation for herself and for the whole human race.\" Hence not a few of the ancient Fathers in their preaching gladly assert with him: \"The knot of Eve's disobedience was loosed by the obedience of Mary; what the virgin Eve bound by unbelief, the Virgin Mary loosed by faith\"; and comparing her with Eve, they call Mary the mother of the living, and often affirm: \"Death through Eve, life through Mary.\"","Sacrae Litterae Veteris Novique Testamenti et veneranda Traditio munus Matris Salvatoris in salutis oeconomia modo magis magisque dilucido ostendunt et veluti conspiciendum proponunt. Libri quidem Veteris Testamenti historiam salutis, qua Christi in mundum adventus lento gradu praeparatur, describunt. Quae primaeva documenta, qualiter in Ecclesia leguntur et sub luce ulterioris et plenae revelationis intelliguntur, clarius pedetentim in lucem proferunt figuram mulieris, Matris Redemptoris. Ipsa, sub hac luce, iam prophetice adumbratur in promissione, lapsis in peccatum primis parentibus data, de victoria super serpentem (cf. Gn 3,15). Similiter haec est Virgo quae concipiet et pariet Filium, cuius nomen vocabitur Emmanuel (cf. Is 7,14 cf. Mic. 5, 2-3; Mt 1,22-23). Ipsa praecellit inter humiles ac pauperes Domini, qui salutem cum fiducia ab Eo sperant et accipiunt. Cum Ipsa tandem praecelsa Filia Sion, post diuturnam exspectationem promissionis, complentur tempora et nova instauratur Oeconomia, quando Filius Dei humanam naturam ex ea assumpsit, ut mysteriis carnis suae hominem a peccato liberaret."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p194","II. on the Office of the Blessed Virgin in the Economy of Salvation","This conjunction of the Mother with the Son in the saving work is manifested from the time of the virginal conception of Christ to His death: first, when Mary, rising up with haste to visit Elizabeth, is hailed by her as blessed on account of her faith in the promised salvation and the forerunner exults in his mother's womb (cf. Lk 1:41-45); in the nativity, when the Mother of God joyfully shows to shepherds and Magi her firstborn Son, who did not diminish but consecrated her virginal integrity; when she presented Him to the Lord in the temple, offering the gift of the poor, and heard Simeon foretelling at the same time that the Son would be a sign of contradiction and that the mother's soul would be pierced by a sword, that the thoughts of many hearts might be revealed (cf. Lk 2:34-35). When the child Jesus was lost and sought with sorrow, his parents found him in the temple occupied with the things of His Father, and did not understand the word of the Son. But His mother kept all these things in her heart, pondering them (cf. Lk 2:41-51).","Voluit autem misericordiarum Pater, ut acceptatio praedestinatae matris incarnationem praecederet, ut sic, quemadmodum femina contulit ad mortem, ita etiam femina conferret ad vitam. Quod praecedentissime valet de Matre Iesu, quae ipsam Vitam, omnia renovantem, mundo effudit, et a Deo donis tanto munere dignis praedita est. Unde nil mirum apud Sanctos Patres usum invaluisse quo Deiparam appellarunt totam sanctam et ab omni peccati labe immunem, quasi a Spiritu Sancto plasmatam novamque creaturam formatam. Singularis prorsus sanctitatis splendoribus a primo instante suae conceptionis ditata, Nazarethana Virgo ab Angelo nuntiante, Dei mandato, ut gratia plena salutatur (cf. Lc 1,28), et caelesti nuntio ipsa respondet: Ecce Ancilla Domini, fiat mihi secundum verbum tuum (Lc 1,38). Ita Maria filia Adam, verbo divino consentiens, facta est Mater Iesu, ac salvificam voluntatem Dei, pleno corde et nullo retardata peccato, complectens, semetipsam ut Domini ancillam personae et operi Filii sui totaliter devovit, sub Ipso et cum Ipso, omnipotentis Dei gratia, mysterio redemptionis inserviens. Merito igitur SS.Patres Mariam non mere passive a Deo adhibitam, sed libera fide et oboedientia humanae saluti cooperantem censent. Ipsa enim, ut ait S.Irenaeus, oboediens et sibi et universo generi humano causa facta est salutis, Unde non pauci Patres antiqui in praedicatione sua cum eo libenter asserunt: Hevae inoboedientiae nodum solutionem accepisse per oboedientiam Mariae; quod alligavit virgo Heva per incredulitatem, hoc virginem Mariam solvisse per fidem; et comparatione cum Heva instituta, Mariam matrem viventium appellant, saepiusque affirmant: a mors per Hevam, vita per Mariam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p195","II. on the Office of the Blessed Virgin in the Economy of Salvation","In the public life of Jesus, the Mother appears signally, first at the wedding in Cana of Galilee, when moved with compassion, she by her intercession brought about the beginning of the signs of Jesus the Messiah (cf. Jn 2:1-11). In the course of His preaching, she received the words by which the Son, extolling the Kingdom beyond the reasons and bonds of flesh and blood, proclaimed blessed those who hear and keep the word of God, as she faithfully did (cf. Lk 2:19, 51; cf. Mk 3:35 par.; Lk 11:27-28). Thus the Blessed Virgin also progressed in the pilgrimage of faith, and faithfully sustained her union with her Son even to the cross, where she stood (cf. Jn 19:25), not without divine counsel, vehemently suffering with her Only-begotten and associated herself with maternal heart to His sacrifice, lovingly consenting to the immolation of the victim born of her; and finally by the same Christ Jesus dying on the cross she was given as a mother to the disciple with these words: \"Woman, behold your son\" (cf. Jn 19:26-27).","Haec autem Matris cum Filio in opere salutari coniunctio a tempore virginalis conceptionis Christi ad Eius usque mortem manifestatur; in primis quidem cum Maria, exsurgens cum festinatione ad visitandam Elisabeth, ab ea ob fidem suam in salutem promissam beata salutatur et praecursor in sinu matris exsultavit (cf. Lc 1,41-45); in nativitate vero, cum Deipara Filium suum primogenitum, qui virginalem eius integritatem non minuit sed sacravit, pastoribus et Magis laetabunda ostendit. Cum autem Eum in templo, dono pauperum oblato, Domino praesentavit, audivit Simeonem simul praenuntiantem Filium futurum contradictionis signum et matris animam gladium pertransiturum, ut revelarentur ex multis cordibus cogitationes (cf. Lc 2,34-35). Puerum Iesum deperditum ac cum dolore quaesitum, parentes eius in templo invenerunt in his quae Patris Eius erant occupatum; verbumque Filii non intellexerunt. Mater vero Eius omnia haec in corde suo meditabunda conservabat (cf. Lc 2,41-51)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p196","II. on the Office of the Blessed Virgin in the Economy of Salvation","Since it pleased God not to manifest the sacrament of human salvation solemnly before He would pour out the Spirit promised by Christ, we see the Apostles before the day of Pentecost persevering unanimously in prayer with the women, and Mary the Mother of Jesus, and His brethren (Acts 1:14), Mary also imploring the gift of the Spirit by her prayers, who at the Annunciation had already overshadowed her. Finally, the Immaculate Virgin, preserved immune from all stain of original sin, the course of her earthly life having been completed, was assumed body and soul into heavenly glory, and exalted by the Lord as Queen of the universe, that she might be more fully conformed to her Son, the Lord of lords (cf. Rev 19:16) and conqueror of sin and death.","In vita publica Iesu, Mater Eius signanter apparet, in initio quidem, cum ad nuptias in Cana Galilaeae, misericordia permota, initium signorum Iesu Messiae intercessione sua induxit (cf. Jn 2,1-11). In decursu praedicationis Eius suscepit verba, quibus Filius, Regnum ultra rationes et vincula carnis et sanguinis extollens, audientes et custodientes verbum Dei, sicut ipsa fideliter faciebat (cf. Lc 2,19 Lc 2,51), beatos proclamavit (cf. Mc 3,35 par. Lc 11,27-28. Ita etiam B. Virgo in peregrinatione fidei processit, suamque unionem cum Filio fideliter sustinuit usque ad crucem, ubi non sine divino consilio stetit (cf. Jn 19,25), vehementer cum Unigenito suo condoluit et sacrificio Eius se materno animo sociavit, victimae de se genitae immolationi amanter consentiens; ac demum ab eodem Christo Iesu in cruce moriente uti mater discipulo, hisce verbis data est: Mulier, ecce filius tuus (cf. Jn 19,26-27)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p197","II. on the Office of the Blessed Virgin in the Economy of Salvation","There is one Mediator, according to the words of the Apostle: \"For there is one God, and one Mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave himself a redemption for all\" (1 Tim 2:5-6). But the maternal office of Mary toward men in no way obscures or diminishes this unique mediation of Christ, but rather shows its power. For all the salvific influence of the Blessed Virgin on men does not come from any necessity of the matter, but from the divine good pleasure, and flows from the superabundance of the merits of Christ, rests on His mediation, depends entirely on it, and draws all its power from it; and it in no way impedes the immediate union of believers with Christ but rather fosters it.","Cum vero Deo placuerit humanae salutis sacramentum non ante solemniter manifestare quam promissum a Christo Spiritum effunderet, Apostolos videmus ante diem Pentecostes perseverantes unanimiter in oratione cum mulieribus, et Maria Matre Iesu et fratribus Eius (Ac 1,14), Mariam quoque precibus suis implorantem donum Spiritus, qui in Annuntiatione ipsam iam obumbraverat. Denique Immaculata Virgo, ab omni originalis culpae labe praeservata immunis, expleto terrestris vitae cursu, corpore et anima ad caelestem gloriam assumpta est, ac tamquam universorum Regina a Domino exaltata, ut plenius conformaretur Filio suo, Domino dominantium (cf. Ap 19,16) ac peccati mortisque victori."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p198","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","The Blessed Virgin, predestined from eternity together with the incarnation of the divine Word as the Mother of God, by the design of divine Providence existed on earth as the loving Mother of the divine Redeemer, singularly generous associate, and humble handmaid of the Lord. Conceiving, bearing, nourishing Christ, presenting Him to the Father in the temple, suffering with her Son dying on the cross, she cooperated in a wholly singular way in the work of the Savior, by obedience, faith, hope, and burning charity, to restore supernatural life to souls. On which account she exists as a mother to us in the order of grace.","Unicus est Mediator noster secundum verba Apostoli: Unus enim Deus, unus et Mediator Dei et hominum, homo Christus Iesus, qui dedit redemptionem semetipsum pro omnibus (1Tm 2,5-6). Mariae autem maternum munus erga homines hanc Christi unicam mediationem nullo modo obscurat nec minuit, sed virtutem eius ostendit. Omnis enim salutaris Beatae Virginis influxus in homines non ex aliqua rei necessitate, sed ex beneplacito divino exoritur et ex superabundantia meritorum Christi profluit, Eius mediationi innititur, ab illa omnino dependet, ex eademque totam virtutem haurit; unionem autem immediatam credentium cum Christo nullo modo impedit sed fovet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p199","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","This motherhood of Mary in the economy of grace continues unceasingly, from the consent which she faithfully gave at the Annunciation and which she unhesitatingly sustained under the cross, until the perpetual consummation of all the elect. For assumed into heaven, she did not lay aside this saving office, but by her manifold intercession continues to obtain for us the gifts of eternal salvation. By her motherly charity she cares for the brothers of her Son still pilgrimaging and placed in perils and difficulties, until they be led into the blessed homeland. Therefore the Blessed Virgin is invoked in the Church under the titles of Advocate, Helper, Assister, Mediatrix. Which, however, is so understood that it detracts nothing from the dignity and efficacy of the one Mediator Christ, nor adds anything to it.","Beata Virgo, ab aeterno una cum divini Verbi incarnatione tamquam Mater Dei praedestinata, divinae Providentiae consilio, his in terris exstitit alma divini Redemptoris Mater, singulariter prae aliis generosa socia, et humilis ancilla Domini. Christum concipiens, generans, alens, in templo Patri sistens, Filioque suo in cruce morienti compatiens, operi Salvatoris singulari prorsus modo cooperata est, oboedientia, fide, spe et flagrante caritate, ad vitam animarum supernaturalem restaurandam. Quam ob causam mater nobis in ordine gratiae exstitit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p200","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","For no creature can ever be counted with the incarnate Word and Redeemer; but just as the priesthood of Christ is participated in various ways both by ministers and by the faithful people, and as the one goodness of God is really diffused in diverse ways in creatures, so also the unique mediation of the Redeemer does not exclude, but arouses a varied participated cooperation from creatures from the one source. Such a subordinate office of Mary the Church does not doubt to profess, continually experiences it, and commends it to the heart of the faithful, that, supported by this motherly aid, they may adhere more intimately to the Mediator and Savior.","Haec autem in gratiae oeconomia maternitas Mariae indesinenter perdurat, inde a consensu quem in Annuntiatione fideliter praebuit, quamque sub cruce incunctanter sustinuit, usque ad perpetuam omnium electorum consummationem. In caelis enim assumpta salutiferum hoc munus non deposuit, sed multiplici intercessione sua pergit in aeternae salutis donis nobis conciliandis. Materna sua caritate de fratribus Filii sui adhuc peregrinantibus necnon in periculis et angustiis versantibus curat, donec ad felicem patriam perducantur. Propterea B. Virgo in Ecclesia, titulis Advocatae, Auxiliatricis, Adiutricis, Mediatricis invocatur. Quod tamen ita intelligitur, ut dignitati et efficacitati Christi unius Mediatoris nihil deroget, nihil superaddat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p201","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","The Blessed Virgin, however, by the gift and office of divine motherhood, by which she is united with the Son the Redeemer, and by her singular graces and gifts, is also intimately joined with the Church: the Mother of God is the type of the Church, as St. Ambrose already taught, in the order of faith, charity, and perfect union with Christ. For in the mystery of the Church, which is also justly called mother and virgin, the Blessed Virgin Mary preceded, eminently and singularly presenting the exemplar of both virgin and mother. For, believing and obeying, she brought forth the very Son of the Father on earth, not knowing man, overshadowed by the Holy Spirit, as the new Eve, giving faith not to the ancient serpent but to the messenger of God, no doubt adulterated. She brought forth the Son, whom God placed as the firstborn among many brothers (Rom 8:29), namely the faithful, to whose generation and education she cooperates with motherly love.","Nulla enim creatura cum Verbo incarnato ac Redemptore connumerari umquam potest; sed sicut sacerdotium Christi variis modis tum a ministris tum a fideli populo participatur, et sicut una bonitas Dei in creaturis modis diversis realiter diffunditur, ita etiam unica mediatio Redemptoris non excludit, sed suscitat variam apud creaturas participatam ex unico fonte cooperationem. Tale autem munus subordinatum Mariae Ecclesia profiteri non dubitat, iugiter experitur et fidelium cordi commendat, ut hoc materno fulti praesidio Mediatori ac Salvatori intimius adhaereant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p202","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","Now the Church, contemplating her hidden holiness and imitating her charity, and faithfully fulfilling the will of the Father, by faithfully receiving the word of God also becomes a mother: for by preaching and baptism she brings forth sons, conceived of the Holy Spirit and born of God, to a new and immortal life. And she herself is a virgin, who keeps whole and pure the faith given to the Spouse, and imitating the Mother of her Lord, by the power of the Holy Spirit, keeps intact the whole faith, firm hope, sincere charity.","Beata autem Virgo divinae maternitatis dono et munere, quo cum Filio Redemptore unitur, suisque singularibus gratiis et muneribus, etiam cum Ecclesia intime coniungitur: Deipara est Ecclesiae typus, ut iam docebat S.Ambrosius, in ordine scilicet fidei, caritatis et perfectae cum Christo unionis. In mysterio enim Ecclesiae, quae et ipsa iure mater vocatur et virgo, Beata Virgo Maria praecessit, eminenter et singulariter tum virginis tum matris exemplar praebens. Credens enim et oboediens, ipsum Filium Patris in terris genuit, et quidem viri nescia, Spiritu Sancto obumbrata, tamquam nova Heva, non serpenti antiquo, sed Dei nuntio praestans fidem, nullo dubio adulteratam. Filium autem peperit, quem Deus posuit primogenitum in multis fratribus (Rm 8,29), fidelibus nempe, ad quos gignendos et educandos materno amore cooperatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p203","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","While the Church already attains to perfection in the Blessed Virgin without blemish and wrinkle (cf. Eph 5:27), the faithful still strive, overcoming sin, to grow in holiness; therefore they lift up their eyes to Mary, who shines as an exemplar of virtues for the whole community of the elect. The Church, piously reflecting on her and contemplating her in the light of the Word made man, enters more deeply into the sublime mystery of the incarnation, and more and more conforms herself to her Spouse. For Mary, who intimately entered into the history of salvation, in herself in a certain way unites and echoes the greatest articles of faith, while she is preached and honored, calls believers to her Son and His sacrifice and to the love of the Father. The Church, pursuing the glory of Christ, becomes more like her sublime Type, continually progressing in faith, hope, and charity, and seeking and obeying the divine will in all things. Hence also in her apostolic work the Church rightly looks to her, who gave birth to Christ, thus conceived of the Holy Spirit and born of the Virgin, that through the Church He may be born and grow in the hearts of the faithful also. The Virgin in her life was an example of that motherly affection, by which all cooperating in the apostolic mission of the Church for the regeneration of men ought to be animated.","Iamvero Ecclesia, eius arcanam sanctitatem contemplans et caritatem imitans, voluntatemque Patris fideliter adimplens, per verbum Dei fideliter susceptum et ipsa fit mater: praedicatione enim ac baptismate filios, de Spiritu Sancto conceptos et ex Deo natos, ad vitam novam et immortalem generat. Et ipsa est virgo, quae fidem Sponso datam integre et pure custodit, et imitans Domini sui Matrem, virtute Spiritus Sancti, virginaliter servat integram fidem, solidam spem, sinceram caritatem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p204","III. on the Blessed Virgin and the Church","Mary, exalted by the grace of God after the Son above all angels and men, as the most holy Mother of God, who was present at the mysteries of Christ, is justly honored by the Church with a special cult. And indeed from the most ancient times the Blessed Virgin is venerated under the title of Mother of God, under whose protection the faithful fleeing in all perils and necessities pray. From that time, especially from the Council of Ephesus, the cult of the people of God toward Mary has wonderfully grown in veneration and love, in invocation and imitation, according to her own prophetic words: \"Behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed, for He that is mighty has done great things for me\" (Lk 1:48). This cult, as it has always existed in the Church, is altogether singular, yet it differs essentially from the cult of adoration, which is rendered to the incarnate Word equally to the Father and the Holy Spirit, and most greatly favors it. For the various forms of piety toward the Mother of God, which the Church has approved within the limits of sound and orthodox doctrine, according to the conditions of times and places and the character and genius of the faithful, bring it about that while the Mother is honored, the Son, through whom all things (cf. Col 1:15-16) and in whom it pleased the eternal Father that all fullness should dwell (Col 1:19), is duly known, loved, and glorified, and His commands are kept.","Dum autem Ecclesia in Beatissima Virgine ad perfectionem iam pertingit, qua sine macula et ruga existit (cf. Ep 5,27), christifideles adhuc nituntur, ut devincentes peccatum in sanctitate crescant; ideoque oculos suos ad Mariam attollunt, quae toti electorum communitati tamquam exemplar virtutum praefulget. Ecclesia de Ea pie recogitans Eamque in lumine Verbi hominis facti contemplans, in summum incarnationis mysterium venerabunda penitius intrat, Sponsoque suo magis magisque conformatur. Maria enim, quae, in historiam salutis intime ingressa, maxima fidei placita in se quodammodo unit et reverberat, dum praedicatur et colitur, ad Filium suum Eiusque sacrificium atque ad amorem Patris credentes advocat. Ecclesia vero, gloriam Christi prosequens, praecelso suo Typo similior efficitur, continuo progrediens in fide, spe et caritate, ac divinam voluntatem in omnibus quaerens et obsequens. Unde etiam in opere suo apostolico Ecclesia ad Eam merito respicit, quae genuit Christum, ideo de Spiritu Sancto conceptum et de Virgine natum, ut per Ecclesiam in cordibus quoque fidelium nascatur et crescat. Quae Virgo in sua vita exemplum exstitit materni illius affectus, quo cuncti in missione apostolica Ecclesiae cooperantes ad regenerandos homines animentur oportet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p205","IV. on the Cult of the Blessed Virgin in the Church","This sacred Synod knowingly teaches this catholic doctrine, and at the same time admonishes all the sons of the Church that they generously foster the cult, especially the liturgical, toward the Blessed Virgin, hold in great esteem the practices and exercises of piety toward Her commended by the Magisterium in the course of the centuries, and religiously keep those things which have been decreed in past times concerning the cult of images of Christ, the Blessed Virgin, and the saints. Moreover, it earnestly exhorts theologians and preachers of the divine word that they sedulously abstain equally from all false exaggeration as from too narrow a mind in considering the singular dignity of the Mother of God. Cultivating the study of sacred Scripture, the holy Fathers and Doctors and the liturgies of the Church under the guidance of the Magisterium, let them rightly illustrate the offices and privileges of the Blessed Virgin, which always look to Christ, the source of all truth, holiness, and piety. Let them sedulously avoid whatever could lead separated brothers or any others into error concerning the true doctrine of the Church. Let the faithful, moreover, be mindful that true devotion does not consist in sterile and transitory affection, nor in a certain vain credulity, but proceeds from true faith, by which we are led to acknowledge the excellence of the Mother of God, and are excited to filial love toward our mother and to the imitation of her virtues.","Maria, per gratiam Dei post Filium prae omnibus angelis et hominibus exaltata, utpote sanctissima Dei Mater, quae mysteriis Christi interfuit, speciali cultu ab Ecclesia merito honoratur. Et sane ab antiquissimis temporibus Beata Virgo sub titulo Deiparae colitur, sub cuius praesidium fideles in cunctis periculis et necessitatibus suis deprecantes confugiunt. Inde praesertim ab Ephesina Synodo cultus populi Dei erga Mariam mirabiliter crevit in veneratione et dilectione, in invocatione et imitatione, secundum ipsius verba prophetica: Beatam me dicent omnes generationes, quia fecit mihi magna qui potens est (Lc 1,48). Qui cultus, prout in Ecclesia semper exstitit, singularis omnino quamquam est, essentialiter differt a cultu adorationis, qui Verbo incarnato aeque ac Patri et Spiritui Sancto exhibetur, eidemque potissimum favet. Variae enim formae pietatis erga Dei Genitricem, quas Ecclesia intra limites sanae et orthodoxae doctrinae, pro temporum et locorum conditionibus et pro indole ingenioque fidelium approbavit, id efficiunt ut, dum Mater honoratur, Filius, propter quem omnia (cf. Col 1,15-16) et in quo aeterno Patri complacuit omnem plenitudinem inhabitare (Col 1,19), rite noscatur, ametur, glorificetur, Eiusque mandata serventur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p206","IV. on the Cult of the Blessed Virgin in the Church","In the meantime, the Mother of Jesus, just as she is already glorified in heaven in body and soul, the image and beginning of the Church to be consummated in the future age, so on earth, until the day of the Lord shall come (cf. 2 Pet 3:10), she shines as a sign of sure hope and solace for the pilgrim People of God.","Hanc catholicam doctrinam Sacrosancta Synodus consulto docet, simulque omnes Ecclesiae filios admonet, ut cultum, praesertim liturgicum, erga Beatam Virginem generose foveant, praxes autem et exercitia pietatis erga Eam saeculorum cursu a Magisterio commendata magni faciant et ea quae anteactis temporibus de cultu imaginum Christi, Beatae Virginis et Sanctorum decreta fuere, religiose servent. Theologos autem verbique divini praecones enixe exhortatur, ut aeque ab omni falsa superlatione, quemadmodum et a nimia mentis angustia, in singulari Deiparae dignitate consideranda sedulo abstineant. Studium Sacrae Scripturae, Sanctorum Patrum et Doctorum Ecclesiaeque liturgiarum sub ductu Magisterii excolentes, recte illustrent munera et privilegia Beatae Virginis, quae semper Christum spectant, totius veritatis, sanctitatis et pietatis originem. Sedulo arceant quaecumque sive in dictis sive in factis fratres seiunctos vel alios quoscumque in errorem circa veram Ecclesiae doctrinam inducere possent. Meminerint porro fideles veram devotionem neque in sterili et transitorio affectu, neque in vana quadam credulitate consistere, sed a vera fide procedere, qua ad Dei Genetricis excellentiam agnoscendam adducimur, et ad filialem erga Matrem nostram amorem eiusque virtutum imitationem excitamur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p207","V. Mary, a Sign of Sure Hope and Solace for the Pilgrim People of God","It brings great joy and solace to this sacred Synod that also among separated brothers there are not wanting those who bring due honor to the Mother of the Lord and Savior, especially among the Easterns, who with fervent impulse and devoted mind run to the cult of the Mother of God ever Virgin. Let all Christ's faithful pour forth urgent supplications to the Mother of God and Mother of men, that She, who assisted by her prayers the first-fruits of the Church, now also exalted in heaven above all the blessed and angels, in the Communion of all Saints may intercede with her Son, until all families of peoples, whether they are adorned with the Christian name or yet know not their Savior, may be happily gathered with peace and concord into one People of God, to the glory of the most holy and undivided Trinity.","Interim autem Mater Iesu, quemadmodum in caelis corpore et anima iam glorificata, imago et initium est Ecclesiae in futuro saeculo consummandae, ita his in terris quoadusque advenerit dies Domini (cf. 2P 3,10), tamquam signum certae spei et solatii peregrinanti Populo Dei praelucet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p208","V. Mary, a Sign of Sure Hope and Solace for the Pilgrim People of God","SACROSANCTUM CONCILIUM, since it proposes to increase the Christian life among the faithful day by day, to better accommodate those institutions which are subject to change to the needs of our age, to foster whatever can contribute to the union of all who believe in Christ, and to strengthen whatever can help to call all into the bosom of the Church, judges it to pertain to itself to take care with special solicitude to also restore and foster the Liturgy.","Sacrosanctae huic Synodo magnum affert gaudium et solatium, etiam inter fratres seiunctos non deesse, qui Matri Domini ac Salvatoris debitum afferunt honorem, speciatim apud Orientales, qui ad cultum Deiparae semper Virginis fervido impulsu ac devoto animo concurrunt. Universi christifideles supplicationes instantes ad Matrem Dei et Matrem hominum effundant, ut Ipsa, quae primitiis Ecclesiae precibus suis adstitit, nunc quoque in caelo super omnes beatos et angelos exaltata, in omnium Sanctorum Communione apud Filium suum intercedat, donec cunctae familiae populorum, sive quae christiano nomine decorantur, sive quae Salvatorem suum adhuc ignorant, cum pace et concordia in unum Populum Dei feliciter congregentur, ad gloriam Sanctissimae et individuae Trinitatis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p209","Sacrosanctum Concilium","For the Liturgy, through which, especially in the divine Sacrifice of the Eucharist, the work of our Redemption is exercised, most greatly contributes to the faithful living expressing and manifesting to others the mystery of Christ and the genuine nature of the true Church, whose proper character is to be human and at the same time divine, visible and endowed with invisible, fervent in action and given to contemplation, present in the world and yet pilgrim, and so in her that what is human is ordered to the divine and subordinated to it, what is visible to the invisible, action to contemplation, and what is present to the future city which we seek. Hence, since the Liturgy daily builds those who are within into a holy temple in the Lord, into a dwelling of God in the Spirit, unto the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ, it at the same time wonderfully strengthens their powers to preach Christ, and thus shows the Church to those who are outside as a sign lifted up among the nations, under which the scattered sons of God may be gathered into one, until there be one fold and one shepherd.","SACROSANCTUM CONCILIUM, cum sibi proponat vitam christianam inter fideles in dies augere; eas institutiones quae mutationibus obnoxiae sunt, ad nostrae aetatis necessitates melius accommodare; quidquid ad unionem omnium in Christum credentium conferre potest, fovere; et quidquid ad omnes in sinum Ecclesiae vocandos conducit, roborare; suum esse arbitratur peculiari ratione etiam instaurandam atque fovendam Liturgiam curare."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p210","Sacrosanctum Concilium","Therefore the sacred Synod, concerning the fostering and restoring of the Liturgy, judges that the following principles are to be called to mind and practical norms established. Among these principles and norms, some are held which can and ought to be applied both to the Roman Rite and to all other Rites, although the practical norms which follow are to be understood as pertaining only to the Roman Rite, unless it concerns those things which from the nature of the matter also affect other Rites.","Liturgia enim, per quam, maxime in divino Eucharistiae Sacrificio, opus nostrae Redemptionis exercetur, summe eo confert ut fideles vivendo exprimant et aliis manifestent mysterium Christi et genuinam verae Ecclesiae naturam, cuius proprium est esse humanam simul ac divinam, visibilem invisibilibus praeditam, actione ferventem et contemplationi vacantem, in mundo praesentem et tamen peregrinam; et ita quidem ut in ea quod humanum est ordinetur ad divinum eique subordinetur, quod visibile ad invisibile, quod actionis ad contemplationem, et quod praesens ad futuram civitatem quam inquirimus. Unde cum Liturgia eos qui intus sunt cotidie aedificet in templum sanctum in Domino, in habitaculum Dei in Spiritu, usque ad mensuram aetatis plenitudinis Christi, miro modo simul vires eorum ad praedicandum Christum roborat, et sic Ecclesiam iis qui foris sunt ostendit ut signum levatum in nationes, sub quo filii Dei dispersi congregentur in unum quousque unum ovile fiat et unus pastor."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p211","Sacrosanctum Concilium","Finally, faithfully obeying tradition, the sacred Synod declares that Holy Mother Church holds all legitimately recognized Rites in equal right and honor, and wishes them to be preserved in the future and fostered in every way, and desires that, where necessary, they be carefully and wholly revised according to the mind of sound tradition and be endowed with new vigor according to present circumstances and needs.","Quare Sacrosanctum Concilium, de fovenda atque instauranda Liturgia quae sequuntur principia censet in mentem revocanda et practicas normas statuendas esse. Inter haec principia et normas nonnulla habentur quae tum ad Ritum romanum tum ad omnes alios Ritus applicari possunt ac debent, licet normae practicae quae sequuntur solum Ritum romanum spectare intellegendae sint, nisi agatur de iis quae ex ipsa rei natura alios quoque Ritus afficiant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p212","Sacrosanctum Concilium","God, who wills all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth (1 Tim 2:4), spoke of old in many and various ways to the fathers by the prophets (Heb 1:1), when the fullness of time came, sent His Son, the Word made flesh, anointed with the Holy Spirit, to preach the Gospel to the poor, to heal the contrite of heart, a physician bodily and spiritual, the Mediator of God and men. For His humanity, in the unity of the Person of the Word, was the instrument of our salvation. Therefore in Christ the perfect placation of our reconciliation proceeded, and the fullness of divine worship was given to us.","Traditioni denique fideliter obsequens, Sacrosanctum Concilium declarat Sanctam Matrem Ecclesiam omnes Ritus legitime agnitos aequo iure atque honore habere, eosque in posterum servari et omnimode foveri velle, atque optat ut, ubi opus sit, caute ex integro ad mentem sanae traditionis recognoscantur et novo vigore, pro hodiernis adiunctis et necessitatibus, donentur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p213","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","This work of human redemption and perfect glorification of God, to which the divine marvels in the people of the Old Testament preluded, Christ the Lord accomplished especially through the paschal mystery of His blessed Passion, Resurrection from the dead, and glorious Ascension, by which, dying He destroyed our death, and rising He repaired life. For from the side of Christ sleeping on the cross arose the wonderful sacrament of the whole Church.","Deus, qui omnes homines vult salvos fieri et ad agnitionem veritatis venire (l Tim. 2, 4), multifariam multisque modis olim loquens patribus in prophetis (He 1,1), ubi venit plenitudo temporis, misit Filium suum, Verbum carnem factum, Spiritu Sancto unctum, ad evangelizandum pauperibus, ad sanandos contritos corde, medicum carnalem et spiritualem, Mediatorem Dei et hominum.Ipsius namque humanitas, in unitate personae Verbi, fuit instrumentum nostrae salutis. Quare in Christo nostrae reconciliationis processit perfecta placatio, et divini cultus nobis est indita plenitudo."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p214","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","Therefore, just as Christ was sent by the Father, so He also sent the Apostles, filled with the Holy Spirit, not only that, preaching the Gospel to every creature, they should announce that the Son of God by His death and resurrection had freed us from the power of Satan and death and transferred us into the kingdom of the Father, but also that they should exercise through sacrifice and sacraments, around which the whole liturgical life turns, the work of salvation which they announced. Thus through baptism men are inserted into the paschal mystery of Christ: comortified, consepulted, conresuscitated; they receive the spirit of adoption of sons, in which they cry: Abba, Father (Rom 8:15), and thus become true adorers, whom the Father seeks. Likewise, as often as they eat the Lord's supper, they announce the death of the Lord until He comes.","Hoc autem humanae Redemptionis et perfectae Dei glorificationis opus, cui divina magnalia in populo Veteris Testamenti praeluserant, adimplevit Christus Dominus, praecipue per suae beatae Passionis, ab inferis Resurrectionis et gloriosae Ascensionis paschale mysterium, quo mortem nostram moriendo destruxit, et vitam resurgendo reparavit. Nam de latere Christi in cruce dormientis ortum est totius Ecclesiae mirabile sacramentum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p215","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","Therefore, on the day of Pentecost itself, when the Church appeared to the world, those who received the word of Peter were baptized. \"And they were persevering in the doctrine of the Apostles and in the communication of the breaking of bread and in prayers... praising God and having favor with all the people\" (Acts 2:41-42, 47). From then on the Church never ceased to come together to celebrate the paschal mystery: reading those things which were concerning Him in all the Scriptures (Lk 24:27), celebrating the Eucharist in which the victory and triumph of His death are represented, and at the same time giving thanks to God for His unspeakable gift (2 Cor 9:15) in Christ Jesus, to the praise of His glory (Eph 1:12), through the power of the Holy Spirit.","Ideoque,sicut Christus missus est a Patre, ita et ipse Apostolos, repletos Spiritu Sancto, misit, non solum ut, praedicantes Evangelium omni creaturae, annuntiarent Filium Dei morte sua et resurrectione nos a potestate satanae et a morte liberasse et in regnum Patris transtulisse, sed etiam ut, quod annuntiabant, opus salutis per Sacrificium et Sacramenta, circa quae tota vita liturgica vertit, exercerent. Sic per Baptismum homines paschali Christi mysterio inseruntur: commortui, consepulti, conresuscitati; spiritum accipiunt adoptionis filiorum, in quo clamamus: Abba, Pater (Rm 8,15), et ita fiunt veri adoratores, quos Pater quaerit. Similiter quotiescumque dominicam cenam manducant, mortem Domini annuntiant donec veniat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p216","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","To accomplish so great a work, Christ is always present to His Church, especially in liturgical actions. He is present in the Sacrifice of the Mass, both in the person of the minister, the same now offering through the ministry of priests who then offered Himself on the cross, and especially under the eucharistic species. He is present by His power in the sacraments, so that when anyone baptizes, Christ Himself baptizes. He is present in His word, since He Himself speaks when the sacred Scriptures are read in the Church. He is present finally when the Church prays and sings psalms, He who promised: \"Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them\" (Mt 18:20).","Idcirco, ipso die Pentecostes, qua Ecclesia mundo apparuit, qui receperunt sermonem Petri baptizati sunt. Et erant perseverantes in doctrina Apostolorum et communicatione fractionis panis et orationibus... collaudantes Deum et habentes gratiam ad omnem plebem (Ac 2,41-42,47). Numquam exinde omisit Ecclesia quin in unum conveniret ad paschale mysterium celebrandum: legendo ea in omnibus Scripturis quae de ipso erant (Lc 24,27), Eucharistiam celebrando in qua mortis eius victoria et triumphus repraesentantur,19 et simul gratias agendo Deo super inenarrabili dono (2Co 9,15) in Christo Iesu, in laudem gloriae eius (Ep 1,12), per virtutem Spiritus Sancti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p217","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","Indeed, in so great a work, in which God is perfectly glorified and men are sanctified, Christ always associates with Himself His most beloved Spouse, the Church, who calls upon her Lord and through Him renders worship to the eternal Father. Therefore the Liturgy is justly held as the exercise of the priestly office of Jesus Christ, in which through sensible signs the sanctification of man is signified and brought about in a way proper to each, and the whole public worship is exercised by the mystical body of Jesus Christ, namely by the Head and its members. Hence every liturgical celebration, as the work of Christ the Priest and of His body, which is the Church, is a sacred action pre-eminently, whose efficacy no other action of the Church equals by the same title and in the same degree.","Ad tantum vero opus perficiendum, Christus Ecclesiae suae semper adest, praesertim in actionibus liturgicis. Praesens adest in Missae Sacrificio cum in ministri persona, idem nunc offerens sacerdotum ministerio, qui seipsum tunc in cruce obtulit, tum maxime sub speciebus eucharisticis. Praesens adest virtute sua in Sacramentis, ita ut cum aliquis baptizat, Christus ipse baptizet. Praesens adest in verbo suo, siquidem ipse loquitur dum sacrae Scripturae in Ecclesia leguntur. Praesens adest denique dum supplicat et psallit Ecclesia, ipse qui promisit: Ubi sunt duo vel tres congregati in nomine meo, ibi sum in medio eorum (Mt 18,20)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p218","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","In the earthly liturgy we participate by foretaste in that heavenly liturgy which is celebrated in the holy city of Jerusalem, toward which we pilgrim as strangers, where Christ is sitting at the right hand of God, a minister of the holy and the true tabernacle; we sing the hymn of glory to the Lord with all the host of the heavenly army; venerating the memory of the saints, we hope for some part and fellowship with them; we await the Savior, our Lord Jesus Christ, until He himself shall appear our life, and we shall appear with him in glory.","Reapse tanto in opere, quo Deus perfecte glorificatur et homines sanctificantur, Christus Ecclesiam, sponsam suam dilectissimam, sibi semper consociat, quae Dominum suum invocat et per ipsum Aeterno Patri cultum tribuit. Merito igitur Liturgia habetur veluti Iesu Christi sacerdotalis muneris exercitatio, in qua per signa sensibilia significatur et modo singulis proprio efficitur sanctificatio hominis, et a mystico Iesu Christi Corpore, Capite nempe eiusque membris, integer cultus publicus exercetur. Proinde omnis liturgica celebratio, utpote opus Christi sacerdotis eiusque Corporis, quod est Ecclesia, est actio sacra praecellenter, cuius efficacitatem eodem titulo eodemque gradu nulla alia actio Ecclesiae adaequat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p219","Chapter I. on General Principles for Restoring and Fostering the Sacred Liturgy","The sacred Liturgy does not exhaust the whole action of the Church; for before men can come to the Liturgy, they must be called to faith and conversion: \"How shall they call on him in whom they have not believed? Or how shall they believe him whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear without a preacher? And how shall they preach unless they be sent?\" (Rom 10:14-15). Therefore the Church announces the herald of salvation to unbelievers, that all men may know the only true God and Jesus Christ whom He sent, and may be converted from their ways, doing penance. To believers, however, she must always preach faith and penance, she must also prepare them for the sacraments, teach them to observe all things whatsoever Christ commanded, and allure them to all works of charity, piety, and apostolate, by which works it is made manifest that Christ's faithful are not indeed of this world, but yet are the light of the world and glorify the Father before men.","In terrena Liturgia caelestem illam praegustando participamus, quae in sancta civitate Ierusalem, ad quam peregrini tendimus, celebratur, ubi Christus est in dextera Dei sedens, sanctorum minister et tabernaculi veri; cum omni militia caelestis exercitus hymnum gloriae Domino canimus; memoriam Sanctorum venerantes partem aliquam et societatem cum iis speramus; Salvatorem expectamus Dominum nostrum Iesum Christum, donec ipse apparebit vita nostra, et nos apparebimus cum ipso in gloria."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p220","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Yet the Liturgy is the summit toward which the action of the Church tends and at the same time the source from which all her virtue flows. For apostolic labors are ordered to this, that all, made sons of God by faith and baptism, may come together, praise God in the midst of the Church, participate in the Sacrifice, and eat the Lord's supper. In turn, the Liturgy itself impels the faithful, filled with the paschal sacraments, to become one in piety; it prays that they may hold by living what they have perceived by faith; the renewal of the Lord's covenant with men in the Eucharist draws and kindles the faithful into urgent charity of Christ. From the Liturgy, therefore, especially from the Eucharist, as from a fountain, grace is poured forth upon us, and the sanctification of men and the glorification of God in Christ, toward which, as toward an end, all other works of the Church strive, is obtained with the greatest efficacy.","Sacra Liturgia non explet totam actionem Ecclesiae; nam antequam homines ad Liturgiam accedere possint, necesse est ut ad fidem et conversionem vocentur: Quomodo invocabunt in quem non crediderunt? Aut quomodo credent ei quem non audierunt? Quomodo autem audient sine praedicante? Quomodo vero praedicabunt nisi mittantur? (Rm 10,14-15). Quare Ecclesia non credentibus praeconium salutis annuntiat, ut omnes homines solum Deum verum et quem misit Iesum Christum cognoscant et a viis suis convertantur, paenitentiam agentes. Credentibus vero semper fidem et paenitentiam praedicare debet, eos praeterea debet ad Sacramenta disponere, docere servare omnia quaecumque mandavit Christus, et allicere ad omnia opera caritatis, pietatis et apostolatus, quibus operibus manifestum fiat christifideles de hoc mundo quidem non esse, sed tamen esse lucem mundi eosdemque Patrem glorificare coram hominibus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p221","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","That this full efficacy may be had, however, it is necessary that the faithful come to the sacred Liturgy with right dispositions of mind, accommodate their mind to the voice, and cooperate with heavenly grace, lest they receive it in vain. Therefore sacred pastors should watch that in the liturgical action not only the laws for valid and lawful celebration are observed, but that the faithful participate knowingly, actively, and fruitfully.","Attamen Liturgia est culmen ad quod actio Ecclesiae tendit et simul fons unde omnis eius virtus emanat. Nam labores apostolici ad id ordinantur ut omnes, per fidem et Baptismum filii Dei facti, in unum conveniant, in medio Ecclesiae Deum laudent, Sacrificium participent et cenam dominicam manducent. Vicissim, ipsa Liturgia impellit fideles ut sacramentis paschalibus satiati fiant pietate concordes; orat ut vivendo teneant quod fide perceperunt; renovatio vero foederis Domini cum hominibus in Eucharistia fideles in urgentem caritatem Christi trahit et accendit. Ex Liturgia ergo, praecipue ex Eucharistia, ut e fonte, gratia in nos derivatur et maxima cum efficacia obtinetur illa in Christo hominum sanctificatio et Dei glorificatio, ad quam, uti ad finem, omnia alia Ecclesiae opera contendunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p222","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Yet the spiritual life is not contained solely in participation in the sacred Liturgy. The Christian, called to pray in common, must nevertheless also enter into his chamber to pray to the Father in secret, indeed, the Apostle teaching, pray without ceasing. And we are taught by the same Apostle always to bear about the mortification of Jesus in our body, that the life also of Jesus may be made manifest in our mortal flesh. Therefore we pray to the Lord in the Sacrifice of the Mass that, the offering of the spiritual victim having been received, He may perfect us unto an eternal gift.","Ut haec tamen plena efficacitas habeatur, nesessarium est ut fideles cum recti animi dispositionibus ad sacram Liturgiam accedant, mentem suam voci accommodent, et supernae gratiae cooperentur, ne eam in vacuum recipiant. Ideo sacris pastoribus advigilandum est ut in actione liturgica non solum observentur leges ad validam et licitam celebrationem, sed ut fideles scienter, actuose et fructuose eandem participent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p223","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","The pious practices of the Christian people, as long as they are conformable to the laws and norms of the Church, are strongly commended, especially when they are done by mandate of the Apostolic See. Also, the sacred practices of particular churches, which are celebrated by mandate of the bishops according to customs or books legitimately approved, enjoy special dignity. Yet in such a way, regard being had to the liturgical seasons, the same practices must be ordered that they be congruous to the sacred Liturgy, in some way derive from it, and lead the people to it, since it by its nature far excels them.","Vita tamen spiritualis non unius sacrae Liturgiae participatione continetur. Christianus enim ad communiter orandum vocatus, nihilominus debet etiam intrare in cubiculum suum ut Patrem in abscondito oret, immo, docente Apostolo, sine intermissione orare. Et ab eodem Apostolo docemur mortificationem Iesu semper circumferre in corpore nostro, ut et vita Iesu manifestetur in carne nostra mortali. Quapropter Dominum in Missae Sacrificio precamur ut, hostiae spiritualis oblatione suscepta, nosmetipsos sibi perficiat munus aeternum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p224","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Mother Church greatly desires that all the faithful be led to that full, conscious, and active participation in liturgical celebrations which is demanded by the nature of the Liturgy itself, and to which the Christian people, a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people of acquisition (1 Pet 2:9; cf. 1 Pet 2:4-5), have a right and are bound by virtue of baptism. This full and active participation of the whole people, in restoring and fostering the sacred Liturgy, is to be most highly attended to: for it is the primary and indispensable source from which the faithful are to draw the truly Christian spirit; and therefore in the whole pastoral action, through due instruction, it is to be earnestly sought by the pastors of souls.","Pia populi christiani exercitia, dummodo legibus et normis Ecclesiae conformia sint, valde commendantur, praesertim cum de mandato Apostolicae Sedis fiunt. Speciali quoque dignitate gaudent sacra Ecclesiarum particularium exercitia, quae de mandato Episcoporum celebrantur, secundum consuetudines aut libros legitime approbatos. Ita vero, ratione habita temporum liturgicorum, eadem exercitia ordinentur oportet, ut sacrae Liturgiae congruant, ab ea quodammodo deriventur, ad eam populum manuducant, utpote quae natura sua iisdem longe antecellat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p225","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","But because no hope shines for this to happen unless first the pastors of souls themselves are deeply imbued with the spirit and power of the Liturgy and become masters in it, therefore it is most necessary that the liturgical formation of the clergy be especially provided for. Wherefore the sacred Synod has decided to decree the following.","Valde cupit Mater Ecclesia ut fideles universi ad plenam illam, consciam atque actuosam liturgicarum celebrationum participationem ducantur, quae ab ipsius Liturgiae natura postulatur et ad quam populus christianus, genus electum, regale sacerdotium, gens sancta, populus adquisitionis (1P 2,9 cf. 1P 2,4-5), vi Baptismatis ius habet et officium. Quae totius populi plena et actuosa participatio, in instauranda et fovenda sacra Liturgia, summopere est attendenda: est enim primus, isque necessarius fons, e quo spiritum vere christianum fideles hauriant; et ideo in tota actione pastorali, per debitam institutionem, ab animarum pastoribus est sedulo adpetenda."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p226","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Masters who are placed over teaching the discipline of sacred Liturgy in seminaries, religious houses of study, and theological faculties, are to be properly instituted for their office in institutes specially destined for this purpose with special care. The discipline of sacred Liturgy is to be held among the necessary and principal disciplines in seminaries and religious houses of study, and in theological faculties among the principal disciplines, and is to be taught under the theological, historical, spiritual, pastoral, and juridical aspect. Let the masters of other disciplines, especially of dogmatic theology, sacred Scripture, spiritual and pastoral theology, also take care that, from the intrinsic requirements of their own object, they so cultivate the mystery of Christ and the history of salvation that from it the connection of these with the Liturgy and the unity of priestly formation may clearly appear.","Sed quia, ut hoc evenire possit, nulla spes effulget nisi prius ipsi animarum pastores spiritu et virtute Liturgiae penitus imbuantur in eaque efficiantur magistri, ideo pernecesse est ut institutioni liturgicae cleri apprime consulatur. Quapropter Sacrosanctum Concilium ea quae sequuntur statuere decrevit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p227","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Clerics, in seminaries and religious houses, are to acquire a liturgical formation of spiritual life, both by apt guidance by which they may be able to understand sacred rites and participate in them with their whole spirit, and by the very celebration of the sacred mysteries, as well as by other exercises of piety imbued with the spirit of the sacred Liturgy; likewise let them learn the observance of liturgical laws, so that life in seminaries and religious institutes may be thoroughly informed with the liturgical spirit.","Magistri, qui sacrae Liturgiae disciplinam in seminariis, studiorum domibus religiosis et facultatibus theologicis docendae praeficiuntur, ad munus suum in institutis ad hoc speciali cura destinatis probe instituendi sunt. Disciplina de sacra Liturgia in seminariis et studiorum domibus religiosis inter disciplinas necessarias et potiores, in facultatibus autem theologicis inter disciplinas principales est habenda, et sub aspectu cum theologico et historico, tum spirituali, pastorali et iuridico tradenda. Curent insuper aliarum disciplinarum magistri, imprimis theologiae dogmaticae, sacrae Scripturae, theologiae spiritualis et pastoralis ita, ex intrinsecis exigentiis proprii uniuscuiusque obiecti, mysterium Christi et historiam salutis excolere, ut exinde earum connexio cum Liturgia et unitas sacerdotalis institutionis aperte clarescant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p228","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Priests, both secular and religious, already laboring in the Lord's vineyard, should be helped by all opportune means that they may ever more fully understand what they do in sacred functions, may live the liturgical life, and communicate it to the faithful entrusted to them. Pastors of souls, according to the age, condition, manner of life, and grade of religious culture of the faithful, should sedulously and patiently pursue the liturgical instruction and the active participation of the faithful, both internal and external, fulfilling one of the principal duties of a faithful dispenser of the mysteries of God; and in this matter they should lead their flock not only by word but also by example.","Clerici, in seminariis domibusque religiosis, formationem vitae spiritualis liturgicam acquirant, cum apta manuductione qua sacros ritus intellegere et toto animo participare queant, tum ipsa sacrorum mysteriorum celebratione, necnon aliis pietatis exercitiis spiritu sacrae Liturgiae imbutis; pariter observantiam legum liturgicarum addiscant, ita ut vita in seminariis et religiosorum institutis liturgico spiritu penitus informetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p229","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Transmissions of sacred actions by means of radio and television, especially if it concerns the celebration of the Mass, are to be done discretely and decorously, under the direction and sponsorship of a suitable person designated for this office by the bishops.","Sacerdotes, sive saeculares sive religiosi, in vinea Domini iam operantes, omnibus mediis opportunis iuventur ut plenius semper quae in functionibus sacris agunt intellegant, vitam liturgicam vivant, eamque cum fidelibus sibi commissis communicent. Liturgicam institutionem necnon actuosam fidelium participationem, internam et externam, iuxta ipsorum aetatem, condicionem, vitae genus et religiosae culturae gradum, animarum pastores sedulo ac patienter prosequantur, unum e praecipuis fidelis mysteriorum Dei dispensatoris muneribus absolventes; et gregem suum hac in re non verbo tantum, sed etiam exemplo ducant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p230","II - on Promoting Liturgical Instruction and Active Participation","Pious Mother Church, that the Christian people may more securely obtain the abundance of graces in the sacred Liturgy, desires to sedulously take care for the general restoration of the Liturgy itself. For the Liturgy consists of an unchangeable part, as divinely instituted, and parts subject to change, which can vary or even ought to vary in the course of time, if perchance things which have crept in which less well respond to the intimate nature of the Liturgy itself, or have become less apt. In this restoration, texts and rites must be so ordered that they more clearly express the holy things which they signify, and that the Christian people, as far as possible, be able easily to perceive them and to participate in them with a full, active, and community-like celebration. Therefore the sacred Synod decrees the following more general norms.","Transmissiones actionum sacrarum ope radiophonica et televisifica, praesertim si agatur de Sacro faciendo, discrete ac decore fiant, ductu et sponsione personae idoneae, ad hoc munus ab Episcopis destinatae."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p231","III - on the Restoration of the Sacred Liturgy","- The regulation of the sacred Liturgy depends solely on the authority of the Church: which is with the Apostolic See and, according to the norm of law, with the bishop. 2. By the power granted by law, the regulation of liturgical matter also pertains, within established limits, to various competent territorial episcopal conferences legitimately constituted. 3. Wherefore, no other person at all, even if he be a priest, may add, remove, or change anything in the Liturgy on his own authority.","Pia Mater Ecclesia, ut populus christianus in sacra Liturgia abundantiam gratiarum securius assequatur, ipsius Liturgiae generalem instaurationem sedulo curare cupit. Nam Liturgia constat parte immutabili, utpote divinitus instituta, et partibus mutationi obnoxiis, quae decursu temporum variare possunt vel etiam debent, si in eas forte irrepserint quae minus bene ipsius Liturgiae intimae naturae respondeant, vel minus aptae factae sint. Qua quidem instauratione, textus et ritus ita ordinari oportet, ut sancta, quae significant, clarius exprimant, eaque populus christianus, in quantum fieri potest, facile percipere atque plena, actuosa et communitatis propria celebratione participare possit. Quare Sacrosanctum Concilium generaliores has normas statuit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p232","A) General Norms","That sound tradition may be retained and yet the way to legitimate progress be opened, an accurate theological, historical, and pastoral investigation must always precede the revision of each part of the Liturgy. Moreover, the general laws of the structure and mind of the Liturgy, as well as the experience from recent liturgical restoration and from indults granted here and there, are to be considered. Finally, innovations are not to be made unless the true and certain utility of the Church demands it, and with caution that new forms organically grow in a way from forms already existing. Let it also be taken care, as far as possible, that notable differences of rites between neighboring regions not be had.","1. Sacrae Liturgiae moderatio ab Ecclesiae auctoritate unice pendet: quae quidem est apud Apostolicam Sedem et, ad normam iuris, apud Episcopum. 2. Ex potestate a iure concessa, rei liturgicae moderatio inter limites statutos pertinet quoque ad competentes varii generis territoriales Episcoporum coetus legitime constitutos. 3. Quapropter nemo omnino alius, etiamsi sit sacerdos, quidquam proprio marte in Liturgia addat, demat, aut mutet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p233","A) General Norms","The greatest importance of sacred Scripture in celebrating the Liturgy is to be held. For from it the readings are read and explained in the homily, the psalms are sung, and from its inspiration and impulse prayers, liturgical songs and hymns have been poured forth, and from it actions and signs receive their meaning. Hence, to procure the restoration, progress, and adaptation of the sacred Liturgy, it is necessary to promote that sweet and living affection for sacred Scripture which the venerable tradition of both Eastern and Western rites testifies.","Ut sana traditio retineatur et tamen via legitimae progressioni aperiatur, de singulis Liturgiae partibus recognoscendis accurata investigatio theologica, historica, pastoralis semper praecedat. Insuper considerentur cum leges generales structurae et mentis Liturgiae, tum experientia ex recentiore instauratione liturgica et ex indultis passim concessis promanans. Innovationes, demum, ne fiant nisi vera et certa utilitas Ecclesiae id exigat, et adhibita cautela ut novae formae ex formis iam exstantibus organice quodammodo crescant. Caveatur etiam, in quantum fieri potest, ne notabiles differentiae rituum inter finitimas regiones habeantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p234","A) General Norms","The liturgical books are to be revised as soon as possible, experts being employed and bishops consulted from various regions of the world.","Maximum est sacrae Scripturae momentum in Liturgia celebranda. Ex ea enim lectiones leguntur et in homilia explicantur, psalmi canuntur, atque ex eius afflatu instinctuque preces, oratione et carmina liturgica effusa sunt, et ex ea significationem suam actiones et signa accipiunt. Unde, ad procurandam sacrae Liturgiae instaurationem, progressum et aptationem, oportet ut promoveatur ille suavis et vivus sacrae Scripturae affectus, quem testatur venerabilis rituum cum orientalium tum occidentalium traditio."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p235","A) General Norms","Liturgical actions are not private actions, but celebrations of the Church, which is the sacrament of unity, namely the holy people gathered and ordered under the bishops. Therefore they pertain to the whole body of the Church, manifest and affect it, but they touch individual members of it in a different way, according to the diversity of orders, offices, and actual participation.","Libri liturgici quam primum recognoscantur, peritis adhibitis et Episcopis consultis ex diversis orbis regionibus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p236","B) Norms From the Nature of the Liturgy As an Action Proper to the Hierarchy and","As often as rites, according to the proper nature of each, carry with them a common celebration with the attendance and active participation of the faithful, let it be inculcated that this is to be preferred as far as possible to the individual and as it were private celebration of the same. Which is of special value for the celebration of Mass, the public and social nature of every Mass always being saved, and for the administration of the sacraments. In liturgical celebrations each one, whether minister or faithful, fulfilling his own office, should do all and only that which belongs to him from the nature of the matter and liturgical norms.","Actiones liturgicae non sunt actiones privatae, sed celebrationes Ecclesiae, quae est unitatis sacramentum, scilicet plebs sancta sub Episcopis adunata et ordinata. Quare ad universum Corpus Ecclesiae pertinent illudque manifestant et afficiunt; singula vero membra ipsius diverso modo, pro diversitate ordinum, munerum et actualis participationis, attingunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p237","B) Norms From the Nature of the Liturgy As an Action Proper to the Hierarchy and","Even the ministers, lectors, commentators, and those who belong to the choir, exercise a true liturgical ministry. Therefore let them fulfill their office with such sincere piety and order that it becomes so great a ministry and that the people of God justly require it of them. Hence it is necessary that they be sedulously imbued with the spirit of the Liturgy, each in his own way, and be instituted for their parts to be performed rightly and orderly.","Quoties ritus, iuxta propriam cuiusque naturam, secum ferunt celebrationem communem, cum frequentia et actuosa participatione fidelium, inculcetur hanc, in quantum fieri potest, esse praeferendam celebrationi eorundem singulari et quasi privatae. Quod valet praesertim pro Missae celebratione, salva semper natura publica et sociali cuiusvis Missae, et pro Sacramentorum administratione. In celebrationibus liturgicis quisque, sive minister sive fidelis, munere suo fungens, solum et totum id agat, quod ad ipsum ex rei natura et normis liturgicis pertinet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p238","B) Norms From the Nature of the Liturgy As an Action Proper to the Hierarchy and","To promote active participation, the acclamations of the people, responses, psalmody, antiphons, hymns, as well as actions or gestures and bodily posture are to be fostered. Sacred silence also is to be observed at its proper time. In revising the liturgical books, it is to be carefully attended that the rubrics also provide for the parts of the faithful. In the Liturgy, besides the distinction arising from liturgical office and sacred order, and besides the honors due to civil authorities according to the norms of liturgical laws, no acceptance of private persons or conditions, whether in ceremonies or in exterior pomp, is to be had.","Etiam ministrantes, lectores, commentatores et ii qui ad scholam cantorum pertinent, vero ministerio liturgico funguntur. Propterea munus suum tali sincera pietate et ordine exerceant, quae tantum ministerium decent quaeque populus Dei ab eis iure exigit. Ideo oportet eos spiritu Liturgiae, suo cuiusque modo, sedulo imbui, et ad partes suas rite et ordinate obeundas institui."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p239","B) Norms From the Nature of the Liturgy As an Action Proper to the Hierarchy and","Although the sacred Liturgy is chiefly the worship of the divine majesty, it also contains great instruction for the faithful people. For in the Liturgy God speaks to his people; Christ still announces the Gospel. The people, however, respond to God both by hymns and by prayers. Indeed, the prayers directed to God by the priest, who presides over the assembly in the person of Christ, are said in the name of the whole holy people and of all present. Finally, the visible signs which the sacred Liturgy uses for signifying invisible divine things, have been chosen by Christ or the Church. Hence not only when those things are read which were written for our doctrine (Rom 15:4), but also when the Church prays or sings or acts, the faith of the participants is nourished, minds are raised to God to render Him a rational service, and His grace is received more abundantly.","Ad actuosam participationem promovendam, populi acclamationes, responsiones, psalmodia, antiphonae, cantica, necnon actiones seu gestus et corporis habitus foveantur. Sacrum quoque silentium suo tempore servetur. In libris liturgicis recognoscendis, sedulo attendatur ut rubricae etiam partes fidelium praevideant. In Liturgia, praeter distinctionem ex munere liturgico et Ordine sacro manantem, et praeter honores ad normam legum liturgicarum auctoritatibus civilibus debitos, nulla privatarum personarum aut condicionum, sive in caerimoniis, sive in exterioribus pompis, habeatur acceptio."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p240","C) Norms From the Didactic and Pastoral Nature of the Liturgy","Hence, in the restoration to be done, the following general norms must be observed.","Etsi sacra Liturgia est praecipue cultus divinae maiestatis, magnam etiam continet populi fidelis eruditionem. In Liturgia enim Deus ad populum suum loquitur; Christus adhuc Evangelium annuntiat. Populus vero Deo respondet tum cantibus tum oratione. Immo, preces a sacerdote, qui coetui in persona Christi praeest, ad Deum directae, nomine totius plebis sanctae et omnium circumstantium dicuntur. Signa tandem visibilia, quibus utitur sacra Liturgia ad res divinas invisibiles significandas, a Christo vel Ecclesia delecta sunt. Unde non solum quando leguntur ea quae ad nostram doctrinam scripta sunt (Rm 15,4), sed etiam dum Ecclesia vel orat vel canit vel agit, participantium fides alitur, mentes in Deum excitantur ut rationabile obsequium Ei praestent, gratiamque Eius abundantius recipiant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p241","C) Norms From the Didactic and Pastoral Nature of the Liturgy","Rites are to shine with noble simplicity, be brief and perspicuous, avoid useless repetitions, be accommodated to the capacity of the faithful, and generally not need many explanations. That it may clearly appear in the Liturgy that rite and word are intimately joined: 1) In sacred celebrations, a more abundant, varied, and apt reading of sacred Scripture is to be restored. 2) A more suitable place for the sermon, as part of the liturgical action, is to be indicated in the rubrics as the rite permits, and the ministry of preaching is to be most faithfully and rightly fulfilled. This, however, is to be drawn especially from the source of sacred Scripture and the Liturgy, as an announcement of the wonderful works of God in the history of salvation, that is, the mystery of Christ, which is always present and active in us, especially in liturgical celebrations. 3) Also a more directly liturgical catechesis is to be inculcated in every way; and in the rites themselves, if there be need, brief admonitions by the priest or a suitable minister, at only the more opportune moments, are to be foreseen, to be said in prescribed or similar words. 4) The sacred celebration of the word of God on the vigils of more solemn feasts, on some ferias of Advent and Lent, and on Sundays and feast days, especially in places lacking a priest, is to be fostered: in which case, the celebration is to be directed by a deacon or another delegated by the bishop.","Exinde in instauratione facienda generales normae quae sequuntur observari debent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p242","C) Norms From the Didactic and Pastoral Nature of the Liturgy","- The use of the Latin language, saving particular law, is to be preserved in the Latin rites. 2. Yet since the use of the vernacular language can often be very useful for the people both in the Mass, in the administration of the sacraments, and in other parts of the Liturgy, a wider place can be given to it, especially in the readings and admonitions, in some prayers and hymns, according to the norms which are established individually in the following chapters concerning this matter. 3. These norms having been observed, it pertains to the competent territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned in art. 22, 2, and also, if the case requires, with the counsel of the bishops of neighboring regions of the same language, to decide concerning the use and manner of the vernacular language, the acts being approved or confirmed by the Apostolic See. 4. The translation of the Latin text into the vernacular language to be used in the Liturgy must be approved by the competent territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned above.","Ritus nobili simplicitate fulgeant, sint brevitate perspicui et repetitiones inutiles evitent, sint fidelium captui accommodati, neque generatim multis indigeant explanationibus. Ut clare appareat in Liturgia ritum et verbum intime coniungi: 1) In celebrationibus sacris abundantior, varior et aptior lectio sacrae Scripturae instauretur. 2) Locus aptior sermonis, utpote partis actionis liturgicae, prout ritus patitur, etiam in rubricis notetur; et fidelissime ac rite adimpleatur ministerium praedicationis. Haec vero imprimis ex fonte sacrae Scripturae et Liturgiae hauriatur, quasi annuntiatio mirabilium Dei in historia salutis seu mysterio Christi, quod in nobis praesens semper adest et operatur, praesertim in celebrationibus liturgicis. 3) Etiam catechesis directius liturgica omnibus modis inculcetur; et in ipsis ritibus, si necessariae sint, breves admonitiones, a sacerdote vel competenti ministro, opportunioribus tantum momentis, praescriptis vel similibus verbis, dicendae, praevideantur. 4) Foveatur sacra verbi Dei celebratio in solemniorum festorum pervigiliis, in aliquibus feriis Adventus et Quadragesimae, atque in dominicis et diebus festis, maxime in locis quae sacerdote carent: quo in casu celebrationem diaconus vel alius ab Episcopo delegatus dirigat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p243","C) Norms From the Didactic and Pastoral Nature of the Liturgy","The Church does not wish to impose rigid uniformity in the Liturgy even in those things which do not touch faith or the good of the whole community; nay rather, she cultivates and advances the ornaments and gifts of the spirit of various nations and peoples; and whatever is found in the customs of peoples that is not indissolubly attached to superstitions and errors, she kindly considers and, if possible, keeps whole and intact, indeed sometimes she even admits it into the Liturgy itself, provided it be congruous with the reasons of true and authentic liturgical spirit. The legitimate varieties and adaptations to various groups, regions, and peoples, especially in the Missions, are to be given place, the substantial unity of the Roman Rite being preserved, even when the liturgical books are revised; and this is to be opportunely kept before the eyes in establishing the structure of the rites and the rubrics. Within the limits set in the typical editions of the liturgical books, it will pertain to the competent territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned in art. 22, 2 to define adaptations, especially concerning the administration of the sacraments, concerning sacramentals, processions, liturgical language, sacred music, and the arts, according, however, to the fundamental norms which are contained in this Constitution. Yet when in various places and circumstances a deeper adaptation of the Liturgy urgently presses, and therefore becomes more difficult: 1) The competent territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned in art. 22, 2 shall sedulously and prudently consider what, in this matter, can be appropriately admitted into divine worship from the traditions and genius of individual peoples. Adaptations which are judged useful or necessary are to be proposed to the Apostolic See, to be introduced with its consent. 2) So that adaptation may be done with the necessary circumspection, the same ecclesiastical authority shall be granted by the Apostolic See the faculty, if the case requires, to permit and direct necessary preliminary experiments in certain groups suited for this and for a determined time. 3) Because liturgical laws, concerning adaptation, especially in the Missions, are accustomed to bring special difficulties, in establishing them let men skilled in the matter be at hand.","1. Linguae latinae usus, salvo particulari iure, in ritibus latinis servetur. 2. Cum tamen, sive in Missa, sive in Sacramentorum administratione, sive in aliis Liturgiae partibus, haud raro linguae vernaculae usurpatio valde utilis apud populum exsistere possit, amplior locus ipsi tribui valeat, imprimis autem in lectionibus et admonitionibus, in nonnullis orationibus et cantibus, iuxta normas quae de hac re in sequentibus capitibus singillatim statuuntur. 3. Huiusmodi normis servatis, est competentis auctoritatis ecclesiasticae territorialis, de qua in art. 22 2, etiam, si casus ferat, consilio habito cum Episcopis finitimarum regionum eiusdem linguae, de usu et modo linguae vernaculae statuere, actis ab Apostolica Sede probatis seu confirmatis. 4. Conversio textus latini in linguam vernaculam in Liturgia adhibenda, a competenti auctoritate ecclesiastica territoriali, de qua supra, approbari debet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p244","D) Norms for Adaptation to the Genius and Traditions of Peoples","The bishop is to be regarded as the great priest of his flock, from whom the life of his faithful in Christ is in some way derived and depends. Therefore all ought to hold in great esteem the liturgical life of the diocese around the bishop, especially in the cathedral church, being persuaded that the principal manifestation of the Church is had in the full and active participation of the whole holy people of God in the same liturgical celebrations, especially in the same Eucharist, in one prayer, at one altar, over which the bishop presides, surrounded by his presbyterate and ministers.","Ecclesia, in iis quae fidem aut bonum totius communitatis non tangunt, rigidam unius tenoris formam ne in Liturgia quidem imponere cupit; quinimmo, variarum gentium populorumque animi ornamenta ac dotes colit et provehit; quidquid vero in populorum moribus indissolubili vinculo superstitionibus erroribusque non adstipulatur, benevole perpendit ac, si potest, sartum tectumque servat, immo quandoque in ipsam Liturgiam admittit, dummodo cum rationibus veri et authentici spiritus liturgici congruat. Servata substantiali unitate Ritus romani, legitimis varietatibus et aptationibus ad diversos coetus, regiones, populos, praesertim in Missionibus, locus relinquatur, etiam cum libri liturgici recognoscuntur; et hoc in structura rituum et in rubricis instituendis opportune prae oculis habeatur. Intra limites in editionibus typicis librorum liturgicorum statutos, erit competentis auctoritatis ecclesiasticae territorialis, de qua in art. 22 2, aptationes definire, praesertim quoad administrationem Sacramentorum, quoad Sacramentalia, processiones, linguam liturgicam, musicam sacram et artes, iuxta tamen normas fundamentales quae hac in Constitutione habentur. Cum tamen variis in locis et adiunctis, profundior Liturgiae aptatio urgeat, et ideo difficilior evadat: 1) A competenti auctoritate ecclesiastica territoriali, de qua in art. 22 2, sedulo et prudenter consideretur quid, hoc in negotio, ex traditionibus ingenioque singulorum populorum opportune in cultum divinum admitti possit. Aptationes, quae utiles vel necessariae existimantur, Apostolicae Sedi proponantur, de ipsius consensu introducendae. 2) Ut autem aptatio cum necessaria circumspectione fiat, eidem auctoritati ecclesiasticae territoriali ab Apostolica Sede facultas tribuetur, si casus ferat, ut in quibusdam coetibus ad id aptis et per determinatum tempus necessaria praevia experimenta permittat et dirigat. 3) Quia leges liturgicae difficultates speciales, quoad aptationem, praesertim in Missionibus, secum ferre solent, in illis condendis praesto sint viri, in re de qua agitur, periti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p245","IV - on Fostering Liturgical Life in the Diocese and in the Parish","Since the bishop cannot always and everywhere preside over the whole flock in his own church, he must necessarily establish assemblies of the faithful, among which parishes, locally ordered under a pastor taking the bishop's place, stand out: for they in a certain way represent the visible Church constituted throughout the whole world. Therefore the liturgical life of the parish and its relation to the bishop is to be fostered in the mind and practice of the faithful and clergy; and it is to be labored that the sense of parish community, especially in the common celebration of the Sunday Mass, may flourish.","Episcopus ut sacerdos magnus sui gregis habendus est, a quo vita suorum fidelium in Christo quodammodo derivatur et pendet. Quare omnes vitam liturgicam dioeceseos circa Episcopum, praesertim in ecclesia cathedrali, maximi faciant oportet: sibi persuasum habentes praecipuam manifestationem Ecclesiae haberi in plenaria et actuosa participatione totius plebis sanctae Dei in iisdem celebrationibus liturgicis, praesertim in eadem Eucharistia, in una oratione, ad unum altare cui praeest Episcopus a suo presbyterio et ministris circumdatus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p246","IV - on Fostering Liturgical Life in the Diocese and in the Parish","The study of fostering and restoring the sacred Liturgy is justly regarded as a sign of the providential dispositions of God over our age, as a passage of the Holy Spirit in His Church; and it distinguishes its own life, indeed the whole manner of religious feeling and action of this our time, with a proper mark. Wherefore, to further promote this pastoral liturgical action in the Church, the sacred Synod decrees: It is expedient that by the competent territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned in art. 22, 2 a Liturgical Commission be established, to be helped by men skilled in liturgical science, music, sacred art, and pastoral matters. To this Commission, as far as possible, let a certain Institute of Pastoral Liturgy give help, consisting of members, not excluding, if the matter requires, laity excelling in this matter. It will be of this Commission, under the direction of the territorial ecclesiastical authority mentioned above, to regulate pastoral liturgical action in its territory, and to promote studies and necessary experiments, as often as it concerns adaptations to be proposed to the Apostolic See. In the same way, in each diocese a Commission on sacred Liturgy is to be had, to promote liturgical action under the direction of the bishop. It can sometimes become opportune that several dioceses constitute one Commission, which, with counsel exchanged, may advance the liturgical matter.","Cum Episcopus in Ecclesia sua ipsemet nec semper nec ubique universo gregi praeesse possit, necessario constituere debet fidelium coetus, inter quos paroeciae, localiter sub pastore vices gerente Episcopi ordinatae, eminent: nam quodammodo repraesentant Ecclesiam visibilem per orbem terrarum constitutam. Quare vita liturgica paroeciae eiusque relatio ad Episcopum in mente et praxi fidelium et cleri fovenda est; et adlaborandum ut sensus communitatis paroecialis, imprimis vero in communi celebratione Missae dominicalis, floreat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p247","V - on Promoting Pastoral Liturgical Action","Besides the Commission on sacred Liturgy, in any diocese, as far as possible, Commissions on sacred music and sacred art are also to be constituted. It is necessary that these three Commissions labor with united forces; indeed, not seldom it will be fitting that they coalesce into one Commission.","Sacrae Liturgiae fovendae atque instaurandae studium merito habetur veluti signum providentialium dispositionum Dei super nostra aetate, veluti transitus Spiritus Sancti in sua Ecclesia; et vitam ipsius, immo huius nostri temporis universam rationem religiose sentiendi et agendi, nota propria distinguit. Quapropter, ad hanc actionem pastoralem liturgicam ulterius in Ecclesia fovendam, Sacrosanctum Concilium decernit: A competenti auctoritate ecclesiastica territoriali, de qua in art 22 2, expedit ut instituatur Commissio liturgica, a viris in scientia liturgica, Musica, Arte sacra ac re pastorali peritis iuvanda. Cui Commissioni, in quantum fieri potest, opem ferat quoddam Institutum Liturgiae Pastoralis, constans sodalibus, non exclusis, si res ita ferat, laicis in hac materia praestantibus. Ipsius Commissionis erit, ductu auctoritatis ecclesiasticae territorialis, de qua supra, et actionem pastoralem liturgicam in sua dicione moderari, et studia atque necessaria experimenta promovere, quoties agatur de aptationibus Apostolicae Sedis proponendis. Eadem ratione, in singulis dioecesibus Commissio de sacra Liturgia habeatur, ad actionem liturgicam, moderante Episcopo, promovendam. Opportunum aliquando evadere potest ut plures dioeceses unam Commissionem constituant, quae, collatis consiliis, rem liturgicam provehat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p248","V - on Promoting Pastoral Liturgical Action","1 The restoration of unity among all Christians is one of the principal concerns of the Second Vatican Ecumenical Synod. For the one and only Church was founded by Christ the Lord, yet many Christian Communions present themselves to men as the true heritage of Jesus Christ; indeed all profess themselves to be disciples of the Lord, but they think differently and walk different ways, as if Christ Himself were divided. Which division indeed openly contradicts the will of Christ, is a scandal to the world, and brings detriment to the most holy cause of preaching the Gospel to every creature.","Praeter Commissionem de sacra Liturgia, in quavis dioecesi constituantur, quantum fieri potest, etiam Commissiones de Musica sacra et de Arte sacra. Necessarium est ut hae tres Commissiones consociatis viribus adlaborent; immo non raro congruum erit ut in unam Commissionem coalescant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p249","Unitatis Redintegratio","The Lord of the ages, however, who wisely and patiently pursues the purpose of His grace toward us sinners, has recently begun to pour out more abundantly on Christians separated among themselves a compunction of spirit and a desire for union. Moved by this grace, very many men everywhere have been stirred, and also among our separated brothers a greater movement day by day, the grace of the Holy Spirit fostering, has arisen for restoring the unity of all Christians. This movement of unity, called ecumenical, is participated in by those who invoke the triune God and confess Jesus as Lord and Savior, not only singly separately, but also gathered in assemblies, in which they have heard the Gospel and which each calls his church and that of God. Almost all, however, although in a different way, aspire to the one and visible Church of God, which may be truly universal and sent to the whole world that the world may be converted to the Gospel and thus be saved to the glory of God.","UNITATIS REDINTEGRATIO inter universos Christianos promovenda unum est ex praecipuis Sacrae Oecumenicae Synodi Vaticanae Secundae propositis. Una enim atque unica a Christo Domino condita est Ecclesia, plures tamen christianae Communiones sese ut Iesu Christi veram haereditatem hominibus proponunt; discipulos quidem Domini omnes se esse profitentur at diversa sentiunt et per diversas ambulant vias, ac si Christus Ipse divisus sit. Quae sane divisio et aperte voluntati Christi contradicit et scandalo est mundo atque sanctissimae causae praedicandi Evangelium omni creaturae affert detrimentum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p250","Unitatis Redintegratio","Therefore this sacred Synod, joyfully considering all these things, having already declared the doctrine concerning the Church, moved by the desire for restoring unity among all Christ's disciples, wishes to set forth to all Catholics the aids, ways, and means by which they may be able to respond to this divine vocation and grace.","Dominus vero saeculorum, qui propositum gratiae suae erga nos peccatores sapienter et patienter prosequitur, novissime in Christianos inter se disiunctos animi compunctionem et desiderium unionis abundantius effundere incepit. Qua gratia permulti ubique homines permoti sunt atque inter fratres quoque nostros seiunctos amplior in dies motus, Spiritus Sancti fovente gratia, exortus est ad omnium Christianorum unitatem restaurandam. Hunc autem unitatis motum, oecumenicum nuncupatum, participant qui Deum Trinum invocant atque Iesum confitentur Dominum et Salvatorem, nec modo singuli seiunctim, sed etiam in coetibus congregati, in quibus Evangelium audierunt quosque singuli Ecclesiam dicunt esse suam et Dei. Fere omnes tamen, etsi diverso modo, ad Ecclesiam Dei unam et visibilem adspirant, quae sit vere universalis et ad universum mundum missa ut mundus ad Evangelium convertatur et sic salvus fiat ad gloriam Dei."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p251","Unitatis Redintegratio","2 In this the charity of God has appeared in us, that the only-begotten Son of God was sent by the Father into the world, that, made man, He might regenerate the whole human race by redeeming it and gather it into one. Who before He would offer Himself as an immaculate victim on the altar of the cross, prayed to the Father for believers, saying: \"that they all may be one, as you, Father, in me, and I in you, that they also may be one in us; that the world may believe that you have sent me\" (Jn 17:21), and in His Church He instituted the wonderful sacrament of the Eucharist, by which the unity of the Church is both signified and brought about. He gave to His disciples a new commandment of mutual love, and promised the Paraclete Spirit who, the Lord and Giver of life, would abide with them forever.","Haec igitur Sacra Synodus ea omnia laeto animo considerans, cum iam doctrinam de Ecclesia declaraverit, desiderio unitatis inter omnes Christi discipulos restaurandae permota, Catholicis omnibus proponere vult adiumenta, vias ac modos quibus ipsi possint huic divinae vocationi et gratiae respondere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p252","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Exalted on the cross and glorified, the Lord Jesus poured out the promised Spirit, through whom He called and gathered the people of the New Covenant, which is the Church, into the unity of faith, hope, and charity, as the Apostle teaches: \"One body and one Spirit, as you are called in one hope of your calling. One Lord, one faith, one baptism\" (Eph 4:4-5). For \"all you who are baptized in Christ have put on Christ... For all you are one in Christ Jesus\" (Gal 3:27-28). The Holy Spirit, who dwells in believers and fills and rules the whole Church, brings about that wonderful communion of the faithful and so intimately joins all in Christ that He is the Principle of the Church's unity. He works divisions of graces and ministries, enriching the Church of Jesus Christ with various gifts, \"for the consummation of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the building up of the body of Christ\" (Eph 4:12).","In hoc apparuit caritas Dei in nobis, quod Filius Dei unigenitus a Patre in mundum missus est, ut homo factus totum genus humanum redimendo regeneraret atque in unum congregaret. Qui antequam seipsum in ara crucis hostiam immaculatam offerret, Patrem pro credentibus oravit, dicens: ut omnes unum sint, sicut tu, Pater, in me, et ego in te, ut et ipsi in nobis unum sint; ut credat mundus quia tu me misisti (Jn 17,21), et in Ecclesia sua Eucharistiae mirabile sacramentum instituit, quo unitas Ecclesiae et significatur et efficitur. Discipulis suis novum mutui amoris mandatum dedit atque Paraclitum Spiritum promisit qui, Dominus et vivificans, cum eis maneret in aeternum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p253","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","To establish this holy Church of His everywhere on earth until the consummation of the world, Christ entrusted the office of teaching, ruling, and sanctifying to the College of the Twelve. Among them He chose Peter, upon whom after his confession of faith He decreed to build His Church; to him He promised the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and after the profession of his love He committed all the sheep to him to be confirmed in faith and fed in perfect unity, Christ Jesus Himself remaining the chief cornerstone and the shepherd of our souls forever.","Exaltatus autem in cruce et glorificatus Dominus Iesus Spiritum promissum effudit, per quem Novi Foederis populum, qui est Ecclesia, in unitatem fidei, spei et caritatis vocavit et congregavit, sicut docet Apostolus: Unum corpus et unus Spiritus sicut vocati estis in una spe vocationis vestrae. Unus Dominus, una fides, unum baptisma (Ep 4,4-5). Etenim quicumque in Christo baptizati estis, Christum induistis... Omnes enim vos unus estis in Christo Iesu (Ga 3,27-28 gr.). Spiritus Sanctus, qui credentes inhabitat totamque replet atque regit Ecclesiam, miram illam communionem fidelium efficit et tam intime omnes in Christo coniungit, ut Ecclesiae unitatis sit Principium. Ille divisiones gratiarum et ministrationum operatur, variis muneribus Ecclesiam Iesu Christi ditans ad consummationem sanctorum in opus ministerii, in aedificationem corporis Christi (Ep 4,12)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p254","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Jesus Christ wishes His people to grow and perfects their communion in unity through the faithful preaching of the Gospel by the Apostles and their successors, namely bishops with the successor of Peter as head, through the administration of the sacraments, and through governance in love, the Holy Spirit working: in the confession of one faith, in the common celebration of divine worship, and in the fraternal concord of the family of God. Thus the Church, the one flock of God, as a sign lifted up among the nations, ministering the Gospel of peace to the whole human race, pilgrimages in hope to the goal of the heavenly homeland.","Ad hanc autem sanctam suam Ecclesiam ubique terrarum usque ad consummationem saeculi stabiliendam, Christus munus docendi, regendi et sanctificandi Collegio Duodecim concredidit. Inter eos Petrum elegit, super quem post fidei confessionem Ecclesiam suam aedificare decrevit; cui claves regni caelorum promisit atque, post suae dilectionis professionem, universas oves in fide confirmandas et in perfecta unitate pascendas commisit, Ipso Christo Iesu summo angulari lapide et pastore animarum nostrarum in aeternum manente."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p255","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","This is the sacred mystery of the unity of the Church, in Christ and through Christ, the Holy Spirit working the variety of gifts. The supreme exemplar and principle of this mystery is in the unity of the Persons of the one God the Father and the Son in the Holy Spirit.","Iesus Christus per Apostolorum eorumque successorum, nempe episcoporum cum Petri successore capite, fidelem Evangelii praedicationem sacramentorumque administrationem, et per gubernationem in dilectione, Spiritu Sancto operante, populum suum crescere vult eiusque communionem perficit in unitate: in confessione unius fidei, in divini cultus communi celebratione, necnon in familiae Dei fraterna concordia. Ita Ecclesia, unicus Dei grex, tamquam signum levatum in nationes, Evangelium pacis ministrans toti generi humano, peregrinatur in spe ad patriae supernae metam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p256","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","3 In this one and only Church of God, certain schisms arose from the very beginnings, which the Apostle gravely reprobates as to be condemned; in later centuries, however, greater dissensions were born, and not small communities were separated from the full communion of the Catholic Church, sometimes not without the fault of men on both sides. But those who are now born in such communities and are imbued with the faith of Christ cannot be accused of the sin of separation, and the Catholic Church embraces them with fraternal reverence and love.","Hoc est unitatis Ecclesiae sacrum mysterium, in Christo et per Christum, Spiritu Sancto munerum varietatem operante. Huius mysterii supremum exemplar et principium est in Trinitate Personarum unitas unius Dei Patris et Filii in Spiritu Sancto."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p257","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","For they who believe in Christ and have received baptism rightly, are constituted in a certain communion with the Catholic Church, albeit not perfect. Indeed, on account of the discrepancies existing in various ways between them and the Catholic Church both in doctrinal matters and sometimes in disciplinary matters as well as concerning the structure of the Church, not a few impediments, sometimes graver, oppose full ecclesiastical communion, to overcome which the ecumenical movement tends. Nevertheless, justified by faith in baptism, they are incorporated into Christ, therefore they are justly adorned with the Christian name, and are deservedly recognized by the sons of the Catholic Church as brothers in the Lord.","In hac una et unica Dei Ecclesia iam a primordiis scissurae quaedam exortae sunt, quas ut damnandas graviter vituperat Apostolus; posterioribus vero saeculis ampliores natae sunt dissensiones, et Communitates haud exiguae a plena communione Ecclesiae catholicae seiunctae sunt, quandoque non sine hominum utriusque partis culpa. Qui autem nunc in talibus Communitatibus nascuntur et fide Christi imbuuntur, de separationis peccato argui nequeunt, eosque fraterna reverentia et dilectione amplectitur Ecclesia catholica."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p258","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Moreover, from the elements or goods by which the Church itself is built up and given life, some, indeed many and excellent, can exist outside the visible walls of the Catholic Church: the written word of God, the life of grace, faith, hope, and charity, and other interior gifts of the Holy Spirit as well as visible elements. All these, which come from Christ and lead to Him, pertain by right to the one Church of Christ.","Hi enim qui in Christum credunt et baptismum rite receperunt, in quadam cum Ecclesia catholica communione, etsi non perfecta, constituuntur. Profecto, ob discrepantias variis modis vigentes inter eos et Ecclesiam catholicam tum in re doctrinali et quandoque etiam disciplinari tum circa structuram Ecclesiae, plenae ecclesiasticae communioni opponuntur impedimenta non pauca, quandoque graviora, ad quae superanda tendit motus oecumenicus. Nihilominus, iustificati ex fide in baptismate, Christo incorporantur, ideoque christiano nomine iure decorantur et a filiis Ecclesiae catholicae ut fratres in Domino merito agnoscuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p259","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Not a few also of the sacred actions of the Christian religion are performed among our separated brothers, which, in various ways according to the diverse condition of each Church or Community, without doubt can really generate the life of grace and are to be said to open access to the communion of salvation. Therefore the separated Churches and Communities themselves, although we believe they suffer defects, are by no means deprived of significance and weight in the mystery of salvation. For the Spirit of Christ does not refuse to use them as means of salvation, whose power derives from that fullness of grace and truth which is entrusted to the Catholic Church.","Insuper ex elementis seu bonis, quibus simul sumptis ipsa Ecclesia aedificatur et vivificatur, quaedam immo plurima et eximia exstare possunt extra visibilia Ecclesiae catholicae saepta: verbum Dei scriptum, vita gratiae, fides, spes et caritas, aliaque interiora Spiritus Sancti dona ac visibilia elementa: haec omnia, quae a Christo proveniunt et ad Ipsum conducunt, ad unicam Christi Ecclesiam iure pertinent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p260","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Nevertheless, our separated brothers, whether individually or their Communities and Churches, do not enjoy that unity which Jesus Christ willed to bestow on all those whom He regenerated and vivified together into one body and newness of life, and which the Sacred Scriptures and the venerable Tradition of the Church profess. For only through the Catholic Church of Christ, which is the general aid of salvation, can the fullness of all saving means be attained. For we believe the Lord entrusted all the goods of the New Covenant to the one apostolic college of which Peter is the head, to constitute the one body of Christ on earth, into which all who already in some way belong to the People of God must be fully incorporated. This people, during its earthly pilgrimage, although it remains obnoxious to sin in its members, grows in Christ and is gently led by God according to His hidden counsels, until it arrives joyfully at the whole fullness of eternal glory in the heavenly Jerusalem.","Non paucae etiam christianae religionis actiones sacrae apud fratres a nobis seiunctos peraguntur, quae variis modis secundum diversam condicionem uniuscuiusque Ecclesiae vel Communitatis procul dubio vitam gratiae reapse generare possunt atque aptae dicendae sunt quae ingressum in salutis communionem pandant. Proinde ipsae Ecclesiae et Communitates seiunctae, etsi defectus illas pati credimus, nequaquam in mysterio salutis significatione et pondere exutae sunt. Iis enim Spiritus Christi uti non renuit tamquam salutis mediis, quorum virtus derivatur ab ipsa plenitudine gratiae et veritatis quae Ecclesiae catholicae concredita est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p261","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","4 Since today in many parts of the world, the grace of the Holy Spirit breathing, many efforts are made by prayer, word, and work to approach that fullness of unity which Jesus Christ wills, this sacred Synod exhorts all Catholic faithful that, recognizing the signs of the times, they participate skillfully in the ecumenical work.","Attamen fratres a nobis seiuncti, sive singuli sive Communitates et Ecclesiae eorum, unitate illa non fruuntur, quam Iesus Christus iis omnibus dilargiri voluit quos in unum corpus et in novitatem vitae regeneravit et convivificavit, quamque Sacrae Scripturae et veneranda Ecclesiae Traditio profitentur. Per solam enim catholicam Christi Ecclesiam, quae generale auxilium salutis est, omnis salutarium mediorum plenitudo attingi potest. Uni nempe Collegio apostolico cui Petrus praeest credimus Dominum commisisse omnia bona Foederis Novi, ad constituendum unum Christi corpus in terris, cui plene incorporentur oportet omnes, qui ad populum Dei iam aliquo modo pertinent. Qui populus, durante sua terrestri peregrinatione, quamvis in membris suis peccato obnoxius remaneat, in Christo crescit et a Deo, secundum Eius arcana consilia, suaviter ducitur, usquedum ad totam aeternae gloriae plenitudinem in caelesti Ierusalem laetus perveniat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p262","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","By the ecumenical movement are understood the activities and undertakings which, for the various needs of the Church and opportunities of times, are aroused and ordered to foster the unity of Christians, such as: first, all efforts to eliminate words, judgments, and deeds which do not correspond to the condition of our separated brothers according to equity and truth, and therefore render mutual relations with them more difficult; then, in assemblies of Christians from different Churches or Communities ordered in a religious spirit, a dialogue entered into between experts suitably instructed, in which each explains the doctrine of his Communion more deeply and clearly presents its characteristics. For through this dialogue all acquire a truer knowledge and more equitable estimation of the doctrine and life of each Communion; then those Communions also obtain a wider collaboration in whatever offices demanded by the common good from every Christian conscience, and in unanimous prayer, where it is lawful, they meet. Finally, all examine their own fidelity to the will of Christ concerning the Church and, as is fitting, strenuously undertake the work of renewal and reformation.","Cum hodie in pluribus orbis partibus, afflante Spiritus Sancti gratia, oratione, verbo et opere multi conatus fiant accedendi ad illam plenitudinem unitatis, quam Iesus Christus vult, haec Sancta Synodus cunctos fideles catholicos hortatur ut, signa temporum agnoscentes, operi oecumenico sollerter participent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p263","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","All which, when they are carried out by the faithful of the Catholic Church prudently and patiently under the vigilance of the pastors, contribute to the good of equity and truth, of concord and collaboration, of fraternal spirit and union; that thus, obstacles impeding perfect ecclesiastical communion being gradually overcome, all Christians, in one celebration of the Eucharist, may be gathered into the unity of the one and only Church which Christ gave to His Church from the beginning, which we believe subsists indefectibly in the Catholic Church, and which we hope grows day by day until the consummation of the world.","Per motum oecumenicum intelliguntur activitates et incepta, quae pro variis Ecclesiae necessitatibus et opportunitatibus temporum ad Christianorum unitatem fovendam suscitantur et ordinantur, ut sunt: primum, omnes conatus ad eliminanda verba, iudicia et opera, quae fratrum seiunctorum condicioni secundum aequitatem et veritatem non respondeant, ideoque mutuas cum ipsis rationes difficiliores reddant; dein, in conventibus Christianorum ex diversis Ecclesiis vel Communitatibus in spiritu religioso ordinatis, dialogus inter peritos apte instructos initus, in quo unusquisque suae Communionis doctrinam profundius explicat eiusque characteres perspicue praesentat. Per hunc enim dialogum veriorem utriusque Communionis doctrinae vitaeque cognitionem et magis aequam aestimationem omnes acquirunt; tum etiam illae Communiones eam consequuntur ampliorem collaborationem in quibusvis officiis ad bonum commune ab omni conscientia christiana postulatis, et in oratione unanimi, sicubi licet, conveniunt. Denique omnes suam fidelitatem voluntati Christi circa Ecclesiam examinant atque, ut oportet, opus renovationis nec non reformationis strenue aggrediuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p264","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","It is clear, however, that the work of preparation and reconciliation of those individuals who desire full Catholic communion is by its nature distinct from the ecumenical undertaking; yet no opposition exists, since both proceed from the wonderful disposition of God.","Quae omnia, cum a fidelibus Ecclesiae catholicae sub pastorum vigilantia prudenter et patienter perficiuntur, ad bonum aequitatis et veritatis, concordiae et collaborationis, fraterni animi et unionis conferunt; ut hac via paulatim, superatis obstaculis perfectam communionem ecclesiasticam impedientibus, omnes Christiani, in una Eucharistiae celebratione, in unius unicacque Ecclesiae unitatem congregentur quam Christus ab initio Ecclesiae suae largitus est, quamque inamissibilem in Ecclesia catholica subsistere credimus et usque ad consummationem saeculi in dies crescere speramus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p265","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Catholic faithful in ecumenical action must without doubt be solicitous for their separated brothers, praying for them, communicating with them concerning things of the Church, moving the first steps to them. But first of all they themselves with sincere and attentive mind ought to consider those things which are to be renewed and done in the Catholic Family itself, that its life may more faithfully and clearly bear witness to the doctrine and institutions handed down by Christ through the Apostles.","Patet autem opus praeparationis ac reconciliationis eorum singulorum, qui plenam communionem catholicam desiderant, ab oecumenico incepto natura sua distingui; nulla tamen adest oppositio, cum utrumque ex Dei mirabili dispositione procedat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p266","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","For although the Catholic Church is endowed with all revealed truth and all the means of grace, yet her members do not live from them with all the fervor they ought, so that the face of the Church shines less to our separated brothers and to the whole world, and the growth of the kingdom of God is retarded. Therefore all Catholics ought to tend to Christian perfection and, each according to his condition, strive that the Church, bearing the humility and mortification of Jesus in her body, may be cleansed and renewed day by day, until Christ presents her to Himself glorious, not having spot or wrinkle.","Fideles catholici in actione oecumenica sine dubio de fratribus seiunctis solliciti sint oportet, pro illis orando, de rebus Ecclesiae cum illis communicando, primos gressus ad illos movendo. In primis vero ipsimet sincero attentoque animo ea perpendere debent, quae in ipsa Familia catholica renovanda et efficienda sunt, ut eius vita fidelius et clarius testimonium reddat de doctrina institutisque a Christo per Apostolos traditis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p267","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Keeping unity in necessary things, let all in the Church, according to the office given to each, preserve due liberty in the various forms of spiritual life and discipline, in the diversity of liturgical rites, indeed also in the theological elaboration of revealed truth; but in all things let them cultivate charity. For by this manner of acting they will more and more fully manifest the true catholicity and apostolicity of the Church.","Quamvis enim Ecclesia catholica omni a Deo revelata veritate et omnibus mediis gratiae ditata sit, tamen membra eius non omni quo par est fervore inde vivunt, ita ut vultus Ecclesiae fratribus a nobis seiunctis et universo mundo minus affulgeat atque regni Dei incrementum retardetur. Quapropter Catholici omnes ad perfectionem christianam tendere debent atque, pro sua quisque condicione, eniti ut Ecclesia, humilitatem et mortificationem Iesu in corpore suo portans, de die in diem mundetur et renovetur, donec Christus eam sibi exhibeat gloriosam, non habentem maculam aut rugam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p268","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","On the other hand, it is necessary that Catholics joyfully recognize and esteem the truly Christian goods, proceeding from the common patrimony, which are found among our separated brothers. To recognize the riches of Christ and the works of virtues in the life of others, who bear witness to Christ, sometimes even to the shedding of blood, is equitable and salutary: for God is always wonderful and admirable in His works.","In necessariis unitatem custodientes, omnes in Ecclesia, secundum munus unicuique datum, cum in variis formis vitae spiritualis et disciplinae, tum in diversitate liturgicorum rituum, immo et in theologica veritatis revelatae elaboratione, debitam libertatem servent; in omnibus vero caritatem colant. Hac enim agendi ratione ipsi veri nominis catholicitatem simul et apostolicitatem Ecclesiae in dies plenius manifestabunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p269","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Nor is it to be passed over, that whatever is done by the grace of the Holy Spirit in our separated brothers can also contribute to our edification. Whatever is truly Christian is never opposed to the genuine goods of faith, but always can bring it about that the mystery of Christ and the Church itself is more perfectly attained.","Ex altera parte necessarium est Catholicos cum gaudio agnoscere et aestimare bona vere christiana, a communi patrimonio promanantia, quae apud fratres a nobis seiunctos inveniuntur. Divitias Christi et virtutum opera agnoscere in vita aliorum, qui pro Christo testimonium ferunt, quandoque usque ad sanguinis effusionem, aequum et salutare est: Deus enim semper mirabilis est et mirandus in operibus suis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p270","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","Nevertheless, the divisions of Christians are an impediment to the Church, that she may bring to effect the fullness of catholicity proper to her in those sons who, indeed appointed to her by baptism, are separated from her full communion. Indeed, it becomes more difficult for the Church herself to express the fullness of catholicity in every respect in the reality of her life. This sacred Synod joyfully notices that the participation of Catholic faithful in ecumenical action is increasing day by day, and commends it to bishops everywhere, that it may be promoted skillfully and prudently directed by them.","Neque est praetereundum, quaecumque Spiritus Sancti gratia in fratribus seiunctis efficiuntur, eadem ad nostram quoque aedificationem conferre posse. Quodcumque vere christianum est, numquam germanis fidei bonis adversatur, immo semper efficere potest ut perfectius ipsum mysterium Christi et Ecclesiae attingatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p271","Chapter I. on Catholic Principles of Ecumenism","5 The solicitude for restoring union pertains to the whole Church, both to the faithful and to the pastors, and affects each according to his own virtue, whether in daily Christian life or in theological and historical investigations. This care already in a certain way manifests the fraternal union existing among all Christians, and leads to full and perfect unity according to God's benevolence.","Attamen divisiones Christianorum impedimento Ecclesiae sunt, quominus ipsa ad effectum deducat plenitudinem catholicitatis sibi propriam in iis filiis, qui sibi quidem baptismate appositi, sed a sua plena communione seiuncti sunt. Immo et pro ipsa Ecclesia difficilius fit plenitudinem catholicitatis sub omni respectu in ipsa vitae realitate exprimere. Haec Sacra Synodus cum gaudio advertit participationem fidelium catholicorum in actione oecumenica in dies augeri, eamque episcopis ubique terrarum commendat, ut eadem promoveatur sollerter, et prudenter ab ipsis dirigatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p272","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","6 Since every renewal of the Church essentially consists in increased fidelity to her own vocation, it is without doubt the reason why the movement toward unity tends. The pilgrim Church is called by Christ to this perennial reformation, which she, as a human and earthly institution, perpetually needs; so that if certain things, according to circumstances of times and places, whether in morals, or in ecclesiastical discipline, or even in the manner of enunciating doctrine (which must be sedulously distinguished from the deposit of faith itself), have been less accurately kept, they be rightly and duly restored at the opportune time. This renovation, therefore, obtains notable ecumenical importance. The various ways of life of the Church, by which this renovation is already effected—such as the biblical and liturgical movements, the preaching of the word of God and catechesis, the apostolate of the laity, new forms of religious life, the spirituality of matrimony, the doctrine and activity of the Church in social matters—are to be held as certain pledges and auspices that happily portend future progress of ecumenism.","Ad totam Ecclesiam sollicitudo unionis instaurandae spectat, tam ad fideles quam ad pastores, et unumquemque secundum propriam virtutem afficit, sive in vita christiana quotidiana sive in theologicis et historicis investigationibus. Haec cura fraternam coniunctionem inter omnes Christianos existentem iam quodammodo manifestat, atque ad plenam perfectamque unitatem secundum Dei benevolentiam conducit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p273","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","7 True ecumenism does not exist without interior conversion. For the desires of unity arise and mature from newness of mind, from self-denial, and from the most free outpouring of charity. Therefore we must implore from the divine Spirit the grace of sincere self-denial, humility, and meekness in serving, and of fraternal generosity of spirit toward others. \"I therefore, a prisoner in the Lord, beseech you that you walk worthy of the vocation in which you are called, with all humility and meekness, with patience supporting one another in charity, careful to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace\" (Eph 4:1-3). This exhortation especially regards those who are raised to the sacred order for the purpose of continuing the mission of Christ, who came not to be ministered unto but to minister (Mt 20:28). The testimony of St. John also holds good concerning faults against unity: \"If we say that we have not sinned, we make Him a liar, and His word is not in us\" (1 Jn 1:10). Therefore with humble prayer we ask pardon from God and from our separated brothers, just as we forgive our debtors.","Cum omnis renovatio Ecclesiae essentialiter in aucta fidelitate erga vocationem eius consistat, ea procul dubio ratio est cur motus versus unitatem contendat. Ecclesia in via peregrinans vocatur a Christo ad hanc perennem reformationem qua ipsa, qua humanum terrenumque institutum, perpetuo indiget; ita ut si quae, pro rerum temporumque adiunctis, sive in moribus, sive in ecclesiastica disciplina, sive etiam in doctrinae enuntiandae modo — qui ab ipso deposito fidei sedulo distingui debet — minus accurate servata fuerint, opportuno tempore recte debiteque instaurentur. Haec igitur renovatio insigne obtinet momentum oecumenicum. Varii autem illi modi vitae Ecclesiae, per quos haec renovatio iam efficitur — ut sunt motus biblicus et liturgicus, praedicatio verbi Dei atque catechesis, laicorum apostolatus, vitae religiosae novae formae, matrimonii spiritualitas, doctrina et activitas Ecclesiae in re sociali — tamquam pignora et auspicia quaedam sunt habenda, quae futuros oecumenismi progressus fauste portendunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p274","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","Let all Christ's faithful be mindful that they promote, indeed exercise, the union of Christians the better, the purer life they strive to lead according to the Gospel. For by how much the more intimately they are united with the Father, the Word, and the Spirit, by so much the more intimately and easily they will be able to increase mutual fraternity.","Oecumenismus veri nominis sine interiore conversione non datur. Etenim ex novitate mentis, ex sui ipsius abnegatione atque ex caritatis liberrima effusione proficiscuntur et maturescunt desideria unitatis. Ideo a Spiritu divino imploranda nobis est gratia sincerae abnegationis, humilitatis et mansuetudinis in serviendo, atque fraternae in alios animi liberalitatis. Obsecro itaque vos, ait Apostolus gentium, ego vinctus in Domino, ut digne ambuletis vocatione, qua vocati estis, cum omni humilitate et mansuetudine, cum patientia supportantes invicem in caritate, solliciti servare unitatem Spiritus in vinculo pacis (Ep 4,1-3). Exhortatio haec illos praesertim spectat, qui ad sacrum ordinem eo consilio evecti sunt, ut continuetur missio Christi, qui inter nos non venit ministrari, sed ministrare (Mt 20,28). De culpis etiam adversus unitatem valet testimonium S. Ioannis: Si dixerimus quoniam non peccavimus, mendacem facimus eum, et verbum eius non est in nobis (1Jn 1,10). Humili igitur prece veniam petimus a Deo et a fratribus seiunctis, sicut et nos dimittimus debitoribus nostris."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p275","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","8 This conversion of heart and holiness of life, together with private and public supplications for the unity of Christians, are to be esteemed as the soul of the whole ecumenical movement, and can be rightly called spiritual ecumenism.","Meminerint omnes christifideles se Christianorum unionem eo melius promovere, immo exercere, quo puriorem secundum Evangelium vitam degere studeant. Quo enim arctiore communione cum Patre, Verbo et Spiritu unientur, eo intimius atque facilius mutuam fraternitatem augere valebunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p276","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","For it is solemn for Catholics frequently to meet for that prayer for the unity of the Church, which the Savior Himself on the eve of His death asked from the Father most fervently: \"that all may be one\" (Jn 17:21). In certain peculiar circumstances of matters, such as the prayers which are indicated for unity, and in ecumenical gatherings, it is lawful and indeed desirable that Catholics join in prayer with separated brothers. Common prayers of this kind are a most effective means of obtaining the grace of unity, and a genuine signification of the bonds by which Catholics are still joined with separated brothers: \"For where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them\" (Mt 18:20).","Haec cordis conversio vitaeque sanctitas, una cum privatis et publicis supplicationibus pro Christianorum unitate, tamquam anima totius motus oecumenici existimandae sunt et merito oecumenismus spiritualis nuncupari possunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p277","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","Nevertheless, communication in sacred things is not to be considered as a means indiscriminately to be used for restoring the unity of Christians. This communication depends chiefly on two principles: on signifying the unity of the Church, and on participation in the means of grace. The signification of unity most often forbids communication. The procuring of grace sometimes commends it. Concerning the concrete manner of acting, all circumstances of times, places, and persons having been attended to, let the local episcopal authority prudently decide, unless something else is decided by the episcopal conference according to its own statutes, or by the Holy See.","Sollemne enim est Catholicis frequenter ad illam orationem convenire pro Ecclesiae unitate, quam Salvator Ipse in pervigilio mortis a Patre flagranter precatus est: ut omnes unum sint (Jn 17,21). In quibusdam peculiaribus rerum adiunctis, cuiusmodi sunt precationes quae pro unitate indicuntur, atque in oecumenicis conventibus licitum est immo et optandum, ut Catholici cum fratribus seiunctis in oratione consocientur. Communes eiusmodi preces perefficax sane medium sunt gratiae unitatis impetrandae et genuina significatio vinculorum, quibus Catholici cum fratribus seiunctis adhuc coniunguntur: Ubi enim sunt duo vel tres congregati in nomine meo, ibi sum in medio eorum (Mt 18,20)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p278","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","9 It is necessary to know the mind of our separated brothers. To this end a study is necessarily required, which must be carried out according to truth and with a benevolent spirit. Catholics, duly prepared, must acquire a better knowledge of the doctrine and history, the spiritual and cultual life, the religious psychology and culture, which is proper to the brothers. To accomplish this, meetings from each side for treating especially theological questions, where each acts as an equal with an equal, provided that those who take part in them are truly skilled under the vigilance of the prelates, greatly help. From such dialogue the condition of the Catholic Church will also become more clearly known. By this way the mind of our separated brothers will be better known and our faith more aptly set forth to them.","Attamen communicationem in sacris considerare non licet velut medium indiscretim adhibendum ad Christianorum unitatem restaurandam. Quae communicatio a duobus principiis praecipue pendet: ab unitate Ecclesiae significanda, et a participatione in mediis gratiae. Significatio unitatis plerumque vetat communicationem. Gratia procuranda quandoque illam commendat. De modo concreto agendi, attentis omnibus circumstantiis temporum, locorum et personarum, prudenter decernat auctoritas episcopalis localis, nisi aliud a conferentia episcopali, ad normam propriorum statutorum, vel a Sancta Sede statuatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p279","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","10 The institutions of sacred theology and other disciplines, especially historical, must be taught also under an ecumenical aspect, that they may correspond ever more accurately to the truth of things. For it is of great interest that future pastors and priests excel in theology thus accurately elaborated, not polemically, especially in those things which concern the relations of separated brothers toward the Catholic Church. For from the formation of priests the necessary instruction and spiritual formation of the faithful and religious mostly depends. Also Catholics engaged in missionary work in the same lands as other Christians today especially ought to know the questions and fruits which arise from ecumenism in their apostolate.","Fratrum seiunctorum animum cognoscere oportet. Ad hoc necessario requiritur studium, quod secundum veritatem et animo benevolo peragendum est. Catholici debito modo praeparati acquirant necesse est meliorem cognitionem doctrinae et historiae, vitae spiritualis et cultualis, psychologiae religiosae et culturae, quae fratribus propria est. Ad id assequendum multum iuvant ex utraque parte conventus ad theologicas praesertim quaestiones tractandas, ubi unusquisque par cum pari agat, dummodo qui in illis partem habent sub praesulum vigilantia vere periti sint. Ex tali dialogo etiam clarius innotescet, quae sit revera Ecclesiae catholicae condicio. Hac via et seiunctorum fratrum mens melius cognoscetur eisque fides nostra aptius exponetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p280","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","11 The manner and way of expressing the catholic faith must by no means become an obstacle to dialogue with the brothers. The whole doctrine must be clearly set forth absolutely. Nothing is so alien from ecumenism as that false irenicism by which the purity of catholic doctrine suffers detriment and its genuine and certain sense is obscured. At the same time, the catholic faith is to be explained more profoundly and rightly, in a manner and language which can also be truly understood by separated brothers.","Sacrae theologiae institutiones et aliae disciplinae praesertim historicae tradantur oportet etiam sub aspectu oecumenico, ut usque accuratius rerum veritati respondeant. Multum enim interest futuros pastores et sacerdotes pollere theologia hoc modo adamussim elaborata, non polemice, in iis imprimis, quae fratrum seiunctorum erga Ecclesiam catholicam relationes spectant. A sacerdotum enim formatione necessaria institutio ac formatio spiritualis fidelium et religiosorum quam maxime pendet. Etiam Catholici in missionalia opera in iisdem terris ac alii Christiani incumbentes hodie praesertim cognoscant oportet quaestiones et fructus, quae ex oecumenismo in eorum apostolatu oriuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p281","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","Moreover, in ecumenical dialogue, catholic theologians, adhering to the doctrine of the Church, investigating the divine mysteries together with separated brothers, must proceed with love of truth, charity, and humility. In comparing doctrines, let them be mindful that there exists an order or hierarchy of truths of catholic doctrine, since their connection with the foundation of the Christian faith is different. Thus the way will be paved by which, through this fraternal emulation, all are incited to deeper knowledge and clearer manifestation of the unsearchable riches of Christ.","Modus ac ratio fidem catholicam exprimendi nullatenus obstaculum fieri debet dialogo cum fratribus. Integra doctrina lucide exponatur omnino oportet. Nil ab oecumenismo tam alienum est quam ille falsus irenismus, quo puritas doctrinae catholicae detrimentum patitur et eius sensus genuinus et certus obscuratur. Simul fides catholica et profundius et rectius explicanda est, modo et sermone qui etiam a fratribus seiunctis possit vere comprehendi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p282","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","12 Before all nations, let all Christians profess faith in the one and triune God, in the incarnate Son of God, our Redeemer and Lord, and with common effort, in mutual esteem, bear witness to our hope, which does not confound. Since in modern times cooperation in social matters is most widely established, absolutely all men are called to common work, but especially those who believe in God, and most of all all Christians as marked with the name of Christ. The cooperation of all Christians vividly expresses that union by which they are already joined together among themselves, and sets the face of the servant of Christ in fuller light. This cooperation, already established in not a few nations, must be more and more perfected, especially in regions where social or technical development is accomplished, whether in rightly estimating the dignity of the human person, in promoting the good of peace, in pursuing the social application of the Gospel, in advancing sciences and arts in a Christian spirit, or also in applying remedies of any kind against the afflictions of our age, such as famine and calamities, illiteracy and poverty, shortage of housing and unequal distribution of goods. By this cooperation all who believe in Christ can easily learn how others can better know and esteem one another, and the way to the unity of Christians is paved.","Insuper in dialogo oecumenico theologi catholici, doctrinae Ecclesiae inhaerentes, una cum fratribus seiunctis investigationem peragentes de divinis mysteriis, cum veritatis amore, caritate et humilitate progredi debent. In comparandis doctrinis meminerint existere ordinem seu hierarchiam veritatum doctrinae catholicae, cum diversus sit earum nexus cum fundamento fidei christianae. Sic via sternetur qua per fraternam hanc aemulationem omnes incitentur ad profundiorem cognitionem et clariorem manifestationem investigabilium divitiarum Christi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p283","Chapter II. on the Practice of Ecumenism","13 We turn our eyes to the two principal categories of schisms affecting the seamless tunic of Christ. The first of these occurred in the East, either by the contestation of dogmatic formulas of the Councils of Ephesus and Chalcedon, or, at a later time, by the dissolution of ecclesiastical communion between the Eastern Patriarchates and the Roman See. Others, then, after more than four centuries, arose in the West from the events which commonly come under the name of the Reformation. From then on, several Communities, whether national or confessional, were separated from the Roman See. Among these, in which traditions and catholic structures continue partly to subsist, the Anglican Communion holds a special place. These diverse divisions, however, differ greatly among themselves not only by reason of origin, place, and time, but especially by the nature and gravity of questions pertaining to faith and ecclesiastical structure.","Coram omnibus gentibus Christiani universi fidem in Deum unum et trinum, in Filium Dei incarnatum, Redemptorem et Dominum nostrum profiteantur et communi conatu in mutua aestimatione testimonium reddant spei nostrae, quae non confundit. Cum hodiernis temporibus in re sociali cooperatio latissime instauretur, omnes prorsus homines ad communem operam vocantur, potiore vero ratione ii, qui in Deum credunt, maxime autem omnes Christiani utpote Christi nomine insigniti. Omnium Christianorum cooperatio coniunctionem illam qua iam inter se uniuntur vivo modo exprimit atque Christi servi vultum in pleniorem lucem ponit. Cooperatio haec, in non paucis nationibus iam instaurata, magis magisque perficiatur oportet, in regionibus praesertim, ubi evolutio socialis vel technica peragitur, sive in dignitate personae humanae recte aestimanda, sive in bono pacis promovendo, sive in sociali Evangelii applicatione prosequenda, sive in scientiis et artibus spiritu christiano provehendis, sive etiam in cuiusvis generis remediis adhibendis contra nostrae aetatis aerumnas, cuiusmodi sunt fames et calamitates, analphabetismus et inopia, penuria habitationum et non acqua bonorum distributio. Hac cooperatione omnes qui in Christum credunt facile addiscere possunt, quomodo alii alios melius cognoscere et pluris aestimare queant atque ad unitatem Christianorum via sternatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p284","Chapter III. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated From the Roman Apos","Wherefore, this sacred Synod, neither despising the diverse conditions of the various Christian groups, nor passing over the surviving bonds between them, notwithstanding division, has decided to propose the following considerations for the prudent exercise of ecumenical action.","Ad duas praecipuas scissionum categorias inconsutilem tunicam Christi afficientium oculos convertimus. Primae earum in Oriente evenerunt, sive contestatione dogmaticarum formularum Conciliorum Ephesini et Chalcedonensis, sive, posteriore tempore, per solutionem ecclesiasticae communionis inter Patriarchatus orientales et Sedem Romanam. Aliae dein, post amplius quattuor saecula, in Occidente ortae sunt ex eventibus qui sub nomine Reformationis communiter veniunt. Exinde a Sede Romana plures Communiones sive nationales sive confessionales seiunctae sunt. Inter eas, in quibus traditiones et structurae catholicae ex parte subsistere pergunt, locum specialem tenet Communio anglicana. Hae tamen diversae divisiones inter se valde differunt non solum ratione originis, loci et temporis, sed praesertim natura et gravitate quaestionum ad fidem et structuram ecclesiasticam pertinentium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p285","Chapter III. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated From the Roman Apos","14 The Churches of the East and the West followed their own proper way for not a few centuries, yet joined by fraternal communion of faith and sacramental life, the Roman See moderating common consent, if dissensions concerning faith or discipline arose between them. It is pleasing to the sacred Synod, among other grave matters, to call to mind for all that several particular or local churches flourish in the East, among which the Patriarchal Churches hold the first place, and not a few of them glory to have origin from the Apostles themselves. Hence among the Easterns the solicitude and care of preserving those fraternal relations in the communion of faith and charity, which must flourish between local churches as between sisters, has prevailed and prevails. It is not to be passed over that the Churches of the East from the beginning have a treasure, from which the Church of the West has drawn many things in liturgical matters, in spiritual tradition, and in juridical order. Nor is it to be made little of that the fundamental dogmas of the Christian faith concerning the Trinity and the Word of God, incarnate of the Virgin Mary, were defined in ecumenical councils celebrated in the East. To preserve this faith, those Churches have suffered and suffer many things. The heritage handed down from the Apostles has been accepted in diverse forms and ways, and from the very beginnings of the Church has been variously explained here and there on account of the diversity of genius and conditions of life. All which, besides external causes, on account also of the defect of mutual understanding and charity, have furnished an occasion for separations.","Quam ob rem, haec Sancta Synodus, nec condiciones diversas diversorum Coetuum christianorum parvipendens, neque superstites, non obstante divisione, nexus inter eos praeteriens, ad prudentem actionem occumenicam exercendam sequentes considerationes proponere decernit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p286","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","Wherefore the sacred Synod exhorts all, but especially those who intend to strain for the restoration of the desired full communion between the Eastern Churches and the Catholic Church, that they have due consideration for this peculiar condition of the nascent and growing Churches of the East and for the nature of the relations which flourished between them and the Roman See before the separation, and form for themselves a right estimation concerning all these things. These things accurately kept will most greatly contribute to the intended dialogue.","Ecclesiae Orientis et Occidentis per non pauca saecula suam propriam viam, fraterna tamen communione fidei et vitae sacramentalis coniunctae, secutae sunt, Sede Romana moderante communi consensu, si dissensiones circa fidem vel disciplinam inter eas orirentur. Sacrosanctae Synodo gratum est, inter cetera gravis momenti, omnibus in mentem revocare plures in Oriente florere particulares seu locales Ecclesias, inter quas primum locum tenent Ecclesiae Patriarchales, et ex quibus non paucae ab ipsis Apostolis ortum habere gloriantur. Proinde apud Orientales praevaluit atque praevalet sollicitudo et cura servandi fraternas illas in fidei caritatisque communione necessitudines, quae inter Ecclesias locales, ut inter sorores, vigere debent. Praetermittendum pariter non est Ecclesias Orientis ab origine habere thesaurum, ex quo plura in rebus liturgicis, in traditione spirituali et in ordine iuridico Ecclesia Occidentis deprompsit. Neque illud parvi faciendum est fundamentalia dogmata christianae fidei de Trinitate et de Verbo Dei, ex Virgine Maria incarnato, in Conciliis Oecumenicis in Oriente celebratis definita esse. Ad hanc fidem servandam illae Ecclesiae multa et passae sunt et patiuntur. Tradita autem ab Apostolis haereditas diversis formis et modis acceptata est et inde ab ipsis Ecclesiae primordiis hic et illic varie explicata ob diversitatem quoque ingenii et vitae condicionum. Quae omnia, praeter causas externas, propter defectum etiam mutuae comprehensionis et caritatis separationibus ansam praebuerunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p287","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","15 It is also known to all with how great love Eastern Christians perform sacred liturgical actions, especially the eucharistic celebration, the source of the life of the Church and the pledge of future glory, through which the faithful, united with the bishop, having access to God the Father through the Son the incarnate Word, suffering and glorified, in the outpouring of the Holy Spirit, attain communion with the most holy Trinity, made partakers of the divine nature (2 Pet 1:4). Hence through the celebration of the Eucharist of the Lord in each of these Churches, the Church of God is built up and grows, and through concelebration their communion is manifested. In this liturgical cult, the Easterns magnify the ever-Virgin Mary, whom the Ecumenical Council of Ephesus solemnly proclaimed the most holy Mother of God, that truly and properly Christ, the Son of God and the Son of Man, according to the Scriptures, might be acknowledged, with most beautiful hymns, and they also praise many saints, among whom the Fathers of the universal Church.","Quamobrem Sacrosancta Synodus omnes quidem, sed praesertim eos exhortatur qui in instaurationem plenae communionis optatae inter Ecclesias orientales et Ecclesiam catholicam incumbere intendunt, ut debitam considerationem habeant de hac peculiari condicione nascentium crescentiumque Ecclesiarum Orientis et de indole relationum, quae inter eas et Sedem Romanam ante separationem vigebant atque rectam de his omnibus existimationem sibi efforment. Haec accurate servata ad dialogum intentum summopere conferent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p288","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","Since, however, those Churches, although separated, have true sacraments, especially, by virtue of apostolic succession, the priesthood and the Eucharist, by which they are still joined to us by a most close relationship, a certain communication in sacred things, given opportune circumstances and the ecclesiastical authority approving, is not only possible but also to be recommended.","Omnibus quoque notum est quanto cum amore Christiani orientales liturgica Sacra peragant, praesertim celebrationem eucharisticam, fontem vitae Ecclesiae et pignus futurae gloriae, qua fideles cum episcopo uniti accessum ad Deum Patrem habentes per Filium Verbum incarnatum, passum et glorificatum, in effusione Sancti Spiritus, communionem cum Sanctissima Trinitate consequuntur, divinae consortes naturae (2P 1,4) effecti. Proinde per celebrationem Eucharistiae Domini in his singulis Ecclesiis, Ecclesia Dei aedificatur et crescit, et per concelebrationem communio earum manifestatur. In cultu hoc liturgico Mariam semper Virginem, quam Oecumenica Synodus Ephesina sollemniter Deiparam Sanctissimam proclamavit ut vere et proprie Christus Filius Dei et Filius Hominis secundum Scripturas agnosceretur, Orientales pulcherrimis hymnis magnificant et multos quoque Sanctos, inter quos Patres universalis Ecclesiae, collaudant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p289","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","In the East also are found the riches of those spiritual traditions, which especially monasticism expressed. For there from the glorious times of the holy Fathers, that monastic spirituality flourished, which afterward flowed to the Western parts, and from which the religious institute of the Latins, as from its source, derived its origin and afterwards repeatedly received new vigor. Wherefore it is strongly recommended that Catholics more frequently approach these spiritual riches of the Eastern Fathers, which lift up the whole man to the contemplation of divine things. Let all know that to know, venerate, preserve, and foster the most rich liturgical and spiritual patrimony of the Easterns is of the greatest importance for faithfully preserving the fullness of the Christian tradition and for perfecting the reconciliation of Eastern and Western Christians.","Cum autem illae Ecclesiae, quamvis seiunctae, vera sacramenta habeant, praecipue vero, vi successionis apostolicae, Sacerdotium et Eucharistiam, quibus arctissima necessitudine adhuc nobiscum coniunguntur, quaedam communicatio in sacris, datis opportunis circumstantiis et approbante auctoritate ecclesiastica, non solum possibilis est sed etiam suadetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p290","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","16 Moreover, from the first times the Churches of the East followed their own disciplines, sanctioned by the holy Fathers and Synods, even ecumenical. Since, however, a certain diversity of customs and practices does not stand against the unity of the Church, indeed increases its beauty and contributes not a little to fulfilling its mission, as is mentioned above, the sacred Synod, to remove every doubt, declares that the Churches of the East, mindful of the necessary unity of the whole Church, have the faculty of governing themselves according to their own disciplines, as being more congruent to the character of their faithful and more suitable for providing for the good of souls. The perfect observance of this traditional principle, not always indeed kept, pertains to those things which are altogether required as a condition for restoring union.","In Oriente quoque inveniuntur divitiae illarum traditionum spiritualium, quas praesertim monachismus expressit. Ibi enim inde a gloriosis Sanctorum Patrum temporibus floruit spiritualitas illa monastica, quae dein ad occidentales partes manavit et ex qua religiosum latinorum institutum tamquam e suo fonte originem duxit ac deinceps novum vigorem identidem accepit. Quapropter enixe commendatur ut Catholici frequentius accedant ad has spirituales Patrum Orientalium divitias quae hominem totum ad divina contemplanda evehunt. Ditissimum Orientalium patrimonium liturgicum et spirituale cognoscere, venerari, conservare et fovere omnes sciant maximi esse momenti ad plenitudinem traditionis christianae fideliter custodiendam et ad reconciliationem orientalium et occidentalium Christianorum perficiendam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p291","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","17 What has been said above concerning legitimate diversity, it is pleasing also to declare concerning the diverse theological enunciation of doctrines. For in the investigation of revealed truth, diverse methods and steps toward knowing and confessing God have been used in the East and in the West. Hence it is no wonder that certain aspects of the revealed mystery are sometimes more fittingly perceived and brought into better light by one than by the other, so that then those various theological formulas are often to be said to complement one another rather than to oppose. Concerning the authentic theological traditions of the Easterns, it is to be recognized that they are rooted in an excellent way in the sacred Scriptures, fostered and expressed by the liturgical life, nourished by the living apostolic tradition and the writings of the Eastern Fathers and spiritual authors, and tend to right ordering of life, indeed to the full contemplation of Christian truth. This sacred Synod, giving thanks to God that many Eastern sons of the Catholic Church who preserve this patrimony and wish to live it more purely and more fully already live in full communion with their brothers cultivating the Western tradition, declares that this whole spiritual and liturgical, disciplinary and theological patrimony in its diverse traditions pertains to the full catholicity and apostolicity of the Church.","Praeterea a primis iam temporibus Ecclesiae Orientis disciplinas proprias a Sanctis Patribus atque Synodis, etiam Oecumenicis, sancitas sequebantur. Cum autem unitati Ecclesiae minime obstet, immo decorem eius augeat et ad missionem eius implendam non parum conferat quaedam morum consuetudinumque diversitas, uti supra memoratur, Sacra Synodus, ad omne dubium tollendum, declarat Ecclesias Orientis, memores necessariae unitatis totius Ecclesiae, facultatem habere se secundum proprias disciplinas regendi, utpote indoli suorum fidelium magis congruas atque bono animorum consulendo aptiores. Perfecta huius traditionalis principii observantia, non semper quidem servata, ad ea pertinet quae ad unionem restaurandam tamquam praevia condicio omnino requiruntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p292","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","18 All these things having been well considered, this sacred Synod renews what has been declared by the sacred councils of the past and also by the Roman Pontiffs, namely that to restore or preserve communion and unity nothing more is necessary than to impose... a burden beyond... what is necessary (Acts 15:28). It also most strongly desires that for gradually attaining it all efforts henceforth intend in various institutes and forms of the life of the Church, especially by prayer and fraternal dialogue concerning doctrine and the more urgent needs of pastoral office in our age. In the same way, it commends to the pastors and faithful of the Catholic Church relations with those who live no longer in the East but far from their homeland, that fraternal collaboration with them in the spirit of charity and all spirit of contentious emulation being removed may grow. But if this work be promoted with whole spirit, the sacred Synod hopes that, the wall dividing the Eastern and Western Church being removed, there may at last become one mansion founded on the cornerstone, Christ Jesus, who will make both one.","Quae supra de legitima diversitate dicta sunt, eadem placet etiam de diversa theologica doctrinarum enuntiatione declarare. Etenim in veritatis revelatae exploratione methodi gressusque diversi ad divina cognoscenda et confitenda in Oriente et in Occidente adhibiti sunt. Unde mirum non est quosdam aspectus mysterii revelati quandoque magis congrue percipi et in meliorem lucem poni ab uno quam ab altero, ita ut tunc variae illae theologicae formulae non raro potius inter se compleri dicendae sint quam opponi. Ad traditiones theologicas authenticas Orientalium quod attinet, agnoscendum est eas eximio quidem modo in Sacris Scripturis radicatas esse, vita liturgica foveri et exprimi, viva apostolica traditione scriptisque Orientalium Patrum ac spiritualium auctorum nutriri, ad rectam vitae institutionem, immo ad christianam veritatem plene contemplandam tendere. Haec Sancta Synodus, gratias agens Deo quod multi orientales Ecclesiae catholicae filii, qui hoc patrimonium custodiunt et illud purius pleniusque vivere cupiunt, iam cum fratribus traditionem occidentalem colentibus in plena communione vivunt, declarat, totum hoc patrimonium spirituale ac liturgicum, disciplinare ac theologicum in diversis suis traditionibus ad plenam catholicitatem et apostolicitatem Ecclesiae pertinere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p293","I. on the Peculiar Consideration of the Eastern Churches","19 The Churches and ecclesial Communities which, either in that most grave crisis of affairs which began in the West from the end of the Middle Ages, or in later times, have been separated from the Roman Apostolic See, are joined with the Catholic Church by a peculiar affinity and relationship on account of the long life of the Christian people in past centuries spent in ecclesiastical communion. Since, however, these Churches and ecclesial Communities, on account of the diversity of origin, doctrine, and spiritual life, differ not a little from us, but also among themselves, it is a most difficult work to describe them equally, which we do not intend to attempt here. Although the ecumenical movement and the desire for peace with the Catholic Church have not yet prevailed everywhere, there is hope for us that in all the ecumenical sense and mutual esteem may gradually grow. Yet it must be recognized that between these Churches and Communities and the Catholic Church there are weighty discrepancies, not only of historical, sociological, psychological, cultural nature, but especially of the interpretation of revealed truth. So that, notwithstanding those differences, dialogue may be more easily established, in what follows we wish to bring forth certain things which can and ought to be the foundation and incitement of this dialogue.","His omnibus bene perspectis, haec Sacrosancta Synodus renovat id quod a Sacris praeteritis Conciliis nec non a Romanis Pontificibus declaratum est, nempe ad communionem et unitatem restaurandam vel servandam opus esse nihil ultra imponere... oneris quam... necessaria ( I5, 28). Vehementer etiam exoptat ut ad eam paulatim consequendam omnes conatus exinde intendant in variis institutis et formis vitae Ecclesiae, praesertim oratione et fraterno dialogo circa doctrinam et muneris pastoralis urgentiores aetatis nostrae necessitates. Eodem modo pastoribus et fidelibus Ecclesiae catholicae commendat necessitudines cum eis qui non iam in Oriente, sed procul a patria vitam degunt, ut fraterna collaboratio cum eis in spiritu caritatis et secluso omni spiritu contentiosae aemulationis crescat. Quod si hoc opus toto animo promoveatur, Sacrosancta Synodus sperat fore, ut sublato pariete occidentalem orientalemque Ecclesiam dividente, unica tandem fiat mansio angulari firmata lapide, Christo Iesu, qui faciet utraque unum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p294","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","20 Our mind especially regards those Christians who openly confess Jesus Christ as God and Lord and the one mediator between God and men, to the glory of the one God, Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. We know indeed that there are not light discrepancies from the doctrine of the Catholic Church even concerning Christ the incarnate Word of God and the work of redemption, consequently concerning the mystery and ministry of the Church and the office of Mary in the work of salvation. Yet we rejoice seeing our separated brothers intend Christ as the source and center of ecclesiastical communion. Touched with the desire of union with Christ, they are compelled to seek unity more and more, and also to bear witness to their faith everywhere among the nations.","Ecclesiae et Communitates ecclesiales, quae vel in gravissimo illo rerum discrimine, quod in Occidente iam ab exeunte medio aevo initium sumpsit, vel posterioribus temporibus ab Apostolica Sede Romana separatae sunt, cum Ecclesia catholica peculiari affinitate ac necessitudine iunguntur ob diuturnam populi christiani vitam praeteritis saeculis in ecclesiastica communione peractam. Cum hae vero Ecclesiae et Communitates ecclesiales propter diversitatem originis, doctrinae et vitae spiritualis non tantum a nobis, sed etiam inter se non parum differant, eas aeque describere opus perdifficile est, quod hic aggredi non intendimus. Quamvis motus oecumenicus et desiderium pacis cum Ecclesia catholica nondum ubique invaluerint, spes nobis est fore ut in omnibus sensus oecumenicus et mutua aestimatio paulatim crescant. Attamen agnoscendum est inter has Ecclesias et Communitates atque Ecclesiam catholicam magni ponderis discrepantias adesse, non tantum indolis historicae, sociologicae, psychologicae, culturalis, sed imprimis interpretationis revelatae veritatis. Quo autem facilius, non obstantibus illis differentiis, dialogus oecumenicus instaurari possit, in sequentibus quaedam efferre volumus quae fundamentum huius dialogi atque incitamentum esse possunt ac debent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p295","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","21 The love and veneration, indeed almost cult, of the sacred Scriptures leads our brothers to constant and skillful study of sacred page: for \"the Gospel is the power of God for salvation to every believer, to the Jew first and to the Greek\" (Rom 1:16). Invoking the Holy Spirit, they seek God in the sacred Scriptures themselves as speaking to them in Christ, foretold by the prophets, the Word of God incarnate for us. In them they contemplate the life of Christ and those things which the divine Master taught and did for the salvation of men, especially the mysteries of His death and resurrection. But since the Christians separated from us affirm the divine authority of the sacred books, they think differently—indeed diversely—from us concerning the relation between Scripture and the Church, in which according to catholic faith the authentic magisterium holds a special place in expounding and preaching the written word of God. Nevertheless, the sacred Scriptures are excellent instruments in the powerful hand of God for attaining that unity which the Savior offers to all men.","Mens nostra eos imprimis Christianos respicit, qui Iesum Christum ut Deum ac Dominum et unicum mediatorem inter Deum et homines palam confitentur ad unius gloriam Dei, Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti. Non leves scimus quidem existere discrepantias a doctrina catholicae Ecclesiae etiam de Christo Verbo Dei incarnato et de opere redemptionis, proinde de mysterio ministerioque Ecclesiae et munere Mariae in opere salutis. Laetamur tamen videntes fratres seiunctos in Christum tamquam fontem et centrum communionis ecclesiasticae intendere. Desiderio tacti unionis cum Christo compelluntur ad unitatem magis magisque quaerendam atque etiam ad testimonium fidei suae ubique apud gentes reddendum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p296","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","22 By the sacrament of baptism, whenever it is conferred rightly according to the Lord's institution and received with due disposition of mind, a man is truly incorporated into the crucified and glorified Christ, and is regenerated to the participation of divine life according to the Apostle: \"Buried with him in baptism, in which you also rose again by the faith of the operation of God, who raised him from the dead\" (Col 2:12). Baptism therefore constitutes a sacramental bond of unity existing among all who are regenerated through it. Nevertheless, baptism of itself is only a beginning and an exordium, since it tends wholly to acquire the fullness of life in Christ. Therefore baptism is ordered to the whole profession of faith, to the whole incorporation into the institution of salvation, as Christ Himself willed it, to the whole insertion into eucharistic communion finally.","Sacrarum Scripturarum amor et veneratio ac prope cultus fratres nostros ad constans et sollers Sacrae Paginae studium adducunt: Evangelium virtus enim Dei est in salutem omni credenti, Iudaeo prinum et Graeco (Rm 1,16). Spiritum Sanctum invocantes, in ipsis Sacris Scripturis Deum inquirunt quasi sibi loquentem in Christo, a Prophetis praenuntiato, Verbo Dei pro nobis incarnato. In iis vitam Christi contemplantur et ea quae Divinus Magister ad salutem hominum docuit et peregit, mysteria praesertim mortis eius et resurrectionis. At cum Christiani a nobis seiuncti auctoritatem divinam Sacrorum Librorum affirmant, aliter ac nos — diversi quidem diverse — sentiunt de habitudine Scripturas inter et Ecclesiam, in qua secundum fidem catholicam magisterium authenticum peculiarem obtinet locum in verbo Dei scripto exponendo et praedicando. Nihilominus Sacra Eloquia in ipso dialogo eximia sunt instrumenta in potenti manu Dei ad illam unitatem adipiscendam, quam Salvator omnibus hominibus exhibet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p297","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","The ecclesial Communities separated from us, although the full unity with us flowing from baptism is lacking in them, and although we believe that they, especially on account of the defect of the sacrament of Order, have not preserved the genuine and whole substance of the eucharistic mystery, yet when they make the memory of the Lord's death and resurrection in the Holy Supper, they profess that life in communion with Christ is signified, and await His glorious coming. Therefore the doctrine concerning the Lord's Supper, the other sacraments and cult, and the ministries of the Church ought to become the object of dialogue.","Baptismi sacramento, quandocumque iuxta Domini institutionem rite confertur ac debita animi dispositione accipitur, homo vere Christo crucifixo et glorificato incorporatur atque ad vitae divinae consortium regeneratur iuxta illud Apostoli: consepulti ei in baptismo, in quo et resurrexistis per fidem operationis Dei, qui suscitavit illum a mortuis (Col 2,12). Baptismus igitur vinculum unitatis sacramentale constituit vigens inter omnes qui per illum regenerati sunt. Attamen baptismus per se dumtaxat initium et exordium est, quippe qui totus in acquirendam tendit plenitudinem vitae in Christo. Itaque baptismus ordinatur ad integram fidei professionem, ad integram incorporationem in salutis institutum, prout ipse Christus illud voluit, ad integram denique in communionem eucharisticam insertionem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p298","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","23 The Christian life of these brothers is nourished by faith in Christ, fostered by the grace of baptism and the hearing of the word of God. It is manifested indeed in private prayer, in biblical meditation, in the life of the Christian family, in the cult of the community gathered for the praise of God. Moreover, their cult sometimes bears conspicuous elements of the common ancient liturgy. The faith by which Christ is believed produces fruit in praise and thanksgiving for benefits divinely received; there is added a living sense of justice and sincere charity toward neighbor. This active faith has also produced not a few institutions for relieving spiritual and corporal misery, for cultivating the education of youth, for making social conditions of life more humane, for universally establishing peace.","Communitates ecclesiales a nobis seiunctae, quamvis deficiat earum plena nobiscum unitas ex baptismate profluens, et quamvis credamus illas, praesertim propter sacramenti Ordinis defectum, genuinam atque integram substantiam Mysterii eucharistici non servasse, tamen, dum in Sancta Coena mortis et resurrectionis Domini memoriam faciunt, vitam in Christi communione significari profitentur atque gloriosum Eius adventum exspectant. Quapropter doctrina circa Coenam Domini, cetera sacramenta et cultum ac Ecclesiae ministeria obiectum dialogi constituat oportet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p299","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","But if among Christians many do not always understand the Gospel in the same way as Catholics in moral matters, nor admit the same solutions of the more difficult questions of modern society, nevertheless they wish, like us, to adhere to the word of Christ as the source of Christian virtue, and to obey the apostolic precept: \"All whatsoever you do in word or in work, all things in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ, giving thanks to God and the Father through him\" (Col 3:17). Hence ecumenical dialogue concerning the moral application of the Gospel can begin from this.","Christiana horum fratrum conversatio fide in Christum alitur, baptismatis gratia et verbo Dei audito fovetur. Manifestatur quidem in oratione privata, in meditatione biblica, in familiae christianae vita, in cultu communitatis ad laudem Dei congregatae. Ceteroquin cultus eorum nonnumquam elementa conspicua communis antiquae liturgiae prae se fert. Fides qua Christo creditur fructus edit in laude et gratiarum actione pro beneficiis divinitus acceptis; accedit vivus iustitiae sensus et sincera caritas in proximum. Haec autem operosa fides haud pauca etiam instituta ad miseriam spiritualem et corporalem sublevandam, ad iuventutis educationem excolendam, ad sociales vitae condiciones humaniores reddendas, ad pacem universim constabiliendam protulit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p300","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","24 Thus, having briefly set forth the conditions in which it happens that ecumenical action is exercised, and the principles by which it is to be moderated, we confidently turn our eyes to the future. This sacred Synod exhorts the faithful that they abstain from any levity or imprudent zeal, which can harm the progress of unity. For their ecumenical action cannot be other than fully and sincerely catholic, namely faithful to the truth which we have received from the Apostles and the Fathers, and consonant with the faith which the Catholic Church has always professed, and at the same time tending toward that fullness in which the Lord wishes His body to be increased in the course of times.","Quod si inter Christianos multi non semper eadem ratione atque Catholici Evangelium in re morali intelligunt neque easdem solutiones difficiliorum hodiernae societatis quaestionum admittunt, nihilominus ut nos volunt verbo Christi ut fonti christianae virtutis haerere et apostolico oboedire praecepto: Omne, quodcumque facitis in verbo aut in opere, omnia in nomine Domini Iesu Christi, gratias agentes Deo et Patri per ipsum (Col 3,1 Col 3,7). Hinc dialogus oecumenicus de morali Evangelii applicatione initium sumere potest."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p301","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","25 This sacred Synod earnestly desires that the undertakings of the sons of the Catholic Church, joined with the undertakings of our separated brothers, may progress, without any obstacle being placed in the ways of Providence and without prejudice to future impulses of the Holy Spirit. Moreover, it declares itself conscious that this holy purpose of reconciling all Christians in the unity of the one and only Church of Christ exceeds human forces and gifts. Wherefore it places its hope completely in the prayer of Christ for the Church, in the love of the Father for us, in the power of the Holy Spirit. But hope does not confound, because the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts through the Holy Spirit, who is given to us (Rom 5:5).","Sic post breviter expositas condiciones, quibus exerceri contingit actio oecumenica, et principia quibus moderanda est, fidenter oculos ad futura convertimus. Haec Sacrosancta Synodus hortatur fideles, ut a quavis levitate vel imprudenti zelo se abstineant, quae vero progressui unitatis nocere possint. Eorum enim oecumenica actio non potest esse nisi plene sincereque catholica, fidelis nempe veritati, quam ab Apostolis et Patribus accepimus, et consentanea fidei, quam Ecclesia catholica semper professa est, ac simul in eam plenitudinem tendens, qua Dominus decursu temporum, Corpus Suum vult augeatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p302","II. on Churches and Ecclesial Communities Separated in the West","1 The Catholic Church holds in high esteem the institutions, liturgical rites, ecclesiastical traditions, and the discipline of Christian life of the Eastern Churches. For in them, as they are distinguished by venerable antiquity, shines that tradition which has been handed down from the Apostles through the Fathers, and which constitutes part of the divinely revealed and undivided patrimony of the universal Church. Therefore, having care for the Eastern Churches which are living witnesses of this tradition, this holy and ecumenical Synod, wishing that they may flourish and carry out with new apostolic strength the office entrusted to them, besides those things which pertain to the universal Church, has decided to decree certain points, the rest being remitted to the providence of the Eastern synods and the Apostolic See.","Haec Sacrosancta Synodus instanter exoptat ut filiorum Ecclesiae catholicae incepta cum inceptis fratrum seiunctorum coniuncta progrediantur, quin Providentiae viis ullum ponatur obstaculum et quin futuris Spiritus Sancti impulsionibus praeiudicetur. Insuper se consciam esse declarat hoc sanctum propositum reconciliandi Christianos omnes in unitate unius unicaeque Ecclesiae Christi humanas vires et dotes excedere. Quapropter spem suam in oratione Christi pro Ecclesia, in amore Patris erga nos, in virtute Spiritus Sancti penitus ponit. Spes autem non confundit, quia caritas Dei diffusa est in cordibus nostris per Spiritum Sanctum, qui datus est nobis (Rm 5,5)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p303","Orientalium Ecclesiarum","2 The holy and catholic Church, which is the mystical body of Christ, consists of the faithful, who are organically united in the Holy Spirit by the same faith, the same sacraments, and the same governance, and who, coalescing into various groups joined hierarchically, constitute particular churches or rites. Among them there flourishes a wonderful communion, so that variety in the Church not only does not harm its unity, but rather declares it; for the Catholic Church has this purpose, that the traditions of each particular church or rite remain whole and intact, and at the same time it wishes to adapt its own manner of life to the various needs of times and places.","ORIENTALIUM ECCLESIARUM instituta, ritus liturgicos, traditiones ecclesiasticas atque vitae christianae disciplinam Ecclesia catholica magni facit. In eis enim, utpote veneranda antiquitate praeclaris, elucet ea quae ab Apostolis per Patres est traditio, quaeque partem constituit divinitus revelati atque indivisi universae Ecclesiae patrimonii. Ecclesiarum igitur Orientalium, quae huius traditionis testes sunt vivi, sollicitudinem gerens, haec Sancta et Oecumenica Synodus cupiens ut eaedem floreant et novo robore apostolico concreditum sibi munus absolvant, praeter ea quae ad universam spectant Ecclesiam, capita quaedam statuere decrevit, ceteris ad providentiam Synodorum orientalium nec non Sedis Apostolicae remissis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p304","On Particular Churches or Rites","3 Such particular churches, both of the East and of the West, although they differ in part among themselves in rites, namely liturgy, ecclesiastical discipline, and spiritual patrimony, are nevertheless equally entrusted to the pastoral governance of the Roman Pontiff, who by divine succession succeeds Blessed Peter in primacy over the universal Church. Therefore they enjoy equal dignity, so that none of them excels the others by reason of rite, and they enjoy the same rights and are bound by the same obligations, even concerning the preaching of the Gospel to the whole world (cf. Mk 16:15), under the direction of the Roman Pontiff.","Sancta et catholica Ecclesia, quae est corpus Christi Mysticum, constat ex fidelibus, qui eadem fide, iisdem sacramentis et eodem regimine in Spiritu Sancto organice uniuntur, quique in varios coetus hierarchia iunctos coalescentes, particulares Ecclesias seu ritus constituunt. Inter eas mirabilis viget communio, ita ut varietas in Ecclesia nedum eiusdem noceat unitati, eam potius declaret; Ecclesiae enim catholicae hoc propositum est, ut salvae et integrae maneant uniuscuiusque particularis Ecclesiae seu ritus traditiones, eademque pariter vult suam vitae rationem aptare variis temporum locorumque necessitatibus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p305","On Particular Churches or Rites","4 Provision is therefore to be made everywhere for the protection and increase of all particular churches, and for this purpose parishes and a proper hierarchy are to be constituted, where the spiritual good of the faithful demands it. However, the hierarchs of various particular churches obtaining jurisdiction in the same territory, let them foster unity of action with counsel exchanged in periodic meetings, and, with united forces, help common works for expediting the good of religion and more effectively protecting the discipline of the clergy. Let all clerics and those ascending to sacred orders be well instructed concerning rites and especially the practical norms in interritual matters, indeed also let laity in catechetical instruction be taught concerning rites and their norms. Finally, all and singular Catholics, and also the baptized of any acatholic Church or community coming to the fullness of catholic communion, retain their own rite everywhere, and cultivate it and observe it according to their strength; saving the right of recourse to the Apostolic See in particular cases of persons, communities, or regions, which, as the supreme arbiter of interecclesial relations, will provide for necessities in an ecumenical spirit, either itself or through other authorities, giving opportune norms, decrees, or rescripts.","Huiusmodi particulares Ecclesiae, tum Orientis tum Occidentis, licet ritibus, ut aiunt, nempe liturgia, ecclesiastica disciplina et patrimonio spirituali partim inter se differant, aequali tamen modo concreduntur pastorali gubernio Romani Pontificis, qui Beato Petro in primatu super universam Ecclesiam divinitus succedit. Eaedem proinde pari pollent dignitate, ita ut nulla earum ceteris praestet ratione ritus, atque iisdem fruuntur iuribus et tenentur obligationibus, etiam quod attinet ad Evangelium praedicandum in universum mundum (cf. Mc 16,15), sub moderamine Romani Pontificis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p306","On Particular Churches or Rites","5 History, traditions, and very many ecclesiastical institutions splendidly testify how greatly the Eastern Churches have deserved of the universal Church. Therefore the holy Synod not only pursues this ecclesiastical and spiritual patrimony with due esteem and just praise, but also firmly considers it as the patrimony of the whole Church of Christ. Wherefore it solemnly declares that the Churches of the East, as well as the West, have the right and the duty to govern themselves according to their own particular disciplines, as being commended by venerable antiquity, more congruent to the morals of their faithful, and seen as more suitable for providing for the good of souls.","Provideatur igitur ubique terrarum tuitioni atque incremento omnium Ecclesiarum particularium ac propterea constituantur paroeciae atque propria hierarchia, ubi id postulat bonum spirituale fidelium. Hierarchae vero variarum Ecclesiarum particularium in eodem territorio iurisdictionem obtinentes, curent, collatis consiliis in periodicis conventibus, unitatem actionis fovere, et, viribus unitis, communia adiuvare opera, ad bonum religionis expedite promovendum et cleri disciplinam efficacius tuendam. Clerici omnes et ascendentes ad ordines sacros bene instruantur de ritibus et praesertim de normis practicis in materiis interritualibus, imo et laici in institutione catechetica de ritibus eorumque normis doceantur. Omnes denique et singuli catholici, necnon baptizati cuiusvis Ecclesiae vel communitatis acatholicae ad plenitudinem communionis catholicae convenientes, proprium ubique terrarum retineant ritum eumque colant et pro viribus observent; salvo iure recurrendi ad Sedem Apostolicam in casibus peculiaribus personarum, communitatum, vel regionum, quae, uti suprema relationum interecclesialium arbitra, providebit necessitatibus in spiritu oecumenico, ipsa vel per alias auctoritates, datis opportunis normis, decretis vel rescriptis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p307","On Preserving the Spiritual Patrimony of the Eastern Churches","6 Let all Easterns know and have for certain that they can and ought always to preserve their own legitimate liturgical rites and their own discipline, and that changes are to be introduced only by reason of their own organic progress. All these things, therefore, are to be observed with the greatest fidelity by the Easterns themselves; who indeed must acquire a greater knowledge and more perfect use of these things day by day, and if they have unduly fallen away from them on account of circumstances of times or persons, they should strive to return to their ancestral traditions. But those who, by reason of office or apostolic ministry, have frequent intercourse with the Eastern Churches or with their faithful, are to be accurately instructed in the knowledge and cult of the rites, discipline, doctrine, history, and character of the Easterns, according to the gravity of the office which they hold. It is also strongly recommended to religions and associations of the Latin rite, which work in Eastern regions or among Eastern faithful, that for greater efficacy of apostolate they constitute, as far as possible, houses or even provinces of the Eastern rite.","Historia, traditiones et plurima ecclesiastica instituta praeclare testantur quantopere de universa Ecclesia Orientales Ecclesiae meritae sint. Quapropter Sancta Synodus patrimonium hoc ecclesiasticum et spirituale non solum aestimatione debita et iusta laude prosequitur, sed etiam tamquam patrimonium universae Christi Ecclesiae firmiter considerat. Quamobrem sollemniter declarat, Ecclesias Orientis sicut et Occidentis iure pollere et officio teneri se secundum proprias disciplinas peculiares regendi, utpote quae veneranda antiquitate commendentur, moribus suorum fidelium magis sint congruae atque ad bonum animarum consulendum aptiores videantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p308","On Preserving the Spiritual Patrimony of the Eastern Churches","7 From the most ancient times the patriarchal institution has flourished in the Church, already recognized by the first ecumenical councils. By the name of Eastern patriarch comes a bishop, to whom belongs jurisdiction over all bishops, not excepting metropolitans, the clergy, and the people of his own territory or rite, according to the norm of law and saving the primacy of the Roman Pontiff. Wherever a hierarch of some rite is constituted outside the boundaries of the patriarchal territory, he remains aggregated to the hierarchy of the patriarchate of the same rite according to the norm of law.","Sciant ac pro certo habeant omnes Orientales, se suos legitimos ritus liturgicos suamque disciplinam semper servare posse et debere, ac nonnisi ratione proprii et organici progressus mutationes inducendas esse. Haec omnia, igitur, maxima fidelitate ab ipsis Orientalibus observanda sunt; qui quidem harum rerum cognitionem in dies maiorem usumque perfectiorem acquirere debent, et, si ab iis ob temporum vel personarum adiuncta indebite defecerint, ad avitas traditiones redire satagant. Illi vero qui ratione sive muneris, sive apostolici ministerii frequens cum Orientalibus Ecclesiis aut cum earum fidelibus habeant commercium, in cognitione et cultu rituum, disciplinae, doctrinae, historiae atque indolis Orientalium accurate, pro gravitate officii quod gerunt, instituantur. Religionibus vero et associationibus latini ritus, quae in regionibus orientalibus vel inter fideles orientales operam praestant, enixe commendatur, ut, ad maiorem apostolatus efficaciam, domos aut etiam provincias orientalis ritus, quantum fieri potest, constituant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p309","On Eastern Patriarchs","8 Patriarchs of the Eastern Churches, although later in time one than another, are nevertheless all equal by reason of patriarchal dignity, saving the precedence of honor legitimately established among them.","Ab antiquissimis temporibus in Ecclesia viget institutio patriarchalis, iam a primis Synodis Oecumenicis agnita. Nomine vero Patriarchae orientalis venit episcopus, cui competit iurisdictio in omnes episcopos, haud exceptis metropolitis, clerum et populum proprii territorii vel ritus, ad normam iuris et salvo primatu Romani Pontificis. Ubicumque Hierarcha alicuius ritus extra fines territorii patriarchalis constituitur, manet aggregatus hierarchiae patriarchatus eiusdem ritus ad normam iuris."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p310","On Eastern Patriarchs","9 According to the most ancient tradition of the Church, the patriarchs of the Eastern Churches are to be pursued with singular honor, since each presides over his own patriarchate as father and head. Therefore this holy Synod decrees that their rights and privileges be restored, according to the ancient traditions of each Church and the decrees of the ecumenical councils. These rights and privileges, however, are those which flourished at the time of the union of East and West, even if they are to be somewhat adapted to modern conditions. Patriarchs with their synods constitute the superior instance for any affairs of the patriarchate, not excluding the right of constituting new eparchies and of naming bishops of their own rite within the boundaries of the patriarchal territory, saving the inalienable right of the Roman Pontiff to intervene in individual cases.","Patriarchae Ecclesiarum Orientalium, licet alii aliis tempore posteriores, omnes tamen aequales sunt ratione dignitatis patriarchalis, salva inter eos praecedentia honoris legitime statuta."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p311","On Eastern Patriarchs","10 What has been said concerning patriarchs also holds good, according to the norm of law, for major archbishops, who preside over a certain whole particular church or rite.","Secundum antiquissimam Ecclesiae traditionem, singulari honore prosequendi sunt Ecclesiarum Orientalium patriarchae, quippe qui suo quisque patriarchatui tamquam pater et caput praesint. Ideo statuit haec Sancta Synodus, ut eorum iura atque privilegia instaurentur, iuxta antiquas traditiones uniuscuiusque Ecclesiae et Synodorum Oecumenicarum decreta. Haec autem iura et privilegia sunt illa, quae tempore unionis Orientis et Occidentis viguerunt, etsi ad hodiernas conditiones aliquantum aptanda sint. Patriarchae cum suis synodis superiorem constituunt instantiam pro quibusvis negotiis patriarchatus, non secluso iure constituendi novas eparchias atque nominandi episcopos sui ritus intra fines territorii patriarchalis, salvo inalienabili Romani Pontificis iure in singulis casibus interveniendi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p312","On Eastern Patriarchs","11 Since the patriarchal institution in the Eastern Churches is a traditional form of governance, the holy ecumenical Synod desires that, where necessary, new patriarchates be erected, the constitution of which is reserved to the ecumenical Synod or the Roman Pontiff.","Quae de Patriarchis sunt dicta, valent etiam, ad normam iuris, de Archiepiscopis maioribus, qui universae cuidam Ecclesiae particulari seu ritui praesunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p313","On Eastern Patriarchs","12 The holy ecumenical Synod confirms and praises the ancient discipline concerning the sacraments existing among the Eastern Churches, and likewise the practice which pertains to their celebration and administration, and, if the case requires, desires that the same be restored.","Cum institutum patriarchale in Ecclesiis Orientalibus sit forma regiminis traditionalis, Sancta et Oecumenica Synodus exoptat ut, ubi opus sit, novi erigantur patriarchatus, quorum constitutio Synodo Oecumenicae vel Romano Pontifici reservatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p314","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","13 The discipline concerning the minister of holy chrism existing among the Easterns from the most ancient times is fully to be restored. Therefore, presbyters can confer this sacrament, using chrism blessed by a patriarch or bishop.","Veterem disciplinam de sacramentis apud Orientales Ecclesias vigentem, itemque praxim quae ad eorum celebrationem et ministrationem spectat, Sancta Oecumenica Synodus confirmat et laudat, et, si casus ferat, exoptat ut eadem instauretur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p315","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","14 All Eastern presbyters can validly confer this sacrament, whether together with baptism or separately, to all the faithful of any rite, not excluding the Latin, the prescriptions of both common and particular law as to liceity being observed. Latin rite presbyrators also, according to the faculties which they enjoy concerning the administration of this sacrament, can validly administer it also to the faithful of the Eastern Churches, without prejudice to the rite, observing as to liceity the prescriptions of law, whether common or particular.","Disciplina de ministro S. Chrismatis inde ab antiquissimis temporibus apud Orientales vigens plene instauretur. Idcirco presbyteri hoc sacramentum conferre valent, adhibito Chrismate a patriarcha vel episcopo benedicto."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p316","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","15 The faithful are bound to attend divine liturgy on Sundays and feast days, or, according to the prescription or custom of their own rite, the celebration of divine praises. So that the faithful may more easily be able to fulfill this obligation, it is decreed that the useful time for fulfilling this precept runs from the vespers of the vigil to the end of the Sunday or feast day. It is strongly recommended to the faithful that they receive the holy Eucharist on these days, indeed more frequently and even daily.","Presbyteri omnes orientales hoc sacramentum, sive una cum Baptismo sive separatim, valide conferre possunt omnibus fidelibus cuiusvis ritus, latino haud excluso, servatis ad liceitatem praescriptis iuris tum communis tum particularis. Presbyteri quoque latini ritus, secundum facultates quibus gaudent circa ministrationem huius sacramenti, valent illud etiam fidelibus Ecclesiarum Orientalium ministrare, sine praeiudicio ritui, servatis quoad liceitatem praescriptis iuris sive communis sive particularis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p317","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","16 On account of the daily mixture of faithful of different particular churches in the same region or Eastern territory, the faculty of priests of any rite to hear confessions, duly and without any restriction granted by their own hierarchs, is extended to the whole territory of the conceding and to the places and faithful of any rite in the same territory, unless the hierarch of the place, as to the places of his own rite, has expressly refused.","Fideles obligatione tenentur diebus dominicis et festis interesse divinae liturgiae aut, iuxta praescripta vel consuetudinem proprii ritus, celebrationi divinarum laudum. Quo facilius fideles hanc obligationem adimplere valeant, statuitur tempus utile, pro hoc praecepto adimplendo, decurrere inde a vesperis vigiliae usque ad finem diei Dominicae vel festi. Enixe commendatur fidelibus, ut his diebus, imo frequentius ac vel etiam quotidie, Sacram Eucharistiam suscipiant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p318","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","17 So that the ancient discipline of the sacrament of Order in the Eastern Churches may flourish again, this holy Synod desires that the institution of the permanent diaconate, where it has fallen into disuse, be restored. As to the subdiaconate and the lower orders and their rights and obligations, let the legislative authority of each particular church provide.","Ob quotidianam permixtionem fidelium diversarum particularium Ecclesiarum in eadem regione vel territorio orientali, facultas presbyterorum cuiuscumque ritus excipiendi confessiones, a propriis hierarchis rite et sine ulla restrictione concessa, ad totum territorium extenditur concedentis necnon ad loca et fideles cuiuscumque ritus in eodem territorio, nisi hierarcha loci, quoad loca sui ritus, expresse renuerit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p319","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","18 To prevent invalid marriages when Eastern Catholics marry with baptized Eastern acatholics, and to consult the firmness and sanctity of marriage and domestic peace, the holy Synod decrees that the canonical form of celebration obliges for liceity only for these marriages; for validity, the presence of a sacred minister suffices, other things to be observed according to law.","Ut antiqua sacramenti Ordinis disciplina in Ecclesiis Orientalibus iterum vigeat, exoptat haec Sancta Synodus, ut institutum diaconatus permanentis ubi in desuetudinem venerit, instauretur. Quoad subdiaconatum vero et Ordines inferiores eorumque iura et obligationes, provideat Auctoritas legislativa uniuscuiusque Ecclesiae particularis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p320","On the Discipline of the Sacraments","19 The establishing, transferring, or suppressing of feast days common to all Eastern Churches pertains in the future only to the ecumenical Synod or the Apostolic See. However, establishing, transferring, or suppressing feast days for individual particular churches, besides the Apostolic See, pertains to the patriarchal or archiepiscopal synods, due regard being had to the whole region and the other particular churches.","Ad praecavenda matrimonia invalida, quando catholici orientales cum acatholicis orientalibus baptizatis matrimonium ineunt, et ad consulendum nuptiarum firmitati et sanctitati nec non domesticae paci, Sancta Synodus statuit formam canonicam celebrationis pro his matrimoniis obligare tantum ad liceitatem; ad validitatem sufficere praesentiam ministri sacri, servatis aliis de iure servandis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p321","On Divine Worship","20 Until the desired agreement among all Christians concerning the one day on which the feast of Easter is to be celebrated by all is attained, in the meantime for fostering unity among Christians living in the same region or nation, it is committed to the Patriarchs or the Supreme Ecclesiastical Authorities in the place, that, with unanimous consent and counsel exchanged with those whose interest it is, they agree concerning the feast of Easter to be celebrated on the same Sunday.","Dies festos pro omnibus Ecclesiis Orientalibus communes in posterum constituere, transferre aut supprimere unius est Synodi Oecumenicae vel Sedis Apostolicae. Dies vero festos pro singulis Ecclesiis particularibus constituere, transferre aut supprimere, praeter Apostolicam Sedem, competit Synodis patriarchalibus aut archiepiscopalibus, debita tamen ratione habita totius regionis ceterarumque Ecclesiarum particularium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p322","On Divine Worship","21 Individual faithful staying outside the region or territory of their own rite can fully conform themselves, as to the law of sacred times, to the discipline prevailing in the place where they live. In families of mixed rite, it is lawful to observe this law according to one and the same rite.","Donec perveniatur ad optatam inter omnes christianos conventionem de unico die, quo festivitas Paschatis ab omnibus celebretur, interim ad unitatem inter christianos in eadem regione vel natione degentes fovendam, Patriarchis vel Supremis in loco Auctoritatibus ecclesiasticis committitur, ut, unanimi consensu et collatis consiliis cum iis quorum interest, de festo Paschatis eadem die Dominica celebrando conveniant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p323","On Divine Worship","22 Eastern clerics and religious should celebrate the Divine Praises according to the prescriptions of their own discipline and traditions, which from ancient age have been in great honor among all Eastern Churches. The faithful also, following the example of their fathers, should apply themselves to the Divine Praises according to their strength and with devotion.","Singuli fideles extra regionem vel territorium proprii ritus versantes, quoad legem de temporibus sacris, ad disciplinam in loco ubi degunt vigentem se plene conformare possunt. In familiis mixti ritus hanc legem servare licet iuxta unum eundemque ritum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p324","On Divine Worship","23 The right of moderating the use of languages in sacred liturgical actions pertains to the Patriarch with the Synod or to the Supreme Authority of each Church with the Council of hierarchs, and also, having made relation to the Apostolic See, of approving translations of texts into the vernacular language.","Clerici et religiosi orientales celebrent iuxta propriae disciplinae praescripta et traditiones Laudes Divinas, quae inde ab antiqua aetate magno in honore fuerunt apud omnes Ecclesias Orientales. Fideles quoque, exempla maiorum secuti, in Divinas Laudes pro viribus et devote incumbant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p325","On Divine Worship","24 It pertains to the Eastern Churches in communion with the Roman Apostolic See a particular office of fostering the unity of all Christians, especially of the Easterns, according to the principles of the decree of this holy Synod on Ecumenism, first of all by prayer, by the example of life, by religious fidelity toward the ancient Eastern traditions, by mutual and better knowledge, by collaboration, and by fraternal estimation of matters and spirits.","Ad Patriarcham cum Synodo vel ad Supremam cuiusque Ecclesiae Auctoritatem cum Consilio hierarcharum, ius pertinet moderandi usum linguarum in sacris actionibus liturgicis nec non, facta relatione ad Sedem Apostolicam, probandi versiones textuum in linguam vernaculam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p326","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","25 From Easterns separated, coming into catholic unity under the influx of the grace of the Holy Spirit, let nothing more be required than what the simple profession of catholic faith demands. And since among them the priesthood has been preserved valid, to Eastern clerics coming into catholic unity the faculty is to exercise their own Order, according to the norms established by competent Authority.","Ad Ecclesias Orientales, communionem Cum Sede Apostolica Romana habentes, peculiare pertinet munus omnium christianorum unitatem, orientalium praesertim, fovendi, iuxta principia decreti huius S. Synodi De Oecumenismo, precibus imprimis, vitae exemplis, religiosa erga antiquas traditiones orientales fidelitate, mutua et meliore cognitione, collaboratione ac fraterna rerum animorumque aestimatione."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p327","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","26 Communication in sacred things, which offends the unity of the Church or includes formal adherence to error or peril of deviation in the end, scandal and indifferentism, is prohibited by divine law. But pastoral practice demonstrates that, as to the Eastern brothers, various circumstances of individual persons can be considered and ought to be, in which neither the unity of the Church is harmed, nor the perils to be avoided are present, but the necessity of salvation and the spiritual good of souls urge. Therefore the Catholic Church, according to the circumstances of times, places, and persons, has often used and uses a milder manner of acting, offering to all the means of salvation and the testimony of charity among Christians, through participation in the sacraments and other functions and sacred things. These things having been attended to, the holy Synod, lest we be an impediment by the severity of judgment to those who are saved and to more foster the union with the Eastern Churches separated from us, decrees the following manner of acting.","Ab Orientalibus seiunctis, in unitatem catholicam sub influxu gratiae Sancti Spiritus convenientibus, ne plus exigatur quam quod simplex fidei catholicae professio exigit. Et cum apud eos sacerdotium validum servatum sit, clericis orientalibus, in unitatem catholicam convenientibus, facultas est proprium Ordinem exercendi, iuxta normas a competenti Auctoritate statutas."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p328","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","27 The principles being set down, to Easterns who are found in good faith separated from the Catholic Church, if they spontaneously ask and are rightly disposed, the sacraments of Penance, Eucharist, and Anointing of the Sick can be conferred; indeed, even to Catholics it is lawful to ask the same sacraments from acatholic ministers, in whose Church valid sacraments are had, as often as necessity or true spiritual utility persuades, and access to a catholic priest becomes physically or morally impossible.","Communicatio in sacris, quae unitatem Ecclesiae offendit aut formalem errori adhaesionem vel periculum aberrationis in fine, scandali et indifferentismi includit, lege divina prohibetur. Praxis vero pastoralis demonstrat, ad fratres orientales quod spectat, varia considerari posse et debere singularum personarum adiuncta, in quibus nec unitas Ecclesiae laeditur, nec pericula vitanda adsunt, sed necessitas salutis et bonum spirituale animarum urgent. Ideo Ecclesia catholica, pro temporum, locorum et personarum adiunctis, mitiorem saepe adhibuit et adhibet rationem agendi, salutis media et testimonium caritatis inter christianos omnibus praebens, per participationem in sacramentis aliisque in functionibus et rebus sacris. His attentis, Sancta Synodus, ne impedimento propter sententiae severitatem simus iis qui salvantur et ad magis fovendam unionem cum Ecclesiis Orientalibus a nobis seiunctis, sequentem agendi rationem statuit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p329","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","28 Likewise, the same principles being set down, communication in sacred functions, things, and places between Catholics and separated Eastern brothers is permitted for a just cause.","Positis memoratis principiis, Orientalibus, qui bona fide seiuncti inveniuntur ab Ecclesia catholica, si sponte petant et rite sint dispositi, sacramenta Poenitentiae, Eucharistiae et Unctionis Infirmorum conferri possunt; imo, etiam catholicis eadem sacramenta licet petere ab iis ministris acatholicis, in quorum Ecclesia habentur valida sacramenta, quotiescumque id necessitas aut vera spiritualis utilitas suadeat, et accessus ad sacerdotem catholicum physice vel moraliter impossibilis evadat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p330","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","29 This milder manner of communication in sacred things with brothers of the separated Eastern Churches is committed to the vigilance and moderation of the local hierarchs, that, counsel exchanged among themselves, and, if the case requires, the hierarchs of the separated Churches also having been heard, they may moderate the conversation of Christians with opportune and effective precepts and norms.","Item, positis iisdem principiis, communicatio in sacris functionibus, rebus et locis inter catholicos et fratres seiunctos orientales iusta de causa permittitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p331","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","30 The holy Synod greatly rejoices over the fruitful and active collaboration of the Eastern and Western Catholic Churches, and at the same time declares: all these dispositions of law are established for the present conditions, until the Catholic Church and the separated Eastern Churches come to the fullness of communion. In the meantime, however, all Christians, Eastern as well as Western, are earnestly asked that they pour forth fervent and assiduous, indeed daily, prayers to God that, the most holy Mother of God helping, all may become one. Let them also pray that the fullness of the Paraclete Spirit of strengthening and solace may flow to so many Christians of every Church, who, strenuously professing the name of Christ, suffer and are in anguish. Let us all love one another with the charity of fraternity, preventing one another with honor.","Haec mitior communicationis in sacris cum fratribus Ecclesiarum Orientalium seiunctarum ratio vigilantiae et moderamini hierarcharum locorum committitur, ut, collatis inter se consiliis, et, si casus ferat, auditis etiam hierarchis Ecclesiarum seiunctarum, opportunis efficacibusque praeceptis et normis christianorum moderentur conversationem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-3/index.html","p332","On Conversation With Brothers of the Separated Churches","1 Hearing the word of God religiously and proclaiming it confidently, the sacred Synod follows the words of St. John saying: \"We announce to you the eternal life, which was with the Father and appeared to us: what we have seen and heard we announce to you, that you also may have fellowship with us, and our fellowship may be with the Father and with His Son Jesus Christ\" (1 Jn 1:2-3). Therefore, following in the footsteps of the Councils of Trent and Vatican I, it intends to set forth the genuine doctrine concerning divine revelation and its transmission, that the whole world may, by hearing, believe, by believing, hope, by hoping, love.","Magnopere laetatur Sancta Synodus de fructuosa et actuosa collaboratione Orientalium et Occidentalium Ecclesiarum Catholicarum, simulque declarat: hae omnes iuris dispositiones pro praesentibus conditionibus statuuntur, usquedum Ecclesia catholica et Ecclesiae Orientales seiunctae ad plenitudinem communionis convenient. Interim tamen omnes christiani, Orientales necnon Occidentales, enixe rogantur, ut ferventes atque assiduas, imo quotidianas preces Deo fundant ut, Sanctissima Deipara auxiliante, omnes unum fiant. Orent quoque, ut tot christianis cuiuscumque Ecclesiae, qui, strenue profitentes Christi nomen, patiuntur et angustiantur, Spiritus Sancti Paracliti adfluat plenitudo confortationis et solatii. Omnes caritate fraternitatis invicem diligamus, honore invicem praevenientes."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p333","Dei Verbum","2 It pleased God in His goodness and wisdom to reveal Himself and to make known the mystery of His will (cf. Eph 1:9), by which men through Christ, the Word made flesh, in the Holy Spirit have access to the Father and are made partakers of the divine nature (cf. Eph 2:18; 2 Pet 1:4). By this revelation, therefore, the invisible God (cf. Col 1:15; 1 Tim 1:17) from the abundance of His love speaks to men as friends (cf. Ex 33:11; Jn 15:14-15) and lives among them (cf. Bar 3:38), that He may invite and receive them into fellowship with Himself. This economy of revelation is made by deeds and words intrinsically connected with each other, so that the works, accomplished by God in the history of salvation, manifest and confirm the doctrine and the realities signified by the words, and the words proclaim the works and elucidate the mystery contained in them. Moreover, the intimate truth both of God and of the salvation of man shines forth in Christ through this revelation, who is at once the mediator and the fullness of all revelation.","DEI VERBUM religiose audiens et fidenter proclamans, Sacrosancta Synodus verbis S. Ioannis obsequitur dicentis: Adnuntiamus vobis vitam aeternam, quae erat apud Patrem et apparuit nobis: quod vidimus et audivimus adnuntiamus vobis, ut et vos societatem habeatis nobiscum, et societas nostra sit cum Patre et cum Filio eius Iesu Christo (1Jn 1,2-3). Propterea, Conciliorum Tridentini et Vaticani I inhaerens vestigiis, genuinam de divina revelatione ac de eius transmissione doctrinam proponere intendit, ut salutis praeconio mundus universus audiendo credat, credendo speret, sperando amet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p334","Chapter I. on Revelation Itself","3 God, creating and conserving all things through the Word (cf. Jn 1:3), gives a perpetual testimony of Himself to men in created things (cf. Rom 1:19-20), and, intending to open the way of supernal salvation, moreover manifested Himself to the first parents from the beginning. After their fall, He raised them up in the hope of salvation by promising redemption (cf. Gen 3:15), and He took care of the human race without ceasing, that He might give eternal life to all who seek salvation by patience in good work (cf. Rom 2:6-7). In His own time, He called Abraham, to make him into a great nation (cf. Gen 12:2-3), which after the Patriarchs He instructed through Moses and the prophets to acknowledge Himself the only living and true God, a provident Father and just judge, and to await the promised Savior, and thus through the ages He prepared the way for the Gospel.","Placuit Deo in sua bonitate et sapientia Seipsum revelare et notum facere sacramentum voluntatis suae (cf. Ep 1,9), quo homines per Christum, Verbum carnem factum, in Spiritu Sancto accessum habent ad Patrem et divinae naturae consortes efficiuntur (cf. Ep 2,18 Ep 2 Petr. 1, 4). Hac itaque revelatione Deus invisibilis (cf. Col 1,15 1Tm 1,17) ex abundantia caritatis suae homines tamquam amicos alloquitur (cf. Ex 33,11 Jn 15,14-15) et cum eis conversatur (cf. Ba 3,38), ut eos ad societatem Secum invitet in eamque suscipiat. Haec revelationis oeconomia fit gestis verbisque intrinsece inter se connexis, ita ut opera, in historia salutis a Deo patrata, doctrinam et res verbis significatas manifestent ac corroborent, verba autem opera proclament et mysterium in eis contentum elucident. Intima autem per hanc revelationem tam de Deo quam de hominis salute veritas nobis in Christo illucescit, qui mediator simul et plenitudo totius revelationis exsistit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p335","Chapter I. on Revelation Itself","4 After God spoke in many and various ways through the prophets, \"lastly in these days has spoken to us in the Son\" (Heb 1:1-2). For He sent His Son, the eternal Word, who enlightens all men, to dwell among men and tell them of the intimate things of God (cf. Jn 1:1-18). Jesus Christ, therefore, the Word made flesh, a man sent to men, \"speaks the words of God\" (Jn 3:34), and completes the saving work which the Father gave Him to do (cf. Jn 5:36; 17:4). Therefore He Himself, whom to see is to see the Father (cf. Jn 14:9), completes and perfects revelation, confirming it by divine testimony, by His whole presence and manifestation, by words and works, by signs and miracles, especially by His death and glorious resurrection from the dead, and finally by sending the Spirit of truth, namely that God is with us to free us from the darkness of sin and death and to raise us up to eternal life. The Christian economy, therefore, as the new and definitive covenant, will never pass away, and no new public revelation is to be expected before the glorious manifestation of our Lord Jesus Christ (cf. 1 Tim 6:14; Tit 2:13).","Deus, per Verbum omnia creans (cf. Jn 1,3) et conservans, in rebus creatis perenne sui testimonium hominibus praebet (cf. Rm 1,19-20) et, viam salutis supernae aperire intendens, insuper protoparentibus inde ab initio Semetipsum manifestavit. Post eorum autem lapsum eos, redemptione promissa, in spem salutis erexit (cf. Gn 3,15), et sine intermissione generis humani curam egit, ut omnibus qui secundum patientiam boni operis salutem quaerunt, vitam aeternam daret (cf. Rm 2,6-7). Suo autem tempore Abraham vocavit, ut faceret eum in gentem magnam (cf. Gn 12,2-3), quam post Patriarchas per Moysen et Prophetas erudivit ad se solum Deum vivum et verum, providum Patrem et iudicem iustum agnoscendum, et ad promissum Salvatorem expectandum, atque ita per saecula viam Evangelio praeparavit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p336","Chapter I. on Revelation Itself","5 To God revealing is to be rendered the obedience of faith (cf. Rom 16:26; cf. Rom 1:5; 2 Cor 10:5-6), by which man commits his whole self freely to God, rendering full submission of intellect and will to God revealing and willingly assenting to the revelation given by Him. That this faith may be given, it is necessary that the grace of God prevenient and assisting and the internal aids of the Holy Spirit, who moves the heart and converts it to God, opens the eyes of the mind, and gives to all sweetness in consenting to and believing the truth. So that the understanding of revelation may become ever deeper, the same Holy Spirit continually perfects faith by His gifts.","Postquam vero multifariam multisque modis Deus locutus est in Prophetis, novissime diebus istis locutus est nobis in Filio (He 1,1-2). Misit enim Filium suum, aeternum scilicet Verbum, qui omnes homines illuminat, ut inter homines habitaret iisque intima dei enarraret (cf. Jn 1,1-18). Iesus Christus ergo, Verbum caro factus? homo ad homines missus, \"verba Dei loquitur\" (Jn 3,34), et opus salutare consummat quod dedit ei Pater faciendum (cf. Jn 5,36 Jn 17,4). Quapropter Ipse, quem qui videt, videt et Patrem (cf. Jn 14,9), tota suiipsius praesentia ac manifestatione, verbis et operibus, signis et miraculis, praesertim autem morte sua et gloriosa ex mortuis resurrectione, misso tandem Spiritu veritatis, revelationem complendo perficit ac testimonio divino confirmat, Deum nempe nobiscum esse ad nos ex peccati mortisque tenebris liberandos et in aeternam vitam resuscitandos. Oeconomia ergo christiana, utpote foedus novum et definitivum, numquam praeteribit et nulla iam nova revelatio publica expectanda est ante gloriosam manifestationem Domini nostri Iesu Christi (cf. 1Tm 6,14 cf. Tt 2,13)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p337","Chapter I. on Revelation Itself","6 By divine revelation God wished to manifest and communicate Himself and the eternal decrees of His will concerning the salvation of men, namely to share divine goods, which entirely surpass the understanding of the human mind. The sacred Synod confesses that God, the beginning and end of all things, can be certainly known by the natural light of human reason from created things (cf. Rom 1:20); but it teaches that it is to be attributed to His revelation that those things which in divine matters are not of themselves impervious to human reason can, in the present condition of the human race, be known by all with ease, with firm certainty, and with no admixture of error.","Deo revelanti praestanda est oboeditio fidei (cf. Rm 16 Rm 26 coll. Rm 1,5 2Co 10,5-6), qua homo se totum libere Deo committit plenum revelanti Deo intellectus et voluntatis obsequium praestando et voluntarie revelationi ab Eo datae assentiendo. Quae fides ut praebeatur, opus est praeveniente et adiuvante gratia Dei et internis Spiritus Sancti auxiliis, qui cor moveat et in Deum convertat, mentis oculos aperiat, et det omnibus suavitatem in consentiendo et credendo veritati. Quo vero profundior usque evadat revelationis intelligentia, idem Spiritus Sanctus fidem iugiter per dona sua perficit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p338","Chapter I. on Revelation Itself","7 God most benignly arranged that those things which He had revealed for the salvation of all nations should remain whole forever and be transmitted to all generations. Therefore Christ the Lord, in whom the whole revelation of the most high God is consummated (cf. 2 Cor 1:30; 3:16—4:6), gave mandate to the Apostles that they should preach to all the Gospel, which promised before through the prophets He Himself fulfilled and promulgated with His own mouth, as the source of all saving truth and moral discipline, communicating divine gifts to them. Which indeed was faithfully done, both by the Apostles, who in oral preaching, examples, and institutions handed down those things which they had received either from the mouth, conversation, and works of Christ, or had learned from the suggestion of the Holy Spirit, and by those Apostles and apostolic men, who, under the inspiration of the same Holy Spirit, committed the message of salvation to writing.","Divina revelatione Deus Seipsum atque aeterna voluntatis suae decreta circa hominum salutem manifestare ac communicare voluit, ad participanda scilicet bona divina. quae humanae mentis intelligentiam omnino superant. Confitetur Sacra Synodus, Deum, rerum omnium principium et finem, naturali humanae rationis lumine e rebus creatis certo cognosci posse (cf. Rm 1,20); eius vero revelationi tribuendum esse docet, ut ea, quae in rebus divinis humanae rationi per se impervia non sunt. In praesenti quoque generis humani conditione ab omnibus expedite, firma certitudine et nullo admixto errore cognosci possint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p339","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","That the Gospel might be kept whole and living always in the Church, the Apostles left bishops as their successors, handing over to them their own place of teaching. Therefore this sacred Tradition and the sacred Scripture of both Testaments are as a mirror in which the Church pilgrimaging on earth contemplates God, from whom she receives all things, until she is led to see Him face to face as He is (cf. 1 Jn 3:2).","Quae Deus ad salutem cunctarum gentium revelaverat, eadem benignissime disposuit ut in aevum integra permanerent omnibusque generationibus transmitterentur. Ideo Christus Dominus, in quo summi Dei tota revelatio consummatur (cf. 2Co 1,32 cf. 2Co 3,16-4,6), mandatum dedit Apostolis ut Evangelium, quod promissum ante per Prophetas Ipse adimplevit et proprio ore promulgavit, tamquam fontem omnis et salutaris veritatis et morum disciplinae omnibus praedicarent, eis dona divina communicantes. Quod quidem fideliter factum est, tum ab Apostolis, qui in praedicatione orali, exemplis et institutionibus ea tradiderunt quae sive ex ore, conversatione et operibus Christi acceperant, sive a Spiritu Sancto suggerente didicerant, tum ab illis Apostolis virisque apostolicis, qui, sub inspiratione eiusdem Spiritus Sancti, nuntium salutis scriptis mandaverunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p340","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","8 Therefore apostolic preaching, which is expressed in a special way in the inspired books, was to be preserved by a continuous succession until the end of time. Hence the Apostles, handing down what they themselves had received, warn the faithful to hold the traditions which they have learned either by word or by letter (cf. 2 Th 2:15), and to contend for the faith once delivered to the saints (cf. Jude 3). But what has been handed down by the Apostles encompasses all those things which contribute to leading the life of the People of God holy and to increasing faith, and thus the Church, in her doctrine, life, and worship, perpetuates and transmits to all generations all that she herself is, all that she believes.","Ut autem Evangelium integrum et vivum iugiter in Ecclesia servaretur, Apostoli successores reliquerunt Episcopos, ipsis suum ipsorum locum magisterii tradentes. Haec igitur Sacra Traditio et Sacra utriusque Testamenti Scriptura veluti speculum sunt in quo Ecclesia in terris peregrinans contemplatur Deum, a quo omnia accipit, usquedum ad Eum videndum facie ad faciem sicuti est perducatur (cf. 1Jn 3,2)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p341","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","This tradition which is from the Apostles, under the assistance of the Holy Spirit, progresses in the Church: for there grows the perception of the things handed down, both of words and realities, both from the contemplation and study of believers, who compare these things in their heart (cf. Lk 2:19, 51), and from the intimate understanding of spiritual things which they experience, and from the preaching of those who, with the succession of the episcopate, have received the certain charism of truth. The Church, namely, through the rolling ages, continually tends to the fullness of divine truth, until the words of God are consummated in her.","Itaque praedicatio apostolica, quae in inspiratis libris speciali modo exprimitur, continua successione usque ad consummationem temporum conservari debebat. Unde Apostoli, tradentes quod et ipsi acceperunt, fideles monent ut teneant traditiones quas sive per sermonem sive per epistulam didicerint (cf. 2Th 2,15), utque pro semel sibi tradita fide decertent (cf. Iud. 3) . Quod vero ab Apostolis traditum est, ea omnia complectitur quae ad Populi Dei vitam sancte ducendam fidemque augendam conferunt, sicque Ecclesia, in sua doctrina, vita et cultu, perpetuat, cunctisque generationibus transmittit omne quod ipsa est, omne quod credit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p342","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","The sayings of the holy Fathers testify to the life-giving presence of this Tradition, whose riches are poured into the practice and life of the believing and praying Church. Through the same Tradition the whole canon of the sacred books is known to the Church, and the sacred scriptures themselves are more deeply understood and unceasingly made active; thus God, who spoke of old, converses without ceasing with the Spouse of His beloved Son, and the Holy Spirit, through whom the living voice of the Gospel resounds in the Church, and through her in the world, leads believers into all truth and makes the word of Christ abundantly dwell in them (cf. Col 3:16).","Haec quae est ab Apostolis Traditio sub assistentia Spiritus Sancti in Ecclesia proficit: crescit enim tam rerum quam verborum traditorum perceptio, tum ex contemplatione et studio credentium, qui ea conferunt in corde suo (cf. Lc 2,19 Lc 2,51), tum ex intima spiritualium rerum quam experiuntur intelligentia, tum ex praeconio eorum qui cum episcopatus successione charisma veritatis certum acceperunt. Ecclesia scilicet, volventibus saeculis, ad plenitudinem divinae veritatis iugiter tendit, donec in ipsa consummentur verba Dei."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p343","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","9 Sacred Tradition, therefore, and sacred Scripture are closely connected and communicate with each other. For both, flowing from the same divine source, coalesce in a certain way into one and tend to the same end. For sacred Scripture is the speech of God as it is consigned to writing under the inspiration of the divine Spirit; but sacred Tradition transmits the word of God, entrusted by Christ the Lord and the Holy Spirit to the Apostles, wholly to their successors, that they, under the light of the Spirit of truth, may faithfully keep, expound, and spread it by their preaching; whence it follows that the Church does not derive her certainty concerning all revealed things from sacred Scripture alone. Wherefore, both are to be received and venerated with equal affection of piety and reverence.","Sanctorum Patrum dicta huius Traditionis vivificam testificantur praesentiam, cuius divitiae in praxim vitamque credentis et orantis Ecclesiae transfunduntur. Per eandem Traditionem integer Sacrorum Librorum canon Ecclesiae innotescit, ipsaeque Sacrae Litterae in ea penitius intelliguntur et indesinenter actuosae redduntur; sicque Deus, qui olim locutus est, sine intermissione cum dilecti Filii sui Sponsa colloquitur, et Spiritus Sanctus, per quem viva vox Evangelii in Ecclesia, et per ipsam in mundo resonat, credentes in omnem veritatem inducit, verbumque Christi in eis abundanter inhabitare facit (cf. Col 3,16)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p344","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","10 Sacred Tradition and sacred Scripture constitute one sacred deposit of the word of God entrusted to the Church, adhering to which the whole holy people, united to its pastors, continually perseveres in the doctrine of the Apostles, in communion, in the breaking of bread, and in prayers (cf. Acts 2:42), so that in holding, exercising, and professing the faith handed down, there is a singular conspiracy of the bishops and the faithful.","Sacra Traditio ergo et Sacra Scriptura arcte inter se connectuntur atque communicant. Nam ambae, ex eadem divina scaturigine promanantes, in unum quodammodo coalescunt et in eundem finem tendunt. Etenim Sacra Scriptura est locutio Dei quatenus divino afflante Spiritu scripto consignatur; Sacra autem Traditio verbum Dei, a Christo Domino et a Spiritu Sancto Apostolis concreditum, successoribus eorum integre transmittit, ut illud, praelucente Spiritu veritatis, praeconio suo fideliter servent, exponant atque diffundant; quo fit ut Ecclesia certitudinem suam de omnibus revelatis non per solam Sacram Scripturam hauriat. Quapropter utraque pari pietatis affectu ac reverentia suscipienda et veneranda est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p345","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","The office of authentically interpreting the written or handed down word of God has been entrusted only to the living Magisterium of the Church, whose authority is exercised in the name of Jesus Christ. Which Magisterium is not above the word of God, but serves it, teaching only what has been handed down, insofar as it piously hears, holy guards, and faithfully expounds it by divine mandate and with the assistance of the Holy Spirit, and draws from this one deposit of faith all those things which it proposes to be believed as divinely revealed. It is clear, therefore, that sacred Tradition, sacred Scripture, and the Magisterium of the Church, according to the most wise design of God, are so connected and associated that one does not stand without the others, and all together, each in its own way, under the action of the one Holy Spirit, effectively contribute to the salvation of souls.","Sacra Traditio et Sacra Scriptura unum verbi Dei sacrum depositum constituunt Ecclesiae commissum, cui inhaerens tota plebs sancta Pastoribus suis adunata in doctrina Apostolorum et communione, fractione panis et orationibus iugiter perseverat (cf. Ac 2,42 gr.), ita ut in tradita fide tenenda, exercenda profitendaque singularis fiat Antistitum et fidelium conspiratio."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p346","Chapter II. on the Transmission of Divine Revelation","11 Divinely revealed things, which are contained and set forth in sacred Scripture, have been consigned under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit. For holy Mother Church, from the apostolic faith, holds the books of both the Old and New Testament, with all their parts, as sacred and canonical, because, having been written under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit (cf. Jn 20:31; 2 Tim 3:16; 2 Pet 1:19-21; 3:15-16), they have God as their author, and as such have been handed down to the Church itself. But in composing the sacred books, God chose men, whom He used with their own faculties and powers, so that, He acting in them and through them, they, as true authors, should consign to writing all and only those things which He himself willed. Since, therefore, all that the inspired authors or hagiographers assert should be held as asserted by the Holy Spirit, it is to be professed that the books of Scripture teach firmly, faithfully, and without error that truth which God willed to be consigned to the sacred letters for the sake of our salvation. Thus \"all Scripture, divinely inspired, is useful for teaching, for reproving, for correcting, for instructing in justice: that the man of God may be perfect, furnished to every good work\" (2 Tim 3:16-17).","Munus autem authentice interpretandi verbum Dei scriptum vel traditum soli vivo Ecclesiae Magisterio concreditum est, cuius auctoritas in nomine Iesu Christi exercetur. Quod quidem Magisterium non supra verbum Dei est, sed eidem ministrat, docens nonnisi quod traditum est, quatenus illud, ex divino mandato et Spiritu Sancto assistente, pie audit, sancte custodit et fideliter exponit, ac ea omnia ex hoc uno fidei deposito haurit quae tamquam divinitus revelata credenda proponit. Patet igitur Sacram Traditionem, Sacram Scripturam et Ecclesiae Magisterium, iuxta sapientissimum Dei consilium, ita inter se connecti et consociari, ut unum sine aliis non consistat, omniaque simul, singula suo modo sub actione unius Spiritus Sancti, ad animarum salutem efficaciter conferant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p347","Chapter III. on the Divine Inspiration of Sacred Scripture and on Its Interpreta","12 Since, however, God in sacred Scripture spoke through men in a human way, the interpreter of sacred Scripture, to understand what He wished to communicate to us, should attentively investigate what the hagiographers really intended to signify and what it pleased God to manifest by their words. To ascertain the intention of the hagiographers, among other things, regard must be paid to literary genera. For truth is proposed and expressed differently in texts variously historical, or prophetic, or poetic, or in other kinds of speech. Moreover, it is necessary that the interpreter seek that sense which the hagiographer, in given circumstances, according to the condition of his time and his culture, using the literary genera then in use, intended and expressed. For to rightly understand what the sacred author wished to assert in writing, due attention must be paid both to the accustomed and native ways of feeling, speaking, and narrating which flourished in the time of the hagiographer, and to those which were commonly used in the mutual commerce of men at that age.","Divinitus revelata, quae in Sacra Scriptura litteris continentur et prostrant, Spiritu Sancto afflante consignata sunt. Libros enim integros tam Veteris quam Novi Testamenti, cum omnibus eorum partibus, sancta Mater Ecclesia ex apostolica fide pro sacris et canonicis habet, propterea quod, Spiritu Sancto inspirante conscripti (cf. Jn 20,31 2Tm 3,16 2P 1,19-21 2P 3,15-16), Deum habent auctorem, atque ut tales ipsi Ecclesiae traditi sunt. In sacris vero libris conficiendis Deus homines elegit, quos facultatibus ac viribus suis utentes adhibuit, ut Ipso in illis et per illos agente, ea omnia eaque sola, quae Ipse vellet, ut veri auctores scripto traderent. Cum ergo omne id, quod auctores inspirati seu hagiographi asserunt, retineri debeat assertum a Spiritu Sancto, inde Scripturae libri veritatem, quam Deus nostrae salutis causa Litteris Sacris consignari voluit, firmiter, fideliter et sine errore docere profitendi sunt. Itaque omnis Scriptura divinitus inspirata et utilis ad docendum, ad arguendum, ad corripiendum, ad erudiendum in iustitia: ut perfectus sit homo Dei, ad omne opus bonum instructus (2Tm 3,16-17, gr.) ."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p348","Chapter III. on the Divine Inspiration of Sacred Scripture and on Its Interpreta","But, since sacred Scripture is to be read and interpreted by the same Spirit through whom it was written, to rightly draw out the sense of the sacred texts, no less diligent attention is to be paid to the content and unity of the whole of Scripture, regard being had to the living tradition of the whole Church and the analogy of faith. It is the part of exegetes, according to these rules, to labor to more deeply understand and expound the sense of sacred Scripture, that by study, as it were prepared, the judgment of the Church may ripen. For all these things concerning the manner of interpreting Scripture ultimately are subject to the judgment of the Church, who fulfills the divine mandate and ministry of keeping and interpreting the word of God.","Cum autem Deus in Sacra Scriptura per homines more hominum locutus sit, interpres Sacrae Scripturae, ut perspiciat, quid Ipse nobiscum communicare voluerit, attente investigare debet, quid hagiographi reapse significare intenderint et eorum verbis manifestare Deo placuerit. Ad hagiographorum intentionem eruendam inter alia etiam genera litteraria respicienda sunt. Aliter enim atque aliter veritas in textibus vario modo historicis, vel propheticis, vel poeticis, vel in aliis dicendi generibus proponitur et exprimitur. Oportet porro ut interpres sensum inquirat, quem in determinatis adiunctis hagiographus, pro sui temporis et suae culturae condicione, ope generum litterariorum illo tempore adhibitorum exprimere intenderit et expresserit. Ad recte enim intelligendum id quod sacer auctor scripto asserere voluerit, rite attendendum est tum ad suetos illos nativos sentiendi, dicendi, narrandive modos, qui temporibus hagiographi vigebant, tum ad illos qui illo aevo in mutuo hominum commercio passim adhiberi solebant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p349","Chapter III. on the Divine Inspiration of Sacred Scripture and on Its Interpreta","13 In sacred Scripture, therefore, the saving truth and sanctity of God always being preserved, the admirable condescension of eternal Wisdom is manifested, that we may learn the ineffable benignity of God, and how great condescension of speech He used, having care and providence for our nature. For the words of God, expressed in human languages, have been made like to human speech, just as the Word of the eternal Father, having assumed the flesh of human weakness, was made like to men.","Sed, cum Sacra Scriptura eodem Spiritu quo scripta est etiam legenda et interpretanda sit, ad recte sacrorum textuum sensum eruendum, non minus diligenter respiciendum est ad contentum et unitatem totius Scripturae, ratione habita vivae totius Ecclesiae Traditionis et analogiae fidei. Exegetarum autem est secundum has regulas adlaborare ad Sacrae Scripturae sensum penitius intelligendum et exponendum, ut quasi praeparato studio, iudicium Ecclesiae maturetur. Cuncta enim haec, de ratione interpretandi Scripturam, Ecclesiae iudicio ultime subsunt, quae verbi Dei servandi et interpretandi divino fungitur mandato et ministerio."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p350","Chapter III. on the Divine Inspiration of Sacred Scripture and on Its Interpreta","14 The most loving God, solicitously intending and preparing the salvation of the whole human race, by a singular dispensation chose for Himself a people, to whom He would entrust promises. For having entered into a covenant with Abraham (cf. Gen 15:18) and with the people of Israel through Moses (cf. Ex 24:8), He so revealed Himself to His acquired people as the one true and living God by words and deeds, that Israel might experience what the ways of God with men were, and, God Himself speaking through the mouth of the prophets, might daily understand them more deeply and more clearly and set them forth more widely among the nations (cf. Ps 21:28-29; 95:1-3; Is 2:1-4; Jer 3:17). The economy of salvation, however, foretold, narrated, and explained by the sacred authors, stands as the true word of God in the books of the Old Testament; wherefore these books, divinely inspired, retain perpetual value: \"For whatever things have been written, have been written for our doctrine, that through the patience and consolation of the Scriptures we may have hope\" (Rom 15:4).","In Sacra Scriptura ergo manifestatur, salva semper Dei veritate et sanctitate, aeternae Sapientiae admirabilis condescensio, ut discamus ineffabilem Dei benignitatem, et quanta sermonis attemperatione usus sit, nostrae naturae providentiam et curam habens. Dei enim verba, humanis linguis expressa, humano sermoni assimilia facta sunt, sicut olim Aeterni Patris Verbum, humanae infirmitatis assumpta carne, hominibus simile factum est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p351","Chapter IV. on the Old Testament","15 The economy of the Old Testament was principally disposed for this, that it might prepare, prophetically foretell (cf. Lk 24:44; Jn 5:39; 1 Pet 1:10), and signify by various types (cf. 1 Cor 10:11) the coming of Christ the universal Redeemer and of the Messianic Kingdom. The books of the Old Testament, however, according to the condition of the human race before the time of salvation established by Christ, manifest to all both the knowledge of God and of man, and the ways in which the just and merciful God acts with men. Which books, although they also contain imperfect and temporary things, nevertheless show true divine pedagogy. Hence the same books, which express the living sense of God, in which sublime doctrines concerning God and saving wisdom concerning human life are hidden, and wonderful treasures of prayers, in which finally the mystery of our salvation lies hidden, are to be devoutly received by Christ's faithful.","Amantissimus Deus totius humani generis salutem sollicite intendens et praeparans, singulari dispensatione populum sibi elegit, cui promissiones concrederet. Foedere enim cum Abraham (cf. Gn 15,18) et cum plebe Israel per Moysen (cf. Ex 24,8) inito, populo sibi acquisito ita Se tamquam unicum Deum verum et vivum verbis ac gestis revelavit, ut Israel, quae divinae essent cum hominibus viae experiretur, easque, ipso Deo per os Prophetarum loquente, penitius et clarius in dies intellegeret atque latius in gentes exhiberet (cf. Ps 21,28-29 Ps 95,1-3 Is 2,1-4 Jr 3,17). Oeconomia autem salutis ab auctoribus sacris praenuntiata, enarrata atque explicata, ut verum Dei verbum in libris Veteris Testamenti exstat; quapropter hi libri divinitus inspirati perennem valorem servant: Quaecumque enim scripta sunt, ad nostram doctrinam scripta sunt, ut per patientiam et consolationem Scripturarum spem habeamus (Rm 15,4)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p352","Chapter IV. on the Old Testament","16 God, therefore, the inspirer and author of the books of both Testaments, so wisely disposed that the New lies hidden in the Old, and the Old is made manifest in the New. For, although Christ established the New Covenant in His blood (cf. Lk 22:20; 1 Cor 11:25), yet the books of the Old Testament, whole and entire, are taken up into the proclamation of the Gospel, and in the New Testament acquire and show their complete meaning (cf. Mt 5:17; Lk 24:27; Rom 16:25-26; 2 Cor 3:14-16), and in turn illuminate and explain it.","Veteris Testamenti oeconomia ad hoc potissimum disposita erat, ut Christi universorum redemptoris Regnique Messianici adventum praeparet, prophetice nuntiaret (cf. Lc 24,44 Jn 5,39 1P 1,10) et variis typis significaret (cf. 1Co 10,11). Veteris autem Testamenti libri, pro condicione humani generis ante tempora instauratae a Christo salutis, Dei et hominis cognitionem ac modos quibus Deus iustus et misericors cum hominibus agit, omnibus manifestant. Qui libri, quamvis etiam imperfecta et temporaria contineant, veram tamen paedagogiam divinam demonstrant. Unde iidem libri, qui vivum sensum Dei exprimunt, in quibus sublimes de Deo doctrinae ac salutaris de vita hominis sapientia mirabilesque precum thesauri reconduntur, in quibus tandem latet mysterium salutis nostrae, a Christifidelibus devote accipiendi sunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p353","Chapter IV. on the Old Testament","17 The word of God, which is the power of God for salvation to every believer (cf. Rom 1:16), is presented and shows its power in an excellent way in the writings of the New Testament. For when the fullness of time came (cf. Gal 4:4), the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us full of grace and truth (cf. Jn 1:14). Christ restored the Kingdom of God on earth, manifested His Father and Himself by deeds and words, and completed His work by death, resurrection, and glorious ascension, and the mission of the Holy Spirit. Lifted up from the earth, He draws all to Himself (cf. Jn 12:32), He who alone has the words of eternal life (cf. Jn 6:68). This mystery, however, was not made known to other generations as it has now been revealed to His holy Apostles and Prophets in the Holy Spirit (cf. Eph 3:4-6), that they might preach the Gospel, arouse faith in Jesus Christ as Lord, and gather the Church. Of these things the writings of the New Testament stand as a perpetual and divine testimony.","Deus igitur librorum utriusque Testamenti inspirator et auctor, ita sapienter disposuit, ut Novum in Vetere lateat et in Novo Vetus pateat. Nam, etsi Christus in sanguine suo Novum Foedus condidit (cf. Lc 22,20 1Co 11,25) libri tamen Veteris Testamenti integri in praeconio evangelico assumpti, in Novo Testamento significationem suam completam acquirunt et ostendunt (cf. Mt 5,17 Lc 24,27 Rm 16,25-26 2Co 3,14-16), illudque vicissim illuminant et explicant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p354","Chapter V. on the New Testament","18 It is not unknown to anyone that among all the Scriptures of the New Testament, the Gospels justly excel, as being the principal testimony concerning the life and doctrine of the incarnate Word, our Savior. The Church has always and everywhere held and holds that the four Gospels have apostolic origin. For what the Apostles by mandate of Christ preached, afterwards, the Holy Spirit inspiring, they themselves and apostolic men handed down to us in writing as the foundation of faith, namely the fourfold Gospel according to Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John.","Verbum Dei, quod virtus Dei est in salutem omni credenti (cf. Rm 1,16), in scriptis Novi Testamenti praecellenti modo praesentatur et vim suam exhibet. Ubi enim venit plenitudo temporis (cf. Ga 4,4), Verbum caro factum est et habitavit in nobis plenum gratiae et veritatis (cf. Jn 1,14). Christus Regnum Dei in terris instauravit, factis et verbis Patrem suum ac Seipsum manifestavit, atque morte, resurrectione et gloriosa ascensione missioneque Spiritus Sancti opus suum complevit. Exaltatus a terra omnes ad Seipsum thahit (cf. Jn 12,32, gr.), Ipse qui solus verba vitae aeternae habet (cf. Jn 6,68). Hoc autem mysterium aliis generationibus non est patefactum, sicut nunc revelatum est sanctis Apostolis Eius et Prophetis in Spiritu Sancto (cf. Ep 3,46, gr.), ut Evangelium praedicarent, fidem in Iesum Christum ac Dominum excitarent et Ecclesiam congregarent. Quarum rerum scripta Novi Testamenti exstant testimonium perenne atque divinum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p355","Chapter V. on the New Testament","19 Holy Mother Church has firmly and most constantly held and holds that the four listed Gospels, whose historicity she unhesitatingly affirms, faithfully hand down what Jesus the Son of God, living among men, really did and taught for their eternal salvation, until the day on which He was taken up (cf. Acts 1:1-2). The Apostles, after the Lord's ascension, handed down to their hearers those things which He had said and done with a fuller understanding, which they, instructed by the glorious events of Christ and taught by the light of the Spirit of truth, enjoyed. The sacred authors, however, wrote the four Gospels, selecting some things from the many handed down by word or already in writing, reducing some to a synthesis, explaining some in view of the condition of the churches, always retaining the form of proclamation, that they might communicate to us true and sincere things concerning Jesus. For they wrote with that intention, whether from their own memory and recollection, or from the testimony of those who from the beginning were eyewitnesses and ministers of the word, that we might know the truth concerning those things in which we have been instructed (cf. Lk 1:2-4).","Neminem fugit inter omnes, etiam Novi Testamenti Scripturas, Evangelia merito excellere, quippe quae praecipuum testimonium sint de Verbi Incarnati, Salvatoris nostri, vita atque doctrina. Quattuor Evangelia originem apostolicam habere Ecclesia semper et ubique tenuit ac tenet. Quae enim Apostoli ex mandato Christi praedicaverunt, postea divino afflante Spiritu, In scriptis, ipsi et apostolici viri nobis tradiderunt fidei fundamentum, quadriforme nempe Evangelium, secundum Matthaeum, Marcum, Lucam et Ioannem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p356","Chapter V. on the New Testament","20 The canon of the New Testament, besides the four Gospels, also contains the epistles of St. Paul and other apostolic writings written under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit, by which, according to the wise design of God, those things concerning Christ the Lord are confirmed, His genuine doctrine is more and more declared, the saving power of the divine work of Christ is preached, the beginnings and wonderful diffusion of the Church are narrated and its glorious consummation foretold. For to His Apostles the Lord Jesus, as He had promised, was present (cf. Mt 28:20), and sent to them the Paraclete Spirit, who would lead them into the fullness of truth (cf. Jn 16:13).","Sancta Mater Ecclesia firmiter et constantissime tenuit ac tenet quattuor recensita Evangelia, quorum historicitatem incunctanter affirmat, fideliter tradere quae Iesus Dei Filius, vitam inter homines degens, ad aeternam eorum salutem reapse fecit et docuit, usque in diem qua assumptus est (cf. Ac 1,1-2). Apostoli quidem post ascensionem Domini, illa quae Ipse dixerat et fecerat, auditoribus ea pleniore intelligentia tradiderunt, qua ipsi, eventibus gloriosis Christi instructi et lumine Spiritus veritatis edocti, fruebantur. Auctores autem sacri quattuor Evangelia conscripserunt, quaedam e multis aut ore aut iam scripto traditis seligentes, quaedam in synthesim redigentes, vel statui ecclesiarum attendendo explanantes, formam denique praeconii retinentes, ita semper ut vera et sincera de Iesu nobiscum communicarent. Illa enim intentione scripserunt, sive ex sua propria memoria et recordatione, sive ex textimonio illorum qui ad initio ipsi viderunt et ministri fuerunt sermonis, ut cognoscamus eorum verborum de quibus eruditi sumus, veritatem (cf. Lc 1,2-4)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p357","Chapter V. on the New Testament","21 The Church has always venerated the divine Scriptures just as she has venerated the Lord's body itself, since, especially in the sacred Liturgy, she does not cease to take and to offer to the faithful the bread of life from the table both of the word of God and of the body of Christ. She has always held and holds them, together with sacred Tradition, as the supreme rule of her faith, since, being inspired by God and consigned to writing once for all, they impart the word of God Himself unchangeably, and cause the voice of the Holy Spirit to resound in the words of the Prophets and Apostles. Therefore all ecclesiastical preaching, just as the Christian religion itself, must be nourished and ruled by sacred Scripture. For in the sacred books the Father who is in heaven meets His sons most lovingly and confers with them; and such great force and power is in the word of God that it stands as the support and vigor of the Church, and for the sons of the Church as strength of faith, food of the soul, a pure and perpetual source of spiritual life. Hence the saying excellently applies to sacred Scripture: \"For the word of God is living and efficient\" (Heb 4:12), which is powerful to build up and to give inheritance among all the sanctified (Acts 20:32; cf. 1 Th 2:13).","Novi Testamenti canon praeter quattuor Evangelia etiam epistulas sancti Pauli aliaque scripta apostolica Spiritu Sancto inspirante exarata continet, quibus, ex sapienti Dei consilio, ea quae sunt de Christo Domino confirmantur, genuina Eius doctrina magis magisque declaratur, salutifera virtus divini operis Christi praedicatur, Ecclesiae initia ac admirabilis diffusio narrantur eiusque consummatio gloriosa praenuntiatur. Apostolis enim suis Dominus Iesus sicut promiserat affuit (cf. Mt 28,20) et iis Paraclitum Spiritum misit, qui eos in plenitudinem veritatis induceret (cf. Jn 16,13)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p358","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","22 Access to sacred Scripture must be widely open to Christ's faithful. Wherefore the Church from the beginning received as her own that most ancient Greek version of the Old Testament called the Septuagint; she also always holds in honor other Eastern versions and Latin versions, especially that called the Vulgate. But since the word of God must be available at all times, the Church with motherly solicitude takes care that suitable and correct translations be made into various languages, especially from the primary texts of the sacred books. Which, if, given the opportunity and the authority of the Church approving, they be made with common effort also with our separated brothers, can be used by all Christians.","Divinas Scripturas sicut et ipsum Corpus dominicum semper venerata est Ecclesia, cum, maxime in sacra Liturgia, non desinat ex mensa tam verbi Dei quam Corporis Christi panem vitae sumere atque fidelibus porrigere. Eas una cum Sacra Traditione semper ut supremam fidei suae regulam habuit et habet, cum a Deo inspiratae et semel pro semper litteris consignatae, verbum ipsius Dei immutabiliter impertiant, atque in verbis Prophetarum Apostolorumque vocem Spiritus Sancti personare faciant. Omnis ergo praedicatio ecclesiastica sicut ipsa religio christiana Sacra Scriptura nutriatur et regatur oportet. In sacris enim libris Pater qui in caelis est filiis suis peramanter occurrit et cum eis sermonem confert; tanta autem verbo Dei vis ac virtus inest, ut Ecclesiae sustentaculum ac vigor, et Ecclesiae filiis fidei robur, animae cibus, vitae spiritualis fons purus et perennis exstet. Unde de Sacra Scriptura excellenter valent dicta: Vivus est enim sermo Dei et efficax (He 4,12), qui potens est aedificare et dare hereditatem in sanctificatis omnibus (Ac 20,32 cf. 1Th 2,13)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p359","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","23 The Spouse of the incarnate Word, the Church namely, taught by the Holy Spirit, strives to attain to a deeper understanding of the sacred Scriptures day by day, that she may unceasingly feed her sons on divine words; wherefore she also rightly fosters the study of the holy Fathers both of the East and West and of the sacred Liturgies. Catholic exegetes, however, and other cultivators of sacred theology, with sedulously joined forces, ought to labor, under the vigilance of the sacred Magisterium, to investigate and set forth the divine Scriptures with opportune aids, that as many ministers of the divine word as possible may be able fruitfully to supply the nourishment of the Scriptures to the people of God, which enlightens the mind, strengthens the will, and inflames the hearts of men to the love of God. The sacred Synod gives heart to the sons of the Church, cultivators of biblical matters, that they may continue to carry out the work happily undertaken, with renewed strength day by day, with all study according to the sense of the Church.","Christifidelibus aditus ad Sacram Scripturam late pateat oportet. Qua de causa Ecclesia inde ab initiis graecam illam antiquissimam Veteris Testamenti versionem a LXX viris nuncupatam ut suam suscepit; alias vero versiones orientales et versiones latinas, praecipue illam Vulgatam vocant, semper in honore habet. Cum autem verbum Dei omnibus temporibus praesto esse debeat, Ecclesia materna sollicitudine curat, ut aptae ac rectae exarentur in varias linguas versiones, praesertim ex primigenis Sacrorum Librorum textibus. Quae si, data opportunitate et annuente Ecclesiae auctoritate, conficiantur communi etiam cum fratribus seiunctis nisu, ab omnibus christianis adhiberi poterunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p360","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","24 Sacred theology rests on the written word of God, together with sacred Tradition, as on a perpetual foundation, and in it it is most firmly strengthened and always grows young, investigating all truth hidden in the mystery of Christ under the light of faith. The sacred Scriptures, however, contain the word of God and, because they are inspired, are truly the word of God; therefore the study of sacred page is to be as the soul of sacred theology. By the same word of Scripture, moreover, the ministry of the word, namely pastoral preaching, catechesis, and all Christian instruction, in which the liturgical homily must hold an excellent place, is healthfully nourished and holy grows strong.","Verbi incarnati Sponsa, Ecclesia nempe, a Sancto Spiritu edocta, ad profundiorem in dies Scripturarum Sacrarum intelligentiam assequendam accedere satagit, ut filios suos divinis eloquiis indesinenter pascat; quapropter etiam studium sanctorum Patrum tum Orientis tum Occidentis et sacrarum Liturgiarum rite fovet. Exegetae autem catholici, aliique Sacrae Theologiae cultores, collatis sedulo viribus, operam dent oportet, ut sub vigilantia Sacri Magisterii, aptis subsidiis divinas Litteras ita investigent et proponant, ut quam plurimi divini verbi administri possint plebi Dei Scripturarum pabulum fructuose suppeditare, quod mentem illuminet, firmet voluntates, hominum corda ad Dei amorem accendat. Sacra Synodus Ecclesiae filiis, biblicarum rerum cultoribus, animum addit, ut opus feliciter susceptum, renovatis in dies viribus, omni studio secundum sensum Ecclesiae exsequi pergant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p361","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","25 Wherefore, all clerics, especially the priests of Christ and others who as deacons or catechists lawfully attend to the ministry of the word, must adhere to the Scriptures by assiduous sacred reading and careful study, lest any of them become an empty outward preacher of the word of God, who is not a hearer from within, while he ought to communicate the most ample riches of the divine word, especially in the sacred Liturgy, with the faithful entrusted to him. Likewise the holy Synod strongly and especially exhorts all Christ's faithful, especially religious, that by frequent reading of the divine Scriptures they learn the eminent knowledge of Jesus Christ (Phil 3:8). For ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ. Therefore let them gladly approach the sacred text itself, whether through the sacred Liturgy, rich in divine words, or through pious reading, or through institutions apt for this and other aids, which, the pastors of the Church approving and taking care, are laudably spread everywhere in our time. Let them be mindful, however, that prayer should accompany the reading of sacred Scripture, that it may become a conversation between God and man; for we speak to Him when we pray; we hear Him when we read the divine oracles.","Sacra Theologia in verbo Dei scripto, una cum Sacra Traditione, tamquam in perenni fundamento innititur, in eoque ipsa firmissime roboratur semperque iuvenescit, omnem veritatem in mysterio Christi conditam sub lumine fidei perscrutando. Sacrae autem Scripturae verbum Dei continent et, quia inspiratae, vere verbum Dei sunt; ideoque Sacrae Paginae studium sit veluti anima Sacrae Theologiae. Eodem autem Scripturae verbo etiam ministerium verbi, pastoralis nempe praedicatio, catechesis omnisque instructio christiana, in qua homilia liturgica eximium locum habeat oportet, salubriter nutritur sancteque virescit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p362","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","It pertains to the sacred Bishops, in whom the apostolic doctrine resides, opportunely to instruct the faithful entrusted to them for the right use of the divine books, especially of the New Testament and above all the Gospels, through translations of the sacred texts, which are furnished with necessary and truly sufficient explanations, that the sons of the Church may safely and usefully converse with the sacred Scriptures and be imbued with their spirit. Moreover, editions of sacred Scripture, furnished with apt annotations, for the use also of non-Christians and accommodated to their conditions, are to be prepared, which the pastors of souls or Christians of whatever state may wisely take care to spread.","Quapropter clericos omnes, imprimis Christi sacerdotes ceterosque qui ut diaconi vel catechistae ministerio verbi legitime instant, assidua lectione sacra atque exquisito studio in Scripturis haerere necesse est, ne quis eorum fiat verbi Dei inanis forinsecus praedicator, qui non est intus auditor, dum verbi divini amplissimas divitias, speciatim in sacra Liturgia, cum fidelibus sibi commissis communicare debet. Pariter Sancta Synodus christifideles omnes, praesertim sodales religiosos, vehementer peculiariterque exhortatur, ut frequenti divinarum Scripturarum lectione eminentem scientiam Iesu Christi (Ph 3,8) ediscant. Ignoratio enim Scripturarum ignoratio Christi est. Libenter igitur ad sacrum textum ipsum accedant, sive per sacram Liturgiam divinis eloquiis confertam, sive per piam lectionem, sive per institutiones ad id aptas aliaque subsidia, quae approbantibus et curantibus Pastoribus Ecclesiae ubique nostro tempore laudabiliter diffunduntur. Meminerint autem orationem concomitari debere Sacrae Scripturae lectionem, ut fiat colloquium inter Deum et hominem; nam illum alloquimur, cum oramus; illum audimus, cum divina legimus oracula."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p363","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","26 Thus, through the reading and study of the sacred books, the word of God may run and be glorified (2 Th 3:1), and the treasure of revelation, entrusted to the Church, may more and more fill the hearts of men. Just as from the assiduous frequentation of the eucharistic mystery the life of the Church receives increase, so a new impulse of spiritual life may be hoped from increased veneration of the word of God, which remains forever (Is 40:8; 1 Pet 1:23-25).","Sacris autem Antistitibus, apud quos est apostolica doctrina, fideles sibi commissos ad rectum divinorum libro rum usum, praesertim Novi Testamenti et imprimis Evangeliorum, opportune instituere competit per sacrorum textuum versiones, quae necessariis et vere sufficientibus explicationibus instructae sint, ut tuto ac utiliter Ecclesiae filii cum Scripturis Sacris conversentur earumque spiritu imbuantur. Insuper editiones Sacrae Scripturae, aptis instructae adnotationibus, ad usum etiam non-christianorum eorundemque conditionibus accommodatae, conficiantur, quas quoquomodo sive animarum Pastores sive Christiani cuiuscumque status spargere sapienter curent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p364","Chapter VI. on Sacred Scripture in the Life of the Church","1 Men of our age are becoming more and more conscious of the dignity of the human person, and the number of those who require that men in acting enjoy their own counsel and responsible freedom, moved not by coercion but by the sense of duty, increases. Likewise they demand a juridical delimitation of public power, lest the bounds of legitimate freedom of both the person and of associations be too circumscribed. This exigency of freedom in human society chiefly respects those things which are of the human spirit, especially those which pertain to the free exercise of religion in society. Attentively considering these aspirations of minds, and intending to declare how much they are conformable to truth and justice, this Vatican Synod examines the sacred tradition and doctrine of the Church, from which it ever brings forth new things congruent with old.","Ita ergo lectione et studio Sacrorum Librorum sermo Dei currat et clarificetur (2Th 3,1), thesaurusque revelationis, Ecclesiae concreditus, magis magisque corda hominum impleat. Sicut ex assidua frequentatione mysterii Eucharistici Ecclesiae vita incrementum suscipit, ita novum spiritualis vitae impulsum sperare licet ex adaucta veneratione verbi Dei, quod manet in aeternum (Is 40,8 1P 1,23-25)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p365","Dignitatis Humanae","First therefore the sacred Synod professes that God Himself has made known the way to the human race by which, serving Him, men may be able to be saved and blessed in Christ. This one true Religion we believe to subsist in the catholic and apostolic Church, to which the Lord Jesus entrusted the office of spreading it to all men, saying to the Apostles: \"Go therefore and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you\" (Mt 28:19-20). All men, however, are bound to seek the truth, especially in those things which regard God and His Church, and when known, to embrace and keep it.","DIGNITATIS HUMANAE personae homines hac nostra aetate magis in dies conscii fiunt, atque numerus eorum crescit qui exigunt, ut in agendo homines proprio suo consilio et libertate responsabili fruantur et utantur, non coercitione commoti, sed officii conscientia ducti. Itemque postulant iuridicam delimitationem potestatis publicae, ne fines honestae libertatis et personae et associationum nimis circumscribantur. Quae libertatis exigentia in societate humana ea maxime respicit quae sunt animi humani bona, imprimis quidem ea quae liberum in societate religionis exercitium spectant. Ad has animorum appetitiones diligenter attendens, sibique proponens declarare quantum sint veritati et iustitiae conformes, haec Vaticana Synodus sacram Ecclesiae traditionem doctrinamque scrutatur, ex quibus nova semper cum veteribus congruentia profert."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p366","Dignitatis Humanae","Likewise, the sacred Synod professes that these duties touch and bind the conscience of men, and that truth imposes itself by no other way than by the force of truth itself, which gently and at the same time strongly penetrates minds. Moreover, since religious freedom, which men require in fulfilling the duty of worshipping God, respects immunity from coercion in civil society, it leaves intact the traditional catholic doctrine concerning the moral duty of men and societies toward the true religion and the one Church of Christ. Moreover, in treating of this religious freedom, the sacred Synod intends to develop the doctrine of recent Roman Pontiffs concerning the inviolable rights of the human person and concerning the juridical ordering of society.","Primum itaque profitetur Sacra Synodus Deum Ipsum viam generi humano notam fecisse per quam, Ipsi inserviendo, homines in Christo salvi et beati fieri possint. Hanc unicam veram Religionem subsistere credimus in catholica et apostolica Ecclesia, cui Dominus Iesus munus concredidit eam ad universos homines diffundendi, dicens Apostolis: Euntes ergo docete omnes gentes baptizantes eos in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti, docentes eos servare omnia quaecumque mandavi vobis (Mt 28,19-20). Homines vero cuncti tenentur veritatem, praesertim in iis quae Deum Eiusque Ecclesiam respiciunt, quaerere eamque cognitam amplecti ac servare."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p367","Dignitatis Humanae","2 This Vatican Synod declares that the human person has a right to religious freedom. This freedom consists in this, that all men ought to be immune from coercion on the part of individuals or of social groups and of any human power, and in such a way that in religious matters neither is anyone forced to act against his conscience, nor prevented from acting according to his conscience, privately and publicly, either alone or associated with others, within due limits. Moreover, it declares that the right to religious freedom is truly founded on the very dignity of the human person, as it is known both by the revealed word of God and by reason itself. This right of the human person to religious freedom is to be acknowledged in the juridical ordering of society, that it may become a civil right.","Pariter vero profitetur Sacra Synodus officia haec hominum conscientiam tangere ac vincire, nec aliter veritatem sese imponere nisi vi ipsius veritatis, quae suaviter simul ac fortiter mentibus illabitur. Porro, quum libertas religiosa, quam homines in exsequendo officio Deum colendi exigunt, immunitatem a coercitione in societate civili respiciat, integram relinquit traditionalem doctrinam catholicam de morali hominum ac societatum officio erga veram religionem et unicam Christi Ecclesiam. Insuper, de hac libertate religiosa agens, Sacra Synodus recentiorum Summorum Pontificum doctrinam de inviolabilibus humanae personae iuribus necnon de iuridica ordinatione societatis evolvere intendit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p368","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","According to their dignity, all men, because they are persons, namely endowed with reason and free will and therefore endowed with personal responsibility, are impelled by their own nature and are bound by a moral obligation to seek the truth, especially that which concerns religion. They are also bound to adhere to the truth when known and to order their whole life according to the demands of the truth. Men, however, cannot satisfy this obligation in a manner consonant with their own nature unless they enjoy psychological freedom at the same time as immunity from external coercion. The right to religious freedom is therefore founded not on the subjective disposition of the person but on his very nature. Therefore the right to this immunity persists even in those who do not satisfy the obligation of seeking the truth and adhering to it; nor can its exercise be impeded, provided the just public order is preserved.","Haec Vaticana Synodus declarat personam humanam ius habere ad libertatem religiosam. Huiusmodi libertas in eo consistit, quod omnes homines debent immunes esse a coercitione ex parte sive singulorum sive coetuum socialium et cuiusvis potestatis humanae, et ita quidem ut in re religiosa neque aliquis cogatur ad agendum contra suam conscientiam neque impediatur, quominus iuxta suam conscientiam agat privatim et publice, vel solus vel aliis consociatus, intra debitos limites. Insuper declarat ius ad libertatem religiosam esse revera fundatum in ipsa dignitate personae humanae, qualis et verbo Dei revelato et ipsa ratione cognoscitur. Hoc ius personae humanae ad libertatem religiosam in iuridica societatis ordinatione ita est agnoscendum, ut ius civile evadat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p369","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","3 Which becomes yet more clear to one considering that the supreme norm of human life is the divine law itself, eternal, objective, and universal, by which God, according to the counsel of His wisdom and love, orders, directs, and governs the whole world and the ways of the human community. Of this His law God makes man a partaker, so that man, by divine Providence gently disposing, may be able more and more to recognize the unchangeable truth. Wherefore each has the duty and therefore the right to seek the truth in religious matters, that by using suitable means he may prudently form right and true judgments of conscience.","Secundum dignitatem suam homines cuncti, quia personae sunt, ratione scilicet et libera voluntate praediti ideoque personali responsabilitate aucti, sua ipsorum natura impelluntur necnon morali tenentur obligatione ad veritatem quaerendam, illam imprimis quae religionem spectat. Tenentur quoque veritati cognitae adhaerere atque totam vitam suam iuxta exigentias veritatis ordinare. Huic autem obligationi satisfacere homines, modo suae propriae naturae consentaneo, non possunt nisi libertate psychologica simul atque immunitate a coercitione externa fruantur. Non ergo in subiectiva personae dispositione, sed in ipsa eius natura ius ad libertatem religiosam fundatur. Quamobrem ius ad hanc immunitatem perseverat etiam in iis qui obligationi quaerendi veritatem eique adhaerendi non satisfaciunt; eiusque exercitium impediri nequit dummodo iustus ordo publicus servetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p370","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","Truth, however, is to be sought in a manner proper to the dignity of the human person and his social nature, namely by free investigation, with the help of teaching or instruction, communication, and dialogue, by which men set forth to one another the truth which they have found or think they have found, that they may help one another in seeking the truth; but once known, truth must be firmly adhered to by personal assent.","Quae clarius adhuc patent consideranti supremam humanae vitae normam esse ipsam legem divinam, aeternam, obiectivam atque universalem, qua Deus consilio sapientiae et dilectionis suae mundum universum viasque communitatis humanae ordinat, dirigit, gubernat. Huius suae legis Deus hominem participem reddit, ita ut homo, providentia divina suaviter disponente, veritatem incommutabilem magis magisque agnoscere possit. Quapropter unusquisque officium ideoque et ius habet veritatem in re religiosa quaerendi ut sibi, mediis adhibitis idoneis, recta et vera conscientiae iudicia prudenter efformet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p371","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","Man perceives and acknowledges the dictates of the divine law through his conscience, which he is bound faithfully to follow in all his activity, that he may come to God, his end. He is therefore not to be forced to act against his conscience. Nor is he to be prevented from acting according to his conscience, especially in matters religious. For the exercise of religion, by its very nature, consists chiefly in internal acts, voluntary and free, by which man directly orders himself to God: such acts can neither be commanded nor prohibited by mere human power. The very social nature of man, however, requires that he express internal acts of religion externally, communicate with others in religious matters, and profess his religion in a communal manner.","Veritas autem inquirenda est modo dignitati humanae personae eiusque naturae sociali proprio, libera scilicet inquisitione, ope magisterii seu institutionis, communicationis atque dialogi, quibus alii aliis exponunt veritatem quam invenerunt vel invenisse putant, ut sese invicem in veritate inquirenda adiuvent; veritati autem cognitae firmiter adhaerendum est assensu personali."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p372","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","Therefore it is an injury to the human person and to the very order established by God for men, if free exercise of religion in society is denied to man, the just public order being preserved. Moreover, religious acts, by which men privately and publicly order themselves to God from their mind's intention, by their nature transcend the earthly and temporal order of things. Therefore civil power, whose proper end is to take care of temporal common good, ought indeed to recognize and favor the religious life of citizens, but it is said to exceed its limits if it presumes to direct or impede religious acts.","Dictamina vero legis divinae homo percipit et agnoscit mediante conscientia sua; quam tenetur fideliter sequi in universa sua activitate, ut ad Deum, finem suum, perveniat. Non est ergo cogendus, ut contra suam conscientiam agat. Sed neque impediendus est, quominus iuxta suam conscientiam operetur, praesertim in re religiosa. Exercitium namque religionis, ex ipsa eius indole, consistit imprimis in actibus internis voluntariis et liberis, quibus homo sese ad Deum directe ordinat: huiusmodi actus a potestate mere humana nec imperari nec prohiberi possunt. Ipsa autem socialis hominis natura exigit, ut homo internos religionis actus externe exprimat, cum aliis in re religiosa communicet, suam religionem modo communitario profiteatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p373","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","4 Freedom, that is immunity from coercion in religious matters, which is due to individual persons, is also to be acknowledged to those acting in common. For religious communities are required by the social nature both of man and of religion itself. Therefore, to these communities, as long as the just exigencies of public order are not violated, immunity is of right due, that they may govern themselves according to their own norms, honor the Supreme Being with public worship, help their members in exercising religious life and sustain them with doctrine, and promote those institutions in which the members cooperate to order their own life according to their religious principles.","Iniuria ergo humanae personae et ipsi ordini hominibus a Deo statuto fit, si homini denegetur liberum in societate religionis exercitium, iusto ordine publico servato. Praeterea actus religiosi, quibus homines privatim et publice sese ad Deum ex animi sententia ordinant, natura sua terrestrem et temporalem rerum ordinem transcendunt. Potestas igitur civilis, cuius finis proprius est bonum commune temporale curare, religiosam quidem civium vitam agnoscere eique favere debet, sed limites suos excedere dicenda est, si actus religiosos dirigere vel impedire praesumat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p374","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","It likewise pertains to religious communities to have the right that they be not prevented by legal means or administrative action of civil power from selecting, educating, appointing, and transferring their own ministers, from communicating with religious authorities and communities living in other parts of the world, from erecting religious buildings, and from acquiring and enjoying suitable goods. Religious communities also have the right not to be prevented from publicly teaching and bearing witness to their faith by word and writing. In disseminating and introducing religious faith, however, one must always abstain from any kind of action that seems to savor of coercion or dishonest or less right persuasion, especially when dealing with the more simple or the needy. Such a manner of acting is to be considered as an abuse of one's own right and a violation of the right of others.","Libertas seu immunitas a coercitione in re religiosa, quae singulis personis competit, etiam ipsis in communi agentibus agnoscenda est. Communitates enim religiosae a sociali natura tum hominis tum ipsius religionis requiruntur. His igitur communitatibus, dummodo iustae exigentiae ordinis publici non violentur, iure debetur immunitas, ut secundum proprias normas sese regant, Numen supremum cultu publico honorent, membra sua in vita religiosa exercenda adiuvent et doctrina sustentent atque eas institutiones promoveant, in quibus membra cooperentur ad vitam propriam secundum sua principia religiosa ordinandam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p375","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","Moreover, to religious freedom it pertains that religious communities be not prohibited from freely showing the singular power of their doctrine in ordering society and in vivifying all human activity. Finally, the right of men, moved by their own religious sense, freely to hold meetings or to form educational, cultural, charitable, social associations is founded on the social nature of man and on the very nature of religion.","Communitatibus religiosis pariter competit ius, ne mediis legalibus vel actione administrativa potestatis civilis impediantur in suis propriis ministris seligendis, educandis, nominandis atque transferendis, in communicando cum auctoritatibus et communitatibus religiosis, quae in aliis orbis terrarum partibus degunt, in aedificiis religiosis erigendis, necnon in bonis congruis acquirendis et fruendis. Communitates religiosae ius etiam habent, ne impediantur in sua fide ore et scripto publice docenda atque testanda. In fide autem religiosa disseminanda et in usibus inducendis abstinendum semper est ab omni actionis genere, quod coercitionem vel suasionem inhonestam aut minus rectam sapere videatur, praesertim quando de rudioribus vel de egenis agitur. Talis modus agendi ut abusus iuris proprii et laesio iuris aliorum considerari debet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p376","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","5 To every family, as a society enjoying a proper and primordial right, it pertains to have the right freely to order its own domestic religious life under the direction of the parents. To parents, moreover, it pertains to determine the manner of religious education to be given to their children, according to their own religious persuasion. Therefore the right of parents to choose schools or other means of education in true freedom is to be recognized by civil power, nor are unjust burdens to be imposed on them directly or indirectly on account of this freedom of choice. Moreover, the rights of parents are violated if children are forced to attend school lessons which do not correspond to the religious persuasion of the parents, or if a single educational system is imposed from which all religious formation is wholly excluded.","Praeterea ad libertatem religiosam spectat, quod communitates religiosae non prohibeantur libere ostendere singularem suae doctrinae virtutem in ordinanda societate ac tota vivificanda activitate humana. Tandem in sociali hominis natura atque in ipsa indole religionis fundatur ius quo homines, suo ipsorum sensu religioso moti, libere possunt conventus habere vel associationes educativas, culturales, caritativas, sociales constituere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p377","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","6 Since the common good of society, which is the sum of those conditions of social life by which men can more fully and conveniently attain their own perfection, especially consists in the preserved rights and duties of the human person, the care of the right to religious freedom pertains both to citizens and to social groups and to civil powers and to the Church and to other religious communities, each in its own manner, according to their duty toward the common good. To protect and promote the inviolable rights of man essentially pertains to the office of every civil power. Therefore civil power ought to undertake the protection of the religious freedom of all citizens by just laws and by other suitable means effectively, and to supply favorable conditions for fostering religious life, that citizens may truly be able to exercise the rights of religion and to fulfill its duties, and society itself may enjoy the goods of justice and peace, which proceed from the fidelity of men toward God and His holy will.","Cuique familiae, utpote quae est societas proprio ac primordiali iure gaudens, competit ius ad libere ordinandam religiosam vitam suam domesticam sub moderatione parentum. His autem competit ius ad determinandam rationem institutionis religiosae suis liberis tradendae, iuxta suam propriam religiosam persuasionem. Itaque a civili potestate agnoscendum est ius parentum deligendi, vera cum libertate, scholas vel alia educationis media, neque ob hanc electionis libertatem sunt eis iniusta onera sive directe sive indirecte imponenda. Praeterea iura parentum violantur, si liberi ad frequentandas lectiones scholares cogantur quae parentum persuasioni religiosae non correspondeant, vel si unica imponatur educationis ratio, ex qua formatio religiosa omnino excludatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p378","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","If, the particular circumstances of peoples being attended to, a special civil recognition is given to one religious community in the juridical ordering of the city, it is necessary that at the same time for all citizens and religious communities the right to freedom in religious matters be acknowledged and observed. Finally, by civil power it is to be provided that the juridical equality of citizens, which itself pertains to the common good of society, never be openly or secretly injured on account of religious reasons, nor that discrimination be made among them. Hence it follows that it is unlawful for public power, by force or fear or other means, to impose on citizens the profession or rejection of any religion, or to prevent anyone from entering or leaving a religious community. So much the more is it acting against the will of God and the sacred rights of the person and the family of nations when violence is used in any way to destroy or repress religion either in the whole human race or in any region or in a certain group.","Cum societatis commune bonum, quod est summa earum vitae socialis condicionum, quibus homines suam ipsorum perfectionem possunt plenius atque expeditius consequi, maxime in humanae personae servatis iuribus et officiis consistat, cura iuris ad libertatem religiosam tum ad cives tum ad coetus sociales tum ad potestates civiles tum ad Ecclesiam aliasque communitates religiosas spectat, modo unicuique proprio, pro eorum erga bonum commune officio. Inviolabilia hominis iura tueri ac promovere ad cuiusvis potestatis civilis officium essentialiter pertinet. Debet igitur potestas civilis per iustas leges et per alia media apta efficaciter suscipere tutelam libertatis religiosae omnium civium, ac propitias suppeditare condiciones ad vitam religiosam fovendam, ut cives revera religionis iura exercere eiusdemque officia adimplere valeant et ipsa societas fruatur bonis iustitiae et pacis, quae proveniunt ex fidelitate hominum erga Deum Eiusque sanctam voluntatem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p379","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","7 The right to freedom in religious matters is exercised in human society, therefore its use is subject to certain moderating norms. In the use of all freedoms the moral principle of personal and social responsibility is to be observed: in exercising their rights, individual men and social groups are bound by moral law to have regard for the rights of others and their own duties toward others and the common good of all. All must act toward all according to justice and humanity. Moreover, since civil society has the right to protect itself against abuses which may be had under pretext of religious freedom, it chiefly pertains to civil power to provide such protection; which, however, must be done not arbitrarily or by iniquely favoring one party, but according to juridical norms, conformable to the objective moral order, which are demanded by the effective protection of the rights of all citizens and their peaceful composition, and by the sufficient care of that honest public peace which is ordered conviviality in true justice, and by the due custody of public morality. All these constitute a fundamental part of the common good and come under the notion of public order. Moreover, the custom of integral freedom in society is to be preserved, according to which freedom ought to be acknowledged to man as much as possible, and not restricted unless when and insofar as necessary.","Si attentis populorum circumstantiis peculiaribus uni communitati religiosae specialis civilis agnitio in iuridica civitatis ordinatione tribuitur, necesse est ut simul omnibus civibus et communitatibus religiosis ius ad libertatem in re religiosa agnoscatur et observetur. Denique a potestate civili providendum est, ne civium aequalitas iuridica, quae ipsa ad commune societatis bonum pertinet, unquam sive aperte sive occulte laedatur propter rationes religiosas, neve inter eos discriminatio fiat. Hinc sequitur nefas esse potestati publicae, per vim vel metum aut alia media civibus imponere professionem aut reiectionem cuiusvis religionis, vel impedire quominus quisquam communitatem religiosam aut ingrediatur aut relinquat. Eo magis contra voluntatem Dei et contra sacra personae et familiae gentium iura agitur, quando vis quocumque modo adhibeatur ad religionem delendam vel cohibendam sive in toto genere humano sive in aliqua regione sive in determinato coetu."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p380","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","8 Men of our age are pressed in various ways and come into peril lest they be deprived of their own free counsel. On the other hand, not a few seem so inclined that under the appearance of freedom they reject all subjection and make light of due obedience. Wherefore this Vatican Synod exhorts all, especially those who have care of educating others, that they strive to form men who, obedient to the moral order, obey legitimate authority and are lovers of genuine freedom; men namely who, judging matters in the light of truth by their own counsel, order their activities with a sense of responsibility, and strive to pursue whatever things are true and just, willingly associating their work with others. Religious freedom, therefore, ought also to serve and to be ordered to this, that men may act with greater responsibility in fulfilling their own duties in social life.","Ius ad libertatem in re religiosa exercetur in societate humana, ideoque eius usus quibusdam normis moderantibus obnoxius est. In usu omnium libertatum observandum est principium morale responsabilitatis personalis et socialis: in iuribus suis exercendis singuli homines coetusque sociales lege morali obligantur rationem habere et iurium aliorum et suorum erga alios officiorum et boni omnium communis. Cum omnibus secundum iustitiam et humanitatem agendum est. Praeterea cum societas civilis ius habet sese protegendi contra abusus qui haberi possint sub praetextu libertatis religiosae, praecipue ad potestatem civilem pertinet huiusmodi protectionem praestare; quod tamen fieri debet non modo arbitrario aut uni parti inique favendo, sed secundum normas iuridicas, ordini morali obiectivo conformes, quae postulantur ab efficaci iurium tutela pro omnibus civibus eorumque pacifica compositione, et a sufficienti cura istius honestae pacis publicae quae est ordinata conviventia in vera iustitia, et a debita custodia publicae moralitatis. Haec omnia partem boni communis fundamentalem constituunt et sub ratione ordinis publici veniunt. Ceterum servanda est integrae libertatis consuetudo in societate, secundum quam libertas debet quam maxime homini agnosci, nec restringenda est nisi quando et prout est necessarium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p381","I. General Rationale of Religious Freedom","9 What this Vatican Synod declares concerning the right of man to religious freedom has its foundation in the dignity of the person, the exigencies of which have become more fully known to human reason through the experience of centuries. Indeed, this doctrine of freedom has its roots in divine Revelation, wherefore it is to be the more sacredly kept by Christians. For although Revelation does not expressly affirm the right to immunity from external coercion in religious matters, it nevertheless lays open the dignity of the human person in all its amplitude, demonstrates the respect of Christ for man's freedom in fulfilling the duty of believing the word of God, and teaches us concerning the spirit which disciples of such a Master ought to acknowledge and follow in all things. By which all, the general principles are illustrated upon which the doctrine of this Declaration concerning religious freedom is founded. Especially, religious freedom in society is fully congruent with the freedom of the act of Christian faith.","Nostrae aetatis homines varia ratione premuntur et in periculum veniunt ne proprio libero consilio destituantur. Ex altera autem parte non pauci ita propensi videntur, ut specie libertatis omnem subiectionem reiciant ac debitam oboedientiam parvi faciant. Quapropter haec Vaticana Synodus omnes hortatur, praesertim vero eos qui curam habent alios educandi, ut homines formare satagant, qui ordini morali obsequentes legitimae auctoritati oboediant et genuinae libertatis amatores sint; homines nempe, qui proprio consilio res in luce veritatis diiudicent, activitates suas cum sensu responsabilitatis disponant, et quaecumque sunt vera atque iusta prosequi nitantur, operam suam libenter cum ceteris consociando. Religiosa igitur libertas etiam ad hoc inservire et ordinari debet, ut homines in suis ipsorum officiis adimplendis in vita sociali maiore cum responsabilitate agant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p382","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","10 It is capital among the principal doctrines of catholic doctrine, contained in the word of God and constantly preached by the Fathers, that man ought voluntarily to respond to God by believing; therefore no one is to be forced to embrace the faith against his will. For the act of faith is by its very nature voluntary, since man, redeemed by Christ the Savior and called to the adoption of sons through Jesus Christ, cannot adhere to God revealing Himself unless, the Father drawing him (cf. Jn 6:44), he renders to God the rational and free submission of faith. Therefore it is fully consonant with the nature of faith that in religious matters all coercion on the part of men be excluded. And accordingly the reason of religious freedom contributes not a little to foster that state of affairs in which men can be readily invited to the Christian faith, spontaneously embrace it, and actively confess it in their whole manner of life.","Quae de iure hominis ad libertatem religiosam declarat haec Vaticana Synodus, fundamentum habent in dignitate personae, cuius exigentiae rationi humanae plenius innotuerunt per saeculorum experientiam. Immo haec doctrina de libertate radices habet in divina Revelatione, quapropter eo magis a Christianis sancte servanda est. Quamvis enim Revelatio non expresse affirmet ius ad immunitatem ab externa coercitione in re religiosa, tamen humanae personae dignitatem in tota eius amplitudine patefacit, observantiam Christi erga hominis libertatem in exsequendo officio credendi verbo Dei demonstrat, atque de spiritu nos edocet, quem discipuli talis Magistri debent in omnibus agnoscere et sequi. Quibus omnibus principia generalia illustrantur super quae fundatur doctrina huius Declarationis de libertate religiosa. Praesertim libertas religiosa in societate plene est cum libertate actus fidei christianae congrua."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p383","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","11 God indeed calls men to serve Him in spirit and truth, hence they are bound in conscience, not coerced. For He has regard for the dignity of the human person created by Himself, who ought to be led by his own counsel and enjoy freedom. This appeared most of all in Christ Jesus, in whom God perfectly manifested Himself and His ways. For Christ, who is our Master and Lord, and the same gentle and humble of heart, attracted and invited disciples patiently. He indeed supported and confirmed His preaching by miracles, to excite and prove the faith of hearers, not to exercise coercion on them. He certainly exprobrated the unbelief of hearers, but left vengeance to God on the day of judgment. Sending the Apostles into the world, He said to them: \"He who believes and is baptized shall be saved; he who does not believe shall be condemned\" (Mk 16:16). He himself, however, knowing that zizania is sown with the wheat, commanded that both be allowed to grow until the harvest which will be at the consummation of the world. Unwilling to be a political Messiah ruling by force, He preferred to call Himself the Son of Man, who came to minister and to give His life as a redemption for many (Mk 10:45). He showed Himself as the perfect Servant of God, who \"will not break the bruised reed, nor extinguish the smoking flax\" (Mt 12:20). He acknowledged civil power and its rights, commanding tribute to be given to Caesar, but clearly warned that the superior rights of God are to be kept: \"Render therefore to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's\" (Mt 22:21). Finally, completing the work of redemption on the cross, by which He acquired salvation and true freedom for men, He perfected His revelation. For He bore witness to the truth, but was unwilling to impose it by force on contradictors. For His kingdom is not vindicated by striking, but is established by testifying and hearing the truth, and grows by the love with which Christ lifted up on the cross draws all to Himself.","Caput est ex praecipuis doctrinae catholicae, in verbo Dei contentum et a Patribus constanter praedicatum, hominem debere Deo voluntarie respondere credendo; invitum proinde neminem esse cogendum ad amplectendam fidem. Etenim actus fidei ipsa sua natura voluntarius est, cum homo, a Christo Salvatore redemptus et in adoptionem filiorum per Iesum Christum vocatus, Deo Sese revelanti adhaerere non possit, nisi Patre eum trahente (cf. Jn 6,44), rationabile liberumque Deo praestiterit fidei obsequium. Indoli ergo fidei plene consonum est ut, in re religiosa, quodvis genus coercitionis ex parte hominum excludatur. Ac proinde ratio libertatis religiosae haud parum confert ad illum rerum statum fovendum, in quo homines expedite possint invitari ad fidem christianam, illam sponte amplecti atque eam in tota vitae ratione actuose confiteri."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p384","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","The Apostles, taught by the word and example of Christ, followed the same way. From the very beginnings of the Church, the disciples of Christ labored to convert men to confessing Christ the Lord, not by coercive action nor by artifices unworthy of the Gospel, but especially by the power of the word of God. They strongly announced to all the purpose of God the Savior, who wills all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth (1 Tim 2:4); at the same time, however, they respected the weak even if they were in error, thus showing how each of us will give an account for himself to God (Rom 14:12) and is bound to obey his conscience. Like Christ, the Apostles were always intent on bearing witness to the truth of God, speaking the word of God with confidence (Acts 4:31) more abundantly before the people and princes. For they firmly held that the Gospel itself is indeed the power of God for salvation to every believer. Therefore, all carnal arms having been spurned, following the example of meekness and modesty of Christ, they preached the word of God, fully trusting in the divine power of this word to destroy the powers opposing God and to reduce men to the faith and obedience of Christ. Like the Master, so the Apostles acknowledged legitimate civil authority: \"For there is no power but from God,\" teaches the Apostle, whence he commands: \"Let every soul be subject to higher powers... he who resists the power, resists the ordinance of God\" (Rom 13:1-2). At the same time, however, they did not fear to contradict public power opposing the holy will of God: \"We ought to obey God rather than men\" (Acts 5:29). Innumerable martyrs and faithful have followed this way through the ages and the world.","Deus quidem homines ad inserviendum Sibi in spiritu et veritate vocat, unde ipsi in conscientia vinciuntur, non vero coercentur. Rationem enim habet dignitatis personae humanae ab Ipso conditae, quae proprio consilio duci et libertate frui debet. Hoc autem summe apparuit in Christo Iesu, in quo Deus Seipsum ac vias suas perfecte manifestavit. Etenim Christus, qui Magister et Dominus est noster, idemque mitis et humilis corde, discipulos patienter allexit et invitavit. Miraculis utique praedicationem suam suffulsit et confirmavit, ut fidem auditorum excitaret atque comprobaret, non ut in eos coercitionem exerceret. Incredulitatem audientium certe exprobravit, sed vindictam Deo in diem Iudicii relinquendo. Mittens Apostolos in mundum dixit eis: Qui crediderit et baptizatus fuerit salvus erit; qui vero non crediderit condemnabitur (Mc 16,16). Ipse vero, agnoscens zizaniam cum tritico seminatam, iussit sinere utraque crescere usque ad messem quae fiet in consummatione saeculi. Nolens esse Messias politicus et vi dominans, maluit se dicere Filium Hominis qui venit ut ministraret et daret animam suam redemptionem pro multis (Mc 10,45). Sese praebuit ut perfectum Servum Dei, qui harundinem quassatam non confringet et linum fumigans non extinguet (Mt 12,20). Potestatem civilem eiusque iura agnovit, iubens censum dari Caesari, clare autem monuit servanda esse iura superiora Dei: Reddite ergo quae sunt Caesaris Caesari, et quae sunt Dei Deo (Mt 22,21). Tandem in opere redemptionis in cruce complendo, quo salutem et veram libertatem hominibus acquireret, revelationem suam perfecit. Testimonium enim perhibuit veritati, eam tamen contradicentibus vi imponere noluit. Regnum enim eius non percutiendo vindicatur, sed stabilitur testificando et audiendo veritatem, crescit autem amore, quo Christus exaltatus in cruce homines ad Seipsum trahit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p385","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","12 The Church, therefore, faithful to the gospel truth, follows the way of Christ and the Apostles when she acknowledges and fosters the reason of religious freedom as consonant with the dignity of man and the revelation of God. She has kept and handed down the doctrine received from the Master and the Apostles, through the course of times. Although in the life of the People of God, pilgrimaging through the vicissitudes of human history, a manner of acting less conformable to the gospel spirit, indeed contrary, has sometimes existed, yet the doctrine of the Church that no one is to be forced to the faith has always remained. The evangelical leaven has so long operated in the minds of men and contributed much, that in the course of time men have more widely recognized the dignity of the human person, and the persuasion has matured that in religious matters immunity from any human coercion is to be kept in society.","Apostoli, Christi verbo et exemplo edocti, eamdem viam secuti sunt. Ab ipsis Ecclesiae exordiis discipuli Christi adlaborarunt, ut homines ad Christum Dominum confitendum converterent, non actione coercitiva neque artificiis Evangelio indignis, sed imprimis virtute verbi Dei. Fortiter omnibus nuntiabant propositum Salvatoris Dei, qui omnes homines vult salvos fieri et ad agnitionem veritatis venire (1Tm 2,4); simul autem verebantur debiles etiamsi in errore versabantur, sic ostendentes quomodo unusquisque nostrum pro se rationem reddet Deo (Rm 14,12) et in tantum teneatur conscientiae suae oboedire. Sicuti Christus, Apostoli intenti semper fuerunt ad testimonium reddendum veritati Dei, abundantius audentes coram populo et principibus loqui verbum Dei cum fiducia (Ac 4,31). Firma enim fide tenebant ipsum Evangelium revera esse virtutem Dei in salutem omni credenti. Omnibus ergo spretis armis carnalibus, exemplum mansuetudinis et modestiae Christi sequentes, verbum Dei praedicaverunt plene confisi divina huius verbi virtute ad potestates Deo adversas destruendas atque homines ad fidem et obsequium Christi reducendos. Sicut Magister ita et Apostoli auctoritatem legitimam civilem agnoverunt: Non est enim potestas nisi a Deo docet Apostolus, qui exinde iubet: Omnis anima potestatibus sublimioribus subdita sit; ... qui resistit potestati, Dei ordinationi resistit (Rm 13,1-2). Simul autem non timuerunt contradicere potestati publicae se sanctae Dei voluntati opponenti: Oboedire oportet Deo magis quam hominibus (Ac 5,29). Hanc viam secuti sunt innumeri martyres et fideles per saecula et per orbem."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p386","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","13 Among those things which pertain to the good of the Church, indeed to the good of the earthly city itself, and are always and everywhere to be kept and defended from all injury, this certainly is most excellent, that the Church enjoy so great freedom of action as the care of men's salvation requires. For this freedom is sacred, with which the Only-begotten Son of God enriched the Church acquired by His blood. Indeed it is so proper to the Church that those who impugn it act against the will of God. The freedom of the Church is a fundamental principle in the relations between the Church and public powers and the whole civil order. In human society and before any public power, the Church claims freedom for herself as a spiritual authority, constituted by Christ the Lord, to whom by divine mandate the office of going into the whole world and preaching the Gospel to every creature pertains. Likewise the Church claims freedom for herself as also a society of men who have the right to live in civil society according to the precepts of the Christian faith.","Ecclesia igitur, evangelicae veritati fidelis, viam Christi et Apostolorum sequitur quando rationem libertatis religiosae tamquam dignitati hominis et Dei revelationi consonam agnoscit eamque fovet. Doctrinam a Magistro et ab Apostolis acceptam, decursu temporum, custodivit et tradidit. Etsi in vita Populi Dei, per vicissitudines historiae humanae peregrinantis, interdum exstitit modus agendi spiritui evangelico minus conformis, immo contrarius, semper tamen mansit Ecclesiae doctrina neminem esse ad fidem cogendum. Evangelicum fermentum in mentibus hominum sic diu est operatum atque multum contulit, ut homines temporum decursu latius agnoscerent dignitatem personae suae et maturesceret persuasio in re religiosa ipsam immunem servandam esse in civitate a quacumque humana coercitione."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p387","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","Now, if the reason of religious freedom flourishes, not only proclaimed by words nor only sanctioned by laws, but also reduced to practice with sincerity, then at length the Church obtains a stable condition both in law and in fact for the necessary independence to carry out the divine mission, which ecclesiastical authorities have more and more insistently claimed in society. At the same time, Christ's faithful, like other men, enjoy civil right not to be prevented in living according to conscience. Therefore concord flourishes between the freedom of the Church and that religious freedom which is to be acknowledged as a right to all men and communities and sanctioned in juridical ordering.","Inter ea quae ad bonum Ecclesiae, immo ad bonum ipsius terrenae civitatis spectant et ubique semperque servanda sunt atque ab omni iniuria defendenda, illud certe praestantissimum est, ut Ecclesia tanta perfruatur agendi libertate, quantam salus hominum curanda requirat. Haec enim libertas sacra est, qua Unigenitus Dei Filius ditavit Ecclesiam acquisitam sanguine suo. Ecclesiae sane adeo propria est, ut qui eam impugnant, iidem contra Dei voluntatem agant. Libertas Ecclesiae est principium fundamentale in relationibus inter Ecclesiam et potestates publicas totumque ordinem civilem. In societate humana et coram quavis potestate publica Ecclesia sibi vindicat libertatem, utpote auctoritas spiritualis, a Christo Domino constituta, cui ex divino mandato incumbit officium eundi in mundum universum et Evangelium praedicandi omni creaturae. Libertatem pariter sibi vindicat Ecclesia prout est etiam societas hominum qui iure gaudent vivendi in societate civili secundum fidei christianae praescripta."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p388","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","14 The Catholic Church, that she may obey the divine mandate: \"Teach all nations\" (Mt 28:19), ought to labor with great care that the word of God may run and be glorified (2 Th 3:1). Therefore the Church earnestly asks that, first of all by her sons, \"supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings be made for all men... For this is good and acceptable before God our Savior, who wills all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth\" (1 Tim 2:1-4). Christ's faithful, however, in forming their own conscience, ought diligently to attend to the sacred and certain doctrine of the Church. For by the will of Christ, the Catholic Church is the teacher of truth, and it is her office to pronounce and authentically teach the Truth which is Christ, and at the same time by her authority to declare and confirm principles of the moral order flowing from human nature itself. Moreover, Christians, walking in wisdom toward those who are outside, in the Holy Spirit, in unaffected charity, in the word of truth (2 Cor 6:6-7), should strive to spread the light of life with all confidence and apostolic fortitude, even to the shedding of blood. For the disciple is gravely bound to the Master Christ with the duty of more and more fully knowing the truth received from Him, faithfully announcing it, and strenuously defending it, excluding means contrary to the gospel spirit. At the same time, however, the charity of Christ urges him to act lovingly, prudently, patiently with men who are in error or ignorance concerning the faith. Regard is therefore to be had both to the duties toward Christ the life-giving Word which is to be preached, and to the rights of the human person, and to the measure of grace given by God through Christ to man, who is invited to faith to be accepted and professed freely.","Iamvero, si viget ratio libertatis religiosae non solum verbis proclamata neque solum legibus sancita, sed etiam cum sinceritate in praxim deducta, tunc demum Ecclesia stabilem obtinet et iuris et facti condicionem ad necessariam in missione divina exsequenda independentiam, quam auctoritates ecclesiasticae in societate presse pressiusque vindicarunt. Simulque Christifideles, sicut et ceteri homines, iure civili gaudent ne impediantur in vita sua iuxta conscientiam agenda. Concordia igitur viget inter libertatem Ecclesiae et libertatem illam religiosam, quae omnibus hominibus et communitatibus est tanquam ius agnoscenda et in ordinatione iuridica sancienda."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p389","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","15 It is clear therefore that the men of the present age desire that they may freely profess their religion privately and publicly, indeed religious freedom is already declared as a civil right in most Constitutions and solemnly acknowledged in international documents. Yet there are not lacking regimes in which, although freedom of religious worship is acknowledged in their Constitution, yet the public powers themselves strive to turn citizens away from professing religion and to make life very difficult and perilous for religious communities. This holy Synod, joyfully saluting those favorable signs of this time, but denouncing these deplorable facts with grief, exhorts Catholics, and also entreats all men, that they most attentively consider how necessary religious freedom is in the present condition especially of the human family. For it is manifest that all nations are more and more becoming one, men of diverse culture and religion are being bound together by closer relations, and the consciousness of each one's own responsibility increases. Therefore, that peaceful relations and concord may be established and strengthened in the human race, it is required that religious freedom be everywhere protected by effective juridical protection, and that the supreme duties and rights of men to lead religious life freely in society be observed.","Ecclesia catholica, ut divino obtemperet mandato: docete omnes gentes (Mt 28,19), impensa cura adlaborare debet ut sermo Dei currat et clarificetur (2Th 3,1). Enixe igitur rogat Ecclesia, ut a filiis suis primum omnium fiant obsecrationes, orationes, postulationes, gratiarum actiones pro omnibus hominibus... Hoc enim bonum est et acceptum coram Salvatore nostro Deo, qui omnes homines vult salvos fieri et ad agnitionem veritatis venire (1Tm 2,1-4). Christifideles autem in sua efformanda conscientia diligenter attendere debent ad sacram certamque Ecclesiae doctrinam. Christi enim voluntate Ecclesia catholica magistra est veritatis, eiusque munus est, ut Veritatem quae Christus est enuntiet atque authentice doceat, simulque principia ordinis moralis, ex ipsa natura humana profluentia, auctoritate sua declaret atque confirmet. Insuper Christiani, in sapientia ambulantes ad eos qui foris sunt, in Spiritu Sancto, in caritate non ficta, in verbo veritatis (2Co 6,6-7), lumen vitae cum omni fiducia et fortitudine apostolica, ad sanguinis usque effusionem, diffundere satagant. Etenim discipulus erga Christum Magistrum gravi adstringitur officio, veritatem ab Eo receptam plenius in dies cognoscendi, annuntiandi fideliter, strenueque defendendi, exclusis mediis spiritui evangelico contrariis. Simul tamen caritas Christi urget eum, ut amanter prudenter patienter agat cum hominibus, qui in errore vel ignorantia circa fidem versantur. Respiciendum igitur est tum ad officia erga Christum Verbum vivificans quod praedicandum est, tum ad humanae personae iura, tum ad mensuram gratiae a Deo per Christum tributam homini, qui ad fidem sponte accipiendam et profitendam invitatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p390","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","May God and Father of all bring it about that the human family, carefully keeping the reason of religious freedom in society, through the grace of Christ and the power of the Holy Spirit, be led to that sublime and perpetual freedom of the glory of the sons of God (Rom 8:21).","Constat igitur praesentis aetatis homines optare ut libere possint religionem privatim publiceque profiteri, immo libertatem religiosam in plerisque Constitutionibus iam ut ius civile declarari et documentis internationalibus sollemniter agnosci. At non desunt regimina in quibus, etsi in eorum Constitutione libertas cultus religiosi agnoscitur, tamen ipsae publicae potestates conantur cives a religione profitenda removere et communitatibus religiosis vitam perdifficilem ac periclitantem reddere. Illa fausta huius temporis signa laeto animo salutans, haec vero deploranda facta cum maerore denuntians, Sacra Synodus Catholicos hortatur, exorat autem homines universos, ut perattente considerent quantopere libertas religiosa necessaria sit in praesenti potissimum familiae humanae condicione. Manifestum est enim cunctas gentes magis in dies unum fieri, homines diversae culturae et religionis arctioribus inter se devinciri rationibus, augeri denique conscientiam propriae cuiusque responsabilitatis. Proinde ut pacificae relationes et concordia in genere humano instaurentur et firmentur, requiritur ut ubique terrarum libertas religiosa efficaci tutela iuridica muniatur atque observentur suprema hominum officia et iura ad vitam religiosam libere in societate ducendam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p391","II. Religious Freedom in the Light of Revelation","1 The joy and hope, the grief and anguish of the men of this time, especially of the poor and of all those who are afflicted, are the joy and hope, the grief and anguish of the disciples of Christ as well, and nothing truly human is found that does not resound in their hearts. For their community is made up of men, who, united in Christ, are led by the Holy Spirit in their pilgrimage to the Kingdom of the Father and have received the message of salvation to be proposed to all. Therefore they themselves truly experience themselves intimately joined with the human race and its history.","Faxit Deus et Pater omnium ut familia humana, diligenter servata libertatis religiosae ratione in societate, per gratiam Christi et virtutem Spiritus Sancti adducatur ad sublimem illam ac perennem libertatem gloriae filiorum Dei (Rm 8,21)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p392","Gaudium et Spes","2 Therefore the Second Vatican Council, having more deeply investigated the mystery of the Church, now unhesitatingly turns its discourse not only to the sons of the Church and to all who invoke the name of Christ, but to all men, wishing to set forth to all how the Church conceives its presence and activity in the modern world. It therefore has before its eyes the world of men, namely the whole human family with the totality of things among which it lives; the world, the theater of the history of the human race, marked by its industry, defeats, and victories; the world, which Christ's faithful believe to be created and conserved by the love of the Creator, placed indeed under the slavery of sin, but freed by Christ crucified and risen, the power of the Evil One broken, that according to the purpose of God it may be transformed and attain to its consummation.","GAUDIUM ET SPES, luctus et angor hominum huius temporis, pauperum praesertim et quorumvis amictorum, gaudium sunt et spes, luctus et angor etiam Christi discipulorum nihilque vere humanum invenitur, quod in corde eorum non resonet. Ipsorum enim communitas ex hominibus coalescit, qui, in Christo coadunati, a Spiritu Sancto diriguntur in sua ad Regnum Patris peregrinatione et nuntium salutis omnibus proponendum acceperunt. Quapropter ipsa cum genere humano eiusque historia se revera intime coniunctam experitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p393","To whom the Council directs its discourse","3 In our days, however, the human race, astonished by its own discoveries and its own power, yet often anxiously agitates questions concerning the present development of the world, concerning the place and office of man in the universe, concerning the meaning of its individual and collective effort, finally concerning the ultimate end of things and of men. Therefore the Council, testifying and setting forth the faith of the whole people of God, gathered by Christ, cannot demonstrate its connection, respect, and love toward the whole human family in which it is inserted more eloquently than by entering into dialogue with it concerning those various problems, bringing light from the Gospel, and supplying to the human race salutary forces, which the Church itself, led by the Holy Spirit, receives from its Founder. For the human person is to be saved, and human society is to be restored. Man, therefore, and indeed one and whole, with body and soul, heart and conscience, mind and will, will be the hinge of our whole explanation. Therefore the sacred Synod, professing the highest vocation of man and asserting that a divine seed is inserted in him, offers to the human race the sincere cooperation of the Church for instituting that fraternity of all which corresponds to this vocation. The Church is moved by no earthly ambition, but intends only one thing: namely, under the guidance of the Paraclete Spirit, to continue the work of Christ Himself, who came into the world to bear witness to the truth, to save, not to judge, to minister, not to be ministered unto.","Ideo Concilium Vaticanum Secundum, mysterio Ecclesiae penitius investigato, iam non ad solos Ecclesiae filios omnesque Christi nomen invocantes, sed ad universos homines incunctanter sermonem convertit, omnibus exponere cupiens quomodo Ecclesiae praesentiam ac navitatem in mundo hodierno concipiat. Mundum igitur hominum prae oculis habet seu universam familiam humanam cum universitate rerum inter quas vivit; mundum, teatrum historiae generis humani, eiusque industria, cladibus ac victoriis signatum; mundum, quem christifideles credunt ex amore Creatoris conditum et conservatum, sub peccati quidem servitute positum, sed a Christo crucifixo et resurgente, fracta potestate Maligni, liberatum, ut secundum propositum Dei transformetur et ad consummationem perveniat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p394","On the ministry to be rendered to man","4 To carry out such an office, it is the duty of the Church at all times to scrutinize the signs of the times and to interpret them under the light of the Gospel; that, in a manner accommodated to each generation, it may be able to respond to the perennial questions of men concerning the meaning of present and future life and concerning their mutual relation. It is necessary therefore that the world in which we live and its expectations, aspirations, and often dramatic character be known and understood. Some of the more principal characteristics of the modern world can be delineated as follows. Today the human race is in a new age of its history in which profound and rapid changes gradually extend to the whole world. Excited by the intelligence and creative industry of man, they recoil upon man himself, upon his judgments and individual and collective desires, upon his manner of thinking and acting both concerning things and concerning men. Thus we can already speak of a true social and cultural transformation, which also redounds upon religious life. As happens in any crisis of growth, this transformation does not carry slight difficulties. Thus while man extends his power so widely, he is not always able to reduce it to his service. While he strives more deeply to penetrate the more intimate recesses of his own mind, he often appears more uncertain about himself. While he gradually more clearly discovers the laws of social life, he hesitates about the direction to be impressed upon it. Never has the human race abounded in so many riches, faculties, and economic power, and yet a huge part of the inhabitants of the world is tormented by famine and poverty, and countless numbers labor wholly in illiteracy. Never have men had so acute a sense of freedom as today, while at the same time new kinds of social and psychical servitude arise. While the world so vividly feels its unity and the dependence of individuals upon one another in necessary solidarity, it is nevertheless gravely torn into opposite parts by struggling forces; for acute political, social, economic, racial, and ideological dissensions still persist, and the danger of war destroying all things even to the depths is not lacking. While the communication of ideas increases, the very words by which important concepts are expressed take on rather different senses in distinct ideologies. Finally, a more perfect temporal ordering is sedulously sought, while spiritual growth does not progress equally. Affected by so many complex conditions, many of our contemporaries are prevented from ever duly recognizing perennial values and at the same time from rightly composing them with newly discovered things; hence, driven between hope and anguish, questioning the present course of events, they are pressed by anxiety. This course of events provokes and even constrains men to respond.","Nostris autem diebus, genus humanum, de propriis inventis propriaque potentia admiratione commotum, saepe tamen anxias agitat quaestiones de hodierna mundi evolutione, de loco et munere hominis in orbe universo, de sui individualis et collectivi conaminis sensu, denique de ultimo rerum hominumque fine. Quapropter Concilium, fidem universi populi Dei, a Christo congregati, testificans et exponens, ipsius coniunctionem, observantiam ac dilectionem erga totam hominum familiam cui inseritur, eloquentius demonstrare non valet quam instituendo cum ea de variis illis problematibus colloquium, lumen afferendo ex Evangelio depromptum, atque humano generi salutares vires suppeditando, quae ipsa Ecclesia, Spiritu Sancto ducente, a Fundatore suo accipit. Hominis enim persona salvanda est humanaque societas instauranda. Homo igitur, et quidem unus ac totus, cum corpore et anima, corde et conscientia, mente et voluntate, totius nostrae explanationis cardo erit. Ideo Sacra Synodus, altissimam vocationem hominis profitens et divinum quoddam semen in eo insertum asseverans, generi humano sinceram cooperationem Ecclesiae offert ad instituendam eam omnium fraternitatem quae huic vocationi respondeat. Nulla ambitione terrestri movetur Ecclesia, sed unum tantum intendit: nempe, Spiritus Paracliti ductu, opus ipsius continuare Christi, qui in mundum venit ut testimonium perhiberet veritati, ut salvaret, non ut iudicaret, ut ministraret, non ut sibi ministraretur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p395","Expositio Introductiva: on the Condition of Man in the Modern World","5 The present commotion of minds and the change of life conditions are connected with a wider transformation of things, by which it is brought about that in forming minds, mathematical and natural sciences, and those treating of man himself, acquire growing weight, while in ordering action, technical arts flowing from those sciences do the same. This scientific reason shapes cultural reason and ways of thinking in a different way than before. Technical arts progress to such an extent that they transform the face of the earth and even strive to subjugate extraterrestrial space. The human intellect also extends its dominion in a certain way over times: over the past by means of historical knowledge, over the future by the art of forecasting and planning. Progressing biological, psychological, and social sciences not only help man to better know himself, but also help him, by using technical methods, to directly exert influence on the life of societies. At the same time, the human race already more thinks about foreseeing and ordering its own demographic increase. History itself accelerates at so rapid a pace that individuals can scarcely follow it. The fate of human society becomes one and no longer as if dispersed among various histories. Thus the human race passes from a more static notion of the order of things to a more dynamic and evolutionary notion, whence arises a very great new complexity of problems, which provokes to new analyses and syntheses.","Ad tale munus exsequendum, per omne tempus Ecclesiae officium incumbit signa temporum perscrutandi et sub Evangelii luce interpretandi; ita ut, modo unicuique generationi accommodato, ad perennes hominum interrogationes de sensu vitae praesentis et futurae deque earum mutua relatione respondere possit. Oportet itaque ut mundus in quo vivimus necnon eius expectationes, appetitiones et indoles saepe dramatica cognoscantur et intelligantur. Quaedam autem principaliores mundi hodierni notae sequenti modo delineari possunt. Hodie genus humanum in nova historiae suae aetate versatur in qua profundae et celeres mutationes ad universum orbem gradatim extenduntur. Ab hominis intelligentia et creativa industria excitatae in ipsum hominem recidunt, in eius iudicia et desideria individualia et collectiva, in eius modum cogitandi et agendi tum circa res tum circa homines. Ita iam de vera sociali et culturali transformatione loqui possumus, quae etiam in vitam religiosam redundat. Ut in quavis accretionis crisi contingit, haec transformatio non leves secumfert difficultates. Ita dum homo potentiam suam tam late extendit, eam tamen non semper ad suum servitium redigere valet. Proprii animi intimiora altius penetrare satagens, saepe de seipso magis incertus apparet. Leges vitae socialis pedetentim clarius detegens, de directione ei imprimenda anceps haeret. Numquam genus humanum tantis divitiis, facultatibus et potentia oeconomica abundavit, et tamen adhuc ingens pars incolarum orbis fame et egestate torquetur atque innumeri litterarum ignorantia plane laborant. Numquam homines tam acutum ut hodie sensum libertatis habuerunt, dum nova interea genera socialis et psychicae servitutis exsurgunt. Dum mundus suam unitatem necnon singulorum ab invicem dependentiam in necessaria solidarietate tam vivide persentit, viribus tamen inter se pugnantibus gravissime in opposita distrahitur; etenim acres dissensiones politicae, sociales, oeconomicae, raciales et ideologicae adhuc perserverant, nec periculum deest belli omnia usque ad ima destructuri. Dum idearum communicatio augetur, verba ipsa quibus magni momenti conceptus exprimuntur sensus sat diversos in distinctis ideologiis induunt. Tandem sedulo perfectior quaeritur temporalis ordinatio, quin spirituale incrementum pariter progrediatur. Tot implexis condicionibus affecti, plurimi coaevi nostri impediuntur quominus valore perennes vere dignoscant et simul cum noviter inventis rite componant; exinde, inter spem et angorem agitati, de praesenti rerum cursu sese interrogantes, inquietudine premuntur. Qui rerum cursus homines ad respondendum provocat, immo et constringit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p396","On the deeply changed conditions","6 By this very fact, traditional local communities, such as patriarchal families, clans, tribes, villages, various groups, and relations of social association, experience more and more full changes. The industrial type of society is gradually diffused, leading some nations to economic opulence, and profoundly transforming concepts and conditions of social life established for centuries. Likewise, the cultivation of urban life and study increase, both through the growth of cities and their inhabitants, and through the movement by which urban life spreads to country folk. New and more suitable instruments of social communication contribute to knowing events and to very quickly and widely diffusing ways of thinking and feeling, exciting several connected repercussions. Nor is it to be made little of how many men, induced to migrate by various causes, change their manner of life. Thus the relations of man with his fellows incessantly multiply, and at the same time socialization itself introduces new relations, without always promoting congruent maturation of the person and personal relations (personalization). Such development indeed appears more clearly in nations which already enjoy the advantages of economic and technical progress, but also moves peoples still striving for development, who, for their regions, desire to obtain the benefits of industrialization and urbanization. Those peoples, especially devoted to older traditions, at the same time experience a movement toward a more mature and more personal exercise of liberty.","Hodierna animorum commotio et in vitae condicionibus immutatio cum ampliori rerum transmutatione connectuntur, qua efficitur ut in mentibus efformandis scientiae mathematicae et naturales vel de ipso homine tractantes, in ordine vero agendi technicae artes ex illis scientiis profluentes, crescens pondus acquirant. Haec mens scientifica rationem culturalem modosque cogitandi aliter quam antea fingit. Technicae artes eo progrediuntur ut faciem terrae transforment etiam spatium ultraterrestre subigere conentur. Super tempora quoque humanus intellectus dominium suum quodammodo dilatat: in praeteritum ope cognitionis historicae, in futurum arte prospectiva et planificatione. Progredientes scientiae biologicae, psychologicae et sociales non solum homini ad meliorem sui cognitionem opem ferunt, sed ipsum etiam adiuvant ut, technicis methodis adhibitis, in vitam societatum directe influxum exerceat. Insimul genus humanum de proprio demographico incremento iam praevidendo et ordinando magisque cogitat. Ipsa historia tam rapido cursu acceleratur ut singuli eam vix prosequi valeant. Consortionis humanae sors una efficitur et non amplius inter varias velut historias dispergitur. Ita genus humanum a notione magis statica ordinis rerum ad notionem magis dynamicam atque evolutivam transit, unde quam maxima nascitur problematum nova complexio, quae ad novas analyses et synthesas provocat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p397","Changes in the social order","7 The change of mind and structures frequently calls into question good things received, especially among the young, who are not seldom impatient, even anguished rebels, and, conscious of their own importance in social life, wish to have their part in it more quickly. Hence it is not seldom that parents and educators experience ever greater difficulties in fulfilling their duties. Moreover, institutions, laws, and ways of thinking and feeling handed down by the fathers do not always seem to be well adapted to the present state of things; whence a grave disturbance arises in the manner and in the very norms of acting. Finally, new conditions also affect religious life itself. On the one hand, a sharper faculty of judging purifies it from magical concept of the world and from still wandering superstitions, and more and more demands a more personal and active adherence of faith; whence it comes about that not a few come to a more vivid sense of God. On the other hand, more frequent crowds practically withdraw from religion. Unlike in past times, to deny God or religion, or to withdraw from them, is no longer something unusual and individual: for today it is often presented as if an exigency of scientific progress or of a certain new humanism. All these things in many regions are expressed not only in philosophical opinions, but most widely affect literature, arts, human sciences and the interpretation of history, and the civil laws themselves, so that many are disturbed thereby.","Eo ipso communitates locales traditionales, uti sunt familiae patriarchales, clans, tribus, pagi, varii coetus et consortionis socialis necessitudines, pleniores in dies immutationes experiuntur. Typus industrialis societatis paulatim diffunditur, quasdam nationes ad oeconomicam opulentiam adducens, et notiones et condiciones vitae socialis a saeculis constitutas penitus transformans. Similiter vitae urbanae cultus ac studium augentur sive per urbium earumque incolarum augmentum, sive per motum quo vita urbana ad ruricolas dilatatur. Nova et aptiora communicationis socialis instrumenta ad eventus cognoscendos et ad modos cogitandi et sentiendi quam citissime latissimeque diffundendos conferunt, plures connexas repercussiones excitando. Nec parvipendendum est quot homines, ex variis causis, ad migrandum inducti, vitae suae rationem immutent. Sic necessitudines hominis cum similibus suis indesinenter multiplicantur ac simul ipsa socializatio novas necessitudines inducit, quin tamen congruentem personae maturationem et relationes vere personales (personalizationem) semper promoveat. Huiusmodi quidem evolutio clarius apparet in nationibus quae commodis progressus oeconomici et technici iam gaudent, sed populos quoque movet adhuc ad progressionem nitentes qui, pro suis regionibus beneficia industrializationis et urbanizationis obtinere cupiunt. Qui populi praesertim antiquioribus traditionibus addicti, simul motum experiuntur ad maturius magisque personale libertatis exercitium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p398","Psychological, moral, and religious changes","8 So rapid a change, often proceeding inordinately, and even the sharper consciousness of the discrepancies existing in the world, beget or increase contradictions and imbalances. In the person itself, an imbalance frequently arises between modern practical intellect and the theoretical manner of thinking, which cannot subdue the sum of its knowledge nor order it appropriately into syntheses. Likewise arises an imbalance between solicitude for practical efficiency and the exigencies of moral conscience, and also often between the conditions of collective life and the requirements of personal thought, indeed of contemplation. Finally, an imbalance arises between the specialization of human activity and the universal vision of things. In the family, however, discrepancies arise, whether from pressing demographic, economic, and social conditions, or from difficulties arising between succeeding generations, or from new social relations between men and women. Great discrepancies also arise between races, indeed between different orders of society; between opulent nations and those less powerful and needy; finally, between international institutions, arising from the desire for peace of peoples, and the ambition of spreading their own ideology and collective cupidities existing in nations or other groups. Hence mutual diffidences and enmities, conflicts and miseries, of which man himself is at once the cause and the victim.","Mutatio mentis et structurarum bona recepta frequenter in controversiam vocat, maxime apud iuvenes qui non semel impatientes, immo angore rebelles fiunt et conscii de proprio momento in vita sociali, citius in eadem partes habere cupiunt. Exinde non raro parentes et educatores in muneribus suis adimplendis in dies maiores difficultates experiuntur. Instituta vero, leges atque modi cogitandi et sentiendi a maioribus tradita non semper statui rerum hodierno bene aptari videntur inde gravis perturbatio in modo et in ipsis agendi normis. Ipsam denique vitam religiosam novae condiciones afficiunt. Ex una parte acrior diiudicandi facultas eam a magico mundi conceptu et a superstitionibus adhuc vagantibus purificat atque magis personalem et actuosam adhaesionem fidei in dies exigit; quo fit ut non pauci ad vividiorem Dei sensum accedant. Ex altera vero parte crebriores turbae a religione practice discedunt. Secus ac transactis temporibus, Deum religionemve negare, aut ab iisdem abstrahere, non amplius quid insolitum et individuale sunt: hodie enim non raro quasi exigentia progressus scientifici vel cuiusdam novi humanismi exhibentur. Haec omnia in pluribus regionibus non tantum in philosophorum placitis exprimuntur, sed latissime litteras, artes, scientiarum humanarum et historiae interpretationem, ipsaque leges civiles afficiunt ita ut exinde multi perturbentur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p399","On the imbalances in the modern world","9 In the meantime, the persuasion grows that the human race can and ought not only more and more to strengthen its dominion over created things; but also that it is its part to establish a political, social, and economic order which may better and better serve man and help individuals and groups to affirm and cultivate their own dignity. Hence very many most sharply demand those goods by which, with vivid consciousness they judge themselves deprived by injustice or unequal distribution. Nations on the way to progress, like those recently made independent, desire to participate in the goods of modern civilization not only in the political but also in the economic field, and freely to exercise their parts in the world, while nevertheless the distance of the same increases and at the same time often even economic dependence on other richer nations progressing more quickly. Peoples pressed by hunger call upon opulent peoples. Women claim for themselves, where they have not yet attained it, equality de jure and de facto with men. Workers and farmers desire not only to procure the necessities of life by labor, but also to cultivate the gifts of their own person, indeed to exercise their own parts in ordering economic, social, political, and cultural life. For the first time in human history, all peoples already persuade themselves that the benefits of culture can and ought to be truly extended to all. Beneath all these exigencies, however, lies a deeper and more universal aspiration: the person and the group thirst for a full and free life, worthy of man, subjecting to their own service all things which the modern world can so abundantly provide for them. Moreover, nations strive more and more strongly to attain some universal community. Which things being so, the modern world shows itself at once powerful and weak, capable of achieving the best or the worst, while the way to freedom or servitude, to progress or regression, to fraternity or hatred lies open before it. Moreover, man becomes conscious that it is his part rightly to direct the forces which he himself has aroused and which can oppress him or serve him. Hence he asks himself.","Tam rapida rerum mutatio inordinate saepe progrediens, immo et ipsa discrepantiarum in mundo vigentium acrior conscientia, contradictiones et inaequilibria gignunt vel augent. In ipsa persona frequentius oritur inaequilibrium inter modernum intellectum practicum et theoreticam cogitandi rationem, quae summam cognitionum suarum neque sibi subigere neque in syntheses apte ordinare valet. Oritur pariter inaequilibrium inter sollicitudinem efficientiae practicae et exigentias conscientiae moralis, necnon multoties inter condiciones vitae collectivas et requisita cogitationis personalis, immo et contemplationis. Oritur tandem inaequilibrium inter activitatis humanae specializationem et universalem rerum visionem. In familia autem discrepantiae oriuntur, sive ex prementibus condicionibus demographicis, oeconomicis et socialibus, sive ex difficultatibus inter generationes quae sibi subsequuntur exsurgentibus, sive ex novis necessitudinibus socialibus inter viros ac mulieres. Magnae oriuntur etiam discrepantiae inter stirpes, immo inter varii generis societatis ordines; inter nationes opulentas et minus valentes egentesque; denique, inter instituta internationalia, ex pacis desiderio populorum exorta, et ambitionem propriae ideologiae disseminandae nec non cupiditates collectivas in nationibus aliisve coetibus exsistentes. Inde mutuae diffidentiae et inimicitiae, conflictationes et aerumnae, quarum ipse homo simul causa et victima."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p400","On the more universal aspirations of the human race","10 Indeed, the imbalances under which the modern world labors are connected with that fundamental imbalance which is rooted in the heart of man. For in man himself many elements contend with one another. For while on the one hand, as a creature, he experiences himself limited in many ways, on the other hand he feels himself unlimited in his desires and called to a higher life. Attracted by many solicitations, he is forced always to choose among them and to renounce some. Indeed, weak and sinful, he not seldom does that which he does not wish and does not do that which he would wish. Hence he suffers division in himself, from which also so many and so great discords in society arise. Many indeed, whose life is infected with practical materialism, are turned away from a clear perception of this dramatic state, or, oppressed by misery, are prevented from considering it. Many think they find rest in the interpretation of things proposed in many ways. Some, however, expect the true and full liberation of the human race from human effort alone, and persuade themselves that the future kingdom of man over the earth will fulfill all the desires of his heart. Nor are there lacking those who, despairing of the meaning of life, praise the audacity of those who, judging human existence to be deprived of all proper meaning, strive to give it all meaning from their own genius alone. Nevertheless, facing the present development of the world, more and more numerous become those who either pose the most fundamental questions or perceive them with new acuteness: what is man? What is the meaning of pain, of evil, of death, which, although so much progress has been made, continue to exist? To what purpose are those victories acquired at so great a price? What can man bring to society, what expect from it? What will follow after this earthly life? The Church believes that Christ, dead and raised for all, gives light and strength to man through His Spirit, that he may be able to respond to his highest calling; nor is any other name given under heaven to men, in which they ought to be saved. Likewise it believes that the key, center, and end of all human history is found in its Lord and Master. Moreover, the Church affirms that beneath all changes there are many things which do not change, which have their ultimate foundation in Christ, who is the same yesterday, today, and forever. Under the light of Christ, therefore, the image of the invisible God, the Firstborn of every creature, the Council intends to speak to all to illustrate the mystery of man and to cooperate in finding the solution of the principal questions of our time.","Interea crescit persuasio genus humanum non tantum imperium suum super res creatas in dies magis roborare posse ac debere; sed insuper eius esse ordinem politicum, socialem et oeconomicum statuere qui in dies melius homini inserviat et singulos ac coetus adiuvet ad dignitatem sibi propriam affirmandam et excolendam. Hinc plurimi acerrime exigunt illa bona quibus, per iniustitiam vel non aequam distributionem, orbatos se esse vivida conscientia iudicant. Nationes in via progressus sicut illae recenter sui iuris factae, bona civilizationis hodiernae non tantum in campo politico sed etiam oeconomico participare et libere partibus suis in mundo fungi cupiunt, dum tamen in dies augetur earumdem distantia simul ac persaepe dependentia etiam oeconomica ab aliis ditioribus nationibus citius progredientibus. Populi fame pressi populos opulentiores interpellant. Mulieres sibi vindicant, ubi eam nondum sunt consecutae, paritatem de iure et de facto cum viris. Opifices et ruricolae non solum victui necessaria comparare, sed laborando dotes suae personae excolere, immo in ordinanda vita oeconomica, sociali, politica et culturali suas partes agere volunt. Nunc primum in historia humana universi populi iam persuasum sibi habent culturae beneficia reapse ad cunctos extendi posse ac debere. Sub omnibus autem istis exigentiis latet profundior et universalior appetito: personae scilicet atque coetus plenam atque liberam vitam, homine dignam, sitiunt, omnia quae hodiernus mundus eis tam abundanter praebere potest proprio servitio subicientes. Nationes praeterea in dies fortius enituntur ut universalem quamdam communitatem assequantur. Quae cum ita sint, mundus hodiernus simul potentem ac debilem se exhibet, capacem optima vel pessima patrandi, dum ipsi ad libertatem aut servitutem, ad progressum aut regressum, ad fraternitatem aut odium prostat via. Praeterea, homo conscius fit ipsius esse recte dirigere vires, quas ipse suscitavit et quae eum opprimere aut ei servire possunt. Unde seipsum interrogat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p401","On the deeper questions of the human race","11 The people of God, moved by faith, by which it believes that it is led by the Spirit of the Lord who fills the earth, in events, exigencies, and desires, in which it shares together with the other men of our age, strives to discern what in them are the true signs of the presence or counsel of God. For faith illuminates all things with new light and manifests the divine purpose concerning the whole vocation of man, and therefore directs the mind to solutions fully human. The Council intends first of all to judge under this light those values which are most esteemed today, and to refer them to their divine source. For these values, as they proceed from the human genius divinely given to it, are very good; but often from the corruption of the human heart they are twisted from their due order, so that they need purification.","Revera inaequilibria quibus laborat mundus hodiernus cum inaequilibrio illo fumdamentaliori connectuntur, quod in hominis corde radicatur. In ipso enim homine plura elementa sibi invicem oppugnant. Dum enim una ex parte, utpote creatura, multipliciter sese limitatum experitur, ex altera vero in desideriis suis illimitatum et ad superiorem vitam vocatum se sentit. Multis sollicitationibus attractus, iugiter inter eas seligere et quibusdam renuntiare cogitur. Immo, infirmus ac peccator, non raro illud quod non vult facit et illud quod facere vellet non facit. Unde in seipso divisionem patitur, ex qua etiam tot ac tantae discordiae in societate oriuntur. Plurimi sane, quorum vita materialismo practico inficitur, a clara huiusmodi dramatici status perceptione avertuntur, vel autem, miseria oppressi, impediuntur quominus illum considerent. Multi in interpretatione rerum multifarie proposita quietem se invenire existimant. Quidam vero a solo conatu humano veram plenamque generis humani liberationem expectant, sibique persuasum habent futurum regnum hominis super terram omnia vota cordis eius expleturum esse. Nec desunt qui, de sensu vitae desperantes, audaciam laudant eorum qui, exsistentiam humanam omnis significationis propriae expertem existimantes, ei totam significationem ex solo proprio ingenio conferre nituntur. Attamen, coram hodierna mundi evolutione, in dies numerosiores fiunt qui quaestiones maxime fundamentales vel ponunt vel nova acuitate persentiunt: quid est homo? Quinam est sensus doloris, mali, mortis, quae, quamquam tantus progressus factus est, subsistere pergunt? Ad quid victoriae illae tanto pretio acquisitae? Quid societati homo afferre, quid ab ea exspectare potest? Quid post vitam hanc terrestrem subsequetur? Credit autem Ecclesia Christum, pro omnibus mortuum et resuscitatum, homini lucem et vires per Spiritum suum praebere ut ille summae suae vocationi respondere possit; nec aliud nomen sub caelo datum esse hominibus, in quo oporteat eos salvos fieri. Similiter credit clavem, centrum et finem totius humanae historiae in Domino ac Magistro suo inveniri. Affirmat insuper Ecclesia omnibus mutationibus multa subesse quae non mutantur, quaeque fundamentum suum ultimum in Christo habent, qui est heri, hodie, Ipse et in saecula. Sub lumine ergo Christi, Imaginis Dei invisibilis, Primogeniti omnis creaturae, Concilium, ad mysterium hominis illustrandum atque ad cooperandum in solutionem praecipuarum quaestionum nostri temporis inveniendam, omnes alloqui intendit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p402","Pars prima","What does the Church think of man? What seems to be commended for building modern society? What is the ultimate meaning of human activity in the whole world? An answer is expected to these questions. From it will appear more clearly that the people of God and the human race, in which it is inserted, render service to one another, so that the mission of the Church shows itself to be religious and from this very fact supremely human.","Populus Dei, fide motus, qua credit se a Spiritu Domini duci qui replet orbem terrarum, in eventibus, exigentiis atque optatis, quorum una cum ceteris nostrae aetatis hominibus partem habet, quaenam in illis sint vera signa praesentiae vel consilii Dei, discernere satagit. Fides enim omnia novo lumine illustrat et divinum propositum de integra hominis vocatione manifestat, ideoque ad solutiones plene humanas mentem dirigit. Concilium imprimis illos valores, qui hodie maxime aestimantur, sub hoc lumine diiudicare et ad fontem suum divinum referre intendit. Hi enim valores, prout ex hominis ingenio eidem divinitus collato procedunt, valde boni sunt; sed ex corruptione humani cordis a sua debita ordinatione non raro detorquentur, ita ut purificatione indigeant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p403","Pars prima","12 According to the almost unanimous opinion of believers and unbelievers, all things which are on earth are to be ordered to man, as to their center and summit. But what is man? He has put forth and puts forth many opinions about himself, various and even contrary, by which he often either exalts himself as an absolute rule or sinks to desperation, hence hesitating and anxious. The Church, feeling these difficulties, instructed by God revealing, can bring an answer to them, by which the true condition of man is delineated, his infirmities explained, and at the same time his dignity and vocation rightly acknowledged. For the sacred Scriptures teach that man was created to the image of God, capable of knowing and loving his Creator, constituted by Him as lord over all earthly creatures, that he might rule them and use them, glorifying God. \"What is man that thou art mindful of him? or the son of man that thou visitest him? Thou hast made him a little lower than the angels, thou hast crowned him with glory and honor, and hast set him over the works of thy hands. Thou hast put all things under his feet\" (Ps 8:5-7). But God did not create man alone: for from the beginning \"He created them male and female\" (Gen 1:27), the association of which constitutes the first form of communion of persons. For man by his intimate nature is a social being, and can neither live nor expand his gifts without relations with others. God, therefore, as we read again in sacred page, \"saw all the things that He had made, and they were very good\" (Gen 1:31).","Quid Ecclesia de homine sentit? Quaenam ad societatem hodiernam aedificandam commendanda videntur? Quaenam est significatio ultima humanae navitatis in universo mundo? Ad has quaestiones responsio exspectatur. Exinde luculentius apparebit populum Dei et genus humanum, cui ille inseritur, servitium sibi mutuo praestare, ita ut Ecclesiae missio religiosam et ex hoc ipso summe humanam se exhibeat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p404","Chapter I. on the Dignity of the Human Person","13 Constituted in justice by God, man, however, the Evil One persuading, from the beginning of history abused his freedom, raising himself against God and desiring to attain his end outside God. \"When they knew God, they have not glorified him as God, but their foolish heart was darkened, and they served the creature rather than the Creator\" (Rom 1:21-25). What divine revelation makes known to us, concords with experience itself. For man, looking into his heart, finds himself inclined also to evil and sunk in manifold evils, which cannot proceed from his good Creator. Often refusing to acknowledge God as his principle, he also disrupts the due order toward his ultimate end, as well as his whole ordering toward himself, toward other men, and toward all created things. Therefore man is divided in himself. Therefore the whole life of men, both individual and collective, shows itself as a struggle, and indeed a dramatic one, between good and evil, between light and darkness. Indeed, man finds himself incapable of effectively conquering the assaults of evil by himself, so that each feels himself as if bound with chains. But the Lord Himself came to free and strengthen man, renewing him from within and casting out the prince of this world (cf. Jn 12:31), who held him in the slavery of sin. Sin, however, diminishes man himself, repelling him from the fullness to be attained. In the light of this Revelation, both the sublime vocation and the profound misery which men experience find their ultimate reason.","Secundum credentium et non credentium fere concordem sententiam, omnia quae in terra sunt ad hominem, tamquam ad centrum suum et culmen, ordinanda sunt. Quid est autem homo? Multas opiniones de seipso protulit et profert, varias et etiam contrarias, quibus saepe vel se tamquam absolutam regulam exaltat vel usque ad desperationem deprimit, exinde anceps et anxius. Quas quidem difficultates Ecclesia persentiens, a Deo revelante instructa eisdem responsum afferre potest, quo vera hominis condicio delineetur, explanentur eius infirmitates, simulque eius dignitas et vocatio recte agnosci possint. Sacrae enim Litterae docent hominem ad imaginem Dei creatum esse, capacem suum Creatorem cognoscendi et amandi, ab eo tamquam dominum super omnes creaturas terrenas constitutum, ut eas regeret, eisque uteretur, glorificans Deum. Quid est homo quod memor es eius? aut filius hominis, quoniam visitas eum? Minuisti eum paulo minus ab angelis, gloria et honore coronasti eum, et constituisti eum super opera manuum tuarum. Omnia subiecisti sub pedibus eius (Ps 8,5-7). At Deus non creavit hominem solum: nam inde a primordiis masculum et feminam creavit eos (Gn 1,27), quorum consociatio primam formam efficit communionis personarum. Homo etenim ex intima sua natura ens sociale est, atque sine relationibus cum aliis nec vivere nec suas dotes expandere potest. Deus igitur, sicut iterum in sacra Pagina legimus, vidit cuncta quae fecerat, et erant valde bona (Gn 1,31)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p405","On sin","14 One in body and soul, man by his very bodily condition gathers into himself the elements of the material world, so that through him they attain their summit and lift up their voice to the free praise of the Creator. Therefore man is not allowed to despise bodily life, but on the contrary he is bound to hold his body, as created by God and to be raised up on the last day, good and worthy of honor. Wounded by sin, however, he experiences rebellions of the body. The very dignity of man therefore demands that he glorify God in his body, and not allow it to serve the evil inclinations of his heart. Man, however, is not deceived when he acknowledges himself to be superior to bodily things, and considers himself not only as a particle of nature or an anonymous element of human society. For by interiority he exceeds the totality of things: he returns to this deep interiority when he turns to his heart, where God awaits him, who searches hearts, and where he himself under the eyes of God decides concerning his own lot. Therefore, acknowledging a spiritual and immortal soul in himself, he is not deluded by a fallacious fiction, flowing from physical and social conditions alone, but on the contrary attains the deep truth of things.","In iustitia a Deo constitutus, homo tamen, suadente Maligno, inde ab exordio historiae, libertate sua abusus est, seipsum contra Deum erigens et finem suum extra Deum attingere cupiens. Cum cognovissent Deum, non sicut Deum glorificaverunt, sed obscuratum est insipiens cor eorum et servierunt creaturae potius quam Creatori. Quod Revelatione divina nobis innotescit, cum ipsa experientia concordat. Nam homo, cor suum inspiciens, etiam ad malum inclinatum se comperit et in multiplicibus malis demersum, quae a bono suo Creatore provenire non possunt. Deum tamquam principium suum saepe agnoscere renuens, etiam debitum ordinem ad finem suum ultimum, simul ac totam suam sive erga seipsum sive erga alios homines et omnes res creatas ordinationem disrupit. Ideo in seipso divisus est homo. Quapropter tota vita hominum, sive singularis sive collectiva, ut luctationem et quidem dramaticam se exhibet inter bonum et malum, inter lucem et tenebras. Immo incapacem se invenit homo per seipsum mali impugnationes efficaciter debellandi, ita ut unusquisque se quasi catenis vinctum sentiat. At ipse Dominus venit ut hominem liberaret et confortaret, eum interius renovans ac principem huius mundi (cf. Jn 12,31) foras eiiciens qui eum in servitute peccati retinebat. Peccatum autem minuit ipsum hominem, a plenitudine consequenda eum repellens. In lumine huius Revelationis simul sublimis vocatio et profunda miseria, quas homines experiuntur, rationem suam ultimam inveniunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p406","On the constitution of man","15 Man judges rightly, partaking of the light of the divine mind, that by his intellect he surpasses the totality of things. By tirelessly exercising his genius through the ages, he has truly advanced in empirical sciences, technical and liberal arts. In our times, however, especially in investigating and subjecting the material world to himself, he has obtained distinguished successes. Always, however, he has sought and found deeper truth. For the intellect is not confined to mere phenomena, but can attain intelligible reality with true certainty, even if from the sequel of sin it is partly obscured and weakened. Finally, the intellectual nature of the human person is perfected and is to be perfected by wisdom, which gently attracts the mind of man to seek and love true goods, and by which man, imbued, is led through visible things to invisible. Our age, however, more than past ages, needs such wisdom that all new things discovered by man may become more humane. For the future fate of the world is endangered unless wiser men are raised up. Moreover, it is to be noted that many nations, poorer indeed in economic goods, but richer in wisdom, can offer outstanding benefit to others. By the gift of the Holy Spirit, man approaches to contemplate and savor the mystery of the divine plan by faith.","Corpore et anima unus, homo per ipsam suam corporalem condicionem elementa mundi materialis in se colligit, ita ut, per ipsum, fastigium suum attingant et ad liberam Creatoris laudem vocem attollant. Vitam ergo corporalem homini despicere non licet, sed e contra ipse corpus suum, utpote a Deo creatum et ultima die resuscitandum, bonum et honore dignum habere tenetur. Peccato tamen vulneratus, corporis rebelliones experitur. Ipsa igitur dignitas hominis postulat ut Deum glorificet in corpore suo, neve illud pravis cordis sui inclinationibus inservire sinat. Homo vero non fallitur, cum se rebus corporalibus superiorem agnoscit, et non tantum ut particulam naturae aut anonymum elementum civitatis humanae seipsum considerat. Interioritate enim sua universitatem rerum excedit: ad hanc profundam interioritatem redit, quando convertitur ad cor, ubi Deus eum exspectat, qui corda scrutatur, et ubi ipse sub oculis Dei de propria sorte decernit. Itaque, animam spiritualem et immortalem in seipso agnoscens, non fallaci figmento illuditur, a physicis tantum et socialibus condicionibus fluente, sed e contra ipsam profundam rei veritatem attingit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p407","On the dignity of the intellect, on truth, and on wisdom","16 In the depths of his conscience, man discovers a law which he does not impose on himself, but to which he ought to obey, and whose voice, always calling him to love and do good and avoid evil, when necessary sounds in the ears of his heart: do this, shun that. For man has a law inscribed in his heart by God, to obey which is his very dignity and according to which he himself will be judged. Conscience is the most secret nucleus and sanctuary of man, in which he is alone with God, whose voice resounds in his intimate being. Conscience makes known in a wonderful way that law which is fulfilled in the love of God and neighbor. By fidelity to conscience, Christians are joined with other men to seek truth and to solve in truth the many moral problems which arise both in the life of individuals and in social association. Therefore, the more right conscience prevails, the more persons and groups withdraw from blind arbitrariness and strive to conform to objective norms of morality. Yet not seldom it happens that conscience errs from invincible ignorance, without losing its dignity. But this cannot be said when man cares little about seeking truth and good, and conscience gradually becomes almost blinded from the habit of sin.","Recte iudicat homo, divinae mentis lumen participans, se intellectu suo universitatem rerum superare. Ingenium suum per saecula impigre exercendo ipse in scientiis empiricis, artibus technicis et liberalibus sane profecit. Nostris autem temporibus in mundo materiali praesertim investigando et sibi subiiciendo egregios obtinuit successus. Semper tamen profundiorem veritatem quaesivit et invenit. Intelligentia enim non ad sola phaenomena coarctatur, sed realitatem intelligibilem cum vera certitudine adipisci valet, etiamsi, ex sequela peccati, ex parte obscuratur et debilitatur. Humanae tandem personae intellectualis natura per sapientiam perficitur et perficienda est, quae mentem hominis ad vera bonaque inquirenda ac diligenda suaviter attrahit, et qua imbutus homo per visibilia ad invisibilia adducitur. Aetas autem nostra, magis quam saecula anteacta, tali sapientia indiget ut humaniora fiant quaecumque nova ab homine deteguntur. Periclitatur enim sors futura mundi nisi sapientiores suscitentur homines. Insuper notandum est plures nationes, bonis quidem oeconomicis pauperiores, sapientia vero ditiores, ceteris eximium emolumentum praestare posse. Spiritus Sancti dono, homo ad mysterium consilii divini contemplandum et sapiendum fide accedit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p408","On the dignity of the moral conscience","17 But only freely can man turn to good, which freedom our contemporaries greatly esteem and ardently pursue: and rightly indeed. Yet they often foster it in a depraved way, as license to do whatever pleases, even evil. True freedom, however, is an excellent sign of the divine image in man. For God willed to leave man in the hand of his own counsel, so that he may seek his Creator spontaneously and freely attain full and blessed perfection by adhering to Him. The dignity of man therefore requires that he act according to conscious and free choice, personally moved and induced from within, and not under blind internal impulse or mere external coercion. Man, however, obtains such dignity when, freeing himself from all captivity of passions, he pursues his end in the free choice of good and effectively and skillfully procures suitable means for himself. This ordering toward God, man's freedom wounded by sin, can only make fully active by the help of God's grace. But each will have to render an account of his own life before the tribunal of God, according as he has done either good or evil.","In imo conscientiae legem homo detegit, quam ipse sibi non dat, sed cui obedire debet, et cuius vox, semper ad bonum amandum et faciendum ac malum vitandum eum advocans, ubi oportet auribus cordis sonat: fac hoc, illud devita. Nam homo legem in corde suo a Deo inscriptam habet, cui parere ipsa dignitas eius est et secundum quam ipse iudicabitur. Conscientia est nucleus secretissimus atque sacrarium hominis, in quo solus est cum Deo, cuius vox resonat in intimo eius. Conscientia modo mirabili illa lex innotescit, quae in Dei et proximi dilectione adimpletur. Fidelitate erga conscientiam christiani cum ceteris hominibus coniunguntur ad veritatem inquirendam et tot problemata moralia, quae tam in vita singulorum quam in sociali consortione exsurgunt, in veritate solvenda. Quo magis ergo conscientia recta praevalet, eo magis personae et coetus a caeco arbitrio recedunt et normis obiectivis moralitatis conformari satagunt. Non raro tamen evenit ex ignorantia invincibili conscientiam errare, quin inde suam dignitatem amittat. Quod autem dici nequit cum homo de vero ac bono inquirendo parum curat, et conscientia ex peccati consuetudine paulatim fere obcaecatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p409","On the excellence of freedom","18 Before death, the enigma of the human condition becomes greatest. Not only is man tormented by pain and the progressive dissolution of the body, but also, indeed more, by the fear of perpetual extinction. Rightly, by the instinct of his heart, he judges and repels the total ruin and definitive end of his person. The seed of eternity which he bears in himself, irreducible to matter alone, rises up against death. All the efforts of technical art, although very useful, cannot calm the anxiety of man: for prolonged biological longevity cannot satisfy the desire for further life, which ineluctably lies in his heart. While before death all imagination fails, the Church, however, taught by divine Revelation, affirms that man was created by God for a blessed end, beyond the limits of earthly misery. Moreover, bodily death, from which man would have been exempted if he had not sinned, the Christian faith teaches will be overcome, when man, his salvation lost by his fault, is restored by the all-powerful and merciful Savior. For God called and calls man that he may adhere to Him in perpetual communion of incorruptible divine life with his whole nature. Christ, freeing man from death by His death, attained this victory by rising to life. Therefore, to any man reflecting, faith, offered with solid arguments, offers a response in his anxiety concerning his future lot; at the same time it gives him the faculty of communicating with beloved brothers already taken by death in Christ, giving hope that they have attained true life with God.","At nonnisi libere homo ad bonum se convertere potest, quam libertatem coaevi nostri magni faciunt ardenterque prosequuntur: et recte sane. Saepe tamen eam pravo modo fovent, tamquam licentiam quidquid faciendi dummodo delectet, etiam malum. Vera autem libertas eximium est divinae imaginis in homine signum. Voluit enim Deus hominem relinquere in manu consilii sui, ita ut Creatorem suum sponte quaerat et libere ad plenam et beatam perfectionem ei inhaerendo perveniat. Dignitas igitur hominis requirit ut secundum consciam et liberam electionem agat, personaliter scilicet ab intra motus et inductus, et non sub caeco impulsu interno vel sub mera externa coactione. Talem vero dignitatem obtinet homo cum, sese ab omni passionum captivitate liberans, finem suum in boni libera electione persequitur et apta subsidia efficaciter ac sollerti industria sibi procurat. Quam ordinationem ad Deum libertas hominis, a peccato vulnerata, nonnisi gratia Dei adiuvante, plene actuosam efficere potest. Unicuique autem ante tribunal Dei propriae vitae ratio reddenda erit, prout ipse sive bonum sive malum gesserit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p410","On the mystery of death","19 An excellent reason of human dignity consists in the vocation of man to communion with God. For man is invited to converse with God from his very origin: for he does not exist unless because, created by God from love, he is always conserved from love; nor does he live fully according to truth unless he freely acknowledges that love and commits himself to his Creator. Many, however, of our contemporaries do not at all perceive this intimate and vital connection with God, or explicitly reject it, so that atheism is to be counted among the most serious things of this time and to be subjected to more diligent examination. The word atheism designates phenomena very different among themselves. For while by some God is expressly denied, others think that man can assert nothing at all about Him; others submit the question of God to such a method of examination that it seems to lack sense. Many, unduly transgressing the limits of the positive sciences, either contend that all things are explained by this merely scientific reason, or on the contrary admit no absolute truth at all. Some so exalt man that faith in God almost becomes enervated, more inclined, as it seems, to the affirmation of man than to the denial of God. Others so conceive of God that the figment which they reject is by no means the God of the Gospel. Others do not even approach questions concerning God, since they seem to experience no religious anxiety nor perceive why religion should now be of concern to them. Atheism, moreover, not seldom arises either from a violent protest against evil in the world, or from the characteristic of the absolute unduly assigned to certain human goods, so that these are held in the place of God. Modern civilization itself, not from itself, but as too entangled in earthly things, can often make access to God more difficult. Indeed, those who voluntarily strive to banish God from their hearts and to avoid religious questions, not following the dictate of their conscience, are not without fault; yet even believers themselves often bear a certain responsibility concerning this. For atheism, considered integrally, is not something original, but arises rather from various causes, among which is also a critical reaction against religions and indeed, in some regions, especially against the Christian religion. Wherefore, in this genesis of atheism, believers can have no small part, insofar as, by neglecting the education of faith, or by a fallacious exposition of doctrine, or also by defects in their own religious, moral, and social life, they are to be said rather to veil than to reveal the genuine face of God and religion.","Coram morte aenigma condicionis humanae maximum evadit. Non tantum cruciatur homo dolore et corporis dissolutione progrediente, sed etiam, immo magis, perpetuae extinctionis timore. Recte autem instinctu cordis sui iudicat, cum totalem ruinam et definitivum exitum suae personae abhorret et respuit. Semen aeternitatis quod in se gerit, ad solam materiam cum irreductibile sit, contra mortem insurgit. Omnia technicae artis molimina, licet perutilia, anxietatem hominis sedare non valent: prorogata enim biologica longaevitas illi ulterioris vitae desiderio satisfacere nequit, quod cordi eius ineluctabiliter inest. Dum coram morte omnis imaginatio deficit, Ecclesia tamen, Revelatione divina edocta, hominem ad beatum finem, ultra terrestris miseriae limites, a Deo creatum esse affirmat. Mors insuper corporalis, a qua homo si non peccasset subtractus fuisset, fides christiana docet fore ut vincatur, cum homo in salutem, culpa sua perditam, ab omnipotente et miserante Salvatore restituetur. Deus enim hominem vocavit et vocat ut Ei in perpetua incorruptibilis vitae divinae communione tota sua natura adhaereat. Quam victoriam Christus, hominem a morte per mortem suam liberando, ad vitam resurgens adeptus est. Cuicumque igitur recogitanti homini, fides, cum solidis argumentis oblata, in eius anxietate de sorte futura responsum offert; simulque facultatem praebet cum dilectis fratribus iam morte praereptis in Christo communicandi, spem conferens eos veram vitam apud Deum adeptos esse."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p411","On the forms and roots of atheism","20 Modern atheism often also presents a systematic form, which, besides other causes, carries the desire for the autonomy of man to such an extent that it raises difficulty against any dependence on God. Those who profess such atheism contend that freedom consists in this, that man is his own end, the sole artificer and demiurge of his own history: which they think cannot be reconciled with the acknowledgment of the Lord, the author and end of all things, or at least renders such affirmation altogether superfluous. This doctrine can be favored by the sense of power which modern technical progress gives to man. Among the forms of modern atheism, that is not to be passed over which expects the liberation of man especially from his economic and social liberation. To this liberation, religion of its nature opposes, they contend, insofar as, raising the hope of man in a false future life, it would deter him from building the earthly city. Hence the supporters of such doctrine, when they come to the governance of the republic, vehemently oppress religion, diffusing atheism even by using those means of pressure which public power has, especially in the education of the young.","Dignitatis humanae eximia ratio in vocatione hominis ad communionem cum Deo consistit. Ad colloquium cum Deo iam inde ab ortu suo invitatur homo: non enim exsistit, nisi quia, a Deo ex amore creatus, semper ex amore conservatur; nec plene secundum veritatem vivit, nisi amorem illum libere agnoscat et Creatori suo se committat. Multi tamen ex coaevis nostris hanc intimam ac vitalem cum Deo coniunctionem nequaquam perspiciunt aut explicite reiiciunt, ita ut atheismus inter gravissimas huius temporis res adnumerandus sit ac diligentiori examini subiiciendus. Voce atheismi phaenomena inter se valde diversa designantur. Dum enim a quibusdam Deus expresse negatur, alii censent hominem nihil omnino de Eo asserere posse; alii vero quaestionem de Deo tali methodo examini subiiciunt, ut illa sensu carere videatur. Multi, scientiarum positivarum limites indebite praetergressi, aut omnia hac sola scientifica ratione explicari contendunt aut e contra nullam omnino veritatem absolutam iam admittunt. Quidam hominem tantopere exaltant, ut fides in Deum quasi enervis fiat, magis proclives, ut videntur, ad affirmationem hominis quam ad Dei negationem. Alii Deum sibi ita effingunt, ut illud figmentum, quod repudiant, nullo modo sit Deus Evangelii. Alii quaestiones de Deo ne aggrediuntur quidem, quippe qui inquietudinem religiosam non experiri videantur nec percipiant quare de religione iam sibi curandum sit. Atheismus praeterea non raro oritur sive ex violenta contra malum in mundo protestatione, sive ex nota ipsius absoluti quibusdam humanis bonis indebite adiudicata, ita ut ista iam loco Dei habeantur. Ipsa civilizatio hodierna, non ex se, sed utpote nimis rebus terrestribus intricata accessum ad Deum saepe difficiliorem reddere potest. Sane qui voluntarie Deum a corde suo arcere et quaestiones religiosas devitare conantur, dictamen conscientiae suae non secuti, culpae expertes non sunt; attamen et ipsi credentes quamdam de hoc responsabilitatem saepe ferunt. Atheismus enim, integre consideratus, non est quid originarium, sed potius ex diversis causis oritur, inter quas adnumeratur etiam reactio critica contra religiones et quidem, in nonnullis regionibus, praesertim contra religionem christianam. Quapropter in hac atheismi genesi partem non parvam habere possunt credentes, quatenus, neglecta fidei educatione, vel fallaci doctrinae expositione, vel etiam vitae suae religiosae, moralis ac socialis defectibus, Dei et religionis genuinum vultum potius velare quam revelare dicendi sint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p412","On systematic atheism","21 The Church, faithfully devoted both to God and to men, cannot cease to reprove with all firmness, as she has reproved before, those pernicious doctrines and actions which contradict reason and common human experience and cast man down from his innate excellence. Yet she strives to discover the hidden causes of the denial of God in the mind of atheists, and, conscious of the gravity of the questions which atheism excites and led by charity toward all men, judges that they are to be subjected to a serious and deeper examination. The Church holds that the acknowledgment of God in no way opposes the dignity of man, since such dignity is founded and perfected in God Himself: for man is constituted intelligent and free in society by God the Creator; but especially he is called as a son to the communion of God and to participation in His happiness. She teaches, moreover, that through eschatological hope the importance of earthly duties is not diminished, but rather their fulfillment is supported by new motives. On the contrary, if the divine foundation and the hope of eternal life are lacking, the dignity of man is gravely injured, as often appears today, and the enigmas of life and death, of guilt and sorrow, remain without solution, so that men not seldom sink into despair. Every man, however, remains to himself an unsolved question, obscurely perceived. For no one at certain moments, especially in greater events of life, can altogether avoid the aforesaid question. To this question, God alone fully and with all certainty gives an answer, who calls man to deeper thought and humbler inquiry. The remedy for atheism, however, is to be expected both from an aptly set forth doctrine and from the whole life of the Church and its members. For it is the Church's part to render God the Father and His incarnate Son present and as it were visible, by ceaselessly renewing and purifying herself under the guidance of the Holy Spirit. This is obtained especially by the testimony of a living and mature faith, namely educated to be able clearly to perceive difficulties and to overcome them. A splendid testimony of this faith very many martyrs have given and give. This faith ought to manifest its fruitfulness, penetrating the whole life of believers, even the profane, and moving them to justice and love, especially toward the needy. Finally, the fraternal charity of the faithful, who unanimous in spirit collaborate for the faith of the Gospel and show themselves as a sign of unity, most greatly contributes to manifesting the presence of God. The Church, however, although she altogether rejects atheism, nevertheless sincerely professes that all men, believers and unbelievers, ought to contribute to building up this world, in which they commonly live, which certainly cannot be done without a sincere and prudent dialogue. She therefore complains of the discrimination between believers and unbelievers which certain rulers of cities, not acknowledging the fundamental rights of the human person, unjustly introduce. For believers she demands active freedom that they may be permitted to build the temple of God also in this world. Atheists, however, she humanely invites to consider the Gospel of Christ with open heart. For the Church eminently knows that her message concords with the most secret desires of the human heart, since she vindicates the dignity of the human calling, restores hope to those who already despair of their higher lot. Her message, far from diminishing man, pours out light, life, and freedom for his progress; and besides it nothing is able to satisfy the heart of man: \"You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.\"","Atmosphaera moderna atheismi moderni saepe formam etiam systematicam praebet, quae, praeter alias causas, optatum autonomiae hominis eo usque perducit ut contra qualemcumque a Deo dependentiam difficultatem suscitet. Qui talem atheismum profitentur, libertatem in eo esse contendunt quod homo sibi ipse sit finis, propriae suae historiae solus artifex et demiurgus: quod componi non posse autumant cum agnitione Domini, omnium rerum auctoris et finis, vel saltem talem affirmationem plane superfluam reddere. Cui doctrinae favere potest sensus potentiae quem hodiernus progressus technicus homini confert. Inter formas hodierni atheismi illa non praetermittenda est, quae liberationem hominis praesertim ex eius liberatione oeconomica et sociali exspectat. Huic autem liberationi religionem natura sua obstare contendit, quatenus, in futuram fallacemque vitam spem hominis erigens, ipsum a civitatis terrestris aedificatione deterreret. Unde fautores talis doctrinae, ubi ad regimen reipublicae accedunt, religionem vehementer oppugnant, atheismum diffundentes etiam adhibitis, praesertim in iuvenum educatione, illis pressionis mediis, quibus potestas publica pollet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p413","On the attitude of the Church toward atheism","22 Indeed, only in the mystery of the incarnate Word does the mystery of man truly become clear. For Adam, the first man, was a figure of the future, namely of Christ the Lord. Christ, the new Adam, in the very revelation of the mystery of the Father and His love, fully manifests man to man himself and lays open his most high calling. Nothing therefore is wonderful that in Him the aforesaid truths find their source and attain their summit. He who is the image of the invisible God (Col 1:15), He is the perfect man, who restores the divine likeness to the sons of Adam, deformed from the first sin. Since in Him human nature was assumed, not destroyed, by that very fact it has been raised to a sublime dignity also in us. For He, the Son of God, by His incarnation joined Himself in a certain way with every man. He worked with human hands, thought with a human mind, acted with a human will, loved with a human heart. Born of the Virgin Mary, truly made one of us, He was in all things like to us, sin excepted. The innocent Lamb, by His freely poured out blood, merited life for us, and in Him God reconciled us to Himself and among ourselves, and snatched us from the slavery of the devil and sin, so that each of us can say with the Apostle: \"The Son of God loved me and delivered Himself up for me\" (Gal 2:20). By suffering for us, He not only gave an example that we should follow His footprints, but also restored the way, which if we follow, life and death are sanctified and receive a new meaning. The Christian man, however, made conformed to the image of the Son who is the Firstborn among many brothers, receives the first-fruits of the Spirit (Rom 8:23), by which he becomes capable of fulfilling the new law of love. Through this Spirit, who is the pledge of inheritance (Eph 1:14), the whole man is inwardly restored, to the redemption of the body (Rom 8:23): \"If the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead dwells in you, He who raised Jesus Christ from the dead shall also quicken your mortal bodies, because of His Spirit dwelling in you\" (Rom 8:11). The Christian is certainly urged by necessity and duty to struggle against evil through many tribulations and to suffer death; but associated with the paschal mystery, configured to the death of Christ, he will meet death strengthened by hope of resurrection. Which holds good not only for Christ's faithful, but also for all men of good will in whose heart grace works in an invisible way. For since Christ died for all and the ultimate vocation of man is truly one, namely divine, we must hold that the Holy Spirit offers to all the possibility that, in a way known to God, they may be associated with this paschal mystery. Such and so great is the mystery of man, which is illuminated for believers through Christian Revelation. Through Christ and in Christ, therefore, the enigma of pain and death is illuminated, which apart from His Gospel overwhelms us. Christ rose, by His death destroying death, and gave life to us, that, as sons in the Son, we may cry out in the Spirit: Abba, Father!","Ecclesia, fideliter tum Deo tum hominibus addicta, desistere non potest quin dolenter perniciosas illas doctrinas actionesque, quae rationi et communi experientiae humanae contradicunt hominemque ab innata eius excellentia deiiciunt, omni firmitate reprobet, sicut antehac reprobavit. Abditas tamen in atheorum mente negationis Dei causas deprehendere conatur et, de gravitate quaestionum quas atheismus excitat conscia necnon caritate erga omnes homines ducta, eas serio ac profundiori examini subiiciendas esse censet. Tenet Ecclesia agnitionem Dei dignitati hominis nequaquam opponi, cum huiusmodi dignitas in ipso Deo fundetur et perficiatur: homo enim a Deo creante intelligens ac liber in societate constituitur; sed praesertim ad ipsam Dei communionem ut filius vocatur et ad Ipsius felicitatem participandam. Docet praeterea per spem eschatologicam momentum munerum terrestrium non minui, sed potius eorum adimpletionem novis motivis fulciri. Deficientibus e contra fundamento divino et spe vitae aeternae, hominis dignitas gravissime laeditur, ut saepe hodie constat, atque vitae et mortis, culpae et doloris aenigmata sine solutione manent, ita ut homines in desperationem non raro deiiciantur. Omnis homo interea sibi ipsi remanet quaestio insoluta, subobscure percepta. Nemo enim quibusdam momentis, praecipue in maioribus vitae eventibus, praefatam interrogationem omnino effugere valet. Cui quaestioni solus Deus plene et omni certitudine responsum affert, qui ad altiorem cogitationem et humiliorem inquisitionem hominem vocat. Remedium autem atheismo afferendum, cum a doctrina apte exposita, tum ab integra Ecclesiae eiusque membrorum vita exspectandum est. Ecclesiae enim est Deum Patrem eiusque Filium incarnatum praesentem et quasi visibilem reddere, ductu Spiritus Sancti sese indesinenter renovando et purificando. Id imprimis obtinetur testimonio fidei vivae et maturae, ad hoc scilicet educatae ut difficultates lucide perspicere valeat easque superare. Huius fidei testimonium praeclarum plurimi martyres reddiderunt et reddunt. Quae fides suam fecunditatem manifestare debet, credentium integram vitam, etiam profanam, penetrando, eosque ad iustitiam et amorem, praesertim erga egentes, movendo. Ad praesentiam Dei manifestandam maxime denique confert caritas fraterna fidelium, qui spiritu unanimes collaborant fidei Evangelii, et signum unitatis se exhibent. Ecclesia vero, etsi atheismum omnino reiicit, sincere tamen profitetur homines omnes, credentes et non credentes, ad hunc mundum, in quo communiter vivunt, recte aedificandum opem conferre debere: quod certe fieri non potest sine sincero et prudenti colloquio. Conqueritur igitur de discrimine inter credentes et non credentes, quod quidam civitatum rectores, personae humanae iura fundamentalia non agnoscentes, iniuste inducunt. Pro credentibus vero actuosam libertatem expostulat ut in hoc mundo etiam Dei templum exstruere sinantur. Atheos autem humaniter invitat ut Evangelium Christi corde aperto considerent. Apprime etenim novit Ecclesia nuntium suum cum secretissimis humani cordis desideriis concordare, cum vocationis humanae dignitatem vindicat, illis qui iam de altiore sua sorte desperant spem restituens. Nuntium eius, nedum hominem minuat, lucem, vitam et libertatem ad eius profectum fundit; atque praeter illud nihil cordi hominis satisfacere valet: Fecisti nos ad Te, Domine, et inquietum est cor nostrum, donec requiescat in Te."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p414","On Christ the New Man","23 Among the principal aspects of the modern world, the multiplication of mutual relations among men is to be numbered, to whose development modern technical progress contributes very much. Yet the fraternal conversation of men is perfected not in those progresses, but more deeply in the community of persons, which demands mutual reverence toward their full spiritual dignity. To promote this communion among persons, Christian Revelation brings great aid, and at the same time leads us to a deeper understanding of the higher laws of social life which the Creator inscribed in the spiritual and moral nature of man. Since, however, the more recent documents of the Magisterium of the Church have more fully set forth the Christian doctrine concerning human society, the Council intends only to recall certain principal truths and to set forth their foundations under the light of Revelation. Then it insists on certain consequences which are of greater importance in our days.","Reapse nonnisi in mysterio Verbi incarnati mysterium hominis vere clarescit. Adam enim, primus homo, erat figura futuri, scilicet Christi Domini. Christus, novissimus Adam, in ipsa revelatione mysterii Patris Eiusque amoris, hominem ipsi homini plene manifestat eique altissimam eius vocationem patefacit. Nil igitur mirum in Eo praedictas veritates suum invenire fontem atque attingere fastigium. Qui est imago Dei invisibilis (Col 1,15), Ipse est homo perfectus, qui Adae filiis similitudinem divinam, inde a primo peccato deformatam, restituit. Cum in Eo natura humana assumpta, non perempta sit, eo ipso etiam in nobis ad sublimem dignitatem evecta est. Ipse enim, Filius Dei, incarnatione sua cum omni homine quodammodo Se univit. Humanis manibus opus fecit, humana mente cogitavit, humana voluntate egit, humano corde dilexit. Natus de Maria Virgine, vere unus ex nostris factus est, in omnibus nobis similis excepto peccato. Agnus innocens, sanguine suo libere effuso, vitam nobis meruit, in Ipsoque Deus nos Sibi et inter nos reconciliavit et a servitute diaboli ac peccati eripuit, ita ut unusquisque nostrum cum Apostolo dicere possit: Filius Dei dilexit me et tradidit semetipsum pro me (Ga 2,20). Pro nobis patiendo non solummodo exemplum praebuit ut sequamur vestigia Eius, sed et viam instauravit, quam dum sequimur, vita et mors sanctificantur novumque sensum accipiunt. Christianus autem homo, conformis imagini Filii factus qui est Primogenitus in multis fratribus, primitias Spiritus (Rm 8,23) accipit, quibus capax fit legem novam amoris adimplendi. Per hunc Spiritum, qui est pignus hereditatis (Ep 1,14), totus homo interius restauratur, usque ad redemptionem corporis (Rm 8,23): Si Spiritus Eius, qui suscitavit Iesum a mortuis, habitat in vobis: qui suscitavit Iesum Christum a mortuis, vivificabit et mortalia corpora vestra, propter inhabitantem Spiritum eius in vobis (Rm 8,11). Christianum certe urgent necessitas et officium contra malum per multas tribulationes certandi necnon mortem patiendi; sed mysterio paschali consociatus, Christi morti configuratus, ad resurrectionem spe roboratus occurret. Quod non tantum pro christifidelibus valet, sed et pro omnibus hominibus bonae voluntatis in quorum corde gratia invisibili modo operatur. Cum enim pro omnibus mortuus sit Christus cumque vocatio hominis ultima revera una sit, scilicet divina, tenere debemus Spiritum Sanctum cunctis possibilitatem offerre ut, modo Deo cognito, huic paschali mysterio consocientur. Tale et tantum est hominis mysterium, quod per Revelationem christianam credentibus illucescit. Per Christum et in Christo, igitur, illuminatur aenigma doloris et mortis, quod extra Eius Evangelium nos obruit. Christus resurrexit, morte sua mortem destruens, vitamque nobis largitus est ut, filii in Filio, clamemus in Spiritu: Abba, Pater!"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p415","Chapter II. on the Community of Men","24 God, who has fatherly care for all, willed that all men should form one family and treat one another with fraternal spirit. For all, created to the image of God, \"who made from one every race of men to dwell upon the whole face of the earth\" (Acts 17:26), are called to one and the same end, that is to God Himself. Wherefore the love of God and neighbor is the first and greatest commandment. But from sacred Scripture we are taught that the love of God cannot be separated from the love of neighbor: \"if there be any other commandment, it is summed up in this word: You shall love your neighbor as yourself... Therefore the fullness of the law is love\" (Rom 13:9-10; cf. 1 Jn 4:20). Which is judged to be of the greatest importance for men more and more dependent on one another and for the world more and more unified. Indeed, the Lord Jesus, when He prays the Father that all may be one... even as we are one (Jn 17:21-22), opening up prospects impervious to human reason, suggests a certain likeness between the union of the divine persons and the union of the sons of God in truth and charity. This likeness manifests that man, who is the only creature on earth that God willed for its own sake, cannot fully find himself except by a sincere gift of himself.","Inter praecipuos mundi hodierni aspectus, mutuarum inter homines necessitudinum multiplicatio adnumeratur, ad quam evolvendam hodierni technici progressus plurimum conferunt. Tamen fraternum hominum colloquium non in istis progressibus, sed profundius in personarum communitate perficitur, quae mutuam reverentiam erga plenam earum dignitatem spiritualem exigit. Ad hanc vero communionem inter personas promovendam, Revelatio christiana magnum subsidium affert, simulque ad altiorem vitae socialis legum intelligentiam nos perducit quas Creator in natura spirituali ac morali hominis inscripsit. Quoniam autem recentiora Ecclesiae Magisterii documenta christianam de societate humana doctrinam fusius exposuerunt, Concilium quasdam tantum principaliores veritates in memoriam revocat earumque fundamenta sub luce Revelationis exponit. Deinde in quaedam consectaria insistit quae nostris diebus maioris sunt momenti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p416","On the communitarian nature of the human vocation in the design of God","25 From the social nature of man it appears that the progress of the human person and the increase of society itself depend on one another. For the principle, subject, and end of all social institutions is and ought to be the human person, who by his very nature wholly needs social life. Since therefore social life is not something extrinsic to man, it follows that man grows and can respond to his vocation through commerce with others, mutual duties, conversation with brothers, with respect to all his gifts. From the social bonds which are necessary for the development of man, some, such as the family and political community, more immediately correspond to his intimate nature; others rather proceed from his free will. In our age, for various reasons, mutual relations and interdependencies increasingly multiply; whence arise various associations and institutions, whether public or private. This fact, however, which is called socialization, although not without its perils, nevertheless carries with it many benefits for confirming and increasing the qualities of the human person and protecting his rights. But if the human person receives much from this social life to fulfill his vocation, even religious, it cannot be denied that men from the social conditions in which they live and, from infancy, are immersed, are often turned away from doing good and impelled to evil. It is certain that the disturbances, so frequently occurring in the social order, partly arise from the tension of economic, political, and social forms themselves. But more deeply they arise from the pride and selfishness of men, which also pervert the social environment. Where the order of things is affected by the sequel of sin, man, born prone to evil, finds new incitements to sin, which, without strenuous efforts helped by grace, cannot be overcome.","Deus, qui paternam curam omnium habet, voluit ut cuncti homines unam efficerent familiam fraternoque animo se invicem tractarent. Omnes enim creati ad imaginem Dei, qui fecit ex uno omne genus hominum inhabitare super universam faciem terrae (Ac 17,26), ad unum eumdemque finem, id est ad Deum ipsum, vocantur. Quapropter dilectio Dei et proximi primum et maximum mandatum est. A Sacra autem Scriptura docemur Dei amorem a proximi amore seiungi non posse: ... si quod est aliud mandatum, in hoc verbo instauratur: Diliges proximum tuum sicut teipsum ... Plenitudo ergo legis est dilectio (Rm 13,9-10 cf. 1Jn 4,20). Quod vero hominibus magis in dies ab invicem dependentibus atque mundo magis in dies unificato maximi comprobatur esse momenti. Immo Dominus Iesus, quando Patrem orat ut omnes unum sint..., sicut et nos unum sumus (Jn 17,21-22), prospectus praebens humanae rationi impervios, aliquam similitudinem innuit inter unionem personarum divinarum et unionem filiorum Dei in veritate et caritate. Haec similitudo manifestat hominem, qui in terris sola creatura est quam Deus propter seipsam voluerit, plene seipsum invenire non posse nisi per sincerum sui ipsius donum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p417","On the interdependence of the human person and human society","26 From the ever stricter interdependence and gradually diffused to the whole world, it follows that the common good—that is, the sum of those conditions of social life which permit both groups and individual members to more fully and conveniently attain their own perfection—today more and more becomes universal, and therefore implies rights and duties which respect the whole human race. Every group should have regard for the necessities and legitimate aspirations of other groups, indeed of the common good of the whole human family. At the same time, however, the consciousness grows of the eminent dignity which belongs to the human person, since he excels all things, and his rights and duties are universal and inviolable. Therefore all those things must be made accessible to man which he needs to lead a truly human life, such as food, clothing, habitation, the right freely to choose a state of life and to found a family, to education, to work, to good reputation, to respect, to suitable information, to acting according to the right norm of his conscience, to the protection of private life, and to just freedom even in religious matters. The social order and its progress, therefore, must unceasingly give way to the good of persons, since the ordering of things must be subjected to the order of persons and not the contrary, the Lord Himself intimating this when He said the sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath. That order, to be developed day by day, must be founded in truth, built up in justice, made alive by love; in freedom, however, it must find an ever more human balance. To accomplish these things, the renewal of mind and vast changes in society are to be introduced. The Spirit of God, who by wonderful providence directs the course of times and renews the face of the earth, is present to this development. The evangelical leaven, however, has aroused and arouses in the heart of man the irrepressible exigency of dignity.","Ex sociali hominis indole apparet humanae personae profectum et ipsius societatis incrementum ab invicem pendere. Etenim principium, subiectus et finis omnium institutorum socialium est et esse debet humana persona, quippe quae, suapte natura, vita sociali omnino indigeat. Cum igitur vita socialis non sit homini quid adventicium, ideo commercio cum aliis, mutuis officiis, colloquio cum fratribus, quoad omnes suas dotes grandescit homo, et suae vocationi respondere potest. Ex socialibus vinculis, quae homini excolendo necessaria sunt, alia, uti familia et communitas politica, intimae eius naturae immediatius congruunt; alia potius ex eius libera voluntate procedunt. Nostra hac aetate, variis de causis, mutuae necessitudines et interdependentiae in dies multiplicantur; unde diversa oriuntur consociationes et instituta sive publici sive privati iuris. Hoc autem factum, quod socializatio nuncupatur, licet periculis sane non careat, multa tamen secum emolumenta affert ad confirmandas et augendas humanae personae qualitates eiusque iura tuenda. Sed si personae humanae ad suam vocationem adimplendam, etiam religiosam, ex hac vita sociali multum accipiunt, negari tamen nequit homines ex adiunctis socialibus in quibus vivunt et, inde ab infantia, immerguntur, saepe a bono faciendo averti et ad malum impelli. Certum est perturbationes, tam frequenter in ordine sociali occurrentes, ex ipsa formarum oeconomicarum, politicarum et socialium tensione pro parte provenire. Sed penitius ex hominum superbia et egoismo oriuntur, quae etiam ambitum socialem pervertunt. Ubi autem ordo rerum sequelis peccati afficitur, homo, proclivis ad malum natus, nova deinde ad peccatum incitamenta invenit, quae, sine strenuis gratia adiuvante conatibus, superari nequeunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p418","On promoting the common good","27 Descending to practical and more urgent consequences, the Council inculcates reverence toward man, that each should consider his neighbor, no one excepted, as another self, having regard first of all for his life and the means necessary for living it worthily, lest he imitate the rich man who had no care for poor Lazarus. Especially in our times the obligation presses to make ourselves the neighbor of absolutely any man, and to actively serve him when he occurs, whether he be an old man abandoned by all, or a foreign worker unjustly despised, or an exile, or a child born of an illegitimate union, undeservedly suffering for a sin not committed by him, or a hungry man who calls our conscience, recalling the Lord's voice: \"As long as you did it to one of these my least brethren, you did it to me\" (Mt 25:40). Moreover, whatever things oppose life itself, such as any kind of homicides, genocides, abortion, euthanasia, and voluntary suicide itself; whatever violate the integrity of the human person, such as mutilations, torments inflicted on body or mind, attempts to coerce minds themselves; whatever offend human dignity, such as inhuman living conditions, arbitrary imprisonments, deportations, slavery, prostitution, the commerce of women and young persons; also dishonorable working conditions, in which workers are treated as mere instruments of profit, not as free and responsible persons: all these and other such things are indeed shameful, and while they infect human civilization, they more stain those who so conduct themselves than those who suffer injury, and most greatly contradict the honor of the Creator.","Ex interdependentia in dies strictiore et paulatim ad mundum universum diffusa sequitur bonum commune - seu summam eorum vitae socialis condicionum quae tum coetibus, tum singulis membris permittunt ut propriam perfectionem plenius atque expeditius consequantur - hodie magis magisque universale evadere, et exinde iura officiaque implicare, quae totum humanum genus respiciunt. Quilibet coetus necessitatum et legitimarum appetitionum aliorum coetuum, immo boni communis totius familiae humanae, rationem habere debet. Simul vero conscientia crescit eximiae dignitatis quae personae humanae competit, cum ipsa rebus omnibus praestet, et eius iura officiaque universalia sint atque inviolabilia. Oportet ergo ut ea omnia homini pervia reddantur, quibus ad vitam vere humanam gerendam indiget, ut sunt victus, vestitus, habitatio, ius ad statum vitae libere eligendum et ad familiam condendam, ad educationem, ad laborem, ad bonam famam, ad reverentiam, ad congruam informationem, ad agendum iuxta rectam suae conscientiae normam, ad vitae privatae protectionem atque ad iustam libertatem etiam in re religiosa. Ordo socialis igitur eiusque progressus in bonum personarum indesinenter cedere debent, siquidem rerum ordinatio ordini personarum subiicienda est et non e converso, ipso Domino id innuente cum dixerit sabbatum propter hominem factum esse et non hominem propter sabbatum. Ordo ille in dies evolvendus, in veritate fundandus, in iustitia aedificandus, amore vivificandus est; in libertate autem aequilibrium in dies humanius invenire debet. Ad haec autem implenda mentis renovatio atque amplae societatis immutationes inducendae sunt. Spiritus Dei, qui mirabili providentia temporum cursum dirigit et faciem terrae renovat, huic evolutioni adest. Evangelicum autem fermentum in corde hominis irrefrenabilem dignitatis exigentiam excitavit atque excitat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p419","On reverence toward the human person","28 Reverence and charity ought also to be extended to those who think or act otherwise than we do in social, political, or even religious matters; by how much more indeed we more intimately understand their ways of thinking with humanity and charity, by so much the more easily we will be able to enter into dialogue with them. This charity and benignity, of course, ought by no means to render us indifferent toward truth and good. Indeed, charity itself urges the disciples of Christ to announce the saving truth to all men. But we must distinguish between error, always to be rejected, and the erring person, who always retains the dignity of the human person, even when infected with false or less accurate religious notions. God alone is the judge and searcher of hearts: whence He forbids us to judge concerning the interior guilt of anyone. The doctrine of Christ, which also demands that we forgive injuries, extends the precept of love to all enemies, which is the commandment of the New Law: \"You have heard that it was said: You shall love your neighbor, and hate your enemy. But I say to you: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who persecute and calumniate you\" (Mt 5:43-44).","Ad practica urgentioraque consectaria descendens, Concilium reverentiam inculcat erga hominem, ita ut singuli proximum, nullo excepto, tamquam alterum seipsum considerare debeant, de eius vita et de mediis ad illam digne degendam necessariis rationem imprimis habentes, ne divitem illum imitentur, qui pauperis Lazari nullam curam egit. Nostris praesertim diebus urget obligatio nosmetipsos cuiuslibet omnino hominis proximos efficiendi et illi occurrenti actuose inserviendi, sive sit senex ab omnibus derelictus, sive alienigena operarius iniuste despectus, sive exsul, sive infans ex illegitima unione natus, immerito patiens propter peccatum a se non commissum, vel esuriens qui conscientiam nostram interpellat Domini vocem revocans: Quamdiu fecistis uni ex his fratribus meis minimis? mihi fecistis (Mt 25,40). Quaecumque insuper ipsi vitae adversantur, ut cuiusvis generis homicidia, genocidia, abortus, euthanasia et ipsum voluntarium suicidium; quaecumque humanae personae integritatem violant, ut mutilationes, tormenta corpori mentive inflicta, conatus ipsos animos coercendi; quaecumque humanam dignitatem offendunt, ut infrahumanae vivendi condiciones, arbitrariae incarcerationes, deportationes, servitus, prostitutio, mercatus mulierum et iuvenum; condiciones quoque laboris ignominiosae, quibus operarii ut mera quaestus instrumenta, non ut liberae et responsabiles personae tractantur: haec omnia et alia huiusmodi probra quidem sunt, ac dum civilizationem humanam inficiunt, magis eos inquinant qui sic se gerunt, quam eos qui iniuriam patiuntur et Creatoris honori maxime contradicunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p420","On reverence and love toward adversaries","29 Since all men, possessing a rational soul and created to the image of God, have the same nature and same origin, and, being redeemed by Christ, enjoy the same vocation and divine destiny, fundamental equality among all is more and more to be recognized. Indeed, by various physical capacities and diversity of intellectual and moral forces, not all men are equal. Yet every mode of discrimination in the fundamental rights of the person, whether social or cultural, on account of sex, race, color, social condition, language, or religion, is to be overcome and removed, as contrary to the purpose of God. For it is truly to be mourned that those fundamental rights of the person are not yet everywhere kept whole. As if woman is denied the faculty of freely choosing a spouse and embracing a state of life, or of access to equal education and culture which is acknowledged to man. Moreover, although just diversities exist among men, the equal dignity of persons demands that a more humane and just condition of life be attained. For excessive economic and social inequalities among members or peoples of one human family are scandalous, and oppose social justice, equity, the dignity of the human person, as well as social and international peace. Human institutions, however, whether private or public, should strive to serve the dignity and end of man, at the same time strenuously fighting against any servitude both social and political, and preserving the fundamental rights of man under any political regime. Indeed, such institutions must gradually be congruous with spiritual things, the highest of all, even if sometimes a rather long time is needed to attain the desired end.","Ad illos etiam qui in rebus socialibus, politicis vel etiam religiosis aliter ac nos sentiunt aut faciunt, reverentia et caritas extendi debent; quo magis quidem humanitate et caritate modos sentiendi eorum intimius comprehendemus, eo facilius cum ipsis colloquium inire poterimus. Haec sane caritas et benignitas nequaquam indifferentes erga veritatem et bonum nos reddere debent. Immo caritas ipsa discipulos Christi urget ad veritatem salutarem omnibus hominibus annuntiandam. Sed distinguere oportet inter errorem, semper reiciendum, et errantem, qui dignitatem personae iugiter servat, etiam ubi falsis minusve accuratis notionibus religiosis inquinatur. Deus solus iudex est et scrutator cordium: unde nos vetat de interiore cuiusvis culpa iudicare. Doctrina Christi ut etiam iniuriis ignoscamus postulat praeceptumque amoris ad inimicos omnes extendit, quod est Novae Legis mandatum: Audistis quia dictum est: Diliges proximum tuum, et odio habebis inimicum tuum. Ego autem dico vobis: Diligite inimicos vestros, benefacite his qui oderunt vos: et orate pro persequentibus et calumniantibus vos (Mt 5,43-44)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p421","On the essential equality among all men and on social justice","30 The profound and rapid change of things more urgently demands that no one, not attending to the course of things or torpid with inertia, indulge in a merely individualistic ethics. The duty of justice and charity is more and more fulfilled by this, that each, contributing to the common good according to his capacities and the needs of others, also promotes and helps institutions, whether public or private, which serve to better the living conditions of men. But there are those who, professing generous and more noble opinions, yet so always live in reality as if they had no care for the necessities of society. Indeed, many, in various regions, make little of social laws and prescriptions. Not a few, by various frauds and deceits, do not fear to evade just taxes or other things due to society. Others set little value on certain norms of social life, for example, concerning health, or concerning the regulation of vehicular traffic, not noticing that by such negligence they bring peril to their own life and that of others. It be sacred to all to reckon social relations among the principal duties of modern man, and to observe them. For the more the world is unified, the more openly the duties of men surpass particular groups and gradually extend to the whole world. Which cannot be done unless individual men and their groups cultivate moral and social virtues in themselves and diffuse them in society, that they may truly become new men and artificers of a new humanity, with the necessary help of divine grace.","Cum omnes homines, anima rationali pollentes et ad imaginem Dei creati, eamdem naturam eamdemque originem habeant, cumque, a Christo redempti, eadem vocatione et destinatione divina fruantur, fundamentalis aequalitas inter omnes magis magisque agnoscenda est. Sane varia capacitate physica viriumque intellectualium et moralium diversitate non omnes homines aequiparantur. Omnis tamen discriminandi modus in iuribus personae fundamentalibus, sive socialis sive culturalis, ob sexum, stirpem, colorem, socialem condicionem, linguam aut religionem, superandus et removendus est, utpote Dei proposito contrarius. Vere enim dolendum est iura illa fundamentalia personae adhuc non ubique sarta tecta servari. Ut si mulieri denegetur facultas libere sponsum eligendi et vitae statum amplectendi, vel ad parem educationem et culturam quae viro agnoscitur accedendi. Insuper, quamquam inter homines iustae diversitates adsunt, aequalis personarum dignitas postulat ut ad humaniorem et aequam vitae condicionem deveniatur. Etenim nimiae inter membra vel populos unius familiae humanae inaequalitate oeconomicae et sociales scandalum movent, atque iustitiae sociali, aequitati, personae humanae dignitati, necnon paci sociali et internationali adversantur. Humanae autem institutiones, sive privatae sive publicae, dignitati ac fini hominis subservire nitantur, simul adversus quamlibet servitutem tum socialem tum politicam strenue decertantes, et iura hominum fundamentalia sub omni regimine politico servantes. Immo, huiusmodi institutiones spiritualibus rebus, omnium altissimis, paulatim congruant oportet, etiamsi interdum sat longo tempore opus sit ut ad optatum finem perveniant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p422","That we must progress beyond individualistic ethics","31 So that individual men more accurately fulfill the duty of their conscience both toward themselves and toward the various groups of which they are members, they must be diligently educated to a wider culture of mind, by using the immense aids which are available today to the human race. First of all, the education of the young of whatever social origin is to be so instituted that men and women may be raised up who are not only of cultivated mind but also of great spirit, as being urgently demanded by our time. But man can scarcely reach this sense of responsibility, unless the conditions of life permit him to become conscious of his dignity and to respond to his vocation by spending himself for God and for others. Human freedom, however, often becomes weaker when man falls into extreme poverty, just as it becomes vile when he, indulging in too great facilities of life, encloses himself in a golden solitude. On the contrary, it is strengthened when man accepts the inevitable necessities of social life, assumes the many forms of human association, and obliges himself to the service of the human community. Therefore the will of all to assume their part in common undertakings is to be stimulated. That manner of acting of nations is to be praised, in which the largest part of citizens shares in the public affairs in true freedom. Yet regard must be had for the real condition of each people and the necessary vigor of public authority. That all citizens may be inclined to participate in the life of the various groups of which the social body consists, it is necessary that in these groups they find goods which attract them and dispose them to the service of others. We may rightly think that the future lot of humanity is placed in the hands of those who are able to hand down to future generations the ways of living and hoping.","Profunda et velox rerum immutatio urgentius postulat ut nemo sit qui, ad rerum cursum non attendens vel inertia torpens, ethicae mere individualisticae indulgeat. Iustitiae ac caritatis officium magis ac magis adimpletur per hoc quod unusquisque, ad bonum commune iuxta proprias capacitates et aliorum necessitates conferens, etiam institutiones sive publicas sive privatas promovet et adiuvat quae hominum vitae condicionibus in melius mutandis inserviunt. Sunt autem qui, largas generosioresque opiniones profitentes, ita tamen semper reapse vivunt ac si nullam societatis necessitatum curam habeant. Immo, plures, in variis regionibus, leges et praescriptiones sociales minimi faciunt. Non pauci, variis fraudibus ac dolis, iusta vectigalia vel alia quae societati debentur effugere non verentur. Alii normas quasdam vitae socialis, e. gr., ad valetudinem tuendam, aut ad vehiculorum ductum moderandum statutas, parvi aestimant, non animadvertentes se tali incuria vitae suae et aliorum periculum inferre. Sanctum sit omnibus necessitudines sociales inter praecipua hominis hodierni officia recensere easque observare. Quo magis enim mundus unitur, eo apertius hominum munera particulares coetus superant et ad universum mundum paulatim extenduntur. Quod fieri nequit nisi et singuli homines et ipsorum coetus virtutes morales et sociales in seipsis colant et in societate diffundant, ita ut vere novi homines et artifices novae humanitatis exsistant cum necessario auxilio divinae gratiae."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p423","On responsibility and participation","32 Just as God did not create men to live individually, but to form a social union, so it also pleased Him to sanctify and save men not individually, apart from any mutual connection, but to make them into a people who would acknowledge Him in truth and serve Him holy. From the beginning of the history of salvation, He chose men not only as individuals but as members of a certain community. For God, opening His purpose, called those elect His people (Ex 3:7-12), with whom He moreover made a covenant on Sinai. This communitarian character is perfected and consummated in the work of Jesus Christ. For the Incarnate Word Himself willed to be a partaker of human association. He was present at the wedding of Cana, entered the house of Zacchaeus, ate with publicans and sinners. He revealed the Father's love and the excellent vocation of man, by recalling the most common social things and by using expressions and figures of quite everyday life. He sanctified human relations, especially family ones, from which social relations arise, voluntarily subject to the laws of His country. He willed to lead the life of a worker of His own time and region. In His preaching, He clearly commanded the sons of God to treat one another as brothers. In His prayer, He asked that all His disciples might be one. Indeed, He offered Himself unto death for all, the Redeemer of all. \"Greater love than this no one has, that one lay down his life for his friends\" (Jn 15:13). He commanded the Apostles to preach the Gospel message to all nations, that the human race might become the family of God, in which the fullness of the law would be love. The Firstborn among many brothers, among all who receive Him by faith and charity, after His death and resurrection, by the gift of His Spirit He instituted a new fraternal communion, namely in His body, which is the Church, in which all, each members of one another, according to the diverse gifts granted, render mutual services to one another. This solidarity is always to be increased, until the day when it will be consummated, and when men, saved by grace, as a family beloved by God and Christ the Brother, will render perfect glory to God.","Ut singuli homines suum conscientiae officium accuratius impleant tum erga seipsos, tum erga varios coetus quorum membra sunt, diligenter ad ampliorem animi culturam educandi sunt, ingentibus adhibitis subsidiis quae hodie generi humano praesto sunt. Praeprimis educatio iuvenum cuiuslibet socialis originis ita instituenda est, ut viri mulieresque suscitentur qui non tantum exculti ingenii sed et magni animi sint, utpote qui a nostro tempore vehementer postulentur. Sed ad hunc responsabilitatis sensum homo vix pervenit, nisi vitae condiciones ei permittant ut suae dignitatis conscius fiat, et vocationi suae, seipsum pro Deo et pro aliis impendendo, respondeat. Humana vero libertas saepe debilior fit, ubi homo in extremam incidit egestatem, sicut vilescit, ubi ipse nimiis vitae facilitatibus indulgens, in aurea veluti solitudine seipsum includit. E contra roboratur, cum homo inevitabiles vitae socialis necessitates accipit, multiformes exigentias humanae coniunctionis assumit atque humanae communitatis servitio se obstringit. Ideo omnium exstimulanda est voluntas inceptorum communium suas partes assumendi. Laudanda est autem ratio agendi nationum, in quibus pars quam maxima civium in vera libertate rerum publicarum particeps fit. Ratio tamen habenda est condicionis realis uniuscuiusque gentis et necessarii vigoris publicae auctoritatis. Ut vero omnes cives proni sint ad participandam vitam variorum coetuum, quibus corpus sociale constat, necesse est ut his in coetibus bona inveniant, quae ipsos attrahant eosque ad aliorum servitium disponant. Iure arbitrari possumus futuram humanitatis sortem in illorum manibus reponi, qui posteris generationibus vivendi et sperandi rationes tradere valent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p424","The Incarnate Word and human solidarity","33 By his labor and genius, man has always tried to develop his life more; today, however, especially by means of science and technical art, he has extended and continues to extend his dominion over almost all nature, and with the help especially of the increased means of various commerce among nations, the human family gradually recognizes and constitutes itself as one community in the whole world. Whence it comes about that many goods, which man formerly expected especially from supernal powers, today he procures for himself by his own industry. Before this immense effort, which already pervades the whole human race, many questions arise among men. What is the meaning and value of that laboriousness? How are all these things to be used? Toward what end to be attained do the efforts both of individuals and of societies tend? The Church, which guards the deposit of the word of God, from which principles in the religious and moral order are drawn, although it does not always have a ready answer to every question, wishes to join the light of revelation with the skill of all, that the way may be illuminated, which humanity has recently entered upon.","Sicut Deus homines non ad singulatim vivendum, sed ad socialem unionem efformandam creavit, ita Ipsi etiam placuit ... homines non singulatim, quavis mutua connexione seclusa, sanctificare et salvare, sed eos in populum constituere, qui in veritate Ipsum agnosceret Ipsique sancte serviret. Inde ab initio historiae salutis Ipse homines elegit non ut individuos tantum sed ut membra cuiusdam communitatis. Illos enim electos Deus, suum aperiens consilium, vocavit populum suum (Ex 3,7-12), quocum insuper in Sinai foedus pepigit. Quae indoles communitaria opere Iesu Christi perficitur et consummatur. Ipsum enim Verbum incarnatum humanae consortionis particeps esse voluit. Canae nuptiis interfuit, in domum Zachaei descendit, cum publicanis et peccatoribus manducavit. Patris amorem hominumque eximiam vocationem, communissimas res sociales commemorando et locutiones figurasque vitae plane cotidianae adhibendo, revelavit. Necessitudines humanas, imprimis familiares, ex quibus rationes sociales oriuntur, sanctificavit, legibus suae patriae voluntarie subditus. Vitam opificis sui temporis et regionis propriam ducere voluit. In sua praedicatione clare mandavit filiis Dei ut tamquam fratres ad invicem se gererent. In sua oratione rogavit ut omnes discipuli sui unum essent. Immo Ipse usque ad mortem sese pro omnibus obtulit, omnium Redemptor. Maiorem hac dilectionem nemo habet, ut animam suam ponat quis pro amicis suis (Jn 15,13). Apostolos autem iussit praedicare omnibus gentibus nuntium evangelicum ut genus humanum familia Dei fieret, in qua plenitudo legis esset dilectio. Primogenitus in multis fratribus, inter omnes qui Eum fide ac caritate recipiunt, post mortem et resurrectionem suam, dono sui Spiritus novam fraternam communionem instituit, in Corpore scilicet suo, quod est Ecclesia, in quo omnes, inter se invicem membra, secundum dona diversa concessa, mutua sibi praestarent servitia. Quae solidarietas semper augenda erit, usque ad illam diem qua consummabitur, et qua homines, gratia salvati, tamquam familia a Deo et Christo Fratre dilecta, perfectam gloriam Deo praestabunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p425","Chapter III. on Human Activity in the Whole World","34 This is certain to believers, that human activity, individual and collective, that great effort by which men throughout the course of ages strive to better the conditions of their life, considered in itself, corresponds to the purpose of God. For man, created to the image of God, received the mandate that, subduing the earth with all things contained in it, and ruling the world in justice and holiness, and, acknowledging God the Creator of all, he should refer himself and the totality of things to Him, so that with all things subject to man, the name of God might be wonderful in all the earth. This also regards the most daily work. For men and women who, while procuring the sustenance of life for themselves and their family, so exercise their activities that they duly serve society, can rightly think that by their labor they are developing the work of the Creator, providing for the goods of their brothers, and contributing by personal industry to the fulfilling of the divine plan in history. Christians, therefore, far from thinking that the works which men have produced by their genius and virtue oppose the power of God, and that the rational creature exists as a rival of the Creator, rather are persuaded that the victories of the human race are a sign of the greatness of God and the fruit of His ineffable plan. But the more human power grows, the more the responsibility of individuals and communities extends. Hence it appears that the Christian message does not turn men away from building the world, nor impel them to neglect the good of their fellows, but rather more strictly obliges them to this duty.","Suo labore atque ingenio homo suam vitam amplius evolvere semper conatus est; hodie autem, praesertim ope scientiae et artis technicae, suum dominium in universam fere naturam dilatavit ac iugiter dilatat, et adiuvantibus imprimis auctis inter nationes multimodi commercii mediis, familia humana paulatim tamquam unam in universo mundo communitatem sese agnoscit atque constituit. Quo fit, ut multa bona, quae olim homo a supernis viribus praesertim exspectabat, hodie iam propria industria sibi procuret. Coram immenso hoc conamine, quod totum humanum genus iam pervadit, multae exsurgunt inter homines interrogationes. Quinam est illius operositatis sensus et valor? Quomodo omnibus his rebus utendum est? Ad quem finem assequendum nisus sive singulorum sive societatum tendunt? Ecclesia, quae depositum verbi Dei custodit, ex quo principia in ordine religioso et morali hauriuntur, quin semper de singulis quaestionibus responsum in promptu habeat, lumen revelationis cum omnium peritia coniungere cupit, ut iter illuminetur, quod humanitas nuper ingressa est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p426","On the value of human activity","35 Human activity, however, just as it proceeds from man, so it is ordered to man. For man, when he works, not only changes things and society, but also perfects himself. He learns many things, cultivates his faculties, goes beyond himself and above himself. Such growth, if rightly understood, is of greater value than the external riches which can be gathered. Man is more worth for what he is than for what he has. Likewise, all those things which men do to attain greater justice, wider fraternity, and a more humane ordering in social relations, are worth more than technical progress. For these progresses can as it were provide the matter for human promotion, but they by themselves alone by no means bring it to act. Hence this is the norm of human activity, that it be congruous with the divine plan and will, with the genuine good of the human race, and permit the individual man placed in society the cult and fulfillment of his whole vocation.","Hoc credentibus ratum est, navitatem humanam individualem et collectivam, seu ingens illud conamen, quo homines decursu saeculorum suae vitae condiciones in melius mutare satagunt, in seipso consideratum, Dei proposito respondere. Homo enim, ad imaginem Dei creatus, mandatum accepit ut, terram cum omnibus quae in ea continentur sibi subiciens, mundum in iustitia et sanctitate regeret utque, Deum omnium Creatorem agnoscens, seipsum ac rerum universitatem ad Ipsum referret, ita ut rebus omnibus homini subiectis, admirabile sit nomen Dei in universa terra. Quod etiam opera penitus quotidiana respicit. Viri namque et mulieres qui, dum vitae sustentationem sibi et familiae comparant, navitates suas ita exercent ut societati opportune ministrent, iure existimare possunt se suo labore opus Creatoris evolvere, commodis fratrum suorum consulere, et ad consilium divinum in historia adimplendum personali industria conferre. Christiani itaque, nedum arbitrentur opera, quae homines suo ingenio et virtute pepererunt, Dei potentiae opponi, creaturamque rationalem quasi aemulam Creatoris exsistere, potius persuasum habent humani generis victorias signum esse magnitudinis Dei et fructus ineffabilis Ipsius consilii. Quo magis vero hominum potentia crescit, eo latius ipsorum responsabilitas, sive singulorum sive communitatum extenditur. Unde apparet christiano nuntio homines ab exstruendo mundo non averti, nec ad bonum sui similium negligendum impelli, sed potius officio haec operandi arctius obstringi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p427","On the ordering of human activity","36 Many of our contemporaries, however, seem to fear that from a closer connection of human activity and religion, the autonomy of men or societies or sciences may be impeded. If by the autonomy of earthly things we mean that created things and societies themselves enjoy their own laws and values, to be gradually discovered, used, and ordered by man, it is wholly right to demand it: which is demanded not only by the men of our age, but also is congruent with the will of the Creator. For from the very condition of creation, all things are furnished with their own stability, truth, goodness, and their own laws and order, which man ought to respect, acknowledging the proper methods of each science and art. Therefore methodical investigation in all disciplines, if it proceeds in a truly scientific way and according to the norms of morality, never truly opposes faith, because profane things and the things of faith take their origin from the same God. Indeed, he who strives to investigate the hidden things of things with humble and constant spirit, is led as if unconsciously by the hand of God, who sustains all things and makes them to be what they are. Hence we may deplore certain habits of mind, which have not been lacking among Christians themselves at times, on account of a not sufficiently grasped legitimate autonomy of science, and from contentions and controversies arising therefrom, have led many minds to think that faith and science are opposed to each other. But if by the autonomy of temporal things is meant that created things do not depend on God, and that man can so use them that he does not refer them to the Creator, no one who acknowledges God does not feel how false such opinions are. For the creature without the Creator vanishes. Moreover, all believers, of whatever religion, have always heard the voice and manifestation of Him in the discourse of creatures. Indeed, through forgetfulness of God, creation itself is obscured.","Humana vero navitas, sicut ex homine procedit, ita ad hominem ordinatur. Homo enim, cum operatur, non tantum res et societatem immutat, sed et seipsum perficit. Multa discit, facultates suas excolit, extra se et supra se procedit. Huiusmodi incrementum, si recte intelligatur, maioris pretii est quam externae quae colligi possunt divitiae. Magis valet homo propter id quod est quam propter id quod habet. Pariter, omnia quae homines, ad maiorem iustitiam, ampliorem fraternitatem, humanioremque ordinationem in socialibus necessitudinibus obtinendam agunt, plus quam progressus technici valent. Hi enim progressus quasi materiam humanae promotioni praebere possunt, illam autem per se solos nequaquam ad actum deducunt. Unde haec est humanae navitatis norma, quod iuxta consilium et voluntatem divinam cum genuino humani generis bono congruat, et homini individuo vel in societate posito integrae suae vocationis cultum et impletionem permittat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p428","On the just autonomy of earthly things","37 Sacred Scripture, however, to which the experience of ages consents, teaches the human family that human progress, which is a great good of man, carries with it a great temptation: for the order of values being disturbed and evil mixed with good, individuals and groups consider only their own, not others'. Whence it comes about that the world no longer exists as a space of true fraternity, while the increased power of humanity already threatens to destroy the human race itself. For a hard struggle against the powers of darkness pervades the whole history of men, which, having begun from the origin of the world, will persevere until the last day, the Lord saying. Inserted into this battle, man must continually struggle that he may adhere to good, nor can he attain unity in himself without great efforts, the grace of God helping. Wherefore the Church of Christ, trusting in the counsel of the Creator, while acknowledging that human progress can serve true human happiness, cannot but echo that of the Apostle: \"Be not conformed to this world\" (Rom 12:2), namely to that spirit of vanity and malice which transforms human activity, ordered to the service of God and man, into an instrument of sin. If therefore anyone asks by what way that misery can be overcome, Christians profess that all man's activities, which daily are endangered through pride and inordinate self-love, are to be purified and brought to perfection by the cross and resurrection of Christ. For man, redeemed by Christ and made a new creature in the Holy Spirit, can and ought to love the things created by God. For he receives them from God and respects and reveres them as flowing from the hand of God. Giving thanks to the Benefactor for them, and using and enjoying creatures in the poverty and freedom of spirit, he is introduced into the true possession of the world, \"as having nothing, yet possessing all things\" (2 Cor 6:10). \"For all things are yours; and you are Christ's; and Christ is God's\" (1 Cor 3:22-23).","Multi tamen coaevi nostri timere videntur, ne ex arctiore humanae navitatis et religionis coniunctione autonomia hominum vel societatum vel scientiarum impediatur. Si per terrenarum rerum autonomiam intelligimus res creatas et ipsas societates propriis legibus valoribusque gaudere, ab homine gradatim dignoscendis, adhibendis et ordinandis, eamdem exigere omnino fas est: quod non solum postulatur ab hominibus nostrae aetatis, sed etiam cum Creatoris voluntate congruit. Ex ipsa enim creationis condicione res universae propria firmitate, veritate, bonitate propriisque legibus ac ordine instruuntur, quae homo revereri debet, propriis singularum scientiarum artiumve methodis agnitis. Ideo inquisitio methodica in omnibus disciplinis, si modo vere scientifico et iuxta normas morales procedit, numquam fidei revera adversabitur, quia res profanae et res fidei ab eodem Deo originem ducunt. Immo, qui humili et constanti animo abscondita rerum perscrutari conatur, etsi inscius quasi manu Dei ducitur qui, res omnes sustinens, facit ut sint id quod sunt. Hinc deplorare liceat quosdam animi habitus, qui aliquando inter christianos ipsos, ob non satis perspectam legitimam scientiae autonomiam, non defuerunt et, contentionibus controversiisque exinde suscitatis, plurium animos eo perduxerunt ut fidem et scientiam inter se opponi censerent. At si verbis rerum temporalium autonomia intelligitur res creatas a Deo non pendere, eisque hominem sic uti posse ut easdem ad Creatorem non referat, nemo qui Deum agnoscit non sentit quam falsa huiusmodi placita sint. Creatura enim sine Creatore evanescit. Ceterum, omnes credentes, cuiuscumque sint religionis, vocem et manifestationem Eius in creaturarum loquela semper audierunt. Immo, per oblivionem Dei ipsa creatura obscuratur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p429","On human activity corrupted by sin","38 For the word of God, through whom all things were made, Himself made flesh and dwelling in the land of men, a perfect man, entered into the history of the world, assuming and recapitulating it in Himself. He reveals to us, since \"God is charity\" (1 Jn 4:8), and at the same time teaches us the fundamental law of human perfection, and consequently of the transformation of the world, to be the new commandment of love. He therefore makes certain to those who believe in divine charity that the way of love is open to all men and that the effort to restore universal fraternity is not in vain. At the same time, He warns that this charity is not to be sought only in great things, but especially in the ordinary circumstances of life. Suffering death for us sinners, by His example He teaches us that we too must bear the cross which flesh and world impose on the shoulders of those who seek peace and justice. By His resurrection, the Lord is constituted Christ, to whom all power is given in heaven and on earth, and already by the power of His Spirit He works in the hearts of men, not only arousing the desire for the future age, but by that very fact also animating, purifying, and strengthening those generous desires by which the human family strives to render its own life more humane and to subject the whole earth to this end. The gifts of the Spirit, however, are various: while He calls some to give a manifest testimony to the desire for a heavenly dwelling and to keep it vivid in the human family, He calls others to devote themselves to the earthly service of men, by this ministry preparing the matter for the kingdom of heaven. He frees all, however, that, denying their own love and taking up all earthly forces into human life, they may extend themselves to the future, when humanity itself will become an offering acceptable to God. Of which hope, as an earnest and viaticum of the way, the Lord left to His own in that sacrament of faith, in which the elements of nature, cultivated by men, are converted into the glorious Body and Blood, a supper of fraternal communion and a foretaste of the heavenly banquet.","Sacra vero Scriptura, cui saeculorum consentit experientia, humanam familiam edocet progressum humanum, qui magnum hominis bonum est, magnam tamen tentationem secumferre: ordine enim valorum turbato et malo cum bono permixto, singuli homines ac coetus solummodo quae propria sunt considerant, non vero aliorum. Quo fit ut mundus non iam spatium verae fraternitatis exsistat, dum aucta humanitatis potentia iam ipsum genus humanum destruere minatur. Universam enim hominum historiam ardua colluctatio contra potestates tenebrarum pervadit, quae inde ab origine mundi incepta, usque ad ultimum diem, dicente Domino, perseverabit. In hanc pugnam insertus, homo ut bono adhaereat iugiter certare debet, nec sine magnis laboribus, Dei gratia adiuvante, in seipso unitatem obtinere valet. Quapropter Ecclesia Christi, Creatoris consilio fidens, dum agnoscit progressum humanum verae hominum felicitati inservire posse, non potest tamen quin illud Apostoli resonare faciat: Nolite conformari huic saeculo (Rm 12,2), iili scilicet vanitatis et malitiae spiritui qui humanam navitatem, ad servitium Dei et hominis ordinatam, in instrumentum peccati transmutat. Si quis ergo quaerit, qua ratione miseria illa superari possit, christiani profitentur, omnes hominis navitates, quae per superbiam et inordinatum sui ipsius amorem cotidie in discrimine versantur, Christi cruce et resurrectione purificandas et ad perfectionem deducendas esse. A Christo enim redemptus et in Spiritu Sancto nova creatura effectus, homo ipsas res a Deo creatas amare potest et debet. A Deo enim illas accipit et quasi de manu Dei fluentes respicit et reveretur. Pro illis Benefactori gratias agens et in paupertate et libertate spiritus creaturis utens ac fruens, in veram mundi possessionem introducitur, tamquam nihil habens et omnia possidens. Omnia enim vestra sunt: vos autem Christi, Christus autem Dei (1Co 3,22-23)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-4/index.html","p430","On human activity brought to perfection in the paschal mystery","39 We do not know the time of the consummation of the earth and humanity, nor do we know the manner of the transformation of the universe. The figure of this world indeed passes away, deformed by sin, but we are taught that God prepares a new dwelling and a new earth in which justice dwells, and whose beatitude will fulfill and surpass all desires of peace which arise in the hearts of men. Then, death being conquered, the sons of God will be raised in Christ, and what was sown in weakness and corruption will put on incorruption; and, charity and its work remaining, all creation, which God created for man, will be freed from the servitude of vanity. We are indeed warned that it profits a man nothing if he gain the whole world but lose himself. Yet the expectation of a new earth ought not to weaken, but rather to excite, the care for cultivating this earth, where the body of that new human family grows, which can already offer some adumbration of the new age. Therefore, although earthly progress must be sedulously distinguished from the increase of the kingdom of Christ, insofar as it can however contribute to better ordering human society, it is of great interest to the kingdom of God. For the goods of human dignity, fraternal communion, and freedom—namely all these goods of nature and of our industry—after we have spread them on earth in the Spirit of the Lord and according to His mandate, we will later find them again, cleansed from all stain, illuminated and transfigured, when Christ hands over the eternal and universal kingdom to the Father: a kingdom of truth and life, a kingdom of holiness and grace, a kingdom of justice, love, and peace. On earth this kingdom is already present in mystery; at the coming of the Lord, however, it will be consummated.","Verbum enim Dei, per quod omnia facta sunt, Ipsum caro factum et in hominum terra habitans, perfectus homo in historiam mundi intravit, eam in Se assumens et recapitulans. Ipse nobis revelat, quoniam Deus caritas est (1Jn 4,8), simulque nos docet legem fundamentalem perfectionis humanae, ac proinde transformationis mundi, novam dilectionis esse mandatum. Eos igitur, qui divinae credunt caritati, certos facit, viam dilectionis omnibus hominibus aperiri et conamen fraternitatem universalem instaurandi non esse inane. Simul monet, hanc caritatem non in solis magnis rebus sectandam esse, sed et imprimis in ordinariis vitae adiunctis. Pro nobis omnibus peccatoribus mortem sustinens, suo exemplo nos docet crucem etiam baiulandam esse, quam caro et mundus pacem et iustitiam sectantium humeris imponunt. Sua resurrectione Dominus constitutus, Christus, cui omnis potestas in caelo et in terra data est, per virtutem Spiritus Sui in cordibus hominum iam operatur, non solum venturi saeculi desiderium suscitans, sed eo ipso illa etiam generosa vota animans, purificans et roborans, quibus familia humana suam ipsius vitam humaniorem reddere et totam terram huic fini subiicere satagit. Diversa autem sunt Spiritus dona: dum alios vocat ut caelestis habitationis desiderio manifestum testimonium reddant illudque in humana familia vividum conservent, alios vocat ut terreno hominum servitio se dedicent, hoc suo ministerio materiam regni caelestis parantes. Omnes tamen liberat ut, proprio amore abnegato omnibusque terrenis viribus in vitam humanam assumptis, ad futura se extendant, quando humanitas ipsa fiet oblatio accepta Deo. Cuius spei arrham et itineris viaticum Dominus suis reliquit in illo sacramento fidei, in quo naturae elementa, ab hominibus exculta, in Corpus et Sanguinem gloriosum convertuntur, coena communionis fraternae et caelestis convivii praelibatione."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p431","New earth and new heaven","40 All that we have said concerning the dignity of the human person, concerning the community of men, concerning the deep meaning of human activity, constitutes the foundation of the relation between the Church and the world and likewise the basis of their mutual dialogue. Therefore, in this chapter, all having been presupposed that has already been set forth by this Council concerning the mystery of the Church, the same Church now comes to be considered as she exists in this world, lives and acts with it. Proceeding from the love of the eternal Father, founded in time by Christ the Redeemer, gathered together in the Holy Spirit, the Church has a saving and eschatological end, which can be fully attained only in the future age. She herself, however, is already present here on earth, composed of men, namely of the members of the earthly city who are called to form, already in the history of the human race, the family of the sons of God, always to be increased until the coming of the Lord. Indeed, united on account of heavenly goods and enriched by them, this family was constituted and ordered by Christ in this world as a society, and furnished with suitable means of visible and social union. Thus the Church, at once a visible assembly and a spiritual community, goes forward together with the whole of humanity and experiences the same earthly lot with the world, and exists as a leaven and as a kind of soul of human society to be renewed in Christ and to be transformed into the family of God. This interpenetration of the earthly and heavenly city can only be perceived by faith, indeed it remains a mystery of human history, which is disturbed by sin until the full revelation of the clarity of the sons of God. The Church, indeed, pursuing her own saving end, not only communicates divine life with man, but also in a certain way sheds the repercussed light of that life over the whole world, especially by this that she heals and elevates the dignity of the human person, strengthens the structure of human society, and imbues daily human activity with a deeper meaning and significance. Thus the Church believes that through each of her members and her whole community she can contribute many things to making the human family and its history more humane. Moreover, the Catholic Church gladly esteems that to which other Christian churches or ecclesial communities have contributed and contribute by cooperative work to the same office. At the same time, she firmly persuades herself that she can be helped much and in various ways by the world, both by individual men and by human society, by their gifts and activity, in the preparation of the Gospel. The principles of promoting this mutual commerce and help, in those things which are in a certain way common to the Church and the world, are set forth.","Terrae ac humanitatis consummandae tempus ignoramus, nec universi transformandi modum novimus. Transit quidem figura huius mundi per peccatum deformata, sed docemur Deum novam habitationem novamque terram parare in qua iustitia habitat, et cuius beatitudo omnia pacis desideria, quae in cordibus hominum ascendunt, implebit ac superabit. Tunc, morte devicta, filii Dei in Christo resuscitabuntur, et id quod seminatum fuit in infirmitate ac corruptione, incorruptionem induet; et, manente caritate eiusque opere, a servitute vanitatis liberabitur tota creatura illa, quam Deus propter hominem creavit. Monemur sane nihil prodesse homini, si universum mundum lucretur, seipsum autem perdat. Exspectatio tamen novae terrae extenuare non debet, sed potius excitare, sollicitudinem hanc terram excolendi, ubi Corpus illud novae familiae humanae crescit quod aliqualem novi saeculi adumbrationem iam praebere valet. Ideo, licet progressus terrenus a Regni Christi augmento sedulo distinguendus sit, inquantum tamen ad societatem humanam melius ordinandam conferre potest, Regni Dei magnopere interest. Bona enim humanae dignitatis, communionis fraternae et libertatis, hos omnes scilicet bonos naturae ac industriae nostrae fructus, postquam in Spiritu Domini et iuxta Eius mandatum in terris propagaverimus, postea denuo inveniemus, mundata tamen ab omni sorde, illuminata ac transfigurata, cum Christus Patri reddet regnum aeternum et universale: regnum veritatis et vitae, regnum sanctitatis et gratiae, regnum iustitiae, amoris et pacis. His in terris Regnum iam in mysterio adest; adveniente autem Domino consummabitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p432","Chapter IV. on the Office of the Church in the Modern World","41 Modern man is on the way to more fully developing his personality and daily more discovering and affirming his rights. Since, however, the Church is entrusted with manifesting the mystery of God, who is the ultimate end of man, she at the same time opens to man the meaning of his own existence, namely the intimate truth concerning man. The Church truly knows that only God, whom she serves, responds to the deepest desires of the human heart, which is never fully satisfied by earthly nourishment. She knows, moreover, that man, incessantly incited by the Spirit of God, will never be wholly indifferent concerning the problem of religion, as not only the experience of past ages, but also the manifold testimony of our times proves. For man will always desire to know, at least confusedly, what is the meaning of his life, his activity, his death. The very presence of the Church recalls these problems to his mind. But only God, who created man to His own image and redeemed him from sin, gives the fullest answer to these questions, and that through revelation in His Son who was made man. Whoever follows Christ, the perfect Man, he too becomes more man. From this faith the Church can subtract the dignity of human nature from all changes of opinion, which, for example, either too much depress or immoderately extol the human body. By no human law can the personal dignity and freedom of man be so aptly placed in safety as by the Gospel entrusted to the Church. For this Gospel announces and proclaims the freedom of the sons of God, rejects all servitude ultimately flowing from sin, sacredly respects the dignity of conscience and its free decision, unceasingly admonishes to reduplicate all human talents to the service of God and the good of men, finally commends all to the charity of all. Which corresponds to the fundamental law of the Christian economy. For although the same God is the Savior who is the Creator, the same also is the Lord of human history and of the history of salvation, yet in this same divine order the just autonomy of the creature, and especially of man, far from being taken away, is rather restored to its dignity and firmly established in it.","Omnia quae a nobis dicta sunt de dignitate personae humanae, de hominum communitate, de profundo sensu navitatis humanae, fundamentum relationis Ecclesiam inter et mundum necnon basim eorum mutui dialogi constituunt. Ideo in hoc capite, omnibus praesuppositis ab hoc Concilio de mysterio Ecclesiae iam edictis, eadem Ecclesia nunc consideranda venit prout ipsa, in hoc mundo exsistit et cum eo vivit atque agit. Procedens ex amore Patris aeterni, in tempore fundata a Christo Redemptore, coadunata in Spiritu Sancto, Ecclesia finem salutarem et eschatologicum habet, qui nonnisi in futuro saeculo plene attingi potest. Ipsa autem iam hic in terris adest, ex hominibus collecta, terrestris nempe civitatis membris quae ad hoc vocantur ut iam in generis humani historia familiam filiorum Dei, usque ad adventum Domini semper augendam, efforment. Unita quidem propter bona caelestia iisque ditata, haec familia a Christo in hoc mundo ut societas constituta et ordinata est, atque aptis mediis unionis visibilis et socialis instructa. Ita Ecclesia, insimul coetus adspectabilis et communitas spiritualis, una cum tota humanitate incedit eamdemque cum mundo sortem terrenam experitur, ac tamquam fermentum et veluti anima societatis humanae in Christo renovandae et in familiam Dei transformandae exsistit. Haec quidem terrestris et caelestis civitatis compenetratio nonnisi fide percipi potest, immo mysterium manet historiae humanae, quae usque ad plenam revelationem claritatis filiorum Dei peccato perturbatur. Ecclesia quidem, proprium suum finem salutarem persequens, non solum vitam divinam cum homine communicat, sed etiam lumen eius repercussum quodammodo super universum mundum fundit, potissimum per hoc quod personae humanae dignitatem sanat et elevat, humanae societatis compaginem firmat, atque cotidianam hominum navitatem profundiori sensu et significatione imbuit. Ita Ecclesia per singula sua membra et totam suam communitatem multa se conferre posse credit ad hominum familiam eiusque historiam humaniorem reddendam. Libenter insuper Ecclesia Catholica ea magni aestimat quae ad idem munus adimplendum aliae Ecclesiae christianae vel communitates ecclesiasticae socia opera contulerunt ac conferunt. Simul sibi firmiter persuasum habet se multum varioque modo a mundo, sive a singulis hominibus sive ab humana societate, eorum dotibus ac navitate, in praeparatione Evangelii iuvari posse. Mutui huius commercii atque adiutorii, in illis quae Ecclesiae et mundo quodammodo sunt communia, rite promovendi, principia quaedam generalia exponuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p433","On the help which the Church strives to give to individual men","The Church, therefore, by virtue of the Gospel entrusted to her, proclaims the rights of man and acknowledges and greatly esteems the dynamism of our time, by which these rights are promoted everywhere. This movement, however, must be imbued with the spirit of the Gospel and guarded against every form of false autonomy. For we are subject to the temptation to judge that our personal rights are then only fully preserved when we are freed from every norm of the divine law. By this way, however, the dignity of the human person, far from being saved, rather perishes.","Homo hodiernus in via est ad personalitatem suam plenius evolvendam iuraque sua in dies magis detegenda et affirmanda. Cum autem Ecclesiae concreditum sit manifestare mysterium Dei, qui est ultimus finis hominis, ipsa homini simul aperit sensum propriae eius exsistentiae, intimam scilicet de homine veritatem. Vere novit Ecclesia solum Deum, cui ipsa inservit, profundissimis respondere desideriis humani cordis, quod nutrimentis terrestribus numquam plene satiatur. Novit praeterea hominem, incessanter a Spiritu Dei incitatum, numquam circa problema religionis prorsus indifferentem fore, sicut non solum experientia saeculorum anteactorum, sed multiplici etiam testimonio nostrorum temporum comprobatur. Semper enim homo scire desiderabit, saltem confuse, quae sit significatio suae vitae, suae navitatis ac suae mortis. Ipsa praesentia Ecclesiae haec problemata in eius mentem revocat. Solus autem Deus, qui hominem ad imaginem suam creavit atque a peccato redemit, his quaestionibus plenissimum responsum praebet, idque per revelationem in Filio suo qui homo factus est. Quicumque Christum sequitur, Hominem perfectum, et ipse magis homo fit. Ex hac fide Ecclesia dignitatem naturae humanae omnibus opinionum mutationibus subtrahere potest, quae, exempli gratia, corpus humanum aut nimis deprimunt aut immoderate extollunt. Nulla lege humana personalis dignitas atque libertas hominis tam apte in tuto collocari possunt quam Evangelio Christi Ecclesiae concredito. Hoc enim Evangelium libertatem filiorum Dei annuntiat et proclamat, omnem servitutem ex peccato ultimatim fluentem respuit, dignitatem conscientiae eiusque liberam decisionem sancte veretur, omnia talenta humana in Dei servitium hominumque bonum reduplicare indesinenter monet, omnes denique omnium commendans caritati. Quod legi fundamentali oeconomiae christianae correspondet. Etsi enim idem Deus sit Salvator qui et Creator, idem quoque Dominus et historiae humanae et historiae salutis, tamen in hoc ipso ordine divino iusta creaturae autonomia et praesertim hominis nedum auferatur, potius in suam dignitatem restituitur atque in ipsa firmatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p434","On the help which the Church strives to give to individual men","42 The union of the human family is greatly strengthened and completed by the unity of the family of the sons of God founded in Christ. The proper mission, indeed, which Christ entrusted to His Church, is not of political, economic, or social order: for the end which He set for it is of religious order. Yet from this very religious mission, light and forces flow which can serve to constitute and strengthen the community of men according to the divine law. Likewise, where it is necessary, according to circumstances of times and places, she can arouse, indeed ought, works destined for the service of all, especially the needy, such as works of mercy or others of this kind. The Church moreover acknowledges whatever good is found in the current social dynamism: especially the evolution toward unity, the process of sound socialization and civil and economic association. For the promotion of unity is consistent with the intimate mission of the Church, since she is in Christ as a sacrament, that is, a sign and instrument of intimate union with God and of the unity of the whole human race. Thus she shows to the world that true external social union flows from the union of minds and hearts, namely from that faith and charity by which her unity is indissolubly founded in the Holy Spirit. For the power which the Church can inject into modern human society consists in that faith and charity, brought into effect in life, not however in any external dominion exercised by mere human means. Since, moreover, by virtue of her mission and nature, she is bound to no particular form of human culture or political, economic, or social system, the Church can from her universality exist as a most close bond between the various human communities and nations, provided only that they trust her and truly acknowledge her true freedom to fulfill this mission. Wherefore the Church admonishes her children, but also all men, that in this family spirit of the sons of God, they overcome all dissensions between nations and races and give internal firmness to just human associations. Whatever, therefore, true, good, and just is found in the most diverse institutions which the human race has made for itself and unceasingly makes, the Council considers with great reverence. It declares, moreover, that the Church wishes to help and promote all such institutions, insofar as this depends on her and can be connected with her mission. She desires nothing more ardently than that, serving the good of all, she be able to develop freely under any regime which acknowledges the fundamental rights of the person and the family and the necessities of the common good.","Ecclesia ergo, vi Evangelii sibi concrediti, iura hominum proclamat et hodierni temporis dynamismum, quo haec iura undique promoventur, agnoscit et magni aestimat. Qui motus tamen spiritu Evangelii imbuendus et adversus omnem speciem falsae autonomiae tutandus est. Tentationi enim subiicimur, iudicandi nostra iura personalia tunc tantum plene servari, cum ab omni norma Legis divinae solvimur. Hac autem via, personae humanae dignitas, nedum salvetur, potius perit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p435","On the help which the Church strives to give to human society","43 The Council exhorts Christians, citizens of both cities, that they strive faithfully to fulfill their earthly duties, led by the spirit of the Gospel. They depart from truth who, knowing that we have here no abiding city but seek the one to come, think that they can therefore neglect their earthly duties, not attending that by faith itself they are more bound to fulfill them, according to the vocation by which each is called. But they err no less who, on the contrary, think that they can so immerse themselves in earthly affairs as if these are altogether alien from religious life, which they suppose to consist only in the acts of worship and certain moral duties. That discord between the faith which they profess and their daily life of many is to be counted among the more grave errors of our time. This scandal the Prophets already vehemently rebuked in the Old Testament, and much more in the New Testament Jesus Christ Himself threatened with grave penalties. Lest, therefore, professional and social activities be wrongly opposed to religious life on the one hand, let the Christian, neglecting his temporal duties, neglect his duties toward his neighbor, indeed toward God himself, and bring his eternal salvation into peril. Let Christians rather rejoice, following the example of Christ, who exercised a craft, to be able to exercise all their earthly activities, to gather human, domestic, professional, scientific, or technical efforts into one vital synthesis with religious goods, under the highest ordering of which all things are coordinated to the glory of God. Secular offices and activities properly, although not exclusively, pertain to laity. Therefore, when they act as citizens of the world, whether individually or associated, they will not only keep the laws proper to each discipline, but also strive to acquire true skill in those fields. They will willingly cooperate with men pursuing the same ends. Recognizing the exigencies of faith and endowed with its virtue, they will unhesitatingly, where necessary, devise and bring to effect new undertakings. It pertains to their conscience, already aptly formed, that the divine law be inscribed in the life of the earthly city. From priests, however, laity expect light and spiritual strength. Yet they should not think that their pastors are always so skilled that they can have a concrete solution ready for every emerging question, even a grave one, or that they are sent for this: they themselves rather, illuminated by Christian wisdom and observantly attending to the doctrine of the Magisterium, should assume their own parts. Often the Christian vision of things will itself incline them to a certain determinate solution in certain circumstances. Others, however, not less sincere, as often and indeed legitimately happens, will judge differently concerning the same matter. But if the solutions proposed here and there, even beyond the intention of the parties, are easily connected by many with the Gospel message, they must be mindful that it is lawful for no one in the aforesaid cases to claim the authority of the Church exclusively for his own opinion. But always in sincere dialogue they should strive to enlighten one another, keeping mutual charity and especially solicitous for the common good. Laity, however, who have to play active parts in the whole life of the Church, are held not only to imbue the world with the Christian spirit, but also are called to be witnesses of Christ in all things, in the midst of human society. Bishops, to whom the office of governing the Church of God is committed, with their priests should so preach the message of Christ that all the earthly activities of the faithful may be imbued with the light of the Gospel. Moreover, all pastors should be mindful that by their daily conversation and solicitude they show the face of the Church to the world, from which men judge the power and truth of the Christian message. By life and word, together with religious and their faithful, let them show that the Church by her very presence, with all the gifts she contains, is an inexhaustible source of those virtues which the modern world most needs. By assiduous studies let them so render themselves apt that they may be able to play their part in instituting dialogue with the world and with men of any opinion. Above all, let them have in their heart the words of this Council: \"Because the human race today is more and more coalescing into civil, economic, and social unity, the more it is necessary that priests, with united care and help under the guidance of the bishops and the Supreme Pontiff, remove every form of dispersion, that the whole human race may be led into the unity of the family of God.\" Although the Church, by the power of the Holy Spirit, has remained the faithful spouse of her Lord and never ceases to be the sign of salvation in the world, she yet is not unaware that among her members, both clergy and laity, through the course of many centuries, there have been those who have been unfaithful to the Spirit of God. Even in our age, the Church is not unaware how much the message proclaimed by her and the human weakness of those to whom the Gospel is entrusted differ from each other. Whatever history may judge of these defects, we ought to be conscious of them and strenuously fight against them, lest they bring detriment to the diffusion of the Gospel. Likewise the Church knows how much she must continually mature from the experience of ages in developing her relation with the world. Led by the Holy Spirit, Mother Church unceasingly exhorts her sons to purification and renewal, that the sign of Christ may shine more clearly upon the face of the Church.","Unio familiae humanae unitate familiae filiorum Dei in Christo fundata multum roboratur et completur. Missio quidem propria, quam Christus Ecclesiae suae concredidit, non est ordinis politici, oeconomici vel socialis: finis enim quem ei praefixit ordinis religiosi est. At sane ex hac ipsa missione religiosa munus, lux et vires fluunt quae communitati hominum secundum Legem divinam constituendae et firmandae inservire possunt. Item, ubi opus fuerit, secundum temporum et locorum circumstantias, et ipsa suscitare potest, immo et debet, opera in servitium omnium, praesertim vero egentium destinata, uti opera misericordiae vel alia huiusmodi. Ecclesia insuper agnoscit quidquid boni in dynamismo sociali hodierno invenitur: praesertim evolutionem versus unitatem, processum sanae socializationis et consociationis civilis et oeconomicae. Promotio enim unitatis cum intima Ecclesiae missione cohaeret, cum ipsa sit in Christo veluti sacramentum seu signum et instrumentum intimae cum Deo unionis totiusque generis humani unitatis. Ita ipsa mundo ostendit veram unionem socialem externam ex unione mentium et cordium fluere, ex illa scilicet fide et caritate, quibus in Spiritu Sancto eius unitas indissolubiliter condita est. Vis enim, quam Ecclesia hodiernae hominum societati iniicere valet, in illa fide et caritate, ad effectum vitae adductis, consistit, non autem in dominio aliquo externo mediis mere humanis exercendo. Cum insuper vi suae missionis et naturae ad nullam alligetur particularem culturae humanae formam aut systema politicum, oeconomicum vel sociale, Ecclesia ex hac sua universalitate ligamen arctissimum inter diversas hominum communitates et nationes exsistere potest, dummodo ipsae ei fidant eiusque veram libertatem ad hanc suam missionem adimplendam reapse agnoscant. Qua de causa Ecclesia filios suos, sed etiam omnes homines monet, ut in hoc familiali spiritu filiorum Dei, omnes dissensiones inter nationes et stirpes superent et iustis associationibus humanis internam firmitatem praebeant. Quaecumque igitur vera, bona, iustaque inveniuntur in diversissimis institutionibus, quae genus humanum sibi condidit incessanterque condit, eadem Concilium magna cum reverentia considerat. Declarat insuper Ecclesiam omnes tales institutiones adiuvare et promovere velle, quatenus hoc ab ea dependet et cum eius missione coniungi potest. Ipsa nihil ardentius desiderat quam ut omnium bono inserviens, se libere sub quovis regimine evolvere possit, quod iura fundamentalia personae ac familiae et boni communis necessitates agnoscat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p436","On the help which the Church strives to give to human activity through Christian","44 Just as it is in the interest of the world to acknowledge the Church as a social reality of history and its leaven, so the Church herself does not ignore how much she has received from the history and development of the human race. The experience of past ages, the progress of the sciences, the treasures hidden in the various forms of human culture, by which the very nature of man is more fully manifested and new ways to truth are opened, also profit the Church. For she, from the beginning of her history, has learned to express the message of Christ with the concepts and language of various peoples, and has striven to illustrate it with the wisdom of philosophers as well: to this end namely, that the Gospel might be accommodated to the capacity of all and the exigencies of the wise, as far as was fitting. This accommodated preaching of the revealed word ought to remain the law of all evangelization. For thus the faculty of expressing the message of Christ in its own way is aroused in every nation, and at the same time a living commerce between the Church and the various cultures of peoples is promoted. To increase such commerce, the Church especially in our times, in which things change very quickly and ways of thinking vary greatly, especially needs the help of those who, living in the world, know various institutions and disciplines and understand their intimate mind, whether of believers or of non-believers. It is the part of the whole People of God, especially of pastors and theologians, with the help of the Holy Spirit, to listen to, discern, and interpret the various languages of our time, and to judge them under the light of the divine word, that revealed Truth may always be more deeply perceived, better understood, and more aptly set forth. The Church, since she has a visible social structure, indeed a sign of her unity in Christ, can also be enriched and is enriched by the development of human social life, not as if something were lacking in the constitution given to her by Christ, but to understand it more deeply, to express it better, and to accommodate it more happily to our times. She gratefully perceives that she, in her community no less than in her individual sons, receives varied help from men of any grade or condition. For whoever promote the human community in the order of family, culture, economic and social life, and also national and international politics, according to the purpose of God bring no small help to the ecclesial community as well, insofar as it depends on external things. Indeed, the Church confesses that she has profited much and can profit from the very opposition of those who oppose or persecute her.","Concilium christianos, cives utriusque civitatis, adhortatur ut sua terrestria officia fideliter implere studeant, idque spiritu Evangelii ducti. A veritate discedunt qui, scientes nos non habere hic manentem civitatem sed futuram inquirere, putent se proinde officia sua terrestria negligere posse, non attendentes se per ipsam fidem ad eadem implenda magis teneri, secundum vocationem qua quisque vocatus est. At non minus errant qui, e contrario, opinentur se ita seipsos negotiis terrestribus immergere posse, quasi ista omnino aliena sint a vita religiosa, quippe quia ipsam in solius cultus actibus et officiis quibusdam moralibus implendis consistere arbitrentur. Discidium illud inter fidem quam profitentur et vitam quotidianam multorum, inter graviores nostri temporis errores recensendum est. Scandalum hoc iam in Vetere Testamento Prophetae vehementer redarguebant et multo magis in Novo Testamento ipse Iesus Christus gravibus poenis minabatur. Ne igitur perperam inter se opponantur activitates professionales et sociales ex una parte, vita religiosa ex altera. Christianus, officia sua temporalia negligens, officia sua erga proximum, immo et ipsum Deum negligit suamque aeternam salutem in discrimen adducit. Gaudeant potius christiani, exemplum Christi secuti, qui fabrilem artem exercuit, se omnes suas navitates terrestres exercere posse, conatus humanos, domesticos, professionales, scientificos vel technicos in unam synthesin vitalem cum bonis religiosis colligendo, sub quorum altissima ordinatione omnia in Dei gloriam coordinantur. Laicis proprie, etsi non exclusive, saecularia officia et navitates competunt. Cum igitur, sive singuli sive consociati, ut cives mundi agunt, non solum leges proprias uniuscuiusque disciplinae servabunt, sed veram peritiam in illis campis sibi comparare studebunt. Libenter cum hominibus eosdem fines prosequentibus cooperabuntur. Agnoscentes exigentias fidei eiusque virtute praediti, incunctanter, ubi oportet, nova incepta excogitent atque ad effectum deducant. Ad ipsorum conscientiam iam apte formatam spectat, ut lex divina in civitatis terrenae vita inscribatur. A sacerdotibus vero laici lucem ac vim spiritualem exspectent. Neque tamen ipsi censeant pastores suos semper adeo peritos esse ut, in omni quaestione exsurgente, etiam gravi, solutionem concretam in promptu habere queant, aut illos ad hoc missos esse: ipsi potius, sapientia christiana illustrati et ad doctrinam Magisterii observanter attendentes, partes suas proprias assumant. Pluries ipsa visio christiana rerum eos ad aliquam determinatam solutionem in quibusdam rerum adiunctis inclinabit. Alii tamen fideles, non minore sinceritate ducti, ut saepius et quidem legitime accidit, aliter de eadem re iudicabunt. Quodsi solutiones hinc inde propositae, etiam praeter partium intentionem, a multis facile connectantur cum nuntio evangelico, meminerint oportet nemini licere in praefatis casibus pro sua sententia auctoritatem Ecclesiae sibi exclusive vindicare. Semper autem colloquio sincero se invicem illuminare satagant, mutuam caritatem servantes et boni communis imprimis solliciti. Laici vero, qui in tota vita Ecclesiae actuosas partes gerendas habent, non solum mundum spiritu christiano imbuere tenentur, sed etiam ad hoc vocantur ut in omnibus, in media quidem humana consortione, Christi sint testes. Episcopi vero, quibus munus moderandi Ecclesiam Dei commissum est, cum presbyteris suis nuntium Christi ita praedicent, ut omnes fidelium terrestres activitates Evangelii luce perfundantur. Insuper pastores omnes memores sint se sua cotidiana conversatione et sollicitudine mundo faciem Ecclesiae exhibere, ex qua homines vim et veritatem nuntii christiani iudicant. Vita et verbo, una cum religiosis atque suis fidelibus, demonstrent Ecclesiam sola sua praesentia, cum omnibus quae continet donis, inexhaustum fontem esse illarum virtutum, quibus mundus hodiernus maxime indiget. Studiis assiduis se ita aptos reddant, ut in dialogo cum mundo et hominibus cuiuscumque opinionis instituendo partes suas agere possint. Imprimis vero in corde verba huius Concilii habeant: \"Quia genus humanum hodie magis magisque in unitatem civilem, oeconomicam et socialem coalescit, eo magis oportet ut Sacerdotes, coniuncta cura et ope sub ductu Episcoporum et Summi Pontificis, omnem rationem dispersionis elidant, ut in unitatem familiae Dei totum genus humanum adducatur.\" Quamvis Ecclesia ex virtute Spiritus Sancti fidelis sponsa Domini sui manserit et numquam cessaverit esse signum salutis in mundo, ipsa tamen minime ignorat inter membra sua, sive clericos sive laicos, decurrente multorum saeculorum serie, non defuisse qui Spiritui Dei infideles exstiterint. Etiam hac nostra aetate Ecclesiam non fugit, quantum inter se distent nuntius a se prolatus et humana debilitas eorum quibus Evangelium concreditur. Quidquid de istis defectibus historia iudicet, eorum conscii esse debemus eosdemque strenue impugnare, ne Evangelio diffundendo detrimentum afferant. Pariter novit Ecclesia quantopere ipsa, in sua cum mundo relatione excolenda, ex saeculorum experientia iugiter maturescere debeat. A Spiritu Sancto ducta, Ecclesia Mater indesinenter filios suos ad purificationem et renovationem exhortatur, ut signum Christi super faciem Ecclesiae clarius effulgeat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p437","On the help which the Church receives from the modern world","45 While the Church herself helps the world and receives many things from it, she tends to this one end, that the kingdom of God may come and the salvation of the whole human race be restored. For every good which the people of God can provide to the human family during the time of its earthly pilgrimage flows from this, that the Church is the universal sacrament of salvation, manifesting and at the same time operating the mystery of God's love for man. For the word of God, through whom all things were made, Himself made flesh, so that, perfect Man, He might save all and recapitulate all things. The Lord is the end of human history, the point upon which the desires of history and civilization turn, the center of the human race, the joy of all hearts and the fullness of their desires. He is whom the Father raised from the dead, exalted and placed at His right hand, constituting Him the judge of the living and the dead. In His Spirit, being vivified and gathered together, we pilgrimage toward the consummation of human history, which fully corresponds to the purpose of His love: \"To restore all things in Christ, which are in heaven and which are on earth\" (Eph 1:10). The Lord Himself says: \"Behold, I come quickly, and my reward is with me, to render to each according to his works. I am Alpha and Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End\" (Rev 22:12-13).","Sicut autem mundi interest Ecclesiam ut socialem realitatem historiae eiusque fermentum agnoscere, ita ipsa Ecclesia non ignorat, quantum ex humani generis historia et evolutione acceperit. Praeteritorum saeculorum experientia, scientiarum profectus, thesauri in variis culturae humanae formis absconditi, quibus ipsius hominis natura plenius manifestatur novaeque viae ad veritatem aperiuntur, Ecclesiae quoque prosunt. Ipsa enim, inde ab initio suae historiae, nuntium Christi, ope conceptuum et linguarum diversorum populorum exprimere didicit, eumdemque sapientia insuper philosophorum illustrare conata est: in hunc finem nempe ut Evangelium tum omnium captui tum sapientium exigentiis, in quantum par erat, aptaret. Quae quidem verbi revelati accommodata praedicatio lex omnis evangelizationis permanere debet. Ita enim in omni natione facultas nuntium Christi suo modo exprimendi excitatur simulque vivum commercium inter Ecclesiam et diversas populorum culturas promovetur. Ad tale commercium augendum Ecclesia, imprimis nostris temporibus, in quibus res celerrime mutantur et cogitandi modi valde variantur, peculiariter eorum auxilio indiget qui, viventes in mundo, varias institutiones et disciplinas callent earumque intimam mentem intelligunt, sive de credentibus sive de non credentibus agitur. Totius Populi Dei est, praesertim pastorum et theologorum, adiuvante Spiritu Sancto, varias loquelas nostri temporis auscultare, discernere et interpretari easque sub lumine verbi divini diiudicare, ut revelata Veritas semper penitius percipi, melius intelligi aptiusque proponi possit. Ecclesia, cum visibilem structuram socialem habeat, signum quidem suae unitatis in Christo, etiam evolutione vitae socialis humanae ditari potest et ditatur, non quasi aliquid in constitutione a Christo sibi data deesset, sed ad eamdem profundius cognoscendam, melius exprimendam atque temporibus nostris felicius accommodandam. Ipsa grato animo percipit se, in sua communitate non minus quam in singulis suis filiis, varium adiutorium ab hominibus cuiusvis gradus vel condicionis accipere. Quicumque enim communitatem humanam in ordine familiae, culturae, vitae oeconomicae et socialis, necnon politicae tam nationalis quam internationalis, promovent, secundum consilium Dei communitati quoque ecclesiali, in quantum haec ab externis dependet, adiutorium non parvum afferunt. Immo Ecclesia, ex ipsa oppositione eorum qui ei adversantur vel eam persequuntur, se multum profecisse et proficere posse fatetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p438","On Christ, Alpha and Omega","46 The Council, after having set forth of what dignity the human person is and to what office, both individual and social, in the whole world it is called to be fulfilled, under the light of the Gospel and human experience, now turns the minds of all to certain more urgent necessities of this time, which most affect the human race. Among many things which today excite the solicitude of all, it is especially pleasant to recall: matrimony and the family, human culture, economic-social life, political life, the bond of the family of peoples and peace. Concerning each of these, let the principles and lights flowing from Christ shine forth, by which Christ's faithful may be led and all men illuminated in seeking the solution of so many complex problems.","Ecclesia, dum ipsa mundum adiuvat et ab eo multa accipit, ad hoc unum tendit ut Regnum Dei adveniat et totius humani generis salus instauretur. Omne vero bonum, quod Populus Dei in suae peregrinationis terrestris tempore hominum familiae praebere potest, ex hoc profluit quod Ecclesia est universale salutis sacramentum, mysterium amoris Dei erga hominem manifestans simul et operans. Verbum enim Dei, per quod omnia facta sunt, Ipsum caro factum est, ita ut, perfectus Homo, omnes salvaret et universa recapitularet. Dominus finis est humanae historiae, punctum in quod historiae et civilizationis desideria vergunt, humani generis centrum, omnium cordium gaudium eorumque appetitionum plenitudo. Ille est quem Pater a mortuis suscitavit, exaltavit et a dextris suis collocavit, Eum vivorum atque mortuorum iudicem constituens. In Eius Spiritu vivificati et coadunati, versus historiae humanae peregrinamur consummationem, quae cum consilio Eius dilectionis plene congruit: Instaurare omnia in Christo, quae in caelis et quae in terra sunt (Ep 1,10). Dicit Ipse Dominus: Ecce venio cito, et merces mea mecum est, reddere unicuique secundum opera sua. Ego sum alpha et omega, primus et novissimus, principium et finis (Ap 22,12-13)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p439","Pars secunda","47 The salvation of the person and of human and Christian society is closely connected with the favorable condition of the conjugal and family community. Therefore Christians, together with all who hold the same community in high esteem, sincerely rejoice in the various aids by which men today progress in fostering this community of love and in cultivating life, and spouses and parents are helped in their excellent office; they also expect better benefits from it and strive to promote them.","Concilium, postquam exposuit cuiusnam dignitatis sit persona hominis necnon ad quodnam munus, sive individuale sive sociale, in universo mundo adimplendum sit vocata, sub luce Evangelii et humanae experientiae omnium nunc animos ad quasdam urgentiores huius temporis necessitates convertit, quae maxime genus humanum afficiunt. Inter multa quae hodie sollicitudinem omnium excitant haec praesertim recolere iuvat: matrimonium et familiam, culturam humanam, vitam oeconomicam-socialem ac politicam, coniunctionem familiae populorum et pacem. Circa haec singula clarescant principia et lumina a Christo manantia, quibus christifideles ducantur omnesque homines illuminentur in tot implicatorum problematum solutione quaerenda."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p440","Chapter I. on Fostering the Dignity of Matrimony and the Family","However, not everywhere does the dignity of this institution shine with the same clarity, since it is obscured by polygamy, the plague of divorce, so-called free love, and other deformations; moreover, conjugal love is often profaned by selfishness, hedonism, and illicit practices against generation. Moreover, modern economic, socio-psychological, and civil conditions bring not light disturbances into the family. Finally, in certain parts of the world, problems arising from demographic increase are observed not without solicitude. By all these, consciences are anguished. Yet the force and strength of the marital and family institution also appear from the fact that the profound changes of modern society, notwithstanding the difficulties arising therefrom, often in various ways manifest the true nature of the same institution. Wherefore the Council, by putting certain heads of the Church's doctrine into clearer light, intends to illuminate and strengthen Christians and all men, who strive to protect and promote the native dignity of the marital state and its excellent sacred value.","Salus personae et societatis humanae ac christianae arcte cum fausta condicione communitatis coniugalis et familiaris connectitur. Ideo christiani, una cum omnibus qui eandem communitatem magni aestimant, sincere gaudent de variis subsidiis quibus homines, in hac communitate amoris fovenda et in vita colenda, hodie progrediuntur, et coniuges atque parentes in praecellenti suo munere adiuvantur; meliora insuper exinde beneficia exspectant atque promovere student."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p441","Chapter I. on Fostering the Dignity of Matrimony and the Family","48 The intimate community of life and conjugal love, established by the Creator and furnished with its own laws, is established by the marriage covenant, that is, by the irrevocable personal consent. Thus, by the human act by which spouses mutually give and receive each other, an institution firm by divine order arises, also before society; this sacred bond does not depend on human arbitrariness for the good of spouses, offspring, and society. God himself is the author of matrimony, endowed with various goods and ends; all of which are of the greatest importance for the continuation of the human race, for the personal progress and eternal lot of each member of the family, for the dignity, stability, peace, and prosperity of the family itself and of the whole human society.","Non ubique vero huius institutionis dignitas eadem claritate illucescit, siquidem polygamia, divortii lue, amore sic dicto libero, aliisve deformationibus obscuratur; insuper amor nuptialis saepius egoismo, hedonismo et illicitis usibus contra generationem profanatur. Praeterea hodiernae condiciones oeconomicae, socio-psychologicae et civiles non leves in familiam perturbationes inducunt. In certis denique orbis partibus non absque sollicitudine problemata ex incremento demographico exorta observantur. Quibus omnibus conscientiae anguntur. Verumtamen matrimonialis familiarisque instituti vis et robur ex eo quoque apparent, quod profundae immutationes societatis hodiernae, non obstantibus difficultatibus inde prorumpentibus, saepe saepius veram eiusdem instituti indolem vario modo manifestant. Quapropter Concilium, quaedam doctrinae Ecclesiae capita in clariorem lucem ponendo, christianos hominesque universos illuminare et confortare intendit, qui nativam status matrimonialis dignitatem eiusque eximium valorem sacrum tueri et promovere conantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p442","On the holiness of matrimony and the family","By its own natural nature, the very institution of matrimony and conjugal love are ordered to the procreation and education of offspring and are crowned by them as by their summit. Thus man and woman, who by the marriage covenant are no longer two but one flesh (Mt 19:6), by the intimate conjunction of persons and works render mutual help and service to each other, and experience and more and more fully attain the sense of their unity. This intimate union, as the mutual gift of two persons, like the good of children, demands full fidelity of spouses and urges their indissoluble unity.","Intima communitas vitae et amoris coniugalis, a Creatore condita suisque legibus instructa, foedere coniugii seu irrevocabili consensu personali instauratur. Ita actu humano, quo coniuges sese mutuo tradunt atque accipiunt, institutum ordinatione divina firmum oritur, etiam coram societate; hoc vinculum sacrum intuitu boni, tum coniugum et prolis tum societatis, non ex humano arbitrio pendet. Ipse vero Deus est auctor matrimonii, variis bonis ac finibus praediti; quae omnia pro generis humani continuatione, pro singulorum familiae membrorum profectu personali ac sorte aeterna, pro dignitate, stabilitate, pace et prosperitate ipsius familiae totiusque humanae societatis maximi sunt momenti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p443","On the holiness of matrimony and the family","Christ the Lord abundantly blessed this manifold love, arising from the divine source of charity and established to the pattern of His union with the Church. For just as God once met His people in a covenant of love and fidelity, so now the Savior of men and Spouse of the Church, through the sacrament of matrimony, comes to meet Christian spouses. He remains with them, that, just as He loved the Church and delivered Himself up for her, so spouses, by mutual surrender, may love one another with perpetual fidelity. Genuine conjugal love is assumed into divine love and is ruled and enriched by the redemptive power of Christ and the saving action of the Church, that spouses may be effectively led to God and be helped and strengthened in the sublime office of father and mother. Wherefore Christian spouses, strengthened and as it were consecrated by a special sacrament for the duties and dignity of their state, fulfilling their conjugal and family office imbued with the spirit of Christ, by which their whole life is pervaded with faith, hope, and charity, more and more approach to their own perfection and mutual sanctification, and thus together to the glorification of God.","Indole autem sua naturali, ipsum institutum matrimonii amorque coniugalis ad procreationem et educationem prolis ordinantur iisque veluti suo fastigio coronantur. Vir itaque et mulier, qui foedere coniugali iam non sunt duo, sed una caro (Mt 19,6), intima personarum atque operum coniunctione mutuum sibi adiutorium et servitium praestant, sensumque suae unitatis experiuntur et plenius in dies adipiscuntur. Quae intima unio, utpote mutua duarum personarum donatio, sicut et bonum liberorum, plenam coniugum fidem exigunt atque indissolubilem eorum unitatem urgent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p444","On the holiness of matrimony and the family","Hence, the parents themselves leading the way by example and family prayer, children, indeed all dwelling in the family circle, will more easily find the way of humanity, salvation, and holiness. Spouses, however, adorned with the dignity and office of fatherhood and motherhood, will diligently fulfill the duty of education, especially religious, which pertains especially to them. Children, as living members of the family, contribute to the sanctification of parents in their own way. For they will respond to the benefits of parents with grateful affection, piety, and trust, and assist them in adversities and in the solitude of old age in the manner of children. Widowhood, strongly assumed in the continuity of the conjugal vocation, will be honored by all. The family will generously communicate its spiritual riches also with other families. Hence the Christian family, since it arises from matrimony, which is the image and participation of the covenant of love of Christ and the Church, will lay open to all the living presence of the Savior in the world and the genuine nature of the Church, both by the love of spouses, generous fecundity, unity, and fidelity, and by the loving cooperation of all members.","Christus Dominus huic multiformi dilectioni, e divino caritatis fonte exortae et ad exemplar suae cum Ecclesia unionis constitutae, abundanter benedixit. Sicut enim Deus olim foedere dilectionis et fidelitatis populo suo occurrit, ita nunc hominum Salvator Ecclesiaeque Sponsus, per sacramentum matrimonii christifidelibus coniugibus obviam venit. Manet porro cum eis, ut quemadmodum Ipse dilexit Ecclesiam et Semetipsum pro ea tradidit, ita et coniuges, mutua deditione, se invicem perpetua fidelitate diligant. Germanus amor coniugalis in divinum amorem assumitur atque virtute redemptiva Christi et salvifica actione Ecclesiae regitur ac ditatur, ut coniuges efficaciter ad Deum ducantur atque in sublimi munere patris et matris adiuventur et confortentur. Quapropter coniuges christiani ad sui status officia et dignitatem peculiari sacramento roborantur et veluti consecrantur; cuius virtute munus suum coniugale et familiare explentes, spiritu Christi imbuti, quo tota eorum vita, fide, spe et caritate pervaditur, magis ac magis ad propriam suam perfectionem mutuamque sanctificationem, ideoque communiter ad Dei glorificationem accedunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p445","On the holiness of matrimony and the family","49 The spouses and couples are often invited by the divine word to nourish their betrothal with chaste love and marriage with undivided love. Many men of our age also hold true love between husband and wife in high esteem, manifested in various ways according to the honest customs of peoples and times. This love, however, as eminently human, since it is directed by the will's affection from person to person, embraces the good of the whole person and therefore can enrich the expressions of body and spirit with special dignity and ennoble them as elements and special signs of conjugal friendship. The Lord deigned to heal, perfect, and elevate this love with a special gift of grace and charity. Such love, uniting human and divine, leads spouses to a free and mutual gift of self, proved by tender affection and work, and pervades their whole life; indeed, it is perfected and grows by its generous activity. It therefore far surpasses mere erotic inclination, which, egotistically cultivated, quickly and miserably vanishes. This love is singularly expressed and perfected by the proper work of matrimony. Hence, the acts by which spouses intimately and chastely unite with each other are honest and worthy, and, exercised in a truly human way, signify and foster mutual gift, by which they enrich each other with joyful and grateful spirit. That love, ratified by mutual fidelity, and especially sanctioned by the sacrament of Christ, is indissolubly faithful in body and mind through prosperity and adversity, and therefore remains alien from all adultery and divorce. The equality also of personal dignity of woman and man being acknowledged, the unity of matrimony confirmed by the Lord clearly appears in mutual and full love. For the duties of this Christian vocation to be constantly fulfilled, outstanding virtue is required: wherefore spouses, strengthened by grace for a holy life, will constantly cultivate the firmness of love, greatness of spirit, and the spirit of sacrifice, and obtain them by prayer. Genuine conjugal love will be more highly esteemed and sound public opinion concerning it will be formed, if Christian spouses excel by testimony of fidelity and harmony in the same love and by solicitude in educating children, and play their part in the necessary cultural, psychological, and social renewal in favor of matrimony and the family. Young people are to be suitably and timely instructed concerning the dignity, office, and work of conjugal love, especially in the bosom of the family itself, that, instructed in the cultivation of chastity, they may be able to pass from honest betrothal to marriage at a suitable age.","Unde, ipsis parentibus exemplo et oratione familiari praegredientibus, filii, immo et omnes in familiae convictu degentes, humanitatis, salutis atque sanctitatis viam facilius invenient. Coniuges autem, dignitate ac munere paternitatis et maternitatis ornati, officium educationis praesertim religiosae, quod ad ipsos imprimis spectat, diligenter adimplebunt. Liberi, ut viva familiae membra, ad sanctificationem parentum suo modo conferunt. Gratae enim mentis affectu, pietate atque fiducia beneficiis parentum respondebunt ipsisque in rebus adversis necnon in senectutis solitudine filiorum more assistent. Viduitas, in continuitate vocationis coniugalis forti animo assumpta, ab omnibus honorabitur. Familia suas divitias spirituales cum aliis quoque familiis generose communicabit. Proinde familia christiana, cum e matrimonio, quod est imago et participatio foederis dilectionis Christi et Ecclesiae, exoriatur, vivam Salvatoris in mundo praesentiam atque germanam Ecclesiae naturam omnibus patefaciet, tum coniugum amore, generosa fecunditate, unitate atque fidelitate, tum amabili omnium membrorum cooperatione."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p446","On conjugal love","50 Matrimony and conjugal love are by their nature ordered to the procreation and education of offspring. Children indeed are the most excellent gift of matrimony and greatly contribute to the good of the parents themselves. God himself, who said: \"It is not good for man to be alone\" (Gen 2:18) and who \"made man from the beginning male and female\" (Mt 19:4), wishing to communicate to man a special participation in His own creative work, blessed man and woman saying: \"Increase and multiply\" (Gen 1:28). Hence the true cultivation of conjugal love and the whole manner of family life arising therefrom, the other ends of matrimony not being set aside, tend to this, that spouses be disposed with strong spirit to cooperate with the love of the Creator and Savior, who through them daily enlarges and enriches His family.","Pluries verbo divino sponsi atque coniuges invitantur, ut casto amore sponsalia et indivisa dilectione coniugium nutriant atque foveant. Plures quoque nostrae aetatis homines verum amorem inter maritum et uxorem variis rationibus secundum honestos populorum et temporum mores manifestatum magni faciunt. Ille autem amor, utpote eminenter humanus, cum a persona in personam voluntatis affectu dirigatur, totius personae bonum complectitur ideoque corporis animique expressiones peculiari dignitate ditare easque tamquam elementa ac signa specialia coniugalis amicitiae nobilitare valet. Hunc amorem Dominus, speciali gratiae et caritatis dono, sanare, perficere et elevare dignatus est. Talis amor, humana simul et divina consocians, coniuges ad liberum et mutuum sui ipsius donum, tenero affectu et opere probatum, conducit totamque vitam eorum pervadit; immo ipse generosa sua operositate perficitur et crescit. Longe igitur exsuperat meram eroticam inclinationem, quae, egoistice exculta, citius et misere evanescit. Haec dilectio proprio matrimonii opere singulariter exprimitur et perficitur. Actus proinde, quibus coniuges intime et caste inter se uniuntur, honesti ac digni sunt et, modo vere humano exerciti, donationem mutuam significant et fovent, qua sese invicem laeto gratoque animo locupletant. Amor ille mutua fide ratus, et potissimum sacramento Christi sancitus, inter prospera et adversa corpore ac mente indissolubiliter fidelis est, et proinde ab omni adulterio et divortio alienus remanet. Aequali etiam dignitate personali cum mulieris tum viri agnoscenda in mutua atque plena dilectione, unitas matrimonii a Domino confirmata luculenter apparet. Ad officia autem huius vocationis christianae constanter exsequenda virtus insignis requiritur: quapropter coniuges, gratia ad vitam sanctam roborati, firmitatem amoris, magnitudinem animi et spiritum sacrificii assidue colent et oratione impetrabunt. Germanus autem amor coniugalis altius aestimabitur atque sana circa eum opinio publica efformabitur, si coniuges christiani testimonio fidelitatis et harmoniae in eodem amore necnon sollicitudine in filiis educandis, eminent atque in necessaria renovatione culturali, psychologica et sociali in favorem matrimonii et familiae partes suas agunt. Iuvenes de amoris coniugalis dignitate, munere et opere, potissimum in sinu ipsius familiae, apte et tempestive instruendi sunt, ut, castitatis cultu instituti, convenienti aetate ab honestis sponsalibus ad nuptias transire possint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p447","On the fecundity of matrimony","In the duty of transmitting and educating human life, which is to be considered as their proper mission, spouses know that they are cooperators of the love of God the Creator and as it were its interpreters. Therefore they will fulfill their office with human and Christian responsibility, and with docile reverence toward God, by common counsel and effort, will form for themselves a right judgment, attending both to their own good and the good of children, whether already born or to be foreseen, discerning the conditions of times and state of life both material and spiritual, and finally keeping regard for the good of the family community, temporal society, and the Church itself. This judgment they themselves ultimately, spouses before God, must make. In their manner of acting, however, Christian spouses should be conscious that they cannot proceed at their own arbitrariness, but must always be ruled by conscience itself conformed to the divine law, docile to the Magisterium of the Church, which authentically interprets that law under the light of the Gospel. That divine law shows the full meaning of conjugal love, protects it, and impels it to its truly human perfection. Thus Christian spouses, trusting in divine Providence and cultivating the spirit of sacrifice, glorify the Creator and tend to perfection in Christ, when they exercise the office of procreation with generous, human, and Christian responsibility. Among spouses who in such manner satisfy the office entrusted to them by God, especially to be remembered are those who, by prudent and common counsel, with great spirit undertake to receive a larger number of children to be suitably educated.","Matrimonium et amor coniugalis indole sua ad prolem procreandam et educandam ordinantur. Filii sane sunt praestantissimum matrimonii donum et ad ipsorum parentum bonum maxime conferunt. Ipse Deus qui dixit: non est bonum esse hominem solum (Gn 2,18) et qui hominem ab initio masculum et feminam... fecit (Mt 19,4), volens ei participationem specialem quamdam in Suiipsius opere creativo communicare, viro et mulieri benedixit dicens: crescite et multiplicamini (Gn 1,28). Unde verus amoris coniugalis cultus totaque vitae familiaris ratio inde oriens, non posthabitis ceteris matrimonii finibus, eo tendunt ut coniuges forti animo dispositi sint ad cooperandum cum amore Creatoris atque Salvatoris, qui per eos Suam familiam in dies dilatat et ditat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p448","On the fecundity of matrimony","Matrimony, however, is not instituted only for procreation; but the very nature of the covenant between persons, indissoluble, and the good of children demand that mutual love of spouses also be exhibited in right order, progress, and mature. Therefore, even if offspring, often so much desired, are lacking, matrimony remains as a whole-life community and communion, and retains its value and indissolubility.","In officio humanam vitam transmittendi atque educandi, quod tamquam propria eorum missio considerandum est, coniuges sciunt se cooperatores esse amoris Dei Creatoris eiusque veluti interpretes. Ideo humana et christiana responsabilitate suum munus adimplebunt ac docili erga Deum reverentia, communi consilio atque conatu, rectum iudicium sibi efformabunt, attendentes tum ad suum ipsorum bonum tum ad bonum liberorum, sive iam nati sint sive futuri praevideantur, dignoscentes temporum et status vitae condiciones tum materiales tum spirituales, ac denique rationem servantes boni communitatis familiaris, societatis temporalis ipsiusque Ecclesiae. Hoc iudicium ipsi ultimatim coniuges coram Deo ferre debent. In sua vero agendi ratione coniuges christiani conscii sint se non ad arbitrium suum procedere posse, sed semper regi debere conscientia ipsi legi divinae conformanda, dociles erga Ecclesiae Magisterium, quod illam sub luce Evangelii authentice interpretatur. Lex illa divina plenam amoris coniugalis significationem ostendit, illum protegit et ad eiusdem vere humanam perfectionem impellit. Ita coniuges christiani, divinae Providentiae confidentes et spiritum sacrificii excolentes, Creatorem glorificant atque ad perfectionem in Christo contendunt, cum procreandi munere generosa, humana atque christiana responsabilitate funguntur. Inter coniuges qui tali modo muneri sibi a Deo commisso satisfaciunt, peculiariter memorandi sunt illi qui, prudenti communique consilio, magno animo prolem congruenter educandam etiam numerosiorem suscipiunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p449","On the fecundity of matrimony","51 The Council knows that spouses, in harmoniously ordering conjugal life, are often hindered by certain modern conditions of life, and may be placed in circumstances in which the number of children cannot be increased, at least for a time, and the cultivation of faithful love and full community of life are not preserved without difficulty. But when intimate conjugal life is broken, the good of faith can often be called into peril and the good of children can be harmed: for then the education of children and the strong spirit to receive further children are endangered. There are those who presume to bring dishonest solutions to these problems, indeed do not shrink from killing; but the Church calls to mind that there can be no true contradiction between the divine laws of transmitting life and fostering genuine conjugal love. God, indeed, the Lord of life, entrusted to men the excellent ministry of preserving life, to be fulfilled in a manner worthy of man. Life, therefore, from conception is to be guarded with the greatest care; abortion and infanticide are nefarious crimes. The sexual nature of man and the human faculty of generation wonderfully surpass those which are had in lower grades of life; therefore the acts proper to conjugal life, ordered according to genuine human dignity, are to be revered with great respect. The moral nature, therefore, of the manner of acting, when it concerns reconciling conjugal love with responsible transmission of life, does not depend solely on sincere intention and estimation of motives, but must be determined by objective criteria, taken from the nature of the person and of his acts, which observe the full sense of mutual gift and human procreation in the context of true love; which cannot be done unless the virtue of conjugal chastity is cultivated with sincere spirit. The sons of the Church, based on these principles, may not enter into ways in regulating procreation which the Magisterium, in explaining the divine law, disapproves. Let all, however, know that the life of men and the office of transmitting it are not restricted to this age only nor can be measured and understood only by it, but always look to the eternal destiny of men.","Matrimonium vero, non est tantum ad procreationem institutum; sed ipsa indoles foederis inter personas indissolubilis atque bonum prolis exigunt, ut mutuus etiam coniugum amor recto ordine exhibeatur, proficiat et maturescat. Ideo etsi proles, saepius tam optata, deficit, matrimonium ut totius vitae consuetudo et communio perseverat, suumque valorem atque indissolubilitatem servat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p450","On reconciling conjugal love with respect for human life","52 The family is a school of richer humanity. But that it may be able to attain the fullness of its life and mission, kindly communication of spirit and common counsel of spouses, and sedulous cooperation of parents in the education of children, are required. The active presence of the father greatly profits their formation, but the domestic care of the mother, which the younger children especially need, is to be placed in safety, without the legitimate social promotion of woman being set aside. Children are to be instructed by education that, having grown to adult age, with full sense of responsibility they may be able to follow their vocation, even sacred, and choose a state of life, in which, if they join in matrimony, they may be able to found their own family, in moral, social, and economic conditions favorable to it. It is the part of parents or guardians to be guides to the young, in founding a family, by prudent counsel, willingly heard by them, taking care not to force them by direct or indirect coercion to enter marriage or to choose a partner. Thus the family, in which diverse generations meet and help one another to acquire fuller wisdom and to compose the rights of persons with other exigencies of social life, constitutes the foundation of society. Therefore all who exercise influence on communities and social groups ought effectively to contribute to the promotion of matrimony and the family. Civil power should acknowledge, protect, and promote their true nature, protect public morality, and favor domestic prosperity, as its sacred office. The right of parents to beget and educate children in the bosom of the family is to be protected. By provident legislation and various undertakings, even those who unhappily lack the good of the family are to be protected and helped with suitable aid. Christ's faithful, redeeming the present time and distinguishing eternal from changeable forms, diligently promote the goods of matrimony and the family, both by the testimony of their own life and by concordant action with men of good will, and thus, obstacles intercepted, will provide for the necessities and conveniences of the family, which agree with new times. To obtain this end, the Christian sense of the faithful, right moral conscience of men, and the wisdom and skill of those who are versed in sacred disciplines, will be of great help. Those who are skilled in sciences, especially biological, medical, social, and psychological, can contribute much to the good of matrimony and the family, and to the peace of consciences, if by joined studies they strive to elucidate more deeply the various conditions favoring the honest ordering of human procreation. It is the part of priests, with due knowledge concerning the family, to foster the vocation of spouses by various pastoral means, by preaching the word of God, by liturgical worship, and by other spiritual aids, in their conjugal and family life, and to strengthen them humanely and patiently in difficulties and to comfort them in charity, that truly radiant families may be formed. Various works, especially family associations, will strive to strengthen young people and spouses, especially those recently married, by doctrine and action, and to form them for family, social, and apostolic life. Finally, the spouses themselves, made to the image of the living God and constituted in the true order of persons, are to be united with equal affection, similar mind, and mutual holiness, that, following Christ the principle of life, in the joys and sacrifices of their vocation, through their faithful love, they may become witnesses of that mystery of love which the Lord revealed to the world by His death and resurrection.","Concilium novit coniuges, in vita coniugali harmonice ordinanda, saepe quibusdam hodiernis vitae condicionibus praepediri atque in circumstantiis versari posse in quibus numerus prolis, saltem ad tempus, augeri nequit, et fidelis amoris cultus atque plena vitae consuetudo non sine difficultate conservantur. Ubi autem intima vita coniugalis abrumpitur, bonum fidei non raro in discrimen vocari et bonum prolis pessumdari possunt: tunc enim educatio liberorum necnon fortis animus ad prolem ulteriorem suscipiendam periclitantur. Sunt qui his problematibus solutiones inhonestas afferre praesumunt, immo ab occisione non abhorrent; at Ecclesia in memoriam revocat veram contradictionem inter divinas leges vitae transmittendae et germani amoris coniugalis fovendi adesse non posse. Deus enim, Dominus vitae, praecellens servandi vitam ministerium hominibus commisit, modo homine digno adimplendum. Vita igitur inde a conceptione, maxima cura tuenda est; abortus necnon infanticidium nefanda sunt crimina. Indoles vero sexualis hominis necnon humana generandi facultas mirabiliter exsuperant ea quae in inferioribus vitae gradibus habentur; proinde ipsi actus vitae coniugali proprii, secundum germanam dignitatem humanam ordinati, magna observantia reverendi sunt. Moralis igitur indoles rationis agendi, ubi de componendo amore coniugali cum responsabili vitae transmissione agitur, non a sola sincera intentione et aestimatione motivorum pendet, sed obiectivis criteriis, ex personae eiusdemque actuum natura desumptis, determinari debet, quae integrum sensum mutuae donationis ac humanae procreationis in contextu veri amoris observant; quod fieri nequit nisi virtus castitatis coniugalis sincero animo colatur. Filiis Ecclesiae, his principiis innixis, in procreatione regulanda, vias inire non licet, quae a Magisterio, in lege divina explicanda, improbantur. Omnibus vero compertum sit vitam hominum et munus eam transmittendi non ad hoc saeculum tantum restringi neque eo tantum commensurari et intelligi posse, sed ad aeternam hominum destinationem semper respicere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p451","On the promotion of matrimony and the family to be cared for by all","53 It pertains to the person of man that he only approach true and full humanity through culture, that is, by cultivating the goods and values of nature. Therefore wherever human life is treated, nature and culture are most intimately connected. By the word culture in a general sense are indicated all those things by which man develops and perfects the manifold gifts of mind and body; strives to subject the orb of the earth to his power by knowledge and labor; renders social life, both in the family and in the whole civil association, more humane by the progress of morals and institutions; finally expresses, communicates, and conserves in his works the great spiritual experiences and aspirations throughout the course of time, that they may serve for the progress of many, indeed of the whole human race. Hence it follows that human culture necessarily carries with it a historical and social aspect, and the word culture often assumes a sociological and ethnological sense. In this sense, the plurality of cultures is spoken of. For from the diverse manner of using things, of performing labor and expressing oneself, of cultivating religion and forming morals, of establishing laws and juridical institutions, of advancing sciences and arts and cultivating beauty, diverse common conditions of living and diverse forms of composing the goods of life arise. Thus from handed-down institutions a patrimony proper to each human community is brought about. Thus also a defined and historical environment is constituted, into which man of each people or age is inserted, and from which he draws goods for promoting human and civil culture.","Familia schola quaedam uberioris humanitatis est. Ut autem vitae ac missionis suae plenitudinem attingere valeat, benevola animi communicatio communeque coniugum consilium necnon sedula parentum cooperatio in filiorum educatione requiruntur. Praesentia actuosa patris eorumdem efformationi magnopere prodest, sed et cura domestica matris, qua liberi praesertim iuniores indigent, in tuto ponenda est, quin legitima mulieris promotio socialis posthabeatur. Liberi ita educatione instruantur ut ad aetatem adultam provecti pleno responsabilitatis sensu vocationem etiam sacram sequi ac vitae statum eligere queant, in quo, si nuptiis iungantur, familiam propriam, in condicionibus moralibus, socialibus et oeconomicis eidem propitiis, condere possint. Parentum vel tutorum est se iunioribus, in fundanda familia, prudenti consilio, ab eis libenter audiendo, duces praebere, caventes tamen ne eos coactione directa vel indirecta ad matrimonium ineundum aut ad electionem compartis adigant. Ita familia, in qua diversae generationes conveniunt ac sese mutuo adiuvant ad pleniorem sapientiam acquirendam atque iura personarum cum aliis vitae socialis exigentiis componenda, fundamentum societatis constituit. Ideoque omnes qui influxum in communitates et coetus sociales exercent, ad promotionem matrimonii et familiae efficaciter conferre debent. Potestas civilis veram eorumdem indolem agnoscere, protegere et provehere, moralitatem publicam tueri atque prosperitati domesticae favere, ut sacrum suum munus consideret. Ius parentum prolem procreandi et in sinu familiae educandi tutandum est. Provida legislatione variisque inceptis etiam illi protegantur aptoque adiumento subleventur qui bono familiae infeliciter carent. Christifideles, praesens tempus redimentes atque aeterna a mutabilibus formis discernentes, bona matrimonii et familiae, tum propriae vitae testimonio tum concordi actione cum hominibus bonae voluntatis, diligenter promoveant, et sic, interceptis difficultatibus, providebunt familiae necessitatibus et commodis, quae novis temporibus conveniunt. Ad quem finem obtinendum sensus christianus fidelium, recta hominum conscientia moralis necnon sapientia ac peritia eorum qui in sacris disciplinis versati sunt, magno auxilio erunt. Qui scientiis, praecipue biologicis, medicis, socialibus et psychologicis eruditi sunt, multum bono matrimonii et familiae, pacique conscientiarum inservire possunt, si collatis studiis diversas condiciones honestae ordinationi procreationis humanae faventes, penitius elucidare conentur. Sacerdotum est, debita de re familiari eruditione accepta, vocationem coniugum diversis mediis pastoralibus, verbi Dei praedicatione, cultu liturgico aliisve adiumentis spiritualibus in vita eorum coniugali et familiari fovere, eosque humaniter et patienter in difficultatibus roborare atque in caritate confortare ut vere radiosae familiae efformentur. Varia opera, praesertim familiarum consociationes, iuvenes ipsosque coniuges, praecipue nuper iunctos, doctrina et actione confirmare eosque ad vitam familiarem, socialem et apostolicam formare satagent. Ipsi denique coniuges, ad imaginem Dei vivi facti et in vero ordine personarum constituti, affectu compari, mente consimili et mutua sanctitate adunati sint, ut Christum, vitae principium secuti, in gaudiis et sacrificiis vocationis suae, per suum fidelum amorem, illius testes fiant mysterii dilectionis, quod Dominus morte et resurrectione sua mundo revelavit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p452","Chapter II. on the Right Promotion of the Progress of Culture","54 The conditions of life of modern man under the social and cultural respect have profoundly changed, so that it is permitted to speak of a new age of human history. Hence new ways open for perfecting and more widely spreading culture. These have been prepared by the immense increase of natural and human sciences, also social, by the increase of technical arts, and by the progress in cultivating and rightly disposing the instruments by which men communicate with one another. Hence modern culture is marked by particular notes: the sciences which are called exact greatly develop critical judgment; recent studies of psychology more profoundly explain human activity; historical disciplines greatly contribute to the viewing of things under the aspect of their mutability and evolution; customs and manners become more and more uniform; industrialization, urbanization, and other causes which promote community life, create new forms of culture (mass-culture), from which new ways of feeling, acting, and using leisure are born; increased commerce at the same time among various peoples and social groups more widely opens the treasures of the various forms of culture to all and each, and thus a more universal form of human culture is gradually prepared, which, the more it promotes and expresses the unity of the human race, the more it observes the particularities of different cultures.","Ad ipsam personam hominis pertinet ut nonnisi per culturam, hoc est bona naturae valoresque colendo, ad veram plenamque humanitatem accedat. Ubicumque ergo de vita humana agitur, natura et cultura quam intime connectuntur. Voce cultura sensu generali indicantur omnia quibus homo multifarias dotes animi corporisque perpolit atque explicat; ipsum orbem terrarum cognitione et labore in suam potestatem redigere studet; vitam socialem, tam in familia quam in tota consortione civili, progressu morum institutorumque humaniorem reddit; denique magnas experientias spirituales atque appetitiones decursu temporum in operibus suis exprimit, communicat atque conservat, ut ad profectum multorum, quinimmo totius generis humani, inserviant. Inde sequitur culturam humanam aspectum historicum atque socialem necessario prae se ferre, atque vocem cultura saepe sensum sociologicum necnon ethnologicum assumere. Hoc autem sensu de culturarum pluralitate sermo fit. Ex diverso enim modo utendi rebus, laborem praestandi et sese exprimendi, religionem colendi moresque formandi, statuendi leges et iuridica instituta, augendi scientias et artes atque colendi pulchrum, diversae oriuntur communes vivendi condiciones et diversae formae bona vitae componendi. Ita ex traditis institutis efficitur patrimonium cuique humanae communitati proprium. Ita etiam constituitur ambitus definitus et historicus, in quem homo cuiusque gentis vel aetatis inseritur, et ex quo bona ad humanum civilemque cultum promovendum haurit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p453","Sectio I: ON THE CONDITIONS OF CULTURE IN THE MODERN WORLD","55 The number of men and women of every group or nation who are conscious of being themselves the artificers and authors of their community's culture increases day by day. In the whole world, the sense of autonomy and at the same time of responsibility grows more and more, which is of the greatest importance for the spiritual and moral maturity of the human race. This appears more clearly if we place the unification of the world before our eyes and the office imposed on us, that we build a better world in truth and justice. Thus we are witnesses that a new humanism is being born, in which man is defined especially by his responsibility toward his brothers and toward history.","Condiciones vitae hominis moderni sub respectu sociali et culturali profunde immutatae sunt, ita ut de nova historiae humanae aetate loqui liceat. Exinde ad culturam perficiendam ampliusque spargendam novae patent viae. Quas paraverunt ingens augmentum scientiarum naturalium et humanarum, etiam socialium, incrementum technicarum artium, necnon progressus in excolendis et recte disponendis instrumentis quibus homines inter se communicant. Hinc cultura hodierna particularibus signatur notis: scientiae, quae exactae nuncupantur, iudicium criticum maxime excolunt; recentiora psychologiae studia humanam activitatem profundius explicant; disciplinae historicae valde conferunt ut res sub specie suae mutabilitatis atque evolutionis adspiciantur; vitae consuetudines et mores in dies magis uniformes efficiuntur; industrializatio, urbanizatio aliaeque causae quae vitam communitariam promovent, novas culturae formas creant (mass-culture), ex quibus novi modi sentiendi, agendi otioque utendi nascuntur; aucta simul inter varias gentes societatisque coetus commercia thesauros diversarum culturae formarum omnibus et singulis latius aperiunt, et sic paulatim universalior paratur culturae humanae forma, quae eo magis humani generis unitatem promovet ac exprimit, quo melius diversarum culturarum particularitates observat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p454","Man, author of culture","56 In these conditions, it is not to be wondered that man, who feels his responsibility for the progress of culture, nourishes higher hope, but also looks with anxious mind at the multiple existing antinomies, which he himself must resolve: What must be done, that the more frequent commerce of cultures, which ought to lead to true and fruitful dialogue between various groups and nations, not disturb the life of communities, nor overturn the wisdom of the fathers, nor call into peril the proper character of peoples? How is the dynamism and expansion of new culture to be favored, without the living fidelity toward the heritage of traditions perishing? Which presses in a particular way when culture, which arises from the immense progress of sciences and technical arts, must be composed with that cultivation of mind which is nourished by studies according to various classical traditions. How can so swift and progressive dispersion of particular disciplines be conformed with the necessity of forming their synthesis, and of preserving in men the faculty of contemplation and admiration, which leads to wisdom? What is to be done, that all men may become participants of cultural goods in the world, while at the same time human culture of the more skilled always becomes more sublime and complex? How, finally, as the autonomy which culture claims for itself is to be acknowledged as legitimate, is one to avoid falling into a merely terrestrial humanism, indeed even opposed to religion itself? In the midst of those antinomies, human culture must therefore be so developed today that it may cultivate the whole human person in right order and help men in the offices, to which all, especially Christ's faithful, in one human family fraternally united, are called.","Maior in dies fit numerus virorum ac mulierum cuiusvis coetus vel nationis, qui conscii sunt suae communitatis culturae artifices se esse atque auctores. In universo mundo magis magisque crescit autonomiae simulque responsabilitatis sensus, quod pro spirituali ac morali maturitate generis humani maximi est momenti. Illud clarius apparet, si ante oculos unificationem mundi ponimus atque munus nobis impositum, ut in veritate et iustitia meliorem aedificemus mundum. Tali ergo modo testes sumus novum humanismum nasci, in quo homo imprimis sua responsabilitate erga suos fratres historiamque definitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p455","Difficulties and duties","57 Christ's faithful, pilgrimaging to the heavenly city, ought to seek and savor the things that are above; by which, however, far from being diminished, the importance of their office together with all men to build a more humane world increases. And indeed the mystery of the Christian faith provides excellent incentives and aids to more earnestly fulfill that office, and especially to discover the full meaning of such work, by which human culture obtains its excellent place in the whole vocation of man. For when man cultivates the earth by the work of his hands or by means of technical arts, that it may bear fruit and become a worthy dwelling for the whole human family, and when he consciously assumes his part in the life of social groups, he executes the plan of God, laid open from the beginning of times, of subduing the earth and perfecting creation, and at the same time cultivates himself; likewise he keeps the great command of Christ, spending himself in the service of brothers. Moreover, when man applies himself to the various disciplines of philosophy, history, and mathematical and natural science, and practices the arts, he can greatly contribute that the human family be raised to higher reasons of truth, goodness, and beauty and to the judgment of universal value, and thus be more clearly illuminated by the wonderful Wisdom, which from eternity was with God, setting all things with Him, playing in the world, delighting to be with the sons of men. By that very fact, the mind of man, more freed from the servitude of things, can be more readily raised to the very worship and contemplation of the Creator. Indeed, under the impulse of grace, it is disposed to acknowledge the Word of God, who, before He was made flesh to save all things and recapitulate them in Himself, was already in the world, as the true light which enlightens every man (Jn 1:9). Indeed, the modern progress of sciences and technical arts, which by the force of their method cannot penetrate to the intimate reasons of things, can favor a certain phenomenalism and agnosticism, when the method of investigation, which those disciplines use, is undeservedly held as the supreme rule of all truth to be found. Indeed, the peril is that man, too trusting in modern discoveries, may think he suffices for himself and no longer seek higher things. However, these unhappy things do not necessarily follow from modern culture, nor should they lead us into temptation that we do not acknowledge its positive values. Among which are numbered: the study of sciences and exact fidelity toward truth in scientific investigations, the necessity of working together with others in technical groups, the sense of international solidarity, the ever more vivid consciousness of the responsibility of experts toward helping and indeed protecting men, the will to make the conditions of life more favorable to all, especially to those who labor under privation of responsibility or poverty of culture. All these can provide a certain preparation for receiving the message of the Gospel, which can be informed by divine charity from Him who came to save the world.","Quibus in condicionibus, non est mirandum, hominem, qui responsabilitatem suam sentit pro culturae progressu, altiorem spem nutrire, sed etiam anxio animo adspicere multiplices antinomias exsistentes, quas ipse resolvere debet: Quid faciendum est, ne frequentiora culturarum commercia, quae inter diversos coetus et nationes ad verum et fructuosum dialogum adducere deberent, vitam communitatum perturbent, neve sapientiam maiorum evertant, neve propriam populorum indolem in discrimen adducant? Quomodo dynamismo atque expansioni novae culturae est favendum, quin fidelitas viva erga traditionum haereditatem pereat? Quod particulari modo urget ubi cultura, quae ex ingenti scientiarum artiumque technicarum progressu oritur, componenda est cum eo ingenii cultu qui studiis secundum varias traditiones classicis alitur. Quomodo tam velox atque progrediens disciplinarum particularium dispersio conformari potest cum necessitate formandi earum synthesim, necnon servandi apud homines facultates contemplationis ac admirationis, quae ad sapientiam adducunt? Quid faciendum est, ut universi homines bonorum culturalium participes fiant in mundo, cum insimul cultus humanus peritiorum semper sublimior atque complexior evadat? Quomodo denique, prout legitima agnoscenda est autonomia, quam cultura sibi vindicat, quin ad humanismum mere terrestrem, immo ipsi religioni adversantem deveniatur? In medio quidem illarum antinomiarum cultura humana ita hodie evolvatur oportet, ut integram personam humanam aequo ordine excolat atque homines iuvet in muneribus, ad quae adimplenda omnes, praecipue autem christifideles, in una familia humana fraterne uniti, vocantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p456","Sectio II: ON CERTAIN PRINCIPLES FOR THE RIGHT PROMOTION OF CULTURE","58 Many connections are found between the message of salvation and human culture. For God, revealing Himself to His people up to the full manifestation of Himself in the incarnate Son, spoke according to the culture proper to various ages. Likewise the Church, living in various conditions through the course of times, has used the discoveries of diverse cultures to spread and explain the message of Christ to all nations, to investigate and more deeply understand it, to better express it in liturgical celebration and in the life of the manifold community of the faithful. But at the same time, sent to all peoples of any age and region, the Church is not exclusively and indissolubly bound to any race or nation, any particular manner of morals, any ancient or new custom. Adhering to her own tradition and at the same time conscious of her universal mission, she can enter into communion with the various forms of culture, by which both the Church herself and the various cultures are enriched. The good news of Christ continually renews the life and culture of fallen man, and impugns and removes errors and evils flowing from the ever threatening seduction of sin. It unceasingly purifies and elevates the morals of peoples. It fertilizes from within the ornaments and gifts of the mind of any people or age with heavenly riches, strengthens, completes, and restores them in Christ. Thus the Church, while fulfilling her own office, by that very fact already impels and contributes to human and civil culture, and by her action, even liturgical, educates man to interior freedom.","Christifideles, ad civitatem caelestem peregrinantes, ea quae sursum sunt quaerere et sapere debent; quo tamen nedum minuatur, potius crescit momentum muneris eorum una cum omnibus hominibus adlaborandi ad aedificationem mundi humanius exstruendi. Et revera fidei christianae mysterium praestantia incitamenta et adiumenta eis praebet ad munus illud impensius adimplendum et praesertim ad plenum huiusmodi operis sensum detegendum, quo cultus humanus in integra hominis vocatione suum eximium obtineat locum. Cum enim homo opere manuum suarum vel ope technicarum artium terram excolit, ut fructum afferat et dignum universae familiae humanae habitaculum fiat, et cum conscie partes assumit in socialium coetuum vita, ipse exsequitur consilium Dei, initio temporum patefactum, terrae subiiciendae creationisque perficiendae, atque seipsum excolit; insimul magnum Christi mandatum servat, sese in servitium fratrum impendendi. Insuper cum homo in varias philosophiae, historiae atque scientiae mathematicae et naturalis disciplinas incumbit et artibus versatur, maxime conferre potest, ut familia humana ad sublimiores veri, boni et pulchri rationes atque ad iudicium universi valoris elevetur et sic clarius illuminetur mirabili Sapientia, quae ab aeterno cum Deo erat, cuncta cum Eo componens, ludens in orbe terrarum, esse cum filiis hominum in deliciis habens. Eo ipso animus hominis, a rerum servitute magis solutus, expeditius ad ipsum Creatoris cultum et contemplationem evehi potest. Immo impulsu gratiae ad agnoscendum Dei Verbum disponitur, quod, antequam caro fieret ad omnia salvanda et in Se recapitulanda, iam in mundo erat, tamquam lux vera quae illuminat omnem hominem (Jn 1,9). Sane hodiernus progressus scientiarum artiumque technicarum, quae vi methodi suae usque ad intimas rerum rationes penetrare nequeunt, cuidam phaenomenismo et agnosticismo favere potest, quando methodus investigandi, qua disciplinae istae utuntur, immerito pro suprema totius veritatis inveniendae regula habetur. Immo periculum adest, ne homo, inventis hodiernis nimis fidens, se sibi sufficere aestimet et altiora amplius iam non quaerat. Haec tamen infausta non necessario ex hodierna cultura sequuntur, nec nos in tentationem inducere debent, ne eius valores positivos non agnoscamus. Inter quos adnumerantur: scientiarum studium atque exacta fidelitas erga veritatem in inquisitionibus scientificis, necessitas laborandi una cum aliis in coetibus technicis, sensus solidarietatis internationalis, conscientia in dies vividior responsabilitatis peritorum erga homines adiuvandos immo et protegendos, voluntas faustiores reddendi vitae condiciones omnibus, praesertim illis qui vel responsabilitatis privatione vel culturae paupertate laborant. Haec omnia aliquam praeparationem ad nuntium Evangelii accipiendum afferre valent, quae informari potest caritate divina ab Eo qui venit ut mundum salvaret."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p457","On the manifold connection between the good news of Christ and human culture","59 For the reasons stated above, the Church calls to the mind of all that culture is to be referred to the whole perfection of the human person, to the good of the community, and of the whole human society. Therefore the mind ought to be so cultivated that the faculty of admiration, of reading into things, of contemplating, and of forming personal judgment and of cultivating the religious, moral, and social sense may be promoted. For culture, since it flows immediately from the rational and social nature of man, constantly needs just freedom to develop itself and a legitimate, according to its own principles, autonomous faculty of action. It therefore rightly demands reverence and enjoys a certain inviolability, the rights of the person and community, both particular and universal, being preserved within the bounds of the common good. The sacred Synod, recalling what the First Vatican Council taught, declares that there is a double order of knowledge distinct, namely faith and reason, nor does the Church forbid the cultures of human arts and disciplines to use their own principles and proper method in their own sphere; therefore, acknowledging this just freedom, she affirms the legitimate autonomy of human culture and especially of the sciences. All these things also demand that man, the moral order and common utility being preserved, be free to seek truth and to declare and spread his opinion, and to cultivate any art; that finally he be informed of public events according to truth. To public authority, however, it pertains not to determine the proper character of forms of culture, but to foster the conditions and aids for promoting cultural life among all, even within the minorities of a nation. Therefore it is especially to be insisted, that culture be not forced to serve political or economic powers, turned away from its own end.","Inter nuntium salutis et culturam humanam multiplices nexus inveniuntur. Nam Deus, populo suo sese revelans usque ad plenam sui manifestationem in Filio incarnato, locutus est secundum culturam diversis aetatibus propriam. Pariter Ecclesia, decursu temporum variis in condicionibus vivens, diversarum culturarum inventa adhibuit, ut nuntium Christi in sua praedicatione ad omnes gentes diffundat et explicet, illud investiget et altius intelligat, in celebratione liturgica atque in vita multiformis communitatis fidelium melius exprimat. At simul, ad omnes populos cuiusvis aetatis et regionis missa, Ecclesia nulli stirpi aut nationi, nulli particulari morum rationi, nulli antiquae aut novae consuetudini exclusive et indissolubiliter nectitur. Propriae traditioni inhaerens et insimul missionis suae universalis conscia, communionem cum diversis culturae formis inire valet, qua tum ipsa Ecclesia tum variae culturae ditescunt. Bonum Christi nuntium hominis lapsi vitam et cultum continenter renovat, et errores ac mala, ex semper minaci peccati seductione manantia, impugnat et removet. Mores populorum indesinenter purificat et elevat. Animi ornamenta dotesque cuiuscumque populi vel aetatis supernis divitiis velut ab intra fecundat, communit, complet atque in Christo restaurat. Sic Ecclesia, proprium implendo munus, iam eo ipso ad humanum civilemque cultum impellit atque confert, et actione sua, etiam liturgica, hominem ad interiorem libertatem educat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p458","On the various ways to be rightly composed in the forms of human culture","60 Since the opportunity is now given to free very many men from the misery of ignorance, it is the duty most consonant with our age, especially for Christians, to work strenuously that, both in economic and political matters, both in the national and international field, those fundamental judgments be made by which the right of all to human and civil culture, congruent with the dignity of the person, without discrimination of race, sex, nation, religion, or social condition, may be acknowledged and realized everywhere. Therefore a sufficient supply of cultural goods is to be provided for all, especially those which constitute so-called fundamental culture, lest very many be prevented from truly human cooperation for the common good by illiteracy and lack of responsible activity. It is to be striven, therefore, that men whose mental powers permit be able to ascend to higher orders of studies; in such a way that they may, as far as possible, emerge in human society to offices, duties, and services according to their talent and the skill they have acquired. Thus every man and social group of any people will be able to attain to the full development of their cultural life, congruent with their gifts and traditions. Moreover, it is to be strenuously labored that all become conscious both of the right to culture and of the duty by which they are bound to cultivate themselves and to help others. For conditions of life and work sometimes exist which impede the cultural endeavors of men and destroy the study of culture in them. This holds especially for farmers and workers, to whom such conditions of performing their work must be provided which do not impede but promote their human culture. Women already work in almost all fields of life; it is fitting, however, that they be able to fully assume their parts according to their proper character. It will be the part of all that the proper and necessary participation of women in cultural life be acknowledged and promoted.","Supradictis rationibus, Ecclesia in mentem omnium revocat culturam ad integram personae humanae perfectionem, ad bonum communitatis et totius humanae societatis esse referendam. Quare oportet animum ita excolere, ut promoveatur facultas admirandi, intus legendi, contemplandi atque efformandi iudicium personale et excolendi sensum religiosum, moralem ac socialem. Cultura enim, cum ex hominis indole rationali et sociali immediate fluat, indesinenter indiget iusta libertate ad sese explicandam atque legitima, secundum propria principia, sui iuris agendi facultate. Iure merito ergo postulat reverentiam et quadam gaudet inviolabilitate, servatis utique iuribus personae et communitatis, sive particularis sive universalis, intra fines boni communis. Sacra Synodus, recolens ea quae Concilium Vaticanum Primum docuit, declarat duplicem esse ordinem cognitionis distinctum, nempe fidei et rationis, nec sane Ecclesiam vetare ne humanarum artium et disciplinarum culturae ... in suo quaeque ambitu propriis utantur principiis et propria methodo; quare iustam hanc libertatem agnoscens, cultus humani et praesertim scientiarum legitimam autonomiam affirmat. Haec omnia postulant quoque ut homo, morali ordine communique utilitate servatis, libere possit verum inquirere et opinionem suam declarare ac vulgare, atque artem qualemcumque colere; ut denique secundum veritatem de publicis eventibus certior fiat. Ad publicam vero auctoritatem pertinet, non proprium cultus humani formarum indolem determinare, sed condiciones et subsidia ad vitam culturalem inter omnes promovendam fovere, etiam intra minoritates alicuius nationis. Ideo praeprimis instandum est, ne cultura, a proprio fine aversa, potestatibus politicis vel oeconomicis servire cogatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p459","Sectio III: ON CERTAIN MORE URGENT DUTIES OF CHRISTIANS CONCERNING CULTURE","61 There is today a greater difficulty than once in reducing the various disciplines of knowledge and arts into synthesis. For while the mass and diversity of the elements which constitute culture grow, at the same time the faculty for individuals to perceive them and organically compose them is diminished, so that the universal image of man more and more vanishes. Yet the office of keeping the reason of the whole human person, in which the values of intelligence, will, conscience, and fraternity excel, all of which are founded in God the Creator and wonderfully healed and elevated in Christ, remains for each man.","Cum nunc facultas praebeatur plurimos homines ab ignorantiae miseria liberandi, officium nostrae aetati maxime consentaneum est, praesertim pro christianis, strenue adlaborandi ut, tam in re oeconomica quam in re politica, tam in campo nationali quam internationali, ferantur iudicia fundamentalia, quibus ius omnium ad humanum civilemque cultum, personae dignitati congruum, sine discrimine stirpis, sexus, nationis, religionis aut socialis condicionis, ubique terrarum agnoscatur et ad rem deducatur. Ideo sufficiens bonorum culturalium copia omnibus providenda est, praesertim eorum quae constituunt culturam sic dictam fundamentalem, ne plurimi litterarum ignorantia responsabilisque actuositatis privatione a cooperatione vere humana ad bonum commune impediantur. Contendendum est itaque ut homines, quorum ingenii vires id ferant, ad altiores studiorum ordines ascendere queant; ita quidem ut iidem, quoad fieri possit, in humana societate ad munera, officia et servitia emergant, tum suo ingenio, tum peritiae quam acquisierint consentanea. Sic quilibet homo et coetus sociales cuiuscumque populi ad plenam vitae suae culturalis explicationem, suis dotibus atque traditionibus congruam, pertingere valebunt. Enixe insuper adlaborandum est ut omnes conscii fiant tum iuris ad culturam, tum officii quo astringuntur sese excolendi aliosque adiuvandi. Condiciones enim vitae et laboris quandoque exstant, quae impediunt contentiones culturales hominum et in ipsis studium culturae destruunt. Res speciali ratione pro ruricolis et opificibus valet, quibus praebeantur oportet tales condiciones operam suam praestandi, quae humanam eorum culturam non praepediant sed promoveant. Mulieres in fere omnibus vitae campis iam adlaborant; convenit autem ut partes suas secundum propriam indolem plene assumere valeant. Omnium erit, ut mulierum propria necessariaque participatio vitae culturalis agnoscatur atque promoveatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p460","On education for the whole culture of man","The family is first of all as mother and nurse of this education, in which children, fostered by love, more easily learn the right order of things, while approved forms of human culture are as it were naturally transfused into the progressing spirit of adolescence. For the same education in modern societies there are opportunities, especially from the increased diffusion of books and the new instruments of cultural and social communication, which can favor universal culture. For, everywhere, the reduced time of labor increasingly increases the advantages for many men. Leisure is to be rightly expended for relaxation of mind and health of body and spirit, through free industries and studies, travel to other regions (tourism), by which the genius of man is cultivated, but also men are enriched by mutual knowledge, through sporting exercises and manifestations, which contribute to preserving the equilibrium of mind, also in community, and to establishing fraternal relations among men of all conditions, nations, or diverse race. Christ's faithful, therefore, should cooperate that the manifestations of culture and collective actions, which are proper to our age, be imbued with the human and Christian spirit. Yet all these advantages of education cannot perfect the education of man to his whole culture, if at the same time the deep questioning concerning the meaning of culture and science for the human person is neglected.","Maior hodie adest difficultas quam olim varias cognitionis disciplinas et artes in synthesim redigendi. Dum enim crescunt moles et diversitas elementorum, quae culturam constituunt, insimul minuitur facultas pro singulis hominibus eadem percipiendi et organice componendi, ita ut imago hominis universalis magis ac magis evanescat. Attamen unicuique homini remanet officium retinendi rationem totius personae humanae, in qua eminent intelligentiae, voluntatis, conscientiae et fraternitatis valores, qui omnes in Deo Creatore fundantur et in Christo mirabiliter sanati et elevati sunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p461","On education for the whole culture of man","62 Although the Church has contributed much to the progress of culture, yet experience shows that the composition of culture with Christian instruction from contingent causes has not always proceeded without difficulties. These difficulties do not necessarily bring damage to the life of faith, indeed they can excite the mind to a more accurate and deeper understanding of faith. For the more recent studies and discoveries of sciences, also of history and philosophy, raise new questions, which carry consequences also for life, and also demand new investigations from theologians. Moreover, theologians, the proper methods and exigencies of theological science being preserved, are invited always to seek a more apt manner of communicating doctrine with the men of their time, because it is one thing that the deposit of Faith itself, namely the truths, is another the manner in which they are enunciated, preserving the same sense and the same meaning. In pastoral care, not only theological principles, but also the discoveries of profane sciences, especially of psychology and sociology, are to be sufficiently acknowledged and used, that even the faithful be led to a purer and more mature life of faith. Litterature and the arts also are of great importance in their own way for the life of the Church. For they strive to learn the proper character of man, his problems and his experience in the effort to know and perfect himself and the world; to discover his situation in history and in the whole world and to illustrate the miseries and joys, the necessities and forces of men, and to better adumbrate the lot of man. Thus they can elevate human life, expressed in manifold forms according to times and regions. Therefore it is to be labored that the cultivators of those arts feel themselves recognized by the Church in their activity, and, enjoying ordered freedom, may establish easier commerce with the Christian community. New forms of art also, which are adapted to our contemporaries according to the character of various nations and regions, are to be recognized by the Church. Let them be received in the sanctuary, when, in an accommodated manner of speech and conformed to the exigencies of the Liturgy, they raise the mind to God. Thus the knowledge of God is better manifested and the preaching of the Gospel becomes more perspicuous in the understanding of men and appears as it were inborn to their conditions.","Quasi mater et nutrix huius educationis est imprimis familia, in qua liberi, amore foti, rectum rerum ordinem facilius condiscunt, dum probatae cultus humani formae quasi naturaliter in progredientis adolescentiae animum transfunduntur. Pro eadem educatione in societatibus hodiernis exstant opportunitates, praesertim ex aucta librorum diffusione atque novis instrumentis communicationis culturalis et socialis, quae universali culturae favere possunt. Imminuto enim passim laboris spatio in dies augescunt pro pluribus hominibus commoda. Otia ad animum relaxandum et mentis ac corporis sanitatem roborandam rite insumantur, per liberas industrias et studia, versus alias regiones itinera (turismus), quibus ingenium hominis expolitur, sed et homines mutua cognitione locupletantur, per exercitationes quoque et manifestationes sportivas, quae ad animi aequilibrium, etiam in communitate, servandum necnon ad fraternas relationes inter homines omnium condicionum, nationum vel diversae stirpis statuendas, adiumentum praebent. Christifideles ergo cooperentur ut culturae manifestationes actionesque collectivae, quae nostrae aetatis sunt propriae, spiritu humano et christiano imbuantur. Haec autem omnia commoda educationem hominis ad integrum sui cultum perficere non valent, si insimul profunda interrogatio de sensu culturae et scientiae pro persona humana negligitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p462","On rightly composing human and civil culture with Christian instruction","Therefore let the faithful live most closely with the other men of their time, and perfectly strive to perceive their ways of thinking and feeling, which are expressed by the cultivation of the mind. Let them join the knowledge of new sciences and doctrines and of the newest discoveries with Christian morals and instruction of Christian doctrine, that the cultivation of religion and probity of spirit may proceed in equal step with the knowledge of sciences and the daily progressing technical arts, and therefore they may be able to approve and interpret all things with a whole Christian sense. Those who apply themselves to theological disciplines in seminaries and universities of studies should strive to cooperate with joined forces and counsels with those who excel in other sciences. Theological investigation should at the same time pursue a deeper knowledge of revealed truth and not neglect connection with its own time, that it may be able to help men skilled in various disciplines to a fuller knowledge of faith. This joint work will greatly profit the instruction of sacred ministers who will be able to explain the doctrine of the Church concerning God, man, and the world more aptly to our contemporaries, that that word may also be more willingly received by them. Indeed, it is to be desired that many laity acquire sufficient instruction in sacred disciplines, and that not a few among them cultivate and more deeply produce these studies, given the work. So that they may be able to exercise their office, there be acknowledged for the faithful, whether clerics or laity, a just freedom of investigation, of thinking, and of opening their mind in humility and fortitude in those things in which they enjoy skill.","Quamvis Ecclesia ad culturae progressum multum contulerit, experientia tamen constat compositionem culturae cum christiana institutione ex causis contingentibus non semper sine difficultatibus procedere. Istae difficultates non necessario vitae fidei damnum afferunt, immo ad accuratiorem et altiorem intelligentiam fidei mentem excitare possunt. Etenim scientiarum, necnon historiae ac philosophiae recentiora studia et inventa novas suscitant quaestiones, quae sequelas pro vita quoque secumferunt et etiam a theologis novas investigationes postulant. Praeterea theologi, servatis propriis scientiae theologicae methodis et exigentiis, invitantur ut aptiorem modum doctrinam cum hominibus sui temporis communicandi semper inquirant, quia aliud est ipsum depositum Fidei seu veritates, aliud modus secundum quem enuntiantur, eodem tamen sensu eademque sententia. In cura pastorali non tantum principia theologica, sed etiam inventa scientiarum profanarum, imprimis psychologiae et sociologiae, satis agnoscantur et adhibeantur, ita ut etiam fideles ad puriorem et maturiorem fidei vitam ducantur. Suo quoque modo litterae et artes pro vita Ecclesiae magni sunt momenti. Indolem enim propriam hominis, eius problemata eiusque experientiam in conatu ad seipsum mundumque cognoscendum et perficiendum ediscere contendunt; situationem eius in historia et in universo mundo detegere necnon miserias et gaudia, necessitates et vires hominum illustrare atque sortem hominis meliorem adumbrare satagunt. Ita vitam humanam, multiplicibus formis secundum tempora et regiones expressam, elevare valent. Exinde adlaborandum est ut artium illarum cultores se ab Ecclesia in sua navitate agnitos sentiant et, ordinata libertate fruentes, faciliora commercia cum communitate christiana instituant. Novae quoque formae artis, quae coaevis nostris aptantur iuxta variarum nationum et regionum indolem, ab Ecclesia agnoscantur. In sanctuario autem recipiantur, cum, modo dicendi accommodato et liturgiae exigentiis conformi, mentem ad Deum erigunt. Sic notitia Dei melius manifestatur ac praedicatio evangelica in intellectu hominum magis perspicua fit et eorum condicionibus quasi insita apparet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p463","On rightly composing human and civil culture with Christian instruction","63 Even in economic-social life, the dignity of the human person and his whole vocation, and the good of the whole society, are to be honored and promoted. For man is the author, center, and end of all economic-social life. Modern economy, no less than other provinces of social life, is marked by a growing domination of man over nature, by denser and more intense relations and mutual dependence among citizens, groups, and peoples, and by more frequent intervention of political power. At the same time, progress in the ways of producing and exchanging goods and services has made economy a suitable instrument by which the increased needs of the human family can be better provided for. Yet there are not lacking reasons of disquiet. Not a few men, especially in economically progressed regions, seem to be ruled by the economy, so that almost their whole personal and social life is imbued with a certain economistic spirit, both in nations which favor collectivistic economy and in others. At the same time as the increase of economic life, provided it be reasonably and humanely directed and coordinated, could mitigate social inequalities, it often turns into their exasperation, or even, somewhere, into a regression of the social condition of the weak and contempt of the poor. While a huge multitude still lacks things altogether necessary, some, even in less progressed regions, live opulently or waste goods. Luxury and misery exist together. While the few enjoy the greatest power of decision, many lack almost every possibility of acting with their own initiative and responsibility, often even in conditions of life and labor unworthy of the human person. Similar defects of economic and social balance are also observed between agriculture, industry, and services, as well as between various regions of one and the same nation. A graver opposition day by day becomes between economically more progressed nations and other nations, which can call into peril the very peace of the world. Our contemporaries perceive these disparities with an ever more vivid consciousness, since they are thoroughly persuaded that the greater technical and economic capacities which the modern world enjoys can and ought to correct this unhappy state of things. Hence many reforms in economic-social life and a conversion of mind and attitude are required of all. To this, the Church has, in the course of the ages, drawn up and especially in these last times set forth the principles of justice and equity, demanded by right reason, for individual and social life, and for international life, under the light of the Gospel. The sacred Council intends to strengthen these principles according to the circumstances of this age and to set forth certain orientations, especially regarding the exigencies of economic development.","Fideles ergo coniunctissime cum aliis suae aetatis hominibus vivant, et perfecte eorum cogitandi atque sentiendi modos, qui per ingenii cultum exprimuntur, percipere studeant. Novarum scientiarum et doctrinarum necnon novissimorum inventorum notitias cum christianis moribus christianaeque doctrinae institutione coniungant, ut religionis cultus animique probitas apud ipsos pari gressu procedant cum scientiarum cognitione et cotidie progredientibus technicorum artibus, et ideo ipsi valeant res omnes integro christiano sensu probare atque interpretari. Qui theologicis disciplinis in Seminariis et Studiorum Universitatibus incumbunt, cum hominibus qui in aliis scientiis excellunt, collatis viribus atque consiliis, cooperari studeant. Theologica inquisitio insimul profundam veritatis revelatae cognitionem prosequatur et coniunctionem cum proprio tempore ne negligat, ut homines variis disciplinis excultos ad pleniorem fidei scientiam iuvare possit. Haec socia opera plurimum proderit institutioni sacrorum ministrorum qui Ecclesiae doctrinam de Deo, de homine et de mundo aptius coaevis nostris explanare poterunt, ita ut verbum illud etiam libentius ab eis suscipiatur. Immo optandum ut plures laici congruam in disciplinis sacris institutionem adipiscantur, nec pauci inter eos haec studia, data opera, colant et altius producant. Ut vero munus suum exercere valeant, agnoscatur fidelibus, sive clericis sive laicis, iusta libertas inquirendi, cogitandi necnon mentem suam in humilitate et fortitudine aperiendi in iis in quibus peritia gaudent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p464","Chapter III. on Economic-social Life","64 Today, more than before, to provide for the increase of population and to satisfy the growing desires of the human race, the increase of production of agricultural and industrial goods and the provision of services is rightly intended. Therefore technical progress, the spirit of innovation, the study of creating and expanding undertakings, methods of production adapted, and the strenuous efforts of all who exercise production are to be favored: namely all those elements which serve this progression. The fundamental finality of this production, however, is not mere increase of products, nor profit or domination, but the service of man, of the whole man, regard being had for the order of his material necessities and the exigencies of his intellectual, moral, spiritual, and religious life, of man, we say, of every man, and of every group of men, of every race or region of the world. Therefore economic activity, according to its own methods and laws, is to be exercised within the bounds of the moral order, that the purpose of God concerning man may be fulfilled.","Etiam in vita oeconomica-sociali personae humanae dignitas eiusque integra vocatio, totiusque societatis bonum, honoranda atque promovenda sunt. Homo enim totius vitae oeconomicae-socialis auctor, centrum et finis est. Oeconomia hodierna, non secus atque aliae vitae socialis provinciae, crescenti super naturam hominis dominatione notatur, densioribus impensioribusque relationibus atque mutua dependentia, inter cives, coetus et populos, necnon frequentiori politicae potestatis interventione. Insimul progressus in rationibus producendi atque in bonis ac servitiis commutandis, oeconomiam aptum effecerunt instrumentum, quo adauctis familiae humanae necessitatibus melius provideri possit. Non desunt tamen rationes inquietudinis. Homines non pauci, praesertim in regionibus oeconomice progressis, re oeconomica quasi regi videntur, ita ut fere tota eorum vita personalis ac socialis spiritu quodam oeconomistico imbuatur, tam in nationibus quae oeconomiae collectivisticae favent quam in aliis. Eo temporis momento quo vitae oeconomicae incrementum, dummodo rationabiliter humaniterque dirigatur atque coordinetur, inaequalitates sociales mitigare posset, saepe saepius in earum exasperationem, vel etiam alicubi in regressum condicionis socialis debilium et in despectum pauperum vertitur. Dum rebus omnino necessariis ingens multitudo adhuc caret, aliqui, etiam in regionibus minus progressis, opulenter vivunt vel bona dissipant. Luxus et miseria simul exsistunt. Dum pauci maxima deliberandi potestate fruuntur, multi omni paene possibilitate carent propria initiativa ac responsabilitate agendi, saepe etiam in condicionibus vitae et laboris persona humana indignis versantes. Similes aequilibrii oeconomici socialisque defectus inter agriculturam, industriam ac servitia, sicut etiam inter diversas regiones unius eiusdemque nationis advertuntur. Inter nationes oeconomice magis progressas aliasque nationes gravior in dies oppositio fit, quae ipsam pacem mundi in discrimen vocare potest. Has disparitates coaevi nostri conscientia in dies vividiore persentiunt, cum eis persuasissimum sit, ampliores technicas et oeconomicas facultates, quibus mundus hodiernus gaudet, hunc infaustum statum rerum corrigere posse atque debere. Inde autem reformationes multae in vita oeconomica-sociali atque mentis et habitudinis conversio ab omnibus requiruntur. Ad hoc Ecclesia iustitiae et aequitatis principia, tam pro vita individuali et sociali, quam pro vita internationali, a recta ratione postulata, in decursu saeculorum sub luce Evangelii exaravit atque his praesertim ultimis temporibus protulit. Sacrum Concilium haec principia secundum adiuncta huius aetatis roborare orientationesque quasdam proferre intendit, exigentias progressionis oeconomicae imprimis respiciens."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p465","Sectio I: ON ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT","65 Economic development must remain under the counsel of man; nor is it to be left to the sole arbitrariness of a few men or groups wielding too great economic power, nor to that of the political community alone, nor of certain more powerful nations. It ought, on the contrary, that at any level, as many men as possible, and when it concerns international relations, all nations, play active parts in directing it. Likewise, it is necessary that the spontaneous work of individual men and free groups be coordinated with the efforts of public authorities, and be aptly and coherently composed. Increase is to be left neither to the quasi-mechanical course of the economic activity of individuals nor to the sole power of public authority. Wherefore those doctrines are to be faulted which, under the appearance of false freedom, oppose necessary reforms, as well as those which postpone the fundamental rights of individual persons and groups to the organization of collective production. Let citizens, moreover, be mindful that it is their right and duty, also to be acknowledged by civil power, to contribute to the true progress of their own community according to their ability. Especially in economically less progressed regions, where all resources are urgently to be used, the common good is gravely called into peril by those who keep their resources unfruitful or, saving the right of personal migration, deprive their community of aids, whether material or spiritual, which it needs.","Hodie, magis quam antea, ut augmento populi consulatur et crescentibus generis humani optatis satisfiat, incrementum productionis bonorum agriculturae et industriae necnon praestationis servitiorum iure intenditur. Ideo favendum est progressui technico, spiritui innovationis, studio inceptuum creandorum atque ampliandorum, methodis productionis aptandis, ac strenuis quorumcumque productionem exercentium conatibus: omnibus nempe elementis quae huic progressioni inserviunt. Huius autem productionis finalitas fundamentalis non est merum productorum incrementum, neque lucrum vel dominatus, sed hominis servitium, hominis quidem integri, attento ordine materialium eius necessitatum atque eius vitae intellectualis, moralis, spiritualis ac religiosae exigentiarum, hominis, dicimus, cuiuscumque, et cuiuscumque hominum coetus, cuiusvis stirpis vel mundi regionis. Itaque navitas oeconomica, secundum methodos et leges proprias, intra fines ordinis moralis exercenda est ita ut Dei de homine consilium adimpleatur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p466","On economic development under the counsel of man","66 That the exigencies of justice and equity be satisfied, it is to be strenuously striven that, the rights of persons and the proper character of each people being preserved, the great economic inequalities which now exist and often grow, together with individual and social discrimination, be removed as soon as possible. Likewise, in many regions, the peculiar difficulties of agriculture having been attended to, both in producing and in selling goods, farmers are to be helped, both to increase and sell production, and to introduce necessary developments and innovations, and to obtain a just income, lest, as often happens, they remain in the condition of citizens of inferior order. Farmers themselves, especially the young, should skillfully apply themselves to perfecting their professional skill, without which the development of agriculture cannot take place. Justice and equity likewise require that mobility, which is necessary in progressing economic affairs, be so ordered that the life of individual men and their families not become uncertain and precarious. Towards workers, however, who, coming from another nation or region, contribute by their labor to the economic progress of a people or province, any discrimination concerning conditions of remuneration or work is to be sedulously avoided. Moreover, all, especially public powers, ought to regard them not simply as mere instruments of production but as persons, and help them that they may be able to bring their family to themselves and to provide a decent habitation for themselves, and to favor their insertion into the social life of the receiving people or region. Yet, as far as possible, sources of work are to be created in their own regions. In economic affairs today subject to change, as in new forms of industrial society in which, for example, automation progresses, care is to be taken that suitable work, convenient for each, be provided, together with the possibility of fitting technical and professional formation, and that the livelihood and human dignity of those especially who suffer graver difficulties from sickness or age be placed in safety.","Progressio oeconomica sub hominis consilio manere debet; nec soli arbitrio paucorum hominum aut coetuum nimia potentia oeconomica pollentium, nec solius communitatis politicae, nec quarundam potentiorum nationum remittenda est. Oportet e contra ut, in quocumque gradu, homines quam plurimi atque, ubi de relationibus internationalibus agitur, omnes nationes in ea dirigenda activas partes habeant. Pariter opus est ut opera spontanea singulorum hominum liberorumque coetuum cum auctoritatum publicarum conatibus coordinentur atque apte et cohaerenter componantur. Incrementum nec soli cursui quasi mechanico activitatis oeconomicae singulorum nec soli potestati auctoritatis publicae relinquendum est. Quare erroris arguendae sunt, tam doctrinae quae specie falsae libertatis reformationibus necessariis obstant, quam illae quae iura fundamentalia personarum singularum et coetuum organizationi productionis collectivae postponunt. Meminerint ceterum cives suum esse ius et officium, etiam a potestate civili agnoscendum, ad verum propriae communitatis progressum pro sua possibilitate conferre. Praesertim in regionibus oeconomice minus progressis, ubi omnes opes urgenter adhibendae sunt, bonum commune graviter in discrimen vocant qui opes suas infructuosas retinent vel - salvo iure personali migrationis - communitatem suam subsidiis sive materialibus sive spiritualibus privant quibus illa eget."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p467","On the removal of great economic-social differences","67 Human labor, which is exercised in producing and exchanging goods or in supplying economic services, excels the other elements of economic life, which are only as instruments. For this labor, whether undertaken on one's own or hired from another, proceeds immediately from the person, who signs things of nature as it were with his own seal and subjects them to his will. By his labor, man ordinarily sustains his own and his family's life, joins with his brothers and serves them, can exercise genuine charity, and offers cooperative work to the perfection of divine creation. Indeed, by labor offered to God, we hold that man is consociated with the work of redemption of Jesus Christ, who conferred an eminent dignity on labor, when He worked with His own hands in Nazareth. Hence arises for each the duty of working faithfully and also the right to work; it is the part of society, according to the conditions prevailing in it, to help citizens for its part that they may find sufficient occasion of work. Finally, labor is to be so remunerated that it give to man the faculties to cultivate worthily his own and his family's material, social, cultural, and spiritual life, regard being had to the office and productiveness of each, as well as to the conditions of the workshop and the common good.","Ut exigentiis iustitiae et aequitatis satisfiat, strenue enitendum est ut, servatis iuribus personarum atque propria indole cuiusque populi, ingentes quae nunc sunt et saepe crescunt inaequalitates oeconomicae cum discriminatione individuali et sociali coniunctae, quam citius removeantur. Pariter, in pluribus regionibus, attentis peculiaribus agriculturae difficultatibus sive in gignendis sive in vendendis bonis, adiuvandi sunt ruricolae cum ad productionem augendam et vendendam, tum ad necessarias evolutiones ac innovationes inducendas, tum ad aequum redditum consequendum, ne, ut saepius accidit, in condicione civium inferioris ordinis maneant. Ipsi autem agricolae, praesertim iuvenes, sese sollerter applicent ad suam peritiam professionalem perficiendam, sine qua progressio agriculturae dari nequit. Iustitia et aequitas item requirunt ut mobilitas, quae in progredientibus rebus oeconomicis necessaria est, ita ordinetur, ne vita hominum singulorum eorumque familiarum incerta et praecaria fiat. Erga opifices vero, qui ex alia natione vel regione oriundi, ad promotionem oeconomicam populi vel provinciae labore suo conferunt, quaevis discriminatio quoad condiciones remunerationis vel laboris sedulo vitanda est. Insuper universi, imprimis publicae potestates, illos non simpliciter veluti mera productionis instrumenta sed ut personas habere debent, eosque adiuvare ut familiam suam apud se arcessere et decentem habitationem sibi providere possint, atque eorum insertioni in vitam socialem populi vel regionis recipientis favere. Attamen, quantum fieri potest, in propriis regionibus fontes laboris creentur. In rebus oeconomicis hodie mutationi obnoxiis uti in novis societatis industrialis formis in quibus e. g. automatio progreditur, curandum est ut labor sufficiens et unicuique conveniens simul ac possibilitas congruae formationis technicae et professionalis praebeatur, et in tuto collocentur victus atque dignitas humana eorum praesertim qui ob morbum vel aetatem gravioribus laborant difficultatibus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p468","Sectio II: ON CERTAIN PRINCIPLES RULING THE WHOLE ECONOMIC-SOCIAL LIFE","Since economic activity is generally done by the associated labor of men, it is unjust and inhuman to so compose and order it that it be to the detriment of any of the laborers. Often, however, it happens even in our days that those who work are in a certain way reduced to the servitude of their own work. Which is by no means justified by the so-called laws of economics. The whole process of fruitful work, therefore, must be accommodated to the needs of the person and his living conditions; especially his domestic life, regard always being had for sex and age. Moreover, the workers be given the faculty of developing their own qualities and their own person in the very exercise of labor. Applying their time and strength to this exercise with due responsibility, yet let all enjoy sufficient rest and leisure to cultivate family, cultural, social, and religious life. Indeed, let them have the opportunity freely to develop the forces and powers which perhaps they can little cultivate in professional labor.","Labor humanus, qui in bonis gignendis ac commutandis vel in servitiis oeconomicis suppeditandis exercetur, ceteris elementis vitae oeconomicae praestat, quippe quae tantum rationem instrumentorum habeant. Hic enim labor, sive proprio marte assumatur sive ab alio conducatur, a persona immediate procedit, quae res naturae quasi suo sigillo signat easque suae voluntati submittit. Labore suo, homo ordinarie suam suorumque vitam sustentat, cum fratribus suis coniungitur eisque inservit, germanam caritatem exercere potest atque creationi divinae perficiendae sociam operam praebere. Immo, per laborem Deo oblatum, tenemus hominem ipsi redemptionis operi Iesu Christi consociari, qui praecellentem labori detulit dignitatem, cum in Nazareth propriis manibus operaretur. Exinde oritur pro unoquoque officium fideliter laborandi atque etiam ius ad laborem; societatis vero est, iuxta adiuncta in ea vigentia, pro sua parte cives adiuvare ut sufficientis laboris occasionem invenire possint. Denique ita remunerandus est labor ut homini facultates praebeantur suam suorumque vitam materialem, socialem, culturalem spiritualemque digne excolendi, spectatis uniuscuiusque munere et productivitate necnon officinae condicionibus et bono communi."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p469","Sectio II: ON CERTAIN PRINCIPLES RULING THE WHOLE ECONOMIC-SOCIAL LIFE","68 In economic enterprises, persons are associated, namely free and independent men, created to the image of God. Therefore, the offices of each having been attended to, whether of owners, or of those who hire the work, or of directors, or of workers, and the necessary unity of the direction of the work being saved, the active participation of all is to be promoted, in the management of enterprises, in ways aptly determined. Since, however, it is often not in the enterprise itself, but higher by institutions of superior order, that decisions concerning economic and social conditions are made, on which the future lot of workers and their children depends, they should also have a part in establishing these, and indeed through themselves or through freely elected delegates. Among the fundamental rights of the human person is to be numbered the right of workers to freely form associations which can truly represent them and contribute to the right ordering of economic life, and also the right to freely participate in their activity without peril of retaliation. Through such ordered participation, together with progressing economic and social formation, the consciousness of their own office and burden will day by day increase among all, whereby they are led that, according to their capacities and aptitudes proper to them, they feel themselves partners in the whole work of economic and social development and of the procuring of the whole common good. But when economic-social conflicts arise, it is to be striven to come to a peaceful solution of them. Although, however, one must always first have recourse to sincere dialogue between the parties, a strike, also in present circumstances, can remain a necessary, if ultimate, aid for defending one's own rights and for fulfilling the just demands of workers. As soon as possible, however, let the ways to resume negotiation and conciliation dialogue be sought.","Cum navitas oeconomica plerumque consociato hominum labore fiat, iniquum et inhumanum est illam ita componere et ordinare ut quibusvis laborantibus detrimento sit. Saepius autem accidit, etiam nostris diebus, ut opus facientes quodammodo in servitutem proprii operis redigantur. Quod sic dictis legibus oeconomicis nequaquam iustificatur. Universus igitur fructuosi laboris processus necessitatibus personae eiusque vitae vivendi rationibus accommodandus est; imprimis eius vitae domesticae, praesertim quod ad matres familiae spectat, respectu semper habito et sexus et aetatis. Laborantibus praebeatur insuper facultas proprias qualitates personamque suam in ipso laboris exercitio explicandi. Huic exercitio tempus viresque suas debita cum responsabilitate applicantes, tamen ad vitam familiarem, culturalem, socialem et religiosam colendam etiam sufficienti quiete et otio omnes gaudeant. Immo opportunitatem habeant vires ac potentias libere evolvendi, quas fortasse in labore professionali parum excolere valent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p470","On participation in enterprises and in the whole disposition of economic affairs","69 God destined the earth with all that is in it for the use of all men and peoples, so that created goods should flow to all in equal measure, justice leading, charity accompanying. Whatever forms of property there may be, accommodated to the legitimate institutions of peoples, according to diverse and changeable circumstances, regard must always be had to this universal destination of goods. Wherefore man, using those goods, ought to hold external things which he legitimately possesses not only as his own, but also as common, in the sense that they be able to profit not only himself but also others. Moreover, the right to have a part of goods sufficient for himself and his family belongs to all. So the Fathers and Doctors of the Church have felt, teaching that men are bound to relieve the poor, and indeed, not only from the superfluous. But he who is in extreme need has the right to procure the necessities for himself from the riches of others. Since there are so many in the world oppressed by famine, the sacred Council urges all, both individuals and authorities, mindful of that saying of the Fathers: \"Feed the dying of hunger, because if you have not fed him, you have killed him,\" that they truly communicate and spend their goods according to the ability of each, especially supplying those aids, whether individuals or peoples, by which they themselves may be able to help and develop themselves.","In inceptis oeconomicis personae consociantur, homines scilicet liberi et sui iuris, ad imaginem Dei creati. Ideo, attentis muneribus uniuscuiusque, sive proprietariorum, sive conductorum operis, sive dirigentium, sive operariorum, atque salva necessaria directionis operis unitate, promoveatur, modis apte determinandis, omnium actuosa participatio in inceptorum curatione. Cum autem saepius non iam in ipso incepto, sed altius a superioris ordinis institutis de oeconomicis et socialibus condicionibus decernatur, e quibus sors futura laborantium eorumque liberorum pendet, etiam in his statuendis partem habeant, et quidem per seipsos vel per delegatos libere electos. Inter fundamentalia personae humanae iura adnumerandum est ius pro laborantibus consociationes libere condendi, quae eos vere repraesentare et ad vitam oeconomicam recto ordine disponendam conferre possint, necnon ius earum navitatem sine ultionis periculo libere participandi. Per huiusmodi ordinatam participationem, cum progrediente formatione oeconomica et sociali iunctam, in dies augebitur apud omnes proprii muneris onerisque conscientia, qua ipsi eo adducantur ut, secundum capacitates aptitudinesque sibi proprias, totius operis progressionis oeconomicae et socialis necnon universi boni communis procurandi socios se sentiant. Cum vero conflictus oeconomici-sociales oriuntur, ut ad pacificam eorum solutionem deveniatur enitendum est. Licet autem semper praeprimis ad sincerum inter partes colloquium sit recurrendum, operistitium tamen, et in hodiernis adiunctis, ad propria iura defendenda et ad iusta laborantium quaesita implenda, adiumentum necessarium, etsi ultimum, manere potest. Quamprimum vero viae ad negotiationem et conciliationis colloquium resumendum quaerantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p471","On the universal destination of earthly goods to all men","In economically less progressed societies, the common destination of goods is not seldom sufficiently satisfied by customs and traditions proper to the community, by which absolutely necessary goods are provided to each member. Yet it is to be avoided that certain customs be held as altogether immovable, if they no longer respond to the new exigencies of this time; on the other hand, that one not act imprudently against honest customs, which, as long as they be aptly accommodated to present circumstances, do not cease to be very useful. Similarly, in economically highly progressed nations, a certain body of social institutions, looking to caution and security, can for its part bring the common destination of goods into act. Family and social services are to be further promoted, especially those which provide for cultivation of mind and for education. In instituting all these, it must be watched, however, that citizens not be induced to a certain inertness toward society nor reject the burden of the office undertaken and repudiate service.","Deus terram cum omnibus quae in ea continentur in usum universorum hominum et populorum destinavit, ita ut bona creata aequa ratione ad omnes affluere debeant, iustitia duce, caritate comite. Quaecumque formae proprietatis sint, legitimis institutis populorum accommodatae, secundum diversa atque mutabilia adiuncta, ad hanc bonorum universalem destinationem semper attendendum est. Quapropter homo, illis bonis utens, res exteriores quas legitime possidet non tantum tamquam sibi proprias, sed etiam tamquam communes habere debet, eo sensu ut non sibi tantum sed etiam aliis prodesse queant. Ceterum, ius habendi partem bonorum sibi suisque familiis sufficientem omnibus competit. Ita Patres Doctoresque Ecclesiae senserunt, docentes ad pauperes sublevandos homines obligari, et quidem, non tantum ex superfluis. Qui autem in extrema necessitate degit, ius habet ut ex aliorum divitiis necessaria sibi procuret. Cum tot sint in mundo fame oppressi, Sacrum Concilium omnes sive singulos sive auctoritates urget ut, illius sententiae Patrum memores: Pasce fame morientem, quia si non pavisti occidisti, pro uniuscuiusque facultate, bona sua revera communicent et impendant, praesertim illos, sive singulos sive populos, auxiliis muniendo, quibus ipsi sese adiuvare atque evolvere possint."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p472","On the universal destination of earthly goods to all men","70 Investments, for their part, ought to tend to procure occasions of work and sufficient income both for the present people and for the future. Whoever decides concerning these investments and the ordering of economic life—whether individuals, groups, or public authorities—are bound to keep these ends before their eyes, and to acknowledge their grave obligation on the one hand to watch that the necessities for a decent life, both of individuals and of the whole community, be provided for, on the other hand to foresee the future and to constitute a just balance between the necessities of present consumption, both individual and collective, and the exigencies of investing goods for the future generation. Always also the urgent necessities of economically less progressed nations or regions must be kept before their eyes. In monetary matters, however, care is to be taken lest the good of one's own nation nor that of other nations be injured. Let it be provided, moreover, that the economically weak not suffer unjustly from changes in the value of money.","In societatibus oeconomice minus progressis non raro destinationi communi bonorum ex parte per consuetudines et traditiones communitati proprias satis fit, quibus unicuique membro bona prorsus necessaria praebentur. Vitandum tamen est ne consuetudines quaedam ut omnino immutabiles habeantur, si novis exigentiis huius temporis non iam respondeant; altera vero parte, ne contra honestas consuetudines imprudenter agatur quae, dummodo hodiernis adiunctis apte accommodentur, perutiles esse non desinunt. Similiter in nationibus oeconomice valde progressis, corpus quoddam socialium institutionum, ad cautionem et securitatem spectantium, communem bonorum destinationem pro sua parte ad actum adducere potest. Ulterius promovenda sunt servitia familiaria et socialia, praesertim quae animi cultui atque educationi consulunt. In his omnibus instituendis, invigilandum tamen est ne cives ad quamdam erga societatem inertiam inducantur neve suscepti officii onus reiiciant et servitium repudient."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p473","On investments and on money matters","71 Since property and other forms of private dominion over external goods contribute to the expression of the person, and since they also give him the occasion to exercise his office in society and economy, it is very important that accession to some external dominion over goods, whether of individuals or of communities, be fostered. Private property or a certain dominion over external goods confers a quite necessary space for personal and family autonomy to each, and is to be considered as a kind of extension of human freedom. Finally, because it adds incentives to exercise office and burden, it constitutes a condition of civil liberties. The forms of such dominion or property are today various and day by day still more diversified. All, however, not with standing social funds, rights, and services provided by society, remain a cause of not-small-importance for security. Which is to be said not only of material properties, but also of immaterial goods, such as professional capacities.","Bonorum collocationes, ex sua parte, tendere debent ad occasiones laboris redditusque sufficientes tam populo hodierno quam futuro procurandos. Quicumque de his collocationibus et vitae oeconomicae ordinatione decernunt - sive singuli, sive coetus, sive auctoritates publicae -, hos fines prae oculis habere tenentur, atque gravem suam obligationem agnoscere ex una parte invigilandi, ut necessariis ad vitam decentem requisitis, sive singulorum sive totius communitatis, provideatur, ex altera parte futura praevidendi et iustum aequilibrium constituendi inter necessitates hodiernae consumptionis, sive individualis sive collectivae, et exigentias collocandi bona pro generatione ventura. Semper etiam prae oculis habeantur urgentes nationum vel regionum oeconomice minus progressarum necessitates. In re autem monetaria caveatur ne propriae nationis necnon aliarum nationum bono offendatur. Provideatur insuper ne oeconomice debiles ex valoris pecuniae immutatione iniuste detrimentum patiantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p474","On accession to property and private dominion of goods; and on large estates","The right of private dominion, however, does not stand against that right which inheres in various forms of public property. The transfer of goods to public property, indeed, can be done only by competent authority, according to the exigencies of the common good and within its limits, just compensation having been offered. Moreover, it pertains to public authority to watch that no one abuse private property against the common good. Private property, moreover, also has a social character by its very nature, which is founded on the law of the common destination of goods. With this social character neglected, property often becomes an occasion of cupidities and grave disturbances, so that a pretext is given to impugners to call the very right into peril. In many economically less progressed regions, large or even very wide rural estates exist, moderately cultivated or remaining without any culture for profit, while the greater part of the people either lacks lands or enjoys only the smallest fields, and, on the other hand, the increase of fructification of fields clearly urgent appears. Not seldom those who are hired for labor by the lords, or who cultivate a part of their land under a lease, receive only wages or income unworthy of man, are deprived of decent habitation, and are despoiled by mediators. Lacking all security, they live under such personal servitude that almost all faculty of acting spontaneously and with responsibility is taken from them, and all promotion in human culture and all part in social and political life is forbidden to them. For various cases, therefore, reforms are necessary: that incomes increase, conditions of work be amended, security in hiring increase, and stimulus to work spontaneously be given; indeed, that fields not sufficiently cultivated be distributed to those who can render the same lands fruitful. In which case, things and means necessary are to be supplied, especially aids of education and faculties of just cooperative ordering. As often, however, as the common good has required the removal of property, compensation is to be estimated from equity, all circumstances having been weighed.","Cum proprietas ac aliae in bona exteriora dominii privati formae ad expressionem personae conferant, cum insuper occasionem ei praebeant suum munus in societate et oeconomia exercendi, valde interest ut, sive singolorum sive communitatum, ad quoddam bonorum exteriorum dominium accessio foveatur. Proprietas privata aut dominium quoddam in bona exteriora spatium plane necessarium ad personalem et familiarem autonomiam unicuique conferunt, et velut extensio libertatis humanae considerari debent. Demum, quia ad munus onusque exercendum stimulos addunt, condicionem quamdam libertatum civilium constituunt. Formae talis dominii vel proprietatis sunt hodie diversae et in dies adhuc magis diversificantur. Omnes autem, non obstantibus fundis socialibus, iuribus et ministeriis a societate procuratis, causa non parvipendenda securitatis manent. Quod non tantum de proprietatibus materialibus dicendum est, sed etiam de immaterialibus bonis, uti sunt capacitates professionales."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p475","On accession to property and private dominion of goods; and on large estates","72 Christians who play active parts in modern economic-social development and fight for justice and charity, should be persuaded that they can contribute much to the prosperity of humanity and the peace of the world. In these activities, whether individually or associated, let them shine by example. Having acquired the skill and experience altogether necessary, let them keep the right order among earthly activities, in fidelity to Christ and His Gospel, that their whole life, both individual and social, may be imbued with the spirit of the Beatitudes, notably poverty. He who, obeying Christ, first seeks the kingdom of God, from it receives a stronger and purer love, to help all his brothers and to perfect the work of justice, inspired by charity.","Ius autem privati dominii illi iuri non obstat quod variis formis proprietatum publicarum inest. Translatio quidem bonorum in publicam proprietatem non nisi a competenti auctoritate, iuxta boni communis exigentias et intra eius limites, aequa compensatione oblata, fieri potest. Praeterea, ad publicam auctoritatem pertinet praecavere ne quis privata proprietate contra bonum commune abutatur. Ipsa autem proprietas privata et indolem socialem natura sua habet, quae in communis destinationis bonorum lege fundatur. Qua sociali indole neglecta, proprietas multoties occasio cupiditatum et gravium perturbationum fieri accidit, ita ut ad ipsum ius in discrimen vocandum impugnatoribus praetextus detur. In pluribus regionibus oeconomice minus progressis, magni vel etiam latissimi rustici fundi existunt, mediocriter exculti vel lucri causa sine ulla cultura manentes, dum maior pars populi vel terris caret vel minimis tantum agris gaudet, atque, ex altera parte, incrementum fructificationis agrorum evidenter urgens apparet. Non raro ii qui a dominis ad laborem conducuntur, vel qui partem illorum titulo locationis colunt, nonnisi stipendium vel proventum homine indignum recipiunt, decenti habitatione privantur, necnon a mediatoribus exspoliantur. Omni securitate carentes, sub tali personali famulatu vivunt, ut fere omnis facultas sponte et cum responsabilitate agendi eis tollatur, omnisque promotio in cultu humano et omnis pars in vita sociali et politica illis prohibeantur. Pro variis igitur casibus reformationes necessariae sunt: ut crescant reditus, emendentur condiciones laboris, augeatur securitas in conductione, deturque incitamentum ad sponte operandum; immo ut distribuantur fundi non satis exculti iis qui easdem terras fructuosas reddere valeant. Quo in casu suppeditanda sunt res et media necessaria, praesertim educationis subsidia et iustae ordinationis cooperativae facultates. Quoties autem proprietatis ademptionem bonum commune exegerit, compensatio ex aequitate, omnibus adiunctis perpensis, aestimanda est."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p476","On economic-social activity and the kingdom of Christ","73 In our times, profound transformations are also observed in the structure and institutions of peoples, which follow their cultural, economic, and social development; which transformations exert great influence on the life of the political community, especially as concerns the rights and duties of all in the exercise of civil liberty and in attaining the common good, and in ordering the relations of citizens among themselves and with public authority. From the more vivid consciousness of human dignity arises, in various regions of the world, a zeal for establishing a political-juridical order in which the rights of the person in public life may be better protected, such as the right freely to assemble, to associate, to express one's own opinions, and to profess religion privately and publicly. For the protection of the rights of the person is a necessary condition that citizens, whether individually or associated, may be able to participate actively in the life and governance of the republic. Together with cultural, economic, and social progress, among many the desire is strengthened to assume a greater part in ordering the life of the political community. In the conscience of many, the zeal increases that the rights of minorities of any part of a nation be preserved, their duties toward the political community not being neglected; moreover, reverence day by day increases toward men professing another opinion or another religion; at the same time a wider cooperation is instituted, that all citizens, and not only a few endowed with privileges, may truly enjoy personal rights. However, whatever forms of politics in some regions are reproved, which impede civil or religious freedom, multiply the victims of cupidities and political crimes, and turn the exercise of authority from the common good to the advantage of a certain faction or of the rulers themselves. To restore truly human political life, nothing is better than to foster an interior sense of justice and benevolence and of service of the common good, and to strengthen fundamental persuasions concerning the true nature of the political community and also concerning the end, right exercise, and limits of public authority.","Christiani qui activas partes in hodierna progressione oeconomica-sociali agunt et iustitiam caritatemque propugnant, persuasum sibi habeant se multum ad humanitatis prosperitatem et mundi pacem conferre posse. In his activitatibus sive singuli sive consociati exemplo fulgeant. Acquisitis quidem peritia experientiaque omnino necessariis, inter terrestres navitates rectum ordinem servent, in fidelitate erga Christum Eiusque Evangelium, ita ut integra eorum vita, tam individualis quam socialis, spiritu Beatitudinum, notabiliter paupertatis, imbuatur. Quicumque Christo obediens, primum quaerit Regnum Dei, inde validiorem ac puriorem amorem suscipit, ad omnes fratres suos adiuvandos et ad opus iustitiae, inspirante caritate, perficiendum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p477","Chapter IV. on the Life of the Political Community","74 Men, families, and various groups, which constitute civil society, are conscious of their own insufficiency to order a fully human life and perceive the necessity of a wider community, in which all, to ever better procure the common good, daily contribute their own strengths. Hence they constitute the political community according to various forms. The political community, therefore, exists for that common good, in which it obtains its full justification and sense, and from which it draws its own primary and proper right. The common good, however, embraces the sum of those conditions of social life by which men, families, and associations can more fully and conveniently attain their own perfection. Many and diverse, however, are the men who gather in the political community and can legitimately turn to diverse counsels. Lest, therefore, each going off into his own opinion, the political community be distracted, authority is required, which directs the strengths of all citizens to the common good, not mechanically nor despotically, but especially as a moral force, which rests on freedom and the consciousness of duty and burden undertaken. It is clear, therefore, that the political community and public authority are founded in human nature and therefore pertain to the order preestablished by God; although the determination of government and the designation of rulers are left to the free will of citizens. It follows likewise that the exercise of political authority, whether in the community as such, or in the institutions representing the republic, must always be brought to effect within the bounds of the moral order, to procure the common good—and indeed dynamically conceived—according to a juridical order legitimately established or to be established. Then citizens are bound to obey from conscience. From this, however, appears the responsibility, dignity, and importance of those who govern. But where, by public authority exceeding its competence, citizens are pressed, they themselves should not refuse what is objectively required by the common good; but it is lawful for them to defend their own and their fellow citizens' rights against abuse of this authority, preserving those limits which the natural and evangelical law delineates. The concrete ways, however, by which the political community orders its own structure and the tempering of public authority, can be various according to the different character of peoples and the progress of history; but they must always serve to form the cultivated, peaceful, and beneficent man, for the benefit of the whole human family.","Nostris temporibus profundae advertuntur transformationes etiam in compage et institutionibus populorum, quae ipsorum evolutionem culturalem, oeconomicam ac socialem consequuntur; quae transformationes magnum influxum in communitatis politicae vitam exercent, praesertim quod attinet ad omnium iura et officia in libertatis civilis exercitio ac in bono communi attingendo et ad civium relationes inter se et cum publica auctoritate ordinandas. Ex vividiore humanae dignitatis conscientia exoritur, in variis mundi regionibus, studium ordinem politicum-iuridicum instaurandi, in quo personae iura in vita publica melius protegantur, ut sunt iura libere sese coadunandi, consociandi, proprias opiniones exprimendi ac religionem privatim publiceque profitendi. Tutamen enim personae iurium condicio necessaria est ut cives, sive singuli sive consociati, in rei publicae vita et moderamine actuose participare possint. Una cum progressu culturali, oeconomico ac sociali apud plures roboratur desiderium assumendi maiorem partem in communitatis politicae vita ordinanda. In multorum conscientia studium augetur ut iura minorum alicuius nationis partium serventur, earum officiis erga communitatem politicam non neglectis; insuper reverentia in dies increscit erga homines aliam opinionem vel aliam religionem profitentes; simul latior instituitur cooperatio, ut omnes cives, et non tantum quidam privilegiis ornati, iuribus personalibus reapse frui possint. Reprobantur autem quaecumque formae politicae, in aliquibus regionibus vigentes, quae libertatem civilem vel religiosam praepediunt, victimas cupiditatum et criminum politicorum multiplicant ac exercitium auctoritatis a bono communi ad commodum cuiusdam factionis vel ipsorum moderatorum detorquent. Ad vitam politicam vere humanam instaurandam nihil melius est quam interiorem iustitiae et benevolentiae ac servitii boni communis sensum fovere atque persuasiones fundamentales circa veram indolem communitatis politicae necnon circa finem, rectum exercitium et limites publicae auctoritatis corroborare."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p478","On the nature and end of the political community","75 It fully accords with human nature that political-juridical structures be found which give to all citizens always better and without any discrimination the effective possibility of freely and actively participating both in establishing the juridical foundations of the political community, and in the governance of the republic and in determining the fields and limits of various institutions, and in the election of rulers. Therefore let all citizens be mindful of the right and at the same time of the duty of using their free vote to promote the common good. The Church holds the work of those to be worthy of praise and consideration who, in the service of men, devote themselves to the good of the republic and undertake the burdens of this office. That the cooperation of citizens, joined with the consciousness of duty, may happily attain its effect in the daily life of the public affairs, a positive order of law is required, in which a convenient division of offices and institutions of public authority and at the same time an effective protection of rights, subject to no one, be established. The rights of all persons, families, and groups, and their exercise, are to be acknowledged, preserved, and promoted, together with the duties by which all citizens are bound. Among which duties it is necessary to be mindful of providing material and personal services of the republic, which are required for the common good. Let rulers take care that family, social, or cultural groups, bodies, or intermediate institutions not be impeded, nor be deprived of their legitimate and effective action, but rather be willing and orderly to promote them. Citizens, however, whether individually or associated, should take care not to attribute too great power to public authority, nor to inopportunely demand too many advantages and utilities from it, thus diminishing the duty and burden of persons and social groups. From the more complex circumstances of our age, public authority is often forced to intervene in social, economic, and cultural matters to introduce more apt conditions by which citizens and groups may be more effectively helped freely to pursue the whole human good. According, however, to various regions and the development of peoples, the relations between socialization and personal autonomy and progress can be understood in different ways. But where the exercise of rights is temporarily restricted for the common good, freedom, the circumstances having changed, is to be restored as soon as possible. But it is inhuman that political authority fall into totalitarian forms or into dictatorial forms which injure the rights of the person or of social groups. Citizens should cultivate piety toward the fatherland magnanimously and faithfully, yet without narrowness of mind, namely so that at the same time they always intend the good of the whole human family, which is joined by various bonds among races, peoples, and nations. All Christ's faithful should feel a special and proper vocation in the political community, by which they ought to shine by example insofar as they are bound by the consciousness of duty and serve to cultivate the common good, that they may show by deeds also how authority with freedom, personal initiative with the union and necessity of the whole social body, opportune unity with profitable diversity, are composed. Concerning ordering temporal matters, let them acknowledge legitimate, yet differing, opinions, and respect citizens, even associated, who honestly defend them. Political parties, however, should promote those things which in their judgment are required for the common good; they may never, however, prefer their own utility to the common good. Civil and political education, today very necessary both for the people and especially for the young, is to be sedulously taken care of, that all citizens may be able to play their part in the life of the political community. Those who are suitable or can become suitable for the art of politics, difficult and at the same time most noble, should prepare themselves and strive to exercise it, unmindful of their own advantage and venal benefit. Against injury and oppression, the arbitrary domination and intolerance of one man or political party, let them act with integrity of morals and prudence; but with sincerity and equity, indeed with charity and political fortitude, let them devote themselves to the good of all.","Homines, familiae et varii coetus, qui communitatem civilem constituunt, propriae insufficientiae ad vitam plene humanam instituendam conscii sunt et necessitatem amplioris communitatis percipiunt, in qua omnes, ad commune bonum semper melius procurandum, cotidie proprias vires conferant. Quapropter communitatem politicam secundum varias formas constituunt. Communitas ergo politica propter illud commune bonum exsistit, in quo suam plenam iustificationem et sensum obtinet, et ex quo ius suum primigenum et proprium depromit. Bonum vero commune summam complectitur earum vitae socialis condicionum, quibus homines, familiae et consociationes, suam ipsorum perfectionem plenius atque expeditius consequi possint. Multi autem et diversi sunt homines, qui in communitatem politicam conveniunt et legitime in diversa consilia declinare possunt. Ne igitur, unoquoque in suam sententiam abeunte, communitas politica distrahatur, auctoritas requiritur, quae omnium civium vires in bonum commune dirigat, non mechanice nec despotice, sed imprimis ut vis moralis, quae libertate et suscepti officii onerisque conscientia nititur. Patet ergo communitatem politicam et auctoritatem publicam in natura humana fundari ideoque ad ordinem a Deo praefinitum pertinere; etsi regiminis determinatio et moderatorum designatio liberae civium voluntati relinquantur. Sequitur item auctoritatis politicae exercitium sive in communitate ut tali, sive in institutis rem publicam repraesentantibus, semper intra fines ordinis moralis ad effectum deducendum esse, ad commune bonum - et quidem dynamice conceptum - procurandum, secundum ordinem iuridicum legitime statutum vel statuendum. Tunc cives ad oboedientiam praestandam ex conscientia obligantur. Exinde vero patet responsabilitas, dignitas et momentum eorum, qui praesunt. Ubi autem a publica auctoritate, suam competentiam excedente, cives premuntur, ipsi, quae a bono communi obiective postulantur, ne recusent; fas vero sit eis contra abusum huius auctoritatis sua conciviumque suorum iura defendere, illis servatis limitibus, quos lex naturalis et evangelica delineat. Modi vero concreti, quibus communitas politica propriam compagem et publicae auctoritatis temperationem ordinat, varii esse possunt secundum diversam populorum indolem et historiae progressum; semper autem ad hominem excultum, pacificum et erga omnes beneficum efformandum inservire debent, ad totius familiae humanae emolumentum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p479","On the cooperation of all in public life","76 It is of great importance, especially where a pluralistic society flourishes, that a right respect of the relation between the political community and the Church be had, and that between those things which Christ's faithful, whether individually or associated, in their own name as citizens, led by Christian conscience, do, and those things which they do in the name of the Church together with their pastors, be clearly distinguished. The Church, which by reason of her office and competence is by no means confused with the political community, nor bound to any political system, is at once the sign and protection of the transcendence of the human person. The political community and the Church are independent and autonomous in their own field. Both, however, albeit by a different title, serve the personal and social vocation of the same men. Which service they will exercise the more effectively for the good of all, the better both cultivate sound cooperation among themselves, the circumstances of places and times being also attended to. For man is not confined to the temporal order alone, but living in human history keeps his eternal vocation wholly. The Church, however, founded in the love of the Redeemer, contributes to this, that within the boundaries of the nation and between nations justice and charity may flourish more widely. By preaching the gospel truth and illuminating all provinces of human activity through her doctrine and the testimony of Christ's faithful, she also reverences and promotes the political freedom and responsibility of citizens. The Apostles themselves and their successors and their cooperators, since they are sent to announce to men Christ the Savior of the world, in exercising their apostolate rely on the power of God, who often manifests the power of the Gospel in the weakness of witnesses. For whoever devote themselves to the ministry of the word of God ought to use the ways and means proper to the Gospel, which differ in many things from the aids of the earthly city. Earthly things indeed and those which exceed the condition of men this world are closely joined, and the Church herself uses temporal things as much as her own mission demands. She does not, however, place her hope in privileges offered by civil authority; indeed, she will renounce the exercise of certain legitimately acquired rights, when it is established that their use may call the sincerity of her testimony into doubt or that new conditions of life require another ordering. Always and everywhere, however, it be lawful for her with true freedom to preach the faith, to teach her social doctrine, to exercise her office among men expeditiously, and to make moral judgment, even concerning those things which respect the political order, when the fundamental rights of the person or the salvation of souls demand it, using all and only those aids which are congruous with the Gospel and the good of all according to the diversity of times and conditions. Faithfully adhering to the Gospel and exercising her mission in the world, the Church, whose office it is to foster and elevate whatever true, good, and beautiful is found in the human community, strengthens peace among men to the glory of God.","Cum humana natura plene congruit ut structurae politicae-iuridicae inveniantur, quae omnibus civibus semper melius ac sine ulla discriminatione possibilitatem effectivam praebeant libere et actuose participandi tum in fundamentis iuridicis communitatis politicae statuendis, tum in rei publicae moderamine et variorum institutorum campis et finibus determinandis, tum in moderatorum electione. Memores ergo omnes cives sint iuris simul et officii suo libero suffragio utendi ad bonum commune promovendum. Ecclesia laude et consideratione dignum opus illorum habet, qui in hominum servitium rei publicae bono se devovent et huius officii onera suscipiunt. Ut civium cooperatio, cum officii conscientia coniuncta, in cotidiana publicae rei vita effectum suum felicem attingat, requiritur positivus ordo iuris, in quo conveniens divisio munerum et institutorum auctoritatis publicae atque simul efficax tuitio iurium, neminique obnoxia, instaurentur. Omnium personarum, familiarum ac coetuum iura eorumque exercitium agnoscantur, serventur et promoveantur, simul cum officiis, quibus cuncti cives obstringuntur. Inter quae officia meminisse oportet rei publicae materialia et personalia servitia praestandi, quae ad bonum commune requiruntur. Caveant moderatores ne coetus familiares, sociales aut culturales, corpora aut instituta intermedia, impediant, neve ea sua legitima et efficaci actione privent, quam potius libenter et ordinatim promovere satagant. Cives vero, sive singuli sive consociati, caveant ne nimiam potestatem publicae auctoritati tribuant, neve nimia commoda et utilitates ab ipsa inopportune postulent, ita ut personarum, familiarum necnon coetuum socialium officii onus imminuant. Ab implicatioribus nostrae aetatis adiunctis publica auctoritas saepius in res sociales et oeconomicas atque culturales se interponere cogitur ad aptiores inducendas condiciones, quibus cives ac coetus ad integrum humanum bonum libere prosequendum efficacius iuventur. Secundum autem diversas regiones et populorum evolutionem diverso modo intellegi possunt relationes inter socializationem et personae autonomiam ac progressum. Sed ubi exercitium iurium propter bonum commune ad tempus restringitur, libertas, circumstantiis mutatis, quam primum restituatur. At inhumanum est quod auctoritas politica incidat in formas totalitarias vel in formas dictatorias quae iura personae vel socialium coetuum laedant. Cives pietatem erga patriam magnanimiter et fideliter excolant, sine tamen mentis angustia, ita scilicet ut ad bonum totius familiae humanae, quae variis nexibus inter stirpes, gentes ac nationes coniungitur, semper simul animum intendant. Christifideles omnes in communitate politica specialem et propriam vocationem sentiant, qua exemplo praefulgere debent quatenus officii conscientia sunt adstricti et communi bono excolendo inserviunt, ita ut factis quoque commonstrent quomodo auctoritas cum libertate, inceptio personalis cum totius corporis socialis coniunctione ac necessitudine, unitas opportuna cum proficua diversitate componantur. Circa rem temporalem ordinandam legitimas, at inter se discrepantes, opiniones agnoscant, civesque, etiam consociatos, qui eas honeste defendunt, revereantur. Partes autem politicae ea promovere debent, quae earum iudicio ad bonum commune requiruntur; numquam vero propriam utilitatem communi bono praeponere licet. Educatio vero civilis et politica, hodie tum populo tum praesertim iuvenibus maxime necessaria, sedulo curanda est, ut omnes cives in communitatis politicae vita partes suas agere valeant. Qui idonei sunt aut fieri possunt ad artem politicam, difficilem simul et nobilissimam, sese praeparent et eam, proprii commodi et venalis beneficii immemores, exercere satagant. Contra iniuriam et oppressionem, unius hominis vel partis politicae arbitrarium dominatum et intolerantiam, integritate morum ac prudentia agant; sinceritate autem et aequitate, immo caritate et fortitudine politica, bono omnium se devoveant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p480","The political community and the Church","77 In these our years, in which the miseries and anguishes flowing from rampant or impending war still persist most grievously among men, the whole human family has come to a moment of highest crisis in its process of maturity. Gradually gathered into one and now more conscious of its unity everywhere, it can not accomplish the work incumbent on it, namely to build a truly more humane world for all men everywhere, unless all turn to the truth of peace with renewed spirit. Hence it is that the gospel message, being congruent with the deeper studies and desires of the human race, shines with new clarity in our times, since it proclaims the peacemakers blessed, because they shall be called the children of God (Mt 5:9). Therefore the Council, illustrating the true and most noble reason of peace, and condemning the immanity of war, intends fervently to call upon Christians that, with the help of Christ the author of peace, they cooperate with all men to establish peace among them in justice and love and to prepare the instruments of peace.","Magni momenti est, praesertim ubi societas pluralistica viget, ut rectus respectus relationis inter communitatem politicam et Ecclesiam habeatur, utque inter ea, quae christifideles, sive singuli sive consociati, suo nomine tamquam cives, christiana conscientia ducti, et ea, quae nomine Ecclesiae una cum pastoribus suis agunt, clare distinguatur. Ecclesia, quae, ratione sui muneris et competentiae, nullo modo cum communitate politica confunditur, neque ad ullum systema politicum alligatur, simul signum est et tutamentum transcendentiae humanae personae. Communitas politica et Ecclesia in proprio campo ab invicem sunt independentes et autonomae. Ambae autem, licet diverso titulo, eorumdem hominum vocationi personali et sociali inserviunt. Quod servitium eo efficacius in omnium bonum exercebunt, quo ambae melius sanam cooperationem inter se colunt, attentis quoque locorum temporumque adiunctis. Homo enim ad solum ordinem temporalem non coarctatur, sed in historia humana vivens aeternam suam vocationem integre servat. Ecclesia vero, in Redemptoris amore fundata, ad hoc confert ut intra nationis terminos et inter nationes iustitia et caritas latius vigeant. Evangelicam veritatem praedicando atque omnes navitatis humanae provincias, per suam doctrinam et testimonium a christifidelibus exhibitum, illuminando, etiam politicam civium libertatem et responsabilitatem reveretur atque promovet. Apostoli ipsorumque successores et horum cooperatores, cum mittantur ut hominibus Christum mundi Salvatorem annuntient, in suo apostolatu exercendo Dei potentia innituntur, qui persaepe in testium infirmitate virtutem Evangelii manifestat. Quicumque enim Dei verbi ministerio se devovent, utantur oportet viis et subsidiis Evangelio propriis, quae in pluribus a terrenae civitatis subsidiis differunt. Res quidem terrenae et ea, quae in hominum condicione hunc mundum exsuperant, arcte inter se iunguntur, et ipsa Ecclesia rebus temporalibus utitur quantum propria eius missio id postulat. Spem vero suam in privilegiis ab auctoritate civili oblatis non reponit; immo quorundam iurium legitime acquisitorum exercitio renuntiabit, ubi constiterit eorum usu sinceritatem sui testimonii vocari in dubium aut novas vitae condiciones aliam exigere ordinationem. Semper autem et ubique ei fas sit cum vera libertate fidem praedicare, socialem suam doctrinam docere, munus suum inter homines expedite exercere necnon iudicium morale ferre, etiam de rebus quae ordinem politicum respiciunt, quando personae iura fundamentalia aut animarum salus id exigant, omnia et sola subsidia adhibendo, quae Evangelio et omnium bono secundum temporum et condicionum diversitatem congruant. Fideliter Evangelio adhaerens et suam missionem in mundo exercens, Ecclesia, cuius est, quidquid verum, bonum et pulchrum in communitate humana invenitur, fovere ac elevare, pacem inter homines ad Dei gloriam roborat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p481","Chapter V. on Fostering Peace and Promoting the Community of Nations","78 Peace is not a mere absence of war, nor is it reduced to the stable equilibrium of opposing forces, nor does it arise from imperious domination, but it is rightly and properly said to be a work of justice (Is 32:17). It is the fruit of the order of human society insufflated by its divine Founder and brought into act by men always thirsting for more perfect justice. For since the common good of the human race is ruled in its primary notion by the eternal law, but, as to what it concretely demands, is subject to unceasing changes as time progresses, peace is never acquired once for all, but is perpetually to be built. Since, moreover, the human will is slippery and wounded by sin, the procuring of peace demands constant domination of passions and vigilance of legitimate authority. This, however, does not suffice. This peace on earth cannot be obtained unless the good of persons be placed in safety and men spontaneously communicate the riches of their soul and genius to one another with confidence. A firm will to reverence other men and peoples and their dignity, and a zealous exercise of fraternity, are altogether necessary to build peace. Thus peace is also the fruit of love, which goes beyond what justice can do. Earthly peace, however, which arises from love of neighbor, is the figure and effect of the peace of Christ, proceeding from God the Father. For He, the incarnate Son, the prince of peace, reconciled all men to God by His cross, and, restoring the unity of all in one People and one Body, destroyed hatred in His own flesh, and, exalted by resurrection, poured the Spirit of love into the hearts of men. Wherefore all Christians are earnestly called to, doing truth in charity (Eph 4:15), unite themselves with truly peaceful men to implore and restore peace. Moved by the same spirit, we cannot but praise those who, in vindicating rights, renouncing violent action, have recourse to means of defense which are otherwise available to even the weaker, provided this can be done without injury to the rights and obligations of others or of the community. Insofar as men are sinners, the peril of war threatens them, and will threaten until the coming of Christ; insofar, however, as they, joined in charity, overcome sin, they overcome violence as well, until the word be fulfilled: \"And they shall beat their swords into ploughshares, and their spears into pruning hooks. Nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they be exercised any more to war\" (Is 2:4).","Hisce nostris annis, quibus aerumnae et angustiae ex grassante vel impendente bello profluentes adhuc gravissimae inter homines perdurant, universa familia humana ad horam summi discriminis in suae maturitatis processu pervenit. In unum paulatim congregata atque ubivis suae unitatis melius iam conscia, opus quod ei incumbit, mundum scilicet pro omnibus hominibus ubique terrarum vere humaniorem aedificandi, peragere nequit nisi cuncti ad veritatem pacis renovato animo convertantur. Hinc fit ut evangelicum nuntium, cum altioribus generis humani studiis atque optatis congruens, nostris temporibus nova claritate elucescat dum pacis artifices beatos proclamat, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur (Mt 5,9). Ideo Concilium veram et nobilissimam pacis rationem illustrans, belli immanitate damnata, christianos ferventer evocare intendit ut, auxiliante Christo auctore pacis, cum omnibus hominibus ad pacem in iustitia et amore inter eos firmandam et ad instrumenta pacis apparanda cooperentur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p482","On the nature of peace","79 Although recent wars have inflicted most grievous damages, both material and moral, on our world, war still pursues its devastations daily in some part of the world. Indeed, while scientific weapons of every kind are used in war, its cruel character threatens to lead combatants to barbarism which far surpasses that of past times. Moreover, the complexity of the present condition and the intrication of relations between nations permit that, by new methods, insidious and subversive, disguised wars be prolonged. In many circumstances, the use of terrorism methods is held as a new manner of waging war. Having before his eyes this dejected state of humanity, the Council intends first of all to call to mind the permanent force of the natural law of nations and its universal principles. The very conscience of the human race more and more firmly proclaims these principles. Actions, therefore, which deliberately oppose them, and commands which prescribe such actions, are criminal, nor can blind obedience excuse those who obey them. Among these actions, those are first to be counted by which, by a certain reason and method, a whole people, nation, or ethnic minority are exterminated: which as horrendous crimes are to be vehemently condemned. But most to be approved is the spirit of those who are not afraid openly to resist those commanding such things. There exist various international conventions concerning wars to which many nations have subscribed, to make military actions and their consequences less inhuman: such are the conventions which concern the lot of wounded or captive soldiers, and various stipulations of this kind. These pacts are to be kept; indeed, all, especially public authorities and those skilled in these matters, are bound to strive as much as they can that they be perfected and thus better and more effectively contribute to restraining the immanity of wars. Moreover, it seems equitable that laws humanely provide for the case of those who, for reason of conscience, refuse to use arms, while nevertheless accepting another form of service to the community of men. War, of course, has not been eradicated from human things. But as long as the peril of war shall remain, and the competent international authority be lacking, so long, all peaceful means of negotiation having indeed been exhausted, the right of legitimate defense cannot be denied to governments. It therefore pertains to the rulers of states and to others who share the responsibility of the republic, gravely conducting such grave matters, to protect the safety of the peoples entrusted to them. But it is one thing to wage military affairs to defend a people justly, another to wish to subjugate other nations. Nor does military power make all its military or political use legitimate. Nor, war having already unfortunately broken out, by that very fact all things become lawful between the adverse parties.","Pax non est mera absentia belli, neque ad solum adversarum virium aequilibrium stabiliendum reducitur neque ex imperioso dominatu oritur, sed recte proprieque dicitur opus iustitiae (Is 32,7). Fructus exsistit ordinis humanae societati a divino suo Fundatore insiti et ab hominibus perfectiorem semper iustitiam sitientibus in actum deducendi. Cum enim generis humani bonum commune primaria quidem sua ratione lege aeterna regatur, sed, quoad id quod concrete exigit, progrediente tempore incessantibus mutationibus subiciatur, numquam pax pro semper acquisita est, sed perpetuo aedificanda. Cum insuper labilis sit humana voluntas necnon peccato sauciata, procuratio pacis constantem uniuscuiusque exposcit passionum dominationem et legitimae auctoritatis invigilantiam. Hoc tamen non sufficit. Pax haec in terris obtineri non potest nisi bonum personarum in tuto collocetur et homines cum fiducia divitias sui animi atque ingenii inter se sponte communicent. Firma voluntas alios homines et populos eorumque dignitatem reverendi studiosumque fraternitatis exercitium ad pacem construendam omnino necessaria sunt. Ita pax fructus etiam amoris exsistit, qui ultra ea progreditur quae iustitia praestare valet. Pax autem terrena, quae ex dilectione proximi oritur, figura et effectus est pacis Christi, a Deo Patre promanantis. Ipse enim Filius incarnatus, princeps pacis, per crucem suam omnes homines Deo reconciliavit ac, restituens omnium unitatem in uno Populo et uno Corpore, in propria sua carne occidit odium, et, resurrectione exaltatus, Spiritum caritatis in corda hominum diffudit. Quapropter omnes christiani enixe evocantur ut, veritatem facientes in caritate (Ep 4,15), cum hominibus vere pacificis sese uniant ad pacem implorandam et instaurandam. Eodem spiritu moti, non possumus non laudare eos, qui in iuribus vindicandis actioni violentae renuntiantes, ad media defensionis recurrunt quae ceteroquin etiam debilioribus praesto sunt, dummodo hoc sine laesione iurium et obligationum aliorum vel communitatis fieri possit. Quatenus homines peccatores sunt, eis imminet periculum belli, et usque ad adventum Christi imminebit; quatenus autem, caritate coniuncti, peccatum superant, superantur et violentiae, donec impleatur verbum: Conflabunt gladios suos in vomeres et lanceas suas in falces. Non levabit gens contra gentem gladium, nec exercebuntur ultra ad praelium (Is 2,4)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p483","Sectio I: ON AVOIDING WAR","Those who, devoted to the service of the fatherland, are in the army, should also hold themselves as ministers of the security and liberty of peoples, and, while they rightly exercise this office, truly contribute to establishing peace. But the horror and perversity of war are immensely increased by the growth of scientific arms. For military actions, using these arms, can cause immense indiscriminate destructions which therefore far exceed the limits of legitimate defense. Indeed, if these means, such as are already found in the arsenals of great nations, should be thoroughly used, from this the almost full and entirely reciprocal slaughter of each side would be had, to say nothing of the many devastations arising in the world and the deadly effects following from the use of such arms. All which force us to institute an examination of war with an altogether new mind. Let the men of this age know that they will render a grave account concerning their military actions. For from their present counsels the course of future times will much depend. These things having been attended to, this sacrosanct Synod, making its own the condemnations of total war already enunciated by recent Roman Pontiffs, declares: Every military action which tends to the indiscriminate destruction of whole cities or large regions with their inhabitants is a crime against God and against man himself, which is to be firmly and unhesitatingly condemned. The particular peril of modern war consists in this, that it offers an occasion to those who possess more recent scientific arms to perpetrate such crimes, and, by a certain inexorable connection, can impel human wills to most atrocious counsels. Lest this ever happen in the future, the bishops of the whole world gathered together, beseech all, especially the rulers of nations and those who are in charge of military affairs, that they unceasingly consider so great a responsibility before God and before the whole of humanity.","Quamvis recentia bella nostro mundo gravissima damna tum materialia tum moralia intulerint, adhuc cotidie in aliqua terrarum parte bellum suas vastationes persequitur. Immo, dum arma scientifica cuiuslibet generis in bello adhibentur, saeva eius indoles proeliantes ad barbariem adducere minatur quae illam anteactorum temporum longe superet. Porro condicionis hodiernae complexitas ac relationum inter nationes intricatio permittunt ut novis methodis, iisque insidiosis et subversivis, bella larvata protrahantur. In pluribus adiunctis usus methodorum terrorismi tamquam nova ratio bellandi habetur. Deiectum istum humanitatis statum prae oculis habens, Concilium ante omnia in memoriam revocare intendit permanentem vim iuris naturalis gentium eiusque principiorum universalium. Ipsa generis humani conscientia haec principia firmiter magis magisque proclamat. Actiones ergo quae iisdem deliberate adversantur necnon iussa quibus tales actiones praescribuntur scelesta sunt, nec caeca oboedientia illos qui iis parent excusare valet. Inter has actiones illae ante omnia recensendae sunt quibus, ratione quadam et methodo, universa gens, natio aut minoritas ethnica exterminantur: quae tamquam crimina horrenda vehementer condemnandae sunt. Maxime vero probandus est animus illorum qui talia praecipientibus aperte resistere non timescunt. Exstant de rebus bellicis variae conventiones internationales quibus sat multae nationes subscripserunt, ut minus inhumanae efficiantur actiones militares earumque sequelae: huiusmodi sunt conventiones quae pertinent ad militum vulneratorum aut captivorum sortem, variaeque huius generis stipulationes. Quae pactiones servandae sunt; immo tenentur omnes, praesertim auctoritates publicae et de his rebus periti, quantum possunt conari ut illae perficiantur sicque melius et efficacius ad bellorum immanitatem refrenandam conducant. Insuper aequum videtur ut leges humaniter provideant pro casu illorum qui ex motivo conscientiae arma adhibere recusant, dum tamen aliam formam communitati hominum serviendi acceptant. Utique bellum non est e rebus humanis eradicatum. Quamdiu autem periculum belli aderit, auctoritasque internationalis competens congruisque viribus munita defuerit, tamdiu, exhaustis quidem omnibus pacificae tractationis subsidiis, ius legitimae defensionis guberniis denegari non poterit. Civitatum rectoribus aliisque qui rei publicae responsabilitatem participant, incumbit igitur officium ut populorum sibi commissorum salutem tueantur, res tam graves graviter gerentes. At aliud est res militares gerere ut populi iuste defendantur, aliud alias nationes subiugare velle. Nec potentia bellica omnem eiusdem militarem vel politicum usum legitimum facit. Nec bello infeliciter iam exorto, eo ipso omnia inter partes adversas licita fiunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p484","Sectio I: ON AVOIDING WAR","81 Scientific arms are indeed accumulated not only to be used in time of war. For since the firmness of the defense of each side is thought to depend on the fulminant capacity to strike back at the adversary, this accumulation of arms, which grows worse every year, serves in an unusual way to deter possible insurgent adversaries. Which is held by many as the most effective of all means by which a certain peace among nations can now be placed in safety. Whatever may be of that dissuasive manner, let men be persuaded that the race to armaments, to which many nations have recourse, is not a secure way to keep peace firmly, nor is the so-called equilibrium resulting from that a certain and true peace. The causes of war from it, far from being eliminated, rather threaten to gradually become more severe. While abundant riches are spent on ever new armaments, a sufficient remedy for so many miseries of the whole world cannot be provided. Rather than truly and radically healing dissensions between nations, other parts of the world are infected by them. New ways, beginning from a reformed spirit, will have to be chosen to remove this scandal, and, the world being freed from the anxiety by which it is oppressed, true peace can be restored. Wherefore it must again be declared: the race to armaments is a most grave plague of humanity, and intolerably injures the poor. But it is greatly to be feared that, if it continues, it will at some time bring forth deadly calamities, the means of which it already prepares. Admonished by the calamities which the human race has made possible, let us use the respite granted to us from above, that, more conscious of our own responsibility, we may find ways by which we may be able to compose our controversies in a manner more worthy of man. Divine Providence urgently requires us to free ourselves from the ancient servitude of war. But if we refuse to make such an effort, we know not whither we are led on this evil way which we have entered.","Qui vero, patriae servitio addicti, in exercitu versantur, et ipsi tamquam securitatis libertatisque populorum ministros sese habeant, et, dum hoc munere recte funguntur, vere ad pacem stabiliendam conferunt. Horror pravitasque belli scientificorum armorum incremento in immensum augentur. Bellicae enim actiones, his armis adhibitis, ingentes indiscriminatasque inferre possunt destructiones quae proinde limites legitimae defensionis longe excedunt. Immo, si haec media, qualia iam in magnarum nationum armamentariis inveniuntur, penitus adhiberentur, ex eo internecio fere plena et omnino reciproca uniuscuiusque partis a parte adversa haberetur praetermissis multis vastationibus in mundo oboriundis et exitialibus effectibus ex usu huiusmodi armorum consequentibus. Quae omnia cogunt nos ut de bello examen mente omnino nova instituamus. Sciant huius aetatis homines se de suis bellicis actionibus gravem rationem esse reddituros. Ab eorum enim hodiernis consiliis temporum futurorum decursus multum pendebit. His attentis, haec Sacrosancta Synodus, suas faciens condemnationes belli totalis iam a recentibus Summis Pontificibus enuntiatas, declarat: Omnis actio bellica quae in urbium integrarum vel amplarum regionum cum earum incolis destructionem indiscriminatim tendit, est crimen contra Deum et ipsum hominem, quod firmiter et incunctanter damnandum est. Singulare belli hodierni periculum in hoc consistit quod illis qui recentiora arma scientifica possident quasi occasionem praebet talia scelera perpetrandi et, connexione quadam inexorabili, hominum voluntates ad atrocissima consilia impellere potest. Ne vero hoc in futurum unquam eveniat, Episcopi totius orbis terrarum in unum congregati, omnes, nationum moderatores praesertim, necnon eos qui rei militari praesunt, obsecrant, ut tantam responsabilitatem coram Deo et coram universa humanitate incessanter perpendant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p485","On the race to armaments","82 It is clear, therefore, that we must strive with all our strength to prepare times in which, with the consent of nations, any war whatsoever can be altogether forbidden. Which indeed requires that some universal public authority, acknowledged by all, be instituted, which may have effective power to place safety, observance of justice, and reverence for rights in safety for all. But before such a desirable authority can be instituted, it is necessary that the present supreme international bodies ardently devote themselves to the studies of means more apt for procuring common security. Since peace must arise from mutual trust of nations rather than be imposed on nations by the terror of arms, all must strive that the race to armaments finally have an end; that the diminution of arms really begin, not unilaterally indeed, but proceeding step by step from agreement, furnished with true and effective cautions. In the meantime, the attempts which have already been made and are still being made to remove the peril of war are not to be made little of. Rather, the good will of very many is to be helped, who, burdened with the greatest cares of their highest offices, moved by the most grave duty by which they are bound, strive to eliminate the war they abhor, even if they cannot prescind from the complexity of things as they are. But we must earnestly pray to God that He give them the strength to persevere in undertaking and to fortify in perfecting this work of highest love of men, by which peace is manfully built. Which today most certainly requires that they extend their mind and spirit beyond the boundaries of their own nation, lay down national selfishness and ambition to dominate other nations, and nourish a deep reverence for the whole of humanity, which is already so laboriously hastening to its greater unity. Concerning the problems of peace and disarmament, investigations already strenuously and unceasingly protracted, and international congresses which have dealt with this matter, are to be considered as first steps to solving so grave questions, and are to be more urgently promoted in the future to obtain practical effects. Nevertheless, let men take care not to commit themselves only to the efforts of some, without taking care of their own minds. For the rulers of peoples, who are sponsors of the common good of their own nation and at the same time promoters of the good of the whole world, depend most greatly on the opinions and feelings of the multitudes. Nothing profits them to strive to build peace, as long as feelings of hostility, contempt, and distrust, racial hatreds, and obstinate ideologies divide men and oppose them to one another. Hence the greatest necessity urgently demands a renewed education of minds and a new inspiration in public opinion. Those who devote themselves to the work of education, especially of the young, or to form public opinion, should regard as a most grave office the care of instructing the minds of all to new peaceful senses. We all must indeed change our hearts, looking to the whole world and to those offices which we, together, can do to make our race advance to better things. Nor let false hope deceive us. Unless, enmities and hatreds having been laid aside, firm and honest pacts of universal peace be concluded in the future, humanity, which is already in grave crisis, although endowed with wonderful science, may perhaps be led fatally to that hour in which it may experience no other peace than the horrendous peace of death. Nevertheless, while uttering this, the Church of Christ, placed in the midst of the anxiety of this time, does not cease most firmly to hope. To our age she intends to propose, opportunely and importunely, the apostolic message: \"Behold, now is the acceptable time\" for hearts to be changed, \"behold, now is the day of salvation.\"","Arma quidem scientifica non ad hoc unice accumulantur ut tempore belli adhibeantur. Cum enim firmitas uniuscuiusque partis defensionis a capacitate fulminea adversarium repercutiendi dependere aestimetur, haec armorum accumulatio, quae quotannis ingravescit, inconsueto quidem modo ad deterrendos adversarios forte insurgentes inservit. Quod a multis habetur tamquam omnium mediorum efficacissimum quibus nunc pax quaedam inter nationes in tuto poni possit. Quidquid sit de illo dissuasioni modo, persuasum habeant homines cursum ad arma apparanda, ad quem sat multae nationes confugiunt, non securam esse viam ad pacem firmiter servandam, neque sic dictum aequilibrium ex illo manans certam ac veram esse pacem. Belli exinde causae quin eliminentur, potius paulatim aggravari minantur. Dum in arma semper nova apparanda perabundantes divitiae erogantur, tot miseriis hodiernis mundi universi remedium sufficiens praeberi non potest. Potius quam dissensiones inter nationes vere ac funditus sanentur, iisdem aliae mundi partes inficiuntur. Novae viae, ex reformato animo initium sumentes, eligendae erunt ut hoc scandalum removeatur et, mundo ab anxietate qua opprimitur liberato, vera pax restitui possit. Quapropter denuo declarandum est: cursum ad arma apparanda gravissimam plagam humanitatis esse, ac pauperes intolerabiliter laedere. Valde autem timendum est ne, si perduret, aliquando omnes exitiales clades pariat, quarum media iam praeparat. Calamitatibus commonefacti quas humanum genus possibiles effecit, moram, nobis desuper concessam, qua gaudemus, adhibeamus ut propriae responsabilitatis magis conscii, vias inveniamus quibus controversias nostras modo homine digniore componere possimus. Providentia divina a nobis instanter requirit ut nosmetipsos ab antiqua belli servitute liberemus. Quod si huiusmodi conamen facere renuerimus, quo ducamur in hac via mala quam ingressi sumus, nescimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p486","On war to be altogether forbidden and on international action to avoid war","83 To build peace, first of all the causes of discords between men, by which wars are nourished, especially injustices, are to be eradicated. Not a few of them arise from excessive economic inequalities, and also from retarded necessary remedies. Others, however, arise from the spirit of domination and contempt of persons, and, if we inquire into deeper causes, from human envy, distrust, pride, and other selfish passions. Since man cannot bear so many defects of order, from this it follows that, even war not raging, the world is unceasingly infected with contentions and violences between men. Since, moreover, the same evils are also found in relations between nations themselves, it is altogether necessary that, to overcome or prevent them, and to restrain unbridled violences, international institutions cooperate and coordinate better and more firmly, and the creation of organisms to promote peace be unceasingly stimulated.","Patet ergo nobis enitendum esse ut viribus omnibus tempora praeparemus quibus, consentientibus nationibus, bellum quodlibet omnino interdici possit. Quod sane requirit ut quaedam publica auctoritas universalis, ab omnibus agnita, instituatur, quae efficaci potestate polleat ut pro omnibus tum securitas, tum iustitiae observantia, tum iurium reverentia in tuto ponantur. Antequam vero haec optanda auctoritas institui possit, opus est ut hodierna suprema gremia internationalia studiis mediorum ad securitatem communem procurandam aptiorum acriter se dedicent. Cum pax e mutua gentium fiducia nasci oporteat potius quam armorum terrore nationibus imponi, omnibus adlaborandum est ut cursus ad arma apparanda finem tandem habeat; ut diminutio armorum re incipiat, non unilateraliter quidem sed pari passu ex condicto progrediatur, veris efficacibusque cautionibus munita. Interea non parvipendenda sunt conamina quae iam facta sunt et adhuc fiunt ut periculum belli amoveatur. Potius adiuvanda est bona voluntas permultorum qui, ingentibus suorum supremorum munerum curis onerati, gravissimo autem quo obstringuntur officio moti, bellum quod abhorrent eliminare satagunt, etiamsi a complexitate rerum quales sunt, praescindere non possunt. Deum autem enixe rogare oportet ut illis vim det perseveranter aggrediendi ac fortiter perficiendi hoc summi amoris hominum opus quo pax viriliter aedificatur. Quod hodie certissime ab eis exigit ut mentem et spiritum ultra fines propriae nationis extendant, egoismum nationalem atque ambitionem aliis nationibus dominandi deponant, profundamque reverentiam erga totam humanitatem nutriant, quae iam ad maiorem sui unitatem tam laboriose properat. Circa pacis et armorum depositionis problemata perscrutationes iam strenue et indesinenter protractae, internationalesque congressus, qui hac de re egerunt, tamquam primi passus ad solvendas tantopere graves quaestiones consideranda sunt atque urgentiori modo ad obtinendos effectus practicos in futurum promovenda. Nihilominus caveant homines ne solummodo conatibus quorumdam se committant, quin de propriis mentibus curent. Nam populorum moderatores, qui boni communis propriae gentis sponsores sunt et simul boni universi orbis promotores, ex multitudinum opinionibus et animi sensibus quam maxime pendent. Nihil eis prodest ut paci aedificandae instent, quamdiu hostilitatis, contemptus et diffidentiae sensus, racialia odia necnon obstinatae ideologiae, homines dividunt atque inter se opponunt. Hinc maxima necessitas urget renovatae mentium educationis novaeque in publica opinione inspirationis. Qui operi educationis se devovent, praesertim iuvenum, aut opinionem publicam efformant, tamquam gravissimum officium reputent curam mentium omnium ad novos pacificos sensus instituendi. Nos omnes quidem commutare corda nostra oportet, universum orbem et illa munera prospicientes quae nos, una simul, agere possumus ut genus nostrum ad meliora proficiat. Nec falsa spes nos decipiat. Nisi enim, dimissis inimicitiis et odiis, firma honestaque pacta de pace universali in futuro concludantur, humanitas quae iam in gravi discrimine versatur, quamvis scientia mirabili praedita, forsan funeste ad illam horam adducetur in qua non aliam pacem quam horrendam mortis pacem experiatur. Attamen, dum haec profert, Ecclesia Christi, in media anxietate huius temporis constituta, firmissime sperare non cessat. Aetati nostrae iterum iterumque, opportune importune, nuntium apostolicum proponere intendit: ecce nunc tempus acceptabile ut mutentur corda, ecce nunc dies salutis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p487","Sectio II: ON BUILDING THE INTERNATIONAL COMMUNITY","84 So that, the close bonds of mutual necessity increasing in this time between all citizens and all peoples of the world, the universal common good may be aptly sought and more effectively obtained, it is already necessary that the community of nations constitute for itself an order which is congruous with present offices, especially relating to those many regions which still suffer intolerable poverty. To achieve these ends, the institutions of the international community must for their part provide for various needs of men, both in the fields of social life to which pertain livelihood, health, education, labor, and in certain particular conditions which can arise elsewhere, such as the necessity of favoring the general development of progressing nations, of meeting the miseries of refugees scattered through the whole world, or of helping migrants and their families. International institutions, universal or regional, which already exist, certainly well deserve of the human race. They appear as first attempts to lay international foundations of the whole human community to solve the most grave questions of our times, and indeed to promote progress everywhere and to prevent wars in any form. In all these fields, the Church rejoices in the spirit of true fraternity flourishing between Christians and non-Christians which strives that efforts always be intensified to relieve immense misery.","Ad pacem aedificandam ante omnia requiritur ut eradicentur causae discordiarum inter homines, quibus bella aluntur, praesertim iniustitiae. Non paucae earum ex nimiis inaequalitatibus oeconomicis proveniunt, necnon ex necessariis remediis retardatis. Aliae vero ex spiritu dominationis et personarum contemptu oriuntur et, si in causas profundiores inquirimus, ex humana invidia, diffidentia, superbia aliisque egoisticis passionibus. Cum tot ordinis defectus homo ferre non possit, ex iis consequitur ut, etiam bello non saeviente, mundus indesinenter contentionibus inter homines et violentiis inficiatur. Cum insuper eadem mala in relationibus inter ipsas nationes inveniantur, necessarium omnino est ut, ad illa vincenda vel praevenienda, et ad effrenatas violentias coercendas, melius et firmius cooperentur et coordinentur institutiones internationales necnon indefesse stimuletur creatio organismorum qui pacem promoveant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p488","On the community of nations and on international institutions","85 The present conjunction of the human race also demands the restoration of a greater international cooperation in the economic field. For, although almost all peoples have become independent, they are far, however, from being already free from excessive inequalities and from all form of undue dependence, nor do they altogether avoid the peril of grave internal difficulties. The development of any nation depends on human and pecuniary aids. The citizens of each nation are to be prepared by education and professional formation for assuming various offices of economic and social life. To this, however, the aid of foreign experts is required, who, while they give help, should not conduct themselves as masters but as helpers and cooperators. Material aid to progressing nations will not be procured unless the customs of present commerce in the world be profoundly changed. Other aids besides are to be provided by progressed nations in the form of gifts or loans or investments of money; which are to be provided with generosity and without cupidity on the one hand, received with all honesty on the other. To restore a true universal economic order, excessive desires of profit, national ambitions, the appetite for political domination, military calculations, and machinations to propagate and impose ideologies are to be abolished. Many economic and social systems are proposed; it is to be desired that in these the skilled find common foundations of sound world commerce; which will happen more easily if each rejects his own prejudices and makes himself ready to sincerely pursue dialogue.","Ut, crescentibus hoc tempore arctis mutuae necessitudinis nexibus inter omnes cives et omnes populos orbis terrarum, bonum commune universale apte quaeratur et efficacius obtineatur, iam necesse est communitatem gentium sibi constituere ordinem qui cum hodiernis muneribus congruat, praesertim relate ad illas numerosas regiones quae intolerabilem egestatem adhuc patiuntur. Ad hos fines assequendos, institutiones communitatis internationalis variis hominum necessitatibus pro sua parte providere debent, tam in vitae socialis campis ad quos pertinent victus, sanitas, educatio, labor, quam in nonnullis condicionibus particularibus quae alicubi oriri possunt, ut sunt necessitas generali nationum progredientium incremento fovendi, aerumnis profugorum per universum mundum dispersorum occurrendi, vel etiam migrantes eorumque familias adiuvandi. Institutiones internationales, universales vel regionales, quae iam exsistunt certe de genere humano bene merentur. Eaedem tamquam primi conatus apparent internationalia fundamenta totius communitatis humanae ponendi ut gravissimae nostrorum temporum quaestiones solvantur, et quidem ad progressum ubique terrarum promovendum et ad bella in quacumque forma praecavenda. In omnibus istis campis gaudet Ecclesia de spiritu verae fraternitatis inter christianos et non christianos florentis qui enititur ut conamina semper intensiora fiant ad ingentem miseriam sublevandam."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p489","On international cooperation in the economic field","86 For this cooperation, the following norms seem opportune: a) Progressing nations should greatly take care that as the end of progress they expressly and firmly aspire to the full human perfection of their citizens. Let them be mindful that progress arises and grows from labor and above all from the genius of the nations themselves, which must be founded primarily not on foreign resources alone, but on their own fully developed genius and on cultivating their own genius and tradition. In this matter, those who have greater influence on others ought to excel. b) The most grave duty of progressed nations is to help progressing peoples to fulfill the above offices. Therefore, let them perfect among themselves the mental and material accommodations which are required for establishing this universal cooperation. Thus in commerce with weaker and poorer nations let them sedulously regard the good of those; for these need the proceeds received from the sale of things produced by them for their own sustenance. c) It is the part of the international community to compose and stimulate development, yet in such a way that resources ordered for this are disposed of as effectively as possible and with full equity. It also pertains to this community, the principle of subsidiarity being preserved, to order economic ways throughout the world that they be developed to the norm of justice. Apt institutions are to be established to promote and order international affairs, especially with less progressed nations, and to compensate for defects which flow from excessive inequality of power between nations. Such ordering, together with technical, cultural, and monetary aids, ought to provide necessary subsidies to nations striving for development, that they may be able conveniently to attain the increase of their own economy. d) In many cases the need to recognize economic and social structures presses; but care is to be taken from solutions technically immaturely proposed, especially from those which, while they provide material advantages to man, oppose his spiritual character and progress. For man does not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God (Mt 4:4). But every part of the human family in itself and in its better traditions bears with it a part of the spiritual treasure entrusted by God to humanity, although many do not know from what source it proceeds.","Hodierna generis humani coniunctio etiam instaurationem maioris cooperationis internationalis in campo oeconomico expostulat. Etenim, etsi omnes fere populi sui iuris facti sunt, longe tamen abest ut a nimiis inaequalitatibus et ab omni indebitae dependentiae forma iam liberi sint omneque gravium difficultatum internarum periculum effugiant. Incrementum alicuius nationis ex adiumentis humanis et pecuniariis pendet. Cives uniuscuiusque nationis per educationem et formationem professionalem ad varia munera vitae oeconomicae et socialis obeunda praeparandi sunt. Ad hoc autem requiritur auxilium peritorum alienigenarum qui, dum opem ferunt, non ut dominatores se gerant sed ut adiutores et cooperatores. Auxilium materiale nationibus progredientibus non procurabitur, nisi consuetudines hodierni commercii in mundo profunde mutentur. Alia insuper auxilia a nationibus progressis praestanda sunt sub forma sive donorum sive mutuorum sive pecuniarum collocationum; quae praestentur cum generositate et sine cupiditate ex una parte, accipiantur cum omni honestate ex altera. Ad verum ordinem oeconomicum universalem instaurandum abolenda sunt nimia lucrorum studia, ambitiones nationales, appetitus dominationis politicae, calculi ordinis militaristici necnon machinationes ad ideologias propagandas et imponendas. Plura oeconomica et socialia systemata proponuntur; optandum est ut in his periti communia fundamenta sani commercii mundialis inveniant; quod facilius continget si singuli propria praeiudicia abnuant et ad dialogum sincere gerendum promptos se praebeant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p490","On certain opportune norms","87 International cooperation becomes very necessary relating to those peoples who today rather frequently, besides so many other difficulties, are especially pressed by those which arise from rapid population increase. The necessity urges that, by full and eager cooperation of all, especially of richer nations, it be explored how those things which are necessary for food and for suitable instruction of men can be prepared and communicated with the whole human community. Some peoples, however, could make their conditions of life much better if, instructed with due instruction, they would pass from ancient methods for producing agricultural goods to new technical arts, applying them with necessary prudence to their conditions, a better social order being moreover restored and the distribution of land possession more equitably ordered. Governments indeed have the rights and duties, as to the problem of population in their nation, within the limits of their competence; for example, concerning social legislation and legislation concerning the family, concerning the migration of farmers to cities, concerning information regarding the state and needs of the nation. Since the minds of men are so vehemently agitated concerning this problem today, it is also to be desired that concerning all these things catholic experts, especially in universities, skillfully prosecute and more widely develop studies and undertakings. Since, however, it is affirmed by many that the increase of the world's population, or at least of certain nations, is altogether to be diminished by all means and every kind of intervention of public authority, the Council exhorts all to take care from solutions, promoted or imposed publicly or privately, which contradict the moral law. For according to the inalienable right of man to marriage and to the generation of offspring, the deliberation concerning the number of offspring to be begotten depends on the right judgment of parents and can by no means be entrusted to the judgment of public authority. Since, however, the judgment of parents presupposes a rightly formed conscience, it is of great importance that access be given to all to cultivate a right and truly human responsibility which regards the divine law, the circumstances of things and times having been attended to; this indeed demands that pedagogical and social conditions be everywhere changed for the better and above all that religious formation or at least whole moral instruction be provided. Moreover, concerning scientific advances in exploring methods by which spouses can be helped in ordering the number of offspring, whose firmness is well tested and whose congruence with the moral order is known, let men be wisely informed.","Pro hac cooperatione sequentes normae opportunae videntur: a) Gentes progredientes valde cordi habeant ut tamquam finem progressus expresse et firmiter plenam perfectionem humanam suorum civium appetant. Meminerint ex labore ante omnia et ingenio ipsarum gentium oriri et crescere progressum quippe qui non solis opibus alienis, sed propriis plene explicandis necnon ingenio et traditione propria colendis imprimis inniti debeat. Qua in re illi excellant oportet, qui maiorem influxum in alios exercent. b) Gentium autem progressarum officium gravissimum est progredientes populos ad supradicta munera implenda adiuvandi. Quapropter mentales et materiales accommodationes, quae ad hanc universalem cooperationem stabiliendam requiruntur, apud seipsas perficiant. Ita in negotiatione cum debilioribus et pauperioribus nationibus bonum illarum sedulo respiciant; nam hae proventibus quos ex venditione rerum a se productarum accipiunt, ad propriam suam sustentationem indigent. c) Communitatis vero internationalis est incrementum componere et stimulare, ita tamen ut de opibus ad hoc ordinatis quam efficacissime et plena cum aequitate disponatur. Ad hanc quoque communitatem pertinet, principio subsidiarietatis utique servato, rationes oeconomicas in toto mundo ordinare ut ad normam iustitiae explicentur. Condantur instituta apta ad promovenda et ordinanda negotia internationalia, praesertim cum nationibus minus progressis et ad compensandos defectus qui ex nimia inaequalitate potentiae inter nationes manant. Huiusmodi ordinatio, cum adiumentis technicis, culturalibus et nummariis coniuncta, nationibus ad progressum intendentibus subsidia necessaria praestare debet ut incrementa oeconomiae suae convenienter consequi valeant. d) In multis casibus urget necessitas recognoscendi oeconomicas socialesque structuras; sed cavendum est a solutionibus technicis immature propositis, imprimis ab illis quae, dum homini commoda materialia praebent, eius spirituali indoli ac profectui adversantur. Nam non in solo pane vivit homo, sed in omni verbo quod procedit de ore Dei (Mt 4,4). Quaelibet autem humanae familiae pars in seipsa et in suis melioribus traditionibus aliquam partem thesauri spiritualis a Deo humanitati concrediti secumfert, etsi multi nesciunt ex qua origine procedat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p491","On international cooperation concerning population increase","88 To build the international order with true respect for legitimate freedoms and amicable fraternity of all, Christians should willingly and with whole heart cooperate, and this the more because the greater part of the world still labors under such poverty that Christ Himself in the poor as if with a loud voice calls upon the charity of His disciples. Lest it be a scandal to men that some nations, whose greater number of citizens is often adorned with the Christian name, abound with an abundance of goods, while others are deprived of things necessary for life and are tormented by hunger, diseases, and all misery. For the spirit of poverty and charity is the glory and testimony of the Church of Christ. Let those Christians therefore be praised and helped, especially the young, who spontaneously offer themselves to provide aids to other men and peoples. Indeed, it is the part of the whole People of God, the bishops leading the way by word and example, to relieve the miseries of this time according to their strength, and this, as the ancient custom of the Church bore, not only from the superfluous but even from the substance. The manner of collecting and distributing subsidies, not being rigidly and uniformly ordered, is to be disposed in right order in dioceses, nations, and the whole world, with the united action of Catholics with other Christian brothers, wherever it seems opportune. For the spirit of charity does not forbid, but rather imposes, the provident and orderly exercise of social and charitable action. Therefore it is necessary that those who intend to devote themselves to serving progressing nations be also duly formed by suitable institutions.","Maxime necessaria evadit cooperatio internationalis relate ad illos populos qui hodie sat frequenter, praeter tot alias difficultates, ea peculiariter premuntur quae ex rapido incremento populationis oritur. Urget necessitas ut per plenam et impensam cooperationem omnium, praesertim ditiorum nationum, exploretur quomodo ea quae ad victum et ad congruam instructionem hominum necessaria sunt, parari et cum tota communitate humana communicari possint. Nonnulli vero populi suae vitae condiciones multo meliores reddere possent si, debita instructione exculti, a methodis antiquis pro bonis agrariis gignendis ad novas artes technicas transirent, eas cum necessaria prudentia suis condicionibus applicantes, ordine sociali meliori insuper instaurato et terrarum possessionis distributione aequius ordinata. Gubernii quidem sunt iura et officia, problema populationis in sua natione quod attinet, intra propriae competentiae limites; utputa in ordine ad legislationem socialem et ad familias respicientem, ad transitum ruricolarum ad urbes, ad informationes circa statum et necessitates nationis. Cum hodie mentes hominum de hoc problemate tam vehementer agitentur, optandum quoque est ut de his omnibus periti catholici, praesertim in Universitatibus, studia et incepta sollerter prosequantur et latius evolvant. Cum autem a multis affirmetur incolarum orbis incrementum, vel saltem quarumdam nationum, omnibus mediis et cuiusvis generis interventu auctoritatis publicae funditus omnino minuendum esse, Concilium omnes hortatur ut caveant a solutionibus, publice vel privatim promotis et quandoque impositis, quae legi morali contradicunt. Nam iuxta inalienabile hominis ius ad matrimonium et generationem prolis, deliberatio circa numerum prolis gignendae a recto iudicio parentum pendet ac nullo modo auctoritatis publicae iudicio committi potest. Cum autem parentum iudicium conscientiam recte formatam supponat, magni momenti est ut omnibus aditus praebeatur ad colendam rectam et vere humanam responsabilitatem quae legem divinam, attentis adiunctis rerum et temporum, respiciat; hoc vero exigit ut passim condiciones paedagogicae et sociales in melius mutentur et imprimis ut formatio religiosa vel saltem integra moralis institutio praebeatur. De progressibus porro scientificis in explorandis methodis quibus coniuges iuvari possint in ordinando numero prolis, quarum firmitas bene probata est et congruentia cum ordine morali comperta habetur, homines sapienter certiores fiant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p492","On the office of Christians in providing subsidies","89 The Church, since by her divine mission she preaches the Gospel to all men and imparts the treasures of grace, everywhere contributes to establishing peace and laying a solid foundation for the fraternal association of men and peoples: namely the knowledge of the divine and natural law. Wherefore the Church ought to be altogether present in the international community itself to foster and excite cooperation among men; and indeed both through her public institutions and through the full and sincere collaboration of all Christians, inspired by the sole desire to serve all. Which will be more effectively attained if the faithful themselves, conscious of their human and Christian responsibility, already in their own sphere strive to excite the will readily to cooperate with the international community. Special care in this matter is to be spent on forming the young, both in religious and in civil education.","Ad ordinem internationalem cum vera observantia legitimarum libertatum et amica fraternitate omnium aedificandum, christiani libenter et toto corde cooperentur, idque eo magis quod maior pars mundi tanta adhuc egestate laborat ut in pauperibus Christus Ipse quasi alta voce caritatem suorum discipulorum evocet. Ne igitur scandalo sit hominibus aliquas nationes, quarum saepius maior numerus civium christiano nomine ornatur, bonorum copia abundare, dum aliae rebus ad vitam necessariis priventur ac fame, morbis omnimodaque miseria cruciantur. Sunt enim spiritus paupertatis et caritatis gloria et testimonium Ecclesiae Christi. Laudandi igitur et adiuvandi sunt illi christiani, iuvenes praesertim, qui sponte seipsos ad aliis hominibus et populis auxilia praestanda offerunt. Immo totius Populi Dei est, Episcopis verbo et exemplo praeeuntibus, miserias huius temporis pro viribus sublevare, idque, ut antiquus mos ferebat Ecclesiae, non ex superfluis tantum, sed etiam ex substantia. Modus subsidia colligendi et distribuendi, quin sit rigide et uniformiter ordinatus, recto tamen ordine disponatur in dioecesibus, nationibus et in universo mundo, coniuncta, ubicumque opportunum videtur, actione catholicorum cum ceteris fratribus christianis. Spiritus enim caritatis providum ordinatumque actionis socialis et caritativae exercitium nedum prohibeat, potius id imponit. Quare necesse est eos qui se ad nationibus progredientibus inserviendum devovere intendunt, idoneis etiam institutis apte efformari."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p493","On the efficacious presence of the Church in the international community","90 An excellent form of international activity of Christians is without doubt the cooperative work which, whether individually or associated, they provide in the Institutions themselves established or to be established for promoting cooperation between nations. To build the community of nations in peace and fraternity, various catholic international associations can also serve in many ways, which are to be strengthened, increased in number of well-formed cooperators, with the aids they need and with apt coordination of forces. For in our times, both the efficacy of actions and the necessity of dialogue demand associated undertakings. Such associations also contribute not a little to cultivating the universal sense, certainly congruent with Catholics, and to forming a consciousness of truly universal solidarity and responsibility. Finally, it is to be desired that Catholics, to rightly fulfill their office in the international community, strive to cooperate actively and positively both with separated brothers who together with them profess evangelical charity, and with all men thirsting for true peace. The Council, however, regard being had to the immensity of the miseries by which the greater part of the human race is still vexed, and to foster justice at the same time as the love of Christ toward the poor everywhere, judges it very opportune to create some universal organism of the Church, whose office be to excite the catholic community to promote the progress of needy regions and social justice between nations.","Ecclesia, cum, divina sua missione innixa, omnibus hominibus Evangelium praedicat et thesauros gratiae elargitur, ubique terrarum ad pacem firmandam et solidum fundamentum ponendum consortionis fraternae hominum et populorum confert: cognitionem scilicet legis divinae et naturalis. Quapropter Ecclesia in ipsa communitate gentium omnino praesens esse debet ad cooperationem inter homines fovendam et excitandam; et quidem tam per suas institutiones publicas quam per plenam ac sinceram collaborationem omnium christianorum, solo desiderio omnibus inserviendi inspiratam. Quod efficacius attingetur si ipsi fideles, suae responsabilitatis humanae et christianae conscii, iam in proprio ambitu vitae voluntatem prompte cooperandi cum communitate internationali excitare satagunt. Cura peculiaris hac in re iuvenibus formandis impendatur, tam in educatione religiosa quam civili."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p494","On the parts of Christians in international institutions","91 Those things which are set forth by this sacred Synod from the treasures of the doctrine of the Church intend to help all men of our time, whether they believe in God or do not explicitly acknowledge Him, that, perceiving more clearly their whole vocation, they may conform the world more to the eminent dignity of man, aspire to a more universal and more deeply founded fraternity, and, under the impulse of love, respond to the urgent demands of our age by generous and associated effort. Indeed, before the immense diversity both of the state of things and of forms of human culture in the world, this proposition in many of its parts intentionally carries a merely general character: indeed, although it puts forth doctrine already received in the Church, since it often concerns matters subject to unceasing evolution, it will still need to be prosecuted and amplified. We trust, however, that many things which we have set forth founded on the word of God and the spirit of the Gospel, can bring strong help to all, especially after adaptation to individual peoples and mentalities has been brought into act by Christ's faithful under the direction of pastors.","Praecellens quaedam forma navitatis internationalis christianorum absque dubio socia opera est quam, sive singuli sive consociati, in ipsis Institutis ad cooperationem inter nationes provehendam conditis vel condendis praestant. Communitati gentium in pace et fraternitate aedificandae insuper multipliciter inservire possunt variae consociationes catholicae internationales, quae roborandae sunt, auctis numero cooperatorum bene formatorum, subsidiis quibus indigent et apta virium coordinatione. Nostris enim temporibus et actionum efficacitas et colloquii necessitas consociata incepta postulant. Tales consociationes insuper haud parum conferunt ad universalem sensum excolendum, catholicis certe congruum, et ad formandam conscientiam vere universalis solidarietatis et responsabilitatis. Optandum denique est ut catholici, ad munus suum in communitate internationali rite implendum, actuose et positive cooperare studeant sive cum fratribus seiunctis qui una cum eis evangelicam caritatem profitentur, sive cum omnibus hominibus veram pacem sitientibus. Concilium vero, ratione habita immensitatis aerumnarum quibus maior pars generis humani etiam nunc vexatur, et ad iustitiam simul ac amorem Christi erga pauperes ubique fovendum, valde opportunum aestimat creationem alicuius Ecclesiae universalis organismi, cuius sit catholicorum communitatem excitare ut progressus indigentium regionum necnon iustitia socialis inter nationes promoveantur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-5/index.html","p495","On the office of individual faithful and particular churches","92 The Church, by virtue of her mission of illuminating the whole world with the gospel message and of gathering all men of any nation, race, or culture into one Spirit, becomes a sign of that fraternity which permits and strengthens sincere dialogue. Which, however, requires that first of all in the Church herself we promote mutual esteem, reverence, and concord, all legitimate diversity being acknowledged, to ever more fruitfully institute dialogue among all who constitute one People of God, whether pastors or other Christ's faithful. For stronger are those things which unite the faithful than those which divide them: let there be unity in necessary things, freedom in doubtful things, charity in all things. Our mind at the same time embraces brothers not yet living in full communion with us and their communities, to whom we are nevertheless joined by confession of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit and by the bond of charity, mindful namely that the unity of Christians is also expected and desired today by many who do not believe in Christ. For by how much this unity, under the powerful power of the Holy Spirit, shall progress in truth and charity, by so much the more it will be a foretaste of unity and peace for the whole world. Wherefore, with united forces and in forms more and more apt for effectively attaining this glorious end, let us strive that, more and more conformed to the Gospel, we may fraternally cooperate to render service to the human family which, in Christ Jesus, is called into the family of the sons of God. We therefore also turn our mind to all who acknowledge God and in their traditions preserve precious religious and human elements, hoping that open dialogue may lead us all to faithfully receive the impulses of the Spirit and willingly fulfill them. The desire of such dialogue, which is led only by charity toward truth, due prudence being preserved, excludes no one on our part, neither those who cultivate the excellent goods of the human mind, but do not yet acknowledge their Author, nor those who oppose the Church and persecute her in various ways. Since God the Father is the beginning of all and the end, all are called to be brothers. And therefore, called by this same human and divine vocation, we can and ought to cooperate, without violence, without deceit, to build the world in true peace.","Ea quae ab hac Sacra Synodo ex thesauris doctrinae Ecclesiae proponuntur, omnes homines nostrorum temporum, sive in Deum credant sive Eum non explicite agnoscant, adiuvare intendunt ut, suam integram vocationem clarius percipientes, mundum praecellenti dignitati hominis magis conforment, universalem altiusque fundatam fraternitatem appetant atque, sub impulsu amoris, generoso atque consociato conamine, urgentibus nostrae aetatis postulationibus respondeant. Sane coram immensa diversitate tum rerum status tum culturae humanae formarum in mundo, propositio haec in compluribus suis partibus consulto nonnisi indolem generalem prae se fert: immo, licet doctrinam iam in Ecclesia receptam proferat, cum non raro de rebus incessanti evolutioni subiectis agitur, adhuc prosequenda et amplianda erit. Confidimus vero multa quae verbo Dei et spiritu Evangelii innixi protulimus, omnibus validum adiutorium conferre posse, praesertim postquam adaptatio ad singulas gentes et mentalitates a christifidelibus sub Pastorum moderamine ad actum deducta fuerit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p496","On dialogue between all men","93 Christians, mindful of the word of the Lord: \"By this shall all men know that you are my disciples, if you have love one for another\" (Jn 13:35), can desire nothing more ardently than to serve the men of this world ever more generously and effectively. Therefore, faithfully adhering to the Gospel and enjoying its forces, joined with all who love and cultivate justice, they have undertaken the great work on earth to be fulfilled, of which they must render an account to Him who will judge all on the last day. Not all who say \"Lord, Lord\" will enter the kingdom of heaven, but those who do the will of the Father and lay a strong hand to the work. The Father wills that we recognize Christ our brother in all men and love Him effectively, both by word and deed, thus bearing witness to the Truth, and with others communicating the mystery of the love of the heavenly Father. By this way, men will be aroused to a living hope throughout the whole world, which is the gift of the Holy Spirit, that at length in peace and highest happiness they may be received into the homeland which shines with the glory of the Lord.","Ecclesia, vi suae missionis universum orbem nuntio evangelico illuminandi et omnes homines cuiusvis nationis, stirpis vel culturae in unum Spiritum coadunandi, signum evadit illius fraternitatis quae sincerum dialogum permittit atque roborat. Quod autem requirit ut imprimis in ipsa Ecclesia mutuam aestimationem, reverentiam et concordiam promoveamus, omni legitima diversitate agnita, ad fructuosius semper colloquium inter omnes instituendum qui unum Populum Dei constituunt, sive pastores sive ceteri christifideles sint. Fortiora enim sunt ea quibus uniuntur fideles quam ea quibus dividuntur: sit in necessariis unitas, in dubiis libertas, in omnibus caritas. Animus autem noster simul complectitur fratres nondum nobiscum in plena communione viventes eorumque communitates, quibus tamen coniungimur confessione Patris et Filii et Spiritus Sancti ac vinculo caritatis, memores scilicet christianorum unitatem hodie etiam a multis in Christum non credentibus exspectari et desiderari. Quo magis enim haec unitas, sub potenti virtute Spiritus Sancti, in veritate et caritate proficiet, eo magis universo mundo erit praesagium unitatis et pacis. Quare, unitis viribus et in formis huic praeclaro fini hodie efficaciter assequendo magis magisque aptatis, studeamus ut, Evangelio in dies melius conformati, fraterne cooperemur ad servitium familiae humanae praestandum quae, in Christo Iesu, in familiam filiorum Dei vocatur. Animum nostrum proin etiam ad omnes convertimus qui Deum agnoscunt et in traditionibus suis pretiosa elementa religiosa et humana conservant, optantes ut apertum colloquium omnes nos adigat ad impulsiones Spiritus fideliter accipiendas et alacriter implendas. Desiderium talis colloquii, quod sola caritate erga veritatem ducatur, servata utique congrua prudentia, ex nostra parte neminem excludit, neque illos qui praeclara animi humani bona colunt, eorum vero Auctorem nondum agnoscunt, neque illos qui Ecclesiae opponuntur eamque variis modis persequuntur. Cum Deus Pater principium omnium exsistat et finis, omnes, ut fratres simus, vocamur. Et ideo, hac eadem humana et divina vocatione vocati, sine violentia, sine dolo ad aedificandum mundum in vera pace cooperari possumus et debemus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p497","On building and perfecting the world","","Christiani, memores verbi Domini in hoc cognoscent omnes quia discipuli mei estis, si dilectionem habueritis ad invicem (Jn 13,35), nihil ardentius optare possunt quam ut hominibus mundi huius temporis semper generosius et efficacius inserviant. Itaque, Evangelio fideliter adhaerentes eiusque viribus fruentes, cum omnibus qui iustitiam diligunt et colunt coniuncti, ingens opus in his terris adimplendum susceperunt, de quo Ei, qui omnes iudicabit ultimo die, rationem reddere debent. Non omnes qui dicunt: Domine, Domine, intrabunt in regnum caelorum, sed ii qui faciunt voluntatem Patris validamque manum operi apponunt. Vult autem Pater ut in omnibus hominibus Christum fratrem agnoscamus et efficaciter diligamus, tam verbo quam opere, ita testimonium perhibentes Veritati, et cum aliis mysterium amoris Patris caelestis communicemus. Hac via in toto orbe terrarum homines ad vivam spem excitabuntur, quae Spiritus Sancti donum est, ut tandem aliquando in pace ac beatitudine summa suscipiantur, in patria quae gloria Domini effulget."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p498","On building and perfecting the world","HOMILY OF THE SUPREME PONTIFF TO THE COUNCIL FATHERS, CELEBRATING THE CONCELEBRATION OF THE MASS, IN THE LAST PUBLIC SESSION OF THE ECUMENICAL SYNOD, IN WHICH THE DECLARATION ON RELIGIOUS LIBERTY, THE DECREES ON THE MISSIONARY ACTIVITY OF THE CHURCH AND ON THE MINISTRY AND LIFE OF PRESBYTERS, AND THE PASTORAL CONSTITUTION ON THE CHURCH IN THE MODERN WORLD WERE APPROVED AND PROMULGATED","HOMILIA AD PATRES CONCILIARES HABITA A SUMMO PONTEFICE, MISSAE CONCELEBRATIONE PERAGENTE, IN ULTIMA OECUMENICAE SYNODI PUBLICA SESSIONE, IN QUA DECLARATIO DE LIBERTATE RELIGIOSA, DECRETA DE ACTIVITATE MISSIONALI ECCLESIAE ET DE PRESBYTERORUM MINISTERIO ET VITA, CONSTITUTIO PASTORALIS DE ECCLESIA IN MUNDO HUIUS TEMPORIS APPROBATA ET PROMULGATA SUNT"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p499","Venerable Brothers,","Today we conclude the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council, and we conclude it most firmly and validly. Which both this most numerous gathering of yours demonstrates, and the ordered structure of this Assembly testifies, and the legitimate outcome of the Council's labors confirms, and the conspiracy of minds and wills as it were proclaims. But if not a few questions, posed during the celebration of the Council, still await to be congruently resolved, this certainly signifies that the Council concludes its labors not in the exhaustion of its forces, but rather in that vitality which this universal Synod has excited, and which, after the Council is dismissed, will turn itself wholly to resolving the same questions, with God's help. This Council of ours will hand down to posterity such an image of the Church as this hall represents, namely filled with sacred Pastors, who profess the same faith and breathe the same charity; who are joined with one another in the society of prayers, discipline, alacrity; who—which is indeed wonderful—all seek this one thing, that, not otherwise than Christ, our Master and Lord, they may devote themselves for the life of the Church and the salvation of the world. But the Council hands down to posterity not only an image of the Church, but also the patrimony of her doctrine and her precepts, that is, the deposit entrusted to her by Christ; this nations through the course of the ages have always meditated, have turned as it were into their own juice and blood, and have in a way expressed in their morals; this now, in many parts illustrated, is established and ordered in its integrity. This, since it is living on account of the divine power of truth and grace from which it consists, is therefore to be deemed fit to give life to any man who piously receives it and nourishes his life by it.","Hodie Concilium Oecumenicum Vaticanum secundum concludimus, illudque maxime adhuc firmum validumque concludimus. Quod et hic conspectus vester frequentissimus demonstrat, et ordinata huius Consessus compago testatur, et legitimus Concilii laborum exitus confirmat, et mentium voluntatumque conspiratio veluti proclamat. Quodsi quaestiones non paucae, inter Concilii celebrationem positae, adhuc exspectant donec congruenter dissolvantur, id profecto significat Concilium labores suos, non in virium suarum defatigatione concludere, sed in ea potius vivacitate, quam universalis haec Synodus excitavit, quaeque, post dimissum Concilium, Deo iuvante, ad easdem quaestiones dissolvendas se totam convertet. Concilium hoc nostrum posteris eiusmodi Ecclesiae imaginem tradet, qualem haec aula repraesentat, sacris videlicet referta Pastoribus, qui eandem fidem profitentur, eandemque caritatem spirant; qui societate precationum, disciplinae, alacritatis inter se coniuncti sunt, qui - quod mirum sane est - unum hoc expetunt omnes, ut, non secus atque Christus, Magister et Dominus noster, se pro vita Ecclesiae et mundi salute devoveant. At Concilium posteris non tantum imaginem Ecclesiae tradit, sed etiam patrimonium eius doctrinae eiusque praeceptorum, hoc est depositum a Christo ipsi commissum; hoc gentes per saeculorum decursum semper meditatae sunt, in suum quasi sucum et sanguinem verterunt, moribusque suis quodammodo expresserunt; hoc nunc, pluribus partibus illustratum, in sua integritate statutum atque ordinatum est. Hoc, cum vivum sit ob divinam veritatis et gratiae virtutem, ex qua constat, idcirco, idoneum est putandum quod hominem quemlibet vivificet, qui pie illud accipiat, eoque vitam suam alat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p500","Venerable Brothers,","What this Council has truly been, or what it has effected, would be the subject of this our final meditation. But that would demand too much attention and time; nor at this last solemn hour do we dare to comprehend matters of such weight in a brief space. We prefer therefore to devote these grave moments of time to such a reflection which at once inclines our minds to humility and at the same time lifts them up to our highest desires. We prefer, namely, to ask ourselves: what has been the religious significance of our Council? With what word does our mind intend to signify our relations with God, which certainly declare why the Church exists, what she believes, what she hopes, what she loves, what she is, what she does?","Quid re vera hoc Concilium fuerit, quidve ipsum effecerit, nostrae huius extremae meditationis argumentum esset. Sed nimium id attentionis et temporis postularet; neque hac suprema sollemnique hora audemus res tanti ponderis brevi comprehendere. Malumus propterea gravia haec temporis momenta eiusmodi cogitationi tribuere, quae simul nostros animos ad humilitatem inclinet, simul ad summa optata nostra extollat. A nobismetipsis nempe quaerere malumus: quodnam fuerit religiosum nostri Concilii momentum: quo vocabulo significare mens est nostras cum Deo necessitudines, quae profecto declarant quare Ecclesia exsistat, quid credat, quid speret, quid amet, quid sit, quid faciat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p501","Venerable Brothers,","Can we confess that we have procured praise for God, have sought His knowledge and love, have progressed in the struggle to contemplate Him, in solicitude to celebrate Him, in the art of announcing Him to men who look to us as to Pastors and Masters of the ways of God? We sincerely think that it is truly so, also for this reason, that from this first and principal counsel followed that purpose in which the whole, as it were, reason for celebrating the Council lay. Still resound in this Petrine Basilica the words which were uttered in the inaugural allocution of this Council by our predecessor of happy memory, John XXIII, whom we can rightly acknowledge as the author of this Ecumenical Synod. Then that Pontiff spoke thus: \"What is of greatest interest to the Ecumenical Council is this, that the sacred deposit of Christian doctrine be preserved and set forth by a more effective manner... It is truly true that Christ the Lord pronounced this sentence: 'Seek first the kingdom of God and His justice.' This word 'first' declares to what especially our strength and thoughts ought to be directed\" (Discourses, 1962, p. 583). And the event indeed responded to that purpose. That event, that we may estimate it according to the truth of the matter, we must well recall in what age it occurred. Namely, it occurred in an age in which, as is manifest to all, men are turned rather to the dominion of this world than to the kingdom of God; we say an age in which forgetfulness of God has come into custom, as if the processes of progressing sciences themselves suggest it; an age in which the principal act of the human person, having acquired a clearer consciousness concerning itself and its freedom, tends to claim full liberty for itself, subject to no law transcending the order of natural things; an age in which the opinions of laicism seem rightly to proceed from the advances of modern doctrines, and the same seem to be held as the wisest norm, according to which human society is to be ordered; moreover, an age in which human reason has come to express those things which are very absurd and take away all hope; finally, an age in which the greatest religions of the ethnic nations are subject to disturbances and changes, which they have never before experienced. In this age, therefore, our Council has been celebrated to the praise of God, in the name of Christ, with the Holy Spirit breathing, who searches all things, and who even now animates the Church within, that we may know what things are given us by God (cf. 1 Cor 2:10-12), namely, effecting that the Church profoundly and wholly knows human life and the world. By the help of this Council, the theocentric and theological doctrine, as they say, of human nature and of the world, turns the minds of men to itself, as it were provoking those who think it alien and extraneous from the reason of our age; and it claims such things for itself which the world indeed first judges absurd, but afterwards, as we trust, will freely acknowledge as human, wise, and salutary: namely that God is. Certainly God is; He truly exists; He lives; He is a person; He is provident, endowed with infinite goodness, and indeed good not only in Himself, but most greatly also toward us; He is our Creator, our truth, our happiness; so much so that man, when he strives to fix his mind and heart on God, giving himself to contemplation, elicits an act of his spirit which is to be held as most noble and perfect; we say an act from which even in our times innumerable fields of human activity can and ought to receive the grade of their dignity.","Possumusne fateri nos laudem Deo comparavisse, eius cognitionem et amorem quaesivisse, in contentione eum contemplandi profecisse, in sollicitudine eum celebrandi, in arte eum hominibus nuntiandi, qui ad nos tamquam ad Pastores viarumque Dei Magistros respiciunt? Ita re vera esse, Nos sincere opinamur, hanc etiam ob causam, quod ex hoc primo ac praecipuo consilio secutum est propositum illud, in quo tota veluti ratio celebrandi Concilii sita fuit. Adhuc resonant in hac Petriana Basilica verba, quae in auspicali huius Concilii allocutione prolata fuerunt a Decessore Nostro fel. rec. Ioanne XXIII, quem iure optimo auctorem huius Oecumenicae Synodi agnoscere possumus. Tunc ille Pontifex ita locutus est: «Quod Concilii Oecumenici maxime interest hoc est, ut sacrum doctrinae christianae depositum efficaciore ratione custodiatur atque proponatur . . . Verum sane est, Christum Dominum hanc pronuntiasse sententiam: - «Quaerite primum regnum Dei et iustitiam eius» - Quae vox primum declarat, quo potissimum vires et cogitationes nostras dirigi oporteat» (Discorsi, 1962, p. 583). Atque proposito illi eventus reapse respondit. Quod eventum ut pro rei veritate aestimemus, probe recolendum est, qua aetate illud contigerit. Scilicet aetate contigit, qua, ut apud omnes constat, homines ad huius mundi dominatum potius quam ad Dei regnum sunt conversi; aetate dicimus, qua Dei oblivio in consuetudinem venit, quasi eam suadeant ipsi progredientium scientiarum processus; aetate qua actus praecipuus personae humanae, de seipsa de suaque libertate clariorem conscientiam adeptae, eo contendit ut plenam libertatem sibi vindicet, nulli legi obnoxiam, quae rerum naturalium ordinem transcendat; aetate, qua laicismi placita videntur ex hodiernis doctrinarum incrementis iure proficisci, eademque quasi norma sapientissima habentur, secundum quam humana societas sit ordinanda; aetate praeterea, qua humana ratio, eo venit, ut ea exprimat, quae perabsurda sint omnemque spem auferant; aetate denique, qua maximae ethnicarum gentium religiones perturbationibus atque mutationibus obnoxiae sunt, quas antea numquam sunt expertae. Hac igitur aetate nostrum Concilium celebratum est ad Dei laudem, in nomine Christi, afflante Spiritu Sancto, qui omnia scrutatur, quique etiamnunc Ecclesiam intus animat, ut sciamus quae a Deo donata sunt nobis (cfr. 1 Cor. 2, 10-12), scilicet efficiens, ut Ecclesia hominis vitam ac mundum penitus omnique ex parte cognoscat. Ope huius Concilii, doctrina theocentrica ac theologica, uti aiunt, de humana natura ac de mundo ad se hominum mentes convertit, quasi eos provocans, qui illam a nostrae aetatis ratione alienam atque extraneam putent; atque talia sibi arrogat, quae mundus primum quidem absurda iudicet, sed postea, ut fore confidimus, humana, sapientia ac salutaria ultro agnoscet: scilicet Deum esse. Utique Deus est; reapse exsistit; vivit; persona est; est providus, infinita bonitate praeditus, et quidem bonus non solum in se, sed maximopere etiam erga nos; est noster Creator, nostra veritas, nostra felicitas; adeo ut homo, cum mentem et cor suum in Deo defigere nititur, contemplationi vacando, actum animi sui eliciat, qui omnium nobilissimus ac perfectissimus est habendus; actum dicimus, a quo nostris etiam temporibus innumeri humanae navitatis campi suae dignitatis gradum sumere possunt ac debent."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p502","Venerable Brothers,","Someone might say that the Council has been less occupied in treating divine truths than—and this especially—in considering the Church, her nature, her structure, her ecumenical office, her apostolic and missionary work. For this very ancient religious society, that is, the Church, has studied to think about herself, that she might better know herself, better define herself, and from these compose her mind and precepts. This is true. But this self-recognition was not the only end she proposed to herself, was not the display of a merely earthly cultivation of genius. For the Church, recalling herself to herself, penetrated into the intimate recesses of her conscience, not to delight herself in learned investigations into religious psychology or the history of her affairs, nor to purposely obtain again her rights or describe her laws, but to, living in herself and, through the Holy Spirit, better understanding the effective word of Christ, and to more deeply scrutinize the mystery, that is the plan and presence of God concerning and within herself, and to more and more nourish the fire of faith in herself, which is the secret power on which her firmness and wisdom rest, and the fire of love by which she is impelled to sing the praises of God without ceasing, since, as St. Augustine says, \"to sing belongs to the lover\" (Serm. 336; P.L. 38, 1472). This very thing, and indeed the principal religious purpose, clearly appears from the documents of the Council, especially from those which treat of divine Revelation, of the Liturgy, of the Church, of Priests, of Religious, of Laity; by which it is shown how clear, warm, and rich is the spiritual vein which, from the living contact with the living God, bursts forth from the bosom of the Church and through her is poured upon the dry clods of this our earth.","Dixerit aliquis Concilium minus versatum esse in pertractandis veritatibus divinis quam - idque praecipue - in consideranda Ecclesia, eius natura, eius compage eius oecumenico munere, eius opera apostolica et missionali. Haec enim pervetus societas religiosa, hoc est, Ecclesia studuit de se ipsa cogitare, quo melius se cognosceret, melius se definiret atque ex his mentem ac praecepta sua componeret. Hoc est verum. Sed haec sui ipsius recognitio non finis fuit, quem solum sibi proponeret, non fuit ostensio solius terreni cultus ingenii. Ecclesia enim, se ad se ipsam revocans, in intimas conscientiae suae latebras penetravit, non ut se oblectaret in eruditis pervestigationibus, quibus in psychologiam religiosam vel historiam rerum suarum inquireret, aut ut dedita opera iterum iura sua obtineret vel leges suas describeret, sed ut, in se ipsa vivens atque, per Spiritum Sanctum, efficax verbum Christi melius intellegeret, utque altius scrutaretur mysterium, id est consilium ac praesentiam Dei circa se et intra se, et ut ignem fidei in se magis magisque aleret, quae est vis arcana, qua firmitas eius et sapientia innititur, ignemque amoris, quo impellitur ad laudes Dei sine intermissione canendas, siquidem; ut ait S. Augustinus, cantare amantis est (Serm. 336; P.L. 38, 1472). Hoc ipsum ac quidem praecipuum propositum religiosum clare patefit e documentis Concilii, imprimis ex iis, quae agunt de divina Revelatione, de Liturgia, de Ecclesia, de Sacerdotibus, de Religiosis, de Laicis; quibus ostenditur quam limpida, egelida, dives sit spiritualis vena, quae, ob vivum Dei viventis contactum, ex Ecclesiae sinu erumpit et per hanc in aridas glebas huius terrae nostrae effunditur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p503","Venerable Brothers,","But it is not lawful to omit something which is of greatest importance when we investigate the religious force of this Council: for it was especially this, that it should scrutinize the world of our age. Never perhaps has the Church, as in the time of this Synod, felt herself led into the necessity of knowing, as it were approaching, rightly estimating, inserting herself into, serving, and handing over the message of the Gospel to the human society which is around, and of as it were seeking it out, in a way pursuing it, which changes swiftly and continuously. This habit of mind, arising from the fact that the Church in the past age, especially this century, was absent and separated from profane cultivation of genius, this habit of mind, which the saving and principal office of the Church at all times ingests, has effectively and continuously obtained in the Council. Wherefore a suspicion has been instilled into some, that they thought that in the men and acts of the Synod there unduly and too indulgently prevailed that reason which inheres in the external world from the doctrine of \"relativism,\" in things which pass with fleeting course, in new ways which prevail in human culture, in arising necessities, in the thoughts of others; and that to the detriment of the fidelity owed to the doctrine received from the fathers, and with damage to the religious mind and will, which ought to be proper to the Council. We do not think such perdition is to be attributed to it, if its true and recondite counsels and genuine acts are regarded. We rather wish to point out that the religion of our Council was first of all charity; on account of which manifest declared will, the Council can be noted by no one as if it were irreligious, or had failed from the sacred Gospel, if we remember that Christ himself teaches us that \"by this all will know that you are his disciples, if you have love one for another\" (cf. Jn 13:35); if we likewise let these words of the Apostle resound within our spirits: \"Religion clean and undefiled before God and the Father is this: to visit orphans and widows in their tribulation, and to keep oneself unspotted from this world\" (Jas 1:27); and this other: \"If... he does not love his brother whom he sees, how can he love God whom he does not see?\" (1 Jn 4:20).","Sed non licet quiddam praetermittere, quod summi momenti est, cum religiosam vim huius Concilii pervestigamus: hoc enim ei potissimum fuit, ut mundum huius aetatis nostrae scrutaretur. Numquam fortasse Ecclesia sicut huius Synodi tempore in eam necessitatem se sensit adductam, ut societatem hominum, qui circa sunt, cognosceret, quasi adiret, recte aestimaret, in eam se inferret, ei serviret et Evangelii nuntium traderet, atque ut eam veluti appeteret, quodammodo prosequens eam, quae velociter et continenter mutatur. Hic habitus mentis, ortus ex eo quod Ecclesia superiore aetate, praeterito ac maxime hoc saeculo, abfuit et disiuncta est a profano ingenii cultu, hic habitus mentis, quem munus Ecclesiae salvificum ac primarium nullo non tempore ingerit, in Concilio efficaciter et continenter obtinuit. Quam ob rem quibusdam ea suspicio est iniecta, ut putarent in hominibus et actis Synodi plus aequo ac nimis indulgenter praeponderasse illam rationem, quae ex «relativismi» doctrina inhaeret mundo externo, rebus, quae fugaci cursu geruntur, novis modis, qui in humano cultu invalescunt, necessitatibus, quae oriuntur, cogitatis aliorum; idque cum detrimento fidelitatis, quae a maioribus acceptae doctrinae debetur et cum damno religiosae mentis ac voluntatis, quas Concilii proprias esse oportet. Non equidem arbitramur ei tribuendam esse talem perniciem, si eius vera et recondita consilia et germani actus spectantur. Animadvertere potius cupimus, Concilii nostri religionem in primis fuisse caritatem; quam ob praecipuam declaratam voluntatem, Concilium a nemine notari poterit quasi irreligiosum fuerit, vel a sacro Evangelio defecerit, si meminerimus ipsum Christum nos docere, fore ut «in hoc cognoscant omnes, quia discipuli eius sumus, si dilectionem habuerimus ad invicem» (cfr. Io. 13, 35); si pariter sinamus haec Apostolorum verba intus in animis nostris resonare: «Religio munda et immaculata apud Deum et Patrem haec est: visitare pupillos et viduas in tribulatione eorum, et immaculatum se custodire ab hoc saeculo (Iac. 1, 27); et haec alia: Qui . . . non diligit fratrem suum, quem videt, Deum, quem non videt, quomodo potest diligere?» (1 Io. 4, 20)."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p504","Venerable Brothers,","For truly the Church, gathered in the Council, most greatly turned her consideration—besides into herself, and into the relation by which she is joined with God—also into man, into man as he must be contemplated in reality in this time: man, we say, who lives; man, who is devoted to his own promotion alone; man, who not only deems himself worthy to have all study turned to him alone, as to a certain center, but also does not fear to affirm himself to be the principle and reason of every thing. The whole phenomenal man—to use a word of the recent age—clothed with his innumerable mental habits, in which he comes into view, presented himself to the Fathers of the Council, who are themselves men, indeed all Pastors and brothers, with intent care and loving charity: man, who bravely complains of his own mournful fortunes; man, who judges others placed beneath himself both in the past and in our time, and therefore always unstable and feigned, desirous of himself and fierce; man displeasing to himself, who gives forth laughter and sheds tears; man versatile to all things, easy to play any parts; man sharply intent on one investigation of science; man who, as such, thinks, loves, sweats in labors, always attends to something, like the growing son (Gen 49:22); man who is to be considered with a certain sacred reverence, on account of the innocence of his infancy, on account of the mystery of his poverty, on account of the piety which his sufferings move; man hence only studious of himself, hence favoring society; man at once the praiser of past time, at the same time awaiting the future time, and dreaming it happier than the past; man on the one hand obnoxious to crimes, on the other hand adorned with holy morals; and so on in turn. That lay and profane study of humanity, with its immense magnitude, at last comes forth, and the same, as it were, challenged the Council to a contest. Religion, that is, the worship of God, who willed to become man, and religion—for such it is to be esteemed—that is, the worship of man, who wishes to become God, have met together. But what happened? A contest, a battle, an anathema? That could indeed have been, but it plainly did not happen. That old story of the good Samaritan was an example and norm, to which the spiritual reason of our Council was directed. For an immense love for men thoroughly pervaded the Council. The necessities of men, which become the more troublesome the more the son of this earth grows, having been perceived and again considered, occupied the whole study of our Synod. Give this praise at least to the Council, you, cultivators of humanity in our age, who refuse the truths transcending the nature of things, and at the same time acknowledge our new study of humanity: for we also, indeed we before others, are cultivators of man.","Verum enimvero Ecclesia, in Concilio collecta, suam considerationem summopere intendit - praeterquam in semetipsam, atque in necessitudinem, quam cum Deo coniungitur - in hominem etiam, in hominem, sicuti reapse hoc tempore se conspiciendum praebet: hominem, dicimus, qui vivit; hominem, qui sibimetipsi uni provehendo deditus est; hominem, qui non modo sese dignum existimat, ad quem unum, veluti ad quoddam centrum, omne studium conferatur, sed etiam affirmare non veretur, se esse cuiusvis rei principium atque rationem. Totus homo phaenomenicus - ut recentioris aetatis verbo utamur - suis innumeris animi habitibus indutus, quibus in conspectum venit, se Concilii Patribus obiecit, qui et ipsi homines, immo omnes Pastores atque fratres sunt, intenta cura atque amanti caritate praediti: homo, qui suas luctuosas fortunas animose conqueritur; homo, qui et praeterito et nostro hoc tempore alios infra se positos existimat, ideoque semper fluxus atque fucatus, sui cupidus et ferox est; homo sibi displicens, qui risus edit et lacrimas fundit; homo ad omnia versatilis, ad quaslibet partes agendas facilis; homo in unam scientiae pervestigationem acriter intentus; homo, qui uti talis cogitat, amat, in laboribus desudat, semper ad aliquid animum advertit, sicut ille filius accrescens (Gen. 49, 22); homo, qui sacra quadam cum religione est considerandus, ob suae infantiae innocentiam, ob suae inopiae arcanum, ob pietatem, quam suae aegritudines movent; homo hinc sui ipsius tantum studiosus, hinc societati favens; homo simul laudator temporis acti, simul posterum tempus praestolans, illudque felicius quam praeteritum somnians; homo ex altera parte criminibus obnoxius, ex altera Sanctis moribus ornatus; et deinde deinceps. Humanitatis illud laicum atque profanum studium, immani qua est magnitudine, tandem aliquando prodit, idemque ad certamen, ut ita dicamus, Concilium lacessivit. Religio, id est cultus Dei, qui homo fieri voluit, atque religio - talis enim est aestimanda - id est cultus hominis, qui fieri vult Deus, inter se congressae sunt. Quid tamen accidit? Certamen, proelium, anathema? Id sane haberi potuerat, sed plane non accidit. Vetus illa de bono Samaritano narratio exemplum fuit atque norma, ad quam Concilii nostri spiritualis ratio directa est. Etenim, immensus quidam erga homines amor Concilium penitus pervasit. Perspectae et iterum consideratae hominum necessitates, quae eo molestiores fiunt, quo magis huius terrae filius crescit, totum nostrae huius Synodi studium detinuerunt. Hanc saltem laudem Concilio tribuite, vos, nostra hac aetate cultores humanitatis, qui veritates rerum naturam transcendentes renuitis, iidemque novum nostrum humanitatis studium agnoscite: nam nos etiam, immo nos prae ceteris, hominis sumus cultores."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p505","Venerable Brothers,","For what did this most ample Assembly perceive in human nature, or what, having obtained the light of Deity, did it strive to know? It wished to see through its two faces, which it always presents, namely, the infirmity of man and his gentle dignity, then his greatest evil with which he labors as if with an incurable and no doubtful disease, then the good which remains in him, distinguished by hidden beauty and singular excellence. But it is frankly to be confessed, this Council of ours, when it made its judgment concerning man, was more concerned with contemplating this serene face than the sad one; in which indeed it knowingly interpreted all things for the best. For the Council bestowed much study and admiration on the men of our age. Errors indeed were rejected, as charity itself as well as truth demanded, but men were only admonished concerning error, the precept of reverence and love always being preserved. Thus it came about that, instead of a recognition of diseases which would break the spirit, salutary remedies full of solace were brought forth; that the Council, not using ill-omened prognostications, but with words of hope and trust, addressed the men of this memory. For the goods which are in them, it not only cultivated with due cult, but also honored with much praise, and it strongly approved all their undertakings and studies, reduced to a certain integrity. You see, therefore—to suppose a few examples—how innumerable discourses, which are currently used everywhere, have been introduced into the sacred order of rites, that they might render human words to God and again divine words to men; how that natural inclination of man, as man, to attain many rights has been approved, and also the lot which exceeds his nature; how those things which he desires with highest wishes, namely to enjoy life and his dignity and honest liberty and abundance of doctrine and renewed state of social things and justice and peace—we say all these, as it were corrected, have been incited to fuller progress; how finally all men, by a voice which befits sacred pastors and workers, have been consecrated to receive the light of the Gospel into themselves. We now speak too briefly of very many and very ample questions concerning the true prosperity of man, which have been treated in the Council; nor did the Council intend to dissolve all the more urgent questions of modern life; some of which are referred to more diligent investigations, which the Church will institute in the future; many others, however, have been treated too compendiously and generally, and therefore admit subtler explanations and various effects. But now it is pleasant to point out that the Church through her Magisterium, although she wished to define no head of doctrine by extraordinary dogmatic sentences, has nevertheless with authority set forth her doctrine concerning very many questions, to the norm of which men today are held to conform their conscience and their manner of acting. The Church, moreover, as we might say, entered into dialogue with the men of our time; and, always retaining her authority and power, she used a manner of speaking easy and friendly, which is proper to pastoral charity. She wished, therefore, to be heard and understood by men. Wherefore, she turned her words not only to the intellect of man, but used a kind of speech which is commonly used today in weaving dialogues, which indeed, from the use of life on which they rest and from the sincere sentiments of humanity by which they are affected, derive greater power to allure and persuade. The Church, namely, spoke with the men of our age as they are.","Quid enim amplissimus hic Consessus in humana natura perspexit, quidve, lumen Deitatis consecutus, cognoscere studuit? Os eius, duas quod semper facies praefert, penitus perspicere voluit, hominis scilicet infirmitate mitemque dignitatem, tum eius summum malum quo veluti morbo insanabili neque dubio ipse laborat, tum eius quod manet bonum, arcana quidem venustate singularique praestantia distinctum. Verum id est aperte fatendum, nostrum hoc Concilium, cum suum fecerit de homine iudicium, magis in serena hac eius fronte quam in tristi contuenda esse versatum; in quo quidem res omnes in optimam sane partem scienter esse interpretatum. Multum enim studii atque admirationis in nostrae aetatis homines Concilium contulit. Errores profecto sunt reiecti, quemadmodum caritas ipsa perinde atque veritas postulabant, sed homines, salvo semper observantiae et amoris praecepto, tantum de errore sunt admoniti. Ita nimirum factum est, ut pro morborum cognitione, quae animos frangeret, salutaria in medium proferrentur remedia plena solacii; ut Concilium, non infaustis usum ominibus, sed nuntiis spei ac fiduciae verbis, huiusce memoriae homines alloqueretur. Nam quae iis sunt bona, ea non solum debito cultu, sed multo etiam honore affecit, omniaque eius coepta confirmavit atque studia, ad integritatem quandam redacta, vehementer probavit. Videatis igitur - ut aliquot supponamus exempla - quomodo innumeri, qui hodie passim usurpentur, sermones ita in sacrum sint inducti ordinem rituum, ut humana ad Deum verba rursumque divina ad homines verba redderent; quomodo hominis, uti hominis, ea sit comprobata naturalis ad plurima assequenda iura proclivitas atque etiam sors illa, quae ipsius exsuperet, naturam; quomodo ea, quae summis ipse votis concupiscat, ut vivat nempe et sua dignitate et honesta libertate et doctrinae copia et renovato rerum socialium statu et iustitia et pace perfruatur, haec omnia dicimus quasi emendata ad uberiorem sint incitata profectum; quomodo universi demum homines ad Evangelii lumen in se recipiendum, voce quae pastores deceat sacrosque operarios, sint votati. Nimis breviter nunc Nos de plurimis iisque amplissimis quaestionibus loquimur, ad germanam hominis prosperitatem attinentibus, de quibus in Concilio actum est; nec Concilio propositum fuit omnes hodiernae vitae urgentiores quaestiones dissolvere; quarum nonnullae ad diligentiores investigationes reiciuntur, quas Ecclesia in posterum instituet; plures aliae vero nimis presse ac generatim pertractatae sunt, ac propterea subtiliores explicationes variasque effectiones admittunt. Nunc vero animadvertere iuvat, Ecclesiam per suum magisterium, quamvis nullum doctrinae caput sententiis dogmaticis extraordinariis definire voluerit, nihilominus circa plurimas quaestiones cum auctoritate doctrinam proposuisse suam, ad cuius normam homines hodie tenentur conscientiam suam suamque agendi rationem conformare. Ecclesia praeterea, ut ita dicamus, cum nostrorum temporum hominibus colloquium iniit; semperque auctoritatem virtutemque suam retinens, ipsam tamen loquendi rationem adhibuit facilem et amicam, quae caritatis pastoralis propria est. Voluit enim ab hominibus audiri atque intellegi. Quapropter, non ad solam hominis intellegentiam verba sua convertit, sed dicendi genere usa est, quod hodie communiter usurpatur in serendis colloquiis, quae quidem ex vitae usu, quo nituntur, et ex sinceris humanitatis sensibus, quibus afficiuntur, maiorem vim capiunt ad alliciendum et ad persuadendum. Ecclesia scilicet collocuta est cum nostrae aetatis hominibus, uti sunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p506","Venerable Brothers,","There is also another thing which we think worthy of consideration: that such rich abundance of doctrine tends to this one end, to serve man, in all circumstances of his life, in all his infirmity, and in all his necessity. The Church in a way professed herself to be the handmaid of the human race, and indeed at the time when her Magisterium and her pastoral governance, on account of the solemn celebrations of the Ecumenical Council, showed themselves endowed with clearer light and stronger power; indeed, the purpose of exercising the ministry truly obtained the principal place. All these things which we have said concerning the Council, and which we could further say concerning its human significance, did they turn the mind of the Church during the celebration of the Council toward the modern cultivation of the mind, which wholly consists in man? It must be said that the Church did not depart from the right way, but directed it in that direction. But those who rightly weigh this principal study, by which the Council considered human and temporal goods, cannot but acknowledge that such study is to be attributed to the pastoral solicitude which the Council wished to follow as a proper mark of its labors; likewise they must acknowledge that this same study is never separated from the true study of religion, whether on account of the charity by which it is thoroughly affected (and where charity is, there God is!), or on account of the close connection, always confirmed and promoted by the Council, which human and temporal goods have with truly spiritual, religious, and eternal goods. The Church bends toward man and the world, but at the same time is raised to the kingdom of God. The men of our times, whose mental habits impel them to measure the excellence of things from their conveniences, ought to acknowledge the great weight and importance of the Ecumenical Council, at least for this cause, because it tended to the utility of man. Let no one therefore ever affirm that the catholic religion is useless, which, when it expresses the most conscious and effective form of its action, that is, when it celebrates an Ecumenical Council, plainly declares at the same time that it is for man and favors his utilities. Which things being so, it must truly be confessed that the catholic religion and human life are joined by a friendly covenant, and that both conspire to one certain human good: namely, the catholic religion is for the human race, and the life of the human race is in a way for it. Indeed, it is to be called life on account of the excellent doctrine, and that perfect in every respect, which it hands down concerning man (is not man, left to himself, an arcane thing to himself?); which doctrine it therefore hands down because it draws it from the knowledge which it has of God. For that we may fully know man, true man, whole man, we must first know God Himself. To prove this, it now suffices to recall these burning words of St. Catherine of Siena: \"In your nature, eternal God, I shall know my nature.\" Then the catholic religion is life, because it shows the nature of man and his supreme end, and attributes a fuller sense to him; finally, it is life, because it is to be held as the supreme law of life, and because it injects such a secret power into life itself as to make it truly divine.","Aliud est etiam, quod consideratione dignum putamus: huiusmodi divitem doctrinae copiam eo unite spectare, ut homini serviat, in omnibus cius vitae adiunctis, in omni eius infirmitate, et in omni eius necessitate. Ecclesia quodammodo se professa est humani generis ancillam, et quidem eo tempore, quo ipsius magisterium ipsiusque pastorale regimen, ob sollemnes Concilii Oecumenici celebrationes, clariore lumine validioreque robore praedita se praestiterunt: immo vero ministerii exercendi propositum reapse praecipuum obtinuit locum. Quae omnia de Concilio diximus, quaeque dicere insuper possimus ad humanum ipsius momentum quod attinet, numquid inter Concilium celebrandum, Ecclesiae mentem deflexerunt ad hodiernae mentis culturam, quae tota in homine consistit? Dicendum est, Ecclesiam non a recto itinere decessisse, sed hoc in illam partem direxisse. At qui probe perpendant hoc praecipuum studium, quo Concilium bona humana et temporalia consideravit, facere iidem non poterunt, quin agnoscant huiusmodi studium pastorali illi sollicitudini tribuendum esse, quam Concilium tamquam propriam laborum suorum notam sequi voluit; itemque agnoscere debebunt, hoc idem studium numquam a vero religionis studio disiungi, sive ob caritatem, qua penitus afficitur (et ubi est caritas ibi Deus est!), sive ob arctam conexionem, a Concilio semper confirmatam et provectam, quae humana ac temporalia bona cum bonis vere spiritualibus, religiosis et aeternis habent. Ecclesia ad hominem et ad mundum se inclinat, sed simul etiam ad Dei regnum extollitur. Homines nostrorum temporum, quorum mentis habitus eos impellit ut rerum praestantiam ex earum commodis metiantur, magnum Concilii Oecumenici pondus atque momentum agnoscere debent, saltem hanc ob causam, quod ipsum ad hominum utilitatem unite spectavit. Nemo igitur umquam affirmet inutilem catholicam religionem esse, quae, cum suae actionis formam maxime consciam atque eficacem exprimit, hoc est cum Concilium Oecumenicum celebrat, plane simul declarat se pro homine esse eiusque utilitatibus favere. Quae cum ita sint, fatendum revera est, catholicam religionem et humanam vitam inter se amico foedere iungi, et utramque simul conspirare ad unum quoddam humanum bonum: religionem scilicet catholicam pro humano genere esse, humanique generis esse quodammodo vitam. Vita quidem dicenda est ob doctrinam excelsam eamque omni ex parte perfectam, quam ipsa de homine tradit (nonne homo, sibi relictus, sibimetipsi arcana res est?); quam sane doctrinam idcirco tradit, quia illam e scientia, quam de Deo habet, haurit. Etenim ut nos hominem, hominem verum, hominem integrum penitus noscamus, Deum ipsum antea cognoscamus necesse est. Ad hoc probandum satis nunc sit haec recolere Sanctae Catharinae Senensis flammantia verba: «In tua natura, aeterne Deus, naturam meam cognoscam». Tum catholica religio vita est, quia hominis naturam eiusque supremum finem ostendit, plenioremque ei sensum attribuit; vita denique est, quia veluti suprema lex vitae est habenda, et quia vitae ipsi talem arcanam vim inicit, ut eam vere divinam efficiat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p507","Venerable Brothers,","But if, Venerable Brothers and beloved sons, all of you present here, we remember that in the face of any man, especially if it is made translucent by tears and sorrows, the face of Christ (cf. Mt 25:40), the Son of Man, is to be recognized; and if in the face of Christ the face of the heavenly Father is to be recognized, according to that: \"He who sees me, sees the Father also\" (Jn 14:9), our manner of evaluating human things is changed into Christianity, which is wholly directed to God as to its center; so that we can also express it in this way: namely, it is necessary to know man, to know God. Is it not, therefore, the part of this Council, which especially and studiously intended man, to propose liberation and consolation to the world of our age, by which as if by steps it may ascend? Does it not finally teach us by a simple, new, solemn way to love man, that we may love God? To love man, we say, not as an instrument, but as a first, as it were, end, by which we may come to the supreme end, transcending human things. Thus this Council is wholly turned in its religious force, by which it is comprehended and terminated, since it is nothing other than a certain invitation, vehement and friendly, by which the human race is called to find, by the help of fraternal love, that God, \"from whom to turn away is to fall, to whom to be converted is to rise, in whom to remain is to stand... to whom to return is to revive, in whom to dwell is to live\" (St. Augustine, Soliloquies I, 1, 3; P.L. 32, 870). With this hope, we are held at the end of this Second Vatican Ecumenical Council and at the beginning of the human and religious renewal, which it proposed to consider and promote; we trust that this may happen to ourselves, Venerable Brothers and Fathers of the Council; we hope that the same will befall the whole human race, which we have here learned to love more and to serve better.","Quodsi, Venerabiles Fratres ac dilecti filii, omnes, qui hic praesentes adestis, meminimus in vultu cuiusvis hominis, maxime si lacrimis ac doloribus effectus est translucidus, agnoscendum esse vultum Christi (cfr. Matth. 25, 40), Filii hominis; ac si in vultu Christi agnoscendus est vultus Patris caelestis, secundum illud: «Qui videt me, videt et Patrem» (Io. 14, 9), modus noster res humanas aestimandi mutatur in Christianismum, qui in Deum ut in medium totus dirigitur; ita ut rem hoc etiam modo enuntiare possimus: scilicet opus esse cognoscere hominem, ut cognoscatur Deus. Nonne igitur huius Concilii, quod in hominem praecipue ac studiose mentem intendit, est mundo huius aetatis nostrae proponere liberationem et consolationem, quibus, veluti per gradus possit ascendere? Nonne denique nos Concilium docet ratione simplici, nova, sollemni amare hominem, ut amemus Deum? Amare hominem, dicimus, non ut instrumentum, sed ut primum veluti finem, quo ad supremum finem, humanas res transcendentem, perveniamus. Itaque hoc Concilium totum vertitur in sua vi religiosa qua comprehenditur ac terminatur, cum aliud non sit quam invitamentum quoddam, vehemens et amicum, quo humanum genus vocatur ad inveniendum, ope fraterni amoris, illum Deum, «a quo averti cadere, in quem converti resurgere, in quo manere consistere . . ., in quem redire reviviscere, in qua habitare vivere est» (S. August., Sol. I, 1, 3; P.L. 32, 870). Hac profecto spe tenemur in exitu huius Concilii Oecumenici Vaticani Secundi et in exordio renovationis humanae ac religiosae, quam ut consideraret ac proveheret, illud sibi proposuit; hoc ut nobismet ipsis contingat, Venerabiles Fratres ac Patres Concilii, fore confidimus; idem obventurum esse speramus universo humano generi, quod magis amare et cui melius servire hic docti sumus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p508","Venerable Brothers,","Finally, that these things may happily come to pass, we again invoke as intercessors for us St. John the Baptist and St. Joseph, Patrons of the ecumenical Synod, the holy Apostles Peter and Paul, the foundations and defenses of the holy Church, and together with them St. Ambrose the Bishop, whose feast day we celebrate today, by him as it were joining the Eastern and Western Church, likewise we implore from the heart the help of the Blessed Virgin Mary, Mother of Christ, who has been called by us also Mother of the Church, and with one voice, unanimous, we give thanks to God and proclaim His glory, who is the living and true God, the one and most high God, Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. Amen.","Dum denique, ut haec feliciter eveniant, iterum invocamus deprecatores nostros Sanctos Ioannem Baptistam et Ioseph, Patronos oecumenicae Synodi, Sanctos Apostolos Petrum et Paulum, fundamenta ac praesidia Sanctae Ecclesiae, et una cum his Sanctum Ambrosium Episcopum, cuius festum diem hodie celebramus, per eum veluti coniungentes Ecclesiam Orientalem et Occidentalem, pariter et ex animo imploramus auxilium Beatae Mariae Virginis, Matris Christi, quae a Nobis etiam Mater Ecclesiae est appellata, atque una voce, unanimi Deo gratias agimus et gloriam eius praedicamus, qui est Deus vivus et verus, Deus unus et summus, Pater, Filius et Spiritus Sanctus. Amen."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p509","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Your eminences, venerable brothers, representatives of governments, gentlemen of the city of Rome, authorities and citizens of the entire world! You, observers belonging to so many different Christian denominations, and you, faithful and sons here present, and you also scattered across the earth and united with us in faith and charity!","Ad vos, eminentissimi Patres cardinales; ad vos, venerabiles fratres; ad vos, legati nationum; ad vos, viri urbis Romae; ad vos, auctoritates et cives universi terrarum orbis; ad vos, observatores a diversis christiana religione seiunctis communitatibus; ad vos, christifideles et filii hodie hic praesentes, etiam ad vos per orbem terrae dispersi nobiscumque fide et caritate coniuncti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p510","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","You will hear shortly, at the end of this holy Mass, a reading of some messages which, at the conclusion of its work, the ecumenical council is addressing to various categories of persons, intending to consider in them the countless forms in which human life finds expression. And you will also hear the reading of our official decree in which we declare terminated and closed the Second Vatican Ecumenical Council. This is a moment, a brief moment of greetings. Then, our voice will be silent. This council is completely terminated, this immense and extraordinary assembly is disbanded.","Audietis hac peracta sacra missa nonnulla legit documenta, quae, ut suum perficiat laborum cursum, concilium oecumenicum ad diversas hominum ordines dirigit, volens in ipsis significare immensam illam multitudinem formarum, quibus humana vita exprimitur. Audiétis insuper nostrum decretum, sive breviarium, quo Nos ipsum Concilium Vaticanum II terminatum et clausum esse declaramus. Hoc est momentum, breve momentum salutationis. Tum autem obmutescet vox nostra, conciliumque perfectum erit, haecque immensa et extraordinaria coetus concio dimittetur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p511","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Hence, this greeting which we address to you has particular significance, which we take the liberty of pointing out to you, not to distract you from prayer, but to occupy the better your attention in this present celebration.","Quare salutatio nostra, quam vobis offerimus, peculiarem habet significationem; eam licet vobis quasi signare, non ut ab oratione avocentur, sed ut attentiore animo in hac praesenti celebratione maneatis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p512","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","This greeting is, before all, universal. It is addressed to all of you assisting and participating here in this sacred rite: to you, venerable brothers in the episcopate; to you, representatives of nations; to you, people of God. And it is extended and broadened to the entire world. How could it be otherwise if this council was said to be and is ecumenical, that is to say, universal? Just as the sound of the bell goes out through the skies, reaching each one within the radius of its sound waves, so at this moment does our greeting go out to each and every one of you. To those who receive it and to those who do not, it resounds pleadingly in the ear of every man. From this Catholic center of Rome, no one, in principle, is unreachable; in principle, all men can and must be reached. For the Catholic Church, no one is a stranger, no one is excluded, no one is far away. Every one to whom our greeting is addressed is one who is called, who is invited and who, in a certain sense, is present. This is the language of the heart of one who loves. Every loved one is present! And we, especially at this moment, in virtue of our universal pastoral and apostolic mandate, we love all, all men.","Est haec salutatio primum omnium universalis. Dirigitur enim ad universos vos, qui huic sacro ritui adstatis et participatis: ad vos, venerabiles fratres episcopos; ad vos, legatos nationum; ad vos, populum Dei. Extenditur et dilatatur ad omnem mundum. Quomodo aliter eveniret, cum concilium oecumenicum, id est universale, dictum sit et sit? Quemadmodum campanae sonus per auras propagatur, ad singulos suae vibrationis ambitu comprehensos accedens, ita hoc momento pervenit nostra salutatio ad unumquemque vestrum. Ea ad eos, qui eam suscipiunt, et ad eos, qui eam non suscipiunt, velut supplicans resonat in auribus hominum. Nullus, in principio, ab hoc catholico Romanae urbis centro separatus habetur; in principio, omnes homines potest et debet salutare. Ecclesiae enim catholicae nemo exsul, nemo exclusus, nemo longinquus est. Quilibet, ad quem nostra salutatio dirigitur, vocatus, invitatus, et quadam ratione praesens est. Hic est sermo cordis amantis: omnis qui amatur praesens est! Atque nos, hoc praesertim tempore, vi universalis mandati pastoralis et apostolici, omnes, omnes homines diligimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p513","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Hence, we say this to you good and faithful souls who, absent in person from this gathering of believers and of nations, are here present in spirit with your prayer. The Pope is thinking of you too, and with you he celebrates this sublime moment of universal communion.","Hoc igitur dicimus vobis, bonae et fideles animae, quae, corpore ab hoc fidelium et gentium coetu absentes, spiritu hic praesentes estis cum vestris precibus. De vobis etiam cogitans, Papa, simul et vobiscum, hoc sublimis communionis universalis celebrat momentum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p514","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","We say this to you, you who suffer like prisoners of your infirmities, to you who, if you were without the comfort of our heartfelt greeting, would, because of your spiritual solitude, experience a redoubling of your pain.","Hoc dicimus vobis, qui velut infirmitatum carceribus detenti patimini; quibus si deesset cordialis salutationis nostrae solacium, propter spiritualem solitudinem velut geminatum doleretis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p515","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","This we say especially to you, brothers in the episcopate, who through no fault of your own were missing from the council and now leave voids in the ranks of your brother bishops and still more in their hearts and ours, a void which gives us such sufferings and which condemns the injustices which shackle your liberty — would that this were all that was wanting to enable you to come to our council.","Hoc maxime dicimus vobis, fratres episcopi, qui nulla vestra culpa in concilio defuistis, et nunc vacua in vestrorum fratrum episcoporum ordinibus, multo autem magis in eorum et in nostro animo deseritis, vacuum quod ita nos affligit et iniquitates illas vituperat, quae vestram libertatem coartant; utinam hoc unum deesset quominus ad nostrum concilium veniretis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p516","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Greetings to you, brothers, who are unjustly detained in silence, in oppression, and in the privation of the legitimate and sacred rights owed to every honest man, and much more to you who are the workmen of nothing but good, piety and peace. To hindered and humiliated brethren, the Church is with you. She is with your faithful and with all those who have a part in your painful condition! May this also be the civil conscience of the world!","Salvete, fratres, qui iniuste in silentio detinetis, in oppressione, in privatione legitimorum et sacrorum iurium, quae cuilibet honesto homini debentur, multo magis vobis, qui nihil aliud nisi bonum, pietatem et pacem perficitis. Vobis, fratribus impeditís et humiliatis, Ecclesia adest. Ecclesia vestris christifidelibus adest, et omnibus, qui vestram participabant condicionem! Idem faciat mundi etiam conscientia civilis!"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p517","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Lastly, our universal greeting goes out to you, men who do not know us, men who do not understand us, men who do not regard us as useful, necessary or friendly. This greeting goes also to you, men who, while perhaps thinking they are doing good, are opposed to us. A sincere greeting, an unassuming greeting but one filled with hope and, today, please believe that it is filled with esteem and love.","Denique salutatio nostra universalis ad vos dirigitur, homines qui nos ignoratis, qui non intellegitis, qui nos non putatis utiles, necessarios, benevolos. Ad vos quoque dirigitur, homines qui, fortasse existimantes vos bene facere, nobis adversamini. Sincera salutatio, non arrogans salutatio, sed spe plena et, hodie, quaesumus, credite, aestimatione et amore referta."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p518","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","This is our greeting. But please be attentive, you who are listening to us. We ask you to consider how our greeting, differently from what ordinarily happens in day to day conversation, would serve to terminate a relationship of nearness or discourse. Our greeting tends to strengthen and, if necessary, to produce a spiritual relationship whence it draws its meaning and its voice. Ours is a greeting, not of farewell which separates, but of friendship which remains, and which, if so demanded, wishes to be born. It is even precisely in this last expression that our greeting, on the one hand, would desire to reach the heart of every man, to enter therein as a cordial guest and speak in the interior silence of your individual souls, the habitual and ineffable words of the Lord: “My peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you, but not as the world gives it” — Christ has His own special way of speaking in the secrets of hearts — and in the other hand, our greeting wants to be a different and higher relationship because it is not only a two-sided exchange of words among us people of this earth, but it also brings into the picture another present one, the Lord Himself, invisible but working in the framework of human relationships. It invites Him and begs of Him to arouse in him who greets and in him who is greeted new gifts of which the first and highest is charity.","Haec est salutatio nostra. Sed attentiores vos esse quaesumus, qui nos auditis. Considerare vos rogamus, quomodo nostra salutatio, aliter ac fieri solet in quotidiana vitae consuetudine, si forte distaremus vel colloquium intermitteremus, non ut relationem concluderet sed ut spiritualem coniunctionem, unde significationem suam et sonum ducit, confirmaret et, si opus esset, produceret. Nostra est salutatio non valedictoria quae separat, sed amicitiae quae manet, et quae, si hoc postuletur, nasci vult. Ac veluti in hac ipsa extrema interpretatione verbum internum invenit salutatio nostra, qua pervenire ad cor hominis cupit, ut tamquam suavis hospes illuc descendat et in interiori conscientiae silentio illa Domini solita et ineffabilia verba proferat: «Pacem meam do vobis, pacem relinquo vobis, non quomodo mundus dat pacem» — suum loquendi modum habet Christus in absconditis cordium —. Altera autem ex parte, vult esse coniunctio diversa et altior, cum non sit tantum mutuum colloquium inter eos qui sumus, verum etiam ex alio quodam praesente Domino, qui invisibilis quidem est, sed in ipsa tamen hominum conversatione operatur; illum autem invitat et rogat, ut suscitet in salutante et in salutato nova quaedam dona, quorum primum et maximum est caritas."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p519","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Behold, this is our greeting. May it rise as a new spark of divine charity in our hearts, a spark which may enkindle the principles, doctrine and proposals which the council has organized and which, thus inflamed by charity, may really produce in the Church and in the world that renewal of thoughts, activities, conduct, moral force and hope and joy which was the very scope of the council.","Ecce, haec est salutatio nostra. Haec tamquam nova divinae caritatis scintilla in cordibus nostris assurgat, quae incendi possit principia, doctrinam et proposita, quae concilium ordinatione quadam edidit; quaeque, ita caritatis igne inflammata, in Ecclesia et in mundo realiter efficere possit eam cogitationum, actionum, agendi rationis, virium moralium, spei et laetitiae renovationem, quae ipsum concilii propositum fuit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p520","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Consequently, our greeting is in the ideal order. Is it a dream? Is it poetry? Is it only a conventional and meaningless exaggeration, as often happens in our day-to-day expression of good wishes? No. This greeting is ideal, but not unreal. Here we would ask for a further moment of your attention. When we men push our thoughts and our desires toward an ideal conception of life, we find ourselves immediately in a utopia, in rhetorical caricature, in illusion or delusion. Man preserves an unquenchable yearning toward ideal and total perfection, but of himself he is incapable of reaching it, perhaps not in concept or much less with experience or reality. This we know, it is the drama of man, the drama of the fallen king.","Quapropter in ideali ordine consistit salutatio nostra. An est somnium? Estne poesis? Estne sola locutio conventionalis, insubstantiali exaggeratione constans, ut saepe nobis contingit in cotidianis bene precandi formulis? Minime. Haec salutatio idealis est, sed non est irrealis. Hie enixe rogamus ut animum vestrum momentum aliud attendatis. Cum nos homines nostras cogitationes et vota ad idealem vitae notionem dirigimus, statim in utopiam, in falsam verbositatem, in augurationem vel deceptionem decidimus. Homo inexstinctum desiderium idealis et perfectae perfectionis servat, sed per se capax non est ad illam attingendum fortasse in conceptu, multo minus in experimento et realitate. Hoc novimus. Haec est hominis fabula, haec est fabula regis caduci."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p521","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","But note what is taking place here this morning. While we close the ecumenical council, we are honoring Mary Most Holy, the mother of Christ, and consequently, as we declared on another occasion, the mother of God and our spiritual mother. We are honoring Mary Most Holy, the Immaculate One, therefore innocent, stupendous, perfect. She is the woman, the true woman who is both ideal and real, the creature in whom the image of God is reflected with absolute clarity, without any disturbance, as happens in every other human creature.","Sed videte quid hoc mane agatur. Dum oecumenicum concilium claudimus, Mariam Sanctissimam, Christi Matrem, proinde, ut alio tempore ediximus, Dei Matrem et nostram spiritualem Matrem, colimus. Mariam Sanctissimam, Immaculatam, ideoque innocentem, praeclaram, perfectam, colimus. Ipsa est mulier, vera mulier, quae simul idealis et realis est; creatura, in qua effigies Dei clara nitet absolutissima luce, absque ulla perturbatione, ut in qualibet alia hominis creatura evenit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p522","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Is it not perhaps in directing our gaze on this woman who is our humble sister and at the same time our heavenly mother and queen, the spotless and sacred mirror of infinite beauty, that we can terminate the spiritual ascent of the council and our final greeting? Is it not here that our post-conciliar work can begin? Does not the beauty of Mary Immaculate become for us an inspiring model, a comforting hope?","Fortasse nonne in hanc Mariam, quae humilis soror nostra, simulque caelestis mater et regina, mundum et sacrum infinitae pulchritudinis speculum, oculos nostros convertentes, spiritualem concilii profectum et extremam nostram salutationem concludere possumus? Nonne hinc opus nostrum postconciliares exordium sumet? Nonne Mariae Immaculatae pulchritudo fiet nobis exemplar incitans ad virtutem, spes nos consolans?"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p523","Oratio Clausionis Pauli Pp. VI in Solemni Sessione Concilii Vaticani II Habita","Oh, brothers, sons, and gentlemen who are listening to us, we think it is so for us and for you. And this is our most exalted and, God willing, our most valuable greeting.","O fratres, o filii, o viri qui nos auditis, nos ita esse et nobis et vobis putamus. Hic est summus et, Deo dante, pretiosissimus noster nuntius."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p524","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","At this solemn moment, we, the Fathers of the 21st ecumenical council of the Catholic Church, on the point of disbanding after four years of prayer and work, with the full consciousness of our mission toward mankind, address ourselves respectively and confidently to those who hold in their hands the destiny of men on this earth, to all those who hold temporal power.","To Rulers (read by Achille Cardinal Lienart of Lille, France, assisted by Bernard Cardinal Alfrink of Utrecht, the Netherlands, and Giovanni Cardinal Colombo of Milan, Italy)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p525","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","We proclaim publicly: We do honor to your authority and your sovereignty, we respect your office, we recognize your just laws, we esteem those who make them and those who apply them. But we have a sacrosanct word to speak to you and it is this: Only God is great. God alone is the beginning and the end. God alone is the source of your authority and the foundation of your laws.","Hoc sollemni momento, Nos Patres Concilii XXI Oecumenici Ecclesiae catholicae, cum quattuor annis precibus et laboribus peractis iam sumus dimissuri, plena conscii missionis nostrae erga homines, reverenter et fidenter ad eos nos convertimus, qui in suis manibus tenent hominum sortem super terram, ad omnes qui politica potestate fruuntur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p526","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","Your task is to be in the world the promoters of order and peace among men. But never forget this: It is God, the living and true God, who is the Father of men. And it is Christ, His eternal Son, who came to make this known to us and to teach us that we are all brothers. He it is who is the great artisan of order and peace on earth, for He it is who guides human history and who alone can incline hearts to renounce those evil passions which beget war and misfortune. It is He who blesses the bread of the human race, who sanctifies its work and its suffering, who gives it those joys which you can never give it, and strengthens it in those sufferings which you cannot console.","Palam profitemur: Nos vestram auctoritatem vestrumque principatum honoramus, vestrum officium observamus, vestras iustas leges agnoscimus, eos qui eas ferunt atque exsequuntur aestimamus. At sacrosanctum verbum vobis dicendum habemus: Solus Deus magnus est. Solus Deus est principium et finis. Solus Deus vestrae est auctoritatis fons et vestrarum legum fundamentum."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p527","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","In your earthly and temporal city, God constructs mysteriously His spiritual and eternal city, His Church. And what does this Church ask of you after close to 2,000 years of experiences of all kinds in her relations with you, the powers of the earth? What does the Church ask of you today? She tells you in one of the major documents of this council. She asks of you only liberty, the liberty to believe and to preach her faith, the freedom to love her God and serve Him, the freedom to live and to bring to men her message of life. Do not fear her. She is made after the image of her Master, whose mysterious action does not interfere with your prerogatives but heals everything human of its fatal weakness, transfigures it and fills it with hope, truth and beauty.","Munus vestrum est mundi esse effectores ordinis et pacis inter homines. At numquam hoc obliviscimini: Deus est, vivus et verus Deus, pater hominum. Et Christus est, eius Filius aeternus, qui hoc nobis notum facere venit nosque omnes fratres esse docuit. Ipse est magnus opifex ordinis et pacis in terris; Ipse enim historiam humanam dirigit, et solus potest corda inclinare ad renuntiandas illas malas cupiditates, quae bellum pariunt et mala. Ipse est qui panem hominum benedicit, eorum labores et dolores sanctificat, datque eos gaudia, quae ei vos dare numquam potestis, eosque confirmat in doloribus, quos vos solari nequitis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p528","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","Allow Christ to exercise His purifying action on society. Do not crucify Him anew. This would be a sacrilege for He is the Son of God. This would be suicide for He is the Son of man. And we, His humble ministers, allow us to spread everywhere without hindrance the Gospel of peace on which we have meditated during this council. Of it, your peoples will be the first beneficiaries, since the Church forms for you loyal citizens, friends of social peace and progress.","In vestra terrestri et temporali civitate, Deus suam spiritualem et aeternam civitatem aedificat, Ecclesiam suam. Et quid haec Ecclesia postulat a vobis, post duo fere annorum milia experimentorum omnium generis in suis necessitudinibus cum vobis, potestatibus huius terrae? Quid hodie Ecclesia a vobis petit? Unis vobis e vobis in magnis huius Concilii documentis rem aperit. Libertatem tantum rogat, libertatem credendi et suam fidem praedicandi, libertatem Deum suum amandi et Ipsi serviendi, libertatem vivendi et hominibus nuntium vitae suae deferendi. Nolite eam timere. Condita est ad imaginem Magistri sui, cuius arcana actio vestris praerogativis non officit, sed omne id quod humanum est a fatali imbecillitate medetur, illud transfigurat, et spe veritate pulchritudineque complet."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p529","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","On this solemn day when she closes the deliberations of her 21st ecumenical council, the Church offers you through our voice her friendship, her services, her spiritual and moral forces. She addresses to you all her message of salvation and blessing. Accept it, as she offers it to you with a joyous and sincere heart and pass it on to your peoples.","Christum sinite suam purificantem actionem in societatem exercere. Nolite Ipsum iterum crucifigere. Id sacrilegium esset, ipse cum Filius Dei sit. Id suicidium esset, ipse cum Filius hominis sit. Et nos, Eius humiles ministri, nobis permittite ut passim sine impedimento Evangelium pacis, de quo in hoc Concilio meditati sumus, diffundamus. Eius populi vestri primi beneficiarii erunt, quandoquidem Ecclesia vobis cives fideles creat, amicos socialis pacis et progressionis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p530","Nuntius ad Moderatores Rerum Publicarum","","Hac sollemni die, qua XXI sui Concilii Oecumenici deliberationes claudit, Ecclesia vobis per nostram vocem suam amicitiam, sua officia, suas spirituales et morales vires offert. Ad vos totum suum salutis et benedictionis nuntium dirigit. Eum accipite, prout vobis eum laeto et sincero animo offert, et vestris populis transmittite."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p531","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","A very special greeting to you, seekers after truth, to you, men of thought and science, the explorers of man, of the universe and of history, to all of you who are pilgrims enroute to the light and to those also who have stopped along the road, tired and disappointed by their vain search.","To Men of Thought and Science (read by Paul Emile Cardinal Leger of Montreal, assisted by Antonio Cardinal Caggiano of Buenos Aires and Norman Cardinal Gilroy of Sydney, Australia)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p532","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","Why a special greeting for you? Because all of us here, bishops and Fathers of the council, are on the lookout for truth. What have our efforts amounted to during these four years except a more attentive search for and deepening of the message of truth entrusted to the Church and an effort at more perfect docility to the spirit of truth.","Plane peculiaris est vobis salutatio, vos veritatis inquisitores, vos viri cogitationis et scientiae, investigatores hominis, universi et historiae, vos omnes, qui ad lucem peregrinamini, atque illis etiam, qui in via subsistentes, fessi et vana indagatione irritati."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p533","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","Hence our paths could not fail to cross. Your road is ours. Your paths are never foreign to ours. We are the friends of your vocation as searchers, companions in your fatigues, admirers of your successes and, if necessary, consolers in your discouragement and your failures.","Cur peculiaris vobis salutatio? Quia omnes nos, hic, episcopi et Patres conciliares, veritatem intendimus exspectamusque. Quid valuerunt horum quattuor annorum conatus nostri, nisi ut attentior quaereretur ac profundius patefieret veritatis nuntius Ecclesiae concreditus, atque ut eniteremur ad perfectionem docilitatis erga Spiritum veritatis?"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p534","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","Hence for you also we have a message and it is this: Continue your search without tiring and without ever despairing of the truth. Recall the words of one of your great friends, St. Augustine: “Let us seek with the desire to find, and find with the desire to seek still more.” Happy are those who, while possessing the truth, search more earnestly for it in order to renew it, deepen it and transmit it to others. Happy also are those who, not having found it, are working toward it with a sincere heart. May they seek the light of tomorrow with the light of today until they reach the fullness of light.","Quare semitae nostrae inter se congredi non potuerunt quin coniungerentur. Via vestra nostra est. Vestra itinera numquam ab nostra aliena sunt. Nos amici sumus vestrae vocationis indagatorum, socii laborum vestrorum, miratores successuum vestrorum et, si opus fuerit, consolatores in animorum demissione et in vestris inceptis frustratis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p535","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","But do not forget that if thinking is something great, it is first a duty. Woe to him who voluntarily closes his eyes to the light. Thinking is also a responsibility, so woe to those who darken the spirit by the thousand tricks which degrade it, make it proud, deceive and deform it. What other basic principle is there for men of science except to think rightly?","Quapropter etiam vobis nuntium habemus atque hic est: Pergite inquisitionem vestram sine fatigatione et unquam de veritate desperantes. Memoriam revocate verborum magni cuiusdam amici vestri, S. Augustini: «Quaeramus inveniendi desiderio et inveniamus quaerendi desiderio.» Beati qui, veritatem possidentes, eam studiosius quaerunt ut renovent eam, altius perscrutentur et transmittant aliis. Beati quoque qui, nondum inventa, sincero animo ad eam contendunt. Lucem crastini diei cum hodiernae luce ita quaerant ut ad plenitudinem lucis perveniant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p536","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","For this purpose, without troubling your efforts, without dazzling brilliance, we come to offer you the light of our mysterious lamp which is faith. He who entrusted this lamp to us is the sovereign Master of all thought, He whose humble disciples we are, the only one who said and could have said: “I am the light of the world, I am the way, the truth and the life.”","At nolite oblivisci: si cogitare magnum aliquid est, est tamen prius officium. Vae illi qui sponte claudit oculos suos ad lucem. Cogitare etiam responsalitas est, vae illis qui obscurant animum mille artibus, quae illi officiunt, quae superbire faciunt, quae decipiunt et depravant. Quodnam aliud principium pro viris scientiae exstat nisi ut recte cogitent?"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p537","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","These words have meaning for you. Never perhaps, thank God, has there been so clear a possibility as today of a deep understanding between real science and real faith, mutual servants of one another in the one truth. Do not stand in the way of this important meeting. Have confidence in faith, this great friend of intelligence. Enlighten yourselves with its light in order to take hold of truth, the whole truth. This is the wish, the encouragement and the hope, which, before disbanding, is expressed to you by the Fathers of the entire world assembled at Rome in council.","Hac ratione, vestros conatus non turbantes, sine splendore vos praestringente, venimus vobis lucem nostrae mysteriosae lampadis offerre, quae fides est. Qui hanc lampadem nobis concredidit, est summus Magister omnis cogitationis, cuius humiles discipuli sumus, Unus qui dixit eique dicere licuit: «Ego sum lux mundi, via, veritas et vita.»"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p538","Nuntius ad Viros Cogitationis et Scientiae","","Haec verba vobis dicunt aliquid. Numquam fortasse, Deo gratias, tam aperta fuit facultas ac hodie profundae necessitudinis inter veram scientiam veramque fidem, sibi invicem ancillantes in una veritate. Ne huic magno occursui obstaculum ponatis. Fiduciam habete in fide, hac magna intellegentiae amica. Eius lumine vos illuminate ut veritatem comprehendatis, omnem veritatem. Hoc votum est, hoc invitamentum, haec spes quae, priusquam concilium dimittatur, a Patribus universi orbis Romae in Concilium congregatis vobis exprimitur."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p539","Nuntius ad Artifices","We now address you, artists, who are taken up with beauty and work for it: poets and literary men, painters, sculptors, architects, musicians, men devoted to the theater and the cinema. To all of you, the Church of the council declares to you through our voice: if you are friends of genuine art, you are our friends.","To Artists (read by Leo Cardinal Suenens of Malines Brussels, Belgium, assisted by Lawrence Cardinal Shehan of Baltimore and Jaime Cardinal de Barros Camara of Rio de Janeiro)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p540","Nuntius ad Artifices","The Church has long since joined in alliance with you. You have built and adorned her temples, celebrated her dogmas, enriched her liturgy. You have aided her in translating her divine message in the language of forms and figures, making the invisible world palpable. Today, as yesterday, the Church needs you and turns to you. She tells you through our voice: Do not allow an alliance as fruitful as this to be broken. Do not refuse to put your talents at the service of divine truth. Do not close your mind to the breath of the Holy Spirit.","Ad vos nunc convertimur, artifices, qui pulchritudine occupamini eique operam datis: poetae et litterarum scriptores, pictores, sculptores, architecti, musici, homines theatro ac cinematographiae dediti. Ad vos universos Ecclesia, Concilii Patrum nomine, nostra vobiscum voce loquitur: si verae artis estis amici, nostri estis amici."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p541","Nuntius ad Artifices","This world in which we live needs beauty in order not to sink into despair. It is beauty, like truth, which brings joy to the heart of man and is that precious fruit which resists the wear and tear of time, which unites generations and makes them share things in admiration. And all of this is through your hands. May these hands be pure and disinterested. Remember that you are the guardians of beauty in the world. May that suffice to free you from tastes which are passing and have no genuine value, to free you from the search after strange or unbecoming expressions. Be always and everywhere worthy of your ideals and you will be worthy of the Church which, by our voice, addresses to you today her message of friendship, salvation, grace and benediction.","Ecclesia iam dudum vobiscum quasi in societatem coniuncta est. Vos eius templa aedificastis et ornastis, dogmata celebravistis, liturgiam locupletastis. Eam adiuvistis, ut nuntium suum divinum transferret in linguam formarum et figurarum, rerum invisibilium efficientes veluti palpabiles. Hodie sicut heri Ecclesia vobis indiget et ad vos convertitur. Hoc vobis per nostram vocem dicit: Ne patiamini ut tam fructuosa societas dissolvatur. Nolite vestra ingenia divinae veritati servire recusare. Nolite animum vestrum obcludere Spiritui Sancto inspiranti."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p542","Nuntius ad Artifices","","Hic mundus, in quo vivimus, eget pulchritudine, ne in desperationem ruat. Pulchritudo est, sicut et veritas, quae gaudium cordi hominis infert, estque pretiosus fructus, qui vetustati obsistit et consummationi, qui generationes coniungit et eas facit admirationis participes. Atque haec omnia manibus vestris perficiuntur. Sint hae purae et gratuitae. Mementote vos pulchritudinis in mundo esse custodes. Hoc sufficiat ut vos expediat a saporibus fluxis nihilque vere valentibus, ut vos expediat ab indagatione novarum ineptarumve formarum. Estote semper et ubique vestris idealibus digni, et eritis digni Ecclesia, quae, hac nostra voce, hodie ad vos mittit suum amicitiae, salutis, gratiae et benedictionis nuntium."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p543","Nuntius ad Mulieres","And now it is to you that we address ourselves, women of all states — girls, wives, mothers and widows, to you also, consecrated virgins and women living alone — you constitute half of the immense human family. As you know, the Church is proud to have glorified and liberated woman, and in the course of the centuries, in diversity of characters, to have brought into relief her basic equality with man. But the hour is coming, in fact has come, when the vocation of woman is being achieved in its fullness, the hour in which woman acquires in the world an influence, an effect and a power never hitherto achieved. That is why, at this moment when the human race is undergoing so deep a transformation, women impregnated with the spirit of the Gospel can do so much to aid mankind in not falling.","To Women (read by Leon Cardinal Duval of Algiers, Algeria, assisted by Julius Cardinal Doepfner of Munich, Germany, and Raul Cardinal Silva of Santiago, Chile)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p544","Nuntius ad Mulieres","You women have always had as your lot the protection of the home, the love of beginnings and an understanding of cradles. You are present in the mystery of a life beginning. You offer consolation in the departure of death. Our technology runs the risk of becoming inhuman. Reconcile men with life and above all, we beseech you, watch carefully over the future of our race. Hold back the hand of man who, in a moment of folly, might attempt to destroy human civilization.","Et nunc ad vos convertimur, mulieres cuiuslibet condicionis — puellae, uxores, matres et viduae — ad vos etiam, virgines sacrae ac mulieres solae viventes — vos constituitis dimidium immensae familiae humanae. Perlibenter, ut nostis, Ecclesia gloriatur se mulierem glorificasse et liberasse atque, decursu saeculorum, pro varietate ingeniorum, effecisse ut praecipua eius cum viro aequalitas in lucem proferretur. Sed hora venit, immo iam venit, qua mulieris vocatio ad plenitudinem suam adducitur, hora qua mulier in mundo consecutura est vim, efficacitatem potentiamque, qualis numquam antea est consecuta. Quapropter, hoc momento, cum humanum genus tam profundam transformationem subit, mulieres, evangelico spiritu imbutae, plurimum possunt ad homines adiuvandos, ne ruant."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p545","Nuntius ad Mulieres","Wives, mothers of families, the first educators of the human race in the intimacy of the family circle, pass on to your sons and your daughters the traditions of your fathers at the same time that you prepare them for an unsearchable future. Always remember that by her children a mother belongs to that future which perhaps she will not see.","Vos mulieres vestrae sortis semper habuistis tutelam domus, amorem initiorum, cunarum sapientem curam. Adestis in vitae nascentis mysterio. In mortis autem digressu solacium praebetis. Periculum est ne technica nostra ratio evadat inhumana. Reconciliate homines cum vita et, ante omnia, vos obsecramus, diligenter prospicite futuro generis humani. Manum viri cohibete, qui momento dementiae suae humanam文明em delere temptaret."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p546","Nuntius ad Mulieres","And you, women living alone, realize what you can accomplish through your dedicated vocation. Society is appealing to you on all sides. Not even families can live without the help of those who have no families. Especially you, consecrated virgins, in a world where egoism and the search for pleasure would become law, be the guardians of purity, unselfishness and piety. Jesus who has given to conjugal love all its plenitudes, has also exalted the renouncement of human love when this is for the sake of divine love and for the service of all.","Uxores, matres familiarum, primae humani generis educatrices in familiari convictu ac secretiore, tradite filiis et filiabus vestris maiorum vestrorum traditiones, simul autem eas parate ad inscrutabile futurum. Mementote semper matrem per suos liberos ad illud futurum pertinere, quod forsitan ipsa non videbit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p547","Nuntius ad Mulieres","Lastly, women in trial, who stand upright at the foot of the cross like Mary, you who so often in history have given to men the strength to battle unto the very end and to give witness to the point of martyrdom, aid them now still once more to retain courage in their great undertakings, while at the same time maintaining patience and an esteem for humble beginnings.","Et vos, mulieres solae viventes, percipite quid efficere possitis per vestram deditam vocationem. Societas undique vos appellat. Ne familiae quidem sine auxilio vivere possunt earum qui carent familia. Maxime vos, virgines sacrae, in mundo, in quo egoismus et voluptatis appetitus lex fieret, estote custodes puritatis, abnegationis et pietatis. Iesus qui amori coniugali plenitudines suas omnes tribuit, etiam renuntiationem amoris humani extulit, cum haec divino amori et servitio omnium fiat."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p548","Nuntius ad Mulieres","Women, you do know how to make truth sweet, tender and accessible, make it your task to bring the spirit of this council into institutions, schools, homes and daily life. Women of the entire universe, whether Christian or non-believing, you to whom life is entrusted at this grave moment in history, it is for you to save the peace of the world.","Postremo, vos mulieres in tribulatione, quae pedi Crucis veluti Maria erectae stabatis, vos quae in historia totiens viribus homines donastis, ut ad extremum usque pugnarent et usque ad martyrium testimonium perhiberent, eos hodie rursus adiuvate ad retinendam fortitudinem in magnis inceptis, servata simul patientia et erga humilia initia observantia."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p549","Nuntius ad Mulieres","","Mulieres, vos veritatem suavem, mitem et accessibilem facere scitis, curate ut huius Concilii spiritus in instituta, scholas, domos et cotidianam vitam inducatur. Mulieres totius universi, sive christianae sive non credentes, quibus hoc gravi historiae momento vita concreditur, vestrum est pacem mundi servare."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p550","Nuntius ad Pauperes, Infirmos et Adflictos","To all of you, brothers in trial, who are visited by suffering under a thousand forms, the council has a very special message. It feels on itself your pleading eyes, burning with fever or hollow with fatigue, questioning eyes which search in vain for the why of human suffering and which ask anxiously when and whence will come relief.","To the Poor, the Sick and the Suffering (read by Paul Cardinal Meouchi, Maronite-rite patriarch of Antioch; assisted by Stefan Cardinal Wyszynski of Warsaw and Peter Cardinal Doi of Tokyo)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p551","Nuntius ad Pauperes, Infirmos et Adflictos","Very dear brothers, we feel echoing deeply within our hearts as fathers and pastors your laments and your complaints. Our suffering is increased at the thought that it is not within our power to bring you bodily help nor the lessening of your physical sufferings, which physicians, nurses and all those dedicated to the service of the sick are endeavoring to relieve as best they can.","Ad vos omnes, fratres in tentatione constituti, quos mille modis vis doloris adit, peculiarem habet nuntium Concilium. Sentit in se vestros supplicantes oculos vel morbo ardentes vel lassitudine cavos, interrogantes oculos qui frustra humanorum dolorum quaerunt quare et sollicite rogant quando et unde veniet levamen."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p552","Nuntius ad Pauperes, Infirmos et Adflictos","But we have something deeper and more valuable to give you, the only truth capable of answering the mystery of suffering and of bringing you relief without illusion, and that is faith and union with the Man of Sorrows, with Christ the Son of God, nailed to the cross for our sins and for our salvation. Christ did not do away with suffering. He did not even wish to unveil to us entirely the mystery of suffering. He took suffering upon Himself and this is enough to make you understand all its value. All of you who feel heavily the weight of the cross, you who are poor and abandoned, you who weep, you who are persecuted for justice, you who are ignored, you the unknown victims of suffering, take courage. You are the preferred children of the kingdom of God, the kingdom of hope, happiness and life. You are the brothers of the suffering Christ, and with Him, if you wish, you are saving the world.","Carissimi fratres, penitus in cordibus nostris, ut patrum et pastorum, querelas vestras et conquestiones vestras resonantes sentimus. Dolor noster augetur cogitantes non esse in nostra potestate vobis ferre adiumentum corporale neque minorationem corporalium dolorum vestrorum, quam medici, aegrorum ministrae omnesque aegrorum servitio dediti, pro viribus mitigare student."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p553","Nuntius ad Pauperes, Infirmos et Adflictos","This is the Christian science of suffering, the only one which gives peace. Know that you are not alone, separated, abandoned or useless. You have been called by Christ and are His living and transparent image. In His name, the council salutes you lovingly, thanks you, assures you of the friendship and assistance of the Church, and blesses you.","At habemus altius aliquid et pretiosius vobis donandum, unicam veritatem doloris mysterio respondentem et vobis sine fallacia levamen afferentem: fidem nempe et coniunctionem cum Viro dolorum, cum Christo, Dei Filio, propter nostra peccata et propter nostram salutem Cruci affixo. Christus non abstulit dolorem. Neque nobis totum doloris aperire mysterium voluit. In se suscepit dolorem et hoc satis est ut vos omnem eius valorem intellegatis. Vos omnes qui crucis pondus graviter sentitis, vos pauperes et derelicti, vos qui fletis, vos qui propter iustitiam persecutionem patimini, vos qui ignoramini, vos ignotae victimae doloris, bono animo estote. Estis praedilecti filii regni Dei, regni spei, laetitiae et vitae. Estis fratres Christi dolentis et cum Ipso, si vultis, mundum salvificatis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p554","Nuntius ad Pauperes, Infirmos et Adflictos","","Haec est christiana doloris scientia, unica quae pacem donat. Scitote vos non solos esse, seiunctos, derelictos aut inutiles. A Christo vocati estis estisque Eius viva et perspicua imago. In Eius nomine, Concilium vos amanter salutat, vobis gratias agit, amicitiae et adiumenti Ecclesiae vos certiores facit, vobisque benedicit."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p555","Nuntius ad Operarios","In the course of this council, we, the Catholic bishops of the five continents, have, among many other subjects, reflected together on the grave questions posed for human conscience by the economic and social conditions of the contemporary world, the coexistence of nations, the problem of armaments, of war and peace. We are fully aware of the repercussions which the solution provided for these problems can have on the concrete life of the working men and women of the entire world. Thus, at the end of our deliberations, we wish to address to all of them a message of confidence, peace and friendship.","To Workers (read by Paul Cardinal Zoungrana of Ouagadougou, Upper Volta, assisted by Jose Cardinal Quintero of Caracas, Venezuela, and Jose Cardinal Bueno y Monreal of Seville, Spain)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p556","Nuntius ad Operarios","Very loved sons, rest assured first of all that the Church is aware of your sufferings, your struggles and your hopes, and that she appreciates highly the virtues which ennoble your souls — namely courage, dedication, professional conscience, love of justice — and that she recognizes fully the immense services which, each in his own place and in positions often the most obscure and the most ignored, you render to the whole of society. The Church is grateful to you for this and thanks you through our voice.","In hoc Concilii decursu, Nos, catholici Episcopi quinque continentium, inter multa alia simul cogitavimus de gravibus quaestionibus, quae humanae conscientiae ab oeconomicis et socialibus condicionibus hodierni mundi, a nationum coexistentia, a quaestione armorum, belli et pacis proponuntur. Probe conscii sumus repercussionum quas harum problematum solutio afferre potest in concretam operariorum et operariarum totius orbis vitam. Itaque, exeuntibus nostris deliberationibus, ad eos omnes nuntium fiduciae, pacis et amicitiae dirigere cupimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p557","Nuntius ad Operarios","In these recent years, she has never ceased to keep before her eyes the increasingly complex problems of the working world and the echo which recent pontifical encyclicals have found in your ranks has proved to what degree the soul of the working man of our time was attuned to that of his highest spiritual leaders. Pope John XXIII who enriched the patrimony of the Church with his incomparable messages knew how to find the road to your heart. He, in his own person, gave a shining example of the Church's love for the working man as well as for truth, justice, liberty and charity, on which is founded the peace of the world. We wish also to be before you witnesses of this love of the Church for you working men, and we declare to you with all the conviction of our souls: The Church is your friend. Have confidence in her. In the past, regrettable misunderstandings have, over too long a period, maintained a spirit of mistrust and lack of understanding between us, and both the Church and the working class have suffered from this. Today the hour for reconciliation has sounded and the Church of the council invites you to celebrate this hour without suspicion.","Dilectissimi filii, imprimis hoc certum habete: Ecclesiam dolores vestros, certationes vestras spesque vestras non latere eamque magni aestimare virtutes illas, quae animos vestros nobilitant — id est animum, dedicationem, conscientiam professionalem, iustitiae amorem — planeque agnoscere immensa officia, quae, unusquisque suo in loco et saepe in obscurissimis neglectissimisque muneribus, toti societati praestatis. Hac de re gratam esse Ecclesiam vobisque per nostram vocem gratias agere."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p558","Nuntius ad Operarios","The Church is ever seeking to understand you better. But on your part you must endeavor to understand what the Church means for you, working men, who are the chief artisans of the prodigious changes which the world is undergoing today. For you know full well that unless a mighty spiritual inspiration animates these changes, they will cause disaster for humanity instead of bringing it happiness. It is not hatred which serves the world. It is not only the bread of this earth which can satisfy man's hunger. Thus, accept the message of the Church. Accept the faith which she offers you to light your path. It is the faith of the successor of Peter and of the 2,000 bishops assembled in council. It is the faith of the Christian people. May it be your light. May it be your guide. May it bring you to the knowledge of Jesus Christ, your Companion in work, Master and Savior of the whole human race.","Hisce proximis annis, numquam destitit ante oculos habere problemata, magis in dies implicata, operarii mundi necnon argumento fuit resonantis in vestris ordinibus recentium Litterarum Encyclicarum Pontificiarum, qua probatum est quatenus animus hodierni operarii cum animo summi sui ducis spiritalis consonaret. Summus Pontifex Ioannes XXIII, qui inaestimabilibus suis nuntiis Ecclesiae patrimonium auxit, viam ad cor vestrum invenire novit. Ipse sua in persona praeclarum dedit exemplum amoris Ecclesiae erga operarium necnon erga veritatem, iustitiam, libertatem et caritatem, in quibus pax mundi fundatur. Nos quoque huius amoris Ecclesiae in vos operarios testes esse cupimus vobisque omni animi nostri persuasione declaramus: Ecclesia amica vestra est. In ea fiduciam habete. Praeterito tempore, dolendi errores, nimium diu diffidentiae et inintellegentiae inter nos animum foverunt, et ex eo tam Ecclesia quam operaria classis passa est. Hodie reconciliatonis hora cecinit et Ecclesia Concilii vos invitat ut hanc horam sine suspicione celebretis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p559","Nuntius ad Operarios","","Ecclesia contendit semper ut vos melius intellegat. Sed vos, vicissim, eniti debetis ad intellegendum quid Ecclesia vobis operariis significet, qui praecipui estis effectores mirabilium mutationum, quibus hodie mundus subicitur. Optime enim nostis, nisi ingens spiritalis animatio has mutationes vivificet, hominibus potius exitium, quam laetitiam ea allaturas esse. Non odium mundo prodest. Non solus panis terrae famem hominis explere potest. Itaque nuntium Ecclesiae accipite. Accipite fidem, quam vobis offert, ut vestram illustret viam. Fides est Petri successoris et duorum milium episcoporum in Concilium congregationum. Fides est populi christiani. Haec vestra lux sit; haec sit vobis dux; haec vos perducat ad Iesum Christum cognoscendum, socium vestrum in labore, Magistrum et Salvatorem totius generis humani."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p560","Nuntius ad Iuvenes","Lastly, it is to you, young men and women of the world, that the council wishes to address its final message. For it is you who are to receive the torch from the hands of your elders and to live in the world at the period of the most gigantic transformations ever realized in its history. It is you who, receiving the best of the example of the teaching of your parents and your teachers, are to form the society of tomorrow. You will either save yourselves or you will perish with it.","To Youth (read by Gregorio Cardinal Agagianian of the Roman curia, assisted by Joseph Cardinal Ritter of St. Louis and Valerian Cardinal Gracias of Bombay)"],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p561","Nuntius ad Iuvenes","For four years the Church has been working to rejuvenate her image in order to respond the better to the design of her Founder, the great Living One, the Christ who is eternally young. At the term of this imposing re-examination of life, she now turns to you. It is for you, youth, especially for you that the Church now comes through her council to enkindle your light, the light which illuminates the future, your future. The Church is anxious that this society that you are going to build up should respect the dignity, the liberty and the rights of individuals. These individuals are you. The Church is particularly anxious that this society should allow free expansion to her treasure ever ancient and ever new, namely faith, and that your souls may be able to bask freely in its helpful light. She has confidence that you will find such strength and such joy that you will not be tempted, as were some of your elders, to yield to the seductions of egoistic or hedonistic philosophies or to those of despair and annihilation, and that in the face of atheism, a phenomenon of lassitude and old age, you will know how to affirm your faith in life and in what gives meaning to life, that is to say, the certitude of the existence of a just and good God.","Denique ad vos, iuvenes viri ac mulieres mundi, vult Concilium suum ultimum dirigere nuntium. Vos enim estis, qui facem de manibus maiorum accepturi estis et in mundo victuri tempore immanissimarum transformationum, quae umquam in historia factae sunt. Vos estis, qui, optimo accepto exemplorum ac magisterii parentum praeceptorumque vestrorum, societatem crastini diei formaturi estis. Aut vosmet ipsos servabitis, aut una cum ea peribitis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p562","Nuntius ad Iuvenes","It is in the name of this God and of His Son, Jesus, that we exhort you to open your hearts to the dimensions of the world, to heed the appeal of your brothers, to place your youthful energies at their service. Fight against all egoism. Refuse to give free course to the instincts of violence and hatred which beget wars and all their train of miseries. Be generous, pure, respectful and sincere, and build in enthusiasm a better world than your elders had.","Iam per quadriennium Ecclesia laboravit, ut effigiem suam redderet iuveniliorem, eo melius ut Conditoris sui consilio responderet, Magni Viventis, Christi, qui est aeternum iuvenis. Exeunte hac magna recognitione vitae, nunc ad vos se vertit. Ad vos, iuvenes, potissimum nunc Ecclesia venit, per suum Concilium, ad vestram accendendam lucem, lucem quae futurum illuminat, vestrum futurum. Ecclesiae curae est ut societas, quam constructuri estis, dignitatem, libertatem et iura personarum observet. Hae personae vos estis. Peculiari sollicitudine Ecclesia urgetur ut eius thesaurus, antiquus semper semperque novus, nempe fides, libere in hac societate dilatetur, eiusque salutari lumine animi vestri libere frui possint. Firmam fiduciam habet vos tantam fortitudinem tantumque gaudium inventuros, ut — quemadmodum aliqui natu maiores vestri — neque philosophiarum egoisticarum aut voluptariae consectationis neque desperationis ac perditionis illecebris cedatis, et ut, coram atheismo, defatigationis et senectutis phaenomeno, fidem vestram in vitam et in id quod vitae sensum tribuit, scilicet certitudinem exsistentiae iusti et boni Dei, affirmare sciatis."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p563","Nuntius ad Iuvenes","The Church looks to you with confidence and with love. Rich with a long past ever living in her, and marching on toward human perfection in time and the ultimate destinies of history and of life, the Church is the real youth of the world. She possesses what constitutes the strength and the charm of youth, that is to say the ability to rejoice with what is beginning, to give oneself unreservedly, to renew one's self and to set out again for new conquests. Look upon the Church and you will find in her the face of Christ, the genuine, humble and wise Hero, the prophet of truth and love, the companion and friend of youth. It is in the name of Christ that we salute you, that we exhort and bless you.","In huius Dei Eiusque Filii Iesu nomine vos adhortamur ut corda vestra amplitudinibus mundi aperiatis, ut fratrum vestrorum appellationi aurem praebeatis, ut vestras iuveniles vires eorum servitio addicatis. Pugnate contra omnem egoismum. Nolite liberum cursum instinctibus violentiae et odii permittere, quae bella pariunt et omnem miseriarum comitatum. Este generosi, puri, observantes, sinceri, et aedificate studio pleni mundum meliorem eo quem maiores vestri habuerunt."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p564","Nuntius ad Iuvenes","The Second Vatican Ecumenical Council, assembled in the Holy Spirit and under the protection of the Blessed Virgin Mary, whom we have declared Mother of the Church, and of St. Joseph, her glorious spouse, and of the Apostles SS. Peter and Paul, must be numbered without doubt among the greatest events of the Church. In fact it was the largest in the number of Fathers who came to the seat of Peter from every part of the world, even from those places where the hierarchy has been very recently established. It was the richest because of the questions which for four sessions have been discussed carefully and profoundly. And last of all it was the most opportune, because, bearing in mind the necessities of the present day, above all it sought to meet the pastoral needs and, nourishing the flame of charity, it has made a great effort to reach not only the Christians still separated from communion with the Holy See, but also the whole human family.","Ecclesia vos respicit cum fiducia et cum amore. Dives longi praeteriti, in se semper viventis, et ad humanam perfectionem in tempore contendens et ad ultimos historiae vitaeque fines, Ecclesia vera est iuventus mundi. Ea possidet quod iuventutis robur ac venustatem constituit, scilicet facultatem gaudendi cum eo quod incipit, se sine ulla retractione donandi, se renovandi, et ad novas expugnationes proficiscendi. In Ecclesiam intuemini et in ea reperietis vultum Christi, germanum, humilem sapientemque Heroem, prophetam veritatis et amoris, comitem et amicum iuventutis. In Christi nomine vos salutamus, vos adhortamur, vobis benedicimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p565","Litterae Apostolicae in Spiritu Sancto Quibus Concilium Vaticanum II Clauditur","At last all which regards the holy ecumenical council has, with the help of God, been accomplished and all the constitutions, decrees, declarations and votes have been approved by the deliberation of the synod and promulgated by us. Therefore we decided to close for all intents and purposes, with our apostolic authority, this same ecumenical council called by our predecessor, Pope John XXIII, which opened October 11, 1962, and which was continued by us after his death.","Concilium Oecumenicum Vaticanum II, in Spiritu Sancto congregatum et sub praesidio Beatae Mariae Virginis, quam Matrem Ecclesiae declaramus, et sancti Ioseph, eiusdem gloriosi Sponsi, et sanctorum Apostolorum Petri et Pauli, inter maxima Ecclesiae eventa haud dubie est numerandum. Fuit enim numero maximum propter Patres, qui ad Petri sedem ex omnibus terrarum partibus convenerunt, iis etiam ex locis, ubi Episcoporum ordo recentissime est constitutus; ditissimum propter quaestiones, quae per quattuor sessiones accurate ac penitus sunt discussae; postremo aptissimum, quia, habita ratione hodiernarum necessitatum, in primis pastoralibus necessitatibus prospicere studuit et, caritatis flamma refovens, magnopere contendit ut non solum christianos, qui adhuc ab Apostolicae Sedis communione seiuncti sunt, sed universam etiam humanam familiam attingeret."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p566","Litterae Apostolicae in Spiritu Sancto Quibus Concilium Vaticanum II Clauditur","We decided moreover that all that has been established synodally is to be religiously observed by all the faithful, for the glory of God and the dignity of the Church and for the tranquillity and peace of all men. We have approved and established these things, decreeing that the present letters are and remain stable and valid, and are to have legal effectiveness, so that they be disseminated and obtain full and complete effect, and so that they may be fully convalidated by those whom they concern or may concern now and in the future; and so that, as it be judged and described, all efforts contrary to these things by whomsoever or whatever authority, knowingly or in ignorance be invalid and worthless from now on.","Tandem Deo adiuvante, omnia, quae ad sacrum Concilium Oecumenicum spectant, perfecta sunt et universae Constitutiones, Decreta, Declarationes, suffragia synodali decreto comprobata ac a Nobis sunt promulgata. Quapropter idem Concilium Oecumenicum a fel. rec. Decessore Nostro Ioanne Papa XXIII indictum, die XI mensis octobris anno MCMLXII initum ac post eius mortem a Nobis continuatum, ex omni effectu, auctoritate Nostra Apostolica concludere statuimus."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p567","Litterae Apostolicae in Spiritu Sancto Quibus Concilium Vaticanum II Clauditur","Given in Rome at St. Peter's, under the [seal of the] ring of the fisherman, Dec. 8, on the feast of the Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary, the year 1965, the third year of our pontificate.","Decernimus praetorea ut omnia, quae synodali ratione sancita sunt, ab omnibus fidelibus religiose serventur, ad Dei gloriam et Ecclesiae dignitatem, ad omnium hominum tranquillitatem et pacem. Haec denique Nos comprobavimus atque constituimus, decernentes has Litteras firmas, validas atque efficaces semper exstare ac permanere, atque ab iis, ad quos pertinent aut pertinere poterunt nunc et in posterum, plenarie ab omnibus observari, et, prout iudicatum et descriptum erit, ab iis, ad quos pertinent aut pertinere poterunt, obtineri; sicque rite iudicari et definiri oportere, atque irritum esse et inane, si secus super his a quoquam, quavis auctoritate, scienter vel ignoranter contigerit attentari."],["vol2/vatican-ii/part-6/index.html","p568","Litterae Apostolicae in Spiritu Sancto Quibus Concilium Vaticanum II Clauditur","","Datum Romae, apud Sanctum Petrum, sub anulo Piscatoris, die VIII mensis Decembris, in festo Immaculatae Conceptionis Beatae Mariae Virginis, anno MCMLXV, Pontificatus Nostri tertio."]];